const COURSES = [{"id":"earth","name":"Earth","glyph":"🌍","pat":"topo","kind":"facts"},{"id":"astro","name":"Astronomy","glyph":"🔭","pat":"star","kind":"facts"},{"id":"physical","name":"Physical Science","glyph":"🧲","pat":"wave","kind":"facts"},{"id":"micro","name":"Microbiology","glyph":"🦠","pat":"cell","kind":"facts"},{"id":"ancient","name":"Ancient History","glyph":"🏺","pat":"brick","kind":"facts"},{"id":"america","name":"American History","glyph":"🦅","pat":"scroll","kind":"facts"},{"id":"utah","name":"Utah History","glyph":"🐝","pat":"topo","kind":"facts"},{"id":"world","name":"World History","glyph":"🌐","pat":"star2","kind":"facts"},{"id":"economics","name":"Economics","glyph":"🪙","pat":"coin","kind":"facts"},{"id":"body","name":"Your Body","glyph":"🩺","pat":"pulse","kind":"facts"},{"id":"animals","name":"Animals","glyph":"🦁","pat":"fur","kind":"species"},{"id":"plants","name":"Plants","glyph":"🌻","pat":"leaf","kind":"species"}];
globalThis.COURSES = COURSES;
const LESSONS = [{"id":"earth-rocks","course":"earth","kind":"facts","section":"rocks","title":"Rocks & Crystals","glyph":"💎","n":12,"idea":"Almost half of every rock is oxygen, and salt is a mineral. Look closer at the ground, and find out why a ruby and a sapphire are the same stone.","pages":[{"icon":"🪨","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Pick up a rock and hold it. Here is a secret about what is inside. Nearly half of it, by weight, is oxygen, the same element you are breathing right now, locked into minerals instead of floating loose."},{"t":"fact","text":"Oxygen makes up 46 percent of Earth's crust. Silicon is another 28 percent, and aluminum is about 8. Most rock is those three joined together, with some iron mixed in.","label":"By weight"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which element makes up the biggest share of Earth's crust?","options":["Iron","Silicon","Oxygen"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the first number on the page. It is the same element you breathe.","explain":"Oxygen, at 46 percent. Together with silicon and aluminum, it makes up more than four fifths of everything you walk on."}}]},{"icon":"🔍","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Look at a polished granite countertop. All those speckles look like one speckled stone. They are not."},{"t":"fact","text":"Each speck is a separate mineral, grown together with its neighbors. Glassy gray grains are quartz. Blocky pink or white ones are feldspar. The black flecks are mica, and they peel off in shiny sheets."},{"t":"try","text":"Put your eye close to a granite counter, step or gravestone. You can usually pick out three or four different kinds of grain without a magnifier."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What are the speckles you see in granite?","options":["Separate minerals grown together","Paint that was added to the stone","Tiny pieces of glass"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the page again. Quartz, feldspar and mica are all in there.","explain":"Granite is a mixture. You are looking at several minerals, each with its own look."}}]},{"icon":"🧂","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Here is a mineral you have probably already eaten today."},{"t":"term","word":"halite","text":"The proper name for table salt. It is a rock that people eat every day."},{"t":"fact","text":"Halite always breaks along the same three directions, so its grains come apart into tiny cubes."},{"t":"try","text":"Tip a little salt onto dark paper and look closely. The cubes are right there."}]},{"icon":"✋","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"How do you tell one mineral from another? You find out what scratches what."},{"t":"fact","text":"A mineral's hardness is really a list of what it can scratch. Talc, number 1, gives way to a fingernail. Quartz, number 7, cuts glass. Diamond sits at 10, and the only thing that reliably scratches a diamond is another diamond.","label":"The hardness scale"},{"t":"try","text":"Your fingernail is about 2.5 on the scale, and a copper coin is about 3. A rock your nail marks is softer than calcite. A rock that shrugs off a steel nail is harder than about 5.5."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"A rock shrugs off a steel nail. What do you know about it?","options":["It is softer than your fingernail","It is harder than about 5.5","It must be a diamond"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the try it box. The steel nail sits at about 5.5.","explain":"It is harder than about 5.5. That is all this test tells you. It does not say which mineral it is."}}]},{"icon":"📏","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The scale is named for Friedrich Mohs, a German mineral expert. In 1812 he lined up ten minerals in the order of which scratched which. He never measured a force or built a machine. He just made a ranking, and it is still in every geology kit."},{"t":"fact","text":"The scale looks like a ruler, but it is not one. Corundum, a very hard mineral, is 9 and diamond is 10, one step apart. Yet measured properly with a hard point, diamond resists about four times better than corundum.","label":"Not a ruler"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Corundum is 9 and diamond is 10. How much harder is diamond, really?","options":["Exactly one step harder","Just a tiny bit harder","About four times harder"],"answer":2,"hint":"The numbers are only an order of finish, not a measurement.","explain":"About four times better at resisting a hard point. The gaps between the ten steps are all different sizes."}}]},{"icon":"💎","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Now a magic trick. Ruby and sapphire are the same mineral."},{"t":"term","word":"corundum","text":"The mineral that both rubies and sapphires are. It is aluminum and oxygen."},{"t":"fact","text":"A trace of chromium turns it red, and it gets called ruby. Traces of iron and titanium turn it blue, and it gets called sapphire. Same hardness, same recipe, a different pinch of something extra, and a wildly different price."}]},{"icon":"🌋","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Volcanoes make rocks that do surprising things. Start with glass."},{"t":"fact","text":"Obsidian is glass that a volcano made. The lava cooled so fast that its minerals never got to line up into crystals. With no grain to follow, a flake of obsidian can come to an edge a few nanometers across. A nanometer is a billionth of a meter, and that edge is far thinner than a steel razor's. People chipped it into knives and arrowheads."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why can obsidian get such a sharp edge?","options":["It is the hardest mineral there is","It has no crystal grain to follow","It is heated until it is soft"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the second sentence. It says what the minerals never got to do.","explain":"The minerals never lined up into crystals, so it breaks to an edge only a few nanometers across."}}]},{"icon":"🫧","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"And here is a rock that floats."},{"t":"fact","text":"Pumice is rock full of frozen gas bubbles. After some eruptions, whole rafts of it drift across the open sea, and ships and satellites track them for months. In the end the pumice waterlogs and sinks."},{"t":"try","text":"Drop a piece in a glass of water and it bobs. Leave it long enough, and water works into the holes and it goes under."}]},{"icon":"🕳️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Now the biggest crystals anyone has ever found."},{"t":"fact","text":"Miners at Naica in Mexico broke into a chamber packed with clear gypsum beams. The largest measure about 37 feet (11 m) long and 13 feet (4 m) thick, and weigh roughly 55 tons. They are single crystals.","label":"Naica, Mexico"},{"t":"p","text":"The chamber sits near 136 degrees F (58 C), and the air is almost completely saturated with water. Even in cooled suits, people could only stay for a short time, well under an hour. In October 2015 the mine's pumps were switched off, and the water came back and covered the crystals again."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How long could people stay inside the Naica crystal chamber, even in cooled suits?","options":["A whole working day","Several hours","Well under an hour"],"answer":2,"hint":"The chamber sits near 136 degrees F, and the air is full of water.","explain":"Well under an hour at a time. It was far too hot and wet to stay longer."}}]},{"icon":"📜","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Genesis names minerals early. Describing the land of Havilah in chapter 2, it lists gold, bdellium and onyx stone."},{"t":"fact","text":"Onyx is banded quartz, number 7 on the hardness scale, and people still cut and polish it."},{"t":"p","text":"So the next time you pick up a rock, look closer. Grains, cubes, a pinch of chromium. There is a lot to read in one small stone."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Obsidian","Glass that a volcano made"],["Halite","Breaks into tiny cubes"],["Corundum","The mineral behind ruby and sapphire"],["Naica","The Mexican mine of giant crystals"],["Pumice","Full of bubbles, so it floats"],["Mica","Black flecks that peel in shiny sheets"]]},"picgame":{"type":"tapall","title":"Rock Hunt","goal":"Tap everything that is a rock or mineral.","items":[{"icon":"🪨","name":"rock","good":true},{"icon":"🧂","name":"salt","good":true},{"icon":"💎","name":"diamond","good":true},{"icon":"🍎","name":"apple","good":false},{"icon":"🥛","name":"milk","good":false},{"icon":"🌲","name":"tree","good":false},{"icon":"🐟","name":"fish","good":false},{"icon":"🧸","name":"teddy bear","good":false}]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"On the Mohs hardness scale, what does a mineral's number actually tell you?","options":["What it can scratch","How much it weighs","How old it is"],"answer":0,"explain":"Talc (1) gives way to a fingernail; quartz (7) cuts glass; only another diamond scratches a diamond (10)."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How did Friedrich Mohs come up with his hardness scale in 1812?","options":["He lined up ten minerals by what scratched what","He measured the exact force needed with a machine","He weighed each mineral on a scale"],"answer":0,"explain":"He never measured a force — he just ranked ten samples and wrote the order down."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Ruby and sapphire are actually the same mineral. Which one?","options":["Corundum","Quartz","Halite"],"answer":0,"explain":"A trace of chromium turns corundum red (ruby); iron and titanium turn it blue (sapphire)."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why does obsidian have no crystal grain at all?","options":["Lava cooled too fast for crystals to form","It's a mix of several different minerals","It's volcanic rock that was never melted"],"answer":0,"explain":"With no grain to follow, a flake can come to an edge thinner than a steel razor."},{"type":"tf","prompt":"Table salt is actually a mineral called halite.","answer":0,"explain":"It's soft — about 2 to 2.5 on the hardness scale — which is why it crushes under a spoon."}]},{"id":"earth-volcanoes","course":"earth","kind":"facts","section":"volcanoes","title":"Volcanoes","glyph":"🌋","n":12,"idea":"Two words mean almost the same thing, and mixing them up is a trap. Find out how anyone knows what is hiding under a volcano when nobody has ever looked.","pages":[{"icon":"🌋","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Two words that mean almost the same thing, and mixing them up is a trap: magma and lava."},{"t":"term","word":"magma","text":"The melted rock underground."},{"t":"term","word":"lava","text":"The melted rock once it comes out of the volcano."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is melted rock called once it comes out of the vent?","options":["Magma","Rock","Lava"],"answer":2,"hint":"It changes its name when it leaves the ground.","explain":"Once the melted rock is out, it is called lava."}}]},{"icon":"🏔️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Here’s a surprise: measured from its base on the sea floor, Mauna Kea in Hawaii is taller than Mount Everest."},{"t":"fact","text":"Mauna Kea rises about 30,610 feet (9,330 m) from the sea floor. Everest stands 29,032 feet (8,849 m) above sea level."},{"t":"p","text":"But most of Mauna Kea is underwater. Only 13,803 feet (4,207 m) of it sticks out above the water."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Measured from the sea floor, which mountain is taller?","options":["Mount Everest","Mauna Kea","They are the same height"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the first number on the page.","explain":"Mauna Kea rises about 30,610 feet from the sea floor, making it taller than Everest."}}]},{"icon":"🎬","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Films often get it wrong. Most lava flows creep along slower than you walk, and people stand next to them taking photographs."},{"t":"fact","text":"The real killer is the pyroclastic flow: a ground-hugging cloud of hot ash and gas that comes down the slope faster than 50 mph (80 km/h)."},{"t":"p","text":"That is what reached Pompeii and Herculaneum. Volcanologists worry less about where the lava is going and more about whether the eruption column is about to collapse."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which volcano hazard is the real killer?","options":["The pyroclastic flow","The glowing lava river","The small earthquakes before the eruption"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the fact box for a description of the killer.","explain":"The pyroclastic flow moves faster than 50 mph and can devastate everything in its path."}}]},{"icon":"🔥","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"How hot is lava? At Kilauea in Hawaii, it comes out around 2,140 degrees F (1,170 C)."},{"t":"fact","text":"That temperature is not guessed at. Geologists walk up to the flow and push a metal probe called a thermocouple straight into it."},{"t":"p","text":"Some flows cannot be safely reached. Then they point calibrated thermal cameras at those instead. The camera readings are checked against the ones taken with the probe."}]},{"icon":"⚫","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Most lava glows red hot, but one volcano spits out something much cooler."},{"t":"fact","text":"Ol Doinyo Lengai in Tanzania erupts the coolest lava on Earth, about 930 to 1,110 degrees F (500 to 600 C). It is cool enough that it does not glow in daylight."},{"t":"p","text":"This lava pours out looking like runny black oil. When rain hits it, the hardened flows turn chalk white."},{"t":"p","text":"Most lava is made of the same minerals as ordinary rock. Ol Doinyo Lengai's lava is made of sodium and potassium salts instead. That is why it is so runny and cool, and why water turns it white."}]},{"icon":"📜","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The eruption that buried Pompeii in AD 79 had an eyewitness. Pliny the Younger watched it from across the bay."},{"t":"term","word":"Plinian","text":"A type of eruption with a tall column punching straight up into the sky, named after Pliny the Younger's description."},{"t":"fact","text":"Pliny the Younger wrote two letters to historian Tacitus describing a cloud shaped like an umbrella pine. It had a tall trunk with branches spreading at the top."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did Pliny say the cloud looked like?","options":["A tall wall of water","A volcanic cone","An umbrella pine"],"answer":2,"hint":"His description mentions a tall trunk with branches spreading at the top.","explain":"Pliny described the cloud as looking like an umbrella pine."}}]},{"icon":"💥","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Krakatoa exploded on the morning of 27 August 1883."},{"t":"fact","text":"People 3,000 miles away on Rodrigues Island heard a distant roar that sounded like heavy guns. Perth, Australia, heard it too, and it was 1,930 miles off."},{"t":"p","text":"Barographs recorded something strange happening. These instruments scratch a line of air pressure onto paper. The lines showed the pressure wave passing seven times over five days."},{"t":"p","text":"It went out to the far side of the world and back, out and back, until it faded. One island's blast had been felt around the whole planet, more than once."}]},{"icon":"🥶","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Tambora in Indonesia erupted in April 1815 and blew about 4,200 feet off its own summit."},{"t":"fact","text":"The next year, farmers in New England wrote down frost in every single month and a snowstorm in June. They called 1816 the year without a summer, and also Eighteen Hundred and Froze to Death."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did people call 1816?","options":["The year of the great flood","The year without a summer","The year of endless sunshine"],"answer":1,"hint":"People wrote down frost in every month that year.","explain":"They called it the year without a summer because there was frost in every single month and even snow in June."}}]},{"icon":"📊","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"How many volcanoes are out there? About 1,350 on land could erupt."},{"t":"fact","text":"Of those, about 500 have actually erupted within written history. On a typical day, roughly 20 are erupting somewhere in the world."},{"t":"p","text":"About 160 of those volcanoes sit in the United States and its territories. That is why the USGS keeps watch on them with volcano observatories in Hawaii, Alaska, California, Washington, and Yellowstone."}]},{"icon":"🔍","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Here is the puzzle: nobody has ever looked into a magma chamber."},{"t":"fact","text":"Their size and depth are worked out from how earthquake waves slow down when passing through melted rock and from the ground swelling by a few inches before an eruption.","label":"Worked out, not seen"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How do we know about magma chambers?","options":["Worked out from earthquake waves slowing and swelling ground","By drilling down and sending a camera into each chamber","By melting a piece of cooled lava and timing how it hardens"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It tells you what scientists measure.","explain":"Earthquake waves slow down in melted rock, and the ground swells before an eruption. These clues help scientists work out the details."}}]},{"icon":"📜","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Volcanoes throw things up out of the ground. So where does the written account put the water of the Flood?"},{"t":"fact","text":"In Genesis 7, on the same day the fountains of the great deep were broken up and the windows of heaven were opened. The water out of the ground is named first.","label":"The written account says"}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Magma chamber","Worked out from earthquake waves"],["Plinian eruption","Named after Pliny the Younger's description"],["Ol Doinyo Lengai","Erupts coolest lava on Earth"],["Tambora","Caused the year without a summer"],["USGS","Keeps watch on volcanoes in the United States"]]},"picgame":{"type":"catch","title":"Volcano Catch","goal":"Catch what comes out of a volcano.","items":[{"icon":"🔥","name":"lava","good":true},{"icon":"☁️","name":"ash cloud","good":true},{"icon":"💨","name":"hot gas","good":true},{"icon":"🪨","name":"rock","good":true},{"icon":"🍎","name":"apple","good":false},{"icon":"🎈","name":"balloon","good":false},{"icon":"⭐","name":"star","good":false},{"icon":"🧸","name":"teddy bear","good":false}]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What's the real difference between magma and lava?","options":["Magma is underground, lava is the same rock once it's out of the vent","Magma is cooler than lava","Lava only comes from explosive volcanoes"],"answer":0,"explain":"Geologists are strict about this — it's the same melted rock with a different address."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What actually kills people in most volcanic eruptions, according to volcanologists?","options":["A pyroclastic flow of hot ash and gas racing downhill","The slow-moving lava itself","Falling ash alone"],"answer":0,"explain":"Most lava creeps slower than walking pace. The pyroclastic flow that hit Pompeii moved faster than 50 mph."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Nobody has ever seen inside a magma chamber. How do geologists know it's there?","options":["Earthquake waves slow down passing through it, and the ground above swells","Drilling equipment has reached one directly","Satellite cameras can see through rock"],"answer":0,"explain":"Instruments read it. No one has ever looked into one."},{"type":"tf","prompt":"Mauna Kea, measured from the sea floor it stands on, is actually taller than Mount Everest.","answer":0,"explain":"Most of Mauna Kea is underwater, which is why it doesn't get the title."},{"type":"tf","prompt":"The 1815 eruption of Tambora caused New England farmers to record frost in every month the following year.","answer":0,"explain":"1816 became known as 'the year without a summer' — and 'Eighteen Hundred and Froze to Death.'"}]},{"id":"earth-quakes","course":"earth","kind":"facts","section":"quakes","title":"Earthquakes","glyph":"🫨","n":12,"idea":"The ground under your feet never stops moving. Find out how we measure it, and how a ten-year-old on a beach used what she had learned.","pages":[{"icon":"🌍","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Stand still for a moment. It feels like you are not moving at all. But Earth's surface is cracked into giant slabs called plates, and they creep along at about the speed your fingernails grow."},{"t":"fact","text":"Nobody has watched a plate move. But GPS receivers are bolted to bedrock all over the world, and each one reports its own position. Year after year, those positions shift by a few inches.","label":"How we know"},{"t":"try","text":"Draw a line on paper 4 inches long. That is about as far as the quickest plates travel in a whole year. The slowest move about one inch."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What shows us that the plates are moving?","options":["Cameras that film the ground all year","Drills that measure how hot the rock is","GPS receivers bolted to bedrock"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the How we know box. It names what is bolted to the rock.","explain":"Each receiver reports its own position, and those positions keep shifting by a few inches a year."}}]},{"icon":"📈","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Where plates grind past each other, the ground sometimes jerks. That is an earthquake. Most are far too small to feel, and thousands are recorded every single day."},{"t":"term","word":"magnitude","text":"The number that tells you how big an earthquake is. You may hear it called the Richter scale, but today's numbers come from a newer scale that agrees with it in the middle."},{"t":"fact","text":"Across the years 2000 to 2021, the world averaged about 135 quakes of magnitude 6 a year, around 13 of magnitude 7, and roughly one of magnitude 8 or stronger.","label":"Big ones are rare"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"About how many magnitude 7 earthquakes happen in a typical year?","options":["About 1","About 13","About 135"],"answer":1,"hint":"Each step up gets rarer. Magnitude 6 is about 135 a year, and magnitude 8 is about one.","explain":"Around 13 a year. Magnitude 6 is about 135, and 8 or stronger is about one."}}]},{"icon":"🔟","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Here is the surprise about magnitude. The numbers do not climb like a ruler."},{"t":"fact","text":"Every whole step up the scale means the shaking is ten times bigger, and about 32 times as much energy comes out."},{"t":"p","text":"So a magnitude 7 is not a little worse than a 6. It would take roughly 32 sixes to add up to one seven."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"About how many magnitude 6 earthquakes would it take to match the energy of one magnitude 7?","options":["About 10","About 32","About 100"],"answer":1,"hint":"One whole step up is about 32 times the energy.","explain":"About 32. That is why a single step up the scale is such a big jump."}}]},{"icon":"🌊","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"So how big can an earthquake get? The strongest ever measured struck southern Chile, near Valdivia, on 22 May 1960."},{"t":"fact","text":"Magnitude 9.5. The shaking lasted about ten minutes, and hundreds of miles of coastline tore at once.","label":"The record"},{"t":"p","text":"And it was not over. The tsunami it launched was still killing people in Hawaii and Japan a day later."}]},{"icon":"✈️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A tsunami is a wave, but nothing like the ones you jump in at the beach."},{"t":"fact","text":"In deep water it crosses the ocean at over 500 mph, about the speed of an airliner, while lifting the surface only a few feet."},{"t":"p","text":"A ship sitting right on top of one feels nothing unusual. The wave only piles up into something terrible when it reaches shallow water. Buoys in the deep ocean feel it slip underneath and radio a warning to the coast."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"A ship is floating right on top of a tsunami, far out at sea. What does the crew notice?","options":["A wall of water towering over the deck","Almost nothing","The sea draining away"],"answer":1,"hint":"In deep water the wave lifts the surface only a few feet.","explain":"Almost nothing. The wave only piles up when it reaches shallow water."}}]},{"icon":"🏖️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"In 2004, a ten-year-old girl named Tilly Smith was standing on Mai Khao Beach in Thailand. The water looked wrong to her."},{"t":"fact","text":"A tsunami announces itself. Sometimes the sea drains away first and leaves fish stranded. Sometimes the water comes in and in and never goes out, frothing oddly.","label":"The warning signs"},{"t":"p","text":"Tilly knew those signs from a geography lesson two weeks earlier. She told her parents. Her father told a hotel security guard. The beach was cleared before the wave arrived, and Mai Khao was one of very few beaches on Phuket where nobody was killed."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How did Tilly know that something was wrong with the water?","options":["She had felt the ground shake beneath her feet","She had learned the signs in a geography lesson","A lifeguard on the beach warned her to run away"],"answer":1,"hint":"She had learned about it two weeks before.","explain":"A geography lesson, two weeks earlier. What she had learned protected a whole beach."}}]},{"icon":"📈","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Every earthquake sends out two kinds of wave from the same spot at the same moment. One races ahead and one lags behind. The gap between them tells you how far away the quake was."},{"t":"term","word":"P wave","text":"The push-pull wave. It is the fastest through rock, so it always arrives first."},{"t":"term","word":"S wave","text":"The sideways wave. It lags behind the P wave, and it does more damage."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What can you work out from the gap between the P wave and the S wave?","options":["How far away the earthquake was","How many buildings it knocked down","How long the shaking lasted"],"answer":0,"hint":"One wave is faster than the other, so the gap grows with the distance they travel.","explain":"The farther away the quake, the longer the wait between the two waves."}}]},{"icon":"⛏️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Now a puzzle. How far down have people actually been? The deepest hole ever drilled is the Kola borehole in Russia, finished in 1989 at 40,230 feet (12,262 m). That is 7.6 miles straight down."},{"t":"fact","text":"The rock at the bottom was around 356 degrees F (180 C), far hotter than planned, and the drilling stopped. Kola never came close to leaving the crust."},{"t":"p","text":"Nobody has ever been below the crust, and nobody has seen the core. So what do we know about it?"}]},{"icon":"🔔","blocks":[{"t":"fact","text":"Sideways S waves cannot travel through liquid, and they vanish partway down. So the outer core is reasoned to be molten.","label":"Worked out, not dug up"},{"t":"p","text":"In 1936, a Danish seismologist named Inge Lehmann noticed waves arriving where a fully liquid core should have left silence. She argued there was a solid inner core inside the liquid one. No camera and no sample, just wiggles on a chart."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why do we think Earth's outer core is liquid?","options":["A drill brought up melted rock","A camera was lowered into the core","Sideways waves vanish partway down"],"answer":2,"hint":"Nobody has been there. Think about what earthquake waves do.","explain":"S waves cannot pass through liquid, and they vanish partway down. It is worked out, not seen."}}]},{"icon":"🏗️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"People do not just measure earthquakes. They build to survive them."},{"t":"fact","text":"Some buildings do not sit on the ground at all. They sit on thick pads of rubber layered with steel and lead. When the ground jerks sideways, the pads squash and bend sideways, and the building above lags behind, moving far less than the dirt under it."},{"t":"p","text":"Hospitals, museums and emergency centers are built this way in Japan, New Zealand and California."}]},{"icon":"📜","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"So where did the ground come from in the first place?"},{"t":"fact","text":"On day three in Genesis, the waters under the sky are gathered into one place, and dry land appears. Water first, and then land standing out of it, which the account calls Earth.","label":"The written account says"}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["P wave","The push-pull wave that arrives first"],["S wave","The sideways wave that does more damage"],["Chile 1960","The strongest earthquake ever measured"],["Tsunami in deep water","Moves over 500 mph, barely lifts the sea"],["Tilly Smith","The ten-year-old who cleared her beach"],["Kola borehole","The deepest hole ever drilled"]]},"picgame":{"type":"sort","title":"Fast or Slow","goal":"Which one is fast? Which is slow?","bins":[{"icon":"🐆","label":"fast"},{"icon":"🐌","label":"slow"}],"items":[{"icon":"🌊","name":"tsunami","bin":0},{"icon":"✈️","name":"airliner","bin":0},{"icon":"🚄","name":"bullet train","bin":0},{"icon":"🌍","name":"earth plate","bin":1},{"icon":"✋","name":"fingernail","bin":1},{"icon":"🐢","name":"turtle","bin":1}]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why is a magnitude 7 earthquake not just \"a bit worse\" than a magnitude 6?","options":["Each whole step releases about 32 times more energy","The scale just counts how many buildings fall","Magnitude only measures how long the shaking lasts"],"answer":0,"explain":"It would take roughly 32 sixes added together to equal the energy of one seven."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How do scientists know what's happening at the Earth's core, since no one has ever been there?","options":["They work it out from how earthquake waves bend, slow, and stop","Deep drilling has reached the core directly","Heat sensors buried underground measure it"],"answer":0,"explain":"Sideways waves can't travel through liquid and vanish partway down — that's why the outer core is reasoned to be molten."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What actually causes the two different kinds of shaking wave in one earthquake?","options":["Both waves start at the same spot at the same moment, but travel at different speeds","Each wave comes from a separate, smaller earthquake","One wave is caused by the quake, the other by the tsunami"],"answer":0,"explain":"The fast P wave arrives first; the slower, more damaging S wave lags behind — measuring the gap tells you how far away the quake was."},{"type":"tf","prompt":"The strongest earthquake ever measured, in Chile in 1960, was a magnitude 9.5.","answer":0,"explain":"The shaking lasted about ten minutes, and the tsunami it caused was still killing people in Hawaii and Japan a day later."},{"type":"tf","prompt":"Earth's tectonic plates drift at roughly the speed your fingernails grow.","answer":0,"explain":"That's about one to four inches a year, measured by GPS receivers bolted to bedrock."}]},{"id":"earth-water","course":"earth","kind":"facts","section":"water","title":"Oceans & Rivers","glyph":"🌊","n":12,"idea":"Water covers most of Earth, but we know surprisingly little about the sea floor. Find out how deep the ocean can be and what lets people explore its lowest point.","pages":[{"icon":"🥤","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A cold glass of water looks like it is leaking on a warm day. But it is not leaking at all."},{"t":"fact","text":"The water on the outside came out of the air in the room. Air carries invisible water, and chilling it makes the air let go."},{"t":"try","text":"Fill a glass with ice water and leave it a few minutes. Wipe the outside completely dry, then watch it go wet again. Nothing came through the glass. The room handed it over."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where did the water on the outside of the cold glass come from?","options":["Inside the glass","A leak in the glass","The air in the room"],"answer":2,"hint":"The glass was wiped dry and got wet again without any liquid inside leaking out.","explain":"The room's damp air condensed on the cold surface, making the glass look like it was leaking."}}]},{"icon":"🌊","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Most of Earth's water is salt water in the oceans."},{"t":"fact","text":"96.5 percent of Earth’s water is salty ocean water. Of the fresh water, nearly 69 percent is locked up as ice and about 30 percent is underground. Lakes and rivers get the leftovers."},{"t":"term","word":"fresh water","text":"Water that does not have any salt in it. Lakes and rivers are full of fresh water."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where is most of the fresh water on Earth?","options":["Locked up as ice","In lakes and rivers","Flowing underground"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the last two sentences of the fact box. One of them gives the biggest share.","explain":"Nearly 69 percent of fresh water is locked up as ice, making it not available for use."}}]},{"icon":"🏞️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"All the rivers on Earth together hold just a tiny bit of water."},{"t":"fact","text":"Every river put together holds only about one millionth of the planet's water. Even so, rivers supply a large share of the water we drink, wash in, and grow food with, because rain keeps refilling them."},{"t":"p","text":"The Amazon River is a giant. It pours about 7.6 million cubic feet of fresh water into the Atlantic every second, which is roughly one fifth of all river water reaching the sea."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How much of the river water reaching the sea comes from the Amazon?","options":["About one third","About one fifth","About half"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the end of the sentence about the Amazon. It says what share of all river water the Amazon carries.","explain":"The Amazon pours about 7.6 million cubic feet of fresh water into the Atlantic each second, which is roughly one fifth of all river water reaching the sea."}}]},{"icon":"🌙","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"High tide arrives about 50 minutes later each day, just like the moon."},{"t":"fact","text":"The moon takes 24 hours and 50 minutes to come back to the same place in your sky. So does high tide."},{"t":"p","text":"Tide tables are printed a year ahead. Boats stake their keels on those tables being right."}]},{"icon":"⚓","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"In the Bay of Fundy, Canada, the tides rise and fall about 52 feet twice a day."},{"t":"fact","text":"Boats that are floating at breakfast sit on mud by lunchtime."},{"t":"p","text":"Around 100 billion tons of water move in and out with each tide cycle, more than all the rivers in the world combined."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How far do the Fundy tides rise and fall?","options":["About 26 feet","About 52 feet","About 71 feet"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the first number on the page.","explain":"The tides rise and fall about 52 feet twice a day in the Bay of Fundy."}}]},{"icon":"🕳️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The deepest spot in the ocean is called the Challenger Deep."},{"t":"term","word":"Challenger Deep","text":"The lowest point in the ocean, found in the Mariana Trench."},{"t":"fact","text":"It is 35,876 feet (10,935 m) below the surface. Drop Mount Everest into it and more than a mile of water would still cover the summit."}]},{"icon":"🤿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Imagine you are at the bottom of the Challenger Deep. The pressure there is over a thousand times what it is on the surface."},{"t":"fact","text":"The pressure is about eight tons per square inch, enough to crush anything with air inside it, including a person."},{"t":"p","text":"People have still been down there, but they go in thick steel spheres that can stand the crushing force."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What lets people reach the bottom of the Challenger Deep?","options":["Special diving suits made of rubber","Thin plastic bubbles","Thick steel spheres"],"answer":2,"hint":"The thick steel spheres keep the pressure from crushing the people inside.","explain":"The spheres are strong enough to withstand the immense pressure at the bottom."}}]},{"icon":"🗺️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"We know a lot about the ocean, but surprisingly little about the sea floor."},{"t":"fact","text":"The ocean covers about 70 percent of Earth, yet only about a quarter of the sea floor has been mapped in detail with modern sonar. The moon has been mapped in finer detail than our own sea floor."},{"t":"p","text":"Mapping is not the same as seeing. A camera or a person has looked at less than one thousandth of one percent of the deep sea floor, which is an area about the size of Rhode Island."}]},{"icon":"🧭","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A trench in the ocean can be very deep, but how it got there is a story we work out."},{"t":"fact","text":"The depth of a trench is measured, but how it formed is worked out from where earthquakes happen and line up.","label":"The measured part"},{"t":"p","text":"Earthquakes below a trench line up on a slanting sheet that runs down and away. This pattern tells us that one slab of sea floor bends under another."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which part of the trench story is measured?","options":["How deep it is","How it got there","Where it will be in 10 years"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the first sentence of the fact box. It names the part that is measured.","explain":"We measure how deep a trench is. The pattern of earthquakes helps us work out how it formed."}}]},{"icon":"📜","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"In Genesis, water is there before anything has a shape."},{"t":"fact","text":"On day two, the waters are divided into water below and water above. On day three, the water below is gathered into one place so that dry land can appear.","label":"The written account says"},{"t":"p","text":"The written account makes water the setting everything else gets arranged in."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Bay of Fundy","Tides of about 52 feet twice a day"],["Amazon","About a fifth of the river water reaching the sea"],["Challenger Deep","The lowest point in the ocean"],["Tides","Rise and fall with the moon"],["Thick steel spheres","Protect divers from extreme pressure"],["Fresh water","Water without salt in it"]]},"picgame":{"type":"tapall","title":"Water Everywhere","goal":"Tap everything made of water.","items":[{"icon":"🌊","name":"ocean","good":true},{"icon":"🧊","name":"ice","good":true},{"icon":"🏞️","name":"river","good":true},{"icon":"☁️","name":"cloud","good":true},{"icon":"🔥","name":"fire","good":false},{"icon":"🔩","name":"nail","good":false},{"icon":"⚽","name":"ball","good":false},{"icon":"🎸","name":"guitar","good":false}]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What's actually at the bottom of the Challenger Deep, the ocean's deepest known point?","options":["Water pressing in at over a thousand times surface pressure","Nothing — no one has ever measured it directly","A pressure about the same as at sea level"],"answer":0,"explain":"About eight tons on every square inch — enough to crush anything with air inside it."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Of all the fresh water on Earth, where is most of it actually locked up?","options":["As ice","In lakes and rivers","Underground"],"answer":0,"explain":"Nearly 69 percent of fresh water is ice; lakes and rivers get the leftovers — well under one percent of all fresh water."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Scientists are confident ocean trenches form where one slab of sea floor bends under another. What's that explanation actually based on?","options":["The pattern of earthquakes lining up along a slanting sheet underground","Divers who have measured the bending slab directly","Core samples pulled up from the trench floor"],"answer":0,"explain":"The depth of a trench is measured; how it formed is worked out from where the quakes happen, which is a different kind of claim."},{"type":"tf","prompt":"The Amazon River pours more fresh water into the ocean than any other river on Earth.","answer":0,"explain":"About five times the Congo and twelve times the Mississippi — roughly a fifth of all river water reaching the sea."},{"type":"tf","prompt":"More of the Moon's surface has been mapped in detail than the bottom of Earth's own ocean.","answer":0,"explain":"Only about 29 percent of the sea floor has been mapped in detail with modern sonar."}]},{"id":"earth-weather","course":"earth","kind":"facts","section":"weather","title":"Weather","glyph":"⛈️","n":12,"idea":"Lightning heats the air five times hotter than the sun's surface. Find out how to measure the distance of a lightning strike by counting.","pages":[{"icon":"⚡","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A storm is coming. Imagine you see a lightning bolt flash across the sky."},{"t":"fact","text":"A lightning bolt heats the air in its path to about 50,000 F (27,800 C). The surface of the sun is around 10,000 F (5,500 C). So the air inside a lightning channel is roughly five times hotter than the surface of the sun."},{"t":"p","text":"But it only lasts for about a ten-thousandth of a second. Lightning is a huge current of electricity pushing through air, which resists it so much that it heats up the air around it."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How hot is the air in a lightning bolt compared with the sun's surface?","options":["About fifty times hotter","About five times hotter","Only about twice as hot"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the first fact on the page. It tells you the temperatures.","explain":"The air inside a lightning channel is about five times hotter than the surface of the sun."}}]},{"icon":"🔊","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"So what about that boom that follows lightning? Thunder is the sound of air superheated by a lightning bolt blowing outward."},{"t":"fact","text":"Light gets to you almost instantly, but sound crawls at about one mile every five seconds. Count from the flash to the bang, divide by five, and that tells you roughly how many miles off the strike was."},{"t":"try","text":"The next time there's a storm, count one-Mississippi, two-Mississippi from the flash until the thunder arrives. Divide by five for miles."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"You count ten seconds from the flash to the bang. How far away is the strike?","options":["About two miles","About one mile","About five miles"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It tells you how to work it out.","explain":"Counting ten seconds means the lightning strike was about two miles away."}}]},{"icon":"☁️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Look up at a puffy cumulus cloud. It holds around 1.1 million pounds of water, about as much as a loaded jumbo jet."},{"t":"fact","text":"The water is chopped into droplets so small they cannot fall quickly. Rising air underneath keeps shoving them back up, and the droplets are falling extremely slowly."},{"t":"p","text":"Rain starts when those tiny droplets bump into each other and merge. Once they get heavy enough, they win the battle and start to fall."}]},{"icon":"🧊","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Have you ever seen hail? The biggest one in the US fell on Vivian, South Dakota."},{"t":"fact","text":"It was 8 inches across and weighed 1.94 pounds, about the size of a soccer ball made of ice."},{"t":"p","text":"By the time it was measured, it had shrunk a little from sitting in a home freezer. So when it first landed, it was even bigger than that record."}]},{"icon":"🧅","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Cut a big hailstone open and you find layers, like an onion."},{"t":"fact","text":"The old idea was that each layer is one trip up and down inside the storm. That is only a guess. Nobody can follow a hailstone around inside a thundercloud.","label":"Worked out"},{"t":"p","text":"The layers form as the stone drifts through patches of different temperature and wetness. Clear layers mean water landed on the stone faster than it could freeze, so the water spread out and the air bubbles escaped. Cloudy white layers mean it froze on contact and trapped the bubbles."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why can we not say exactly how a hailstone grew?","options":["Scientists have never found a hailstone that has layers","Hailstones are made on the ground, not in the clouds","Nobody can follow one around inside a thundercloud"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the Worked out box. It says nobody can follow a hailstone inside a thundercloud.","explain":"Nobody can follow a hailstone around inside a thundercloud, so we cannot track its exact path and growth."}}]},{"icon":"❄️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Have you ever tried to look at a snow crystal up close? Wilson Bentley did."},{"t":"fact","text":"On 15 January 1885, he made the first photograph of a single snow crystal. He was nineteen years old and lived on a farm in Jericho, Vermont. It had taken him two winters of failures to get that one picture."},{"t":"p","text":"Bentley kept going for the rest of his life. He photographed more than 5,000 snow crystals. His book \"Snow Crystals\" came out in 1931 with 2,453 pictures in it."}]},{"icon":"⛄","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Snow crystals have six sides for a reason."},{"t":"term","word":"crystal","text":"A solid made up of molecules that lock into a repeating pattern."},{"t":"fact","text":"When water freezes, its molecules lock into a repeating six-sided pattern. The snow crystal grows outward on this frame, so it ends up with six sides."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why do snow crystals have six sides?","options":["Because the wind carves every flake into six sharp points.","Because ice molecules lock into a six-sided pattern.","Because every snowflake grows from six drops of water."],"answer":1,"hint":"The fact box tells you why the molecules form a six-sided pattern.","explain":"Ice molecules lock into a six-sided pattern, and the crystal grows outward from there."}}]},{"icon":"🔥","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The hottest air temperature ever officially recorded is 134 F (56.7 C). It was read at Greenland Ranch in Death Valley, California, on 10 July 1913."},{"t":"fact","text":"Some researchers say the observer might have misread the thermometer that week. But Death Valley still regularly gets past 125 F (52 C) today.","label":"Still argued about"}]},{"icon":"🥶","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"At the other end, the coldest air temperature ever recorded was minus 128.6 F (minus 89.2 C)."},{"t":"fact","text":"This record was set at the Soviet Vostok station in Antarctica on 21 July 1983. July is midwinter there."},{"t":"p","text":"If you throw a cup of boiling water into the air at that temperature, it can turn to ice crystals before it lands."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where was the coldest air ever recorded?","options":["Vostok station in Antarctica","Greenland Ranch in Death Valley","The North Pole"],"answer":0,"hint":"The fact box tells you the exact location.","explain":"The coldest air was measured at Vostok station in Antarctica."}}]},{"icon":"🌪️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The fastest wind ever measured in a tornado was read by a radar mounted on a truck near Bridge Creek, Oklahoma."},{"t":"fact","text":"On 3 May 1999, the radar read about 301 mph (484 km/h), give or take about 20 mph."},{"t":"p","text":"The radar was looking at wind a few hundred feet up, not at ground level. No weather instrument on the ground survives a tornado, so tornado strength is normally rated afterward from what the damage looks like."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How is a tornado's strength normally rated?","options":["By a weather instrument left standing in its path","By how long it lasts","Afterward, from the damage"],"answer":2,"hint":"The page says ground instruments do not survive.","explain":"Since no ground instruments survive a tornado, its strength is rated afterward by looking at the damage."}}]},{"icon":"📜","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"On day two, the written account says the waters were divided, with some below and some above."},{"t":"fact","text":"The water above is overhead right now. One ordinary afternoon cloud carries a million pounds of it.","label":"The written account says"},{"t":"p","text":"That a cloud holds about a million pounds of water is weighed and calculated. Where the water above came from is read out of Genesis."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Lightning bolt","Heats air five times hotter than the sun"],["Wilson Bentley","First to photograph a snow crystal"],["Vostok station","Coldest temperature recorded"],["Bridge Creek, Oklahoma","Fastest wind in a tornado measured"],["cumulus cloud","Holds about a million pounds of water"]]},"picgame":{"type":"sort","title":"Hot or Cold","goal":"Which is hot? Which is cold?","bins":[{"icon":"🔥","label":"hot"},{"icon":"🥶","label":"cold"}],"items":[{"icon":"⚡","name":"lightning","bin":0},{"icon":"☀️","name":"sun","bin":0},{"icon":"🏜️","name":"death valley","bin":0},{"icon":"❄️","name":"snowflake","bin":1},{"icon":"🧊","name":"hail","bin":1},{"icon":"⛄","name":"snowman","bin":1}]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why is the air inside a lightning bolt hotter than the surface of the sun?","options":["Air is a terrible conductor, and resisting the current is what dumps the heat","Lightning bolts are made of fire","The sun is actually cooler than most people think"],"answer":0,"explain":"The same reason a thin wire glows orange inside a toaster — about 50,000°F, roughly five times the sun's surface."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What's the old explanation for a hailstone's onion-like layers, and why is it wrong?","options":["One layer per loop through the storm — but nobody can track a hailstone's path inside a cloud","The layers form after the hailstone lands","Hailstones don't actually have layers"],"answer":0,"explain":"The layers are read as patches of different temperature and wetness, mostly from one pass across the updraft, not repeated loops."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How can you tell roughly how far away a lightning strike was?","options":["Count the seconds between the flash and the thunder, then divide by five","Count how many seconds the thunder itself lasts","Compare how bright the flash looked"],"answer":0,"explain":"Light arrives almost instantly; sound crawls at about one mile every five seconds."},{"type":"tf","prompt":"A single ordinary puffy cloud can carry over a million pounds of water.","answer":0,"explain":"It stays up because the water is chopped into droplets far too small to fall quickly."},{"type":"tf","prompt":"No two snowflakes have ever actually been proven to be completely unique.","answer":0,"explain":"Nobody has compared every snowflake that's ever fallen — simple small crystals actually do look identical."}]},{"id":"earth-caves","course":"earth","kind":"facts","section":"caves","title":"Caves & Canyons","glyph":"🕳️","n":13,"idea":"A hole can open under a house, and a cave can hold animals that never see the sun. Find out how big caves get, and what nobody has ever watched happen.","pages":[{"icon":"🕳️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"On 8 May 1981, the ground opened up in Winter Park, Florida."},{"t":"fact","text":"Over two days, the hole widened to 320 feet across and 90 feet deep. It swallowed a three-bedroom house, a huge sycamore tree, five Porsches from a repair shop, a pickup truck, and even the town swimming pool."},{"t":"p","text":"Florida is built on limestone riddled with holes. This particular hole let go during a stretch of record-low water in the aquifer underneath, when the water that had been helping hold the roof up was gone."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How wide did the Winter Park hole grow?","options":["90 feet","320 feet","500 feet"],"answer":1,"hint":"The fact box gives you the width and depth of the hole.","explain":"The hole grew to 320 feet across."}}]},{"icon":"💧","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Have you ever seen a cave grow? No, nobody has."},{"t":"fact","text":"Rain picks up carbon dioxide from the air and soil, turns into a weak acid, and slowly eats away at limestone along cracks. This is how caves form, but nobody has watched it happen.","label":"Worked out"},{"t":"term","word":"limestone","text":"A type of rock that forms in layers under water and can be eaten away by acid."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What has nobody ever done with a cave?","options":["Dived into one","Drawn a map of one","Watched one form"],"answer":2,"hint":"The fact box talks about what nobody has witnessed directly.","explain":"Nobody has watched a cave actually form. We know the process, but we haven't seen it happen in real time."}}]},{"icon":"🧪","blocks":[{"t":"term","word":"stalactite","text":"A type of rock formation that hangs from the ceiling."},{"t":"term","word":"stalagmite","text":"A type of rock formation that stands on the ground."},{"t":"fact","text":"A stalactite starts as a hollow tube about a fifth of an inch across, called a soda straw. When the tube gets clogged, water runs down the outside and the stalactite thickens. On average, it grows about 0.005 inches each year.","label":"How they start"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which one hangs from the ceiling?","options":["The stalactite","The stalagmite","Both of them"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the term definitions.","explain":"Stalactites hang from the ceiling, while stalagmites stand on the ground."}}]},{"icon":"📏","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Mammoth Cave in Kentucky is the longest cave known on Earth, with 426 miles of mapped passage."},{"t":"fact","text":"No other cave comes close. The figure is not an estimate. Volunteers from the Cave Research Foundation crawled every inch with tape measures and compasses, survey station by survey station."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How was Mammoth Cave measured?","options":["Volunteers crawled it with tape measures and compasses","Pilots flew over the cave and mapped it from the air","Rangers walked above it and measured the ground with radar"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the fact box for how the volunteers did it.","explain":"The volunteers from the Cave Research Foundation used tape measures and compasses to map every inch of the cave."}}]},{"icon":"⬇️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The deepest known cave is Veryovkina, in the country of Georgia, on the Black Sea coast. Its floor sits 7,257 feet below its entrance."},{"t":"fact","text":"When it was first written up in 1968, explorers thought Veryovkina was only 377 feet deep. Now we know it goes well over a mile straight down."},{"t":"p","text":"The entrance is just a hole about 10 feet by 13 feet in a mountainside. Reaching the bottom takes days, and expeditions have been chased back out by floodwater rising up the shafts."}]},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"In 1990, a man named Ho Khanh was out in the jungle looking for agarwood to sell when he stumbled on something very big."},{"t":"fact","text":"He found the entrance to Hang Son Doong, the largest cave passage ever measured. It is about 660 feet high and 490 feet wide, running for more than 3 miles. A Boeing 747 could fly down the middle without touching either wall."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What was Ho Khanh looking for when he found Hang Son Doong?","options":["Gold","Onyx","Agarwood"],"answer":2,"hint":"The page mentions what he was searching for to sell.","explain":"He was looking for agarwood in the jungle."}}]},{"icon":"🦇","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"In 1986, workers digging near the Black Sea stumbled on Movile Cave in Romania. It is sealed off from the surface."},{"t":"fact","text":"Fifty-seven animal species live in Movile Cave, and thirty-seven of them are found nowhere else on Earth. Most do not have eyes."},{"t":"p","text":"Nothing down there runs on sunlight. The bottom of the food chain is bacteria pulling energy out of hydrogen sulfide and methane in the water."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is at the bottom of the food chain in Movile Cave?","options":["Sunlight","Bacteria","Fish"],"answer":1,"hint":"The page tells you what pulls energy from gases.","explain":"The bacteria eat hydrogen sulfide and methane, not sunlight."}}]},{"icon":"🌋","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"On 19 March 1982, Mount St. Helens erupted and melted snow in its crater."},{"t":"fact","text":"A huge flood of water burst out and tore down the North Fork Toutle River valley, carving as it went. Geologists had surveyed lines across the channel weeks before. By 20 March, ten miles downstream, the riverbed had dropped an average of 9 feet overnight."},{"t":"p","text":"Nobody watched the Grand Canyon get cut. People watched this one happen."}]},{"icon":"🏜️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The Grand Canyon is so big you can measure it. It runs 278 river miles long, up to 18 miles wide, and about a mile deep."},{"t":"fact","text":"The South Rim stands near 7,000 feet, and the North Rim stands near 8,000. From rim to river, it is about one mile straight down."},{"t":"p","text":"But here's the thing: nobody watched the Grand Canyon get cut. We only know how big it is, not exactly how it got that way."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which part of the Grand Canyon story is measured?","options":["How big it is","How it was cut","When it formed"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the fact box for the exact measurements.","explain":"The size of the Grand Canyon is measured with instruments. How it was cut is reasoned out from what we see now, and geologists still disagree about that part."}}]},{"icon":"📜","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Standing on the rim of the Grand Canyon, you see flat stacked layers with a canyon cut down through them."},{"t":"fact","text":"The written account in Genesis says the fountains of the great deep burst open and the windows of heaven were opened, and water covered the whole earth.","label":"The written account says"},{"t":"p","text":"The layers are there and can be photographed. The canyon is a mile deep and can be measured. But which event laid the layers and cut the canyon is something no tape measure can settle."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Mammoth Cave","The longest cave known, 426 miles"],["Veryovkina","The deepest known cave on Earth"],["Winter Park","The sinkhole that swallowed a house"],["Ho Khanh","The man who found Hang Son Doong"],["Movile Cave","Home to animals with no eyes"],["Mount St. Helens","Caused a massive flood when it erupted"]]},"picgame":{"type":"tapall","title":"Sinkhole Snack","goal":"Tap what the sinkhole swallowed.","items":[{"icon":"🏠","name":"house","good":true},{"icon":"🌳","name":"big tree","good":true},{"icon":"🚗","name":"car","good":true},{"icon":"🛻","name":"pickup truck","good":true},{"icon":"🏊","name":"swimming pool","good":true},{"icon":"🐘","name":"elephant","good":false},{"icon":"🚢","name":"ship","good":false},{"icon":"✈️","name":"airplane","good":false}]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"How do geologists actually know how caves form, since nobody has ever watched one form?","options":["They can measure that water leaving cave springs is carrying dissolved limestone right now","Someone has watched a small cave form in a lab","Ancient written records describe it happening"],"answer":0,"explain":"Rain picks up carbon dioxide, becomes a weak acid, and eats limestone — the chemistry is measured even though the forming isn't observed."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What's the easy trick for remembering stalactites from stalagmites?","options":["C is for ceiling, G is for ground","Stalactites are always bigger","Stalagmites only form in warm caves"],"answer":0,"explain":"They usually come as a matched pair, since one drip builds both."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What proved a canyon as deep as the Grand Canyon could be cut in a single day?","options":["A 1982 flood at Mount St. Helens that cut a riverbed overnight","Satellite photos taken a year apart","A team that measured the Grand Canyon forming in real time"],"answer":0,"explain":"Ten miles below the crater, the riverbed dropped an average of 9 feet overnight — people watched this one happen."},{"type":"tf","prompt":"Mammoth Cave in Kentucky is the longest known cave, and its length was measured by volunteers crawling it with tape measures.","answer":0,"explain":"It holds 426 miles of mapped passage — no other known cave comes close."},{"type":"tf","prompt":"Movile Cave in Romania is full of animals that depend on sunlight to survive.","answer":1,"explain":"Nothing down there runs on sunlight — the bottom of the food chain is bacteria living on hydrogen sulfide and methane."}]},{"id":"earth-ice","course":"earth","kind":"facts","section":"ice","title":"Ice & Poles","glyph":"🧊","n":12,"idea":"Ice floats, ice flows, and ice hides whole mountains. Find out what melting ice would do to the sea, and where the sun rises only once a year.","pages":[{"icon":"🌬️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Have you ever seen your breath on a cold day? It looks like a cloud coming from your mouth."},{"t":"fact","text":"Your breath is full of invisible water vapor. When it hits something colder, it turns into fog on the glass. If it's below freezing, it skips being liquid and goes directly to ice crystals, which is frost."},{"t":"try","text":"Breathe on a cold window or mirror and watch what happens."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is frost?","options":["Drops of rain that froze into a thin layer of ice","Vapor that turned straight into ice crystals","Snow that melted a bit and froze again at night"],"answer":1,"hint":"Frost forms when water vapor turns directly into ice crystals.","explain":"Frost forms when water vapor hits something below freezing and turns directly into ice crystals."}}]},{"icon":"🧊","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"You have probably seen pictures of icebergs. They look like mountains of ice in the sea. But what you see is only the tip."},{"t":"fact","text":"About nine-tenths of an iceberg is underwater. Ice is about nine-tenths as heavy as seawater, so it sinks until it has pushed aside its own weight in water."},{"t":"p","text":"This is why icebergs can roll over without warning. As the underwater part melts unevenly, the whole berg becomes top-heavy and flips."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How much of an iceberg is underwater?","options":["About one-tenth","Almost all of it","About nine-tenths"],"answer":2,"hint":"The fact box tells you how much is underwater.","explain":"About nine-tenths. Only that last tenth sticks up where you can see it."}}]},{"icon":"🥤","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Imagine you have an ice cube floating in a glass filled to the brim. When it melts, the water level stays exactly the same."},{"t":"fact","text":"Floating sea ice melting does not raise sea level because it is already displacing its own weight in water. Ice sitting on land, however, is a different case. That water is not in the ocean yet and will raise the sea level when it melts."},{"t":"try","text":"Float an ice cube in a glass filled to the brim and mark the level. When it melts, the glass still does not overflow. Now set a second cube on a saucer, let it melt, and pour that water into the glass. That one is land ice."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which melting would raise the sea?","options":["Ice sitting on land","Ice floating at sea","Both of them equally"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the fact box to see where the ice starts.","explain":"Ice sitting on land raises the sea level when it melts because it was not in the ocean to begin with."}}]},{"icon":"💙","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Snow is white and glacier ice is blue, but both are made of the same clear stuff."},{"t":"term","word":"glacier","text":"A huge mass of ice that slowly moves over land."},{"t":"fact","text":"Snow is packed with tiny surfaces that bounce every color of light straight back out at you. Thick solid ice lets light travel far enough inside to soak up the reds, and blue is what comes back. Punch a deep hole into a glacier and the bottom goes blue."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why does thick ice look blue?","options":["Light travels far inside it and the reds are soaked up","The deep cold of the ice turns the light a bluish color","The blue sky bounces off the ice and tints all of it"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It explains how light travels through thick ice.","explain":"Thick ice looks blue because the light travels far enough inside to soak up the reds, leaving only the blue."}}]},{"icon":"🐌","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Jakobshavn Glacier in Greenland moves over 150 feet a day in summer."},{"t":"fact","text":"That is about an inch and a quarter every minute, so a stake driven into the ice shifts several feet while you eat lunch."},{"t":"p","text":"It moves that fast because meltwater gets down to the bottom and the whole slab slides on it. Most glaciers are much slower, moving only a few inches a day."}]},{"icon":"🌲","blocks":[{"t":"term","word":"permafrost","text":"Ground that has stayed at or below 32 F (0 C) for at least two years."},{"t":"fact","text":"About a quarter of the land in the northern half of the world has this permafrost, and in some places it goes down more than 4,900 feet (1,500 m)."},{"t":"p","text":"The top layer thaws every summer and refreezes every winter. Trees, moss, and mosquitoes live on a thin lid over frozen ground. Houses are lifted off the ground to avoid sinking as the ground thaws."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is permafrost?","options":["Giant chunks of ice drifting on the cold Arctic Ocean","Deep snow that never melts on the tops of high mountains","Ground that has stayed frozen for at least two years"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the definition of permafrost at the top of the page.","explain":"Permafrost is ground that stays at or below 32 F (0 C) for at least two years."}}]},{"icon":"🧭","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"At the South Pole, the ice is about 9,000 feet thick, nearly two miles of frozen water standing on the ground."},{"t":"fact","text":"The rock under all that ice is close to sea level. So almost all of the Pole's 9,301-foot elevation is from the ice itself."},{"t":"p","text":"Every New Year's Day, the marker at the geographic South Pole is dug up and moved. This is because the ice it stands on drifts about 33 feet a year toward the Weddell Sea."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why is the South Pole marker moved every year?","options":["Warm summer sun melts the ice it stands on every year","The ice it stands on drifts about 33 feet a year","The spinning Earth slowly slides the pole sideways"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph that talks about how much the ice drifts each year.","explain":"The ice drifts about 33 feet a year, so the marker needs to be moved to stay at the exact South Pole."}}]},{"icon":"🏔️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The thickest ice in Antarctica is almost 3 miles deep. It sits in a place called the Astrolabe Subglacial Basin."},{"t":"fact","text":"Across the whole continent, ice covers about 98 percent of Antarctica. Most maps of it are really maps of the lid."},{"t":"p","text":"Under all that ice, there are mountain ranges and lakes that almost no one has ever seen. Radar can map them. Only a few of the lakes have ever been drilled into."},{"t":"p","text":"The pressure and warmth from the rock keep some of these lakes liquid, even under miles of ice."}]},{"icon":"🧮","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Imagine all of Antarctica's ice melted. What would that do to the sea?"},{"t":"fact","text":"If every bit of Antarctica's ice melted, sea level would go up about 190 feet (58 m). Nobody has measured that, obviously. Those numbers are worked out from the measured volume of the ice and the measured area of the oceans.","label":"Worked out"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Is the 190 feet rise in sea level measured or worked out?","options":["Measured directly by ships at sea","Taken from records of past floods","Worked out from the measured ice"],"answer":2,"hint":"The page says those numbers are sums, not measurements.","explain":"The number is worked out from the volume of ice and the area of oceans."}}]},{"icon":"🌅","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"At the South Pole, the sun rises once a year and sets once a year."},{"t":"fact","text":"The sun comes up around late September, then circles round and round the sky without setting for six months, and goes down around late March. One day and one night, each lasting six months."},{"t":"p","text":"It does not climb straight up or go straight down either. The sun spirals slowly to about 23 degrees above the horizon at midsummer, then spirals back down. Sunrise at the Pole takes days."}]},{"icon":"📜","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"What tells day from night when there is no sunrise to count by? Look at where the written account puts it."},{"t":"fact","text":"On day one in Genesis, light is divided from darkness, and they are named Day and Night. All of that happens before the sun is made on day four. At the South Pole, Day and Night are six months long apiece.","label":"The written account says"}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Iceberg","Nine-tenths of it is underwater"],["Astrolabe Subglacial Basin","Where the ice is almost 3 miles thick"],["South Pole","Sun rises once a year"],["Antarctica melt","Sea level rise if ice melted"],["Permafrost","Frozen ground for at least two years"],["Jakobshavn Glacier","Moves over 150 feet daily in summer"]]},"picgame":{"type":"sort","title":"Ice or Water","goal":"Is it ice, or is it water?","bins":[{"icon":"❄️","label":"ice"},{"icon":"💧","label":"water"}],"items":[{"icon":"🧊","name":"iceberg","bin":0},{"icon":"🏔️","name":"glacier","bin":0},{"icon":"⛄","name":"snow","bin":0},{"icon":"🌊","name":"ocean","bin":1},{"icon":"🌧️","name":"rain","bin":1},{"icon":"🚰","name":"tap water","bin":1}]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why does only about a tenth of an iceberg stick up above the water?","options":["Ice is only slightly less dense than seawater, so it sinks until it displaces its own weight","Icebergs are hollow underneath","The underwater part is a different, heavier kind of ice"],"answer":0,"explain":"For the same amount of space, ice is roughly nine-tenths as heavy as seawater."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why doesn't melting floating sea ice raise sea level, while melting land ice does?","options":["Floating ice is already displacing its own weight in water; land ice isn't in the ocean yet","Sea ice melts into a different, lighter kind of water","Land ice is actually much colder"],"answer":0,"explain":"An ice cube melting in a full glass doesn't overflow it — but ice melting on a saucer and then poured in does."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why are at the South Pole, day and night each six months long?","options":["The sun circles the sky without setting for half the year, then stays down the other half","It's always completely dark there","The sun rises and sets normally, just more slowly"],"answer":0,"explain":"The sun rises around late September, circles for six months, then sets around late March."},{"type":"tf","prompt":"Snow looks white and glacier ice looks blue, even though both are made of the exact same clear substance.","answer":0,"explain":"Snow's tiny surfaces bounce every color straight back; thick solid ice absorbs reds and lets blue through."}]},{"id":"earth-sky","course":"earth","kind":"facts","section":"sky","title":"Sun, Moon & Sky","glyph":"🌙","n":12,"idea":"Your shadow moves like clock hands, and Earth's tilt makes seasons change. Find out why the moon looks the same size as the sun in our sky.","pages":[{"icon":"🕰️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Your shadow moves all day long like a big hand on a clock."},{"t":"fact","text":"The Earth is spinning underneath you at about 1,000 mph near the equator. You do not feel it because you are moving with the Earth, just like in a smooth airplane ride."},{"t":"try","text":"Stand in a sunny spot and have someone mark where your shadow points. Come back an hour later, stand in the same spot again, and mark where your shadow is now."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why does your shadow swing around during the day?","options":["The sun moves across the sky","Your body changes shape","The Earth is turning"],"answer":2,"hint":"Mark where your shadow points in the morning and again in the afternoon. You will see it move as the Earth turns.","explain":"The Earth is spinning underneath you, which makes your shadow move like the hand of a clock."}}]},{"icon":"🔄","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Earth turns once every 23 hours, 56 minutes, and 4 seconds against the stars."},{"t":"fact","text":"Our day is 24 hours because we time it by the sun, not the stars. As Earth spins, it also moves along its orbit, so it needs to turn a little extra to face the sun again."},{"t":"p","text":"Those four missing minutes each day add up. The stars come back around four minutes earlier each night. That is two hours a month. So the night sky in January is not the same as in July."}]},{"icon":"🌍","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"So why do we have summer and winter?"},{"t":"fact","text":"Seasons come from Earth's 23.4-degree tilt, not how close the sun is. In early January, Earth is actually closest to the sun, right in the middle of northern winter."},{"t":"p","text":"Australia has summer while Canada has winter at the same time. The planet is the same distance from the sun for both. It is the tilt that makes summer by leaning toward the sun and letting the light hit at a steeper angle."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What makes summer?","options":["Earth's tilt","Earth getting closer to the sun","The sun giving off more heat in summer"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the fact about Australia and Canada having different seasons.","explain":"Tilt makes the light hit at a steeper angle, squeezing it onto less ground and making it warmer."}}]},{"icon":"☀️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The sun you see out the window is actually a bit of history."},{"t":"fact","text":"Sunlight takes about 8 minutes and 20 seconds to reach us, crossing roughly 93 million miles (150 million km). So the sunlight you see now left the sun about eight minutes ago."},{"t":"p","text":"Moonlight takes only 1.3 seconds to get here. Light from the nearest star after the sun, though, takes over four years."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How old is the sunlight you see out the window?","options":["About forty minutes","About eight minutes","Nearly four years"],"answer":1,"hint":"The fact box tells you exactly how long sunlight takes to reach us.","explain":"Sunlight takes about 8 minutes and 20 seconds to travel from the sun to Earth."}}]},{"icon":"🌕","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The moon does spin, but it spins very slowly, one full turn per orbit around Earth."},{"t":"fact","text":"The moon takes about 27 days to complete one spin, so the same face always points at us."},{"t":"p","text":"Its orbit is a bit lopsided and tilted, which makes the moon appear to rock slightly from our view. Over a month, this rocking lets us see about 59 percent of its surface."}]},{"icon":"🚀","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Before October 1959, nobody had seen the far side of the moon. Then a Soviet probe named Luna 3 flew past and sent back pictures."},{"t":"fact","text":"Calling it the dark side is wrong. It gets as much sunlight as the side we see. It is the hidden side, not the dark one."},{"t":"p","text":"The handful of grainy photos showed something surprising. The far side is covered in craters. It has almost none of the big dark smooth plains that make the near side look like a face."}]},{"icon":"🌘","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The sun is about 400 times wider than the moon, but it also sits about 400 times farther away from us."},{"t":"fact","text":"Those two numbers cancel out, so the sun and the moon look almost exactly the same size in our sky. That is why the moon can cover the sun during a total eclipse."},{"t":"p","text":"No other planet with moons gets such a neat fit. The moon drifts about an inch and a half further from Earth each year. One day it will look too small to cover the sun completely."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why can the moon cover the sun?","options":["The moon is much larger than the sun and sits right beside it","The sun is about 400 times wider but about 400 times farther away","The moon's shadow is much wider than the moon, so it hides the sun"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the first fact on the page. It explains why they look the same size.","explain":"The sun is about 400 times wider but also about 400 times farther away, so they appear the same size in our sky."}}]},{"icon":"🌊","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Most coasts get two high tides a day, not just one."},{"t":"term","word":"tide","text":"The regular rise and fall of the ocean level."},{"t":"fact","text":"The moon's pull heaps the ocean up on the side facing it, and also on the far side where the water gets left behind. Earth turns through both bulges daily.","label":"Worked out, not seen"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why do we get two high tides a day?","options":["The ocean bulges on the side facing the moon and the side away","The moon rises twice a day, so the ocean is pulled toward it twice","The water sloshes from side to side like a bathtub twice a day"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the fact box for the explanation.","explain":"The ocean bulges on the side facing the moon and also on the far side, which creates two high tides as Earth turns."}}]},{"icon":"☄️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Have you ever seen a shooting star streak across the sky?"},{"t":"fact","text":"Most shooting stars are made by something between a grain of sand and a pea. It is moving so fast that the air in front of it gets crushed and heated until the grain glows white."},{"t":"p","text":"The grain is not burning up from rubbing against the air, which is what people usually think. Instead, it is the air itself that gets compressed and superheated in front of it. About 48 tons of that dust hits the atmosphere every single day."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What makes a shooting star glow?","options":["The grain rubbing against the air","The grain being melted by sunlight","Air crushed and heated in front of it"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the fact box for what happens to the air.","explain":"It is the air in front of the grain that gets crushed and heated until it glows."}}]},{"icon":"🌌","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Have you ever seen a sky light up with dancing curtains of color?"},{"t":"fact","text":"Auroras are made when particles streaming off the sun get steered by Earth's magnetic field down toward the poles. They crash into oxygen and nitrogen high in the atmosphere and make them glow.","label":"Worked out, not watched"},{"t":"p","text":"Nobody can see the particles, but scientists have worked it out. Satellites measure the solar wind before it arrives, and lab tests show which gases glow what colors. Oxygen gives green from about 60 to 120 miles up, and red above that."},{"t":"p","text":"The southern version is called the aurora australis. It often lights up at the same moment as the northern one, in a matching shape."}]},{"icon":"📜","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"So what does the sky do in the written account?"},{"t":"fact","text":"On day four, the sun, moon, and stars are set in place for signs and seasons, days and years. The sky is a calendar.","label":"The written account says"},{"t":"p","text":"Every piece of that calendar can be measured. A full turn takes 23 hours and 56 minutes. One full moon to the next is about 29 and a half days. The tilt is 23.4 degrees."},{"t":"p","text":"The written account adds one thing to the numbers. They were put there on purpose, to be read."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Sunlight","Takes about 8 minutes to reach us"],["Luna 3","Soviet probe that saw the far side"],["Auroras","Dancing curtains of color in the sky"],["Shooting star","Tiny grain of sand glowing in air"],["Aurora australis","Southern version of aurora borealis"]]},"picgame":{"type":"catch","title":"Night Lights","goal":"Catch what shines in the night sky.","items":[{"icon":"🌠","name":"shooting star","good":true},{"icon":"⭐","name":"star","good":true},{"icon":"🌕","name":"full moon","good":true},{"icon":"🌙","name":"crescent moon","good":true},{"icon":"☀️","name":"sun","good":false},{"icon":"🌈","name":"rainbow","good":false},{"icon":"🍎","name":"apple","good":false},{"icon":"🎈","name":"balloon","good":false}]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why does the same side of the Moon always face Earth?","options":["It spins at exactly the same rate it orbits us","It doesn't spin at all","It's locked in place by Earth's gravity with no motion"],"answer":0,"explain":"One turn per orbit, about 27 days — if it stopped spinning, we'd start seeing every side."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What actually causes most coasts to get two high tides a day instead of one?","options":["The Moon's pull bulges the ocean on the near side AND the far side, and Earth turns through both","The Sun and Moon pull in opposite directions twice a day","Ocean currents push water back and forth"],"answer":0,"explain":"On the far side, water is pulled least and gets left behind — Earth rotates through both bulges daily."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why do Earth's seasons happen, if we're actually closest to the sun in January?","options":["Earth's 23.4-degree axis tilt, not distance from the sun","The sun gets hotter and cooler throughout the year","Clouds block more sunlight in winter"],"answer":0,"explain":"Australia has summer while Canada has winter — same planet, same distance, same day."},{"type":"tf","prompt":"The sun and moon look almost the same size in our sky purely by coincidence of their size and distance canceling out.","answer":0,"explain":"The sun is about 400 times wider than the moon, and also about 400 times farther away."},{"type":"tf","prompt":"Nobody saw the far side of the Moon until a spacecraft photographed it in 1959.","answer":0,"explain":"A Soviet probe, Luna 3, sent back the first photos — and the far side turned out to be covered in craters."}]},{"id":"earth-deserts","course":"earth","kind":"facts","section":"deserts","title":"Deserts & Dunes","glyph":"🏜️","n":6,"idea":"Some places are so dry that weather stations there have never caught a drop of rain. Visit the biggest deserts and the sand dunes that move.","pages":[{"icon":"🏜️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"About one-third of the land on Earth is very dry."},{"t":"fact","text":"That includes polar lands, which are sometimes called cold deserts."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How much of Earth's land is very dry?","options":["Almost none","One-third","Nearly all"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"About one-third of the land on Earth is very dry."}}]},{"icon":"🤔","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"In the Atacama Desert in Chile, the average rainfall in one region is just 0.039 in (1 mm) a year."},{"t":"fact","text":"Some weather stations there have never received any rain."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How much rain falls each year in the driest part of the Atacama?","options":["0.39 in","0.039 in","0.0039 in"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"In the Atacama Desert in Chile, the average rainfall in one region is just 0.039 in (1 mm) a year."}}]},{"icon":"📏","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The Atacama Desert is a strip of land about 1,000 mi (1,600 km) long in northern Chile."},{"t":"fact","text":"It covers about 41,000 sq mi (105,000 km2)."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where is the Atacama Desert?","options":["Southern Peru","Northern Chile","Western Egypt"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"The Atacama Desert is a strip of land about 1,000 mi (1,600 km) long in northern Chile."}}]},{"icon":"📐","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The Sahara in North Africa is the largest hot desert in the world."},{"t":"fact","text":"It covers 3,600,000 sq mi (9,200,000 square kilometers). Only the deserts of Antarctica and the Arctic are bigger."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is the largest hot desert in the world?","options":["The Atacama","The Sahara","The Arctic"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"The Sahara in North Africa is the largest hot desert in the world."}}]},{"icon":"⚖️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Many sand dunes in the Sahara rise over 590 ft (180 m)."},{"t":"term","word":"dune","text":"A hill made of sand, like the dunes in the Sahara."},{"t":"fact","text":"The desert also has rocky plateaus, gravel plains, dry lakes and salt flats."}]},{"icon":"💨","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A group of sand dunes in China moved more than 328 ft (100 m) every year from 1954 to 1959."},{"t":"fact","text":"Dunes in the Western Desert of Egypt move that fast too."}]},{"icon":"🔎","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A dry land. A strip of desert in Chile. A huge hot desert. Dunes that move."},{"t":"p","text":"Deserts are big."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Atacama","Weather stations that never got rain"],["Sahara","The largest hot desert"],["Dune","A hill made of sand"],["Cold deserts","Polar lands that are very dry"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What have some weather stations in the Atacama never received?","options":["Sun","Rain","Wind"],"answer":1,"explain":"Some weather stations in the Atacama have never received any rain."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How far did a group of dunes in China move each year?","options":["Only 3 ft","More than 328 ft","About 30 ft"],"answer":1,"explain":"A group of sand dunes in China moved more than 328 ft (100 m) every year."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which country is the Atacama Desert in?","options":["Egypt","Chile","China"],"answer":1,"explain":"The Atacama Desert is in northern Chile."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What are sand dunes made of?","options":["Ice","Sand","Salt"],"answer":1,"explain":"A dune is a hill made of sand."}]},{"id":"earth-islands","course":"earth","kind":"facts","section":"islands","title":"Islands & Reefs","glyph":"🏝️","n":6,"idea":"An island has water on every side. Meet a whole chain of volcanic islands, and a reef you can see from space.","pages":[{"icon":"🏝️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"An island is a piece of land completely surrounded by water."},{"t":"fact","text":"Some islands were once part of a continent. Others, called oceanic islands, never were."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is an island?","options":["A tall hill that rises above a flat plain","A piece of land completely surrounded by water","A wide river with a low bank on each side"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"An island is a piece of land completely surrounded by water."}}]},{"icon":"🏠","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Up to 10% of the world's people live on islands."},{"t":"fact","text":"Many people also visit islands for their natural beauty and their unique cultures."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How much of the world's population lives on islands?","options":["1%","10%","90%"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"Up to 10% of the world's people live on islands."}}]},{"icon":"📏","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The Great Barrier Reef is the world's largest coral reef system."},{"t":"fact","text":"It has over 2,900 individual reefs and 900 islands, and it stretches for over 1,400 mi (2,300 km)."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many individual reefs make up the Great Barrier Reef?","options":["Only 29","Over 2,900","About 290"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. The answer is there.","explain":"It has over 2,900 individual reefs and 900 islands, and it stretches for over 1,400 mi (2,300 km)."}}]},{"icon":"🔧","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Billions of tiny animals called coral polyps built the Great Barrier Reef."},{"t":"term","word":"reef","text":"A long line of coral in the sea."},{"t":"fact","text":"It can be seen from outer space. It is the biggest single structure made by living things."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What tiny animals built the Great Barrier Reef?","options":["Little fish","Coral polyps","Sea turtles"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"Billions of tiny animals called coral polyps built the Great Barrier Reef."}}]},{"icon":"📐","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The state of Hawaii is made of 137 volcanic islands."},{"t":"fact","text":"They stretch for 1,500 mi (2,400 km)."}]},{"icon":"🏡","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Hawaii is in the Pacific Ocean, about 2,000 mi (3,200 km) southwest of the U.S. mainland."},{"t":"fact","text":"It is the only state that is not on the North American mainland, and the only state that is an archipelago."}]},{"icon":"🔎","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Land with water around it. A huge reef built by tiny animals. A chain of volcanic islands."},{"t":"p","text":"Islands are great."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Island","Land surrounded by water"],["Great Barrier Reef","Seen from outer space"],["Coral polyps","Tiny animals that built the reef"],["Hawaii","137 volcanic islands"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many volcanic islands make up Hawaii?","options":["13","137","1,370"],"answer":1,"explain":"The state of Hawaii is made of 137 volcanic islands."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How much of the world's population lives on islands?","options":["About 1%","Up to 10%","Over 90%"],"answer":1,"explain":"Up to 10% of the world's people live on islands."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What built the Great Barrier Reef?","options":["Big whales","Coral polyps","Sea birds"],"answer":1,"explain":"Billions of tiny animals called coral polyps built it."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How far is Hawaii from the U.S. mainland?","options":["About 20 mi","About 2,000 mi","About 200 mi"],"answer":1,"explain":"Hawaii is about 2,000 mi (3,200 km) southwest of the U.S. mainland."}]},{"id":"earth-mountains","course":"earth","kind":"facts","section":"mountains","title":"Mountains","glyph":"🏔️","n":6,"idea":"Mountains stand thousands of feet high. Climb with us from the top of Mount Everest to the farthest point from the center of the Earth.","pages":[{"icon":"🏔️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Mount Everest in the Himalayas is the highest mountain on Earth."},{"t":"fact","text":"Its top is 29,035 ft (8,850 m) above sea level."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is the highest mountain on Earth?","options":["Chimborazo","Mount Everest","Mauna Kea in Hawaii"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"Mount Everest in the Himalayas is the highest mountain on Earth."}}]},{"icon":"📏","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Mauna Kea in Hawaii rises 30,610 ft (9,330 m) from its base at the bottom of the ocean."},{"t":"fact","text":"Measured from base to peak, some scientists say it is the tallest mountain on Earth."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where is Mauna Kea?","options":["Nepal","Hawaii","Ecuador"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"Mauna Kea in Hawaii rises 30,610 ft (9,330 m) from its base at the bottom of the ocean."}}]},{"icon":"🧭","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The closest sea to Mount Everest's summit is the Bay of Bengal, almost 430 mi (700 km) away."},{"t":"term","word":"summit","text":"The very top of a mountain."},{"t":"fact","text":"In 1990, a team led by Tim Macartney-Snape climbed the whole height, from the coast. Most climbers start at base camps above 16,404 ft (5,000 m)."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is the distance from the top of Mount Everest to the closest sea?","options":["43 mi","430 mi","4,300 mi"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"The closest sea to Mount Everest's summit is the Bay of Bengal, almost 430 mi (700 km) away."}}]},{"icon":"📐","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"More than 100 peaks in the Himalayas rise above 23,600 ft (7,200 m)."},{"t":"fact","text":"The range spans five countries: Nepal, India, China, Bhutan and Pakistan."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many countries does the Himalayas range span?","options":["Two","Five","Nine"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. The answer is there.","explain":"The range spans five countries: Nepal, India, China, Bhutan and Pakistan."}}]},{"icon":"⚖️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The Andes are the longest mountain range on land in the world."},{"t":"fact","text":"They run along the western edge of South America and are 5,500 mi (8,900 km) long."}]},{"icon":"🤔","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The peak of Chimborazo in Ecuador is farther from the center of the Earth than any other place on its surface."},{"t":"fact","text":"The Earth bulges at the equator because it spins."}]},{"icon":"🔎","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The highest mountain. A mountain rising from the sea. Peaks in five countries. The longest range."},{"t":"p","text":"Mountains are tall."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Mount Everest","Highest mountain on Earth"],["Mauna Kea","Rises from the ocean floor"],["Himalayas","Five countries and over 100 peaks"],["Andes","The longest mountain range on land"],["Chimborazo","Farthest from the Earth's center"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"How high is the top of Mount Everest above sea level?","options":["2,903 ft","29,035 ft","290 ft"],"answer":1,"explain":"Mount Everest's top is 29,035 ft (8,850 m) above sea level."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which mountain range is the longest on land?","options":["The Himalayas","The Andes","The Rockies"],"answer":1,"explain":"The Andes are the longest mountain range on land in the world."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why does the Earth bulge at the equator?","options":["It is flat","It spins","It melts"],"answer":1,"explain":"The Earth bulges at the equator because it spins."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"In which year did a team climb the whole height of Everest from the coast?","options":["1790","1990","1890"],"answer":1,"explain":"In 1990, a team led by Tim Macartney-Snape climbed the whole height, from the coast."}]},{"id":"earth-lakes","course":"earth","kind":"facts","section":"lakes","title":"Lakes & Waterfalls","glyph":"💦","n":6,"idea":"Lake Baikal is the deepest lake in the world, and Angel Falls is the tallest waterfall. See how deep, how clear and how tall water can get.","pages":[{"icon":"💦","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Lake Baikal is the deepest lake in the world, 5,387 ft (1,642 m) deep."},{"t":"fact","text":"It holds 22 to 23% of the world's fresh surface water, more than all the North American Great Lakes combined."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which lake is the deepest in the world?","options":["Lake Powell","Lake Baikal","Lake Erie"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"Lake Baikal is the deepest lake in the world, 5,387 ft (1,642 m) deep."}}]},{"icon":"👀","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"In winter, the water in Lake Baikal is so clear that you can see down 100 to 130 ft (30 to 40 m)."},{"t":"fact","text":"In summer you can see 15 to 25 ft (5 to 8 m)."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How far can you see down into Lake Baikal in winter?","options":["10 to 13 ft","100 to 130 ft","1 to 3 ft"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"In winter, the water in Lake Baikal is so clear that you can see down 100 to 130 ft (30 to 40 m)."}}]},{"icon":"🤔","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Many kinds of living things live in Lake Baikal."},{"t":"term","word":"species","text":"The kinds of plants and animals, like those in Lake Baikal."},{"t":"fact","text":"More than 1,000 species of plants and 2,500 species of animals live there."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many species of animals live in Lake Baikal?","options":["25,000","2,500","250"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. The answer is there.","explain":"More than 1,000 species of plants and 2,500 species of animals live there."}}]},{"icon":"💨","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Niagara Falls is huge."},{"t":"fact","text":"In busy hours, more than 5.9 million cubic feet (168,000 cubic meters) of water goes over the top each minute."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How much water goes over Niagara Falls every minute?","options":["About 5.9 thousand cubic feet","Over 5.9 million cubic feet","Nearly 59,000 cubic feet"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. The answer is there.","explain":"In busy hours, more than 5.9 million cubic feet (168,000 cubic meters) of water goes over the top each minute."}}]},{"icon":"📏","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Angel Falls is the world's tallest waterfall with no break in the drop."},{"t":"fact","text":"It is 3,212 ft (979 m) high, and the main plunge is 2,648 ft (807 m)."}]},{"icon":"🧑","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Angel Falls is named after Jimmie Angel, a U.S. pilot."},{"t":"fact","text":"He was the first person to fly over the falls, on November 16, 1933."}]},{"icon":"🔎","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A deep lake. Clear water. Many kinds of plants and animals. A huge waterfall."},{"t":"p","text":"Water is big."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Lake Baikal","The deepest lake"],["Niagara Falls","Over 5.9 million cubic feet a minute"],["Angel Falls","The tallest waterfall"],["Jimmie Angel","First person to fly over the falls"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"How deep is Lake Baikal?","options":["538 ft","5,387 ft","53 ft"],"answer":1,"explain":"Lake Baikal is the deepest lake in the world, 5,387 ft (1,642 m) deep."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many species of plants live in Lake Baikal?","options":["About 10","More than 1,000","Only 100"],"answer":1,"explain":"More than 1,000 species of plants live in Lake Baikal."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How high is Angel Falls?","options":["321 ft","3,212 ft","32 ft"],"answer":1,"explain":"Angel Falls is 3,212 ft (979 m) high."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Who was Angel Falls named after?","options":["Tim Angel","Jimmie Angel","Sam Niagara"],"answer":1,"explain":"Angel Falls is named after Jimmie Angel, a U.S. pilot."}]},{"id":"earth-rivers","course":"earth","kind":"facts","section":"rivers","title":"Great Rivers","glyph":"🏞️","n":6,"idea":"The Nile is the longest river in the world, and the Amazon carries more water than the next seven rivers put together. Follow the great rivers.","pages":[{"icon":"🏞️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The Nile is the longest river in the world, 4,404 mi (7,088 km) long."},{"t":"fact","text":"It flows north into the Mediterranean Sea."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is the longest river in the world?","options":["The Amazon","The Nile","The Missouri"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"The Nile is the longest river in the world, 4,404 mi (7,088 km) long."}}]},{"icon":"⚙️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Eleven countries share the Nile's basin."},{"t":"term","word":"basin","text":"The land where the water of the Nile comes from."},{"t":"fact","text":"Most of its water comes from rain in Ethiopia, Kenya, Tanzania and Uganda."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where does most of the Nile's water come from?","options":["Springs","Rain","Glaciers"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. The answer is there.","explain":"Most of its water comes from rain in Ethiopia, Kenya, Tanzania and Uganda."}}]},{"icon":"📏","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The Amazon River carries more water than the next seven biggest rivers put together."},{"t":"fact","text":"It delivers 20% of all the river water that flows into the world's oceans."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How much of the world's river water flows from the Amazon?","options":["2%","20%","80%"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. The answer is there.","explain":"It delivers 20% of all the river water that flows into the world's oceans."}}]},{"icon":"📐","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The Amazon River is about 4,000 mi (6,400 km) long."},{"t":"fact","text":"Its drainage basin is the largest in the world, about 2,700,000 sq mi (7,000,000 square kilometers)."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is the length of the Amazon River?","options":["About 400 mi","About 4,000 mi","About 14,000 mi"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"The Amazon River is about 4,000 mi (6,400 km) long."}}]},{"icon":"⚖️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The Mississippi River is 2,340 mi (3,766 km) long."},{"t":"fact","text":"It starts at Lake Itasca in Minnesota and ends in the Gulf of Mexico. It is the second-longest river in the U.S."}]},{"icon":"🔭","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The Mississippi collects water from all or part of 32 U.S. states."},{"t":"fact","text":"Two Canadian provinces drain into it too."}]},{"icon":"🔎","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A very long river. A basin of rain. A river with a lot of water. A river that ends in the Gulf of Mexico."},{"t":"p","text":"Rivers are long."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Nile","Longest river in the world"],["Amazon","Carries 20% of the river water into the oceans"],["Mississippi","Second-longest river in the U.S."],["Basin","Land that drains into a river"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which sea does the Nile flow into?","options":["The Gulf of Mexico","The Mediterranean Sea","The Pacific Ocean"],"answer":1,"explain":"The Nile flows north into the Mediterranean Sea."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many countries share the Nile's basin?","options":["Two","Eleven","Forty"],"answer":1,"explain":"Eleven countries share the Nile's basin."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where does the Mississippi River start?","options":["Lake Baikal","Lake Itasca","Niagara Falls"],"answer":1,"explain":"The Mississippi starts at Lake Itasca in Minnesota."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many U.S. states does the Mississippi collect water from?","options":["3","32","320"],"answer":1,"explain":"The Mississippi collects water from all or part of 32 U.S. states."}]},{"id":"earth-storms","course":"earth","kind":"facts","section":"storms","title":"Tornadoes & Hurricanes","glyph":"🌪️","n":6,"idea":"A tornado is a column of air that spins fast enough to rip buildings off their foundations. Find out how big tornadoes and hurricanes get.","pages":[{"icon":"🌪️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A tornado is a column of air that spins very fast, from the ground up to a cloud."},{"t":"term","word":"tornado","text":"A column of air that spins very fast."},{"t":"fact","text":"Most have winds under 110 mph (180 km/h) and are about 250 ft (80 m) across."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How wide is a typical tornado?","options":["About 25 ft","About 250 ft","About 2,500 ft"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. The answer is there.","explain":"Most have winds under 110 mph (180 km/h) and are about 250 ft (80 m) across."}}]},{"icon":"💨","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The strongest tornadoes can have winds of more than 300 mph (480 km/h)."},{"t":"fact","text":"They can be more than 2 mi (3 km) wide and stay on the ground for more than 62 mi (100 km)."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How fast can the winds of the strongest tornadoes blow?","options":["Only about 30 mph","More than 300 mph","Barely over 3 mph"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"The strongest tornadoes can have winds of more than 300 mph (480 km/h)."}}]},{"icon":"⚙️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The Enhanced Fujita Scale rates tornadoes by the damage they cause."},{"t":"fact","text":"An EF0, the weakest, damages trees but not buildings. An EF5, the strongest, rips buildings off their foundations."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does the Enhanced Fujita Scale measure?","options":["The speed of sound","The damage","The size of clouds"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"The Enhanced Fujita Scale rates tornadoes by the damage they cause."}}]},{"icon":"🏆","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Around 80 to 90 named tropical cyclones form around the world each year."},{"t":"fact","text":"More than half of them reach hurricane-force winds of 75 mph (120 km/h) or more."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"About how many named tropical cyclones form each year?","options":["8 to 9","80 to 90","800 to 900"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"Around 80 to 90 named tropical cyclones form around the world each year."}}]},{"icon":"🔧","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Hurricanes form over warm ocean water."},{"t":"fact","text":"They get their energy from water that evaporates off the sea."}]},{"icon":"📏","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A tropical cyclone is typically between 62 and 1,243 mi (100 and 2,000 km) wide."},{"t":"fact","text":"That is much wider than a tornado."}]},{"icon":"🔎","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A spinning column of air. Winds over 300 mph. A scale for damage. Storms over warm water."},{"t":"p","text":"Storms are big."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Tornado","A spinning column of air"],["EF5","The strongest tornado rating"],["Hurricane","Forms over warm ocean water"],["Tropical cyclone","Between 62 and 1,243 mi wide"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does an EF5 tornado do?","options":["Only bends the trees","Rips buildings off their foundations","Does no damage at all"],"answer":1,"explain":"An EF5, the strongest, rips buildings off their foundations."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where do hurricanes form?","options":["Over cold land","Over warm ocean water","Inside tall clouds"],"answer":1,"explain":"Hurricanes form over warm ocean water."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where do hurricanes get their energy?","options":["From the wind alone","From water that evaporates","From the ground"],"answer":1,"explain":"They get their energy from water that evaporates off the sea."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How wide can the strongest tornadoes be?","options":["Less than 2 ft","More than 2 mi","About 20 ft"],"answer":1,"explain":"They can be more than 2 mi (3 km) wide."}]},{"id":"earth-lightning","course":"earth","kind":"facts","section":"lightning","title":"Lightning & Thunder","glyph":"⚡","n":6,"idea":"Lightning heats the air to about 54,000°F in an instant. Learn why thunder booms and why you see the flash first.","pages":[{"icon":"⚡","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Lightning heats the air around it."},{"t":"fact","text":"It heats it to about 54,000°F (30,000°C) in an instant."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How hot does the air around lightning get?","options":["5,400°F","54,000°F","540°F"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. The answer is there.","explain":"It heats it to about 54,000°F (30,000°C) in an instant."}}]},{"icon":"📏","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"An average bolt of negative lightning carries 30,000 amperes of electric current."},{"t":"fact","text":"The biggest positive bolts can carry up to 120 kA."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How much electric current does an average bolt carry?","options":["3,000 amperes","30,000 amperes","300,000 amperes"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"An average bolt of negative lightning carries 30,000 amperes of electric current."}}]},{"icon":"⚙️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Thunder is a shock wave."},{"t":"term","word":"shock wave","text":"A fast push of air, like thunder."},{"t":"fact","text":"Lightning heats the air so fast that the air blasts outward faster than sound would normally travel."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is thunder?","options":["A flash of light","A shock wave","A kind of wind"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"Thunder is a shock wave."}}]},{"icon":"👀","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Close to the lightning, thunder is usually 165 to 180 decibels loud."},{"t":"fact","text":"Sometimes it is over 200 decibels."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How loud is thunder close to the lightning?","options":["16 to 18 decibels","165 to 180 decibels","1 to 2 decibels"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"Close to the lightning, thunder is usually 165 to 180 decibels loud."}}]},{"icon":"💨","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"You see lightning before you hear thunder. Light travels much faster than sound."},{"t":"fact","text":"In dry air, sound goes about 1,130 ft (343 m) every second."}]},{"icon":"📐","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A thunderstorm cloud, called a cumulonimbus, can reach more than 12 mi (20 km) high."},{"t":"fact","text":"It grows when warm, moist air moves up and cools."}]},{"icon":"🔎","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A very hot flash. A big electric current. A shock wave. A loud boom. A tall cloud."},{"t":"p","text":"Lightning is hot."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Lightning","Heats the air to about 54,000°F"],["Thunder","A shock wave"],["Decibels","How loud thunder is"],["Cumulonimbus","A thunderstorm cloud"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What do you see before you hear thunder?","options":["A cloud","Lightning","A rainbow"],"answer":1,"explain":"You see lightning before you hear thunder."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why do you see lightning first?","options":["Sound travels faster than light","Light travels faster than sound","Thunder happens later"],"answer":1,"explain":"Light travels much faster than sound."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How high can a thunderstorm cloud reach?","options":["Only 12 ft","More than 12 mi","About 120 ft"],"answer":1,"explain":"A thunderstorm cloud can reach more than 12 mi (20 km) high."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How fast does sound travel in dry air?","options":["About 11 ft each second","About 1,130 ft each second","About 113 ft each second"],"answer":1,"explain":"In dry air, sound goes about 1,130 ft (343 m) every second."}]},{"id":"astro-sun","course":"astro","kind":"facts","section":"sun","title":"Our Star","glyph":"☀️","n":14,"idea":"The sun is more than just a bright ball in the sky. Find out how its light travels and what happens when we look closely at its spots and colors.","pages":[{"icon":"🕳️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The sun is so bright that looking straight at it can damage your eyes, but you won’t feel any pain."},{"t":"fact","text":"The retina in your eye has no pain nerves. So when sunlight burns it, nothing hurts while the damage happens."},{"t":"try","text":"Poke a small hole in a card and stand with your back to the sun. Let the light fall onto another card on the ground. You will see a tiny round image of the sun."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why can staring at the sun do damage without any pain?","options":["The retina has no pain nerves","The sun is too bright","Sunglasses block all the harmful rays"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It explains why you feel no pain.","explain":"The retina in your eye has no pain nerves, so you won’t feel any pain even though the damage is happening."}}]},{"icon":"📏","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The sun is huge, but we can work out just how big it is using simple geometry."},{"t":"fact","text":"The sun is about 864,000 miles across. If you lined up Earths edge to edge along that width, you would need 109 of them."},{"t":"p","text":"We know the distance to the sun because radar bounces off nearby planets, giving us precise distances in the solar system. Knowing how far away it is and how big it looks lets us measure its actual size."}]},{"icon":"⏱️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Sunlight may seem to appear instantly, but it takes time to travel from the sun to Earth."},{"t":"fact","text":"Sunlight takes about 8 minutes 20 seconds to cross the 93 million miles (150 million km) to Earth. Both the distance and the speed of light are measured precisely."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How long does sunlight take to reach us?","options":["About 3 minutes 45 seconds","About 14 minutes 30 seconds","About 8 minutes 20 seconds"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the fact box on this page.","explain":"Sunlight takes about 8 minutes 20 seconds to travel from the sun to Earth."}}]},{"icon":"🌡️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The sun has two temperatures that we talk about, but they are found in different ways."},{"t":"fact","text":"The surface of the sun is about 10,000 °F (5,500 °C), and that temperature is measured directly. But the center is about 27 million °F (15 million °C), worked out from how the sun rings and wobbles, like telling a cracked bell from a whole one."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which sun temperature is measured directly?","options":["The center","The surface","Both temperatures"],"answer":1,"hint":"The fact box tells you which temperature is measured directly and which one is worked out.","explain":"The surface temperature of about 10,000 °F is measured directly. The center's temperature is reasoned from how the sun moves."}}]},{"icon":"⚫","blocks":[{"t":"term","word":"sunspot","text":"A cooler patch on the sun's surface. It looks black in a photograph, but it is not."},{"t":"fact","text":"A sunspot looks dark because it is cooler than the surface around it, about 6,300 °F compared to roughly 10,000 °F for the surface."},{"t":"p","text":"People have been drawing sunspots since telescopes came along in 1610. In 1843, Heinrich Schwabe noticed that the number of sunspots rises and falls in an eleven-year cycle after he spent seventeen years counting them nearly every day."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why does a sunspot look dark?","options":["It is much hotter than the area around it","It is cooler than the surface around it","It is a shadow made by a passing planet"],"answer":1,"hint":"The fact box explains why a sunspot looks dark.","explain":"A sunspot looks dark because it is cooler than the surface around it."}}]},{"icon":"🌀","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The sun is not a solid ball like Earth. It moves differently at different spots."},{"t":"fact","text":"The equator of the sun turns around in about 25 days, but regions near the poles take closer to 35 days to complete one turn."},{"t":"p","text":"Sunspots close to the equator come back around sooner than spots farther north or south. A solid ball like Earth could never do that."}]},{"icon":"⚡","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"On 1 September 1859, Richard Carrington was sketching sunspots when a patch of the sun flared white."},{"t":"fact","text":"About 17 hours later, telegraph wires across Europe and North America sparked. Operators on the Boston-to-Portland line unhooked their batteries but kept sending messages using the current from the sky.","label":"The Carrington Event"},{"t":"p","text":"Auroras were reported as far south as the Caribbean. Because so many people wrote it down at once, it is one of the best-documented things the sun has ever done."}]},{"icon":"🌈","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"When the sun throws out a burst of particles, they light up the sky over the far north and far south."},{"t":"fact","text":"The sky glows green and red. That is the aurora."},{"t":"try","text":"Space weather forecasts are public. Search for the NOAA aurora forecast, and when the number called Kp climbs to 7 or higher, it is worth going somewhere dark and looking north."}]},{"icon":"🚀","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"On December 24, 2024, NASA's Parker Solar Probe got really close to the sun."},{"t":"fact","text":"The probe flew 3.8 million miles above the sun's surface at about 430,000 miles per hour. Nothing made by people has ever gone faster or closer.","label":"Close and fast"},{"t":"p","text":"Two days later, it radioed back to say it was doing just fine."}]},{"icon":"🟡","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Helium was found in the sun before anyone found it on Earth. On August 18, 1868, Jules Janssen saw a strange yellow line during an eclipse."},{"t":"fact","text":"Norman Lockyer saw the same yellow line and named the new element after the Greek word for the sun. It turned up on Earth in volcanic gas at Vesuvius fourteen years later in 1882."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where was helium found first?","options":["In party balloons","At Vesuvius","In the sun"],"answer":2,"hint":"The yellow line in sunlight was seen before it was found on Earth.","explain":"Helium was first spotted as a yellow line in sunlight, not on Earth."}}]},{"icon":"📜","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"So why do we mark days and years?"},{"t":"fact","text":"The lights in the sky were set for signs and seasons, and for days and years. The sun marks a day as Earth turns once, and a year as it circles the sun.","label":"The written account says"},{"t":"p","text":"Our calendar counts the sun. Whether it is on a wall or in a phone, it all comes back to the same star."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Carrington Event","1859 solar flare that sparked telegraph wires"],["Helium","Named after the sun before found on Earth"],["Parker Solar Probe","NASA's fastest, closest spacecraft to the sun"],["Sunspot","Cooler area that looks dark"],["Aurora","Sky glow seen during solar storms"]]},"picgame":{"type":"sort","title":"Sun or Earth","goal":"Is it on the sun or on Earth?","bins":[{"icon":"☀️","label":"sun"},{"icon":"🌍","label":"Earth"}],"items":[{"icon":"⚫","name":"sunspot","bin":0},{"icon":"💥","name":"solar flare","bin":0},{"icon":"🌡️","name":"hot surface","bin":0},{"icon":"🌌","name":"aurora","bin":1},{"icon":"⚡","name":"sparking wires","bin":1},{"icon":"🌲","name":"tree","bin":1}]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why is it so dangerous to stare at the sun, even though it doesn't hurt at the time?","options":["The retina has no pain nerves, so damage happens with no warning","The sun's light is too dim to notice damage","Sunglasses already block all the dangerous light"],"answer":0,"explain":"There's no treatment for the damage, called solar retinopathy — and sunglasses are nowhere near dark enough."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How did scientists first discover the element helium, before it was ever found on Earth?","options":["An unexplained yellow line showed up in sunlight during a solar eclipse","A volcanic eruption released it into the air","It was found inside a meteorite"],"answer":0,"explain":"Jules Janssen spotted it in 1868; it wasn't found on Earth, in volcanic gas, until 1882."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How do scientists know the sun's center is about 27 million degrees, if nobody has ever been inside it?","options":["They work it out from how the sun rings and wobbles, plus what its light contains","A probe has flown through the sun's core","It's measured the same way as the surface temperature"],"answer":0,"explain":"The surface temperature (about 10,000°F) is measured directly; the core figure is reasoned out, like telling a cracked bell from a whole one by its sound."},{"type":"tf","prompt":"In 1859, a solar flare was strong enough to make telegraph wires spark across Europe and North America.","answer":0,"explain":"That's the Carrington Event — some operators unhooked their batteries and kept sending messages on the current the sky was pushing through the wires."},{"type":"tf","prompt":"A sunspot looks black because it's actually much colder than the rest of the sun.","answer":1,"explain":"It's not cold at all — about 6,300°F. It just looks dark next to the surface around it, which is roughly 10,000°F."}]},{"id":"astro-moon","course":"astro","kind":"facts","section":"moon","title":"The Moon","glyph":"🌙","n":14,"idea":"The moon is visible during the day more often than you think, and it smells like gunpowder. Discover how we measure its distance with lasers, and why the temperature swings are so extreme.","pages":[{"icon":"🌤️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The moon is up in the daytime more often than you might think."},{"t":"fact","text":"For most of the month, it is visible during daylight, pale blue-white against the blue sky. Most people never notice it because they never look."},{"t":"try","text":"Look for the moon in the afternoon sky today. It is up in daytime more often than you think."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"When can you see the moon?","options":["Only after the sun has set, and never in daylight","For most of the month it is up during the day too","Only in the morning, right before the sun comes up"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the first fact block on the page.","explain":"The moon is up in the sky for much of the day, but many people miss seeing it because they do not look for it."}}]},{"icon":"📐","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The moon may look close up there, but it is really far away."},{"t":"fact","text":"The moon is 2,159 miles across, a bit over a quarter of Earth's width. It sits an average of 238,855 miles away, about thirty Earths laid side by side."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"About how many Earths laid side by side reach the moon?","options":["About five","About one hundred","About thirty"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the end of the fact box. It says how many Earths fit.","explain":"Thirty Earths laid side by side reach the moon. Lasers measure this distance on clear nights."}}]},{"icon":"🪨","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Six Apollo crews landed on the moon and brought back a piece of it."},{"t":"fact","text":"They carried back 842 pounds (382 kg) of rock, dust, and drilled cores, about 2,200 separate samples. Most of them are kept in Houston."},{"t":"p","text":"Some containers were sealed on purpose for later. Scientists wanted to study untouched moon material with better tools decades later."}]},{"icon":"👃","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"When the Apollo astronauts took off their helmets inside the lunar module, they noticed something strange."},{"t":"fact","text":"Moon dust smelled like spent gunpowder. Charlie Duke and Harrison Schmitt both said it, but the smell faded before the samples reached Earth."},{"t":"p","text":"The bootprints the astronauts left are still there. No wind, no rain, nothing to sweep them away. Only slow dusting from tiny impacts will wear them down."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did Apollo crews say the moon dust smelled like?","options":["Spent gunpowder","Freshly baked bread","Fresh grass after rain"],"answer":0,"hint":"Both Charlie Duke and Harrison Schmitt reported the same smell.","explain":"The moon dust smelled like spent gunpowder to the astronauts."}}]},{"icon":"🌡️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The temperature on the moon swings wildly because there is no air to move heat around."},{"t":"fact","text":"Near the equator, the ground reaches about 250 °F (121 °C) in the sun and drops to around -207 °F (-133 °C) in the dark."},{"t":"p","text":"Some polar craters never see sunlight at all. NASA's Lunar Reconnaissance Orbiter has measured spots inside those craters below -410 °F (-246 °C)."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why are the temperature swings so brutal on the moon?","options":["The sun burns far hotter on the moon","There is no air to move heat around","The moon spins much faster than Earth"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the first paragraph. It explains why the swings are so extreme.","explain":"Without air to spread heat, the temperature changes are very dramatic."}}]},{"icon":"🔭","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The worst time to look at craters is when the moon is full."},{"t":"fact","text":"At full moon, the sun is straight overhead, so nothing casts a shadow and the surface looks flat and washed out."},{"t":"term","word":"terminator","text":"The line dividing the lit side from the unlit side of the moon. Along this line, shadows are longest because the sun sits low."},{"t":"try","text":"Look at the edge of the lit part through binoculars or just your eyes. The craters stand up in relief along the terminator."}]},{"icon":"🙈","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Until 7 October 1959, nobody had seen the far side of the moon."},{"t":"fact","text":"That day, the Soviet probe Luna 3 looped behind it and took 29 photographs. The pictures showed almost no dark plains like those on the near side."},{"t":"p","text":"The far side is not the dark side. It gets just as much sunlight as the side we see. When the moon looks black from Earth, the far side is in full daylight."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"When the moon looks black from Earth, what is the far side doing?","options":["It is in full daylight","It looks black too","It is hidden by Earth"],"answer":0,"hint":"The far side gets just as much sunlight as the near side.","explain":"The far side of the moon is in full daylight when the moon appears black from Earth."}}]},{"icon":"🎯","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The moon always shows us the same face, but it is not perfectly still."},{"t":"term","word":"libration","text":"The slight rocking and wobbling of the moon as it orbits Earth."},{"t":"fact","text":"Over time, we can see 59 percent of the moon’s surface because of this rocking and wobbling. Not half, a bit more than half."},{"t":"p","text":"Two things cause this wobble: the moon's orbit is not perfectly round, so it speeds up and slows down, and its axis is tilted about 6.7 degrees. This lets us peek slightly around each side, as well as over the top and under the bottom."}]},{"icon":"🪞","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Imagine if you could measure how fast something in space is moving with a laser."},{"t":"fact","text":"Apollo 11, 14, and 15 left special mirrors, called corner-cubes, on the moon. Observatories fire lasers at them to time the round trip. This shows the moon drifting away at about 1.5 inches each year, which is roughly as fast as your fingernails grow."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How fast is the moon drifting away?","options":["About 1.5 inches a year","About 9 inches a year","About 2 feet each year"],"answer":0,"hint":"The fact box gives the exact speed.","explain":"The moon drifts away at about 1.5 inches each year, which is as fast as a fingernail grows."}}]},{"icon":"〰️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Apollo crews left seismometers on the moon. Between 1972 and 1977, they recorded 28 shallow moonquakes."},{"t":"fact","text":"The strongest was about magnitude 5.5, and some kept shaking for over ten minutes. Dry cracked rock with no water in it rings on instead of settling down."},{"t":"p","text":"Nobody has drilled deep into the moon, so what we know about its middle is worked out from seismometers and the laser wobble."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How is the inside of the moon known?","options":["Seen directly by a robot sent down a very deep hole","Known from X-ray pictures taken from the Earth","Worked out from seismometers and the laser wobble"],"answer":2,"hint":"The last paragraph explains how scientists figure it out.","explain":"Seismometers recorded the ground shaking, and mirrors let lasers measure the moon's wobble. Together, they give clues about what is inside."}}]},{"icon":"📜","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The moon keeps track of time."},{"t":"fact","text":"In Genesis, the lesser light is set over the night to mark signs and seasons. The moon goes from new to full and back in about 29 and a half days. That is where the month came from.","label":"The written account says"}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Moon","Visible during the day too"],["Gunpowder","How moon dust smelled"],["Laser","Measures moon's distance"],["Libration","Slight rocking of the moon"],["Corner-cube","Reflects laser beams back"],["Seismometer","A machine that records moonquakes"]]},"picgame":{"type":"tapall","title":"Moon Souvenirs","goal":"Tap what Apollo crews left or took home.","items":[{"icon":"👣","name":"bootprints","good":true},{"icon":"🪞","name":"mirror","good":true},{"icon":"🪨","name":"moon rock","good":true},{"icon":"🌳","name":"tree","good":false},{"icon":"🐘","name":"elephant","good":false},{"icon":"🏠","name":"house","good":false},{"icon":"🐟","name":"fish","good":false},{"icon":"🍕","name":"pizza","good":false}]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"How much of the moon's surface have people actually been able to see from Earth, counting its slight wobble?","options":["About 59 percent","Exactly half","Nearly 100 percent"],"answer":0,"explain":"The wobble, called libration, lets us peek a little around each edge over time."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How do scientists know the moon is drifting away from Earth, a tiny bit every year?","options":["Lasers are bounced off mirrors astronauts left on the surface, and the round-trip is timed","Telescopes measure its size getting smaller","Its orbit has been recalculated using new math"],"answer":0,"explain":"The measurement is good to a few millimeters — the moon drifts about 1.5 inches farther away each year."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Without wind, rain, or water, why are Apollo astronauts' bootprints still on the moon today?","options":["Nothing on the moon can sweep them away except very slow dust from tiny impacts","Special material was sprayed on the ground to preserve them","They were photographed and then physically protected"],"answer":0,"explain":"Orbiting cameras have photographed the landing sites, and the tracks the crews walked and drove still show up."},{"type":"tf","prompt":"The far side of the moon is actually called the \"dark side\" because it never gets any sunlight.","answer":1,"explain":"It gets just as much sunlight as the near side — it simply never faces Earth."},{"type":"tf","prompt":"The best time to see craters clearly on the moon is during a full moon.","answer":1,"explain":"At full moon the sun is overhead with no shadows; craters show up best along the line between lit and unlit ground."}]},{"id":"astro-rocky","course":"astro","kind":"facts","section":"rocky","title":"Rocky Worlds","glyph":"🪨","n":14,"idea":"Explore the planets and learn surprising facts about each one. Find out which planet is hotter than Mercury, and which moon rises in the west.","pages":[{"icon":"🌑","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet Mercury, the smallest planet. It is just 3,032 miles across."},{"t":"fact","text":"Two moons beat it in size. Ganymede, which orbits Jupiter, and Titan, which orbits Saturn, are both wider than Mercury.","label":"Smaller than a moon"},{"t":"p","text":"Mercury also has the widest temperature swing. Where the sun hits it, it is about 800 °F. On the dark side, it drops to about -290 °F. Mercury has almost no air to carry heat from the sunny side to the dark side."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why does Mercury have such a big temperature swing?","options":["It has almost no atmosphere","It is wrapped in thick clouds","It spins too fast"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the last sentence of the page. It says what is missing to carry the heat around.","explain":"Without an atmosphere, there is nothing to carry the heat from the sunny side to the dark side."}}]},{"icon":"🔥","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Mercury is the closest planet to the sun, but it is not the hottest."},{"t":"fact","text":"Venus is the hottest planet. It sits much farther out than Mercury and still runs at roughly 870 °F (465 °C), hot enough to melt lead. A thick blanket of carbon-dioxide air traps the heat.","label":"Hottest planet"},{"t":"p","text":"Venus's air is about 96% carbon dioxide, and the pressure at its surface is around 93 times that of Earth’s."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which planet is the hottest?","options":["Mercury","Mars","Venus"],"answer":2,"hint":"The fact box explains why Venus is hotter than Mercury.","explain":"Venus is the hottest because its thick blanket of carbon-dioxide air traps heat, making it much warmer than Mercury."}}]},{"icon":"🔄","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Venus does something really strange: it turns backwards."},{"t":"fact","text":"One rotation of Venus takes 243 Earth days, but one trip around the sun takes just 225 days. So one full spin takes longer than a whole trip around the sun."},{"t":"p","text":"If you could stand on Venus and see through the clouds, the sun would come up in the west instead of the east."}]},{"icon":"📸","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"In 1982, a Soviet lander named Venera 13 touched down on Venus."},{"t":"fact","text":"It reached the surface on March 1st and was built to last about 30 minutes. Instead, it lasted 127 minutes and sent back the first color photographs of Venus's surface."},{"t":"p","text":"The pictures show flat orange rock under a sky like weak tea. Nothing with a camera has landed on Venus since, so those photos are still our only color views of that surface."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How long did Venera 13 last on the surface of Venus?","options":["About 30 minutes","127 minutes","A full day"],"answer":1,"hint":"It was built to survive about 30 minutes but lasted much longer.","explain":"Venera 13 was designed for a short time on Venus, but it ended up lasting 127 minutes."}}]},{"icon":"🌍","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Imagine seeing Earth from so far away that it just looks like a tiny dot."},{"t":"fact","text":"The famous Blue Marble photo of the whole Earth was taken on 7 December 1972 by the Apollo 17 crew, outbound to the moon. It shows Africa and the Antarctic ice cap, and it became one of the most reproduced photographs ever."},{"t":"p","text":"On 14 February 1990, Voyager 1 took a picture from 3.7 billion miles out. Earth was smaller than a single pixel in that photo."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How big did Earth look to Voyager 1?","options":["About as wide as ten pixels","Smaller than a single pixel","Larger than a hundred pixels"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the last paragraph for the size of Earth in the Voyager picture.","explain":"From Voyager's distance, Earth was smaller than a single pixel in the photo."}}]},{"icon":"🧭","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Nobody has ever been to the middle of Earth. The deepest hole we dug, at Kola in Russia, went 7.6 miles down."},{"t":"fact","text":"The Kola hole drilled down 40,230 feet, but that is only under a fifth of one percent of the way to the center.","label":"How deep"},{"t":"p","text":"What we know about Earth's core is worked out from how earthquake waves bend and where they refuse to travel. Certain waves cannot pass through liquid. When some waves do not show up on the far side of a big quake, it means there is liquid in the middle."}]},{"icon":"🏔️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Olympus Mons on Mars is a giant volcano. It stands about 16 miles high, which is three times taller than Mount Everest."},{"t":"fact","text":"It also spreads roughly 370 miles across, wide enough to cover Arizona. The slope is so broad and gentle that you might not realize you were on a mountain if you stood there."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How tall is Olympus Mons?","options":["About 8 miles","About 16 miles","About 24 miles"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the first number on the page.","explain":"It stands about 16 miles high, three times the height of Everest."}}]},{"icon":"💙","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Mars looks rusty orange all day, but its sunset turns a surprising color."},{"t":"fact","text":"The sunset goes blue because fine iron-rich dust in the thin air scatters blue light forward, bunching it up around the sun while the rest of the sky stays butterscotch."},{"t":"p","text":"Curiosity filmed this from Gale Crater on 15 April 2015. It is the exact reverse of Earth, where the sky is blue and the sunset is red."}]},{"icon":"🌗","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Mars has two small moons, both found by Asaph Hall in 1877."},{"t":"fact","text":"Phobos goes around Mars in just 7 hours and 39 minutes, which means it circles the planet three times in one Martian day. It rises in the west and sets in the east because it moves faster than Mars spins."}]},{"icon":"🧪","blocks":[{"t":"term","word":"MOXIE","text":"A small box that rode on the Perseverance rover and pulled oxygen out of Martian air."},{"t":"fact","text":"Across 16 runs, MOXIE made about 4 ounces (122 grams) of oxygen. The last run finished on 7 August 2023, and the oxygen was at least 98% pure."},{"t":"p","text":"Now a different Mars fact. Gravity on Mars is much less than on Earth, only about 38 percent as strong. A kid who weighs 70 pounds on Earth would weigh about 26 pounds on Mars."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How much would a 70-pound kid weigh on Mars?","options":["About 40 pounds","About 26 pounds","About 100 pounds"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the last paragraph. It tells you how much less gravity pulls there.","explain":"On Mars, gravity is only about 38 percent of Earth's, so a 70-pound kid would weigh about 26 pounds."}}]},{"icon":"📜","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"So what about those lights in the sky? The written account says they were set for signs and seasons."},{"t":"fact","text":"The lights in the sky are set for signs and seasons, and Venus keeps that appointment very plainly.","label":"The written account says"},{"t":"p","text":"It is the brightest thing in the sky after the sun and the moon. It hangs in the evening for months on end, then swaps over and does the same before dawn."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Mercury","Smaller than some moons"],["Olympus Mons","A volcano about 16 miles high"],["Venus","Hottest planet due to thick air"],["Venera 13","First color photos of Venus's surface"],["MOXIE","Pulled oxygen from Martian air"],["Phobos","The moon that rises in the west"]]},"picgame":{"type":"tapall","title":"Red Planet Hunt","goal":"Tap what belongs to Mars.","items":[{"icon":"🌋","name":"giant volcano","good":true},{"icon":"🌑","name":"little moon","good":true},{"icon":"🤖","name":"rover","good":true},{"icon":"🧪","name":"oxygen box","good":true},{"icon":"🌳","name":"tree","good":false},{"icon":"🐘","name":"elephant","good":false},{"icon":"🚢","name":"ship","good":false},{"icon":"🏠","name":"house","good":false}]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"Mercury is closest to the sun, so why isn't it the hottest planet?","options":["Venus's thick carbon-dioxide air traps heat, even though it's farther from the sun","Mercury is actually farther from the sun than it appears","Venus is closer to the sun than Mercury"],"answer":0,"explain":"Venus runs about 870°F — hot enough to melt lead — because of its thick, heat-trapping atmosphere."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why would a sunset on Venus actually rise in the west instead of the east?","options":["Venus spins backward, and its day is even longer than its year","Venus doesn't rotate at all","Venus orbits the sun in the opposite direction"],"answer":0,"explain":"One Venus rotation takes 243 Earth days; one trip around the sun takes only 225."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How do scientists know what's inside Earth's core, since nobody has ever drilled anywhere near it?","options":["They study where certain earthquake waves simply never arrive on the far side of the planet","Deep-sea submarines have reached the core","It's measured the same way ocean depth is measured"],"answer":0,"explain":"That gap in the readings — a shadow certain waves can't cross — is the evidence pointing to a liquid outer core."},{"type":"tf","prompt":"A 70-pound kid would weigh less on Mars because its gravity is weaker than Earth's.","answer":0,"explain":"Mars's gravity is about 38 percent of Earth's, so that same kid would weigh about 26 pounds there."},{"type":"tf","prompt":"Mercury is the smallest object in the solar system that people call a planet.","answer":0,"explain":"Two moons — Ganymede and Titan — are actually wider than Mercury, but they orbit planets instead of the sun."}]},{"id":"astro-giants","course":"astro","kind":"facts","section":"giants","title":"Giant Planets","glyph":"🪐","n":14,"idea":"Jupiter has moons with surprising secrets, like volcanoes and oceans. Discover how a planet tipped on its side and an unseen planet found with math.","pages":[{"icon":"🔭","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"On 7 January 1610, Galileo pointed his homemade telescope at Jupiter and saw three little stars beside it."},{"t":"fact","text":"On 13 January a fourth appeared. Night after night, they shifted their positions around Jupiter."},{"t":"try","text":"Steady a pair of binoculars against a wall or a fence and look at Jupiter. You will see three or four tiny points strung out in a line, just like Galileo did. Look again the next night and you will see them move."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did Galileo conclude the little stars were doing?","options":["Floating away into space","Going round Jupiter","Moving closer to Earth"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It says he saw them shifting their positions.","explain":"He noticed that these little stars changed position each night, which led him to conclude they were going round Jupiter."}}]},{"icon":"🪐","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Jupiter is huge. Imagine eleven Earths lined up side by side. That is how wide Jupiter is."},{"t":"fact","text":"Jupiter is 88,846 miles across at the equator and holds more than twice as much material as all the other planets combined."},{"t":"p","text":"It spins really fast too. One full rotation takes just 9 hours and 55 minutes."},{"t":"p","text":"Spinning that quickly has a noticeable effect. Jupiter is squashed at the poles, making it look a bit like a flattened ball. You can see this flattening in a good telescope."}]},{"icon":"🔴","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Jupiter has a giant storm that has lasted for centuries. It is called the Great Red Spot."},{"t":"fact","text":"In the 1800s, the long axis of the Great Red Spot was about 24,000 miles. Hubble now measures it at about 10,250 miles."},{"t":"p","text":"This shrinking happened while people watched. Amateur astronomers noticed the shrinking speeding up in 2012."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What has the Great Red Spot been doing?","options":["Growing bigger","Moving to a different spot","Shrinking"],"answer":2,"hint":"The measurements show it was much larger in the 1800s.","explain":"The Great Red Spot has been shrinking over time, and people have watched this happen."}}]},{"icon":"🌋","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Linda Morabito was looking at navigation photos from Voyager 1 on March 9, 1979."},{"t":"fact","text":"She noticed an odd bulge off the edge of Jupiter's moon Io. It was a plume from an erupting volcano, about 170 miles tall."},{"t":"term","word":"plume","text":"A tall cloud of gas and dust thrown up from a volcano."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did Linda Morabito find?","options":["A volcano erupting on Io","A huge storm over Jupiter","A comet hitting Saturn"],"answer":0,"hint":"The bulge she noticed was actually a plume.","explain":"Linda Morabito found a volcanic plume over 170 miles tall. It was the first active volcano seen beyond Earth."}}]},{"icon":"🌊","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Europa is one of Jupiter's moons, covered in cracked ice."},{"t":"fact","text":"Something under that ice bends Jupiter's magnetic field in a way that fits a salty ocean. Nobody has drilled through to see it. The ocean is worked out from a magnetic reading and the pattern of cracks.","label":"Worked out, not seen"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How do we know about Europa's ocean?","options":["It was seen by cameras on a rover that drove across the ice","It is worked out from a magnetic reading and the pattern of cracks","A probe was lowered through the ice and sent back pictures"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It tells you how scientists figured it out.","explain":"The best answer comes from how Jupiter's magnetic field bends and the way the ice cracks."}}]},{"icon":"💍","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Saturn's rings stretch out to about 175,000 miles from the planet."},{"t":"fact","text":"They are only around 30 feet thick and made up of over 99% water ice. Seen edge-on, something that wide thins out and nearly vanishes."},{"t":"p","text":"Saturn itself is lighter than water for its size. Given a big enough ocean, it would float."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"About how thick are Saturn's rings?","options":["A few miles","Several thousand feet","About 30 feet"],"answer":2,"hint":"The fact box tells you exactly how thick the rings are.","explain":"Saturn's rings are only about 30 feet thick."}}]},{"icon":"💨","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Enceladus is a small icy moon of Saturn with four long cracks near its south pole, nicknamed the tiger stripes."},{"t":"fact","text":"Jets of water vapor and ice grains fire out of those cracks into space. Cassini flew straight through the spray and analyzed it, which is like a spacecraft tasting a moon without ever landing on it."},{"t":"p","text":"Cassini spent thirteen years at Saturn and was then destroyed on purpose. On 15 September 2017, it flew into Saturn's atmosphere and burned up."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How did Cassini taste Enceladus?","options":["It flew through the spray","It landed on the surface","It crashed into a moon"],"answer":0,"hint":"The fact box tells you exactly how Cassini analyzed the jets from Enceladus.","explain":"Cassini flew through the spray of water vapor and ice grains coming from Enceladus."}}]},{"icon":"🧡","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"On 14 January 2005, the Huygens probe parachuted down through Titan's orange haze and landed."},{"t":"fact","text":"No spacecraft has ever landed farther from Earth than Huygens did on Titan. It transmitted for 72 minutes from the surface, sent back the sound of the wind, and photographed channels cut by flowing liquid methane."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What liquid cut the channels on Titan?","options":["Water","Acid rain","Methane"],"answer":2,"hint":"The fact box tells you the type of liquid that carved the channels.","explain":"On Titan, the liquid cutting channels is methane, not water."}}]},{"icon":"🛞","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Imagine a planet that is tipped over so far it rolls around the sun on its side."},{"t":"fact","text":"Uranus is tipped over by about 98 degrees, rolling on its side instead of spinning upright."},{"t":"p","text":"Its rings circle its equator, but because it is tipped over, they stand up like a target aimed straight at you."}]},{"icon":"🔱","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Imagine finding a planet with just a pencil and paper."},{"t":"fact","text":"Neptune was found this way. Uranus was not moving as it should, so Urbain Le Verrier worked out where an unseen planet would have to sit to tug it that way. Johann Galle aimed his telescope there on 23 September 1846 and found Neptune within one degree."},{"t":"p","text":"Neptune gets very little sunlight, yet it has the fastest winds in our solar system. Voyager 2 flew past on 25 August 1989 and clocked them near 1,200 miles per hour around a storm called the Great Dark Spot."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which came first for Neptune?","options":["The calculation","Seeing it through a telescope","Naming it"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It explains how Neptune was found.","explain":"The calculation came first. Urbain Le Verrier figured out where Neptune should be, and then Johann Galle looked there."}}]},{"icon":"📜","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The written account says the lights in the sky were set for signs and seasons."},{"t":"fact","text":"The lights in the sky are set for signs and seasons. Jupiter and Saturn are two of them, both bright enough to see without any equipment. On 21 December 2020, they came within a tenth of a degree of each other, a fifth of the moon's width apart, the closest since 1623.","label":"The written account says"}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Linda Morabito","Found a volcano on Io"],["Great Red Spot","Jupiter's giant shrinking storm"],["Enceladus","Moon with tiger stripes spewing water"],["Huygens","Probe that landed on Titan"],["Neptune","Planet found with calculations"],["Uranus","Planet rolling on its side"]]},"picgame":{"type":"catch","title":"Moon Fountains","goal":"Catch what shoots out of the moons.","items":[{"icon":"☁️","name":"dust cloud","good":true},{"icon":"💦","name":"water spray","good":true},{"icon":"🧊","name":"ice grains","good":true},{"icon":"🍎","name":"apple","good":false},{"icon":"🎈","name":"balloon","good":false},{"icon":"🧸","name":"teddy bear","good":false},{"icon":"🪁","name":"kite","good":false},{"icon":"🌳","name":"tree","good":false}]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What's unusual about Saturn, compared to every other planet?","options":["It's the only planet less dense than water — it would float in a big enough ocean","It's the only planet with rings","It's the only planet that spins backward"],"answer":0,"explain":"Its average density is 0.69 grams per cubic centimeter; water is 1."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How was Neptune actually discovered, before anyone pointed a telescope at it?","options":["A mathematician calculated where an unseen planet must be, from how it tugged on Uranus","It was spotted by accident during a routine sky survey","A probe detected its gravity from a distance"],"answer":0,"explain":"Urbain Le Verrier did the math; Johann Galle then found Neptune within one degree of the predicted spot."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How do scientists think Europa has a hidden ocean under its ice, without ever drilling through?","options":["Something under the ice bends Jupiter's magnetic field in a way that fits a salty ocean","A probe has already landed and confirmed it directly","Telescopes can see through the ice to the water below"],"answer":0,"explain":"It's a conclusion drawn from a magnetic reading and the pattern of cracks — never actually observed."},{"type":"tf","prompt":"Jupiter's Great Red Spot has been shrinking since the 1800s, which scientists have actually measured over time.","answer":0,"explain":"In 1879 it measured about 24,000 miles across; Hubble now measures it at about 10,250 miles."},{"type":"tf","prompt":"Cassini was deliberately crashed into Saturn's atmosphere so it could never accidentally contaminate one of Saturn's moons.","answer":0,"explain":"It burned up on purpose on September 15, 2017, so it could never carry Earth bacteria onto a moon worth protecting."}]},{"id":"astro-smallstuff","course":"astro","kind":"facts","section":"smallstuff","title":"Rocks & Comets","glyph":"☄️","n":14,"idea":"The sky is full of small objects that follow their own schedules. Discover how a comet's tail points away from the sun and which of two missions got its asteroid samples home first.","pages":[{"icon":"🌌","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Films show the asteroid belt as a shooting gallery where you have to dodge rocks. The real belt is mostly empty space."},{"t":"fact","text":"On average, asteroids are about 600,000 miles apart. That's more than twice the distance from Earth to the moon."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How far apart are asteroids in the belt on average?","options":["About 100 miles","About 600,000 miles","About 1 million miles"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It gives the exact number.","explain":"The asteroids are about 600,000 miles apart, making the belt mostly empty space."}}]},{"icon":"🪨","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The largest thing in the asteroid belt is Ceres, about 585 miles across."},{"t":"fact","text":"Giuseppe Piazzi found it on the first night of 1801, making it the very first asteroid anyone spotted."},{"t":"p","text":"In 2015, NASA's Dawn spacecraft arrived and went into orbit around Ceres. That made Ceres the first dwarf planet ever visited."},{"t":"p","text":"Ceres holds two titles at once. It is both the biggest asteroid in the belt and the nearest dwarf planet to us."}]},{"icon":"🗳️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"In Prague on 24 August 2006, astronomers voted to define what a planet is. A planet must go around the sun, be round itself, and clear its orbital lane of other objects."},{"t":"term","word":"dwarf planet","text":"A world that goes around the sun, is round, but has not cleared its orbital lane."},{"t":"fact","text":"Pluto passes the first two rules but fails the third, because it shares its region with many other icy bodies. So Pluto became a dwarf planet. On 14 July 2015, New Horizons flew past it at about 32,000 miles an hour, 7,800 miles above the surface."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What makes Pluto a dwarf planet?","options":["It has no moons circling around it","It circles the sun the wrong way","It has not cleared its orbital lane"],"answer":2,"hint":"The facts box tells you why Pluto is a dwarf planet.","explain":"Pluto shares its orbit with other bodies, so it has not cleared its orbital lane."}}]},{"icon":"⚫","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The largest meteorite anyone has ever found is still right where it landed."},{"t":"fact","text":"The Hoba meteorite, in Namibia, weighs over 60 tons and measures about 9 feet by 9 feet by 3 feet thick. A farmer's plow struck it in 1920, and nobody has ever moved it because nobody can."},{"t":"p","text":"It is a slab of metal, mostly iron and nickel, with just a trace of cobalt. People have chipped pieces off over the years, so it weighs a little less now than when it was found. You can walk right up to it and touch it."}]},{"icon":"💥","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"On 15 February 2013, a rock about 60 feet across came in over a city called Chelyabinsk in Russia."},{"t":"fact","text":"The rock blew apart roughly 18 miles up. Nothing hit the city; the shock wave did. It blew in windows across a wide strip of ground and cut about 1,500 people with flying glass."},{"t":"p","text":"Nobody was killed. The airburst released energy on the order of 400 to 600 kilotons of TNT, making it the largest such event over a populated place in living memory."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What hurt people in Chelyabinsk?","options":["The shock wave","Pieces of the rock falling","The heat from the explosion"],"answer":0,"hint":"The shock wave blew in windows and cut people with flying glass.","explain":"The shock wave from the explosion was what hurt people, not pieces of the rock or other effects."}}]},{"icon":"✨","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Have you ever seen a shooting star? It looks like a quick streak of light across the sky."},{"t":"fact","text":"A shooting star is usually just a grain of sand flashing through the air 50 to 75 miles (80 to 120 km) above Earth."},{"t":"p","text":"The bright streak happens because the air in front of it glows white as it moves so fast. Most burn out in under a second, and the whole thing happens way above where any airplane can fly."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is a shooting star usually?","options":["A tiny star","A grain of sand","A piece of metal"],"answer":1,"hint":"The streak of light comes from something tiny burning up high in the sky.","explain":"It’s usually a grain of sand, moving fast enough that the air ahead of it glows white."}}]},{"icon":"☄️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Have you ever seen a comet in the sky? It has a bright head and a long tail."},{"t":"fact","text":"A comet’s tail does not trail behind it like smoke off a train. It points away from the sun, always."},{"t":"p","text":"There are usually two tails on a comet, and they point slightly differently. The gas tail glows bluish and runs almost exactly away from the sun. The dust tail is broader, whiter, and curved."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which way does a comet's tail point?","options":["Away from the sun","Behind the comet","Towards the comet"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It says the tail points away from the sun.","explain":"The tail always points away from the sun because sunlight and the stream of particles blowing off the sun push the dust and gas straight outward."}}]},{"icon":"🔄","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Edmond Halley noticed something special in 1705. He saw that bright comets seen in 1531, 1607, and 1682 all followed the same path."},{"t":"fact","text":"Halley said it was one comet coming back and predicted its next return for 1758. It arrived sixteen years after he died."},{"t":"p","text":"Chinese astronomers had logged the same comet as far back as 240 BC. It came again in 1835, 1910, and 1986."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"When did Halley predict the comet's return?","options":["1705","1758","1786"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It says he predicted it would return in 1758.","explain":"In 1705, Halley predicted the comet would return in 1758, sixteen years after his death."}}]},{"icon":"🚀","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"On 12 November 2014, a lander named Philae tried to touch down on comet 67P."},{"t":"fact","text":"Its harpoons failed to fire, so it bounced and drifted for nearly two hours before landing about half a mile away in shadow."},{"t":"p","text":"Because Philae ended up out of the sunlight, its solar panels could not keep the batteries charged. Even so, it worked for around 64 hours and sent its measurements home before it went quiet."},{"t":"p","text":"It was the first landing on a comet ever. The mother ship Rosetta took ten years to get there."}]},{"icon":"🧪","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"On 24 September 2023, a capsule landed in the Utah desert with 4.29 ounces (121.6 g) of asteroid Bennu inside."},{"t":"fact","text":"OSIRIS-REx touched Bennu for just a few seconds and grabbed a handful. It needed at least 60 grams to count as a success, but it brought back twice that amount."},{"t":"p","text":"Japan's Hayabusa2 mission beat it home. On 6 December 2020, it dropped a capsule into the South Australian desert with 5.4 grams of Ryugu inside. Its older sister, Hayabusa, had already brought back a few tiny grains from the asteroid Itokawa in 2010."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which of these two missions brought its asteroid samples home first?","options":["NASA's OSIRIS-REx","Both at the same time","Japan's Hayabusa2"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the dates in the page text.","explain":"Hayabusa2 brought its samples home on 6 December 2020, before OSIRIS-REx's return in September 2023."}}]},{"icon":"📜","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Comets come back on schedule, and meteor showers keep the calendar."},{"t":"fact","text":"Genesis 1:14 says the lights in the sky were set \"for signs and for seasons, and for days and years.\"","label":"The written account says"},{"t":"p","text":"Edmond Halley predicted his comet's return decades ahead, and it arrived. The Perseids turn up in the same week of every August."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["OSIRIS-REx","Grabbed samples from Bennu"],["Hayabusa2","Dropped Ryugu samples in Australia"],["Philae","Bounced on a comet"],["Halley's Comet","Predicted return in 1758"],["Perseids","Show up every August"]]},"picgame":{"type":"catch","title":"Small Stuff","goal":"Catch the little things in space.","items":[{"icon":"🪨","name":"asteroid","good":true},{"icon":"☄️","name":"comet","good":true},{"icon":"🌠","name":"shooting star","good":true},{"icon":"☀️","name":"sun","good":false},{"icon":"🌍","name":"earth","good":false},{"icon":"🌕","name":"moon","good":false},{"icon":"🪐","name":"planet","good":false},{"icon":"⭐","name":"star","good":false}]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"Movies show the asteroid belt as a crowded obstacle course. What's it really like?","options":["Mostly empty — asteroids average about 600,000 miles apart","Just as crowded as the movies show","Dense enough that spacecraft must carefully steer around rocks"],"answer":0,"explain":"That's more than twice the Earth-moon distance — spacecraft fly straight through without steering around anything."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why does a comet's tail always point away from the sun, no matter which direction the comet is moving?","options":["Sunlight and the solar wind push the dust and gas straight outward","The tail is just smoke trailing behind it like a train","Gravity pulls the tail away from the sun"],"answer":0,"explain":"A comet heading back out of the solar system actually travels tail-first."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why was Pluto reclassified as a \"dwarf planet\" in 2006?","options":["It hasn't cleared its own orbital neighborhood of other similar-sized objects","It turned out to be much smaller than first thought","It doesn't orbit the sun"],"answer":0,"explain":"Pluto shares its region of the solar system with Eris and other bodies of similar size — nothing about Pluto itself changed, the definition did."},{"type":"tf","prompt":"The largest meteorite ever found, in Namibia, is still lying exactly where it landed because no one can move it.","answer":0,"explain":"Hoba weighs over 60 tons — a farmer's plow struck it in 1920, and it's never been moved since."},{"type":"tf","prompt":"Edmond Halley actually lived to see his famous comet return in 1758, just as he predicted.","answer":1,"explain":"It arrived sixteen years after he died — he predicted it decades in advance."}]},{"id":"astro-stars","course":"astro","kind":"facts","section":"stars","title":"Stars","glyph":"⭐","n":14,"idea":"Stars twinkle because they are faraway points of light, while planets stay steady. Discover why some stars look red and others blue, and why a star like Betelgeuse can suddenly dim.","pages":[{"icon":"✨","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Have you ever wondered why stars twinkle, but planets do not?"},{"t":"fact","text":"Stars are so far away that they arrive as single points of light, and any wobble in the air shoves that whole point sideways, so they twinkle. Planets arrive as tiny discs, which means many points of light. The wobbles cancel each other out, so planets stay steady."},{"t":"try","text":"Next clear night, look for a bright light that does not flicker. It is likely a planet."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why do planets not twinkle?","options":["They shine with their own steady glow, not sunlight","They are above the air, where nothing bends light","They arrive as tiny discs and the wobbles cancel out"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It explains why the wobbles cancel for planets.","explain":"Planets appear as tiny discs made up of many points, so the air's wobbles cancel each other out."}}]},{"icon":"🌡️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Have you ever noticed that some stars look red and others look blue? The color of a star is like a thermometer reading."},{"t":"fact","text":"Betelgeuse, the orange star on Orion's shoulder, is about 6,000 °F (3,500 K). Rigel, the blue-white one at his foot, is around 21,000 °F (12,000 K). Red is cool, blue is hot."},{"t":"try","text":"On a winter evening, find Orion. Look at Betelgeuse at the top left of the four corners, then Rigel at the bottom right. Go back and forth a few times. Betelgeuse is clearly orange, Rigel is white with a blue edge."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which stars are hotter?","options":["Red ones","Blue ones","Orange ones"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It shows the temperatures of Betelgeuse and Rigel.","explain":"Blue stars like Rigel are much hotter than red stars like Betelgeuse."}}]},{"icon":"🔆","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Look up at the night sky, and you will see a star that outshines all others. That's Sirius, the brightest star in the night sky, with a magnitude of minus 1.46."},{"t":"fact","text":"The brightness scale runs backwards because of Hipparchus. He sorted stars into six ranks, calling the brightest ones first magnitude. So now, bigger numbers mean fainter stars."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"On the magnitude scale, what does a bigger number mean?","options":["A fainter star","A brighter star","A closer star"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It explains that Hipparchus sorted stars with bigger numbers for fainter stars.","explain":"Bigger numbers on the magnitude scale mean fainter stars, thanks to Hipparchus's system."}}]},{"icon":"👆","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Here is how astronomers measure how far away a star is, and you can do it with one finger."},{"t":"term","word":"parallax","text":"The jump you see when you switch eyes. It is how astronomers measure the distance to nearby stars."},{"t":"try","text":"Hold your thumb at arm’s length and blink one eye, then the other. Now bring it in to a foot away and try again. The nearer it is, the further it jumps."},{"t":"fact","text":"Earth's position in January and its position in July are like two eyes, and the star appears to jump between them. Astronomers use this jump to measure the distance to nearby stars."}]},{"icon":"📐","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"In 1838, Friedrich Bessel became the first person to measure the distance to a star."},{"t":"fact","text":"He measured the parallax of 61 Cygni and found it was about 10 light-years away. The modern figure is 11.4 light-years, so he was very close."},{"t":"p","text":"The nearest star after the sun is Proxima Centauri, which is 4.24 light-years away. But you cannot see it with your eyes alone. It is too faint, around magnitude 11."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Who first measured the distance to a star?","options":["Friedrich Bessel","Galileo Galilei","Johannes Kepler"],"answer":0,"hint":"The fact box says who was the first to measure the distance to 61 Cygni.","explain":"Friedrich Bessel was the first to measure the distance to a star, in 1838."}}]},{"icon":"🛰️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"In 1838, Friedrich Bessel measured the distance to a star. Now, a spacecraft named Gaia has done the same for more than a billion stars."},{"t":"fact","text":"Gaia measured positions for about 1.8 billion stars, and parallaxes for about 1.5 billion of them. Look up at almost any star you can see with your eyes. Its distance is now a measured number, not just a guess."},{"t":"p","text":"Gaia had to measure angles smaller than a thousandth of an arcsecond. That is about the width of a human hair seen from ten miles away. It did this by scanning the entire sky over and over, comparing where every star appeared to sit."}]},{"icon":"🏹","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The stars of Orion are not a real family. They only look like a picture because of where we stand."},{"t":"fact","text":"The three belt stars in Orion look evenly spaced, but they are at very different distances. Alnitak is around 1,260 light-years away, while Alnilam is nearly 2,000 light-years away. These distances are worked out based on how bright the stars appear.","label":"Worked out"},{"t":"p","text":"Move far enough sideways and the hunter falls apart. The pattern would change completely because its stars are at very different distances."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why would Orion fall apart if you moved sideways?","options":["Its stars are all the same color","Its stars are at very different distances","Its stars move in circles around each other"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the worked out distances of Alnitak and Alnilam.","explain":"The stars that make up Orion are at very different distances from us, so moving sideways would change their apparent positions and the pattern of the constellation."}}]},{"icon":"🔁","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Algol is a star that dims and brightens over and over again. It does this every 2 days and 21 hours."},{"t":"fact","text":"Algol loses about two thirds of its light, and then gets it back, repeating this cycle every 2 days and 21 hours."},{"t":"p","text":"In 1783, John Goodricke, a deaf teenager from York, figured out why. He said a second star was passing in front of the first one."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What makes Algol dim?","options":["A cloud of dust drifts in front of it","It spins and shows its dark side","A second star passes in front of it"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the fact box that explains the cycle.","explain":"John Goodricke figured out that a second star passes in front, causing Algol to dim."}}]},{"icon":"😮","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Betelgeuse, one of the brightest stars in the sky, suddenly grew dimmer."},{"t":"fact","text":"Between late 2019 and February 2020, Betelgeuse lost roughly two thirds of its brightness. The Very Large Telescope in Chile saw the star's disk go lopsided as a dark patch appeared."},{"t":"p","text":"A cloud of dust that had been thrown off the star drifted across about a quarter of its face. This cloud was more than a hundred million miles wide."},{"t":"p","text":"By the end of 2020, Betelgeuse was back to its usual brightness."}]},{"icon":"💥","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"On July 4, 1054, Chinese astronomers saw a guest star near the southern horn of Taurus."},{"t":"fact","text":"This star was bright enough to see in broad daylight for 23 days and visible at night for 653 days before it faded out."},{"t":"p","text":"No European record of it survives. Now in that same spot, there is a faint tangle of glowing gas called the Crab Nebula."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How long was the guest star visible in daylight in 1054?","options":["7 days","23 days","653 days"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It gives the days in daylight and the days at night.","explain":"The guest star was visible in broad daylight for exactly 23 days."}}]},{"icon":"📜","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Under a truly dark sky, an ordinary eye can pick out about 9,100 stars across the whole sky."},{"t":"fact","text":"Psalm 147:4 says of God, \"he determines the number of the stars; he gives to all of them their names.\"","label":"The written account says"},{"t":"p","text":"Somebody counted first. The figure comes from star catalogs of everything brighter than magnitude 6.5, which totals 9,096 stars over the entire sky."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Parallax","The jump seen when switching eyes"],["Hipparchus","Sorted stars into six ranks"],["Algol","Star that dims every 2 days and 21 hours"],["Betelgeuse","Bright star that dimmed in 2019"],["Gaia","Spacecraft measuring billions of stars"]]},"picgame":{"type":"tapall","title":"Find the Stars","goal":"Tap everything that is a star.","items":[{"icon":"⭐","name":"star","good":true},{"icon":"🌟","name":"bright star","good":true},{"icon":"☀️","name":"sun","good":true},{"icon":"🪐","name":"planet","good":false},{"icon":"🌕","name":"moon","good":false},{"icon":"🛰️","name":"spacecraft","good":false},{"icon":"☄️","name":"comet","good":false},{"icon":"🌍","name":"earth","good":false}]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does a star's color actually tell you?","options":["How hot its surface is — blue is hotter, red is cooler","How far away it is","How old the star is"],"answer":0,"explain":"The same way a poker in a fire goes from dull red to orange to white as it heats up."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What's the simplest trick for telling a planet from a star in the night sky?","options":["Planets don't twinkle the way stars do","Planets are always brighter than stars","Stars only appear in winter"],"answer":0,"explain":"A star arrives as a single point of light that wobbles in the air; a planet arrives as a tiny disc, and the wobbles cancel out."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How is the distance to a nearby star actually measured?","options":["Parallax — watching how the star appears to shift as Earth orbits the sun","By measuring how bright the star looks","By timing how long its light takes to arrive"],"answer":0,"explain":"It's the same trick as holding up a finger and closing one eye, then the other — Earth's two eyes are its position in January and July."},{"type":"tf","prompt":"The three stars in Orion's belt are actually close neighbors to each other in space.","answer":1,"explain":"They only look evenly spaced from here — Alnitak sits around 1,260 light-years away, Alnilam nearly 2,000."},{"type":"tf","prompt":"A deaf teenager figured out why the star Algol dims and brightens every few days, by carefully timing it.","answer":0,"explain":"John Goodricke worked out in 1783 that a second star was passing in front of the first — he was 18 at the time."}]},{"id":"astro-deepsky","course":"astro","kind":"facts","section":"deepsky","title":"Deep Sky","glyph":"🌌","n":14,"idea":"Some of the lights in the winter sky are not stars at all. Find out what a nebula really is, and how a patch of sky that looked empty held about 3,000 galaxies.","pages":[{"icon":"🌌","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Step outside on a clear winter night. Some of the lights are not stars."},{"t":"fact","text":"The Milky Way looks like a band because we are inside it, looking along its flat part. You see more stars when you look along the disc than when you look out of it.","label":"Why it looks like a band"},{"t":"p","text":"In 1610, Galileo pointed his telescope at the Milky Way. He saw that the glow broke into separate stars. This proved that it was made of faint stars."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why does the Milky Way look like a band?","options":["We are inside it, looking along its flat part","It is a thin layer of dust high in our air","The stars in it are all the same brightness"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the first fact on the page. It explains why we see a band.","explain":"We are inside the Milky Way and looking along its flat part, so we see more stars in that direction."}}]},{"icon":"✨","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Look up at the night sky, and find a tiny group of stars that looks like a dipper."},{"t":"fact","text":"Most people can see six or seven stars in it with just their eyes. Binoculars turn that into dozens."},{"t":"p","text":"This cluster is called the Pleiades. It sits about 444 light-years away. That distance was measured by the Gaia spacecraft using a method called parallax."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many stars do most people pick out in the Pleiades?","options":["Two or three","Six or seven","Forty or more"],"answer":1,"hint":"Most people see six or seven stars with just their eyes, but binoculars show dozens.","explain":"With your eyes alone, you can usually see six or seven stars in the Pleiades. Binoculars reveal many more."}}]},{"icon":"🧭","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Hanging below Orion's belt is a fuzzy patch you can see with your eyes on a clear winter night. That is the Orion Nebula."},{"t":"fact","text":"The Orion Nebula is about 1,344 light-years away. People call it a stellar nursery because they think stars form inside it, but nobody has watched a star form there."},{"t":"p","text":"Right in the middle of the Orion Nebula, a small telescope shows four points of light in a tight lopsided box. They are called the Trapezium."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why do people call the Orion Nebula a stellar nursery?","options":["Because it is shaped like a cradle","Because baby stars were filmed being born there","Because people think stars form inside it"],"answer":2,"hint":"The nebula is thought to be a place where stars form, but no one has seen it happen.","explain":"While people think stars form inside the Orion Nebula, nobody has actually watched a star form there."}}]},{"icon":"☁️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The word nebula means cloud in Latin. A long time ago, it was used for any fuzzy patch seen through a telescope."},{"t":"term","word":"nebula","text":"Just Latin for cloud, once meaning any blurry spot in the sky."},{"t":"fact","text":"Better telescopes sorted them out. Some turned out to be swarms of stars too close together to separate, and others were actually clouds of gas."},{"t":"p","text":"Lord Rosse had a big 72-inch telescope in Ireland. In 1845, it showed a spiral shape inside M51, something nobody had seen before in one of these patches."}]},{"icon":"🎨","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Photos show nebulae in all sorts of colors, but through a telescope they look gray."},{"t":"fact","text":"Your eye has two kinds of light sensor, and the one that works in the dark cannot see color at all. A camera can sit collecting light for an hour, but your eye starts over roughly every tenth of a second."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why does a nebula look gray through a telescope?","options":["The part of your eye that works in the dark cannot see color","The mirrors inside a telescope can only show shades of gray","The light travels so far from a nebula that its color fades"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It explains why your eye sees gray.","explain":"Your eye’s night vision sensor can’t see colors, so nebulae appear gray through a telescope."}}]},{"icon":"🔵","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A globular cluster is a ball of stars packed so tight the middle looks solid."},{"t":"term","word":"globular cluster","text":"A ball of stars so tightly packed that it looks like a solid glow in the center."},{"t":"fact","text":"M13, found by Halley in 1714, holds somewhere between a hundred thousand and six hundred thousand stars. It appears to the naked eye when the sky is clear and the moon is away."},{"t":"p","text":"In a small telescope, the edge of the ball breaks into separate points, but the center stays a solid glow. The stars near the middle are really crowded much closer together than stars around here."}]},{"icon":"📖","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Charles Messier loved to hunt comets, but he kept getting fooled by fuzzy patches in the sky."},{"t":"fact","text":"He wrote a list of those patches that just sat still, so he wouldn't waste another night on them. Now it is the favorite catalog for amateur astronomers: 110 objects from M1 to M110."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why did Messier make his list?","options":["So he could keep a record of the stars used to steer ships","Because the king asked him to count every star in the sky","So he would stop being fooled by fuzzy patches while hunting comets"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the first sentence of the page.","explain":"He wanted to know which objects were just sitting still, so he wouldn't waste time thinking they were comets."}}]},{"icon":"🕳️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"In December 1995, Hubble was pointed at a scrap of sky that looked completely empty."},{"t":"fact","text":"The spot was about as wide as a dime seen from 75 feet away. Hubble stared there for ten days and took 342 pictures. What they found: roughly 3,000 galaxies in what had looked like nothing at all."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many galaxies did Hubble find in the empty patch of sky?","options":["About 342","About 3,000","About 75"],"answer":1,"hint":"The page says how many exposures were taken and how many galaxies were found.","explain":"Hubble found about 3,000 galaxies where it had looked like there was nothing at all."}}]},{"icon":"🌑","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"In the southern sky, some shapes are not made of stars at all."},{"t":"fact","text":"The Coalsack, beside the Southern Cross, is a place where the Milky Way simply stops. It is a cloud of dust that blocks everything behind it. Aboriginal Australians mapped it as the head of an emu whose body stretches on toward Scorpius."},{"t":"p","text":"The Coalsack is one of the few dark nebulae obvious to the unaided eye, and it is about 600 light-years away."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is the Coalsack?","options":["A tight group of bright stars shaped like a sack","A cloud of dust that blocks the stars behind it","A faint galaxy hiding far beyond the Milky Way"],"answer":1,"hint":"The page describes what the Coalsack blocks.","explain":"The Coalsack is a cloud of dust that blocks the stars behind it, making it look like a dark shape in the sky."}}]},{"icon":"🔭","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"In the night sky, there is one thing so far away that it almost seems impossible to see without help. It's the Andromeda galaxy."},{"t":"fact","text":"The Andromeda galaxy is about 2.5 million light-years away. This distance is worked out by comparing how bright certain pulsing stars look with how bright they are taken to be.","label":"Worked out, not seen directly"},{"t":"p","text":"Edwin Hubble figured this out first in 1923 using a big telescope on Mount Wilson. He looked at stars whose brightness changes in a way that tells us how far away they are."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How is Andromeda's distance worked out?","options":["By sending a radio signal toward it and timing how long the echo takes to return","By counting how many stars it holds and comparing that number to the Milky Way","By comparing how bright certain pulsing stars look with how bright they are taken to be"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It explains how the brightness of certain stars helps calculate the distance.","explain":"The distance is worked out by looking at how bright certain pulsing stars in Andromeda appear and comparing that to how bright they are assumed to be."}}]},{"icon":"📜","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"In the night sky, some things have carried the same names for a very long time."},{"t":"fact","text":"Job 38:31 asks a question. \"Can you bind the chains of the Pleiades or loose the cords of Orion?\" Both are still up there, and most Bibles use these two names for them. You can go outside on a winter night and look at them.","label":"The written account says"},{"t":"p","text":"Very few old books can be checked this way. The Pleiades is a small knot of stars in Taurus, and Orion is the brightest figure in the winter sky. To the eye, neither one has changed."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Pleiades","A small knot of stars"],["Orion","The brightest winter sky figure"],["Job 38:31","An old verse asking about star clusters"],["Andromeda galaxy","Farthest thing seen without a telescope"],["Coalsack","A dark nebula blocking stars"]]},"picgame":{"type":"sort","title":"Stars or Clouds","goal":"Is it a group of stars, or a cloud?","bins":[{"icon":"⭐","label":"stars"},{"icon":"☁️","label":"cloud"}],"items":[{"icon":"🌌","name":"milky way","bin":0},{"icon":"✨","name":"pleiades","bin":0},{"icon":"🔵","name":"star ball","bin":0},{"icon":"🏹","name":"belt stars","bin":0},{"icon":"🌫️","name":"orion nebula","bin":1},{"icon":"🌑","name":"coalsack","bin":1}]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"The Orion Nebula is called a \"stellar nursery\" because people think stars form there. What has actually been observed happening?","options":["Nobody has ever watched a star form inside it","Multiple stars have been filmed forming","Only the birth of planets, not stars"],"answer":0,"explain":"The nebula's distance (1,344 light-years) has been measured by parallax — but no one has watched what's happening inside it."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why do photographs of nebulae show vivid color, while looking through a telescope shows only gray?","options":["The eye's dark-adapted sensors can't see color at all, but a camera can collect light for hours","Telescopes remove the color on purpose","The colors only appear in deep space, not through glass"],"answer":0,"explain":"A camera can sit collecting light for an hour; your eye resets roughly every tenth of a second."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why does the Milky Way look like a band across the sky instead of the spiral shape we see in photos of other galaxies?","options":["We're inside it, looking along its flat disk from within","It only forms a band during certain seasons","Clouds block the rest of its shape"],"answer":0,"explain":"Galileo settled what it was made of in 1610, pointing a telescope at it and watching the glow break into separate stars."},{"type":"tf","prompt":"When Hubble stared at an apparently empty patch of sky for ten days in 1995, it found nothing there.","answer":1,"explain":"That 'empty' patch came back with roughly 3,000 galaxies in it."},{"type":"tf","prompt":"The Pleiades star cluster actually has more than a thousand stars in it, even though most people only see six or seven.","answer":0,"explain":"Binoculars turn those six or seven visible stars into dozens — the full cluster holds over a thousand."}]},{"id":"astro-looking","course":"astro","kind":"facts","section":"looking","title":"How We Look","glyph":"🔭","n":14,"idea":"Your eyes are your best telescope, but using them right at night takes care. Discover how telescopes collect light and the surprising fix for Hubble's blurry vision.","pages":[{"icon":"👀","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Your eyes are the best telescope you already own, but you have to treat them right."},{"t":"fact","text":"It takes half an hour for your eyes to switch over to night mode, and just one glance at a phone undoes all that. The sensors in your eyes rely on a special pigment that takes time to rebuild, and bright light bleaches it out in a second.","label":"Night mode"},{"t":"p","text":"Red light barely touches this pigment, so observers use red flashlights. This way they can see what they need to without ruining their night vision."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why do observers use red flashlights?","options":["The color red helps the faint stars look brighter and clearer","Red light keeps telescope mirrors from getting too hot","Red light barely disturbs the pigment that works in the dark"],"answer":2,"hint":"Bright light bleaches out the pigment, but red light barely touches it.","explain":"Red light is gentle enough to let you see without disturbing your night vision."}}]},{"icon":"🔭","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The best first instrument for looking at the night sky isn't a telescope. It's a pair of 10x50 binoculars."},{"t":"fact","text":"In 10x50, the first number is ten times magnification and the second is the width of the front lenses in millimeters, which is how much light gets in."},{"t":"p","text":"Astronomers often recommend 7x50 or 10x50 binoculars because anything above about 10x is hard to hold steady."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does the second number in 10x50 tell you?","options":["The width of the front lenses in millimeters, how much light gets in","The power of the lenses, how many times they enlarge things","The weight of the whole binoculars in ounces, how heavy they are"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It explains what each number means.","explain":"The second number is the width of the front lenses in millimeters. This tells you how much light gets in, which is really important for seeing well in the dark."}}]},{"icon":"🪣","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A telescope's real job is not about making things look bigger. It is about collecting more light."},{"t":"fact","text":"A 6-inch mirror gathers around 500 times as much light as your eye can on its own. That is why it shows you things your eye cannot see at all."},{"t":"p","text":"Magnification is a separate setting. You change it by swapping the eyepiece. But too much magnification just gives you a big, dim, mushy blob."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is a telescope's real job?","options":["Making things look bigger","Collecting light","Changing colors"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the first number on the page. It shows how much more light a 6-inch mirror can collect.","explain":"A telescope collects light, which is why it can show you things your eye cannot see at all."}}]},{"icon":"🗜️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Galileo's telescope was not fancy by today's standards. It magnified about twenty times and showed a tiny bit of sky at a time."},{"t":"fact","text":"Yet with that, he found four points of light beside Jupiter that moved each night, mountains on the moon, and phases of Venus."},{"t":"p","text":"He called those four points of light the Medicean Stars, but everyone else called them the Galilean moons. You can see them yourself with binoculars."},{"t":"try","text":"Brace a pair of binoculars against a fence or door frame and look at Jupiter. Three or four tiny dots beside it are the Galilean moons. Look again another night and you'll see they have shuffled positions."}]},{"icon":"🎹","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"William Herschel was a musician who made his own telescopes. On 13 March 1781, he was scanning the sky from his garden in Bath."},{"t":"fact","text":"He found a small disk that was not a star. He thought it was a comet at first, but it turned out to be a planet, Uranus, the first ever found with a telescope."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did Herschel find?","options":["A comet, the first one seen through a telescope","A planet, the first found with a telescope","A new star, the closest one beyond the sun"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It tells what he thought he saw at first.","explain":"He spotted a small disk and thought it was a comet, but it turned out to be a new planet."}}]},{"icon":"🪞","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"In 1948, the 200-inch Hale telescope opened on Palomar Mountain. Its mirror is a single disk of Pyrex, over 16 feet across and weighing 14.5 tons."},{"t":"fact","text":"The Hale stayed the most capable telescope on Earth for 45 years. Each Keck telescope in Hawaii now uses 36 hexagons to make its mirror, along with lasers to cancel out the blur of the air."},{"t":"p","text":"Here is the trick. A laser fired into the sky lights up sodium atoms about 56 miles up, creating an artificial star."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many hexagons make up a Keck mirror?","options":["16","36","45"],"answer":1,"hint":"Each Keck telescope uses 36 hexagons.","explain":"Each Keck mirror is made of 36 hexagonal pieces."}}]},{"icon":"🛰️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Hubble launched on 24 April 1990, but there was a problem. Its mirror was ground the wrong shape."},{"t":"fact","text":"The edge of Hubble's mirror was too flat by about two microns, which is a fiftieth the thickness of a hair. Every picture came back blurry."},{"t":"p","text":"In December 1993, astronauts flew up and fixed it. They fitted corrective optics to Hubble, like glasses."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How was Hubble fixed?","options":["Astronauts fitted corrective optics, like glasses","A new mirror was installed","The telescope was brought back to Earth for repairs"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the last paragraph. It says what the astronauts fitted, like a pair of glasses.","explain":"Astronauts flew up and fitted corrective optics, like glasses, to fix Hubble's blurry vision."}}]},{"icon":"🌟","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Imagine a telescope so big that it has to fold up for its journey into space."},{"t":"fact","text":"Webb's mirror is 21 feet 4 inches across and made of 18 gold-covered hexagons. It sits about a million miles from Earth behind a five-layer sunshield the size of a tennis court, looking in infrared."},{"t":"term","word":"infrared","text":"Heat you cannot see. Webb has to be colder than anything it looks at, so its sunshield holds about 570 degrees F between its hot side and its cold side."},{"t":"p","text":"Every unfolding step had to work perfectly once it was launched. And they did."}]},{"icon":"📡","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Some telescopes have no lens and no eyepiece because they catch radio waves instead of light."},{"t":"fact","text":"The Green Bank dish in West Virginia is 328 feet (100 m) across, covers over two acres, and steers to most of the sky. What comes out is a measurement, not a picture."},{"t":"p","text":"The Arecibo dish in Puerto Rico was once the largest single dish for over fifty years. But its support cables gave way, and the platform crashed into it on December 1, 2020."},{"t":"p","text":"Now China's FAST is the biggest, at 1,640 feet. It can only aim about 1,000 feet (300 m) of itself at a time."}]},{"icon":"🏔️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Big telescopes end up in awkward places because they need still, dry, dark air above them."},{"t":"fact","text":"Mauna Kea in Hawaii sits at 13,800 feet, high enough to make people ill. The Atacama in Chile is one of the driest deserts on Earth. Both give sharper pictures."},{"t":"p","text":"Water vapor in the air soaks up infrared, so dry sites see colors wet ones cannot. Height gets you above part of the atmosphere and most of the turbulence. Nobody builds a major observatory somewhere convenient."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why do big telescopes sit in awkward places?","options":["They are cheaper to build on hills","They get free power from the wind","They need still, dry, dark air"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the first fact on the page about Mauna Kea and the Atacama.","explain":"Big telescopes need still, dry, and dark air for the best views."}}]},{"icon":"📜","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"So how do we look at the sky? Every instrument is an extension of one simple activity."},{"t":"fact","text":"In Genesis 1:14, the lights in the sky are \"for signs and for seasons, and for days and years.\"","label":"The written account says"},{"t":"p","text":"Think of Galileo's little tube or the giant Webb telescope. Each one is just a tool to help us do what we have always done."},{"t":"p","text":"The oldest working instrument is still the naked eye. It is not retired. Bessel’s parallax, Goodricke's timing of Algol, and the Chinese record of the guest star in 1054 all came from watching. So did every constellation ever named."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Red flashlight","Protects night vision"],["6-inch mirror","Collects 500 times more light"],["William Herschel","The musician who found Uranus"],["Keck telescope","Uses 36 hexagons"],["Hubble's fix","Corrective optics like glasses"],["Webb telescope","Looks in infrared"]]},"picgame":{"type":"tapall","title":"Sky Tools","goal":"Tap what helps us look at the sky.","items":[{"icon":"👀","name":"eyes","good":true},{"icon":"🔭","name":"telescope","good":true},{"icon":"📡","name":"radio dish","good":true},{"icon":"🛰️","name":"space telescope","good":true},{"icon":"🔦","name":"red flashlight","good":true},{"icon":"🧸","name":"teddy bear","good":false},{"icon":"🍎","name":"apple","good":false},{"icon":"🚲","name":"bicycle","good":false}]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is a telescope's real job — the thing that actually makes it powerful?","options":["Collecting far more light than your eye can on its own","Magnifying objects to look bigger","Removing the atmosphere's blur completely"],"answer":0,"explain":"A 6-inch mirror gathers around 500 times as much light as a dark-adapted pupil — magnification is a separate, much less important setting."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why are major observatories built in miserable, remote places like mountaintops and deserts?","options":["They need still, dry, dark air, which convenient places don't have","It's cheaper to build there","Telescopes work better at high altitude regardless of air quality"],"answer":0,"explain":"Water vapor soaks up infrared light, and height gets a telescope above most of the atmosphere's turbulence."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How do the Keck telescopes' mirrors correct for the blurring caused by Earth's atmosphere?","options":["A flexible mirror bends hundreds of times a second to cancel out the blur","They're placed in outer space, away from the atmosphere","A computer edits the blur out of photos afterward"],"answer":0,"explain":"They fire a laser to create an artificial star, watch how it wobbles, and bend the mirror to cancel the wobble out."},{"type":"tf","prompt":"When Hubble launched in 1990, its mirror had been ground to the wrong shape, making every picture come back blurry.","answer":0,"explain":"The edge was off by about four microns — a fiftieth the thickness of a hair — fixed by astronauts in 1993."},{"type":"tf","prompt":"The very first thing William Herschel reported after discovering Uranus in 1781 was that he'd found a new planet.","answer":1,"explain":"He first reported it as a comet — it turned out to be the first planet ever found with a telescope."}]},{"id":"astro-astronauts","course":"astro","kind":"facts","section":"astronauts","title":"Astronauts","glyph":"👨‍🚀","n":6,"idea":"A house in space as big as a football field, and the first person ever to travel into space. Meet the astronauts.","pages":[{"icon":"👨‍🚀","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The International Space Station is as big as a football field."},{"t":"fact","text":"It is 358 ft (109 m) by 239 ft (73 m). Inside, it has about as much room as a Boeing 747 airliner."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How big is the space station?","options":["A tennis court","A football field","A basketball hoop"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. It says how big the station is.","explain":"The International Space Station is as big as a football field."}}]},{"icon":"💨","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The space station circles the Earth about every 93 minutes."},{"t":"fact","text":"It moves at 17,200 mph (27,600 km/h). That is 15.5 trips around the planet every day."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How often does the space station circle the Earth?","options":["Once each day","Every 93 minutes","Once each week"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. It says how often.","explain":"The space station circles the Earth about every 93 minutes."}}]},{"icon":"🏆","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"People have lived on the International Space Station without a break since November 2, 2000."},{"t":"fact","text":"It is the longest continuous human presence in space. Visitors from 26 countries, 290 people in all, have come aboard."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many countries have sent visitors to the space station?","options":["6","26","260"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. It gives the number of countries.","explain":"Visitors from 26 countries have come aboard."}}]},{"icon":"📅","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"On April 12, 1961, Yuri Gagarin became the first person to travel into space."},{"t":"fact","text":"He circled the Earth one time, and the flight took 108 minutes."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How long was Gagarin's flight?","options":["10 minutes","108 minutes","8 hours"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. It tells how long the flight took.","explain":"Gagarin's flight took 108 minutes."}}]},{"icon":"🧭","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"In March 1966, Neil Armstrong flew Gemini 8 with David Scott."},{"t":"term","word":"docking","text":"Joining two spacecraft together in space."},{"t":"fact","text":"They made the first docking of two spacecraft in space. A stuck thruster then sent them into a dangerous roll, so the mission ended early."}]},{"icon":"🥇","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"In the United States, a person earns astronaut wings by flying above 50 miles (80 km)."},{"t":"fact","text":"The international rule is higher: the Karman line, at 62 miles (100 km)."}]},{"icon":"🔎","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A big house in space. A first flight around the Earth. A docking of two spacecraft. Wings for flying high."},{"t":"p","text":"Astronauts fly higher than 50 miles."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Space station","As big as a football field"],["Yuri Gagarin","First person to travel into space"],["Gemini 8","Made the first docking of two spacecraft"],["Karman line","The international rule for the edge of space"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"Who was the first person to travel into space?","options":["Neil Armstrong","Yuri Gagarin","David Scott"],"answer":1,"explain":"Yuri Gagarin became the first person to travel into space on April 12, 1961."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many trips around the Earth does the space station make every day?","options":["1.5","15.5","155"],"answer":1,"explain":"The space station makes 15.5 trips around the planet every day."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How high is the Karman line?","options":["6 miles","62 miles","620 miles"],"answer":1,"explain":"The Karman line is at 62 miles (100 km)."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did Armstrong and Scott make on Gemini 8?","options":["The first space walk","The first docking","The first moon landing"],"answer":1,"explain":"They made the first docking of two spacecraft in space."}]},{"id":"astro-jupiter","course":"astro","kind":"facts","section":"jupiter","title":"Jupiter","glyph":"🟤","n":6,"idea":"Jupiter has nearly 2.5 times the mass of all the other planets put together. Meet its giant storm and its four big moons.","pages":[{"icon":"🟤","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Jupiter is 11 times as wide as Earth."},{"t":"fact","text":"It has nearly 2.5 times the mass of all the other planets put together."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many times wider than Earth is Jupiter?","options":["2 times","11 times","100 times"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. It gives the number.","explain":"Jupiter is 11 times as wide as Earth."}}]},{"icon":"🌀","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The Great Red Spot is a giant storm on Jupiter."},{"t":"fact","text":"Its winds blow up to 308 mph (496 km/h). People first saw it in September 1831."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How fast do the winds blow in the Great Red Spot?","options":["30 mph","308 mph","3,080 mph"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. It gives the speed of the wind.","explain":"The winds in the Great Red Spot blow up to 308 mph (496 km/h)."}}]},{"icon":"☀️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The Great Red Spot is red-orange because of a kind of sunburn."},{"t":"fact","text":"Sunlight blasts chemicals high up in Jupiter's clouds."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What makes the Great Red Spot red-orange?","options":["Red sand blowing around","A kind of sunburn","Hot red lava"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. It says what makes the color.","explain":"The Great Red Spot is red-orange because of a kind of sunburn."}}]},{"icon":"🧪","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Jupiter's air is 76% hydrogen and 24% helium."},{"t":"term","word":"helium","text":"A gas that makes up 24% of Jupiter's air."},{"t":"fact","text":"It also has a little ammonia, water vapor and other gases."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How much of Jupiter's air is hydrogen?","options":["7%","76%","24%"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. It gives the share of hydrogen.","explain":"Jupiter's air is 76% hydrogen."}}]},{"icon":"🌙","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Jupiter has four big moons."},{"t":"fact","text":"Their names are Ganymede, Callisto, Io and Europa. You can see them with binoculars. Astronomers found them in 1610."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"In what year did astronomers find Jupiter's four big moons?","options":["1010","1610","1910"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. It gives the year.","explain":"Astronomers found the four big moons in 1610."}}]},{"icon":"📅","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"One year on Jupiter is 11.86 Earth years long."},{"t":"fact","text":"That is how long Jupiter takes to go once around the Sun."}]},{"icon":"🔎","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A giant planet. A giant storm. A red-orange glow. Gas and four big moons."},{"t":"p","text":"Jupiter is big."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Great Red Spot","Giant storm on Jupiter"],["Hydrogen","Most of Jupiter's air"],["Ganymede","One of the four big moons"],["Binoculars","Show you the four big moons"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is the Great Red Spot?","options":["A moon of Jupiter","A giant storm on Jupiter","A mountain on Jupiter"],"answer":1,"explain":"The Great Red Spot is a giant storm on Jupiter."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which gas is 76% of Jupiter's air?","options":["Helium","Hydrogen","Ammonia"],"answer":1,"explain":"Jupiter's air is 76% hydrogen and 24% helium."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which is one of Jupiter's four big moons?","options":["Saturn","Europa","Mars"],"answer":1,"explain":"Their names are Ganymede, Callisto, Io and Europa."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How long is one year on Jupiter?","options":["1.86 Earth years","11.86 Earth years","118.6 Earth years"],"answer":1,"explain":"One year on Jupiter is 11.86 Earth years long."}]},{"id":"astro-mars","course":"astro","kind":"facts","section":"mars","title":"Mars","glyph":"🔴","n":6,"idea":"Mars is called the Red Planet because it looks orange-red. It is a desert-like world with a giant volcano and a very long canyon.","pages":[{"icon":"🔴","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Mars is called the Red Planet because it looks orange-red."},{"t":"fact","text":"It is a rocky, desert-like world with very thin air, mostly carbon dioxide."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why is Mars called the Red Planet?","options":["It is covered with red water","It looks orange-red","It is the closest planet to the Sun"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. It says why.","explain":"Mars is called the Red Planet because it looks orange-red."}}]},{"icon":"📏","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Mars is about half as wide as Earth. It is 4,212 mi (6,779 km) across."},{"t":"fact","text":"Its gravity is only about a third of Earth's."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How strong is the gravity on Mars compared with Earth's?","options":["Twice as strong","A third","The same"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. It tells how strong the gravity is.","explain":"The gravity on Mars is only about a third of Earth's."}}]},{"icon":"🌡️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The air on Mars can be as cold as -243°F (-153°C) and as warm as 68°F (20°C)."},{"t":"fact","text":"The air is very thin, only a few thousandths as heavy as Earth's."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How thick is the air on Mars compared with Earth's?","options":["About half as thick","A few thousandths","Twice as thick"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. It gives the exact comparison.","explain":"The air on Mars is only a few thousandths as heavy as Earth's."}}]},{"icon":"🌋","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Olympus Mons is a giant volcano on Mars."},{"t":"fact","text":"It is 69,840 ft (21.287 km) high, about 2.5 times as tall as Mount Everest."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is the tallest volcano on Mars called?","options":["Mount Everest","Olympus Mons","Valles Marineris"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. It names the volcano.","explain":"Olympus Mons is a giant volcano on Mars."}}]},{"icon":"🏜️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Mars has a canyon called Valles Marineris."},{"t":"term","word":"canyon","text":"A very long place in the ground, like Valles Marineris."},{"t":"fact","text":"It is 2,500 mi (4,000 km) long. It is one of the largest canyons in the Solar System."}]},{"icon":"🌙","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Phobos is a tiny moon of Mars."},{"t":"fact","text":"It races around Mars in just 7 hours and 39 minutes. From Mars, it rises in the west and sets in the east, twice every Martian day."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where does Phobos set?","options":["In the west","In the east","It never sets"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. It says where Phobos sets.","explain":"From Mars, Phobos rises in the west and sets in the east."}}]},{"icon":"🔎","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A red planet. Thin air. A giant volcano. A long canyon. A tiny moon that races."},{"t":"p","text":"Mars is a world with a lot to see."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Red Planet","Mars looks orange-red"],["Olympus Mons","Giant volcano on Mars"],["Valles Marineris","Very long canyon on Mars"],["Phobos","Tiny moon of Mars"],["Thin air","Only a few thousandths as heavy as Earth's"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"How wide is Mars compared with Earth?","options":["Twice as wide","About half as wide","The same width"],"answer":1,"explain":"Mars is about half as wide as Earth."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is Olympus Mons?","options":["A moon of Mars","A giant volcano on Mars","A canyon on Mars"],"answer":1,"explain":"Olympus Mons is a giant volcano on Mars."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How long is Valles Marineris?","options":["25 miles","2,500 miles","250,000 miles"],"answer":1,"explain":"Valles Marineris is 2,500 mi (4,000 km) long."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How long does Phobos take to go around Mars?","options":["7 days","7 hours and 39 minutes","39 hours"],"answer":1,"explain":"Phobos races around Mars in just 7 hours and 39 minutes."}]},{"id":"astro-saturn","course":"astro","kind":"facts","section":"saturn","title":"Saturn & Its Rings","glyph":"🪐","n":6,"idea":"Saturn is almost as big as Jupiter, but it has only an eighth of Earth's density. Meet its rings and its giant moon Titan.","pages":[{"icon":"🪐","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Saturn has only an eighth of Earth's density."},{"t":"fact","text":"But it is over 95 times more massive than Earth. It is almost as big as Jupiter, with less than a third of Jupiter's mass."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How much of Earth's density does Saturn have?","options":["Twice as much","An eighth","The same"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. It gives the share.","explain":"Saturn has only an eighth of Earth's density."}}]},{"icon":"🌬️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The wind blows hard on Saturn."},{"t":"fact","text":"Winds on Saturn can blow as fast as 1,100 mph (1,800 km/h)."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How fast can the winds on Saturn blow?","options":["11 mph","1,100 mph","110,000 mph"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. It gives the speed.","explain":"Winds on Saturn can blow as fast as 1,100 mph (1,800 km/h)."}}]},{"icon":"💍","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Saturn has a system of rings."},{"t":"term","word":"rings","text":"Flat bands of ice and dust that circle Saturn."},{"t":"fact","text":"Its rings are made mostly of ice, with some rocky bits and dust. At least 293 moons orbit Saturn, and 63 of them have official names."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What are Saturn's rings made of?","options":["Gold","Ice","Sand"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. It says what the rings are made of.","explain":"The rings are made mostly of ice, with some rocky bits and dust."}}]},{"icon":"🔭","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"In 1610, Galileo became the first person to see Saturn's rings."},{"t":"fact","text":"But he could not see them well enough to tell what they were. In 1655, Christiaan Huygens was the first to describe them as a disk around Saturn."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Who first described the rings as a disk?","options":["Galileo Galilei","Christiaan Huygens","Nobody ever did"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. It names the person.","explain":"In 1655, Christiaan Huygens was the first to describe the rings as a disk."}}]},{"icon":"🌫️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Titan is Saturn's largest moon."},{"t":"fact","text":"It is bigger than the planet Mercury. It is the only moon in the Solar System with a thick atmosphere."}]},{"icon":"⏳","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A year on Saturn is 29.45 Earth years long."},{"t":"fact","text":"That is how long Saturn takes to go once around the Sun."}]},{"icon":"🔎","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A planet that is light for its size. Fast winds. Rings of ice. A big moon with thick air."},{"t":"p","text":"Saturn is big."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Rings","Made mostly of ice"],["Galileo","First person to see the rings"],["Christiaan Huygens","First to describe the rings as a disk"],["Titan","Bigger than the planet Mercury"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many times more massive than Earth is Saturn?","options":["9 times","Over 95 times","950 times"],"answer":1,"explain":"Saturn is over 95 times more massive than Earth."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What are the rings mostly made of?","options":["Gas","Ice","Rock"],"answer":1,"explain":"The rings are made mostly of ice."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which is Saturn's largest moon?","options":["Mercury","Titan","Jupiter"],"answer":1,"explain":"Titan is Saturn's largest moon."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How long is one year on Saturn?","options":["2.945 Earth years","29.45 Earth years","294.5 Earth years"],"answer":1,"explain":"A year on Saturn is 29.45 Earth years long."}]},{"id":"astro-venus","course":"astro","kind":"facts","section":"venus","title":"Venus & Mercury","glyph":"🌕","n":6,"idea":"Venus is the hottest planet, even though Mercury is closer to the Sun. Meet two small planets close to the Sun.","pages":[{"icon":"🔥","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Venus is the hottest planet."},{"t":"term","word":"atmosphere","text":"The air around a planet."},{"t":"fact","text":"The atmosphere at its surface is 867°F (464°C), and the pressure is 92 times greater than Earth's."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which planet is the hottest?","options":["Mercury","Venus","Earth"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. It names the planet.","explain":"Venus is the hottest planet."}}]},{"icon":"⏳","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A day on Venus lasts 116.75 Earth days."},{"t":"fact","text":"That is about half of a Venus year, which is 224.7 Earth days long."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How long is a day on Venus?","options":["11.675 Earth days","116.75 Earth days","1,167 Earth days"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. It gives the length of a day.","explain":"A day on Venus lasts 116.75 Earth days."}}]},{"icon":"🙃","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Venus has no moons."},{"t":"fact","text":"It also spins very slowly, and backward."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many moons does Venus have?","options":["Two moons","No moons","Many moons"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. It says how many.","explain":"Venus has no moons."}}]},{"icon":"⭐","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"People call Venus the morning star or the evening star."},{"t":"fact","text":"Venus shines brighter than any other point of light in the sky."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What do people call Venus in the morning?","options":["A very big moon","The morning star","A tiny red sun"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. It gives the name.","explain":"People call Venus the morning star or the evening star."}}]},{"icon":"📏","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Mercury is the smallest planet in the Solar System."},{"t":"fact","text":"It is 3,030 mi (4,880 km) across, and its biggest crater is about a third as wide as the whole planet."}]},{"icon":"🥶","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Mercury is the closest planet to the Sun, but Venus is hotter."},{"t":"fact","text":"Mercury has no air to hold heat, so near its equator it goes from -270°F at night to 790°F in sunlight (-170°C to 420°C)."}]},{"icon":"🔎","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A very hot planet. A long day. No moons. A bright star. A small planet."},{"t":"p","text":"Venus is hotter than Mercury."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Venus","The hottest planet"],["Mercury","The smallest planet"],["Morning star","What people call Venus"],["Atmosphere","The air around a planet"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which planet is the smallest in the Solar System?","options":["Venus","Mercury","Earth"],"answer":1,"explain":"Mercury is the smallest planet in the Solar System."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many moons does Venus have?","options":["One","None","Two"],"answer":1,"explain":"Venus has no moons."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why is Mercury cooler than Venus?","options":["It has no sunlight","It has no air to hold heat","It spins backward"],"answer":1,"explain":"Mercury has no air to hold heat."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How long is a Venus year?","options":["22.47 Earth days","224.7 Earth days","2,247 Earth days"],"answer":1,"explain":"A Venus year is 224.7 Earth days long."}]},{"id":"astro-eclipses","course":"astro","kind":"facts","section":"eclipses","title":"Eclipses","glyph":"🌑","n":6,"idea":"A total solar eclipse can last over 7 minutes, but in any one place you would wait 360 to 410 years to see one. Find out about solar and lunar eclipses.","pages":[{"icon":"🌑","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A total solar eclipse happens somewhere on Earth about every 18 months."},{"t":"fact","text":"But in any one place, you would wait 360 to 410 years to see one."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"About how often does a total solar eclipse happen somewhere on Earth?","options":["Several times a year","Every 18 months","Once each decade"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. It says how often.","explain":"A total solar eclipse happens somewhere on Earth about every 18 months."}}]},{"icon":"🤔","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A solar eclipse can only be seen from a small part of the world."},{"t":"fact","text":"That is why most people never see one."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How much of the world can see a solar eclipse?","options":["All of it","A small part","Half of it"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. It says how much.","explain":"A solar eclipse can only be seen from a small part of the world."}}]},{"icon":"📏","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A total solar eclipse can last over 7 minutes."},{"t":"term","word":"track","text":"The path the Moon's shadow makes on Earth."},{"t":"fact","text":"The Moon's shadow track on Earth can be up to 166 mi (267 km) wide."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How long can a total solar eclipse last?","options":["Only 7 seconds","Over 7 minutes","Nearly 7 hours"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. It says how long.","explain":"A total solar eclipse can last over 7 minutes."}}]},{"icon":"🔴","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"During a lunar eclipse, the Moon looks orange or red."},{"t":"fact","text":"The sunlight reaching it first passes through Earth's air, which removes blue and violet light."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What color does the Moon turn in a lunar eclipse?","options":["Bright blue","Orange or red","Pure white"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. It names the color.","explain":"During a lunar eclipse, the Moon looks orange or red."}}]},{"icon":"👀","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"You can watch a lunar eclipse without eye protection."},{"t":"fact","text":"It can be seen from anywhere on the night side of Earth, and it often lasts an hour or longer."}]},{"icon":"💨","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The Moon moves about 2,300 mph (1.03 km/s) around Earth."},{"t":"fact","text":"That is why a total lunar eclipse can last up to nearly 107 minutes."}]},{"icon":"🔎","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A dark day. A small part of the world. A red Moon. An hour or longer."},{"t":"p","text":"An eclipse is a big show."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Solar eclipse","Seen from only a small part of the world"],["Lunar eclipse","The Moon looks orange or red"],["Totality","Can last over 7 minutes"],["Shadow track","Up to 166 mi (267 km) wide"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What color does the Moon look in a lunar eclipse?","options":["Green","Orange or red","Black"],"answer":1,"explain":"During a lunar eclipse, the Moon looks orange or red."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How long can a total lunar eclipse last?","options":["10 minutes","Up to 107 minutes","7 minutes"],"answer":1,"explain":"A total lunar eclipse can last up to nearly 107 minutes."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How wide can the Moon's shadow track be?","options":["16 mi","166 mi","1,660 mi"],"answer":1,"explain":"The Moon's shadow track can be up to 166 mi (267 km) wide."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How long would you wait in one place to see a total solar eclipse?","options":["3 to 4 years","360 to 410 years","36 to 41 years"],"answer":1,"explain":"In any one place, you would wait 360 to 410 years."}]},{"id":"astro-rockets","course":"astro","kind":"facts","section":"rockets","title":"Rockets & Spacecraft","glyph":"🚀","n":6,"idea":"From gunpowder rockets in China to a spacecraft that has entered interstellar space. See how rockets took people to the Moon.","pages":[{"icon":"🚀","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Rockets powered by gunpowder were used in China by the 13th century, under the Song dynasty."},{"t":"fact","text":"The idea then spread to the Middle East and to Europe."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where were gunpowder rockets first used?","options":["Europe","China","Florida"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. It names the country.","explain":"Rockets powered by gunpowder were used in China by the 13th century."}}]},{"icon":"🌕","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Apollo 11 was the spaceflight that first landed people on the Moon."},{"t":"fact","text":"It lifted off on a Saturn V rocket from Florida on July 16, 1969, and returned on July 24."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What rocket carried Apollo 11 into space?","options":["A Space Shuttle","A Saturn V","A gunpowder rocket"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. It names the rocket.","explain":"Apollo 11 lifted off on a Saturn V rocket."}}]},{"icon":"🧩","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Only the command module, which held the three astronauts, came back to Earth."},{"t":"fact","text":"The Apollo spacecraft had three parts."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which part of the Apollo spacecraft came back to Earth?","options":["The whole rocket","The command module","The flag"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. It names the part.","explain":"Only the command module came back to Earth."}}]},{"icon":"👨‍🚀","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Neil Armstrong and Buzz Aldrin spent about two and a half hours walking on the Moon."},{"t":"fact","text":"They planted a flag, set up science instruments, and collected 47.5 lb (21.5 kg) of Moon material."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How long did Armstrong and Aldrin walk on the Moon?","options":["A few short minutes","Two and a half hours","Almost two whole days"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. It tells how long.","explain":"They spent about two and a half hours walking on the Moon."}}]},{"icon":"🛸","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Voyager 1 is the most distant human-made object."},{"t":"term","word":"interstellar","text":"The kind of space that Voyager 1 entered in 2012."},{"t":"fact","text":"In 2026 it was 15.9 billion mi (25.6 billion km) from Earth. On August 25, 2012, it became the first spacecraft to enter interstellar space."}]},{"icon":"🛰️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The Space Shuttle program built five orbiters and flew them on 135 missions from 1981 to 2011."},{"t":"fact","text":"They launched satellites, space probes and the Hubble Space Telescope."}]},{"icon":"🔎","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A gunpowder rocket. A trip to the Moon. A far spacecraft. Shuttles with a telescope."},{"t":"p","text":"Rockets have come a long way."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Gunpowder","Powered the first rockets"],["Saturn V","Carried Apollo 11 to the Moon"],["Command module","The part that came back to Earth"],["Voyager 1","First to enter interstellar space"],["Space Shuttle","Flew 135 missions"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which spacecraft was the first to enter interstellar space?","options":["Apollo 11","Voyager 1","The Space Shuttle"],"answer":1,"explain":"Voyager 1 became the first spacecraft to enter interstellar space."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many missions did the Space Shuttles fly?","options":["13","135","1,350"],"answer":1,"explain":"The Space Shuttle program flew 135 missions from 1981 to 2011."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did Armstrong and Aldrin plant on the Moon?","options":["A tent","A flag","A tree"],"answer":1,"explain":"They planted a flag on the Moon."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What powered the first rockets in China?","options":["Steam","Gunpowder","Wind"],"answer":1,"explain":"Rockets powered by gunpowder were used in China by the 13th century."}]},{"id":"physical-forces","course":"physical","kind":"facts","section":"forces","title":"Forces & Motion","glyph":"🏃","n":6,"idea":"Gravity makes a hammer and feather fall together on the moon, but not on Earth. Learn how simple machines like levers help us lift heavy things.","pages":[{"icon":"🔨","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"On the last Apollo 15 moonwalk, David Scott held out a geology hammer and a falcon feather."},{"t":"fact","text":"He let go of both, and with no air in the way they hit the dust together. The camera was running, so everybody could see it happen."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What happened when Scott let go of the hammer and the feather?","options":["The hammer hit first","They hit the dust together","The feather floated away"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It tells you what happened with no air in the way.","explain":"With no air resistance, both the hammer and the feather hit the ground at the same time."}}]},{"icon":"🪶","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"On Earth, a feather falls slower than a hammer because of air."},{"t":"fact","text":"Air pushes back harder the faster you fall and the more spread out you are. When the push equals your weight, you stop speeding up. A skydiver levels off at about 120 mph."},{"t":"try","text":"Drop two identical sheets of paper, one flat and one screwed into a ball. Same paper, same weight, but they will fall very differently."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why does a feather fall slower than a hammer on Earth?","options":["The air pushes back on it","Gravity speeds up a hammer more than a feather","The feather has no weight at all"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It explains how air affects falling objects.","explain":"Air resistance slows down the feather because it pushes back harder, especially on something spread out like a feather."}}]},{"icon":"🛷","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A moving thing keeps moving in a straight line unless something pushes it."},{"t":"fact","text":"On Earth, friction and air are always pushing back. But on ice or in space, you can see it plainly."},{"t":"try","text":"Slide a book across a smooth floor and then across a rug. Notice how the rug makes it stop faster."},{"t":"try","text":"Put a card on a glass and a coin on the card. Flick the card sideways hard. The coin does not come with it — it drops straight into the glass."}]},{"icon":"🔥","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Friction stops everything by turning movement into heat."},{"t":"fact","text":"Brake discs glow hot, and rubbing your hands warms them up. Rough surfaces make more friction, but so do some very smooth ones — two flat sheets of glass grip surprisingly hard."},{"t":"try","text":"Rub your hands together fast. You will feel the heat."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does friction turn movement into?","options":["Wind","Cold","Heat"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the first fact on the page to see what happens when you rub your hands.","explain":"Friction turns movement into heat, which is why rubbing your hands together warms them up."}}]},{"icon":"⚖️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A lever is a simple machine that trades distance for force."},{"t":"term","word":"lever","text":"A tool that lets you push or pull one end to move something else with more force at the other end."},{"t":"fact","text":"Push the far end of a long bar a long way, and the near end moves a short way but with much more force. Scissors, nutcrackers, wheelbarrows, and a seesaw are all levers."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does a lever trade?","options":["Weight for speed","Distance for force","Time for speed"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the first fact on the page to see how the ends of a lever move differently.","explain":"A lever moves one end a long way to get more force at the other end."}}]},{"icon":"🚀","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Every push comes with an equal push the other way."},{"t":"fact","text":"A rocket does not push against the air — it throws gas out of the back, and the gas pushes the rocket forward."},{"t":"p","text":"That is why rockets work better in space. With nothing in the way, the gas still pushes the rocket forward without any resistance."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What pushes a rocket forward?","options":["The pull of the moon","The air pushing against the rocket","The gas it throws out the back"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the first fact on the page to see what the rocket throws out.","explain":"The gas that comes out the back pushes the rocket forward."}}]},{"icon":"🌙","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A hammer and a feather landing together. A brake glowing hot. A lever lifting something heavy. A rocket leaving the ground."},{"t":"p","text":"Four different things, and every one of them follows the same few rules about pushes and motion."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Lever","Moves one end to get more force at the other"],["Rocket","Throws gas out the back to move forward"],["Friction","Turns movement into heat"],["Hammer and Feather","Fall together with no air resistance"],["Brake Discs","Glow hot from friction"],["Nutcrackers","A kind of lever"]]},"picgame":{"type":"sort","title":"Fall Race","goal":"Which falls fast? Which floats down?","bins":[{"icon":"⬇️","label":"falls fast"},{"icon":"🪂","label":"floats down"}],"items":[{"icon":"🔨","name":"hammer","bin":0},{"icon":"🪨","name":"rock","bin":0},{"icon":"⚾","name":"baseball","bin":0},{"icon":"🪶","name":"feather","bin":1},{"icon":"🍃","name":"leaf","bin":1},{"icon":"📄","name":"flat paper","bin":1}]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"On the moon, astronauts dropped a hammer and a feather together. What happened?","options":["They hit the ground at the same time","The hammer landed first, just like on Earth","The feather floated and never landed"],"answer":0,"explain":"With no air in the way, there's nothing to slow the feather down — Galileo argued this but could never prove it on Earth."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why does a rocket actually move forward in empty space, where there's nothing to push against?","options":["It throws gas out the back, and the gas pushes the rocket forward","It pushes against the air behind it","Gravity pulls it forward"],"answer":0,"explain":"Every push comes with an equal push the other way — rockets work better in space, where nothing is in the way."},{"type":"tf","prompt":"A moving object will keep moving forever in a straight line unless something pushes or pulls on it.","answer":0,"explain":"You rarely see this on Earth because friction and air resistance are always pushing back — it shows clearly on ice or in space."}]},{"id":"physical-energy","course":"physical","kind":"facts","section":"energy","title":"Energy","glyph":"⚡","n":6,"idea":"Energy changes form but is never lost. Find out how a rubber band warms up when stretched quickly.","pages":[{"icon":"🎢","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Why does a pendulum never swing back higher than where you let it go?"},{"t":"fact","text":"A pendulum trades height for speed on the way down and speed for height on the way up. Each swing it hands a little energy to the air, as a bit of heat."},{"t":"try","text":"Tie a small weight to a string, hold the string up, and let the weight swing. Watch whether it ever swings back higher than where you let it go."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why does a pendulum never swing higher than where you let it go?","options":["The string gets a bit shorter each time it swings","It hands a little energy to the air each swing","Gravity pulls a little less with each swing"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It explains what happens each time the pendulum swings.","explain":"Each swing, the pendulum hands a little energy to the air, so it never quite reaches as high."}}]},{"icon":"♾️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Imagine a ball rolling down a hill. It loses height but gains speed, and then when it rolls back up, it slows down again. Energy changes form but is never lost."},{"t":"fact","text":"Energy is never made or destroyed, only changed from one form to another. Nobody has ever seen an exception, even though people have looked very hard.","label":"Worked out"},{"t":"p","text":"It is the most tested idea in physics. Every careful measurement anyone has ever made has agreed with it."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why do we trust that energy is never destroyed?","options":["Every careful measurement has agreed with it","Great scientists long ago voted that it must be so","Nobody has ever tried to prove that it is false"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It explains why this idea holds up.","explain":"Every careful measurement anyone has ever made agrees with the idea that energy changes form but is never destroyed."}}]},{"icon":"🎈","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Take a rubber band and stretch it quickly. It gets warmer."},{"t":"fact","text":"Stretch a rubber band fast and you can feel it warm up on your lip. Let it snap back and it cools down again. The work your hands do stretching it ends up as a little heat."},{"t":"try","text":"Touch a rubber band to your lip, then stretch it fast and touch it again. Wait for it to relax and touch one more time."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What happens to a rubber band when you stretch it quickly?","options":["It stays the same temperature","It gets cooler","It gets warmer"],"answer":2,"hint":"Stretch the rubber band fast and touch it to your lip.","explain":"When you stretch a rubber band quickly, it gets warmer, and you can feel that warmth on your lip."}}]},{"icon":"🍎","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Have you ever wondered where the number on a cereal box comes from?"},{"t":"term","word":"Calorie","text":"The heat needed to warm 1 kg (2.2 pounds) of water by 1 degree Celsius (1.8 degrees F)."},{"t":"fact","text":"The numbers behind a cereal box label come from burning food in a sealed metal chamber sitting in water. They measure how much the water warms up."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How does the number on a cereal box get found?","options":["A scale weighs the food and the weight is turned into calories","The food is burned and the water's warming is measured","Testers taste the food and score how sweet it is"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It tells you what they burn and measure.","explain":"The food is burned in a sealed chamber, and the amount of heat it gives off warms the water. That warmth is measured to find the calorie count."}}]},{"icon":"🔋","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"An AA battery holds about as much energy as a single peanut."},{"t":"fact","text":"One gallon of gasoline holds about as much energy as a hundred sticks of dynamite.","label":"Worked out"},{"t":"p","text":"Chemical fuels are very concentrated. That is why batteries are the hard part of every electric machine."}]},{"icon":"🚲","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Imagine the most efficient way to move from one place to another. It turns out, a person on a bicycle is the best."},{"t":"fact","text":"A person walking uses about three times as much energy per pound carried per mile compared to cycling.","label":"Worked out"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which uses about three times as much energy as cycling?","options":["Walking","Sitting still","Swimming"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It tells you how much more walking uses.","explain":"Walking uses about three times as much energy as cycling."}}]},{"icon":"⚡","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Energy swings, stretches, burns and rolls. Through all of it, nobody has ever seen any of it lost."},{"t":"p","text":"So when you pedal or walk, the energy you use does not vanish. It changes into something else."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Pendulum","Trades height for speed"],["Cereal box number","Burned food's warmth measured"],["AA battery","About as much energy as a single peanut"],["Cycling","More efficient than walking"]]},"picgame":{"type":"tapall","title":"Power Up","goal":"Tap what gives power to people or machines.","items":[{"icon":"⛽","name":"gasoline","good":true},{"icon":"🔋","name":"battery","good":true},{"icon":"🥜","name":"peanut","good":true},{"icon":"🥣","name":"cereal","good":true},{"icon":"🧊","name":"ice cube","good":false},{"icon":"🪨","name":"rock","good":false},{"icon":"🔩","name":"bolt","good":false},{"icon":"🧸","name":"teddy bear","good":false}]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"How does the number on a cereal box's \"Calories\" label actually get measured?","options":["The food is burned in a sealed chamber, and scientists measure how much the surrounding water heats up","It's estimated by comparing the food to similar foods","A machine scans the food's chemical ingredients"],"answer":0,"explain":"The device is called a bomb calorimeter — it's exactly as dramatic as it sounds."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What makes a bicycle the most energy-efficient way for a person to travel, compared to walking?","options":["Almost no energy is wasted holding you up","Bicycles use electricity instead of muscle power","Walking requires more oxygen overall"],"answer":0,"explain":"Measured by energy used per pound carried per mile, walking uses about three times as much as cycling."},{"type":"tf","prompt":"Scientists have found cases where energy actually appears out of nowhere or vanishes completely.","answer":1,"explain":"Nobody has ever seen an exception — it's the most tested idea in physics, even though it can't be proven for every event in the universe."}]},{"id":"physical-light","course":"physical","kind":"facts","section":"light","title":"Light & Color","glyph":"🌈","n":6,"idea":"Light travels from the sun to Earth in about 8 minutes. Discover how it bends, scatters, and reflects along the way.","pages":[{"icon":"☀️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Have you ever thought about how long it takes for sunlight to reach us? The sun you see right now isn't exactly what’s happening there right now."},{"t":"fact","text":"Light travels 186,282 miles every second. Sunlight takes 8 minutes and 20 seconds to reach you, so the sun you see is always the sun of eight minutes ago."},{"t":"p","text":"The speed of light was first figured out by watching Jupiter's moons. Now, scientists use lasers to bounce light back and forth and measure it that way."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How old is the sun you see right now?","options":["About 8 seconds old","About 8 minutes old","About 8 hours old"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It says how long sunlight takes to reach you.","explain":"The sunlight you see took about 8 minutes and 20 seconds to reach you, so it shows you the sun as it was eight minutes ago."}}]},{"icon":"🌈","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"White light is actually every color mixed together. You can see all the colors when you use something called a prism."},{"t":"term","word":"prism","text":"A piece of glass or clear material that spreads light into its colors."},{"t":"fact","text":"A prism spreads white light because each color bends a little differently. Newton put a second prism in the way and put the colors back into white light again."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did Newton's second prism do?","options":["Put the colors back into white light","Turned the colors into shades of gray","Split each color into many smaller colors"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the fact box to see what the second prism did.","explain":"The second prism mixed all the colors back together to make white light again."}}]},{"icon":"🌅","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The sky is blue because air scatters short waves, like blue, much more strongly than long ones, like red."},{"t":"fact","text":"Air bounces blue light all over while red light comes straight through. At sunset, the light travels through far more air, so the blue gets scattered away and you see red.","label":"Worked out"},{"t":"try","text":"Put a few drops of milk in a glass of water and shine a flashlight through it from the side. The water looks bluish from the side and orange from the end."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why is the sky blue?","options":["Air scatters red light more strongly","The sun changes color during the day","Air scatters blue light more strongly"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the fact box to see how air affects different colors of light.","explain":"Air bounces blue light all over, making the sky look blue."}}]},{"icon":"🪞","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"When light hits a mirror, it bounces off at exactly the same angle it arrived, like a ball hitting a wall."},{"t":"fact","text":"This one rule explains how every mirror, periscope, and reflector works."},{"t":"try","text":"Shine a flashlight at a mirror from a slant in a dark room with a little dust or steam in the air so the beam shows. Change the angle and watch the reflection change by the same amount."}]},{"icon":"🥛","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Light does something surprising when it crosses from air into water."},{"t":"fact","text":"Light bends as it moves between air and water. That's why a pencil in a glass of water looks broken, and why a pool is deeper than it seems."},{"t":"try","text":"Stand a pencil in a glass of water and look at it from the side. You'll see the pencil appear to bend or break."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why does a pencil in water look broken?","options":["Light bends as it crosses between water and air","The wood softens and bends where it touches the water","The water pushes up on the pencil and bends it"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It explains why light bends between water and air.","explain":"Light bends as it moves between water and air, making the pencil look broken."}}]},{"icon":"🎨","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Mixing light and mixing paint are opposites."},{"t":"fact","text":"Red, green, and blue light together make white. But red, green, and blue paint together make a muddy brown because each paint takes colors away rather than adding them."},{"t":"try","text":"Put a drop of water on a phone screen showing a white picture and look closely. You will see the red, green, and blue stripes the screen is really made of."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What do red, green, and blue paint make together?","options":["A bright yellow","A beautiful purple","A muddy brown"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It explains how mixing paints takes colors away.","explain":"Red, green, and blue paint together make a muddy brown because each paint is taking colors away."}}]},{"icon":"🔦","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A sun that is eight minutes old. White light split by a prism. A blue sky that turns red at sunset. A mirror, a pencil in water, a box of paints."},{"t":"p","text":"One light, and so many ways to watch it change."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Sunlight","Takes 8 minutes to reach Earth"],["Prism","Spreads white light into colors"],["Air","Scatters blue light more than red"],["Mirror","Bounces light at the same angle"],["Water","Makes a pencil look broken"],["Paint","Takes colors away, making a muddy brown"]]},"picgame":{"type":"catch","title":"Light Makers","goal":"Catch everything that makes light.","items":[{"icon":"☀️","name":"sun","good":true},{"icon":"💡","name":"light bulb","good":true},{"icon":"🔦","name":"flashlight","good":true},{"icon":"🕯️","name":"candle","good":true},{"icon":"🪞","name":"mirror","good":false},{"icon":"🧸","name":"teddy bear","good":false},{"icon":"🪨","name":"rock","good":false},{"icon":"🍎","name":"apple","good":false}]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why is the sky blue during the day but red at sunset?","options":["Air scatters blue light more than red; at sunset light travels through far more air, scattering the blue away entirely","The sun itself changes color at sunset","Clouds filter out different colors at different times"],"answer":0,"explain":"Short wavelengths like blue bounce around the sky; red comes straight through."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did Newton prove by putting a SECOND prism in the path of light that a first prism had already split into colors?","options":["That the colors recombine back into white light","That each color is actually a separate kind of light","That prisms only work with sunlight"],"answer":0,"explain":"He did the part people forget — showing the split was reversible, not that he'd created new colors."},{"type":"tf","prompt":"Mixing red, green, and blue light together makes white light, the same way mixing red, green, and blue paint does.","answer":1,"explain":"Mixing light and mixing paint are opposites — mixed paint makes muddy brown, because each paint takes colors away instead of adding them."}]},{"id":"physical-sound","course":"physical","kind":"facts","section":"sound","title":"Sound","glyph":"🔊","n":6,"idea":"Sound is a journey that can be fast or slow, loud or high-pitched. Discover how to measure distance with an echo and why children hear higher pitches than adults.","pages":[{"icon":"🔔","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Sound needs something to travel through. Put a ringing alarm inside a jar and pump the air out, and the ringing fades to nothing while you can still see the hammer moving."},{"t":"fact","text":"Space is genuinely silent because there is nothing for sound to travel through."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What happens to the ringing when the air is pumped out of the jar?","options":["It gets much louder because the jar is now empty inside","It fades to nothing though you can still see the hammer move","The hammer stops moving and the ringing stops with it"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the first sentence on the page.","explain":"With no air in the jar, there's nothing for the sound to travel through, so it fades away even as the hammer keeps moving."}}]},{"icon":"⏱️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Sound moves about 767 mph through air, which means it takes roughly five seconds to travel a mile."},{"t":"fact","text":"Light is so fast that it feels like it arrives instantly. That's why you can measure how far away a storm is by counting the seconds between seeing lightning and hearing thunder."},{"t":"try","text":"When you see lightning, count the seconds until you hear the thunder. Divide that number by five, and you'll know how many miles away the storm struck."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"About how long does sound take to travel one mile?","options":["About five seconds","About 10 seconds","About 20 seconds"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the first sentence on the page.","explain":"Sound takes about five seconds to travel a mile, so you can use this to estimate the distance of a storm."}}]},{"icon":"🐋","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Sound moves much faster in water than in air, and even faster in steel."},{"t":"fact","text":"In water, sound is more than four times faster than in air. In steel, it is even faster still."},{"t":"p","text":"Whales use this to talk over long distances under the sea. People used to listen for trains by putting an ear to a metal rail."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where does sound travel fastest of these?","options":["Air","Water","Steel"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the first fact on the page.","explain":"Sound travels fastest in steel, then water, and slowest in air."}}]},{"icon":"🎼","blocks":[{"t":"term","word":"pitch","text":"How many times a second something vibrates."},{"t":"fact","text":"People hear from about 20 vibrations a second up to about 20,000. Children can hear the high end better than adults can, because the tiny hair cells that catch high sounds wear out as we get older."},{"t":"try","text":"Run your fingernail slowly along a comb, then do it fast. Same comb, same teeth, but different pitch — only the rate changed."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is pitch?","options":["How much space a sound fills up in a room","How many times a second something vibrates","How hard something is struck to make a sound"],"answer":1,"hint":"The definition of pitch is in the term box.","explain":"Pitch is how many times a second something vibrates."}}]},{"icon":"🍷","blocks":[{"t":"fact","text":"Every object has a note it likes to vibrate at. Hit it, and it rings at that note. Sing that same note at it, and it starts to vibrate on its own. That is how a voice can shatter a wine glass."},{"t":"term","word":"resonance","text":"When something vibrates because the sound around it matches its own preferred note."},{"t":"try","text":"Tap a glass and listen for its note. You can also wet your finger and run it slowly round the rim of a wine glass until it sings."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How can a voice shatter a glass?","options":["Shouting so loudly that the sound crushes the glass flat","Blowing a hot breath onto the glass until it heats and cracks","Singing the glass's own note makes it vibrate on its own"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It explains how resonance works.","explain":"When you sing the exact note that the glass likes to vibrate at, it starts vibrating and can even shatter."}}]},{"icon":"🦇","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"An echo is sound that has gone somewhere and come back."},{"t":"fact","text":"Time the round trip, halve it, then multiply by the speed of sound, and you measure a distance without touching it."},{"t":"p","text":"Ships find the seabed this way and bats find moths."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How do you measure distance with an echo?","options":["Time the round trip, halve it, and multiply by the speed of sound","Measure the height of the wall and multiply it by how loud the echo is","Count how many echoes come back and multiply by the size of the room"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It explains the steps to calculate the distance.","explain":"You time how long it takes for the sound to return, halve that time, and then multiply by the speed of sound to get the distance."}}]},{"icon":"🎧","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Sound needs something to travel through. It moves faster in water and faster still in steel. Everything has a note it likes to ring at, and sometimes the sound comes back as an echo."},{"t":"p","text":"So next time you hear a sound, think about its trip, its shake, and maybe even its return."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Echo","Sound that comes back"],["Pitch","How many times something vibrates per second"],["Resonance","When sound makes an object vibrate"],["Speed of sound","About 767 mph in air"],["Whale communication","Uses fast sound travel in water"]]},"picgame":{"type":"tapall","title":"Sound Users","goal":"Tap who uses sound to find or talk.","items":[{"icon":"🐋","name":"whale","good":true},{"icon":"🦇","name":"bat","good":true},{"icon":"🚢","name":"ship","good":true},{"icon":"🌳","name":"tree","good":false},{"icon":"🪨","name":"rock","good":false},{"icon":"🍎","name":"apple","good":false},{"icon":"🧸","name":"teddy bear","good":false},{"icon":"🌻","name":"flower","good":false}]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does a classic classroom experiment with a ringing alarm inside a jar prove, once the air is pumped out?","options":["Sound needs something to travel through — space is truly silent","Sound gets louder in a vacuum","The alarm stops working without air"],"answer":0,"explain":"The ringing fades to nothing even though you can still see the hammer moving — the experiment is over 300 years old."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How can you measure how far away a thunderstorm is, using nothing but your own senses?","options":["Time the gap between the lightning flash and the thunder, since light arrives instantly but sound takes time","Count how loud the thunder sounds","Watch how fast the clouds are moving"],"answer":0,"explain":"Sound moves about a mile every five seconds through air — light is effectively instant by comparison."},{"type":"tf","prompt":"A wine glass can actually be shattered just by someone singing the right note at it.","answer":0,"explain":"Everything has a note it prefers to vibrate at — sing that note at it and it starts vibrating on its own."}]},{"id":"physical-heat","course":"physical","kind":"facts","section":"heat","title":"Heat & Cold","glyph":"🔥","n":6,"idea":"Heat moves from hotter to colder on its own, and that one rule helps explain a fridge, a breeze and a frozen pond. Find out where cold stops.","pages":[{"icon":"🥄","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Take two spoons lying side by side in a drawer. They are at exactly the same temperature, but one feels much colder than the other."},{"t":"fact","text":"A metal spoon feels colder because it carries heat away from your finger fast. Your skin does not measure temperature — it measures how quickly heat is leaving."},{"t":"try","text":"Leave a metal spoon and a wooden one in the same drawer overnight, then touch both."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why does metal feel colder than wood at the same temperature?","options":["Its shiny surface reflects your finger's warmth","It carries heat away from your finger faster","It soaks up cold from the air and holds it inside"],"answer":1,"hint":"Touch both spoons after leaving them in the same place.","explain":"Metal feels colder because it takes heat away from your skin quickly."}}]},{"icon":"➡️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Heat moves from hotter to colder, and that's all it does on its own."},{"t":"fact","text":"A fridge does not make cold. It takes heat out of the inside and dumps it out of the back."},{"t":"try","text":"Put your hand behind a fridge. That warmth is the heat that used to be in your food."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where does a fridge put the heat it takes out?","options":["Out of the back","Inside the fridge","Into the water pipes"],"answer":0,"hint":"Feel the back or underneath of your fridge.","explain":"The warmth you feel there is the heat the fridge took out of your food."}}]},{"icon":"🎈","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Warm air rises because it is thinner than cold air."},{"t":"fact","text":"Warm air rises and cold air slides in underneath. This single fact drives every breeze, every cloud, every hot-air balloon, and even the draft under your door."},{"t":"try","text":"Cut a paper spiral, hang it by a thread over a radiator or lamp, and watch it turn. Nothing is blowing on it."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why does warm air rise?","options":["It is heavier than cold air","It sinks below cold air","It is thinner than cold air"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the first fact box. Warm air rises because it is thinner.","explain":"Warm air is thinner, so it rises and cold air slides in underneath."}}]},{"icon":"🧊","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Most things shrink when they freeze, but water does something different."},{"t":"fact","text":"Water expands by about nine percent when it freezes. That is why ice floats, pipes burst, and the top of a pond freezes while fish keep swimming underneath."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why do fish survive under a frozen pond?","options":["Fish make their own heat to melt a hole in the ice","Ice floats, so the pond freezes from the top","Fish stay warm by swimming around very fast"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the first fact box. It tells you what happens when water freezes.","explain":"Ice floats because it is less dense than liquid water, so only the surface of a pond freezes, leaving fish safe below."}}]},{"icon":"💦","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Have you ever felt cold getting out of a bath? There is a reason for that."},{"t":"fact","text":"Evaporating water takes heat with it. That's why you feel cold after a bath, why sweating cools you down, and why a wet cloth around a bottle keeps the drink cool on a hot day."},{"t":"try","text":"Wrap a wet cloth around a bottle and watch how it cools the drink."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why do you feel cold getting out of a bath?","options":["Evaporating water takes heat with it","Your skin makes cold when it gets wet","Soap leaves a chill on your skin"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the first fact box. It tells you what happens when water evaporates.","explain":"Evaporating water takes heat with it, so as the water on your skin evaporates, it takes your body heat with it, making you feel cold."}}]},{"icon":"🥶","blocks":[{"t":"fact","text":"There is a bottom to cold, at −459.67°F. At this point, nothing has any heat left to give up, and nothing can be colder.","label":"absolute zero"},{"t":"term","word":"absolute zero","text":"The coldest possible temperature, where nothing has any heat left to give up."},{"t":"p","text":"Nobody has ever reached this temperature. Laboratories have gotten within a few billionths of a degree, but each step closer takes enormously more effort than the last."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Is there a coldest possible temperature?","options":["No, cold can go on forever","It changes depending on where you are","Yes, minus 459.67 F"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the fact box about absolute zero.","explain":"The coldest possible temperature is −459.67°F, known as absolute zero. At this point, nothing can be colder."}}]},{"icon":"🔥","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"On its own, heat moves from hotter to colder, like water flowing downhill."},{"t":"p","text":"That is why a fridge is warm at the back, why warm air rises, and why a wet cloth cools a bottle."},{"t":"fact","text":"Heat flows downhill. On its own, it moves from hot to cold. This simple rule explains a lot about our world."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["−459.67°F","The bottom of cold"],["Wet cloth","Cools a drink"],["Fridge","Takes heat out and dumps it behind"],["Warm air","Rises because it is thinner"]]},"picgame":{"type":"tapall","title":"Warm Air Rises","goal":"Tap what rising warm air makes.","items":[{"icon":"🎈","name":"balloon","good":true},{"icon":"💨","name":"breeze","good":true},{"icon":"☁️","name":"cloud","good":true},{"icon":"🚪","name":"door draft","good":true},{"icon":"🪨","name":"rock","good":false},{"icon":"🧸","name":"teddy bear","good":false},{"icon":"🍎","name":"apple","good":false},{"icon":"🌲","name":"tree","good":false}]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"Does a refrigerator actually create cold air?","options":["No — it removes heat from the inside and dumps it out the back","Yes — it generates cold air with a special chemical","No — it just insulates the inside from outside heat"],"answer":0,"explain":"Heat only ever moves from hotter to colder — put your hand behind a fridge and feel the heat it's dumping out."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"A metal spoon and a wooden spoon sitting on the same counter are the exact same temperature. Why does the metal one feel colder?","options":["Metal carries heat away from your skin faster — your skin senses that, not the actual temperature","The metal spoon is always a few degrees colder","Wood somehow generates its own heat"],"answer":0,"explain":"Your skin doesn't measure temperature directly — it measures how quickly heat is leaving your finger."},{"type":"tf","prompt":"Almost every substance shrinks when it freezes — water is one of the rare exceptions that expands instead.","answer":0,"explain":"Water expands by about nine percent when it freezes, which is why ice floats and ponds freeze from the top down."}]},{"id":"physical-electric","course":"physical","kind":"facts","section":"electric","title":"Electricity","glyph":"💡","n":6,"idea":"Electricity is all around us, moving through wires and sometimes giving you a tiny shock. Find out what makes lightning so powerful and why plastic covers wires.","pages":[{"icon":"🎈","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Have you ever rubbed a balloon on your hair and stuck it to the wall? Something moves when you do that."},{"t":"fact","text":"Rubbing two things together moves tiny charges from one to the other, leaving one positive and one negative. They then pull toward each other."},{"t":"try","text":"Rub a balloon on your hair and hold it near a thin stream of water from a faucet. The water bends toward the balloon."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What moves when you rub two things together?","options":["Tiny charges","Tiny hairs","Tiny dust particles"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It tells you what moves between the two things.","explain":"When you rub the balloon on your hair, tiny charges move from your hair to the balloon."}}]},{"icon":"🔁","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Electricity needs a complete loop to flow. Break the loop anywhere, and everything stops."},{"t":"fact","text":"A switch breaks the loop so nothing flows. That is why one dead bulb used to kill a whole string of Christmas lights."},{"t":"try","text":"With a battery, a wire, and a small bulb, find every arrangement that lights it. There are fewer than you expect."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does a switch do?","options":["It makes the bulb shine much brighter","It pumps new electricity into the loop","It opens and closes the loop"],"answer":2,"hint":"The fact box explains what a switch does to the loop.","explain":"A switch breaks the loop, stopping the flow of electricity."}}]},{"icon":"🧲","blocks":[{"t":"term","word":"electron","text":"A particle far too small to see. Electrons are what flows in a wire."},{"t":"fact","text":"We know how many are flowing because a current pushes a magnet by a measurable amount. That push is what an ammeter reads.","label":"Worked out"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How do we know how much electricity is flowing?","options":["A strong microscope lets us watch the electrons flow","A current pushes a magnet by a measurable amount","A tiny machine counts each electron that goes by"],"answer":1,"hint":"The fact box tells you how we measure the flow.","explain":"We cannot see the electrons, but we can measure the push on a magnet to know how much is flowing."}}]},{"icon":"🍋","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A battery makes electricity from a chemical reaction between two different metals with something wet in between."},{"t":"fact","text":"The first battery was a stack of copper and zinc discs, with brine-soaked cardboard between them."},{"t":"try","text":"Push a galvanized nail and a piece of copper wire (an old penny from before 1982 also works) into a lemon, not touching. Touch both with your tongue. That faint tingle is a working battery."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does a battery need?","options":["Two same metals with something dry between them","One metal and a dry sponge","Two different metals with something wet between them"],"answer":2,"hint":"The fact box lists the materials in the first battery.","explain":"A battery needs two different metals with something wet in between to make electricity."}}]},{"icon":"🔌","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Metals let electricity through, but glass, rubber, dry wood, and air do not."},{"t":"fact","text":"Wires are made of copper wrapped in plastic because plastic stops electricity from escaping."},{"t":"p","text":"Pure water does not conduct electricity well, but as soon as you add anything dissolved in it, like salt, it becomes a good conductor."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why are wires wrapped in plastic?","options":["Plastic stops electricity","To make them look colorful","To make them heavier"],"answer":0,"hint":"The fact box explains what plastic does in wires.","explain":"Plastic stops electricity from escaping, keeping the flow inside the wire."}}]},{"icon":"⚡","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Lightning is not just a pretty flash. A bolt carries around 30,000 amps (a lamp at home uses less than one). It heats the air to about 50,000°F, five times hotter than the surface of the sun."},{"t":"fact","text":"The temperature is worked out from the colors in the light. Thunder is that heated air exploding outward.","label":"Worked out"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is thunder?","options":["Clouds crashing together in the sky","Air exploding outward from the bolt","Strong wind rushing between two clouds"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the fact box to see what causes thunder.","explain":"Thunder happens when the heated air around the lightning bolt explodes outward."}}]},{"icon":"🔋","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A spark from a balloon and a bolt from a cloud are the same kind of thing: tiny charges on the move."},{"t":"p","text":"Break the loop and it stops. Close it and it flows."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Tiny charges","Move between two things"],["Switch","Breaks the loop to stop flow"],["Ammeter","Reads push on a magnet"],["Copper and zinc","Metals in the first battery"],["Plastic","Stops electricity from escaping"],["30,000 amps","Current in a lightning bolt"]]},"picgame":{"type":"sort","title":"Flow or Stop","goal":"Does electricity go through it, or stop?","bins":[{"icon":"⚡","label":"goes through"},{"icon":"🛑","label":"stops"}],"items":[{"icon":"🔑","name":"key","bin":0},{"icon":"🪙","name":"coin","bin":0},{"icon":"🔩","name":"bolt","bin":0},{"icon":"🥃","name":"glass","bin":1},{"icon":"🪵","name":"dry wood","bin":1},{"icon":"🛞","name":"rubber tire","bin":1}]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why does breaking an electrical circuit anywhere stop the whole thing from working?","options":["Electricity only flows around a complete, unbroken loop","Electricity needs a battery to restart after any gap","Switches only block part of the current"],"answer":0,"explain":"That's exactly what a switch is — and why one dead bulb used to kill a whole string of old Christmas lights."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How do scientists know how much electric current is flowing through a wire, since electrons are far too small to see?","options":["A current pushes a magnet by a measurable amount, and that's what an ammeter reads","They count the electrons directly with a microscope","They weigh the wire before and after"],"answer":0,"explain":"Nobody has ever watched an electron move down a wire — the effect is measured, and the amount is worked out from it."},{"type":"tf","prompt":"Pure, completely clean water actually conducts electricity poorly — it's the salt and minerals dissolved in real water that make it dangerous.","answer":0,"explain":"The rule is never really about water itself — it's about what's dissolved in it, including in your own body."}]},{"id":"physical-magnets","course":"physical","kind":"facts","section":"magnets","title":"Magnets","glyph":"🧲","n":6,"idea":"Cut a magnet in half and you get two magnets. Find out why a compass does not point at the North Pole, and how a magnet switching on and off makes a motor spin.","pages":[{"icon":"🧲","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Every magnet has two ends, but you can't have just one of those ends on its own."},{"t":"fact","text":"Snap a bar magnet in half and you don’t get a north piece and a south piece — you get two smaller magnets, each with both ends."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What do you get if you snap a bar magnet in half?","options":["Two smaller magnets","One piece with only a north end and one with only a south end","A single piece with no magnetic ends"],"answer":0,"hint":"Each half still has both ends.","explain":"Both halves of the snapped magnet will still have a north and a south end."}}]},{"icon":"🧭","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A compass needle does not point at the North Pole as you might think."},{"t":"fact","text":"It points at the magnetic pole, which is somewhere else entirely. The difference can be tens of degrees depending on where you are standing."},{"t":"p","text":"Maps print the correction for that spot so you can adjust and find your true direction."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where does a compass needle really point?","options":["At the North Pole","Toward the North Star","At the magnetic pole"],"answer":2,"hint":"The needle points at the magnetic pole, not the North Pole.","explain":"The magnetic pole is different from the North Pole, and the difference can be tens of degrees."}}]},{"icon":"🌐","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The magnetic north pole does not stay still. It moves around."},{"t":"fact","text":"Since 1831, scientists have tracked its movement. Right now, it is crossing the Arctic toward Siberia."},{"t":"p","text":"Recently, it moved so fast that navigation models had to be updated early."},{"t":"p","text":"Survey aircraft and satellites measure where it is."}]},{"icon":"🔩","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Not everything is magnetic. Only a few things stick to a magnet."},{"t":"fact","text":"Iron, nickel, and cobalt are magnetic, as well as mixtures that contain them. Aluminum, copper, gold, and silver are not."},{"t":"try","text":"Grab a fridge magnet and test some coins and a spoon. See which ones stick."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which of these is magnetic?","options":["Aluminum","Iron","Gold"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It lists iron as one of the magnetic materials.","explain":"Iron sticks to magnets, but aluminum and gold do not."}}]},{"icon":"🪛","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Electricity and magnetism are like twins wearing different hats. They are the same thing, just showing up in different ways."},{"t":"fact","text":"Wrap wire around an iron nail, connect a battery, and the nail becomes a magnet. Disconnect it and the magnetism stops."},{"t":"term","word":"electromagnet","text":"A magnet that works only while electricity flows through a wire coiled around iron."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What happens to the nail magnet when you disconnect the battery?","options":["The magnet stays strong","The magnetism stops","The nail gets hot"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It tells you what happens when you disconnect the battery.","explain":"When you disconnect the battery, the magnetism in the nail stops."}}]},{"icon":"🌀","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Think about all the motors you use every day: fans, drills, washing machines, even the buzz in your phone."},{"t":"fact","text":"Nearly every motor works by switching an electromagnet on and off. The moving part is always being pulled toward the next position but never quite arrives. A magnet that simply attracts would pull once and stop."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why do motors keep switching the magnet?","options":["The magnet gets too hot and has to be turned off to cool","A fresh magnet is swapped in because the old one wears out","A magnet that only attracts would pull once and stop"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It explains why a magnet that only attracts would pull once and stop.","explain":"A magnet that simply attracts would pull once and stop. The switching keeps it moving."}}]},{"icon":"🚀","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Two ends that never come apart. A compass that does not point at the North Pole. A nail that is a magnet only while a battery is connected."},{"t":"p","text":"And inside every fan and drill, a magnet switching on and off, over and over."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["North Pole","Not where a compass needle points"],["Magnetic pole","Where a compass needle really points"],["Iron","Sticks to magnets"],["Electromagnet","Becomes magnetic with electricity"],["Compass","Points toward the magnetic pole"]]},"picgame":{"type":"tapall","title":"Make a Magnet","goal":"Tap what you need for an electromagnet.","items":[{"icon":"🔩","name":"iron nail","good":true},{"icon":"🔋","name":"battery","good":true},{"icon":"🔌","name":"wire","good":true},{"icon":"🧊","name":"ice cube","good":false},{"icon":"🍎","name":"apple","good":false},{"icon":"🪨","name":"rock","good":false},{"icon":"🧸","name":"teddy bear","good":false},{"icon":"🥛","name":"milk","good":false}]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"If you snap a bar magnet in half, what do you get?","options":["Two smaller magnets, each with its own north and south end","A separate north piece and south piece","A magnet that no longer has any poles"],"answer":0,"explain":"You cannot get a single pole on its own, no matter how many times you break a magnet apart."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What's the quickest way to turn an iron nail into a magnet?","options":["Wrap wire around it and connect a battery","Rub it against another piece of iron for a long time","Leave it outside in a storm"],"answer":0,"explain":"Electricity and magnetism are the same force wearing different hats — disconnect the battery and the magnetism stops."},{"type":"tf","prompt":"A compass needle points directly at the Earth's true North Pole.","answer":1,"explain":"It points at the magnetic pole instead, which can be tens of degrees off depending where you're standing — and it's slowly moving toward Siberia."}]},{"id":"physical-matter","course":"physical","kind":"facts","section":"matter","title":"States of Matter","glyph":"🧊","n":6,"idea":"Water can be a solid, a liquid and a gas before lunch. Find out why a steel ship floats, why you cannot feel the air pressing on you, and how you can run across a liquid.","pages":[{"icon":"💧","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Which thing in your kitchen can be a solid, a liquid, and a gas all in the same day?"},{"t":"fact","text":"Water is the one substance that does it naturally — ice, water, steam. Everything else in your house stays as it is unless you do something drastic to it."},{"t":"try","text":"Put some ice in a pan with a thermometer in it and heat it slowly. Watch for the pause in temperature while the ice melts."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which kitchen substance can be a solid, a liquid, and a gas in one day?","options":["Metal","Water","Wood"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the first fact box on the page.","explain":"Water can be ice, liquid water, and steam. It changes naturally throughout the day."}}]},{"icon":"⛰️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Water does not always boil at 212°F. Up in Denver, a mile up, it boils at about 202°F."},{"t":"fact","text":"There is less air pressing down on it up there, so it boils cooler."},{"t":"p","text":"Food takes longer to cook in Denver, and packet instructions say so. On high enough mountains, you can boil water that is not hot enough to make tea properly."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why does water boil cooler in Denver?","options":["Less air is pressing down on it","The cold wind cools off the water","The water in Denver is much cleaner"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It tells you why water boils cooler.","explain":"In Denver, less air presses down on the water, so it boils at a lower temperature."}}]},{"icon":"🚢","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Whether something floats depends on how heavy it is for its size, not just how heavy it is."},{"t":"fact","text":"A steel ship floats because most of it is air. A steel bolt does not float because it is all steel with no air."},{"t":"try","text":"Take a bowl and a coin. Float the bowl, sink the coin."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why does a steel ship float?","options":["The water is warmer","It is made of wood","Most of it is air"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It explains what makes the difference for the ship.","explain":"Most of the ship is air, so it floats even though it is made of heavy steel."}}]},{"icon":"🌬️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"You might think air is weightless, but it has heft."},{"t":"fact","text":"At sea level, air presses on you at 14.7 pounds on every square inch. That adds up to about 20 tons over your whole body."},{"t":"p","text":"But you do not feel this heavy air pressing down because it pushes from inside you too."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why do you not feel the weight of the air?","options":["It pushes from inside you too","The air weighs nothing at all","Your skin is too thick to feel it"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the paragraph before the check.","explain":"Air pressure pushes from inside you as well, so you do not feel its weight."}}]},{"icon":"🥣","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Imagine a liquid you can run across. That sounds impossible, but mix cornstarch and water and that is exactly what you get."},{"t":"fact","text":"Hit it and it goes hard; leave your hand there and you sink. The grains jam together when pushed suddenly and slide when pushed slowly."},{"t":"try","text":"Mix cornstarch with water until it pours like cream. Punch it fast, then press slowly. Same bowl, two completely different substances."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What happens if you hit cornflour and water?","options":["You sink right in","It spills everywhere","It goes hard"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the fact above the try block.","explain":"When you hit it, the grains jam together, making it go hard."}}]},{"icon":"🔬","blocks":[{"t":"term","word":"atom","text":"The tiny building blocks that make up everything around us."},{"t":"fact","text":"Atoms are smaller than the waves light is made of. That means no ordinary microscope will ever show one.","label":"Worked out, not seen"},{"t":"p","text":"Today's pictures of atoms are made by dragging an extremely fine needle across a surface and recording the bumps."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How are pictures of single atoms made today?","options":["By zooming a very powerful camera in close on a surface","By dragging an extremely fine needle across a surface","By soaking a surface in dye and looking at it under a lamp"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the fact above the check block.","explain":"The needle records the bumps as it drags across, showing us what the atoms look like."}}]},{"icon":"⚛️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"One substance in three forms. Air that weighs something. A ship that floats because it is mostly air. A liquid that goes hard when you hit it."},{"t":"p","text":"And all of it is built from atoms that no ordinary microscope will ever show."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Water","The one substance that is ice, water and steam"],["Denver","Where water boils at about 202°F"],["Steel ship","Floats because most of it is air"],["Cornstarch and water","A liquid that goes hard when hit"],["Air","Presses 14.7 pounds on every square inch"],["Atom","Too small for any ordinary microscope"]]},"picgame":{"type":"sort","title":"Float or Sink","goal":"Does it float, or does it sink?","bins":[{"icon":"🦆","label":"floats"},{"icon":"⬇️","label":"sinks"}],"items":[{"icon":"🚢","name":"steel ship","bin":0},{"icon":"🥣","name":"bowl","bin":0},{"icon":"🧊","name":"ice","bin":0},{"icon":"🔩","name":"steel bolt","bin":1},{"icon":"🪙","name":"coin","bin":1},{"icon":"⚓","name":"anchor","bin":1}]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why does water boil at a lower temperature in a mountain city like Denver than at sea level?","options":["There's less air pressure pushing down on it at higher altitude","Mountain water is naturally purer","Cold mountain air cools the water as it boils"],"answer":0,"explain":"Denver's water boils at about 202°F instead of 212°F — which is why food takes longer to cook there."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"A massive steel ship floats, but a small steel bolt sinks. Why?","options":["Floating depends on how heavy something is for its size, not its total weight","Ships are made of a different, lighter metal","The ocean only supports large objects"],"answer":0,"explain":"Most of a steel ship's volume is actually air — a solid steel bolt has no such space inside it."},{"type":"tf","prompt":"Scientists have taken a real photograph of individual atoms by shining a very bright light on them.","answer":1,"explain":"Atoms are smaller than light waves, so no light-based microscope can show one — the images come from dragging an extremely fine needle across a surface instead."}]},{"id":"physical-machines","course":"physical","kind":"facts","section":"machines","title":"Simple Machines","glyph":"🔧","n":6,"idea":"A simple machine can make a hard job easier. Meet the lever, the pulley and the ramp, and the trade every machine makes.","pages":[{"icon":"🔧","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A simple machine uses one force to move one load."},{"t":"fact","text":"It can make the force bigger, but then the load moves a shorter distance."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What do you give up when a machine makes a force bigger?","options":["The weight","Distance","The load"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. The answer is there.","explain":"It can make the force bigger, but then the load moves a shorter distance."}}]},{"icon":"⚙️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A lever is a beam or rod that pivots on a point called a fulcrum."},{"t":"term","word":"fulcrum","text":"The point a lever pivots on."},{"t":"fact","text":"It is one of six simple machines. A lever makes a small input force into a greater output force."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is the pivot point of a lever called?","options":["A ramp","A fulcrum","A pulley"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"A lever is a beam or rod that pivots on a point called a fulcrum."}}]},{"icon":"🧑","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"In ancient Egypt, workers used levers to move and lift obelisks."},{"t":"fact","text":"The obelisks weighed more than 100 tons."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did workers in ancient Egypt lift with levers?","options":["Wagons","Obelisks","Pulleys"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"In ancient Egypt, workers used levers to move and lift obelisks."}}]},{"icon":"🧩","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A pulley is a wheel on an axle."},{"t":"fact","text":"A cable or belt passes over the wheel, so it can move and change direction."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is a pulley?","options":["A beam on a hinge","A wheel on an axle","A sloping road"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"A pulley is a wheel on an axle."}}]},{"icon":"👤","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Plutarch wrote that Archimedes used a system of pulleys to pull a fully loaded ship toward him."},{"t":"fact","text":"The ship moved as if it were gliding through water."}]},{"icon":"🧰","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"An inclined plane, or ramp, helps move heavy loads over a vertical obstacle."},{"t":"fact","text":"It takes less force to move an object up a ramp than to lift it straight up. The cost is a longer distance."}]},{"icon":"🔎","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A trade of force for distance. A lever on a fulcrum. A wheel with a cable. A ramp for heavy loads."},{"t":"p","text":"Simple machines help."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Lever","A beam that pivots on a fulcrum"],["Pulley","A wheel on an axle"],["Ramp","Helps move heavy loads up"],["Archimedes","Pulled a ship with pulleys"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which simple machine is a wheel on an axle with a cable?","options":["A lever","A pulley","A ramp"],"answer":1,"explain":"A pulley is a wheel on an axle."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does a ramp let you use less of?","options":["Distance","Force","Time"],"answer":1,"explain":"It takes less force to move an object up a ramp than to lift it straight up."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"A lever is one of how many simple machines?","options":["Two","Six","Sixty"],"answer":1,"explain":"A lever is one of six simple machines."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How heavy were the obelisks lifted in ancient Egypt?","options":["Only 10 tons","More than 100 tons","About 1 ton"],"answer":1,"explain":"The obelisks weighed more than 100 tons."}]},{"id":"physical-air","course":"physical","kind":"facts","section":"air","title":"Air & Pressure","glyph":"💨","n":6,"idea":"Air is all around you, and it has weight. Find out what air is made of and how far up it goes.","pages":[{"icon":"💨","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Dry air is 78.08% nitrogen and 20.95% oxygen."},{"t":"fact","text":"Air also holds water vapor, on average around 1% at sea level."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is most of the air made of?","options":["Oxygen","Nitrogen","Water vapor"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"Dry air is 78.08% nitrogen and 20.95% oxygen."}}]},{"icon":"📏","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Three-quarters of the air is within about 6.8 mi (11 km) of the ground."},{"t":"fact","text":"The air gets thinner the higher you go, and there is no clear edge where it ends."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What distance above the ground holds three-quarters of the air?","options":["68 mi","6.8 mi","680 mi"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"Three-quarters of the air is within about 6.8 mi (11 km) of the ground."}}]},{"icon":"📐","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The edge of space is often drawn at the Karman line."},{"t":"fact","text":"It is 62 mi (100 km) above the ground."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How high is the Karman line?","options":["6.2 mi","62 mi","620 mi"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. The answer is there.","explain":"It is 62 mi (100 km) above the ground."}}]},{"icon":"⚙️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Air has weight."},{"t":"term","word":"pressure","text":"The weight of air pushing on every square inch."},{"t":"fact","text":"A column of air 1 square inch wide, from sea level to the top of the atmosphere, weighs about 14.7 lb. That is 14.7 pounds of pressure on every square inch."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How much does a column of air 1 square inch wide weigh?","options":["About 147 lb","About 14.7 lb","About 1.47 lb"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. The answer is there.","explain":"A column of air 1 square inch wide, from sea level to the top of the atmosphere, weighs about 14.7 lb."}}]},{"icon":"🔧","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Wind is air moving."},{"t":"fact","text":"It moves from a place with higher pressure to a place with lower pressure."}]},{"icon":"🧩","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Up to about 62 mi (100 km) high, the air keeps the same mix of gases."},{"t":"fact","text":"Turbulence stirs the air, so the mix stays the same."}]},{"icon":"🔎","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Air with nitrogen and oxygen. Air that thins out. A line for the edge of space. Air with weight. Wind."},{"t":"p","text":"Air is all around."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Nitrogen","Most of the air"],["Karman line","The edge of space"],["Air pressure","The weight of air"],["Wind","Air moving to lower pressure"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"How much of dry air is nitrogen?","options":["7.8%","78.08%","20.95%"],"answer":1,"explain":"Dry air is 78.08% nitrogen."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is wind?","options":["Air standing still","Air moving to lower pressure","Air getting colder"],"answer":1,"explain":"Wind is air moving from higher pressure to lower pressure."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What stirs the air so the gases stay mixed?","options":["Sunlight","Turbulence","Gravity"],"answer":1,"explain":"Turbulence stirs the air, so the mix stays the same."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does the air get as you go higher?","options":["Thicker","Thinner","Wetter"],"answer":1,"explain":"The air gets thinner the higher you go."}]},{"id":"physical-water","course":"physical","kind":"facts","section":"water","title":"Water's Tricks","glyph":"💧","n":6,"idea":"Plain water can do tricks no other common liquid can do. It floats when it freezes, and it can hold up an insect.","pages":[{"icon":"💧","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Water is the most abundant substance on Earth's surface."},{"t":"fact","text":"It is also the only common substance that exists as a solid, a liquid and a gas there."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What three forms does water take on Earth's surface?","options":["Rock, sand and mud","Solid, liquid and gas","Steam, smoke and fog"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. The answer is there.","explain":"It is also the only common substance that exists as a solid, a liquid and a gas there."}}]},{"icon":"🤔","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Ice is an unusual solid because it is less dense than liquid water, by about 8.3%."},{"t":"fact","text":"Water expands about 9% when it freezes."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Is ice more or less dense than liquid water?","options":["More dense","Less dense","Just the same"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"Ice is an unusual solid because it is less dense than liquid water, by about 8.3%."}}]},{"icon":"⚙️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Water is at its densest at 39°F (4°C)."},{"t":"fact","text":"As it gets colder and starts to freeze, it becomes less dense."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"At what temperature is water densest?","options":["32°F","39°F","93°F"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"Water is at its densest at 39°F (4°C)."}}]},{"icon":"🔧","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Water molecules pull on each other strongly, so water has a high surface tension."},{"t":"term","word":"surface tension","text":"The strong pull water molecules have on each other."},{"t":"fact","text":"That lets razor blades and insects called water striders float on top of it."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What insect can float on the surface of water?","options":["Grasshoppers","Water striders","Dragonflies"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"That lets razor blades and insects called water striders float on top of it."}}]},{"icon":"🧩","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Water is called the universal solvent and the solvent of life."},{"t":"fact","text":"It is the most studied chemical compound there is."}]},{"icon":"💡","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Ice is water frozen into a solid."},{"t":"fact","text":"It forms at or below 32°F (0°C)."}]},{"icon":"🔎","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Water as a solid, a liquid and a gas. Ice that is less dense. Water that is densest when cold. A strong pull on the surface."},{"t":"p","text":"Water is special."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Water","Solid, liquid and gas on Earth's surface"],["Ice","Less dense than liquid water"],["Water strider","Floats on water"],["Universal solvent","What water is called"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What happens to water when it freezes?","options":["It shrinks a lot","It expands about 9%","It turns to steam"],"answer":1,"explain":"Water expands about 9% when it freezes."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"At what temperature does water freeze?","options":["39°F","32°F","93°F"],"answer":1,"explain":"Ice forms at or below 32°F (0°C)."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is water called because it can mix with so much?","options":["The common metal","The universal solvent","The heavy gas"],"answer":1,"explain":"Water is called the universal solvent."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What lets insects float on water?","options":["Strong wind","Surface tension","Hot sunlight"],"answer":1,"explain":"Water molecules pull on each other strongly, so water has a high surface tension."}]},{"id":"physical-flight","course":"physical","kind":"facts","section":"flight","title":"How Things Fly","glyph":"✈️","n":6,"idea":"Birds, airplanes and helicopters all stay in the air. Find out about the biggest wingspan and the push called lift.","pages":[{"icon":"✈️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The wandering albatross has the greatest wingspan of any living animal that flies, up to 11 ft (3.5 m)."},{"t":"fact","text":"The great bustard is the heaviest flier, up to 46 lb (21 kg)."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which flying animal has the greatest wingspan?","options":["The great bustard","The wandering albatross","The tiny hummingbird"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"The wandering albatross has the greatest wingspan of any living animal that flies, up to 11 ft (3.5 m)."}}]},{"icon":"🏆","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Some fish can fly."},{"t":"fact","text":"The longest flight ever recorded for a flying fish lasted 45 seconds."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How long was the longest flight of a flying fish?","options":["4 seconds","45 seconds","45 minutes"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. The answer is there.","explain":"The longest flight ever recorded for a flying fish lasted 45 seconds."}}]},{"icon":"⚙️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"When air flows around a wing, the air pushes on the wing."},{"t":"term","word":"lift","text":"The part of the push that is at right angles to the flow."},{"t":"fact","text":"The part of that push that is at right angles to the flow is called lift. Lift usually acts upward, to work against gravity."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What do we call the part of the push that points across the airflow?","options":["Drag","Lift","Weight"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. The answer is there.","explain":"The part of that push that is at right angles to the flow is called lift."}}]},{"icon":"📅","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The Wright brothers invented and flew the first airplane in 1903."},{"t":"fact","text":"It was the first sustained, controlled flight of a powered machine heavier than air."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Who invented and flew the first airplane?","options":["The Boeing brothers","The Wright brothers","The Comet brothers"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"The Wright brothers invented and flew the first airplane in 1903."}}]},{"icon":"⏳","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The first jet aircraft was the German Heinkel He 178, in 1939."},{"t":"fact","text":"The first jet airliner, the de Havilland Comet, was introduced in 1952. The Boeing 707 flew for more than 60 years."}]},{"icon":"🔧","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A helicopter gets its lift and thrust from spinning rotors."},{"t":"fact","text":"That lets it take off and land straight up, hover, and fly forward, backward and sideways."}]},{"icon":"🔎","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A big wingspan. A fish that flies. Lift on a wing. A first airplane. A jet. A helicopter with rotors."},{"t":"p","text":"Flight is big."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Wandering albatross","Greatest wingspan of a living flier"],["Lift","Push at right angles to the flow"],["Wright brothers","Flew the first airplane"],["Helicopter","Spinning rotors give lift and thrust"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which animal that flies is the heaviest?","options":["The wandering albatross","The great bustard","The flying fish"],"answer":1,"explain":"The great bustard is the heaviest flier, up to 46 lb (21 kg)."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What gives a helicopter its lift and thrust?","options":["Flapping wings","Spinning rotors","A big balloon"],"answer":1,"explain":"A helicopter gets its lift and thrust from spinning rotors."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"In what year did the Wright brothers fly the first airplane?","options":["1093","1903","1930"],"answer":1,"explain":"The Wright brothers flew the first airplane in 1903."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How long did the Boeing 707 fly?","options":["Only 6 years","More than 60 years","About 600 years"],"answer":1,"explain":"The Boeing 707 flew for more than 60 years."}]},{"id":"physical-reactions","course":"physical","kind":"facts","section":"reactions","title":"Mixing & Changing","glyph":"⚗️","n":6,"idea":"Rust, fire and the fizz of baking soda are all one thing: one substance turning into another.","pages":[{"icon":"⚗️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Rust is a reddish-brown substance made when iron reacts with oxygen and water."},{"t":"term","word":"rust","text":"A reddish-brown substance made when iron reacts with oxygen and water."},{"t":"fact","text":"Given enough time, an iron object can turn entirely to rust."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What makes iron rust?","options":["Sunlight and sand","Oxygen and water","Cold and snow"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"Rust is a reddish-brown substance made when iron reacts with oxygen and water."}}]},{"icon":"⚙️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Combustion does not always make a flame."},{"t":"fact","text":"A flame is only visible when the burning substances turn to vapor."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"When can you see a flame?","options":["When the air around the fire is perfectly still","When the burning substances turn to vapor","When the sky behind the fire is completely dark"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. The answer is there.","explain":"A flame is only visible when the burning substances turn to vapor."}}]},{"icon":"🔧","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Wood and coal do not burn as solids."},{"t":"fact","text":"First heat turns them into gases, and then those gases burn. That burning makes the heat that turns more of the solid into gas."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What do wood and coal turn into before they burn?","options":["Liquids","Gases","Powders"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. The answer is there.","explain":"First heat turns them into gases, and then those gases burn."}}]},{"icon":"📅","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"In 1785, Antoine Lavoisier found the correct explanation of burning."},{"t":"fact","text":"It is a reaction with oxygen from the air."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did Lavoisier find burning to be?","options":["A trick of the light","A reaction with oxygen","A cooling of the air"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. The answer is there.","explain":"It is a reaction with oxygen from the air."}}]},{"icon":"🧩","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Baking soda reacts with acids."},{"t":"fact","text":"When it does, it gives off carbon dioxide gas."}]},{"icon":"💡","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Heat alone can make baking soda work as a raising agent in baking."},{"t":"fact","text":"Above 180°F (80°C), it releases carbon dioxide."}]},{"icon":"🔎","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Rust from oxygen and water. A flame from vapor. Wood that turns to gas. Oxygen from the air. Baking soda and carbon dioxide."},{"t":"p","text":"Things change."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Rust","Iron, oxygen and water"],["Combustion","Does not always make a flame"],["Lavoisier","Found that burning uses oxygen"],["Baking soda","Gives off carbon dioxide"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What gas does baking soda release?","options":["Water vapor","Carbon dioxide","Pure oxygen"],"answer":1,"explain":"Baking soda gives off carbon dioxide gas."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where does the oxygen for burning come from?","options":["The water","The air","The wood"],"answer":1,"explain":"Lavoisier found that burning is a reaction with oxygen from the air."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"When does baking soda release carbon dioxide from heat alone?","options":["Below 32°F","Above 180°F","Only in water"],"answer":1,"explain":"Above 180°F (80°C), it releases carbon dioxide."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What can an iron object turn entirely into?","options":["Gas","Rust","Water"],"answer":1,"explain":"Given enough time, an iron object can turn entirely to rust."}]},{"id":"physical-gravity","course":"physical","kind":"facts","section":"gravity","title":"Gravity","glyph":"🍎","n":6,"idea":"Gravity is the pull that keeps your feet on the floor. A hammer and a feather fell together on the Moon.","pages":[{"icon":"🍎","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Every object with mass pulls on every other object."},{"t":"fact","text":"The more mass an object has, the stronger its pull. The farther apart the objects are, the weaker the pull."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What makes the pull of gravity stronger?","options":["More distance","More mass","More wind"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"The more mass an object has, the stronger its pull."}}]},{"icon":"🧑","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Albert Einstein described gravity in 1915."},{"t":"fact","text":"He described it as a curving of space and time caused by mass."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Who described gravity as the curving of space and time?","options":["Simon Stevin","Albert Einstein","Galileo Galilei"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"Albert Einstein described gravity in 1915."}}]},{"icon":"👤","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"In 1586, Simon Stevin dropped two cannonballs of different sizes and weights from a tower."},{"t":"fact","text":"They fell at the same rate."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did Simon Stevin drop from a tower?","options":["A hammer and feather","Two cannonballs","Two feathers"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"In 1586, Simon Stevin dropped two cannonballs of different sizes and weights from a tower."}}]},{"icon":"💨","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Near Earth's surface, an object falling in a vacuum speeds up by about 32 ft per second, every second (9.8 m/s2)."},{"t":"fact","text":"That is true no matter how heavy it is."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Does a heavy object fall faster than a light one in a vacuum?","options":["Yes, it does","No","Only on the Moon"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. The answer is there.","explain":"That is true no matter how heavy it is."}}]},{"icon":"👑","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"On August 2, 1971, astronaut David Scott dropped a hammer and a feather on the Moon."},{"t":"fact","text":"They fell at the same rate and hit the ground together. On the Moon, gravity is only about one-sixth as strong as on Earth."}]},{"icon":"🏃","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Air pushes back on a falling object."},{"t":"term","word":"terminal velocity","text":"The top speed a falling skydiver reaches."},{"t":"fact","text":"A skydiver reaches a top speed, called terminal velocity, of around 118 mph (190 km/h)."}]},{"icon":"🔎","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A pull between objects. A curve of space and time. Cannonballs that fall together. A hammer and a feather. A top speed."},{"t":"p","text":"Gravity pulls."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Albert Einstein","Said gravity curves space and time"],["Simon Stevin","Dropped two cannonballs from a tower"],["David Scott","Dropped a hammer and a feather"],["Terminal velocity","A skydiver's top speed"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What fell at the same rate on the Moon?","options":["A rock and a leaf","A hammer and a feather","A ball and a bat"],"answer":1,"explain":"David Scott dropped a hammer and a feather, and they fell at the same rate."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How strong is gravity on the Moon compared with Earth?","options":["About double","About one-sixth","Exactly the same"],"answer":1,"explain":"On the Moon, gravity is only about one-sixth as strong as on Earth."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What do we call a skydiver's top speed?","options":["Vacuum speed","Terminal velocity","Lift speed"],"answer":1,"explain":"A skydiver reaches a top speed, called terminal velocity."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"In which year did Albert Einstein describe gravity as curved space and time?","options":["1195","1915","1951"],"answer":1,"explain":"In 1915, Albert Einstein described gravity as a curving of space and time."}]},{"id":"physical-atoms","course":"physical","kind":"facts","section":"atoms","title":"Atoms & Elements","glyph":"⚛️","n":6,"idea":"Everything you can touch is built from atoms too small to see, and there are only 118 kinds.","pages":[{"icon":"⚛️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Atoms are extremely small, typically around 100 picometers across."},{"t":"fact","text":"More than 99.94% of an atom's mass is in its nucleus."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where is most of an atom's mass?","options":["In the outer shell","In the nucleus","In the empty space"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. The answer is there.","explain":"More than 99.94% of an atom's mass is in its nucleus."}}]},{"icon":"⚙️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"What makes one element different from another is the number of protons in its atoms."},{"t":"term","word":"proton","text":"The number of these in an atom makes it one element."},{"t":"fact","text":"Any atom with 11 protons is sodium. Any atom with 29 protons is copper."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What element has atoms with 29 protons?","options":["Sodium","Copper","Oxygen"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. The answer is there.","explain":"Any atom with 29 protons is copper."}}]},{"icon":"🔧","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Oxygen has 8 protons in each atom."},{"t":"fact","text":"So oxygen is number 8 on the list of elements."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many protons does an oxygen atom have?","options":["80","8","18"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"Oxygen has 8 protons in each atom."}}]},{"icon":"📏","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Today 118 elements are known."},{"t":"fact","text":"The first 94 occur naturally on Earth. The other 24 only occur when scientists make them in laboratories."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many elements occur naturally on Earth?","options":["24","94","118"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. The answer is there.","explain":"The first 94 occur naturally on Earth."}}]},{"icon":"🧑","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Dmitri Mendeleev was a Russian chemist."},{"t":"fact","text":"In 1869, he made the first periodic table that people widely accepted."}]},{"icon":"🤔","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Francium, element 87, is so rare."},{"t":"fact","text":"It has only been photographed as light from tiny amounts."}]},{"icon":"🔎","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Atoms that are very small. Protons that decide the element. Oxygen with 8 protons. 118 elements. A table of elements. A rare element."},{"t":"p","text":"Atoms make everything."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Atom","Extremely small, about 100 picometers across"],["Proton","Decides which element an atom is"],["Copper","Atoms with 29 protons"],["Mendeleev","Made the first accepted periodic table"],["Francium","Element 87, so rare"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which element has atoms with 11 protons?","options":["Copper","Sodium","Oxygen"],"answer":1,"explain":"Any atom with 11 protons is sodium."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many elements are known today?","options":["18","118","1,180"],"answer":1,"explain":"Today 118 elements are known."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Who made the first periodic table that people widely accepted?","options":["David Scott","Dmitri Mendeleev","Albert Einstein"],"answer":1,"explain":"In 1869, Dmitri Mendeleev made the first periodic table that people widely accepted."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many of the 118 elements occur naturally on Earth?","options":["24","94","11"],"answer":1,"explain":"The first 94 occur naturally on Earth."}]},{"id":"micro-cell","course":"micro","kind":"facts","section":"cell","title":"Inside a Cell","glyph":"🔵","n":6,"idea":"Cells are the tiny rooms that make up everything living. Discover what's inside them and how they work.","pages":[{"icon":"🔬","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"In 1665, Robert Hooke sliced a piece of cork so thin he could see through it."},{"t":"fact","text":"He put the slice under his microscope and found it was full of tiny empty boxes."},{"t":"p","text":"These boxes reminded him of the little rooms monks slept in. So he called them cells."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why did Hooke call them cells?","options":["They reminded him of the little rooms monks slept in","He thought of the tiny windows in old stone churches","They looked like the tiny stones that paved old roads"],"answer":0,"hint":"The tiny empty boxes reminded him of something specific.","explain":"He named them after the small rooms because that's what they reminded him of."}}]},{"icon":"💧","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Antonie van Leeuwenhoek, who made cloth, ground lenses better than anyone else."},{"t":"fact","text":"He looked at a drop of pond water and found it full of tiny things swimming around. He called them animalcules."},{"t":"p","text":"Nobody believed him until the Royal Society sent someone to check his work."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did Leeuwenhoek find in a drop of pond water?","options":["Tiny flowers floating","Tiny things swimming","Pieces of cork"],"answer":1,"hint":"He saw living things moving around.","explain":"He found tiny things swimming, which he called animalcules."}}]},{"icon":"🧮","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"You are made of about 30 trillion cells. That is a lot, but nobody counted them all."},{"t":"fact","text":"The number comes from weighing organs and working out the typical size and weight of each kind of cell. Then someone did the math.","label":"Worked out"},{"t":"p","text":"In 2016, a careful recount of the bacteria in and on your body replaced an older figure that was several times too big. A number repeated in textbooks for decades turned out to have been a rough guess nobody had rechecked."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How was the 30 trillion worked out?","options":["By putting a body under a huge microscope and counting every last cell","By measuring the height of many people and guessing how many cells they have","By weighing organs and working out the typical size of each kind of cell"],"answer":2,"hint":"The fact box tells you how it was done.","explain":"The number comes from weighing organs and figuring out the typical size of cells, not by counting them all."}}]},{"icon":"🧼","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Every cell is wrapped in a skin, just two molecules thick. It decides what gets in and out."},{"t":"fact","text":"This skin is made of fat, which is why soap can break germs apart."},{"t":"try","text":"Put a raw egg in vinegar for two days. The shell dissolves, and you are left with the membrane — a bare cell you can hold."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why can soap break some germs apart?","options":["Germs have thin skin made of salt that soap dissolves","Soap fills them with water until they burst open","Their skin is made of fat and soap breaks it up"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It tells you what the skin is made of.","explain":"The skin of germs is made of fat, and soap breaks that up."}}]},{"icon":"🧵","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Inside every cell is a long string of instructions called DNA."},{"t":"term","word":"DNA","text":"The instructions inside your cells that tell them what to do."},{"t":"fact","text":"If you pulled the DNA out of one of your cells and stretched it into a line, it would be about 6 feet long. It fits because it is wound round protein spools and coiled again and again, like a very long rope packed into a very small bag.","label":"Worked out"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"About how long is the DNA in one cell if you stretch it out?","options":["About 6 feet","About 1 foot","About 12 feet"],"answer":0,"hint":"The fact box tells you the exact length when stretched out.","explain":"The DNA is about 6 feet long when stretched out, but it's packed tightly inside the cell."}}]},{"icon":"🔋","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Inside almost every cell, there are hundreds of bean-shaped parts that do nothing but release energy from food."},{"t":"term","word":"mitochondria","text":"The bean-shaped parts inside your cells that release energy from food."},{"t":"fact","text":"Muscle cells that work hard are packed with them; a heart muscle cell can be a third mitochondria by volume."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What do mitochondria do?","options":["Make the cell grow bigger","Release energy from food","Carry instructions for the cell"],"answer":1,"hint":"The first paragraph explains what they do.","explain":"Mitochondria release energy from food, helping cells function."}}]},{"icon":"🧬","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"From a slice of cork in 1665 to the thirty trillion cells in you, each one is wrapped in a skin of fat, packed with DNA and tiny energy makers."},{"t":"p","text":"Not bad for something nobody could see until someone ground a good lens."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Robert Hooke","The man who saw cork cells"],["Antonie van Leeuwenhoek","Found swimming animalcules in pond water"],["DNA","The instructions inside your cells"],["Mitochondria","Bean-shaped energy releasers in cells"],["Cell skin","Made of fat, so soap breaks it up"]]},"picgame":{"type":"catch","title":"Cell Catch","goal":"Catch what is alive and made of cells.","items":[{"icon":"🧒","name":"child","good":true},{"icon":"🌳","name":"tree","good":true},{"icon":"🐟","name":"fish","good":true},{"icon":"🌻","name":"flower","good":true},{"icon":"🪨","name":"rock","good":false},{"icon":"🧊","name":"ice cube","good":false},{"icon":"🔩","name":"bolt","good":false},{"icon":"🧲","name":"magnet","good":false}]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why did Robert Hooke call the tiny boxes he saw in cork \"cells\" in 1665?","options":["They reminded him of the small rooms monks slept in","He named them after his own last name","A colleague suggested the word"],"answer":0,"explain":"The name stuck ever since, from his bestselling book of drawings, Micrographia."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How do scientists know you're made of roughly 30 trillion cells, since nobody has ever counted them one by one?","options":["By weighing organs and working out the typical size and weight of each kind of cell","By scanning the whole body with a special camera","It's an old number nobody has ever questioned"],"answer":0,"explain":"This corrected figure, published in 2016, replaced an older guess that had gone unchecked for decades and was several times too big."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What do the hundreds of bean-shaped parts inside almost every cell actually do?","options":["Release energy from food","Store the cell's DNA","Help the cell move"],"answer":0,"explain":"They're called mitochondria — a hardworking heart muscle cell can be a third mitochondria by volume."},{"type":"tf","prompt":"If you stretched out the DNA from just one of your cells, it would measure about 6 feet long.","answer":0,"explain":"It fits inside the tiny cell because it's wound around protein spools and coiled again and again."}]},{"id":"micro-bacteria","course":"micro","kind":"facts","section":"bacteria","title":"Bacteria","glyph":"🦠","n":6,"idea":"A thousand bacteria fit across the head of a pin, and one can become over a thousand in under four hours. Find out why most of them are on your side.","pages":[{"icon":"📏","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Imagine something so tiny that you could lay a thousand of them across the head of a pin and still have room to spare."},{"t":"fact","text":"A typical bacterium is about two thousandths of a millimeter long. You could fit a thousand of them across the head of a pin, with room left over."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many bacteria fit across the head of a pin?","options":["About a thousand, with room to spare","About ten thousand, packed in tight","About a million, with no room to spare"],"answer":0,"hint":"The fact box says you can fit a thousand with room left over.","explain":"A thousand bacteria fit across the head of a pin, with some space still left."}}]},{"icon":"✂️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Bacteria multiply by splitting in two."},{"t":"term","word":"bacterium","text":"A single tiny living thing that splits into two to make more of itself."},{"t":"fact","text":"In ideal conditions, a common gut bacterium can split every twenty minutes. That means one becomes eight in an hour and more than a thousand in under four hours."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"If one bacterium splits every twenty minutes, how many will there be after an hour?","options":["Four","Sixteen","Eight"],"answer":2,"hint":"Each split doubles the number. Start with one, double it three times (every twenty minutes).","explain":"One becomes two, then four, and finally eight in an hour."}}]},{"icon":"♨️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Some bacteria live where it is so hot that it could cook an egg."},{"t":"fact","text":"One was pulled out of a pool at Yellowstone. It makes a heat-proof copying enzyme used in medical tests all over the world."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where was the heat-proof microbe found?","options":["A cold lake in Alaska","A hot pool at Yellowstone","An ice cave in Iceland"],"answer":1,"hint":"The page mentions one specific place in Wyoming.","explain":"This microbe was found in a hot pool at Yellowstone, where it can survive extreme temperatures."}}]},{"icon":"🕊️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Most of the thousands of kinds of bacteria living on your skin and in your gut are not trying to hurt you."},{"t":"fact","text":"Only a small handful cause disease. Many are actually useful."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many kinds of bacteria cause disease?","options":["Nearly every single one","Thousands of them","Only a small handful"],"answer":2,"hint":"The page mentions that most do nothing to you and many are useful.","explain":"Only a small handful of the thousands of kinds of bacteria cause disease."}}]},{"icon":"⚖️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"You may have heard that there are ten times more bacteria in your body than your own cells. That was a guess from 1972 that got repeated for forty years."},{"t":"fact","text":"When someone finally checked properly, they found it was about one to one. There are still tens of trillions of bacteria, mostly in your large intestine.","label":"Worked out"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"When it was checked properly, how many bacteria are there per body cell?","options":["About one","Ten times more","Half as many"],"answer":0,"hint":"The fact box says the proper check came out at about one to one.","explain":"The number one to one means for every one of your cells, there is about one bacterium."}}]},{"icon":"💊","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"When you take antibiotics to fight bacteria, a few of those bacteria survive by chance."},{"t":"fact","text":"Those surviving bacteria breed, and their offspring also survive the drug. This has been watched happening in laboratories and hospitals."},{"t":"p","text":"That is why doctors tell you to take antibiotics exactly the way they say, and only when you need them."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why should you take antibiotics exactly the way the doctor says?","options":["To keep the pharmacy from running out of it","The few that survive breed and pass it on","Your body gets used to it and stops needing it"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It explains what happens when you do not finish the course.","explain":"Taking them the right way, and only when needed, gives the surviving bacteria less chance to breed and pass on their drug-resistant traits."}}]},{"icon":"🦠","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A thousand across a pin. One becomes eight in an hour. A microbe from a hot pool in Yellowstone that helps with medical tests all over the world."},{"t":"p","text":"Tiny, quick, and mostly on your side. And if you are ever given antibiotics, take them exactly the way the doctor says."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Bacterium","A single tiny living thing"],["Twenty minutes","How often a common gut bacterium splits"],["Yellowstone","Where the heat-proof microbe was found"],["Large intestine","Home to trillions of bacteria"],["Finish course","Stops tough bacteria from breeding"]]},"picgame":{"type":"sort","title":"Tiny or Big","goal":"Too tiny to see, or easy to see?","bins":[{"icon":"🔬","label":"microscope"},{"icon":"👀","label":"eyes"}],"items":[{"icon":"🦠","name":"bacterium","bin":0},{"icon":"🧬","name":"virus","bin":0},{"icon":"📍","name":"pin head","bin":1},{"icon":"🐜","name":"ant","bin":1},{"icon":"🍄","name":"mushroom","bin":1},{"icon":"🌳","name":"tree","bin":1}]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"For decades, people repeated the claim that bacteria outnumber your own cells ten to one. What happened when someone actually checked?","options":["The real ratio turned out to be closer to one to one","The claim turned out to be exactly right","Bacteria turned out to outnumber cells by a hundred to one"],"answer":0,"explain":"The ten-to-one figure came from a rough 1972 estimate that was simply never properly measured."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why do doctors insist you finish an entire course of antibiotics, even once you feel better?","options":["A few surviving bacteria can breed, and their offspring also survive the drug","Stopping early wastes the unused medicine","It takes that long for the drug to enter your bloodstream"],"answer":0,"explain":"This has actually been filmed in laboratories — bacteria creeping into bands of stronger and stronger drug."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"A heat-tolerant bacterium found in a Yellowstone hot spring turned out to be useful for what?","options":["A heat-proof copying enzyme now used in DNA tests worldwide","Making bread rise faster","Producing modern antibiotics"],"answer":0,"explain":"Every DNA test you've heard of depends on a microbe first found in a hot pool in Wyoming."},{"type":"tf","prompt":"Most of the thousands of kinds of bacteria living on your skin and in your gut are actually trying to make you sick.","answer":1,"explain":"Only a small handful cause disease — most are doing nothing to you, and plenty are useful."}]},{"id":"micro-virus","course":"micro","kind":"facts","section":"virus","title":"Viruses","glyph":"🧬","n":6,"idea":"A virus does not eat, grow or copy itself, and nobody agrees whether it counts as alive. Find out why there is no cure for the common cold.","pages":[{"icon":"🧬","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Is a virus alive? It is a bundle of instructions in a coat and does not eat, grow or copy itself on its own."},{"t":"fact","text":"A virus only does anything at all once it is inside a living cell. Whether that counts as alive is argued about and not settled.","label":"Worked out"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where does a virus only do anything?","options":["Inside a living cell","Outside a living cell","On the surface of a rock"],"answer":0,"hint":"The fact box tells you where a virus acts.","explain":"A virus is just a bundle of instructions in a coat until it gets inside a living cell."}}]},{"icon":"🔎","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Viruses are much smaller than bacteria. They can slip right through filters that catch every bacterium."},{"t":"fact","text":"That is how people first worked out something else was there. Nobody saw one until the electron microscope came along in the 1930s."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How were viruses first noticed?","options":["They glowed bright green under a strong lamp","They turned dark red when mixed with warm water","They passed through filters that catch bacteria"],"answer":2,"hint":"Viruses are tiny enough to slip through filters that catch bacteria.","explain":"People knew something was there because it could pass through filters designed to catch bacteria."}}]},{"icon":"🚀","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"There is a virus that attacks bacteria, and it looks like a spacecraft. It has a head full of instructions and a straight body."},{"t":"term","word":"bacteriophage","text":"A virus that attacks bacteria, with a head full of instructions, a straight body, and spindly legs."},{"t":"fact","text":"Spindly legs let it stand on the bacterium before it injects its contents. Photographs of it look invented, but they are real."},{"t":"p","text":"There are thought to be more of these bacteriophages on Earth than any other kind of living or virus-like thing."}]},{"icon":"💊","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Antibiotics are great at fighting bacteria, but they do nothing to a virus. They attack machinery that bacteria have, and viruses don't."},{"t":"fact","text":"Taking antibiotics for a cold cannot help. In fact, it makes resistant bacteria more likely."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Do antibiotics work on a cold?","options":["Yes, they make you feel better quickly","No, they do nothing to a virus","Yes, they make the cold go away in a day"],"answer":1,"hint":"Antibiotics attack bacteria, not viruses.","explain":"Antibiotics do nothing to a virus, and taking them for a cold can make resistant bacteria more likely."}}]},{"icon":"🐄","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"In 1796, Edward Jenner noticed something curious about milkmaids."},{"t":"fact","text":"Those who had caught cowpox did not catch smallpox. He put matter from a milkmaid's sore into a cut on an eight-year-old boy, waited, then deliberately exposed him to smallpox. The boy did not get it."},{"t":"p","text":"It was a shocking thing to do to a child, but it worked. Smallpox is the only human disease ever wiped out, and it was declared gone in 1980."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did Jenner notice about milkmaids?","options":["Those who had caught cowpox did not catch smallpox","Those who drank fresh milk every day never got sick","Those who worked in barns caught fewer colds all winter"],"answer":0,"hint":"Jenner saw a pattern with a specific illness.","explain":"Milkmaids who had cowpox did not get smallpox."}}]},{"icon":"🤧","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"You might think of a cold as one thing, but it is not."},{"t":"fact","text":"More than two hundred different viruses produce the same set of symptoms we call a cold. Beating one virus does not protect you from the other one hundred and ninety-nine."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why is there no cure for the common cold?","options":["One tough virus hides deep inside the body","More than two hundred different viruses cause it","Colds come from a germ that no medicine can kill"],"answer":1,"hint":"Each virus causes similar symptoms, but there are many of them.","explain":"There are more than two hundred different viruses that can cause a cold."}}]},{"icon":"🦠","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Much smaller than a bacterium, borrowing everything from the cell it gets into, and nobody agrees on whether it counts as alive."},{"t":"p","text":"No wonder there is no cure for the cold. There are two hundred viruses behind it, and antibiotics do nothing to any of them."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Edward Jenner","The doctor who tested cowpox on a boy"],["Antibiotics","Medicine that fights bacteria, not viruses"],["Smallpox","The only human disease ever wiped out"],["Common cold","Symptoms from over two hundred viruses"],["Cowpox","The illness that protects against smallpox"]]},"picgame":{"type":"tapall","title":"Jenner's Story","goal":"Tap who was in Jenner's story.","items":[{"icon":"🐄","name":"cow","good":true},{"icon":"🧒","name":"boy","good":true},{"icon":"👩","name":"milkmaid","good":true},{"icon":"👨","name":"doctor","good":true},{"icon":"🐖","name":"pig","good":false},{"icon":"🐕","name":"dog","good":false},{"icon":"🚲","name":"bicycle","good":false},{"icon":"🌳","name":"tree","good":false}]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why is it genuinely debated whether a virus counts as \"alive\"?","options":["It can't eat, grow, or copy itself — it only does anything once inside a living cell","All scientists agree it's definitely alive","It's too small to study closely enough to tell"],"answer":0,"explain":"The usual tests for life simply give contradictory answers for a virus — it's an open question, not a trick one."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why won't antibiotics do anything to help a cold?","options":["Antibiotics attack machinery that bacteria have and viruses don't","Colds are too mild for antibiotics to bother with","Antibiotics only work on old infections"],"answer":0,"explain":"Taking antibiotics for a virus can't help, and makes resistant bacteria more likely."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How did Edward Jenner create the first vaccine in 1796?","options":["He noticed milkmaids who'd had cowpox didn't catch smallpox, then tested the idea on a boy","He isolated the smallpox virus in a laboratory","He studied old medical records from past outbreaks"],"answer":0,"explain":"Smallpox is the only human disease ever wiped out — declared gone in 1980."},{"type":"tf","prompt":"There's no single cure for \"the common cold\" because there's no single virus that causes it.","answer":0,"explain":"More than two hundred different viruses produce the same symptoms — beating one still leaves 199 others."}]},{"id":"micro-fungi","course":"micro","kind":"facts","section":"fungi","title":"Molds & Yeasts","glyph":"🍄","n":6,"idea":"Every hole in a slice of bread is a bubble of yeast breath. Find out where bread mold really comes from, and why a mushroom is only the fruit.","pages":[{"icon":"🍞","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Have you ever looked closely at a slice of bread? Every little hole is actually a bubble of gas."},{"t":"fact","text":"Bread rises because yeast, a kind of fungus, eats the sugar in the dough and gives off carbon dioxide. The gas gets trapped in stretchy bubbles inside the bread."},{"t":"try","text":"Look closely at the holes in a slice of bread. Each one is where a bubble of carbon dioxide was trapped."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What makes bread rise?","options":["Salt gives off oxygen gas as it melts","Butter melts and lifts the dough up","Yeast gives off carbon dioxide gas"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It explains what yeast does with sugar.","explain":"When yeast eats sugar, it gives off carbon dioxide, and those bubbles get trapped in the dough, making it rise."}}]},{"icon":"🧫","blocks":[{"t":"term","word":"spore","text":"A tiny speck that a mold sends into the air. Mold starts out as spores."},{"t":"fact","text":"Mold does not arrive on bread. It is already in the air of every room, as spores, waiting for something damp."},{"t":"try","text":"Seal three slices of bread in bags: one untouched, one rubbed on the floor, and one wiped with a damp cloth. Leave them somewhere warm for a week and compare. Do not open them afterwards."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where does bread mold come from?","options":["Germs that crawl up from the plate","Spores that are already in the air","Old dough that goes bad all by itself"],"answer":1,"hint":"Mold spores are floating in the air of every room, waiting for something damp.","explain":"When you leave bread out, you are not catching mold — you are giving it a place to grow."}}]},{"icon":"🧪","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"In 1928, Alexander Fleming came back from vacation to find something interesting in his lab."},{"t":"fact","text":"One of his dishes had gone moldy, and in a clear ring around the mold, the bacteria were dead."},{"t":"p","text":"He noticed this instead of just throwing it away. Turning that observation into a usable drug took another twelve years and a different team as well."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did Fleming notice about the dish with the mold?","options":["The bacteria were dead in a ring around the mold","The mold was growing much faster than the bacteria were","A thick ring of bacteria had grown around the mold"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It says what he observed.","explain":"In the clear ring around the mold, the bacteria were dead."}}]},{"icon":"🍄","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Fungi are not like plants at all. They have no chlorophyll and cannot make their own food."},{"t":"fact","text":"Instead, they feed on things that are already dead or alive. Their walls are made of chitin, the same stuff as an insect's shell, and not the cellulose that plant walls are made of."},{"t":"p","text":"A mushroom is only the fruit. The organism itself is a mat of threads spread through the soil or wood."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What part of the fungus is a mushroom?","options":["Only the fruit","The roots","The leaves"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the last paragraph. It tells you what a mushroom really is.","explain":"A mushroom is just the fruit. The rest of the fungus is a mat of threads hidden in the soil or wood."}}]},{"icon":"🌲","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"In a forest in Oregon, there is one honey fungus with threads spread across 3.4 square miles."},{"t":"fact","text":"It is one organism, but above ground it shows up as scattered clumps of ordinary-looking mushrooms."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How big is the Oregon honey fungus?","options":["About 1 square mile","3.4 square miles","Over 10 square miles"],"answer":1,"hint":"The fact box says how big it is.","explain":"The underground threads of this one organism cover 3.4 square miles."}}]},{"icon":"🧀","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Some tasty things you eat depend on tiny living things doing something to the food."},{"t":"fact","text":"Bread, cheese, yogurt, soy sauce, vinegar, chocolate, and beer all need microbes to do something on purpose. Blue cheese is mold that someone put there deliberately."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which cheese has mold put in it deliberately?","options":["Cheddar","Mozzarella","Blue cheese"],"answer":2,"hint":"The fact box mentions which cheese has mold added.","explain":"Blue cheese has mold put in it on purpose."}}]},{"icon":"🍄","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Yeast breathing in dough. Spores waiting in every room. A mold that killed bacteria in a dish. A mushroom that is only the fruit of a mat of threads."},{"t":"p","text":"Fungi are not plants, and they do their work out of sight."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Yeast","Makes bread rise"],["Mold","Grows on old bread"],["Alexander Fleming","Discovered mold kills bacteria"],["Honey fungus","Covers 3.4 square miles underground"],["Blue cheese","Has mold added on purpose"]]},"picgame":{"type":"tapall","title":"Fungi Finds","goal":"Tap what fungi grow or help make.","items":[{"icon":"🍄","name":"mushroom","good":true},{"icon":"🍞","name":"bread","good":true},{"icon":"🧀","name":"blue cheese","good":true},{"icon":"💊","name":"medicine","good":true},{"icon":"🍎","name":"apple","good":false},{"icon":"🌻","name":"flower","good":false},{"icon":"🥕","name":"carrot","good":false},{"icon":"🪨","name":"rock","good":false}]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What actually causes bread dough to rise?","options":["Yeast eating sugar and giving off carbon dioxide gas, trapped in bubbles","Air getting whipped into the dough while kneading","Heat from the oven expanding the dough"],"answer":0,"explain":"Every hole in a slice of bread is literally a bubble of yeast breath."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where does mold actually come from when it appears on old bread?","options":["It's already floating in the air as spores, waiting for something damp","It's carried onto the bread by insects","It grows spontaneously from the bread itself"],"answer":0,"explain":"Leaving bread out isn't \"catching\" mold — you're simply providing a damp home for spores already in the air."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did Alexander Fleming actually notice in 1928 that led to penicillin?","options":["Bacteria were dead in a clear ring around mold on a dish he'd left out","A patient recovered unexpectedly fast from an infection","Mold samples under a microscope looked unusual"],"answer":0,"explain":"It took another twelve years and a different team to turn that observation into a usable drug."},{"type":"tf","prompt":"A single fungus found in an Oregon forest has been shown to be one organism spread across 3.4 square miles underground.","answer":0,"explain":"It was mapped by testing samples from all over the forest to see which ones were genetically the same individual."}]},{"id":"micro-pond","course":"micro","kind":"facts","section":"pond","title":"Pond Water","glyph":"💧","n":6,"idea":"You'll discover the hidden world in just one drop of pond water. Learn how each tiny creature moves, eats, and stays alive in its own unique way.","pages":[{"icon":"🫧","blocks":[{"t":"term","word":"amoeba","text":"One tiny cell with no fixed shape and no mouth. It does everything a whole animal does."},{"t":"fact","text":"An amoeba is just one cell, but it does everything a whole animal does — moves, hunts, eats, and gets rid of waste. It moves by pouring part of itself forward, and the rest follows."},{"t":"p","text":"It has no fixed shape and no mouth. When it finds food, it flows around it until the food is surrounded."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How does an amoeba eat?","options":["It flows around something until it is surrounded","It opens a tiny mouth and bites off small pieces","It shoots out sticky threads that pull the food in"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the sentence about how it surrounds its food.","explain":"The amoeba eats by flowing around food until it is completely surrounded."}}]},{"icon":"🌊","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Next, a swimmer covered in hair: the paramecium."},{"t":"fact","text":"A paramecium is covered in thousands of tiny hairs that beat in waves, like a crowd doing the wave at a ball game. It shoots through the water with this motion."},{"t":"p","text":"When it bumps into something, it reverses the beat of its hairs and backs up."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How does a paramecium back up?","options":["It uses its mouth to push backward","It grows a tail to swim backward","It reverses the beat of its hairs"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the sentence about reversing the beat.","explain":"When it bumps into something, it simply reverses the beat of its tiny hairs and backs up."}}]},{"icon":"🟢","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Now a ball that rolls: Volvox."},{"t":"fact","text":"Volvox is a hollow ball made up of hundreds of cells, each with two whips. They all beat together so the ball rolls through the water."},{"t":"p","text":"Inside the ball are smaller balls — the next generation, growing."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is growing inside a Volvox ball?","options":["Tiny hairs like on a paramecium","Smaller balls, the next generation","New sets of whips"],"answer":1,"hint":"The sentence mentions smaller balls inside as the next generation.","explain":"Inside the Volvox ball are smaller balls that are the next generation."}}]},{"icon":"💎","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"And now something that builds with glass: the diatoms."},{"t":"fact","text":"Diatoms build themselves glass boxes with lids. Each kind has a different pattern of holes in their boxes."},{"t":"p","text":"Victorian lens-makers lined them up to test their lenses because the patterns were so precise."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What are diatom shells made of?","options":["Wood","Metal","Glass"],"answer":2,"hint":"The fact mentions that diatoms build themselves glass boxes.","explain":"Diatoms make glass boxes for themselves."}}]},{"icon":"🧱","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Some single cells in pond water build armor out of whatever is lying around."},{"t":"fact","text":"A testate amoeba glues sand grains and bits of diatom shell into a case and carries it, leaving one opening to reach out through."},{"t":"p","text":"Different kinds use different materials and build different cases. That is how you tell them apart."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does a testate amoeba build its case from?","options":["Only thin flakes of metal that it picks up from the bottom of ponds","Whatever is lying around, like sand grains and bits of diatom shell","Only a thick jelly that it makes from its own body and nothing else"],"answer":1,"hint":"The fact mentions that it glues sand grains and bits of diatom shell.","explain":"A testate amoeba uses whatever is lying around to build its case."}}]},{"icon":"🫙","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Any still water has a hidden world of tiny life."},{"t":"fact","text":"Leave a jar of pond water on a windowsill for a week, and it will hold more than it did the day you collected it. You have just made a small pond with good light."},{"t":"try","text":"Leave a jar of pond water on a windowsill for a week, then look at it again."}]},{"icon":"🔍","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"An amoeba that flows round its food. A paramecium that backs up by reversing its hairs. A ball of cells that rolls. A glass box with a lid. A case made of sand."},{"t":"p","text":"All of it in one jar of pond water."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Amoeba","One cell that moves by flowing"],["Paramecium","Covered in tiny hairs that beat like a wave"],["Volvox","Hollow ball with hundreds of cells"],["Diatoms","Build glass boxes with precise patterns"],["Testate amoeba","Builds cases from sand and diatom bits"]]},"picgame":{"type":"tapall","title":"Pond Life","goal":"Tap what lives in a drop of pond water.","items":[{"icon":"🫧","name":"amoeba","good":true},{"icon":"🥿","name":"paramecium","good":true},{"icon":"🟢","name":"volvox","good":true},{"icon":"💎","name":"diatom","good":true},{"icon":"🐚","name":"testate amoeba","good":true},{"icon":"🐘","name":"elephant","good":false},{"icon":"🌳","name":"tree","good":false},{"icon":"🚗","name":"car","good":false}]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"How does an amoeba move, since it has no legs, fins, or fixed shape at all?","options":["It pours part of itself forward and lets the rest follow","It uses tiny hair-like structures to swim","It rolls like a ball through the water"],"answer":0,"explain":"It eats the same way — flowing around something until it's completely surrounded, with no mouth at all."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What are diatoms, the single-celled organisms Victorian scientists used to test their microscope lenses?","options":["Cells that build themselves glass boxes with finely patterned lids","Bacteria that glow in the dark","The fastest-swimming creatures in pond water"],"answer":0,"explain":"The patterns are so fine and regular that when diatoms die, their shells pile up as diatomaceous earth."},{"type":"tf","prompt":"A jar of pond water left on a windowsill for a week will actually have MORE living things in it than the day you collected it.","answer":0,"explain":"Any still water with good light can support a growing population — you've essentially made a small pond."}]},{"id":"micro-helpers","course":"micro","kind":"facts","section":"helpers","title":"Helpful Microbes","glyph":"🥛","n":6,"idea":"Microbes are everywhere and do a lot of work, from making yogurt to helping plants grow. Find out which vitamin gut bacteria make and how microbes help turn milk into yogurt.","pages":[{"icon":"🌱","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Pull up a clover plant and take a close look at its roots. You will see little lumps all over them."},{"t":"fact","text":"Those lumps are full of bacteria that pull nitrogen out of the air and give it to the plant as fertilizer. In return, the plant feeds them sugar."},{"t":"try","text":"Dig up a clover from a lawn. Wash the roots gently in a bowl of water and look for tiny pink or white nodules on the roots."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What do the bacteria in clover roots do for the plant?","options":["They carry water up from the deep soil to the roots","They store sunlight inside the roots to make food","They pull nitrogen out of the air as fertilizer"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It tells you what the bacteria give to the plant.","explain":"The bacteria pull nitrogen out of the air and give it to the plant, which helps the plant grow."}}]},{"icon":"🌍","blocks":[{"t":"term","word":"microbe","text":"A living thing too small to see without a microscope."},{"t":"fact","text":"A single teaspoon of healthy soil is estimated to hold more living organisms than there are people on Earth. The number comes from diluting soil, growing what is in it, and counting.","label":"Worked out, not seen"},{"t":"p","text":"But this count is probably too low because most soil microbes will not grow in a dish at all."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why is the soil count probably too low?","options":["Most soil microbes will not grow in a dish","Only the top inch of soil was ever checked","Soil microbes are too big for the dishes"],"answer":0,"hint":"The fact box mentions that most soil microbes do not grow in a dish.","explain":"Most soil microbes will not grow in a dish, so the count is likely lower than the actual number."}}]},{"icon":"🍂","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Think about every leaf that has ever fallen from a tree."},{"t":"fact","text":"Fungi and bacteria have taken apart every one of those leaves. Without them, the ground would be many feet deep in dead wood and leaves, and nothing new could grow."},{"t":"try","text":"Bury a slice of apple and a piece of plastic in a pot of soil. Dig both up in a month to see what happens."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What would happen without fungi and bacteria?","options":["The leaves would all dry up and blow away and the soil would be bare","The ground would be deep in dead leaves and nothing new could grow","New trees would grow faster and the forests would be much bigger"],"answer":1,"hint":"The fact box says what the ground would look like without fungi and bacteria.","explain":"Without fungi and bacteria, the ground would be many feet deep in dead leaves and nothing new could grow."}}]},{"icon":"🥛","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Yogurt is milk that bacteria have soured under control."},{"t":"fact","text":"The bacteria turn the milk sugar into acid, which makes the milk proteins clump together and stops anything worse from growing."},{"t":"try","text":"Warm milk to about 110°F, stir in two spoons of plain live yogurt, wrap it up warm, and leave it overnight. In the morning, it will be yogurt."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does the acid in yogurt do?","options":["It melts the milk fat and makes the yogurt taste much sweeter","It breaks the milk proteins down into sugar and makes it thin","It clumps the milk proteins and stops anything worse growing"],"answer":2,"hint":"The fact box explains what the acid does to the milk proteins.","explain":"The acid clumps the milk proteins and keeps other bad bacteria from growing."}}]},{"icon":"🍞","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Sourdough bread starts with a mix of flour and water left out until wild yeast and bacteria from the air and the flour move in."},{"t":"fact","text":"Kept fed, a sourdough starter can be passed down for generations, and bakeries have starters older than the baker."},{"t":"try","text":"Mix equal weights of flour and water in a jar. Throw half away and feed it again every day. By about day six it will be bubbling."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where do the microbes in a sourdough starter come from?","options":["The milk in the kitchen","The air and the flour","The oven where bread is baked"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the first sentence on the page for where the microbes come from.","explain":"The wild yeast and bacteria from the air and the flour move into the mix."}}]},{"icon":"🫃","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Inside your large intestine, there are lots of bacteria that help break down fiber you cannot digest."},{"t":"fact","text":"These same bacteria also make vitamin K, which your blood needs to clot properly."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which vitamin do gut bacteria make?","options":["Vitamin K","Vitamin C","Vitamin D"],"answer":0,"hint":"The page talks about a vitamin that helps your blood clot.","explain":"Gut bacteria make vitamin K, which is important for blood clotting."}}]},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Lumps on clover roots. A teaspoon of soil crowded with life. Leaves taken apart, milk turned to yogurt, dough that rises, and a gut that makes its own vitamin K."},{"t":"p","text":"Microbes do all of it, and nobody ever thanks them."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Nitrogen-fixing bacteria","Give clover fertilizer"],["Fungi and bacteria","Take apart every fallen leaf"],["Yogurt bacteria","Sour milk under control"],["Sourdough starter","Mix of flour and water with wild yeast"],["Gut bacteria","Make vitamin K in your large intestine"]]},"picgame":{"type":"catch","title":"Microbe Helpers","goal":"Catch what microbes help with.","items":[{"icon":"🌱","name":"clover","good":true},{"icon":"🥛","name":"yogurt","good":true},{"icon":"🥖","name":"sourdough","good":true},{"icon":"🍂","name":"fallen leaf","good":true},{"icon":"🪨","name":"rock","good":false},{"icon":"🧊","name":"ice cube","good":false},{"icon":"🔩","name":"bolt","good":false},{"icon":"🧸","name":"teddy bear","good":false}]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What do the bacteria living in your large intestine actually do for you?","options":["Break down fiber you can't digest, and make vitamin K your blood needs to clot","Nothing — they just live there without affecting you","Protect you from catching colds"],"answer":0,"explain":"People on long courses of antibiotics sometimes need extra vitamin K for exactly this reason."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What's actually happening in the little lumps on the roots of a pea or clover plant?","options":["Bacteria there pull nitrogen from the air and trade it to the plant for sugar","The plant stores extra water there","Insects lay their eggs in those lumps"],"answer":0,"explain":"It's a genuine trade — the bacteria get fed, and the plant gets natural fertilizer."},{"type":"tf","prompt":"Without fungi and bacteria breaking down dead leaves and wood, the ground would eventually be buried meters deep in them.","answer":0,"explain":"All that material would stay locked up in what had already lived, and nothing new could grow."}]},{"id":"micro-clean","course":"micro","kind":"facts","section":"clean","title":"Staying Well","glyph":"🧼","n":6,"idea":"Learn how simple actions like washing hands and chilling food help keep germs at bay. Discover that a fever is your body’s smart defense mechanism.","pages":[{"icon":"🏥","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"In 1847, a doctor named Ignaz Semmelweis noticed something that nobody wanted to hear."},{"t":"fact","text":"Mothers in the doctors' ward were dying far more often than those in the midwives' ward. The doctors came straight from dissecting the dead."},{"t":"p","text":"He made the doctors wash their hands in chlorine. The deaths dropped away."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did Semmelweis make the doctors do?","options":["Wear gloves while dissecting","Wash their hands in chlorine","Change clothes between wards"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It says what he made them wash their hands in.","explain":"Washing hands in chlorine helped drop the deaths, but he was ignored and mocked because germs were not yet shown."}}]},{"icon":"🧼","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"So how does soap help keep germs away? It’s not about poisoning them."},{"t":"fact","text":"One end of a soap molecule grips grease, and the other grips water. This lifts off grease and everything stuck in it, sending it down the drain. A virus with a fatty coat simply falls apart when soap touches it."},{"t":"try","text":"Sprinkle pepper on a bowl of water, then touch the surface with a soapy finger. The pepper will flee to the edges instantly."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does soap do to germs?","options":["It poisons them with chemicals and kills them on the spot","It sucks all the water out of them until they shrivel up","It lifts them off with the grease and sends them down the drain"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It says how soap molecules work.","explain":"Soap lifts off grease and everything stuck in it, including germs, and sends them down the drain."}}]},{"icon":"⏱️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"You might wonder why we count to twenty when we wash our hands."},{"t":"fact","text":"Twenty seconds is not just a number. It is about how long scrubbing takes to lift things off your skin, especially under nails and between fingers."},{"t":"try","text":"Next time you wash your hands, count to twenty as you scrub."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why do we wash our hands for about twenty seconds?","options":["That is how long it takes to physically lift things off your skin","Soap needs that long to kill every last germ on your skin","Because it takes that long for the water to get hot enough"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It explains why scrubbing takes that long.","explain":"Twenty seconds is about how long careful scrubbing takes to lift everything off your skin, especially in tricky spots like under your nails and between your fingers."}}]},{"icon":"🌡️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Have you ever felt really warm with a fever? That is your body doing something very smart."},{"t":"fact","text":"A fever is not something germs do to you. Your own body turns up the temperature because most germs that can make you sick do not grow as well when it gets hotter."},{"t":"p","text":"This is why a mild fever is usually left alone. It is a weapon being used, not damage being done."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Who turns the temperature up in a fever?","options":["The germs making you sick","Your own body","The medicine you take"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the first fact box on this page. It explains who raises the temperature.","explain":"Your body raises its temperature to fight off what is making you sick."}}]},{"icon":"🧊","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"You know how your fridge keeps food fresh for longer? It does not kill the bacteria. It just slows them down a lot."},{"t":"fact","text":"In a fridge, bacteria take days to reach numbers that could make you sick, instead of just hours."},{"t":"p","text":"Freezing nearly stops bacteria altogether. But when it thaws, they start up again where they left off."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Does a fridge kill bacteria?","options":["Yes, it kills them completely","It speeds them up","No, it only slows them down"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the fact box on this page. It explains how long bacteria take to reach numbers that could make you sick.","explain":"A fridge does not kill bacteria; it just slows them down so much that they take days instead of hours to grow to levels that could make you sick."}}]},{"icon":"🥛","blocks":[{"t":"term","word":"pasteurized","text":"Heated to about 161°F for fifteen seconds and then chilled fast. That kills what causes disease without cooking the milk."},{"t":"fact","text":"The method is named after Louis Pasteur and is on almost every carton you have ever opened."},{"t":"try","text":"Look at a milk carton for the word pasteurized. Then find one that says UHT (ultra-high temperature) and compare the dates. Hotter treatment means much longer life, but a different taste."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"About how hot is milk heated to be pasteurized?","options":["About 161°F","About 100°F","About 200°F"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the first number on the page.","explain":"Milk is heated to about 161°F for fifteen seconds, then quickly chilled. This kills harmful germs without cooking the milk."}}]},{"icon":"🧽","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Wash for twenty seconds. Chill the food. Heat the milk. Let a fever do its work."},{"t":"p","text":"Small habits, and your own body does the rest."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Ignaz Semmelweis","Made doctors wash their hands"],["Chlorine","What Semmelweis made doctors wash in"],["Soap molecule","Grips grease and water"],["Twenty seconds","Time to wash hands"],["Fever","Your own body turning up the temperature"],["Bacteria","Slowed down in a fridge"]]},"picgame":{"type":"tapall","title":"Germ Fighters","goal":"Tap what helps keep germs away.","items":[{"icon":"🧼","name":"soap","good":true},{"icon":"🚰","name":"hand washing","good":true},{"icon":"🧊","name":"cold fridge","good":true},{"icon":"🥛","name":"pasteurized milk","good":true},{"icon":"🌡️","name":"fever","good":true},{"icon":"🧸","name":"teddy bear","good":false},{"icon":"🎈","name":"balloon","good":false},{"icon":"🪁","name":"kite","good":false}]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"In 1847, what did Ignaz Semmelweis discover was killing new mothers in one hospital ward far more than another?","options":["Doctors weren't washing their hands after working with the dead","The midwives' ward had better equipment","One ward used dirtier bed linens"],"answer":0,"explain":"He was ignored and mocked at the time — germs hadn't been demonstrated yet, so nobody could see what he was talking about."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How does soap actually destroy germs, rather than \"poisoning\" them?","options":["One end of the soap molecule grips grease, the other grips water, lifting germs off and down the drain","It changes the pH of the skin so germs can't survive","It coats your skin in a protective barrier"],"answer":0,"explain":"A virus with a fatty coat simply falls apart when soap gets to it."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why does a fever actually happen when you get sick?","options":["Your own body raises its temperature on purpose, since most germs grow worse in heat","The germ itself raises your body temperature as a side effect","It's a sign your immune system has failed"],"answer":0,"explain":"A mild fever is a weapon your body is using, not damage being done."},{"type":"tf","prompt":"A refrigerator actually kills the bacteria in food, the same way cooking does.","answer":1,"explain":"A fridge only slows bacteria down — it takes days instead of hours for them to reach harmful numbers, and they start multiplying again once thawed."}]},{"id":"micro-scope","course":"micro","kind":"facts","section":"scope","title":"The Microscope","glyph":"🔬","n":6,"idea":"A drop of water can magnify print, just like a lens. Find out how a bead of glass and a beam of electrons each let us see smaller things.","pages":[{"icon":"💧","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A single drop of water can do something surprising: it becomes a lens."},{"t":"fact","text":"Hold a drop on a loop of wire over a printed page or put one on clear plastic, and it magnifies. The same principle is used in the glass bead that first showed anyone a bacterium."},{"t":"try","text":"Put a drop of water on clear plastic over some writing and see what happens."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does a drop of water do to print under it?","options":["It darkens it","It magnifies it","It blurs it"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It says it magnifies.","explain":"A drop of water magnifies, making the print bigger and clearer."}}]},{"icon":"🔬","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Van Leeuwenhoek's microscopes were not like the ones you might see today. They were small flat brass plates with one tiny glass bead in a hole."},{"t":"fact","text":"The glass bead was held right up against the eye. About a dozen of these original microscopes still exist and they still work."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What was the lens in Leeuwenhoek's microscope?","options":["One tiny glass bead","A small drop of water","A curved metal mirror"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It mentions one tiny glass bead.","explain":"The lens was just one tiny glass bead held right up against the eye."}}]},{"icon":"🚧","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Every microscope that uses light has a hard limit."},{"t":"fact","text":"About two ten-thousandths of a millimeter. Below that, things are smaller than the light waves themselves.","label":"Worked out"},{"t":"p","text":"No lens, however good, can help you see past this limit. That is why viruses stayed invisible until people stopped using light."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why could light microscopes never show a virus?","options":["A virus moves much too fast for lenses","Viruses are too heavy for lenses to focus on","A virus is smaller than the light waves"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It mentions that things below two ten-thousandths of a millimeter are smaller than the light waves.","explain":"Light waves have a limit. Viruses are smaller than those light waves, so they stayed invisible."}}]},{"icon":"⚡","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"An electron microscope does not use light. Instead, it fires electrons, which behave like much shorter waves."},{"t":"term","word":"electron microscope","text":"A microscope that uses electrons instead of light to see tiny things."},{"t":"fact","text":"The machine works in a vacuum, and everything has to be coated in metal first. This means nothing being looked at can be alive, so you can see a virus but never see it moving."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is the trade with an electron microscope?","options":["You can see a virus but only if it is dripping wet","You can see a virus but it is not clear","You can see a virus but only the biggest kinds","You can see a virus but never see it moving"],"answer":3,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It says that everything has to be coated in metal and nothing can be alive.","explain":"You get to see a virus, but you never get to see it moving because nothing seen is alive."}}]},{"icon":"🎨","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Most cells are clear, like a drop of water. So microscopists dye them so they show up."},{"t":"fact","text":"One old recipe, from Hans Christian Gram in 1884, splits bacteria into two groups based on whether the color washes out of their walls. This simple test still tells doctors which antibiotic to use."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why do microscopists dye cells?","options":["Most cells are transparent","They look prettier in colors","It makes them bigger"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the first sentence on the page.","explain":"Most cells are see-through, so dyes help them stand out under a microscope."}}]},{"icon":"🥣","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Before 1887, scientists grew tiny life forms on slices of potato. Not great, because potatoes rot."},{"t":"fact","text":"In 1887, Julius Petri, an assistant in Robert Koch's lab, invented the flat round dish with a loose lid."},{"t":"p","text":"The lid rests on top but does not seal. That way air gets in and dust stays out."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Who invented the Petri dish?","options":["Louis Pasteur, a chemist working in Paris","Julius Petri, an assistant in Koch's laboratory","Joseph Lister, a surgeon working in Edinburgh"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the fact box for the inventor's name.","explain":"Julius Petri came up with the dish while he was working in Koch's lab."}}]},{"icon":"🔭","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A drop of water. A bead of glass. A beam of electrons. A dye. A dish with a loose lid."},{"t":"p","text":"Each one let someone see a little more of the tiny world."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Water drop","Acts as a magnifying lens"],["Van Leeuwenhoek","Used tiny glass bead lenses"],["Electron microscope","Uses electrons instead of light"],["Petri dish","Flat round dish with loose lid"]]},"picgame":{"type":"tapall","title":"Tiny World Tools","goal":"Tap what helps us see tiny things.","items":[{"icon":"💧","name":"water drop","good":true},{"icon":"🔬","name":"microscope","good":true},{"icon":"🎨","name":"dye","good":true},{"icon":"🧫","name":"petri dish","good":true},{"icon":"🧸","name":"teddy bear","good":false},{"icon":"🍎","name":"apple","good":false},{"icon":"🚲","name":"bicycle","good":false},{"icon":"🪨","name":"rock","good":false}]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why couldn't viruses be seen by anyone until the 1930s, no matter how good their microscope was?","options":["Any light microscope has a hard size limit, since objects smaller than light waves can't be seen that way","Nobody had thought to look for anything that small before","Viruses used to be much larger than they are today"],"answer":0,"explain":"That limit comes from the behavior of light itself, not from trying better lenses — it's why the electron microscope was needed."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What's the trade-off of using an electron microscope to see something as small as a virus?","options":["It works in a vacuum, so nothing being looked at can be alive or moving","It can only see things that are already dead and decayed","It only works on metal objects"],"answer":0,"explain":"You get to see the virus, but you never get to see it moving."},{"type":"tf","prompt":"A single drop of water, held up over a printed page, actually works as a simple magnifying lens.","answer":0,"explain":"It works on the same principle as the tiny glass bead Van Leeuwenhoek used to first see a bacterium."}]},{"id":"micro-algae","course":"micro","kind":"facts","section":"algae","title":"Algae & Plankton","glyph":"🌿","n":6,"idea":"Some algae are giant seaweeds, and the tiny ones called phytoplankton make at least half of the oxygen.","pages":[{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Algae can be microscopic one-celled microalgae or giant seaweeds."},{"t":"term","word":"seaweed","text":"The giant kind of algae, which may grow up to 160 ft long."},{"t":"fact","text":"Some seaweeds may grow up to 160 ft (50 m) long."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How long can giant seaweeds grow?","options":["Only 16 ft","Up to 160 ft","Nearly 1,600 ft"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. The answer is there.","explain":"Some seaweeds may grow up to 160 ft (50 m) long."}}]},{"icon":"🏡","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Most algae live in water, especially in the sea."},{"t":"fact","text":"Most are plankton, carried along by the water, though some big seaweeds hold themselves in place with holdfasts."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where do most algae live?","options":["On dry sand","In water","In tall trees"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"Most algae live in water, especially in the sea."}}]},{"icon":"⚙️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Phytoplankton get their energy from sunlight, by photosynthesis."},{"t":"fact","text":"Trees and other plants do the same on land. So they live in the sunlit surface of oceans and lakes."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where do phytoplankton live?","options":["The dark floor of the deepest caves","The sunlit surface of oceans and lakes","The dry soil under the trees"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. The answer is there.","explain":"So they live in the sunlit surface of oceans and lakes."}}]},{"icon":"📏","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Phytoplankton are only about 1% of all the plant material on Earth."},{"t":"fact","text":"Yet they do about half of all photosynthesis and make at least half of the oxygen."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How much of Earth's oxygen do phytoplankton make?","options":["About 1%","At least half","Almost none"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. The answer is there.","explain":"Yet they do about half of all photosynthesis and make at least half of the oxygen."}}]},{"icon":"📐","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"About 12,000 species of diatoms have been documented."},{"t":"fact","text":"Diatoms are phytoplankton, the most common kind of plankton."}]},{"icon":"⚖️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Diatoms are tiny, generally 20 to 200 micrometers across."},{"t":"fact","text":"They form huge blooms each spring and fall, and make about half of the photosynthesis in the oceans."}]},{"icon":"🔎","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A giant seaweed. Algae in the sea. Phytoplankton that use sunlight. Half of the oxygen. Diatoms in blooms."},{"t":"p","text":"Algae are in the water."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Seaweed","May grow up to 160 ft"],["Plankton","Carried along by the water"],["Phytoplankton","Get their energy from sunlight"],["Diatom","Most common kind of plankton"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What do some big seaweeds use to hold themselves in place?","options":["Branches","Holdfasts","Pebbles"],"answer":1,"explain":"Some big seaweeds hold themselves in place with holdfasts."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"About how many species of diatoms have been documented?","options":["About 120","About 12,000","About 1,200,000"],"answer":1,"explain":"About 12,000 species of diatoms have been documented."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"When do diatoms form huge blooms?","options":["Only on cold nights","Each spring and fall","Just once in a century"],"answer":1,"explain":"They form huge blooms each spring and fall."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How much of the plant material on Earth are phytoplankton?","options":["About half","About 1%","Almost all"],"answer":1,"explain":"Phytoplankton are only about 1% of all the plant material on Earth."}]},{"id":"micro-food","course":"micro","kind":"facts","section":"food","title":"Microbes in Food","glyph":"🧀","n":6,"idea":"Tiny yeasts and bacteria help make bread rise and keep sour foods like yogurt from spoiling.","pages":[{"icon":"🧀","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Fermentation is a change made by tiny yeasts or bacteria."},{"t":"term","word":"fermentation","text":"A change made when yeasts or bacteria turn sugars into acids."},{"t":"fact","text":"They turn sugars and starches into acids and other chemicals."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What causes fermentation?","options":["Bright light from the sun","Yeasts or bacteria","Cold wind off the sea"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"Fermentation is a change made by tiny yeasts or bacteria."}}]},{"icon":"⚙️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Yeast makes carbon dioxide gas that makes bread rise."},{"t":"fact","text":"Bacteria make lactic acid that helps keep sour foods like sauerkraut and yogurt from spoiling."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What makes bread rise?","options":["Lactic acid and sugar","Carbon dioxide gas","Cold water and flour"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"Yeast makes carbon dioxide gas that makes bread rise."}}]},{"icon":"🧑","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Louis Pasteur was a French chemist."},{"t":"fact","text":"In 1856, he showed that yeast is connected to fermentation."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Who connected yeast to fermentation in 1856?","options":["Edward Jenner","Louis Pasteur","Robert Hooke"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"Louis Pasteur was a French chemist."}}]},{"icon":"🍽️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Cheese is made by curdling milk, usually from cows, goats or sheep."},{"t":"fact","text":"Over a thousand types of cheese exist."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What do you curdle to make cheese?","options":["Flour","Milk","Water"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"Cheese is made by curdling milk, usually from cows, goats or sheep."}}]},{"icon":"🍎","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Yogurt is most often made from cow's milk."},{"t":"fact","text":"Milk from water buffalo, goats, ewes, mares, camels and yaks can be used too."}]},{"icon":"📅","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Sourdough bread was carried into Alaska and the Yukon in Canada."},{"t":"fact","text":"It went there during the Klondike Gold Rush of 1898."}]},{"icon":"🔎","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Yeasts that make bread rise. Bacteria that keep yogurt from spoiling. Cheese from milk. Bread in the Gold Rush."},{"t":"p","text":"Tiny life makes food."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Yeast","Makes bread rise"],["Bacteria","Make lactic acid"],["Fermentation","A change made by tiny yeasts"],["Cheese","Made by curdling milk"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What gas does yeast make that makes bread rise?","options":["Pure oxygen","Carbon dioxide","Plain helium"],"answer":1,"explain":"Yeast makes carbon dioxide gas that makes bread rise."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What do bacteria make that helps keep yogurt from spoiling?","options":["Carbon dioxide","Lactic acid","Cold water"],"answer":1,"explain":"Bacteria make lactic acid that helps keep sour foods like yogurt from spoiling."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"In what year did Louis Pasteur show that yeast is connected to fermentation?","options":["1658","1856","1865"],"answer":1,"explain":"In 1856, Louis Pasteur showed that yeast is connected to fermentation."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where was sourdough bread carried during the Klondike Gold Rush?","options":["Texas and New Mexico","Alaska and the Yukon","Mexico and Peru"],"answer":1,"explain":"Sourdough bread was carried into Alaska and the Yukon in Canada."}]},{"id":"micro-hunters","course":"micro","kind":"facts","section":"hunters","title":"Microbe Hunters","glyph":"🔬","n":6,"idea":"Meet the people who first looked at microbes, and the ones who found ways to fight them.","pages":[{"icon":"🔬","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Antonie van Leeuwenhoek made his own microscopes, each with a single lens."},{"t":"term","word":"microscope","text":"A tool with a lens, like the single lens Leeuwenhoek used."},{"t":"fact","text":"He was the first person to observe and experiment with microbes."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Who was the first person to observe microbes?","options":["Christopher Columbus","Antonie van Leeuwenhoek","Alexander Graham Bell"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"Antonie van Leeuwenhoek made his own microscopes, each with a single lens."}}]},{"icon":"🧑","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Antonie van Leeuwenhoek wrote some 190 letters to the Royal Society about his findings."},{"t":"fact","text":"He wrote them in Dutch."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many letters did Van Leeuwenhoek write to the Royal Society?","options":["About 19","About 190","About 1,900"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"Antonie van Leeuwenhoek wrote some 190 letters to the Royal Society about his findings."}}]},{"icon":"👤","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"In 1665, Robert Hooke was one of the first scientists to study living things at microscopic scale."},{"t":"fact","text":"He used a compound microscope that he designed."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Who designed a compound microscope in 1665?","options":["Louis Pasteur","Robert Hooke","Isaac Newton"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"In 1665, Robert Hooke was one of the first scientists to study living things at microscopic scale."}}]},{"icon":"👑","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Louis Pasteur invented a way to treat milk so that bacteria cannot spoil it."},{"t":"fact","text":"The process is now called pasteurization."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is the process Pasteur invented called?","options":["Fermentation","Pasteurization","Evaporation"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. The answer is there.","explain":"The process is now called pasteurization."}}]},{"icon":"🧑‍🔬","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Louis Pasteur's work is credited with saving millions of lives."},{"t":"fact","text":"It saved lives through vaccines for rabies and anthrax."}]},{"icon":"🔎","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"In 1928, Alexander Fleming found penicillin in a mold."},{"t":"fact","text":"It has been called the greatest victory ever won over disease."}]},{"icon":"🎈","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A single lens. Letters in Dutch. A compound microscope. Milk made safe. Vaccines. A mold with penicillin."},{"t":"p","text":"Microbes were found."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Leeuwenhoek","First person to observe microbes"],["Hooke","Designed a compound microscope"],["Pasteur","Invented a way to treat milk"],["Fleming","Found penicillin in a mold"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What was in each of Leeuwenhoek's microscopes?","options":["Two mirrors","A single lens","A tiny flame"],"answer":1,"explain":"Leeuwenhoek made his own microscopes, each with a single lens."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What language did Leeuwenhoek write his letters in?","options":["French","Dutch","Latin"],"answer":1,"explain":"He wrote them in Dutch."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did Pasteur invent a way to treat so that bacteria could not spoil it?","options":["Bread","Milk","Water"],"answer":1,"explain":"Louis Pasteur invented a way to treat milk so that bacteria cannot spoil it."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"In what year did Alexander Fleming find penicillin in a mold?","options":["1298","1928","1982"],"answer":1,"explain":"In 1928, Alexander Fleming found penicillin in a mold."}]},{"id":"micro-tiny","course":"micro","kind":"facts","section":"tiny","title":"Tiny Animals","glyph":"🐛","n":6,"idea":"Meet tiny animals that live in the sea, in fresh water and even in the dust in homes.","pages":[{"icon":"🐛","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Tardigrades are usually about 0.02 in (0.5 mm) long when fully grown."},{"t":"term","word":"tardigrade","text":"The tiny animal usually about 0.02 in long when fully grown."},{"t":"fact","text":"Most range from 0.002 to 0.02 in (0.05 to 0.5 mm)."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is the length of a fully grown tardigrade?","options":["About 0.2 in","About 0.02 in","About 2 in"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"Tardigrades are usually about 0.02 in (0.5 mm) long when fully grown."}}]},{"icon":"🧑","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"In 1773, a German scientist called them 'little water bears'."},{"t":"fact","text":"In 1776, an Italian scientist named them Tardigrada, which means slow walkers."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does the name Tardigrada mean?","options":["Tiny swimmers","Slow walkers","Fast runners"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. The answer is there.","explain":"In 1776, an Italian scientist named them Tardigrada, which means slow walkers."}}]},{"icon":"🏡","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"About 1,500 species of tardigrades are known."},{"t":"fact","text":"They live on land, in fresh water and in the sea."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"About how many species of tardigrades are known?","options":["150","1,500","15,000"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"About 1,500 species of tardigrades are known."}}]},{"icon":"📏","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Now meet the house dust mite."},{"t":"fact","text":"A typical one is only about 0.01 in long."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is the length of a typical house dust mite?","options":["About 0.1 in","About 0.01 in","About 1 in"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. The answer is there.","explain":"A typical one is only about 0.01 in long."}}]},{"icon":"🌍","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Dust mites live in the dust in homes."},{"t":"fact","text":"In a survey, dust mite allergen was found in the beds of about 84% of U.S. homes."}]},{"icon":"👤","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Rotifers are tiny animals with many cells."},{"t":"fact","text":"Scientists did not recognize them as animals until 1838, and now about 2,200 species of rotifers are known."}]},{"icon":"🔎","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A tiny water bear. A mite in house dust. A rotifer with many cells."},{"t":"p","text":"Tiny animals are all around."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Tardigrade","Called little water bears"],["Tardigrada","Means slow walkers"],["Dust mite","Lives in the dust in homes"],["Rotifer","Tiny animal with many cells"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"In what year did a German scientist call them 'little water bears'?","options":["1377","1773","1737"],"answer":1,"explain":"In 1773, a German scientist called them 'little water bears'."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many species of rotifers are known now?","options":["About 22","About 2,200","About 220,000"],"answer":1,"explain":"Now about 2,200 species of rotifers are known."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did scientists not recognize rotifers as until 1838?","options":["Plants","Animals","Rocks"],"answer":1,"explain":"Scientists did not recognize them as animals until 1838."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What share of U.S. homes had dust mite allergen in the beds in a survey?","options":["About 8%","About 84%","About 48%"],"answer":1,"explain":"Dust mite allergen was found in the beds of about 84% of U.S. homes."}]},{"id":"micro-water","course":"micro","kind":"facts","section":"water","title":"Clean Water","glyph":"🚰","n":6,"idea":"A doctor followed a sickness back to one water pump, and that changed how cities handle water.","pages":[{"icon":"🚰","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Dr. John Snow traced a cholera outbreak in London's Soho back to one public water pump."},{"t":"term","word":"cholera","text":"The sickness Dr. Snow traced back to one public water pump."},{"t":"fact","text":"His work helped start the study of how diseases spread."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did John Snow trace the cholera outbreak to?","options":["One crowded city street","One public water pump","One busy fish market"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"John Snow traced a cholera outbreak in London's Soho back to one public water pump."}}]},{"icon":"🧑","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Snow's findings changed how London handled its water and waste."},{"t":"fact","text":"Other cities made the same changes, and public health improved around the world."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which city changed its water and waste systems because of Snow?","options":["Paris","London","Rome"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"Snow's findings changed how London handled its water and waste."}}]},{"icon":"⚙️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The first step in cleaning surface water is to take out big things like sticks, leaves and rubbish."},{"t":"fact","text":"They would get in the way of the later steps."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is the first step in cleaning surface water?","options":["Heating all of the water until it is boiling","Taking out big things like sticks and leaves","Adding a lot of salt to the water supply"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"The first step in cleaning surface water is to take out big things like sticks, leaves and rubbish."}}]},{"icon":"🔧","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"People clean water in many ways."},{"t":"fact","text":"They use filters, slow sand filters, chlorine, and even ultraviolet light."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What kind of light can clean water?","options":["Bright candle light","Ultraviolet light","Warm yellow light"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. The answer is there.","explain":"They use filters, slow sand filters, chlorine, and even ultraviolet light."}}]},{"icon":"🏠","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Chlorine is a common disinfectant."},{"t":"term","word":"disinfectant","text":"Something used in swimming pools to keep them sanitary."},{"t":"fact","text":"It is used in swimming pools to keep them sanitary."}]},{"icon":"🧩","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Boiling or a home filter is not always enough."},{"t":"fact","text":"Water from an unknown place can hold things that they do not remove."}]},{"icon":"🔎","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A pump in London. A big step with sticks and leaves. Filters, chlorine and ultraviolet light."},{"t":"p","text":"Clean water takes care."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Snow","Traced an outbreak in London"],["Cholera","Traced back to one public pump"],["Chlorine","A common disinfectant"],["Filters","Clean water in many ways"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What kind of sickness did Dr. John Snow trace to a water pump?","options":["Smallpox","Cholera","Rabies"],"answer":1,"explain":"Dr. John Snow traced a cholera outbreak to one public water pump."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is chlorine used in swimming pools to do?","options":["Make the water look blue","Keep them sanitary","Keep the water warm all year"],"answer":1,"explain":"Chlorine is used in swimming pools to keep them sanitary."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did Snow's work help start the study of?","options":["How rivers are formed","How diseases spread","How clouds make rain"],"answer":1,"explain":"His work helped start the study of how diseases spread."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Is boiling or a home filter always enough for water from an unknown place?","options":["Yes, always enough","No, not always","Yes, in most homes"],"answer":1,"explain":"Boiling or a home filter is not always enough."}]},{"id":"micro-extremes","course":"micro","kind":"facts","section":"extremes","title":"Microbes Everywhere","glyph":"🌋","n":6,"idea":"Microbes live where it is far too hot for you, deep below the sea floor and in hot springs.","pages":[{"icon":"🌋","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Microbes have been found below the sea floor."},{"t":"fact","text":"They were in sediment as hot as 248°F (120°C), 0.75 mi (1.2 km) down."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How hot was the sediment where microbes were found?","options":["128°F","248°F","842°F"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. The answer is there.","explain":"They were in sediment as hot as 248°F (120°C), 0.75 mi (1.2 km) down."}}]},{"icon":"🏡","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Some microbes thrive inside rocks."},{"t":"fact","text":"They have been found up to 1,900 ft (580 m) below the sea floor, under 8,500 ft (2,600 m) of ocean."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How far below the sea floor were microbes found inside rocks?","options":["190 ft","1,900 ft","19,000 ft"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. The answer is there.","explain":"They have been found up to 1,900 ft (580 m) below the sea floor, under 8,500 ft (2,600 m) of ocean."}}]},{"icon":"🤔","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Spores from a kind of Bacillus bacteria were still alive after being heated."},{"t":"term","word":"spore","text":"A tough form of bacteria that stays alive after being heated."},{"t":"fact","text":"They were heated to 788°F (420°C)."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How hot were the Bacillus spores heated?","options":["478°F","788°F","88°F"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. The answer is there.","explain":"They were heated to 788°F (420°C)."}}]},{"icon":"📅","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"In the 1960s, scientists thought bacteria could not live above about 131°F (55°C)."},{"t":"fact","text":"Soon they found many bacteria that survived and even thrived at higher temperatures."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What temperature did scientists in the 1960s think bacteria could not live above?","options":["311°F","131°F","113°F"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"In the 1960s, scientists thought bacteria could not live above about 131°F (55°C)."}}]},{"icon":"🔧","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A hot-spring bacterium called Thermus aquaticus makes an enzyme that handles heat."},{"t":"fact","text":"Scientists use it in PCR, a way to copy DNA."}]},{"icon":"😮","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Some microbes, called halophiles, grow best in very salty water."},{"t":"fact","text":"That means 50 grams of salt per liter or more. A coral reef has about 35 grams per liter."}]},{"icon":"🔎","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Microbes below the sea floor. Spores still alive after heat. Bacteria in hot springs. Microbes that like salt."},{"t":"p","text":"Microbes live in many places."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Microbes","Found below the sea floor"],["Spores","Alive after heating to 788°F"],["Thermus","Makes an enzyme that handles heat"],["Halophiles","Grow best in very salty water"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What distance below the sea floor were microbes found in the hot sediment?","options":["7.5 mi","0.75 mi","0.075 mi"],"answer":1,"explain":"Microbes have been found in sediment 0.75 mi (1.2 km) below the sea floor."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does Thermus aquaticus make that handles heat?","options":["A mineral","An enzyme","A pigment"],"answer":1,"explain":"A hot-spring bacterium called Thermus aquaticus makes an enzyme that handles heat."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is PCR a way to do?","options":["Clean water","Copy DNA","Make bread"],"answer":1,"explain":"Scientists use it in PCR, a way to copy DNA."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which kind of water do halophiles grow best in?","options":["Very cold water","Very salty water","Very clean water"],"answer":1,"explain":"Some microbes, called halophiles, grow best in very salty water."}]},{"id":"micro-vaccines","course":"micro","kind":"facts","section":"vaccines","title":"Vaccines & Medicine","glyph":"💉","n":6,"idea":"A vaccine trains the body to spot a germ, and the first one was made against smallpox.","pages":[{"icon":"💉","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Edward Jenner was an English doctor."},{"t":"fact","text":"He created the smallpox vaccine. It was the world's first vaccine."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What was the world's first vaccine for?","options":["Measles","Smallpox","Cholera"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. The answer is there.","explain":"He created the smallpox vaccine."}}]},{"icon":"📏","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"In Jenner's time, smallpox killed around 10% of the world's people."},{"t":"fact","text":"In towns and cities, it killed as many as 20%."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What share of the world's people did smallpox kill in Jenner's time?","options":["Around 1%","Around 10%","Around 50%"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"In Jenner's time, smallpox killed around 10% of the world's people."}}]},{"icon":"⚙️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A vaccine trains the body's immune system to see a germ as a threat."},{"t":"term","word":"vaccine","text":"Something that trains the body's immune system to see a germ as a threat."},{"t":"fact","text":"Then the body can destroy that germ if it meets it later."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does a vaccine train?","options":["The heart muscles","The immune system","The leg bones"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"A vaccine trains the body's immune system to see a germ as a threat."}}]},{"icon":"📅","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Edward Jenner wrote about cowpox in 1798."},{"t":"fact","text":"He wrote about its protective effect against smallpox."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What protected people from smallpox in Jenner's study?","options":["Measles","Cowpox","Chickenpox"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"Edward Jenner wrote about cowpox in 1798."}}]},{"icon":"🧑","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Penicillin was one of the first medicines that worked against many bacterial infections."},{"t":"fact","text":"In 1945, three men shared a Nobel Prize for it."}]},{"icon":"👤","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The word antibiotic was first used in 1942 by Selman Waksman and his team."},{"t":"fact","text":"They used it for any substance made by a microorganism that works against the growth of other microorganisms."}]},{"icon":"🔎","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A doctor and a first vaccine. Smallpox in towns and cities. A body that learns to see a germ. Cowpox. Penicillin and a Nobel Prize."},{"t":"p","text":"Medicine began."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Jenner","Created the smallpox vaccine"],["Vaccine","Trains the immune system"],["Cowpox","Protected people from smallpox"],["Penicillin","One of the first medicines"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did Edward Jenner create?","options":["A strong new microscope","The smallpox vaccine","The first steam engine"],"answer":1,"explain":"Edward Jenner created the smallpox vaccine."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How much of the world's people did smallpox kill, at most, in towns and cities?","options":["As many as 2%","As many as 20%","As many as 90%"],"answer":1,"explain":"In towns and cities, it killed as many as 20%."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"In 1945, what did three men share for penicillin?","options":["A gold medal","A Nobel Prize","A large trophy"],"answer":1,"explain":"In 1945, three men shared a Nobel Prize for it."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Who first used the word antibiotic in 1942?","options":["Edward Jenner and his team","Selman Waksman and his team","Louis Pasteur and his team"],"answer":1,"explain":"The word antibiotic was first used in 1942 by Selman Waksman and his team."}]},{"id":"ancient-beginning","course":"ancient","kind":"facts","section":"beginning","title":"The Beginning","glyph":"🌍","n":6,"idea":"The oldest piece of the Bible fits in your palm. Find out how two different totals for the age of creation come from the same lists in Genesis, and why they differ.","pages":[{"icon":"🥈","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The oldest piece of the Bible is small enough to fit in your palm. In 1979, two silver strips were found in a burial cave at Ketef Hinnom in Jerusalem."},{"t":"fact","text":"These silver strips have the priestly blessing from Numbers 6 scratched into the metal."},{"t":"p","text":"They were so fragile that it took three years to figure out how to unroll them without destroying them. Today, they are in the Israel Museum."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is scratched into the silver strips?","options":["The priestly blessing from Numbers 6","The Ten Commandments from Exodus 20","A song of praise from the Psalms"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It names what is scratched into the metal.","explain":"The strips carry the priestly blessing from Numbers 6, carefully etched into the silver."}}]},{"icon":"📚","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The oldest complete Hebrew Bible that still exists is called the Leningrad Codex."},{"t":"fact","text":"It was copied by hand in AD 1008 and is kept in the Russian National Library in St Petersburg today."},{"t":"p","text":"The scribe signed and dated the book at the back, which is how we know it was copied in AD 1008. An even older one, the Aleppo Codex, dates from around AD 930, but parts of it are missing now."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How is the year of the Leningrad Codex known?","options":["A dated coin was found tucked inside it","Scientists tested the ink in a lab","The scribe signed and dated the book"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. It says where the date came from.","explain":"The scribe signed and dated the book at the back, which is how we know it was copied in AD 1008."}}]},{"icon":"📜","blocks":[{"t":"term","word":"genealogy","text":"A family list that names each man and his son, one generation after another."},{"t":"fact","text":"In Genesis 5, ten men from Adam to Noah are listed. For each one, it gives the age he was when his son was born and how long he lived after. Genesis 11 does the same thing again from Noah's son Shem down to Abraham."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does the list give for each man?","options":["The village where he was born and how much land he owned","His age when his son was born and how long he lived afterward","The names of his brothers and what jobs they each had"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It lists exactly what is given for each man.","explain":"Each entry gives two pieces of information: the age of the father when his son was born and how long he lived afterward."}}]},{"icon":"🧮","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"In 1650, an Irish archbishop named James Ussher added up all the ages in Genesis 5 and 11."},{"t":"fact","text":"He said creation happened on the night before October 23rd, 4004 BC. That date is arithmetic, not a verse. Nowhere does the Bible say 4004 BC.","label":"Worked out"},{"t":"p","text":"Ussher anchored his total to a known Babylonian king's death and a dated eclipse."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Is the date 4004 BC written in the Bible?","options":["Yes, it is written in a verse of Genesis","No, it is arithmetic done on the lists","Only in a note added by early scribes"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It states that 4004 BC is arithmetic, not a verse.","explain":"The date 4004 BC comes from adding up the ages listed in Genesis, not from any verse that says exactly that number."}}]},{"icon":"⚖️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The old copies of Genesis do not all carry the same numbers in their genealogies."},{"t":"fact","text":"The Greek Septuagint, an ancient translation, gives longer gaps between generations. Add those up and creation lands nearer 5500 BC, about 1,500 years earlier than Ussher's 4004 BC."},{"t":"p","text":"Both totals are honest arithmetic on a written list. They differ because the old copies carry different numbers in the lists."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why do the two totals for creation differ?","options":["The old copies carry different numbers in the lists","One list counts in years, the other in months","Water damage wiped out some numbers in one list"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It mentions that the Greek Septuagint gives longer gaps.","explain":"The difference comes from the old copies carrying different numbers in their genealogies."}}]},{"icon":"🌅","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The beginning of Genesis tells about six days of making and a seventh of rest."},{"t":"fact","text":"Each day ends with an evening and a morning. Light, sky, sea and land, plants, sun and moon and stars, fish and birds, land animals and people. It is a written record you can read.","label":"The written account says"},{"t":"p","text":"Open it and you can check for yourself. A written record is a different kind of thing from a measurement or a calculation."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["James Ussher","Added up ages to get 4004 BC"],["Greek Septuagint","Has longer gaps between generations"],["Leningrad Codex","Oldest complete Hebrew Bible kept in Russia"],["Ketef Hinnom","Burial cave where silver strips were found"]]},"picgame":{"type":"catch","title":"First Week","goal":"Catch what was made in the first week.","items":[{"icon":"☀️","name":"sun","good":true},{"icon":"🌙","name":"moon","good":true},{"icon":"🌳","name":"tree","good":true},{"icon":"🐟","name":"fish","good":true},{"icon":"🐦","name":"bird","good":true},{"icon":"🚗","name":"car","good":false},{"icon":"📱","name":"phone","good":false},{"icon":"🚀","name":"rocket","good":false}]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"How did Archbishop James Ussher arrive at his famous 4004 BC date for creation?","options":["He added up the ages given in the Genesis family lists","The date is stated directly in the Bible","He used carbon dating on ancient artifacts"],"answer":0,"explain":"Nowhere does the Bible state 4004 BC directly — that number is arithmetic Ussher did on the genealogies, published in 1650."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why do some people land on a creation date nearer 5500 BC instead of Ussher's 4004 BC?","options":["Older copies of Genesis, like the Greek Septuagint, give longer gaps in the same family lists","They used a completely different method than adding genealogies","Scientists have disproven Ussher's calculation"],"answer":0,"explain":"Both totals are honest arithmetic on a written list — they differ because the lists themselves differ."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What makes the Genesis 5 and 11 genealogies unusual compared to most ancient writing?","options":["They actually record how many years passed between one person and the next","They list far more people than other ancient texts","They were written in a unique script"],"answer":0,"explain":"That detail is exactly why building a timeline from them is possible at all — most ancient writing doesn't give that information."},{"type":"tf","prompt":"The oldest known fragment of Bible text is a full scroll, carefully preserved on parchment.","answer":1,"explain":"It's actually two tiny rolled silver strips, small enough to fit in your palm, found in a Jerusalem burial cave."}]},{"id":"ancient-flood","course":"ancient","kind":"facts","section":"flood","title":"Flood & Babel","glyph":"🌊","n":6,"idea":"A boat 450 feet long, a broken clay tablet that tells of a flood, and a tower made of brick. Find out how languages sort into families, and what nobody has managed to join together.","pages":[{"icon":"🚢","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The written account gives us some exact numbers for the Ark. It was 300 cubits long, 50 cubits wide, and 30 cubits high."},{"t":"fact","text":"A cubit is about 18 inches, so that works out to roughly 450 feet long, 75 feet wide, and 45 feet high. That is longer than a football field's 100 yards."},{"t":"try","text":"Take a tape measure outside and mark out 450 feet. Walk from one end to the other and count your steps. That is how long the Ark was."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"About how long was the Ark in feet?","options":["About 450 feet","About 100 feet","About 200 feet"],"answer":0,"hint":"The numbers are in the fact box above.","explain":"The Ark was about 450 feet long. That's longer than a football field."}}]},{"icon":"🏺","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"In the British Museum, there is a broken lump of clay with marks all over it. It tells of a great flood, a boat, and birds sent out to find land."},{"t":"fact","text":"This tablet was dug up at Nineveh and is called the Flood Tablet. It is part of a Babylonian poem called the Epic of Gilgamesh."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does the Flood Tablet tell about?","options":["A long war, a walled city, and a king who sought gold","A great flood, a boat, and birds sent out to find land","A hungry lion, a mountain, and a hero sent to hunt it"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the first sentence on the page.","explain":"The Flood Tablet tells about a great flood, a boat, and birds sent out to find land."}}]},{"icon":"😲","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"In 1872, George Smith was sorting tablets at the British Museum that nobody had read."},{"t":"fact","text":"One day, he made out the flood lines on one of them and got so excited that he jumped up and started taking his clothes off."},{"t":"p","text":"After that, he went to Iraq and found the missing piece of the tablet. That almost never happens."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Who made out the flood lines on the tablet?","options":["A colleague at the museum","The curator","George Smith"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the first sentence in the fact box.","explain":"George Smith made out the flood lines on the tablet and got very excited."}}]},{"icon":"🌍","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Flood stories turn up among peoples all over the world."},{"t":"fact","text":"Many of those peoples had no contact with each other. The list includes Babylon, Greece, India, China, and people across the Americas and the Pacific.","label":"Worked out, not seen directly"},{"t":"p","text":"What this means is argued about. Some say it points back to one real event remembered everywhere; others say big floods happen everywhere and so do stories about them."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is true of the peoples who have flood accounts?","options":["They all lived in the same place","They all wrote their stories at the same time","Many of them had no contact with each other"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the fact box to see which peoples are listed.","explain":"Many of those peoples had no contact with each other, yet they still have flood stories."}}]},{"icon":"🧱","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"After the flood, Genesis 11 tells us that everyone in the world spoke the same language."},{"t":"fact","text":"At Babel, after people tried to build a tower, they could not understand one another and scattered across the earth."},{"t":"p","text":"The written account says the tower was made of brick and tar, not stone. Southern Iraq, where this happened, has almost no building stone. Everything there was built of mud brick."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What was the tower of Babel built of?","options":["Stone blocks","Brick and tar","Wooden planks"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the second paragraph. It says what the tower was made of.","explain":"The tower was made of brick and tar because southern Iraq has almost no building stone."}}]},{"icon":"🗣️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Languages are like big families. Some languages share so many words that they are grouped together."},{"t":"term","word":"language family","text":"A group of languages that share a lot of words and patterns."},{"t":"fact","text":"English, Greek, Hindi, and Farsi share words and endings, making them one family. Hebrew and Arabic form another separate family. Nobody has been able to show that these families, and the many others, all trace back to one root language."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Has anyone linked all the language families to one root?","options":["No","Yes, Latin","Yes, Ancient Egyptian"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the fact on the page to see what it says about linking families.","explain":"Nobody has shown that all language families come from one root. They are grouped based on shared words and patterns."}}]},{"icon":"🌈","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A boat 450 feet long. A clay tablet from Nineveh. A tower of brick and tar. Languages sorted into families."},{"t":"p","text":"All of it written down, and all of it something you can go and read."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Ark","A big boat from the flood"],["Flood Tablet","Clay tablet with a flood story"],["George Smith","The man who discovered the Flood Tablet"],["Babel","City where languages mixed"],["Brick and tar","Materials used to build the tower"],["Language family","Group of languages that share words and patterns"]]},"picgame":{"type":"catch","title":"Flood Tablet","goal":"Catch what the old clay tablet tells about.","items":[{"icon":"🌊","name":"flood","good":true},{"icon":"🚢","name":"boat","good":true},{"icon":"🐦","name":"bird","good":true},{"icon":"🚲","name":"bike","good":false},{"icon":"🍕","name":"pizza","good":false},{"icon":"🎸","name":"guitar","good":false},{"icon":"🎮","name":"game","good":false},{"icon":"🚀","name":"rocket","good":false}]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is the \"Flood Tablet\" in the British Museum, and how is its flood story different from Genesis?","options":["A Babylonian account with different names, gods, and boat details, but clearly about the same kind of event","An exact copy of the Genesis flood account in a different language","A tablet that disproves the Genesis flood story"],"answer":0,"explain":"It's part of the Babylonian Epic of Gilgamesh, dug up at Nineveh."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What's one specific detail in the Genesis account of the Tower of Babel that matches what we know about ancient southern Iraq?","options":["The tower was described as built of brick and tar, since that region has almost no building stone","The tower's exact height is given in cubits","The language spoken there is named directly"],"answer":0,"explain":"That detail about brick only matters if you know the region — and it checks out."},{"type":"tf","prompt":"Flood stories only appear in the written traditions of cultures that had contact with each other near the Middle East.","answer":1,"explain":"They turn up in Babylon, Greece, India, China, and peoples across the Americas and Pacific — many of whom had no contact with each other."}]},{"id":"ancient-egypt","course":"ancient","kind":"facts","section":"egypt","title":"Egypt","glyph":"🏺","n":6,"idea":"Ancient Egypt built massive pyramids and used careful methods to make mummies. Discover how a stone slab helped decipher their writing.","pages":[{"icon":"🔺","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The Great Pyramid is a giant stone building, and the surprise is not how big it is but how exact."},{"t":"fact","text":"The base covers 13 acres, and its four sides differ in length by less than 8 inches. The ground it sits on was leveled to within about an inch across the whole area.","label":"How exact it is"},{"t":"p","text":"It was built about 481 feet high, but it has lost about 26 feet off the top over time, so it stands about 455 feet today."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How much do the four sides of the Great Pyramid differ in length?","options":["More than 80 inches","Less than 8 inches","Nearly 20 inches"],"answer":1,"hint":"The fact box says the difference is less than 8 inches.","explain":"The four sides are incredibly precise, differing by less than 8 inches."}}]},{"icon":"🧱","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"You may have heard that the Great Pyramid is made up of 2.3 million blocks."},{"t":"fact","text":"Nobody has actually counted them. The number comes from measuring the outside volume and dividing it by the average size of the blocks you can see.","label":"Worked out, not counted"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How was the 2.3 million blocks figure found?","options":["By reading the number written in an old record by the builders","By adding up the loads that boats brought down the river","By working out the volume and dividing by the size of the blocks"],"answer":2,"hint":"The fact box explains how the volume is used to estimate the number.","explain":"The figure comes from the outside volume divided by the average block size."}}]},{"icon":"📅","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"You may wonder how we decide when the Great Pyramid was built."},{"t":"fact","text":"Most books say about 2560 BC. This date comes from Egyptian king-lists copied centuries later and lined up with carbon dates from mortar samples.","label":"Worked out, not directly seen"},{"t":"p","text":"Nothing on the pyramid gives its date in our calendar. The workers' marks name the king, but the year in BC is worked out, not read off any stone."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Does the Great Pyramid itself give its date?","options":["No, nothing on it gives a year","Yes, it has a plaque inside","A date in BC is carved over the door"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the fact box and see how the date was determined.","explain":"The pyramid does not have any markings that give a specific year. The date is worked out from other sources."}}]},{"icon":"🪨","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"In a room of the British Museum stands a dark stone slab with writing on it."},{"t":"fact","text":"The Rosetta Stone has the same decree cut into it three times, each in a different script. French soldiers found it dug into a wall at Rashid in 1799."},{"t":"p","text":"One of those scripts was Greek, which people could still read. This made the stone the key to understanding hieroglyphs."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why was the Rosetta Stone the key to deciphering hieroglyphs?","options":["One of its three scripts was Greek, which people could still read","It was the largest stone carving ever found anywhere in Egypt","It was found sitting inside a tomb beside a book of signs"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the fact box and see what made it unique.","explain":"The Rosetta Stone had a script in Greek, which helped unlock the meaning of hieroglyphs."}}]},{"icon":"🔤","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Hieroglyphs are not just pictures. Most signs stand for sounds, like letters in an alphabet."},{"t":"term","word":"cartouche","text":"The oval loop that surrounds a king's name."},{"t":"fact","text":"Jean-François Champollion found his first foothold by looking for the name Ptolemy inside a cartouche."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is a cartouche?","options":["The golden mask over a king's face","The oval loop around a king's name","A prayer carved on a king's coffin"],"answer":1,"hint":"It's the shape that encloses a king's name.","explain":"A cartouche is the oval loop that surrounds a king's name in hieroglyphs."}}]},{"icon":"🏺","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Making a mummy was a long job. First, the body was packed in natron salt for about forty days to dry it out."},{"t":"fact","text":"The lungs, liver, stomach, and intestines were taken out and stored separately. The brain was removed through the nose with a hooked rod and thrown away."},{"t":"p","text":"But there was one organ they always left inside. The heart was kept right where it was because they believed that was where a person did their thinking."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why was the heart left in the body?","options":["They thought it was too hard to remove","They thought it made the mummy look better","They thought a person did their thinking with it"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the last paragraph. It explains why the heart was left in.","explain":"They believed the heart was where a person did their thinking, so it had to stay inside."}}]},{"icon":"🌅","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A pyramid level to within an inch. A block count nobody made. A stone with the same message in three scripts. A heart left inside the body."},{"t":"p","text":"Each one something you can see, and each one something somebody had to work out."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Great Pyramid","Sides differ by less than 8 inches"],["Rosetta Stone","Key to understanding hieroglyphs"],["Cartouche","Oval loop around a king's name"],["Jean-François Champollion","Found foothold by looking for Ptolemy's name"],["Natron salt","Used to dry out the body"],["Heart","Kept inside the mummy"]]},"picgame":{"type":"sort","title":"Mummy Making","goal":"Was it taken out, or left in the mummy?","bins":[{"icon":"📤","label":"taken out"},{"icon":"🔒","label":"left in"}],"items":[{"icon":"🫁","name":"lungs","bin":0},{"icon":"🧠","name":"brain","bin":0},{"icon":"🫀","name":"heart","bin":1},{"icon":"🦴","name":"bone","bin":1},{"icon":"🖐️","name":"hand","bin":1},{"icon":"🦶","name":"foot","bin":1}]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What's actually the most astonishing part of the Great Pyramid, according to the people who've measured it?","options":["The precision — its four sides differ in length by less than 8 inches, on a 13-acre base","Its sheer size compared to other ancient buildings","How quickly it was reportedly built"],"answer":0,"explain":"The ground it sits on was leveled to within about an inch across the whole structure."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How do historians actually arrive at 2560 BC as the Great Pyramid's construction date?","options":["By combining Egyptian king-lists with carbon dates from its mortar","It's carved directly into the pyramid's stone","Ancient Egyptian calendars state it exactly"],"answer":0,"explain":"Nothing on the pyramid gives a year — that date, like any ancient date, is worked out, not read off the stone."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How did the Rosetta Stone help scholars finally read Egyptian hieroglyphs?","options":["The same decree was carved on it in three scripts, one of which (Greek) people could still read","It was the first hieroglyphic text ever discovered","It contained a translation guide written by ancient Egyptian scholars"],"answer":0,"explain":"Jean-François Champollion announced he'd cracked the code in 1822, after finding a king's name inside an oval cartouche."},{"type":"tf","prompt":"Hieroglyphs are mostly picture-writing, where each symbol represents the object it looks like.","answer":1,"explain":"Most hieroglyphic signs actually stand for sounds, not things — which is how Champollion cracked them by looking for a specific name."}]},{"id":"ancient-mesopot","course":"ancient","kind":"facts","section":"mesopot","title":"Mesopotamia","glyph":"🧱","n":6,"idea":"The oldest writing we can read was pressed into wet clay, and a palace fire is the reason a whole library survived. Find out where a famous law code was found, and why it was not in Babylon.","pages":[{"icon":"✍️","blocks":[{"t":"term","word":"cuneiform","text":"The oldest writing we can still read, pressed into wet clay with a cut reed. It means wedge-shaped, because each press leaves a wedge."},{"t":"fact","text":"Hundreds of thousands of these clay tablets have been dug up, and most have never been translated."},{"t":"try","text":"Flatten a lump of modeling clay and press the end of a stick into it at different angles. Try to make marks you can tell apart from every other mark you've made."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does cuneiform mean?","options":["Wedge-shaped","Circle-shaped","Star-shaped"],"answer":0,"hint":"Each press leaves a wedge shape, like the end of a cut reed.","explain":"Cuneiform means wedge-shaped because each press makes a wedge."}}]},{"icon":"📚","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"In Nineveh, archaeologists found a king's library that had about 30,000 clay tablets and fragments."},{"t":"fact","text":"The library belonged to King Ashurbanipal and included letters, omens, medicine, word-lists, and even a poem with a flood story."},{"t":"p","text":"The palace burned down, but the fire did not destroy the library. Fire bakes clay harder, so the tablets survived."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why did the library survive?","options":["The tablets were made of metal","The building had a fireproof safe","The fire baked the clay harder"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the last paragraph. It explains what happened to the clay.","explain":"Fire bakes clay harder, so the tablets in the library survived the blaze."}}]},{"icon":"⚖️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"In the Louvre stands a finger of black stone, 7 feet 5 inches tall, with 282 laws carved all around it."},{"t":"fact","text":"It is the Code of Hammurabi, and the laws cover farm rent, wages, surgery gone wrong, and what happens if a house falls down and kills someone. One of the oldest legal documents still readable."},{"t":"p","text":"This black stone finger was not found in Babylon but at Susa in Iran, in 1901, because somebody had carried it off as loot."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where was the Code of Hammurabi found?","options":["In the old city of Ur, under a temple","At Susa in Iran, carried there as loot","Inside a tomb near Thebes in Egypt"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the last paragraph. It mentions where and when it was found.","explain":"The stone was discovered at Susa in Iran in 1901 after being carried off as loot."}}]},{"icon":"🪜","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The ziggurat at Ur is a big stepped platform made of mud bricks."},{"t":"fact","text":"It has three great staircases meeting at the top. The lowest stage still stands, and people can climb it today."},{"t":"p","text":"Some of the bricks are stamped with a king's name, like finding a builder's mark on a brick."}]},{"icon":"📦","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The Standard of Ur is a wooden box about 19 inches long, covered in shell and blue lapis lazuli."},{"t":"fact","text":"One side shows a feast, and the other side shows wagons with four solid wheels each, pulled by donkeys."},{"t":"p","text":"It came out of a royal tomb dug up by Leonard Woolley in the 1920s. Today it is in the British Museum. Nobody knows what it was used for."}]},{"icon":"⚙️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Some of the earliest wheels we know of were not on carts at all."},{"t":"fact","text":"They are potter's wheels — flat discs spun to shape clay. Wheels for carrying things show up later, made of three planks pegged together into a solid disc."},{"t":"p","text":"A solid wooden wheel is enormously heavy. Spoked wheels came much later and changed everything."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What were some of the earliest wheels used for?","options":["Shaping clay into pots","Carrying heavy loads","Pulling wagons"],"answer":0,"hint":"The earliest wheels are potter's wheels used to shape clay.","explain":"The earliest wheels were flat discs used to shape clay on potter's wheels."}}]},{"icon":"🏺","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Wedges pressed into wet clay. A library baked hard by a fire. Laws on a finger of black stone. A staircase people still climb."},{"t":"p","text":"And before any cart, a flat disc for shaping pots."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Ashurbanipal","King with a vast library"],["Hammurabi","Made the Code of Laws"],["Standard of Ur","A decorated wooden box"],["Leonard Woolley","Archaeologist who found the Standard of Ur"]]},"picgame":{"type":"tapall","title":"Mesopotamia Makers","goal":"Tap what people made in Mesopotamia.","items":[{"icon":"🏺","name":"pot","good":true},{"icon":"🧱","name":"brick","good":true},{"icon":"📦","name":"box","good":true},{"icon":"🪨","name":"stone","good":true},{"icon":"🍩","name":"donut","good":false},{"icon":"🛹","name":"skateboard","good":false},{"icon":"🪁","name":"kite","good":false},{"icon":"🧃","name":"juice","good":false}]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why is cuneiform writing called \"wedge-shaped\"?","options":["Pressing a cut reed into wet clay leaves a wedge-shaped mark","The clay tablets themselves were shaped like wedges","Scribes used wedge-shaped tools to carve stone"],"answer":0,"explain":"Cuneiform comes from Latin for wedge-shaped — hundreds of thousands of these tablets have been dug up."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does the Code of Hammurabi, now in the Louvre, actually contain?","options":["282 laws covering things like farm rent, wages, and what happens if a house collapses","A record of Hammurabi's military victories","A genealogy of Babylonian kings"],"answer":0,"explain":"It's one of the oldest legal documents anyone can still read, and it wasn't even found in Babylon — it turned up in Iran as loot."},{"type":"tf","prompt":"A king's library discovered at Nineveh survived mostly because the palace it was in burned down.","answer":0,"explain":"Fire destroys paper and parchment, but it actually bakes clay tablets harder — the library survived because it was destroyed."}]},{"id":"ancient-israel","course":"ancient","kind":"facts","section":"israel","title":"Israel","glyph":"🕎","n":6,"idea":"A shepherd's thrown stone, a boy wading in a tunnel and a broken piece of black rock all turned up writing from long ago. Find out which of them names David.","pages":[{"icon":"🏺","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"In 1947, a young shepherd was exploring caves near the Dead Sea. He threw a stone into one of them and heard pottery break."},{"t":"fact","text":"Inside the cave were jars filled with scrolls. One of these scrolls is a complete book of Isaiah, 24 feet long, written on 17 sheets of leather sewn together."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did the shepherd hear when the stone landed?","options":["A bat flying away","Pottery breaking","Rocks crumbling"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It names what broke.","explain":"He heard pottery break because inside the cave were jars filled with scrolls."}}]},{"icon":"👑","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"In 1993, a broken piece of black basalt turned up in a wall at Tel Dan in northern Israel."},{"t":"fact","text":"Cut into it, in Aramaic, is a boast by a king about defeating his enemies — and the phrase \"House of David\". It is the earliest mention of David found outside the Bible."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What phrase is cut into the Tel Dan stone?","options":["House of David","King Solomon's mines","Temple of Jerusalem"],"answer":0,"hint":"The fact box mentions the exact phrase.","explain":"The stone boasts in Aramaic about defeating enemies and includes the phrase \"House of David\"."}}]},{"icon":"💧","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Under Jerusalem, there is a tunnel 1,750 feet long, cut through solid rock, that still carries water."},{"t":"fact","text":"Two teams dug it from opposite ends to bring a spring inside the city walls before a siege. The pick marks change direction where they met in the middle."},{"t":"try","text":"Measure out 1,750 feet on a map of your own street. That is how far two teams cut through rock, in the dark, and still met."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How did the two teams of diggers meet?","options":["They started in the middle and dug toward both ends","One team dug while the other hauled away the rock","They cut from opposite ends and met in the middle"],"answer":2,"hint":"The fact box explains where they started and how they met.","explain":"They dug from opposite ends and met in the middle. The pick marks show where they joined."}}]},{"icon":"🗣️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"In 1880, a boy was wading through the water tunnel under Jerusalem when he noticed something on the wall."},{"t":"term","word":"inscription","text":"Writing cut into a hard surface such as stone or a wall."},{"t":"fact","text":"The writing described the last moments of the dig. Two teams called to each other as they cut from opposite ends, and then their pick-axes broke through."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Who found the inscription in the tunnel?","options":["A boy wading in the tunnel","An archaeologist with a flashlight","A tourist exploring the city"],"answer":0,"hint":"The fact box tells you who noticed the writing.","explain":"A boy was wading in the tunnel and spotted the writing on the wall."}}]},{"icon":"🏹","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"An Assyrian king once carved a battle scene along the walls of his palace in Nineveh."},{"t":"fact","text":"The carving shows the town of Lachish under siege, with ramps for attackers, archers, battering rams, and people leaving the town. Today, the wall is in the British Museum."},{"t":"p","text":"Excavators later dug at Lachish and found evidence that matched the carving: a siege ramp, a burned layer, and heaps of sling stones and arrowheads. The carving and the ground agree."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does the carving at Nineveh show?","options":["A victory parade in the king's city","A festival in honor of the gods","The siege of the town of Lachish"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It tells you what the carving shows.","explain":"The carving at Nineveh shows the siege of Lachish, with attackers and defenders, just as the excavations found evidence for it."}}]},{"icon":"🛕","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Imagine a barrel of clay about 9 inches long, covered in ancient writing."},{"t":"fact","text":"Dug up at Babylon in 1879, the Cyrus Cylinder tells us King Cyrus sent captured peoples back to their homes and let them rebuild their temples."},{"t":"p","text":"This is the same policy described in the book of Ezra, but from a different perspective. The cylinder shows that Cyrus really did follow this practice."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does the Cyrus Cylinder say Cyrus let captive peoples do?","options":["Stay in Babylon and work for the king","Go home and rebuild their temples","Build new palaces for themselves"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It tells you what King Cyrus allowed.","explain":"The cylinder says that King Cyrus sent the captured peoples back to their homes and allowed them to rebuild their temples."}}]},{"icon":"📜","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A jar of scrolls in a cave. A stone with a king's boast. A tunnel cut from both ends. A wall carved with a siege. A barrel of clay."},{"t":"p","text":"Each one something you can go and see."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Shepherd","Threw a stone into a Dead Sea cave"],["Tel Dan stone","Mentions \"House of David\""],["Isaiah scroll","24 feet long on leather sheets"],["Cyrus Cylinder","Allowed captives to rebuild their temples"],["Lachish carving","Shows the town under siege"],["Boy wading","Found the inscription in Jerusalem's tunnel"]]},"picgame":{"type":"sort","title":"Cave or Tunnel","goal":"Put each one in the cave or tunnel.","bins":[{"icon":"⛰️","label":"cave"},{"icon":"🕳️","label":"tunnel"}],"items":[{"icon":"🏺","name":"jar","bin":0},{"icon":"📜","name":"scroll","bin":0},{"icon":"🪨","name":"stone","bin":0},{"icon":"💧","name":"water","bin":1},{"icon":"⛏️","name":"pick","bin":1},{"icon":"✍️","name":"writing","bin":1}]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did the Tel Dan inscription, found in 1993, actually prove about King David?","options":["It's the earliest mention of David found outside the Bible, countering claims he was purely legendary","It gives the exact dates of David's reign","It was written by David himself"],"answer":0,"explain":"The broken basalt stone names the 'House of David' in a king's boast about his victories."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What's remarkable about the 1,750-foot tunnel still carrying water under Jerusalem?","options":["Two teams cut it from opposite ends through solid rock, and their paths actually met","It was built using machines far ahead of its time","It was discovered completely by accident in modern times"],"answer":0,"explain":"The tunnel's S-shaped path and changing pick marks show exactly where the two digging teams met."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did the 1947 discovery of the Dead Sea Scrolls near a cave actually show about the Bible?","options":["The text had been copied for a very long time with remarkably few changes","The biblical text had changed significantly over the centuries","The scrolls contained entirely unknown books of the Bible"],"answer":0,"explain":"One scroll is a complete, 24-foot-long book of Isaiah — found when a shepherd's thrown stone broke pottery in a cave."},{"type":"tf","prompt":"The Cyrus Cylinder, found at Babylon, describes Cyrus sending captured peoples back to their homelands to rebuild their temples.","answer":0,"explain":"That matches the same policy described in the book of Ezra, even though the cylinder doesn't name Judah specifically."}]},{"id":"ancient-greece","course":"ancient","kind":"facts","section":"greece","title":"Greece","glyph":"🏛️","n":6,"idea":"The most famous straight-line building in the world has almost no straight lines. Find out what a corroded green lump from a shipwreck turned out to hold.","pages":[{"icon":"🏛️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The Parthenon is the most famous straight-line building in the world, and it has almost no straight lines."},{"t":"fact","text":"The floor rises about 4 inches in the middle of the long sides. The columns lean slightly inwards and bulge in the middle. Measure it and you find hardly anything is truly flat or upright."},{"t":"p","text":"Why the builders did this is argued about — a correction for how eyes work, or simply how it looked best. But the measurements themselves are not in doubt; people have surveyed that building for two hundred years."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How much does the Parthenon floor rise in the middle?","options":["About 4 inches","About 1 inch","About 10 inches"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the first sentence of the fact box. It gives the number.","explain":"The floor rises about 4 inches in the middle, making it far from perfectly flat."}}]},{"icon":"⚙️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"In 1901, sponge divers found a corroded green lump off Antikythera. Inside were at least thirty bronze gears."},{"t":"fact","text":"X-rays and scans have read the inscriptions inside it. It tracked the sun and moon and predicted eclipses. Nothing else of that complexity survives from anywhere near its time."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did the Antikythera gears track?","options":["The ocean tides and sea winds","The sailing days and sea storms","The sun and moon, and eclipses"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It tells you what it tracked.","explain":"The inscriptions show that it tracked the sun and moon, and predicted eclipses."}}]},{"icon":"🔩","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Archimedes figured out how levers make lifting easier, and he said that with a good place to stand, he could move Earth itself."},{"t":"fact","text":"He also invented a screw inside a tube that lifts water uphill when you turn it. Farmers still use this design today."},{"t":"try","text":"Balance a ruler on a pencil with a book on one end. Move the pencil closer to the book and press the other end. Notice how much less effort it takes the further away you push."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does Archimedes' screw do?","options":["It grinds grain into flour","It lifts water uphill","It drills holes in wood"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the second sentence on this page. It tells you what the screw does.","explain":"The screw inside a tube lifts water uphill when you turn it."}}]},{"icon":"🔤","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The Greeks got their letters from the Phoenicians, but they added something new."},{"t":"term","word":"alphabet","text":"The set of letters that make up a writing system. It comes from the first two Greek letters, alpha and beta."},{"t":"fact","text":"The Greeks added vowels to the Phoenician letters. Our A, B, and E are those same letters, handed down through the Romans."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did the Greeks add to the Phoenician letters?","options":["Vowels","Numbers","Pictures"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the second sentence on this page. It tells you what the Greeks added.","explain":"The Greeks added vowels, which the Phoenicians never wrote down."}}]},{"icon":"🥇","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The Greeks kept careful lists of who won at the Olympic Games."},{"t":"fact","text":"The prize was a wreath made of wild olive branches. There was no second place recorded."},{"t":"p","text":"These winner-lists were useful for more than just sports. Greek writers dated other events by counting the number of games that had passed, giving historians a way to measure time."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What was the prize at the Olympic Games?","options":["A sack of silver coins","A wreath of wild olive","A bronze shield and sword"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the first fact box on this page. It tells you what the prize was.","explain":"The prize was a wreath made from wild olive branches."}}]},{"icon":"🏃","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"You may have heard a story about a runner carrying news from Marathon to Athens and then dying. That version came much later."},{"t":"fact","text":"Herodotus wrote closest to the event, and he describes a runner going to Sparta for help instead, which was a much longer run.","label":"Worked out, not seen directly"},{"t":"p","text":"The dying-messenger story turns up centuries later. It might be true, but it’s important to know what the earliest account actually says."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Who wrote closest to the event?","options":["Thucydides","Plato","Herodotus"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the fact box on this page. It tells you who wrote closest to the event.","explain":"Herodotus wrote closest to the event, and he described a different story than the famous one."}}]},{"icon":"🏺","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Curves of columns holding up temples. Gears and screws for moving water. Letters on stone recording events. Wreaths for victors. Accounts written close to the event."},{"t":"p","text":"Each piece tells a part of ancient history."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Parthenon","Floor rises 4 inches in the middle"],["Antikythera","Tracked the sun and moon, predicted eclipses"],["Archimedes' screw","Lifts water uphill when turned"],["Olympic Games prize","Wreath made of wild olive branches"],["Herodotus","Wrote closest to the event about Marathon"],["Phoenicians","Did not write down vowels"]]},"picgame":{"type":"tapall","title":"Greek Makers","goal":"Tap what the old Greeks built or made.","items":[{"icon":"🏛️","name":"building","good":true},{"icon":"⚙️","name":"gears","good":true},{"icon":"🔤","name":"letters","good":true},{"icon":"🔩","name":"screw","good":true},{"icon":"🚲","name":"bike","good":false},{"icon":"🎸","name":"guitar","good":false},{"icon":"🧁","name":"cupcake","good":false},{"icon":"🍿","name":"popcorn","good":false}]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did sponge divers accidentally discover in a 1901 shipwreck off Antikythera?","options":["A corroded device with at least 30 meshed bronze gears that tracked the sun, moon, and eclipses","A cache of ancient Greek coins","A collection of bronze statues"],"answer":0,"explain":"Nothing else of that mechanical complexity survives from anywhere near its time."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did the Greeks add to the alphabet they borrowed from the Phoenicians?","options":["Vowels","Punctuation marks","Numbers"],"answer":0,"explain":"Our letters A, B, and E are those same Greek letters, handed down through the Romans."},{"type":"tf","prompt":"The famous story of a runner dying after carrying news of victory from Marathon to Athens comes from the earliest written account of the battle.","answer":1,"explain":"Herodotus, who wrote closest to the event, actually describes a runner going the opposite direction, to Sparta, to ask for help."}]},{"id":"ancient-rome","course":"ancient","kind":"facts","section":"rome","title":"Rome","glyph":"🦅","n":6,"idea":"Roman concrete gets stronger in seawater, and one dome has stood open to the rain without any steel in it. Find out how a pour of plaster showed us a person from Pompeii.","pages":[{"icon":"🏗️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Have you ever wondered why some old buildings are still standing strong after all these years? Roman concrete harbors are a perfect example. They are still standing in seawater, where modern concrete often wears out within a few decades."},{"t":"fact","text":"The Romans mixed volcanic ash into their concrete. Seawater reacts with the ash and grows new crystals inside the concrete, making it stronger instead of letting it crumble."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did the Romans mix into their concrete?","options":["Iron filings","Volcanic ash","Crushed glass"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It tells you what they mixed in.","explain":"The volcanic ash reacts with seawater to grow new crystals inside the concrete, making it stronger over time."}}]},{"icon":"🏛️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The Pantheon in Rome has a huge concrete dome that is 142 feet across."},{"t":"fact","text":"At the very top of the dome is a 27-foot hole open to the sky. There is no steel in the dome, and it is still the largest unreinforced concrete dome in the world."},{"t":"term","word":"unreinforced","text":"A structure made without any metal supports inside."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is at the top of the Pantheon's dome?","options":["A 27-foot hole open to the sky","A bronze statue of a Roman god","A circle of colored glass windows"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It tells you what is at the top.","explain":"The dome has a 27-foot hole in the middle, and rain still comes through it."}}]},{"icon":"💧","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The aqueduct that brought water to Nîmes runs about 31 miles."},{"t":"fact","text":"It drops only about 55 feet over that whole distance, which is a slope of roughly one inch in every 250 feet."},{"t":"p","text":"They surveyed it with water levels and sighting rods. Too steep and the water tears the channel apart; too flat and it stops."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How steep is the Nîmes aqueduct?","options":["About one inch in every 250 feet","About one foot in every 100 feet","About one inch in every 10 feet"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It gives you the exact slope.","explain":"The aqueduct drops only about 55 feet over 31 miles, so it is about one inch in every 250 feet."}}]},{"icon":"🌋","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"When Vesuvius buried Pompeii, bodies left holes in the hardened ash as they decayed."},{"t":"fact","text":"In 1863 an excavator started pouring plaster into those holes before digging them out. What comes out is the shape of a person in their last moment."},{"t":"p","text":"Pliny the Younger watched the eruption from across the bay as a teenager and wrote two letters describing it. His account fits the ash layers closely."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How were the body shapes made?","options":["Stone carved by artists who copied the people they saw","Wax pressed over each body as soon as it was found","Plaster poured into the holes the bodies left in the ash"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the second sentence. It explains how the shapes were made.","explain":"The excavator poured plaster into the holes, and what came out was the shape of a person."}}]},{"icon":"🏟️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The Colosseum in ancient Rome held about 50,000 people and had 80 entrances."},{"t":"fact","text":"Spectators were given numbered tokens telling them which arch to use and where to sit. The numbers are still carved above the arches today."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What told spectators which arch to use?","options":["Color-coded signs","Shapes carved into the seats","Numbered tokens"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the second sentence. It mentions the numbered tokens.","explain":"Each spectator got a numbered token that matched the numbers carved above the arches."}}]},{"icon":"🔤","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Many of the words we use today have a surprising history."},{"t":"fact","text":"Animal, family, question, city, table, letter, number—all these came from Latin through French. The short blunt ones like house, food, eat, work are older English.","label":"Worked out, not seen directly"},{"t":"try","text":"Read a page of any book and try to sort the words into short blunt ones and longer smoother ones. The long ones are mostly Latin arriving late."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which of these is an older English word?","options":["Animal","House","Number"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the example words given in the fact box.","explain":"Short blunt words like \"house\" are usually older English."}}]},{"icon":"🏺","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Concrete that gets stronger in seawater. A dome with a hole in the top. A water channel that drops one inch in 250 feet. Plaster shapes of people, and numbered tokens for 50,000 seats."},{"t":"p","text":"All of it, still here from Rome."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Roman concrete","Gets stronger with seawater"],["Pantheon dome","Largest unreinforced concrete dome"],["Nîmes aqueduct","Drops only one inch in 250 feet"],["Vesuvius","Buried Pompeii with hardened ash"],["Colosseum","Held about 50,000 people"],["House","Short blunt older English word"]]},"picgame":{"type":"sort","title":"People or Nature","goal":"Did people make it, or did nature?","bins":[{"icon":"🏗️","label":"people made"},{"icon":"🌳","label":"nature"}],"items":[{"icon":"🏟️","name":"arena","bin":0},{"icon":"🏛️","name":"building","bin":0},{"icon":"🎟️","name":"ticket","bin":0},{"icon":"🌉","name":"aqueduct","bin":0},{"icon":"🌋","name":"volcano","bin":1},{"icon":"🌊","name":"sea","bin":1},{"icon":"🌧️","name":"rain","bin":1}]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why do ancient Roman concrete harbors still stand strong in seawater today, while modern concrete would crumble?","options":["Seawater reacts with volcanic ash mixed into the concrete, growing new crystals that make it stronger over time","Roman engineers used a secret ingredient that's since been lost forever","Roman concrete was never actually exposed to seawater"],"answer":0,"explain":"This was discovered by drilling cores out of Roman harbors and studying them under microscopes — the recipe was written down by Vitruvius; it just stopped being used."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How did the Romans survey a 31-mile aqueduct so precisely that it drops only 41 feet over its entire length?","options":["With water levels and sighting rods","Using mathematical formulas we no longer understand","They didn't — the slope was mostly accidental"],"answer":0,"explain":"Too steep and the water would tear the channel apart; too flat and it would stop — the Pont du Gard bridge that carries it is still standing."},{"type":"tf","prompt":"At Pompeii, pouring plaster into holes in hardened volcanic ash reveals the actual shape of a person's body at their last moment.","answer":0,"explain":"The bodies decayed away, leaving holes exactly their shape — pouring plaster in captures that shape when it hardens."}]},{"id":"ancient-patriarchs","course":"ancient","kind":"facts","section":"patriarchs","title":"Abraham & His Family","glyph":"🐪","n":6,"idea":"The written account follows Abram, who left his country, and the family that came from him.","pages":[{"icon":"🐪","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The written account says Yahweh told Abram to leave his country and his father's house."},{"t":"fact","text":"Abram was to go to a land that Yahweh would show him. Yahweh promised to make of him a great nation."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did Yahweh tell Abram to leave?","options":["His small flock","His country","His old tent"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"The written account says Yahweh told Abram to leave his country and his father's house."}}]},{"icon":"🧑","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The written account says Abram was very rich."},{"t":"term","word":"livestock","text":"Animals kept on a farm. Abram had livestock, silver and gold."},{"t":"fact","text":"He had livestock, silver and gold."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What was Abram rich in?","options":["Boats, wheat and cloth","Livestock, silver and gold","Houses, wagons and tools"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. The answer is there.","explain":"He had livestock, silver and gold."}}]},{"icon":"🧭","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Abram lived in the land of Canaan, and Lot lived in the cities of the plain."},{"t":"fact","text":"Lot moved his tent as far as Sodom."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where did Abram live?","options":["The cities of the plain","The land of Canaan","The city of Sodom"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"Abram lived in the land of Canaan, and Lot lived in the cities of the plain."}}]},{"icon":"🗺️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Abraham split the wood for the offering and went to the place God had told him about."},{"t":"fact","text":"On the third day, he looked up and saw the place far off."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"On which day did Abraham see the place far off?","options":["The first day","The third day","The seventh day"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"On the third day, he looked up and saw the place far off."}}]},{"icon":"👤","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"God said that all the nations of the earth would be blessed through Abraham's offspring."},{"t":"fact","text":"Then Abraham returned to his young men, and they went together to Beersheba."}]},{"icon":"👑","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The written account says Joseph was seventeen years old when he was feeding the flock with his brothers."},{"t":"fact","text":"Their father Israel loved Joseph more than all his children and made him a coat of many colors."}]},{"icon":"🔎","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Abram and a land to go to. A rich man with livestock, silver and gold. Lot and Sodom. The third day. Joseph and a coat."},{"t":"p","text":"The written account goes on."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Abram","Told to leave his country"],["Canaan","Where Abram lived"],["Lot","Moved his tent as far as Sodom"],["Joseph","Given a coat of many colors"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"Who did Yahweh tell to leave his country and his father's house?","options":["Lot","Abram","Joseph"],"answer":1,"explain":"The written account says Yahweh told Abram to leave his country and his father's house."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did Israel make for Joseph?","options":["A tent of many poles","A coat of many colors","A staff of gold"],"answer":1,"explain":"Their father Israel loved Joseph more than all his children and made him a coat of many colors."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How old was Joseph when he was feeding the flock with his brothers?","options":["Seven years old","Seventeen years old","Seventy years old"],"answer":1,"explain":"The written account says Joseph was seventeen years old when he was feeding the flock with his brothers."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where did Abraham go together with his young men?","options":["To Sodom","To Beersheba","To Jerusalem"],"answer":1,"explain":"Then Abraham returned to his young men, and they went together to Beersheba."}]},{"id":"ancient-exodus","course":"ancient","kind":"facts","section":"exodus","title":"The Exodus","glyph":"🔥","n":6,"idea":"The written account tells how Israel left Egypt: a lamb, a meal eaten in haste, a dry path through the sea and food called manna.","pages":[{"icon":"🔥","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The written account says Yahweh spoke to Moses and Aaron in Egypt."},{"t":"fact","text":"He said this month would be the first month of the year to them."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Who did Yahweh speak to in the land of Egypt?","options":["Abram and Lot","Moses and Aaron","Joseph and Israel"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"The written account says Yahweh spoke to Moses and Aaron in Egypt."}}]},{"icon":"🏠","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Each family's lamb had to be without defect, a male a year old."},{"t":"fact","text":"They kept it until the fourteenth day of the month."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How old was the lamb to be?","options":["A day old","A year old","Ten years old"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"Each family's lamb had to be without defect, a male a year old."}}]},{"icon":"🍞","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Israel took blood from each family's lamb."},{"t":"term","word":"lintel","text":"The beam across the top of the door posts."},{"t":"fact","text":"Israel was told to put some of the lamb's blood on the two door posts and the lintel of their houses."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where was the blood to be put?","options":["Over the roof and the walls of the house","On the two door posts and the lintel","Under the bed and the floor of the tent"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. The answer is there.","explain":"Israel was told to put some of the lamb's blood on the two door posts and the lintel of their houses."}}]},{"icon":"🧺","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Israel was to eat the Passover meal ready to go."},{"t":"fact","text":"They wore belts on their waists and shoes on their feet, with a staff in the hand. They ate it in haste."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What were they to have in their hand when they ate?","options":["A basket","A staff","A torch"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. The answer is there.","explain":"They wore belts on their waists and shoes on their feet, with a staff in the hand."}}]},{"icon":"🧭","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Moses stretched out his hand over the sea."},{"t":"fact","text":"Yahweh caused the sea to go back by a strong east wind all night. The waters were divided, and the sea became dry land."}]},{"icon":"🍽️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The people called the food manna."},{"t":"fact","text":"It was white like coriander seed, and it tasted like wafers with honey. They ate it for forty years, until they came to the borders of Canaan."}]},{"icon":"🔎","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Moses and Aaron. A lamb a year old. Blood on the door posts. A meal in haste. A sea turned to dry land. Manna to eat."},{"t":"p","text":"Israel was led out."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Moses","Stretched out his hand over the sea"],["Lamb","A male a year old"],["Passover","The meal eaten ready to go"],["Manna","White like coriander seed"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"How long did the people eat manna?","options":["For four days","For forty years","For one week"],"answer":1,"explain":"They ate it for forty years, until they came to the borders of Canaan."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What color was manna?","options":["Red","White","Blue"],"answer":1,"explain":"It was white like coriander seed, and it tasted like wafers with honey."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did Yahweh cause to go back by a strong east wind?","options":["The river","The sea","The cloud"],"answer":1,"explain":"Yahweh caused the sea to go back by a strong east wind all night."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Until which day of the month did each family keep its lamb?","options":["The fourth day","The fourteenth day","The fortieth day"],"answer":1,"explain":"They kept it until the fourteenth day of the month."}]},{"id":"ancient-kings","course":"ancient","kind":"facts","section":"kings","title":"David & Solomon","glyph":"👑","n":6,"idea":"A very tall champion, a shepherd with five smooth stones, and a king who built a house for Yahweh.","pages":[{"icon":"👑","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Goliath of Gath, the Philistine champion, was six cubits and a span tall."},{"t":"term","word":"cubit","text":"A length measure. Goliath was six cubits and a span tall."},{"t":"fact","text":"He wore a brass helmet and a coat of mail that weighed five thousand shekels."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Who was the Philistine champion from Gath?","options":["Jonathan","Goliath","Samson"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"Goliath of Gath, the Philistine champion, was six cubits and a span tall."}}]},{"icon":"🧑","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"David took his staff in his hand and chose five smooth stones out of the brook."},{"t":"fact","text":"He put them in the pouch of his shepherd's bag."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many smooth stones did David choose?","options":["Ten","Five","Three"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"David took his staff in his hand and chose five smooth stones out of the brook."}}]},{"icon":"📏","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The house that King Solomon built for Yahweh was sixty cubits long."},{"t":"fact","text":"It was twenty cubits wide and thirty cubits high."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many cubits long was the house King Solomon built?","options":["Twenty cubits","Sixty cubits","Thirty cubits"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"The house that King Solomon built for Yahweh was sixty cubits long."}}]},{"icon":"🏗️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"King Solomon spent seven years building the house of Yahweh."},{"t":"fact","text":"In the end it was finished throughout all its parts, just as planned."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many years did Solomon spend building the house?","options":["Three years","Seven years","Twelve years"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"King Solomon spent seven years building the house of Yahweh."}}]},{"icon":"👤","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The queen of Sheba heard about Solomon's fame and came to test him with hard questions."},{"t":"fact","text":"She came to Jerusalem with a very great caravan, with camels carrying spices, a lot of gold and precious stones."}]},{"icon":"🧭","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Once every three years the fleet of Tarshish came to King Solomon."},{"t":"fact","text":"It brought gold, silver, ivory, apes and peacocks."}]},{"icon":"🔎","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A tall champion. Five smooth stones. A house for Yahweh. A queen with a caravan. A fleet with gold and ivory."},{"t":"p","text":"Kings and champions."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Goliath","Philistine champion from Gath"],["David","Chose five smooth stones"],["Solomon","Spent seven years building the house"],["Sheba","Queen who came to test Solomon"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did David put his five stones in?","options":["The helmet of a Philistine soldier","The pouch of his shepherd's bag","The coat of a mighty king"],"answer":1,"explain":"He put them in the pouch of his shepherd's bag."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Who came to Jerusalem with a very great caravan to test Solomon?","options":["The king of Tarshish","The queen of Sheba","The priest of Gath"],"answer":1,"explain":"The queen of Sheba heard about Solomon's fame and came to test him with hard questions."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How often did the fleet of Tarshish come to King Solomon?","options":["Every single morning","Once every three years","Just one time ever"],"answer":1,"explain":"Once every three years the fleet of Tarshish came to King Solomon."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What animals did the fleet of Tarshish bring?","options":["Lions and bears","Apes and peacocks","Camels and goats"],"answer":1,"explain":"It brought gold, silver, ivory, apes and peacocks."}]},{"id":"ancient-babylon","course":"ancient","kind":"facts","section":"babylon","title":"Babylon","glyph":"🏛️","n":6,"idea":"Babylon was a very big ancient city with a great gate, a road lined with lions, and famous hanging gardens.","pages":[{"icon":"🏛️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Babylon was an ancient city on the Euphrates river in Mesopotamia."},{"t":"term","word":"ruins","text":"What is left of Babylon, an ancient city in Iraq."},{"t":"fact","text":"Its ruins are in Iraq, about 53 mi (85 km) south of Baghdad."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"On which river was Babylon?","options":["The Mississippi","The Euphrates","The Jordan"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"Babylon was an ancient city on the Euphrates river in Mesopotamia."}}]},{"icon":"📏","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Babylon may have been the first city with more than 200,000 people."},{"t":"fact","text":"It covered an area of about 3.5 sq mi (900 ha)."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many people may have lived in Babylon?","options":["Fewer than 2,000","More than 200,000","About 20"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"Babylon may have been the first city with more than 200,000 people."}}]},{"icon":"📐","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The Ishtar Gate was 49 ft (15 m) high."},{"t":"fact","text":"Its foundations went another 46 ft (14 m) underground."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How high was the Ishtar Gate?","options":["29 ft (9 m)","49 ft (15 m)","94 ft (29 m)"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"The Ishtar Gate was 49 ft (15 m) high."}}]},{"icon":"🏺","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The Processional Way ran through the Ishtar Gate."},{"t":"fact","text":"Its walls showed about 120 lions, bulls, dragons and flowers on yellow and black glazed bricks."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What was the road through the Ishtar Gate called?","options":["The Royal Highway","The Processional Way","The Golden Bridge"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"The Processional Way ran through the Ishtar Gate."}}]},{"icon":"🏗️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The Hanging Gardens of Babylon were one of the Seven Wonders of the Ancient World."},{"t":"fact","text":"They were described as rising tiers of gardens with many trees, shrubs and vines, like a large green mountain."}]},{"icon":"🧑","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The written account says Daniel was taken up out of the den of lions."},{"t":"fact","text":"No kind of harm was found on him, because he had trusted in his God."}]},{"icon":"🔎","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A city on the Euphrates. Ruins in Iraq. A gate and a Processional Way. Gardens like a green mountain. Daniel and the lions."},{"t":"p","text":"Babylon was great."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Babylon","Ancient city on the Euphrates"],["Ishtar Gate","Was 49 ft high"],["Processional Way","Ran through the Ishtar Gate"],["Hanging Gardens","One of the Seven Wonders"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which country are the ruins of Babylon in?","options":["Egypt","Iraq","Italy"],"answer":1,"explain":"Its ruins are in Iraq, about 53 mi (85 km) south of Baghdad."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many Wonders of the Ancient World are there?","options":["Three","Seven","Ten"],"answer":1,"explain":"The Hanging Gardens of Babylon were one of the Seven Wonders of the Ancient World."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What was found on Daniel when he was taken up out of the den of lions?","options":["A deep wound","No harm","A heavy chain"],"answer":1,"explain":"No kind of harm was found on him, because he had trusted in his God."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"About how many lions, bulls, dragons and flowers did the walls show?","options":["About 12","About 120","About 1,200"],"answer":1,"explain":"Its walls showed about 120 lions, bulls, dragons and flowers on yellow and black glazed bricks."}]},{"id":"ancient-persia","course":"ancient","kind":"facts","section":"persia","title":"Persia","glyph":"🏹","n":6,"idea":"The Persian empire was the largest of its time, and its Royal Road let couriers cross it in nine days.","pages":[{"icon":"🏹","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"At its peak, the Persian empire covered about 2.1 million sq mi (5.5 million square kilometers)."},{"t":"fact","text":"It was the largest empire of its time."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What was the area of the Persian empire at its peak?","options":["21,000 sq mi","2.1 million sq mi","Just 2,100 square miles"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"At its peak, the Persian empire covered about 2.1 million sq mi (5.5 million square kilometers)."}}]},{"icon":"📏","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Darius the Great was a king of the Persian empire."},{"t":"fact","text":"Under him, the empire stretched from Southeast Europe and Northeast Africa in the west to the Indus Valley in the east."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Under which king did the empire stretch from Southeast Europe to the Indus Valley?","options":["Alexander the Great","Darius the Great","Peter the Great"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"Darius the Great was a king of the Persian empire."}}]},{"icon":"🧑","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Cyrus is honored in Judaism for freeing the Jewish people from captivity in Babylon."},{"t":"term","word":"captivity","text":"Being held as a prisoner, like the Jewish people in Babylon."},{"t":"fact","text":"He did it by issuing the Edict of Restoration."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did Cyrus do for the Jewish people?","options":["Taught them to read and write","Freed them from captivity in Babylon","Gave them a new king to follow"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"Cyrus is honored in Judaism for freeing the Jewish people from captivity in Babylon."}}]},{"icon":"👤","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"According to the Book of Isaiah, Yahweh anointed Cyrus for this task and called him a messiah."},{"t":"fact","text":"Cyrus is the only non-Jewish person honored in this way."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which book says Yahweh anointed Cyrus?","options":["A letter from Darius","The Book of Isaiah","A song by King David"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"According to the Book of Isaiah, Yahweh anointed Cyrus for this task and called him a messiah."}}]},{"icon":"💨","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Mounted couriers were supposed to travel the 1,677 miles (2,699 km) from Susa to Sardis in nine days."},{"t":"fact","text":"On foot the trip took ninety days."}]},{"icon":"🏗️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The Royal Road was built for fast communication across the western part of the empire, from Susa to Sardis."},{"t":"fact","text":"It was probably perfected under Darius I."}]},{"icon":"🔎","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A huge empire. Darius the Great. Cyrus and the Jewish people. Couriers on the Royal Road."},{"t":"p","text":"Persia was large."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Persia","Largest empire of its time"],["Darius","Empire stretched from Southeast Europe to the Indus Valley"],["Cyrus","Freed the Jewish people"],["Royal Road","Built for fast communication"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many days did mounted couriers take to travel from Susa to Sardis?","options":["Ninety days","Nine days","Nineteen days"],"answer":1,"explain":"Mounted couriers were supposed to travel from Susa to Sardis in nine days."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where did the Royal Road run from?","options":["From Tyre to Babylon","From Susa to Sardis","From Egypt to Persia"],"answer":1,"explain":"The Royal Road was built for fast communication, from Susa to Sardis."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What was the Persian empire, for its time?","options":["The tiniest empire","The largest empire","A very young empire"],"answer":1,"explain":"It was the largest empire of its time."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did Cyrus issue to free the Jewish people?","options":["The Code of Laws","The Edict of Restoration","The Book of Wonders"],"answer":1,"explain":"He did it by issuing the Edict of Restoration."}]},{"id":"ancient-sea","course":"ancient","kind":"facts","section":"sea","title":"Sea Traders","glyph":"⛵","n":6,"idea":"The Phoenicians sailed and traded all over the Mediterranean, and their alphabet had one symbol for each sound.","pages":[{"icon":"⛵","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The Phoenicians were an ancient people who lived in city-states along the eastern Mediterranean coast."},{"t":"fact","text":"Most of their land was in what is now Lebanon."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where did the Phoenicians live?","options":["Across the dry southern desert near the river","Along the eastern Mediterranean coast","Beside the cold northern forests and the lakes"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"The Phoenicians were an ancient people who lived in city-states along the eastern Mediterranean coast."}}]},{"icon":"🧭","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The Phoenicians traded all over the Mediterranean Sea."},{"t":"fact","text":"They went from Cyprus to the Iberian Peninsula. They left thousands of inscriptions."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did the Phoenicians leave behind in many places?","options":["Hundreds of tall towers","Thousands of inscriptions","Millions of gold coins"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. The answer is there.","explain":"They left thousands of inscriptions."}}]},{"icon":"🏡","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Tyre juts out from the coast of the Mediterranean Sea, about 50 mi (80 km) south of Beirut."},{"t":"fact","text":"It is one of the oldest cities in the world that people have lived in without a break."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which sea is Tyre on?","options":["The Caribbean Sea","The Mediterranean Sea","The Southern Ocean"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"Tyre juts out from the coast of the Mediterranean Sea, about 50 mi (80 km) south of Beirut."}}]},{"icon":"✍️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The Phoenician alphabet was one of the first alphabets."},{"t":"fact","text":"It was written horizontally, from right to left, and was the first to have a fixed writing direction."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which way was Phoenician written?","options":["From top to bottom","From right to left","Around in a big circle"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. The answer is there.","explain":"It was written horizontally, from right to left, and was the first to have a fixed writing direction."}}]},{"icon":"📖","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The Phoenician alphabet used one symbol for each sound."},{"t":"fact","text":"That meant there were only a few dozen symbols to learn."}]},{"icon":"🖋️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The Phoenician alphabet used 22 letters, all of them consonants."},{"t":"term","word":"consonants","text":"Letters for sounds that are not vowel sounds."},{"t":"fact","text":"It left the vowel sounds for the reader to supply."}]},{"icon":"🔎","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A people on the eastern coast. Ships to Cyprus. Tyre on the sea. An alphabet from right to left. 22 letters."},{"t":"p","text":"The sea made them traders."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Phoenicians","Traded all over the Mediterranean"],["Tyre","One of the oldest cities"],["Alphabet","One symbol for each sound"],["Consonants","All 22 Phoenician letters"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many letters did the Phoenician alphabet use?","options":["2","22","220"],"answer":1,"explain":"The Phoenician alphabet used 22 letters, all of them consonants."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many symbols did the Phoenician alphabet use for each sound?","options":["Two","One","Ten"],"answer":1,"explain":"The Phoenician alphabet used one symbol for each sound."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where did the Phoenicians go from Cyprus?","options":["The Indus Valley","The Iberian Peninsula","The Black Forest"],"answer":1,"explain":"They went from Cyprus to the Iberian Peninsula."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which country is where most Phoenician land was?","options":["Argentina","Lebanon","Australia"],"answer":1,"explain":"Most of their land was in what is now Lebanon."}]},{"id":"america-explorers","course":"america","kind":"facts","section":"explorers","title":"Explorers","glyph":"🧭","n":6,"idea":"A Norwegian couple found a Viking village by treating old stories as directions. Then see how explorers work out where they are on Earth.","pages":[{"icon":"🏕️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"At L'Anse aux Meadows, on the northern tip of Newfoundland, there are eight turf buildings, a smithy, iron nails, and a bronze cloak pin. They are Norse, which means Viking."},{"t":"fact","text":"They were found in 1960 by a Norwegian couple who went looking after reading the old sagas, long Viking stories, about a land called Vinland."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Who found L'Anse aux Meadows?","options":["A Norwegian couple who treated the sagas as directions","A Canadian farmer who found the ruins while plowing","An American team who followed old pirate treasure maps"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It names who went looking and why.","explain":"Helge and Anne Stine Ingstad took the old sagas as directions to find these Norse remains."}}]},{"icon":"📓","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Columbus's own logbook is lost. What we have is a summary that a priest made from a copy of the original."},{"t":"term","word":"summary","text":"A shorter version made from something longer."},{"t":"fact","text":"Columbus died still believing he had reached the edge of Asia. The continent, however, is named after Amerigo Vespucci, who said plainly it was not Asia."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Whose name is on the continent?","options":["Christopher Columbus","Amerigo Vespucci","Leif Erikson"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the last sentence. It gives the name of the person the continent is named after.","explain":"Amerigo Vespucci said clearly that the land was not Asia, and the continent bears his name."}}]},{"icon":"🧭","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Finding how far north you are is easy. Just measure the sun at noon."},{"t":"fact","text":"Working out how far east or west needs an accurate clock because you compare your local noon with the time somewhere else."},{"t":"try","text":"Push a stick straight into the ground on a sunny day. Mark the end of its shadow every hour. The shortest shadow is your local noon, the moment the sun is highest."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What do you need to work out how far east or west you are?","options":["A strong tailwind","A compass","An accurate clock"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It says you need a clock to compare times.","explain":"You need an accurate clock to compare your noon with the time somewhere else."}}]},{"icon":"📔","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Lewis and Clark set out to explore the West and kept journals every day for two and a half years."},{"t":"fact","text":"They came back with 178 plants and 122 animals that nobody in the eastern states had recorded before. They brought measurements, drawings, and pressed specimens."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many new plants did they write up?","options":["122","178","300"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It gives the exact number of plants.","explain":"They wrote up 178 new plants that nobody in the eastern states had recorded before."}}]},{"icon":"👶","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Sacagawea was a key member of Lewis and Clark’s team, traveling from North Dakota to the Pacific and back with her baby son on her back."},{"t":"fact","text":"When a boat tipped over, she was the one who reached out and saved the instruments, books, and papers floating away. Those were the records of the entire expedition."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did Sacagawea save when the boat tipped?","options":["The guns, powder, and spare bullets","The corn, dried meat, and salt","The instruments, books, and papers"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It mentions exactly what she saved.","explain":"She saved the instruments, books, and papers, which were crucial records of the entire expedition."}}]},{"icon":"🛶","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"John Wesley Powell ran the Colorado River through the Grand Canyon in 1869, even though he had lost his right arm below the elbow in the Civil War."},{"t":"fact","text":"He took four wooden boats on the journey. Nobody had charted this stretch of the river before."},{"t":"p","text":"Three men left partway through and were never seen again. Powell kept a journal as the boats ran the rapids. One boat was smashed on the rocks early in the trip, and food and instruments were lost."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many boats did John Wesley Powell take on his expedition?","options":["Four","Two","Six"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the first sentence of the fact box. It gives the number of boats.","explain":"Powell took four wooden boats on his journey through the Grand Canyon."}}]},{"icon":"🧭","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Logbooks of explorers. Journals charting rivers. A clock to find east and west. Plants nobody in the East had written down. A boat tipping with the records saved."},{"t":"p","text":"Each one is a way of finding out where you are and what is there."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Amerigo Vespucci","Said clearly it was not Asia"],["Accurate clock","Needed to work out how far east or west"],["178 plants","New ones Lewis and Clark recorded"],["Sacagawea","Saved crucial expedition records"],["John Wesley Powell","Ran the Colorado River with a missing arm"]]},"picgame":{"type":"catch","title":"River Rescue","goal":"Catch what Sacagawea saved from the river.","items":[{"icon":"📚","name":"books","good":true},{"icon":"📄","name":"papers","good":true},{"icon":"📓","name":"notebook","good":true},{"icon":"🌽","name":"corn","good":false},{"icon":"🧂","name":"salt","good":false},{"icon":"🍖","name":"meat","good":false},{"icon":"🍎","name":"apple","good":false}]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"How did a Norwegian couple find a real Norse settlement in Newfoundland in 1960?","options":["They read old Viking sagas describing a place called Vinland and took the descriptions as directions","They found it using ground-penetrating radar","Local Indigenous people showed them the site"],"answer":0,"explain":"Most people had assumed the sagas describing Vinland were just stories — the Ingstads took them literally."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"During the Lewis and Clark expedition, who saved the expedition's instruments, books, and papers when a boat tipped over?","options":["Sacagawea","Meriwether Lewis","William Clark"],"answer":0,"explain":"She did this while traveling the entire route with a baby son on her back — Clark later named a river after her."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why did sailors need an extremely accurate clock to figure out how far east or west they'd traveled?","options":["They had to compare their local noon with the time at a known location","Clocks were needed to track how many days they'd been sailing","An accurate clock helped them read star positions"],"answer":0,"explain":"Figuring out north-south position was easy — just measure the sun at noon — but east-west required comparing times."}]},{"id":"america-colonies","course":"america","kind":"facts","section":"colonies","title":"The Colonies","glyph":"⛵","n":6,"idea":"Archaeologists found Jamestown's fort walls where no one expected. Then see how log cabins reached America and how Benjamin Franklin and Benjamin Banneker taught themselves.","pages":[{"icon":"🔍","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"For centuries everyone said the river had washed away the original Jamestown fort."},{"t":"fact","text":"In 1994, an archaeologist dug where he thought the fort should be and found postholes, the dark soil stains where wooden wall posts once stood, within weeks."},{"t":"p","text":"They have since dug up armor, tools, coins, and the graves of some of the first settlers, all inside those walls."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did the archaeologist find first?","options":["The postholes of the fort's wall","The armor and tools of the soldiers","The coins and graves of settlers"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It says what he found in the first few weeks.","explain":"He found the postholes of the fort's wall within weeks of digging."}}]},{"icon":"🚢","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The Mayflower carried 102 passengers plus crew on a ship about 100 feet long, which is roughly as long as two buses."},{"t":"fact","text":"The ship was at sea for 66 days. Living space per person was smaller than a modern parking space. No plans of the ship survive; its dimensions are worked out from other records.","label":"Worked out"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many days was the Mayflower at sea?","options":["About 30 days","66 days","Over 100 days"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It gives the exact number of days.","explain":"The Mayflower was at sea for 66 days."}}]},{"icon":"🪓","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The log cabin is not an English idea. English colonists built timber-frame houses with sawn boards."},{"t":"fact","text":"Log building was brought to America mostly by Swedes and Finns who settled on the Delaware. Stacking whole logs was ordinary where they came from."},{"t":"p","text":"It spread because it needs only an axe and trees, almost nothing else — no sawmill, no nails, no carpenter."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Who brought the log cabin to America?","options":["English settlers in Virginia","Spanish and French in Florida","Swedes and Finns on the Delaware"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It names who brought the idea.","explain":"Swedes and Finns settled on the Delaware and brought their log building techniques with them."}}]},{"icon":"📜","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Colonial children learned to read from a tool called a hornbook."},{"t":"term","word":"hornbook","text":"A wooden paddle with one printed sheet under a thin, see-through layer of cow horn."},{"t":"fact","text":"The thin, see-through layer of cow horn protected the printed sheet from small hands wearing it out. One page, and it had to last."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What protected the printed sheet on a hornbook?","options":["A tight cover of stitched leather","A thin see-through layer of cow horn","A thick coat of hard, clear varnish"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It mentions what protects the printed sheet.","explain":"The thin layer of cow horn kept the printed sheet safe from wear and tear."}}]},{"icon":"✉️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Benjamin Franklin started working in his brother's print shop when he was just twelve years old."},{"t":"fact","text":"He was not allowed to write for the paper, so he wrote letters under a false name—a middle-aged widow—and slid them under the door at night. His brother printed them without knowing who wrote them."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How did Franklin get his writing printed?","options":["He wrote letters as a middle-aged widow and slid them under the door","He wrote articles as a village doctor and mailed them to the newspaper","He paid a stranger to carry his stories to the printer in town"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the second sentence. It explains how he got his letters printed.","explain":"Franklin wrote letters under a false name and slid them under the door at night, and his brother printed them without knowing the truth."}}]},{"icon":"🌟","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Benjamin Banneker was self-taught in astronomy and made his own tables of sunrise, tides, and eclipses."},{"t":"fact","text":"He published these tables as almanacs and sent one to Thomas Jefferson with a letter, getting a reply."},{"t":"p","text":"Early accounts say he also built a striking clock entirely out of wood. He took apart a borrowed pocket watch to see how it worked and then crafted his own clock that kept time for decades."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did Banneker build entirely of wood?","options":["A telescope","A printing press","A striking clock"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the last paragraph on the page. It describes what he built out of wood.","explain":"He took apart a pocket watch to understand its mechanism and then built his own striking clock entirely out of wood."}}]},{"icon":"🏡","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Postholes dug by hand. A crowded ship sailing across the ocean. A cabin built in the wilderness. A paddle of horn. A star-gazer who built a clock of wood."},{"t":"p","text":"And a boy printer who slid his letters under a door. Each one shows how people built a life in the colonies."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Jamestown","Found postholes of the fort's wall"],["Mayflower","66 days at sea"],["Swedes and Finns","Brought log building to America"],["hornbook","Wooden paddle with a sheet under cow horn"],["Benjamin Franklin","Wrote letters as a widow"],["Benjamin Banneker","Built a wooden striking clock"]]},"picgame":{"type":"catch","title":"Log Cabin Needs","goal":"Catch what a log cabin needs.","items":[{"icon":"🪓","name":"axe","good":true},{"icon":"🌲","name":"tree","good":true},{"icon":"🪵","name":"log","good":true},{"icon":"🪚","name":"saw","good":false},{"icon":"🔨","name":"hammer","good":false},{"icon":"🏭","name":"sawmill","good":false},{"icon":"🧰","name":"toolbox","good":false}]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"For centuries, people assumed the original Jamestown fort had washed away. What happened in 1994?","options":["An archaeologist dug where he thought it should be and found the fort's wall within weeks","A flood revealed the fort's remains naturally","Colonial records were found describing its exact location"],"answer":0,"explain":"They've since dug up armor, tools, coins, and graves of the first settlers, all inside those walls."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where did the log cabin — often thought of as a classic American idea — actually come from?","options":["Swedish and Finnish settlers, for whom stacking logs was already ordinary at home","English colonists who adapted their timber-frame style","Native American building techniques"],"answer":0,"explain":"English colonists actually built timber-frame houses with sawn boards — log cabins spread because they needed only an axe and trees."},{"type":"tf","prompt":"Benjamin Franklin got his writing published in his brother's newspaper by pretending to be a middle-aged widow.","answer":0,"explain":"He slid the letters under the door at night under a false name, since he wasn't allowed to write for the paper himself."}]},{"id":"america-founding","course":"america","kind":"facts","section":"founding","title":"A New Country","glyph":"📜","n":6,"idea":"The Declaration of Independence is just one sheet, but it's kept in argon gas now. Find out who copied out the Constitution over a weekend and what Washington's false teeth were really made of.","pages":[{"icon":"📜","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The Declaration of Independence is a single sheet you could carry under your arm. It is about 24 by 30 inches and written out by hand."},{"t":"fact","text":"Today, it rests in a sealed case filled with argon, a gas that will not harm old paper, in Washington, D.C. The signatures have faded badly over time, from years on display in sunlight and from an 1820s copying process that lifted some of the ink."},{"t":"p","text":"John Hancock signed large and bold, and his is still the clearest. That is why his name became slang for a signature."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Whose signature is still the clearest on the Declaration of Independence?","options":["John Hancock","Thomas Jefferson","Benjamin Franklin"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the last sentence. It names whose signature is clear.","explain":"John Hancock signed large and bold, and his signature is still the clearest, which is why his name became slang for a signature."}}]},{"icon":"✏️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Jefferson's rough draft of the Declaration of Independence still exists, and it has all the crossings-out on it."},{"t":"term","word":"crossings-out","text":"The marks where words were changed or removed."},{"t":"fact","text":"You can see which words were argued over and what got cut before the final version. It is unusual to be able to do this with any famous document."},{"t":"try","text":"Look up an image of the rough draft online and find one sentence that was changed. Ask yourself why."}]},{"icon":"🖋️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The Constitution is four sheets of parchment, and one man wrote out the whole thing over a weekend."},{"t":"fact","text":"Jacob Shallus was the clerk who did it, and he got paid thirty dollars for his work. His name appears nowhere on it."},{"t":"p","text":"He made a few small mistakes and fixed them with words written between the lines and one scraped-away spot, where the fixes are still visible today."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Who copied the finished Constitution onto parchment by hand?","options":["The founding fathers","The president of the United States","A clerk named Jacob Shallus"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It names the clerk who did it.","explain":"Jacob Shallus was the clerk who wrote it out over a weekend for thirty dollars."}}]},{"icon":"🪁","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"In 1752, Benjamin Franklin published a letter describing an experiment with a kite in a thunderstorm."},{"t":"fact","text":"The letter describes drawing sparks from the storm with a key on a silk ribbon. It is the only account from that year. The story that Franklin flew the kite himself comes from a friend, Joseph Priestley, who wrote it down fifteen years later."},{"t":"p","text":"The letter warned that the silk had to stay dry and the experimenter must stand under cover. At least one scientist who tried something like it was killed by lightning. Never try this yourself."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is the only account of the kite experiment?","options":["A diary kept by his son","Franklin's own letter","A painting from the time"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It says it is the only account from that year.","explain":"The letter describes drawing sparks with a key on a silk ribbon, and it is the only account from that year."}}]},{"icon":"🔔","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The Liberty Bell cracked on its very first test ring after arriving."},{"t":"fact","text":"It was melted down and recast twice by local workmen, but it cracked again later. The famous wide slot was cut deliberately to try to stop the crack spreading."},{"t":"p","text":"But it did not work, and the bell has not been rung properly since."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why was the slot cut in the Liberty Bell?","options":["To try to stop the crack spreading","To make it look more interesting","To make the bell ring louder"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It explains what the slot was for.","explain":"The slot was cut to try to stop the crack from spreading, but it did not work."}}]},{"icon":"🦷","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"You might have heard that George Washington's false teeth were wooden, but that is not true."},{"t":"fact","text":"His surviving set is made of hippopotamus ivory, human teeth, brass screws, and a lead base. They stained dark over time, which is probably where the wood story came from."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What were Washington's false teeth made of?","options":["Carved maple wood, cow bone, iron nails, and tin","Hippopotamus ivory, human teeth, brass screws, and lead","Solid gold, polished silver, glass beads, and clay"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It lists the materials used.","explain":"They were made of hippopotamus ivory, human teeth, brass screws, and a lead base."}}]},{"icon":"🇺🇸","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"One sheet with signatures. Four sheets with words. A crossed-out draft. A kite letter. A cracked Liberty Bell. A set of false teeth."},{"t":"p","text":"Each one tells part of the story."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Declaration of Independence","Kept in argon gas"],["John Hancock","Signature is still the clearest"],["Jacob Shallus","Clerk who copied out the Constitution"],["Franklin's letter","Describes flying a kite in a storm"],["Liberty Bell","Has a slot to stop the crack spreading"],["George Washington's false teeth","Made of hippopotamus ivory and human teeth"]]},"picgame":{"type":"catch","title":"Washington's Teeth","goal":"Catch what Washington's false teeth were made of.","items":[{"icon":"🦛","name":"hippo","good":true},{"icon":"🦷","name":"tooth","good":true},{"icon":"🔩","name":"screw","good":true},{"icon":"🪵","name":"wood","good":false},{"icon":"🪙","name":"gold","good":false},{"icon":"🪨","name":"stone","good":false},{"icon":"🍬","name":"candy","good":false}]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why are the signatures on the Declaration of Independence so faded today, except John Hancock's?","options":["Years of display in sunlight damaged most signatures, though Hancock's stayed clearest","The ink used was naturally unstable","It was copied poorly from the original"],"answer":0,"explain":"That's actually why 'John Hancock' became slang for a signature."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What makes Jefferson's rough draft of the Declaration unusual among famous historical documents?","options":["It still has the crossings-out on it, showing exactly what was argued over and cut","It's the only copy that still exists","It was written in a code that took centuries to decipher"],"answer":0,"explain":"You can see which words were debated before reaching the final version — an unusual thing to have for any famous document."},{"type":"tf","prompt":"George Washington's famous false teeth were actually made of wood.","answer":1,"explain":"The surviving set is made of hippopotamus ivory, human teeth, brass screws, and a lead base — they just stained dark over time."}]},{"id":"america-inventors","course":"america","kind":"facts","section":"inventors","title":"Inventors","glyph":"💡","n":6,"idea":"Edison's notebooks are full of failures. Meet the inventors behind the telegraph, the safe elevator, the traffic signal, and the telephone.","pages":[{"icon":"💡","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Thomas Edison's laboratory left behind about five million pages of notes, drawings, and letters. They are being scanned and read today."},{"t":"fact","text":"Most of what is in them is failure — sketches, dead ends, and lists of things tried and crossed off."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What fills most of Edison's notebooks?","options":["Clever inventions that worked","Failures and dead ends","Perfectly working designs"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the first sentence of the fact box. It tells you what fills most of the notebooks.","explain":"Most of Edison's notebooks are filled with failures, sketches, and things crossed off."}}]},{"icon":"⚡","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Samuel Morse sent the first long telegraph message from Washington to Baltimore in 1844."},{"t":"fact","text":"The message was four words: What hath God wrought. The paper tape with those dots and dashes still exists today."},{"t":"term","word":"wrought","text":"An old word meaning made or done."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many words was the first long telegraph message?","options":["Four","Two","Eight"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the first sentence of the fact box. It tells you how many words the message had.","explain":"The message had four words: What hath God wrought."}}]},{"icon":"🛗","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"In 1854, Elisha Otis stood on a loaded platform hoisted above a crowd and had an assistant cut the rope with an axe."},{"t":"fact","text":"The safety brake caught. This convinced people that elevators were safe to use."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What happened when the rope was cut?","options":["The platform fell to the ground","The people on board jumped to safety","The safety brake caught the platform"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the second sentence of the fact box. It tells you what happened.","explain":"The safety brake caught the platform, proving that elevators could be safe."}}]},{"icon":"🥜","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Cotton is hard on the land. It takes so much from the soil that it can leave fields tired and empty."},{"t":"fact","text":"George Washington Carver pushed farmers to plant peanuts and sweet potatoes instead. Peanut roots carry tiny bacteria that put back nitrogen, a plant food that cotton had used up. He published hundreds of recipes and products to make sure those crops were worth growing."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why did Carver push peanuts?","options":["Cotton strips the soil, and peanuts help it recover","Cotton spoils in storage, and peanuts keep for years","Peanut farms need no workers, but cotton farms do"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the first sentence on the page. It tells you how cotton affects the soil.","explain":"Peanuts let the soil recover from what cotton takes out of it."}}]},{"icon":"😷","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Garrett Morgan built a breathing hood to protect people from smoke and gas."},{"t":"fact","text":"In 1916, he used his breathing hood to rescue men trapped in a collapsed, gas-filled tunnel under Lake Erie."},{"t":"p","text":"Later, Morgan patented a traffic signal. Signals then had only two positions, stop and go. His added a third that stops everyone at once."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is Morgan's third position on a traffic signal?","options":["Only the cars on one street stop","Everybody stops at once","Everybody goes at once"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the last sentence on the page. It tells you about the third position.","explain":"The third position stops everybody, so people on foot can cross safely."}}]},{"icon":"☎️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"In 1876, Alexander Graham Bell filed his telephone patent."},{"t":"fact","text":"On the very same day, another inventor named Elisha Gray filed a description of something very similar. Which arrived first has been argued about ever since."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Who else filed a similar description on the same day as Bell?","options":["Thomas Edison","Nikola Tesla","Elisha Gray"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the second sentence. It names who else filed that day.","explain":"Both Bell and Elisha Gray filed descriptions of something very similar on the same day in 1876."}}]},{"icon":"🔧","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Failures in notebooks. Words by wire. A rope cut on purpose. A patent race on the same day."},{"t":"p","text":"Each invention, a step built from what was learned."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Thomas Edison","Left behind five million pages of papers"],["Samuel Morse","Sent \"What hath God wrought\""],["Elisha Otis","Demonstrated a safety brake for elevators"],["George Washington Carver","Promoted peanuts and sweet potatoes"],["Garrett Morgan","Invented a breathing hood and traffic signal"],["Bell","Filed his telephone patent on the same day as Gray"]]},"picgame":{"type":"tapall","title":"Inventors' Work","goal":"Tap what these inventors worked on.","items":[{"icon":"☎️","name":"telephone","good":true},{"icon":"🛗","name":"elevator","good":true},{"icon":"🚦","name":"traffic light","good":true},{"icon":"🥜","name":"peanut","good":true},{"icon":"🍠","name":"sweet potato","good":true},{"icon":"🌳","name":"tree","good":false},{"icon":"🐘","name":"elephant","good":false},{"icon":"🌊","name":"wave","good":false}]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What do Edison's roughly five million pages of notebooks mostly contain?","options":["Failures — sketches, dead ends, and lists of things tried and crossed off","Finished diagrams of his successful inventions","Personal letters and business records"],"answer":0,"explain":"Hundreds of filament materials were tested for the light bulb alone, one after another, each written down."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How did Elisha Otis prove his elevator safety brake actually worked, in 1854?","options":["He stood on a platform hoisted above a crowd and had an assistant cut the rope with an axe","He wrote a detailed mathematical paper on the physics involved","He demonstrated it privately to investors only"],"answer":0,"explain":"The brake caught as designed — tall buildings only became worth building once people trusted elevators enough to use them."},{"type":"tf","prompt":"George Washington Carver is credited with inventing peanut butter.","answer":1,"explain":"Peanut butter already existed — what he actually did was find hundreds of uses for peanuts, persuading farmers to grow them to help restore soil cotton had stripped."}]},{"id":"america-frontier","course":"america","kind":"facts","section":"frontier","title":"Heading West","glyph":"🐎","n":6,"idea":"A wagon trail carved a 5-foot-deep groove in rock. Discover how oxen, sod houses, and windmills shaped life on the frontier.","pages":[{"icon":"🛤️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Stand in a groove cut into solid rock by wagon wheels near Guernsey, Wyoming."},{"t":"fact","text":"The Oregon Trail crossed a ridge of soft sandstone there. So many iron-rimmed wheels went over the same line that they cut a groove up to 5 feet deep. It was worn by wheels, one wagon at a time."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How was the groove made?","options":["It was worn by wheels, one wagon at a time","A stream cut it into the rock over many years","Workers cut it out with picks and shovels"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It tells you how the groove formed.","explain":"The groove was worn into the soft sandstone by countless iron-rimmed wheels, one wagon at a time."}}]},{"icon":"🐂","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Almost nobody rode in the wagons because they were crammed with supplies and the ride was too rough."},{"t":"fact","text":"The animals pulling most of the wagons were oxen, not horses. Oxen were cheaper and stronger, could live on rougher grass, and were less likely to be stolen."},{"t":"term","word":"oxen","text":"Big, strong cattle used for work."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What pulled most of the wagons?","options":["Horses","Oxen","Mules"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It says oxen were cheaper and stronger than horses.","explain":"The wagons were mostly pulled by oxen, which were cheaper, stronger, and less likely to be stolen than horses."}}]},{"icon":"🐎","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The Pony Express carried mail nearly 2,000 miles in about ten days, changing horses every 10 to 15 miles."},{"t":"fact","text":"It was famous right away, but it only lasted eighteen months because the telegraph wire reached California and made it pointless overnight."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why did the Pony Express end?","options":["The mountain trail closed for good","The riders all quit to dig for gold","The telegraph reached the coast"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It says the telegraph made it pointless overnight.","explain":"The telegraph reaching the coast made the Pony Express unnecessary, so it ended."}}]},{"icon":"🌾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"On the plains, where there was no timber, houses were built out of the ground itself."},{"t":"fact","text":"Strips of sod were cut with a special plow and stacked like bricks, grass side down. The walls were about 2 ft thick, warm in winter, but they leaked mud when it rained."},{"t":"p","text":"Photographs show families standing in front of these sod houses with cows sometimes even standing on the roof. It was a common enough sight to be a joke at the time."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What were the plains houses built of?","options":["Strips of sod cut from the ground","Logs cut from nearby pine forests","Blocks of dried clay and straw"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It says the walls were made of strips of sod stacked like bricks.","explain":"The houses were built using strips of sod cut with a special plow and stacked grass side down."}}]},{"icon":"💨","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Two inventions made farming the dry plains possible."},{"t":"fact","text":"Barbed wire fenced land where there was no wood for rails. Windmills pumped water up from far below the ground with nobody doing the work."},{"t":"p","text":"The windmills are still standing all over the American West. They are tall metal towers with a small tail that turns the wheel into the wind by itself."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did the windmills do?","options":["Plowed the fields without any animals","Pumped water up from far below the ground","Sent signals to neighbors across the plains"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It says the windmills pumped water up from far below the ground.","explain":"Windmills pumped water up from deep underground without anyone having to operate them."}}]},{"icon":"🍳","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A chuckwagon was a kitchen on wheels that followed the cattle drives."},{"t":"fact","text":"It had a hinged lid that dropped down to make a work surface. Behind it were drawers for flour, coffee, beans, and sourdough."},{"t":"p","text":"A rancher named Charles Goodnight built the first one in 1866 from an old army wagon, with iron axles that would not break."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did the hinged lid make?","options":["A storage compartment","A bed for the cook","A work surface"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the second sentence. It says the hinged lid dropped down to make a work surface.","explain":"The hinged lid made a work surface where the cook could prepare meals."}}]},{"icon":"🌅","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A groove worn into solid rock. Oxen pulling a wagon. A mail rider on horseback. A house of sod. A chuckwagon kitchen on wheels."},{"t":"p","text":"Each one part of moving west."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Groove","Cut by iron-rimmed wheels"],["Oxen","Cheaper and stronger than horses"],["Pony Express","Carried mail for eighteen months"],["Sod houses","Built with strips of ground"],["Windmills","Pumped water without human work"],["Chuckwagon","Kitchen on wheels with a hinged lid"]]},"picgame":{"type":"catch","title":"Frontier Animals","goal":"Catch the animals from the pioneer days.","items":[{"icon":"🐂","name":"ox","good":true},{"icon":"🐎","name":"horse","good":true},{"icon":"🐄","name":"cow","good":true},{"icon":"🐘","name":"elephant","good":false},{"icon":"🐧","name":"penguin","good":false},{"icon":"🦒","name":"giraffe","good":false},{"icon":"🐙","name":"octopus","good":false},{"icon":"🐬","name":"dolphin","good":false}]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"How did wagon wheels carve a groove up to 5 feet deep into solid rock along the Oregon Trail?","options":["So many iron-tyred wheels crossed the same soft sandstone ridge, one wagon at a time, over years","Workers deliberately carved the groove to mark the trail","A flash flood eroded the rock quickly"],"answer":0,"explain":"Nobody dug it — it was worn down by wheels, one wagon at a time."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why did the famous Pony Express only last eighteen months?","options":["The telegraph reached the coast and made it pointless overnight","It ran out of money too quickly to continue","Too many riders quit the dangerous job"],"answer":0,"explain":"It was famous immediately but never made money — it's remembered out of all proportion to how long it actually existed."},{"type":"tf","prompt":"On the treeless plains, settlers built houses out of strips of cut turf, stacked like bricks.","answer":0,"explain":"The walls were about 2 feet thick — warm in winter, but known to leak mud when it rained."}]},{"id":"america-machines","course":"america","kind":"facts","section":"machines","title":"Rails & Machines","glyph":"🚂","n":6,"idea":"When the chief engineer of the Brooklyn Bridge fell ill, his wife Emily Roebling learned the math and carried his plans to the site. Canals, boats, and railroads changed how goods moved.","pages":[{"icon":"🛶","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Have you ever dug a trench in the sand at the beach? Now imagine digging a canal 363 miles long by hand."},{"t":"fact","text":"The Erie Canal ran across New York State, with 83 locks, which are water-filled chambers that lift boats up or lower them to the next level. Boats were pulled by mules walking a towpath beside the water. It cut the cost of moving a ton of freight by about ninety percent."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What pulled the boats along the Erie Canal?","options":["Steam engines on the boat","Mules walking a towpath","Sails raised on tall masts"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It tells you what pulled the boats.","explain":"Mules walked a towpath beside the canal, pulling the boats along."}}]},{"icon":"⛴️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Mississippi steamboats floated in so little water that captains joked they could run on heavy dew."},{"t":"fact","text":"Flat-bottomed hulls and paddle wheels let them work rivers a few feet deep, and they carried enormous loads."},{"t":"term","word":"paddle wheels","text":"The big round wheels on the side of the boat that turn to push it through the water."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why could steamboats work shallow rivers?","options":["Flat-bottomed hulls and paddle wheels","Deep keels and tall canvas sails","Tall smokestacks and strong iron anchors"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It mentions two key parts of the boats.","explain":"Flat-bottomed hulls and paddle wheels allowed them to navigate rivers that were only a few feet deep."}}]},{"icon":"🔨","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Two railroads met at Promontory Summit in 1869 to finish the first cross-country railway."},{"t":"fact","text":"A telegraph wire was attached to the hammer and the last spike. The blow was meant to send a signal down the line, so the word DONE would go out to both coasts at the same moment."},{"t":"p","text":"The man swinging the hammer even missed on the first try, but the telegraph operator sent the signal anyway."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How did the news go out when the spike was hit?","options":["A rider on a fast horse carried the news to town","A large flag was waved from the hilltop to the crowd","A telegraph wire was fixed to the hammer and the spike"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It mentions the telegraph wire attached to both the hammer and the spike.","explain":"The telegraph wire was connected to the hammer and the spike, so when it was hit, the signal went out instantly."}}]},{"icon":"🕰️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Before 1883, every town set its own clock by the sun. Noon in one place was several minutes off from noon in the next."},{"t":"fact","text":"Railway timetables became impossible with all those different times, so the railroads invented four time zones and imposed them."},{"t":"p","text":"The government did not make it law for another thirty-five years. The trains had already settled it."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Who made the time zones?","options":["The government","The railroads","Each town"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It says who created the time zones.","explain":"The railroads invented and imposed the four time zones to make their timetables work."}}]},{"icon":"🚗","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Henry Ford did not invent the car. He found a new way to build it, with a moving assembly line."},{"t":"fact","text":"Instead of workers walking around a car that stayed in one place, he moved the car past the workers, and each worker did one job. Building one chassis, the frame the car body sits on, went from twelve and a half hours to just ninety-three minutes."},{"t":"p","text":"The idea came partly from meat-packing plants. There, carcasses traveled on overhead rails, with workers making specific cuts as they passed by."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How long did building a chassis take after Ford's change?","options":["Ninety-three minutes","Twelve and a half hours","Two hours"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It gives the new time.","explain":"After Ford's change, building a chassis took ninety-three minutes."}}]},{"icon":"🌉","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The chief engineer of the Brooklyn Bridge, Washington Roebling, became too sick to leave his bed after working in the air-filled chambers under the river."},{"t":"fact","text":"His wife, Emily Roebling, filled in for him. She learned the mathematics, carried his instructions to the site every day, and dealt with contractors for eleven years."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Who carried the engineer's instructions to the site?","options":["A hired helper","The bridge workers","His wife Emily"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the second sentence. It names who took over.","explain":"Emily learned the math and carried the instructions daily."}}]},{"icon":"🚂","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Canals and boats. Boats and locks. Rails and trains. Clocks set by the railroads. A bridge and a wife who learned the math."},{"t":"p","text":"All working together to move and lift in new ways."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Erie Canal","363 miles long with locks"],["Mules","Pulled boats along the canal"],["Flat-bottomed hulls","Worked shallow rivers"],["Promontory Summit","Two railroads met here"],["Telegraph wire","Sent signal for last spike"],["Emily","Carried instructions to bridge site"]]},"picgame":{"type":"sort","title":"Water or Land","goal":"Does it go on water or on land?","bins":[{"icon":"🌊","label":"water"},{"icon":"🛣️","label":"land"}],"items":[{"icon":"🛶","name":"boat","bin":0},{"icon":"⛴️","name":"steamboat","bin":0},{"icon":"🚢","name":"ship","bin":0},{"icon":"🚂","name":"train","bin":1},{"icon":"🚗","name":"car","bin":1},{"icon":"🛤️","name":"tracks","bin":1}]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"How did the whole country learn instantly when the transcontinental railroad was completed in 1869?","options":["A telegraph wire was attached to the ceremonial hammer and last spike, sending a signal the moment it struck","Newspapers had reporters stationed at the site to telegraph the news","Church bells were rung in a relay across the country"],"answer":0,"explain":"The man swinging the hammer actually missed on his first try, but the telegraph operator sent the signal anyway."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What was Henry Ford's actual key innovation with the assembly line?","options":["He moved the factory past the car instead of having workers move around a stationary car","He invented the automobile engine","He was the first to use steel instead of iron in car frames"],"answer":0,"explain":"Building one chassis went from twelve and a half hours to just ninety-three minutes — the idea partly came from meat-packing plants."},{"type":"tf","prompt":"Before 1883, every American town set its own clock by the sun, making railroad scheduling nearly impossible.","answer":0,"explain":"The railroads simply invented four time zones and imposed them — the government didn't make it official law for another 35 years."}]},{"id":"america-flight","course":"america","kind":"facts","section":"flight","title":"Flight","glyph":"✈️","n":6,"idea":"The first flight lasted only 12 seconds, shorter than most TV commercials. See how the Wright brothers turned that into minutes aloft.","pages":[{"icon":"✈️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The first airplane flight by the Wright brothers in 1903 lasted just 12 seconds and covered 120 feet. That's shorter than the wingspan of a jumbo jet, which is about 211 ft (64 m)."},{"t":"fact","text":"There were five witnesses and one photograph. The man who took it had never used a camera before, and was told simply to squeeze the bulb."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How long did the first flight last?","options":["5 minutes","12 seconds","2 hours"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the first sentence on the page. It gives the duration.","explain":"The flight lasted just 12 seconds."}}]},{"icon":"🚲","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The Wright brothers paid for their flying experiments entirely from a bicycle shop in Dayton, Ohio."},{"t":"term","word":"sprockets","text":"The toothed wheels that a bike chain wraps around. Their teeth fit into the links and turn the wheel."},{"t":"fact","text":"The bicycle shop also gave them chains, sprockets, and a feel for balance. They shipped the aircraft to North Carolina in crates for steady wind and soft sand."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What paid for the Wrights' flying experiments?","options":["Their bicycle shop","Their inheritance","Government grants"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the first sentence on the page. It mentions what funded their experiments.","explain":"The profits from selling bicycles paid for their experiments."}}]},{"icon":"🌬️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The Wright brothers ran into trouble with the published tables of how wings should work."},{"t":"fact","text":"The tables kept giving them wrong results, so they built a wind tunnel out of a wooden box and a fan."},{"t":"p","text":"In this setup, they tested around two hundred wing shapes themselves and wrote down the numbers."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why did the Wrights build a wind tunnel?","options":["To make their bicycles faster","To launch their airplane into the air","The published tables kept giving wrong results"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the second sentence on the page. It explains why they needed the wind tunnel.","explain":"They found that the published tables were not reliable, so they decided to test wing shapes themselves."}}]},{"icon":"🦅","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The Wright brothers' real invention was not just the wings or the engine. It was control."},{"t":"fact","text":"They twisted the whole wing to make the aircraft tip to one side, which pilots call a roll, inspired by how a buzzard tilts. They worked the twist and the rudder, the upright tail flap that steers left and right, together with one movement."},{"t":"try","text":"Hold a long cardboard box by the ends and twist it gently. That is wing warping. Modern planes do the same job with hinged flaps at the wingtips."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What was the Wrights' real invention?","options":["The engine","The wings","Control"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It explains what control means in their design.","explain":"They figured out how to twist the whole wing to make the aircraft roll, just like a buzzard tilts its wings."}}]},{"icon":"🔄","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Two years after their first flight, the Wright brothers had a much better machine. This one could stay up for 39 minutes and circle a field about 30 times."},{"t":"fact","text":"The aircraft that actually mattered is called Flyer III. It is on display in Dayton."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How long did Flyer III stay up?","options":["39 minutes","5 seconds","1 hour"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the second sentence. It gives the number of minutes.","explain":"Flyer III could stay up for 39 minutes and circle a field 30 times."}}]},{"icon":"🌙","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Flying led all the way to the moon. A bootprint there stays put, because there is no wind or rain to erase it."},{"t":"fact","text":"The astronauts also left mirrors, and laboratories on Earth still bounce lasers off them to measure the distance to the moon."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How is the distance to the moon measured?","options":["By looking through powerful telescopes","By bouncing lasers off mirrors the astronauts left","By sending more astronauts with measuring tapes"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the second sentence. It tells you what laboratories use to measure the distance.","explain":"Labs bounce lasers off the mirrors left by astronauts to measure the distance, showing the moon moving away about an inch and a half a year."}}]},{"icon":"✈️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Twelve seconds in the air, a wooden box with a fan, a twisted wing, a bootprint on the moon."},{"t":"p","text":"It took careful testing, one small step at a time, to get from twelve seconds to the moon."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Flyer III","Could stay up for 39 minutes"],["Wing warping","Twisting the whole wing to roll"],["Wind tunnel","Wooden box with a fan for testing wings"],["Buzzard","Inspired the Wrights' wing control"],["Bouncing lasers","Measure the distance to the moon"],["12 seconds","Duration of the first flight"]]},"picgame":{"type":"catch","title":"Wright Brothers","goal":"Catch what the Wright brothers used.","items":[{"icon":"🚲","name":"bicycle","good":true},{"icon":"📦","name":"box","good":true},{"icon":"✈️","name":"airplane","good":true},{"icon":"💨","name":"wind","good":true},{"icon":"🚀","name":"rocket","good":false},{"icon":"🚗","name":"car","good":false},{"icon":"🚁","name":"helicopter","good":false},{"icon":"🎈","name":"balloon","good":false}]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"How did the Wright brothers solve the problem of published wing-performance tables giving them the wrong results?","options":["They built their own wind tunnel out of a wooden box and a fan, and tested about 200 wing shapes themselves","They hired mathematicians to recalculate the existing tables","They copied designs from European inventors instead"],"answer":0,"explain":"The published tables really were wrong — everyone else had been designing from them anyway."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What was the Wright brothers' real breakthrough invention, more than the wing or the engine itself?","options":["Control — twisting the wing to roll the aircraft, inspired by watching a buzzard tilt","A lightweight, powerful engine","A way to launch the aircraft without a runway"],"answer":0,"explain":"They worked the wing twist and rudder together with one coordinated movement."},{"type":"tf","prompt":"The Wright brothers' famous 1903 flight could barely get off the ground, and their real breakthrough aircraft came two years later.","answer":0,"explain":"Their 1905 machine, Flyer III, stayed up for 39 minutes and circled a field 30 times — almost nobody can name it."}]},{"id":"america-everyday","course":"america","kind":"facts","section":"everyday","title":"Everyday Life","glyph":"🏠","n":6,"idea":"People used to cut ice from ponds and ship it packed in sawdust. Discover how farm families cooperated to bring electricity to rural areas.","pages":[{"icon":"🧊","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Before refrigerators, ice was a crop. Men cut it from frozen New England ponds in blocks."},{"t":"fact","text":"They packed the ice in sawdust and shipped it as far as India. Much of it arrived unmelted."},{"t":"p","text":"Frederic Tudor thought of this idea. Everybody said it was ridiculous, but he made a fortune."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What was the ice packed in?","options":["Wet sand","Cotton","Sawdust"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the first sentence of the fact box. It mentions what the ice was packed in.","explain":"The ice was packed in sawdust to keep it from melting during shipping."}}]},{"icon":"🏠","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"For about thirty years, you could order a house from a catalog. It would arrive by rail as roughly 30,000 numbered pieces with a book of instructions."},{"t":"term","word":"catalog","text":"A big book that lists things you can buy and order."},{"t":"fact","text":"Thousands of these houses are still standing today, and people live in them."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"About how many pieces came in a catalog house?","options":["About 3,000","About 30,000","More than 100,000"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the second sentence on the page. It mentions the number of pieces.","explain":"The house arrived with about 30,000 numbered pieces and a book to help you build it."}}]},{"icon":"💡","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"In 1935, about one farm in ten had electricity. Cities had been lit for fifty years by then."},{"t":"fact","text":"Running lines out to scattered farms cost more than any company could make back. So farm families formed their own cooperatives (neighbors who own a business together), borrowed money from the government, and strung much of the wire themselves."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Who strung the wire to the farms?","options":["The farm families themselves, in cooperatives","Crews of paid soldiers sent out by the army","The power companies, using hired city crews"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It explains who took charge.","explain":"Farm families formed cooperatives and did it themselves because the cost was too high for any company to handle."}}]},{"icon":"🏫","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"In a one-room school, kids of every age were there together."},{"t":"fact","text":"Older children taught the younger ones because the teacher could not be everywhere at once."},{"t":"p","text":"Many schools used the same series of reading books, the McGuffey Readers. This way, if a child moved to another state, they could pick up right where they left off."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Who helped teach the younger children?","options":["The older children","The school janitor","A visiting preacher"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It explains who took charge.","explain":"Older children taught the younger ones because the teacher could not be everywhere at once."}}]},{"icon":"🫙","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Home canning is like giving food a hot bath in a sealed jar."},{"t":"fact","text":"The heat kills the germs inside, and as it cools, the lid gets sucked down tight. When you open a jar, the pop you hear is air going back in for the first time since it was sealed."},{"t":"try","text":"Next time someone opens a jar, listen for that pop."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is the pop when you open a canned jar?","options":["Gas leaking out as the food slowly went bad inside it","Steam rushing out for the first time since it was cooked","Air going back in for the first time since it was sealed"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the second sentence of the fact box. It explains what causes the pop.","explain":"The pop is air going back into the jar for the first time since it was sealed."}}]},{"icon":"📖","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"In many homes, the mail-order catalog was a big book, right up there with the Bible."},{"t":"fact","text":"Children learned to read from it, cut it up for paper dolls, and when a new one came, the old one went out to the outhouse."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did children cut up for paper dolls?","options":["The family Bible","The catalog","A schoolbook"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It mentions cutting the catalog for paper dolls.","explain":"Children cut up the mail-order catalog to make paper dolls."}}]},{"icon":"🏡","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Ice cut from a frozen pond. A house shipped by rail in numbered pieces. Wires strung by neighbors. A catalog read until it fell apart."},{"t":"p","text":"Ice, catalog houses, wires, jars, and books: ordinary things that families in the past had to find their own ways to get."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Frederic Tudor","Made a fortune selling ice"],["Home canning","Like giving food a hot bath in a sealed jar"],["Ice","Shipped packed in sawdust"],["Catalog","Used for paper dolls"],["One-room school","Older children taught younger ones"],["Farm families","Strung wires themselves"]]},"picgame":{"type":"tapall","title":"Long Ago Things","goal":"Tap what families used long ago.","items":[{"icon":"🧊","name":"ice","good":true},{"icon":"🥫","name":"can","good":true},{"icon":"📖","name":"catalog","good":true},{"icon":"🏠","name":"house","good":true},{"icon":"💡","name":"light","good":true},{"icon":"📱","name":"phone","good":false},{"icon":"🎮","name":"game","good":false},{"icon":"🤖","name":"robot","good":false}]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"Before refrigeration existed, how did people get ice, even in places far from any cold climate?","options":["Men cut it from frozen New England ponds and shipped it as far as India, packed in sawdust","It was manufactured using early refrigeration machines","It was imported from northern countries by rail"],"answer":0,"explain":"Everyone told Frederic Tudor the idea was ridiculous, and his first cargo mostly melted — but it eventually made his fortune."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What was unusual about how some Americans got their houses in the early 1900s?","options":["You could order one by mail, and it arrived as 30,000 numbered pieces to assemble yourself","Houses were built entirely by local co-ops for free","Pre-built houses were shipped whole by train"],"answer":0,"explain":"Thousands of these mail-order houses are still standing, and people still live in them today."},{"type":"tf","prompt":"By 1935, electricity had already reached most American farms, just like it had reached cities decades earlier.","answer":1,"explain":"Only about one farm in ten had electricity in 1935 — running lines to scattered farms cost more than companies could profit from, so farmers formed their own co-ops."}]},{"id":"america-revolution","course":"america","kind":"facts","section":"revolution","title":"The Revolution","glyph":"🔔","n":6,"idea":"Tea in a harbor, a midnight ride and a Declaration of Independence: how a new country began.","pages":[{"icon":"🔔","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"On the night of December 16, members of the Sons of Liberty boarded the ship Dartmouth in Boston."},{"t":"term","word":"harbor","text":"A place where a ship like the Dartmouth stays, as in Boston Harbor."},{"t":"fact","text":"They threw 340 or 342 chests of tea into Boston Harbor."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many chests of tea were thrown into Boston Harbor?","options":["Only 34","340 or 342","More than 3,400"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. The answer is there.","explain":"They threw 340 or 342 chests of tea into Boston Harbor."}}]},{"icon":"📅","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"In November 1773 the merchant ship Dartmouth arrived in Boston harbor."},{"t":"fact","text":"It carried the first shipment of tea made under the terms of the Tea Act."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What was the name of the merchant ship that carried the tea?","options":["Mayflower","Dartmouth","Santa Maria"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"In November 1773 the merchant ship Dartmouth arrived in Boston harbor."}}]},{"icon":"🧑","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"In 1750, Paul Revere was 15 years old."},{"t":"fact","text":"He was part of the first group to ring the new bells at Christ Church in Boston, the Old North Church."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How old was Paul Revere when he rang the new bells?","options":["Just 5 years","15 years old","Nearly 50 years"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"In 1750, Paul Revere was 15 years old."}}]},{"icon":"👤","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Paul Revere's midnight ride made him an American folk hero."},{"t":"fact","text":"Henry Wadsworth Longfellow told the story in his 1861 poem Paul Revere's Ride."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Who wrote the poem about Paul Revere's ride?","options":["Elizabeth Cady Stanton","Henry Wadsworth Longfellow","Ralph Waldo Emerson"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. The answer is there.","explain":"Henry Wadsworth Longfellow told the story in his 1861 poem Paul Revere's Ride."}}]},{"icon":"⏳","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The Declaration of Independence was first read in public on July 8, 1776."},{"t":"fact","text":"It was read at noon in three places: Trenton, Easton and Philadelphia."}]},{"icon":"👑","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"In 1775, the Second Continental Congress made an army, the Continental Army."},{"t":"fact","text":"It chose George Washington to lead it."}]},{"icon":"🔎","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Chests of tea in the harbor. A ship named Dartmouth. Bells in Boston. A midnight ride. A Declaration read at noon. An army with Washington."},{"t":"p","text":"The tea, the ride and the Declaration."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Dartmouth","Ship that carried the tea"],["Sons of Liberty","Threw chests of tea into the harbor"],["Paul Revere","Rang the new bells at Old North Church"],["Longfellow","Wrote the poem Paul Revere's Ride"],["Washington","Chosen to lead the Continental Army"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where did the Sons of Liberty throw the tea?","options":["Into a muddy river","Into Boston Harbor","Onto the busy docks"],"answer":1,"explain":"They threw 340 or 342 chests of tea into Boston Harbor."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Who was chosen to lead the Continental Army?","options":["Henry Wadsworth Longfellow","George Washington","Abraham Lincoln"],"answer":1,"explain":"It chose George Washington to lead it."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"In what year did Longfellow's poem about Paul Revere's ride come out?","options":["1681","1861","1816"],"answer":1,"explain":"Henry Wadsworth Longfellow told the story in his 1861 poem Paul Revere's Ride."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"On what date was the Declaration of Independence first read in public?","options":["May 1, 1776","July 8, 1776","October 31, 1776"],"answer":1,"explain":"The Declaration of Independence was first read in public on July 8, 1776."}]},{"id":"america-constitution","course":"america","kind":"facts","section":"constitution","title":"The Constitution","glyph":"📜","n":6,"idea":"The Constitution was written in Philadelphia, and George Washington was the first president.","pages":[{"icon":"📜","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"In 1787, men met at Independence Hall in Philadelphia."},{"t":"fact","text":"They wrote the Constitution there."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"In which city was the Constitution drafted?","options":["New York City","Philadelphia","Washington"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"In 1787, men met at Independence Hall in Philadelphia."}}]},{"icon":"🏆","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The U.S. Constitution is still in use."},{"t":"fact","text":"It is the oldest written national constitution still in use in the world."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Among written national constitutions still in use, the U.S. Constitution is which?","options":["The newest","The oldest","The longest"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"It is the oldest written national constitution still in use in the world."}}]},{"icon":"⚙️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The Bill of Rights is part of the Constitution."},{"t":"term","word":"amendment","text":"A change or addition to the Constitution."},{"t":"fact","text":"It is the first ten amendments to the United States Constitution."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many amendments are in the Bill of Rights?","options":["Six","Ten","Twenty"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. The answer is there.","explain":"It is the first ten amendments to the United States Constitution."}}]},{"icon":"🧑","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"55 delegates drafted the Constitution."},{"t":"fact","text":"They are among the men known as the Founding Fathers of the new nation."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many delegates drafted the Constitution?","options":["5","55","550"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"55 delegates drafted the Constitution."}}]},{"icon":"🔧","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The 9th Amendment is about rights."},{"t":"fact","text":"It says that a right is not missing just because it is not listed in the Bill of Rights."}]},{"icon":"👤","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"George Washington was the first U.S. president."},{"t":"fact","text":"Every vote of the Electoral College elected him in 1789 and again in 1792."}]},{"icon":"🔎","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Men in Philadelphia. The oldest written constitution. Ten amendments. 55 delegates. The first president."},{"t":"p","text":"The Constitution is still in use."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Constitution","Oldest written national constitution still in use"],["Bill of Rights","The first ten amendments to the Constitution"],["Philadelphia","Where the Constitution was written"],["Washington","First U.S. president"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"In what year did men meet at Independence Hall in Philadelphia?","options":["1778","1787","1877"],"answer":1,"explain":"In 1787, men met at Independence Hall in Philadelphia."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Who was the first U.S. president?","options":["Abraham Lincoln","George Washington","Theodore Roosevelt"],"answer":1,"explain":"George Washington was the first U.S. president."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"In which years did the Electoral College elect George Washington?","options":["Only in 1801","1789 and 1792","In 1776 and 1783"],"answer":1,"explain":"Every vote of the Electoral College elected him in 1789 and again in 1792."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is the Bill of Rights?","options":["A list of all the states","The first ten amendments","The names of the delegates"],"answer":1,"explain":"The Bill of Rights is the first ten amendments to the United States Constitution."}]},{"id":"america-presidents","course":"america","kind":"facts","section":"presidents","title":"Presidents","glyph":"🎩","n":6,"idea":"A president born in a log cabin, and one who became president at 42, the youngest ever.","pages":[{"icon":"🎩","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Abraham Lincoln was born in a one-room log cabin in Kentucky."},{"t":"term","word":"log cabin","text":"A small house made from logs, like the cabin where Lincoln was born."},{"t":"fact","text":"He was born on February 12, 1809."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What kind of house was Lincoln born in?","options":["A tall brick mansion","A one-room log cabin","A big stone castle"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"Abraham Lincoln was born in a one-room log cabin in Kentucky."}}]},{"icon":"📅","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"On January 1, 1863, Lincoln said that slaves in the rebel states were free."},{"t":"fact","text":"This was the Emancipation Proclamation."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did the Emancipation Proclamation declare?","options":["Soldiers in the northern states were paid","Slaves in the rebel states were free","Farmers in the western states were rich"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"On January 1, 1863, Lincoln said that slaves in the rebel states were free."}}]},{"icon":"🏆","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Theodore Roosevelt was 42 years old when he was first inaugurated."},{"t":"fact","text":"That made him the youngest person to hold the office of president."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How old was Theodore Roosevelt at his first inauguration?","options":["Just 24","42 years old","About 62 years"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"Theodore Roosevelt was 42 years old when he was first inaugurated."}}]},{"icon":"🥇","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Theodore Roosevelt won the 1906 Nobel Peace Prize."},{"t":"fact","text":"It was for his efforts to end the Russo-Japanese War. He was the first non-European to win a Nobel Prize."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What prize did Theodore Roosevelt win in 1906?","options":["A gold medal of honor","The Nobel Peace Prize","A big trophy cup"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"Theodore Roosevelt won the 1906 Nobel Peace Prize."}}]},{"icon":"🧑","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Theodore Roosevelt went hiking with his family in the Alps in 1869."},{"t":"fact","text":"He found that exercise helped his asthma and lifted his spirits."}]},{"icon":"🏗️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The White House was built between 1792 and 1800."},{"t":"fact","text":"Its outside walls are Aquia Creek sandstone painted white."}]},{"icon":"🔎","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A log cabin. A proclamation that said slaves were free. A young president. A Nobel Peace Prize. A house painted white."},{"t":"p","text":"Presidents did big things."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Lincoln","Born in a one-room log cabin"],["Roosevelt","Youngest person to hold the office"],["White House","Built between 1792 and 1800"],["Nobel Peace Prize","Won in 1906 by Roosevelt"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"In which state was Lincoln born?","options":["Boston","Kentucky","Virginia"],"answer":1,"explain":"Abraham Lincoln was born in a one-room log cabin in Kentucky."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did Theodore Roosevelt find helped his asthma?","options":["Medicine","Exercise","Sleeping"],"answer":1,"explain":"He found that exercise helped his asthma and lifted his spirits."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What are the outside walls of the White House made of?","options":["Red brick from Virginia","Aquia Creek sandstone","White painted wood"],"answer":1,"explain":"Its outside walls are Aquia Creek sandstone painted white."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which president was the youngest person to hold the office?","options":["Abraham Lincoln","Theodore Roosevelt","George Washington"],"answer":1,"explain":"That made him the youngest person to hold the office of president."}]},{"id":"america-civilwar","course":"america","kind":"facts","section":"civilwar","title":"The Civil War","glyph":"🛡️","n":6,"idea":"A very short speech, a surrender at Appomattox, and a woman who rescued enslaved people.","pages":[{"icon":"🛡️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Americans argued about slavery for years."},{"t":"term","word":"elected","text":"Chosen by votes, like Abraham Lincoln when he was elected president."},{"t":"fact","text":"In 1860, Abraham Lincoln was elected president. He did not want slavery to spread."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Who won the 1860 presidential election?","options":["George Washington","Abraham Lincoln","Theodore Roosevelt"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. The answer is there.","explain":"In 1860, Abraham Lincoln was elected president."}}]},{"icon":"📅","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"On April 9, 1865, General Lee surrendered to General Grant at Appomattox Court House."},{"t":"fact","text":"That began the end of the war."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Who surrendered to Grant on April 9, 1865?","options":["General Custer","General Lee","General Sherman"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"On April 9, 1865, General Lee surrendered to General Grant at Appomattox Court House."}}]},{"icon":"🏆","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Lincoln's Gettysburg Address was only 271 words long and ten sentences."},{"t":"fact","text":"The speaker before him talked for two hours and used 13,607 words."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many words was Lincoln's Gettysburg Address?","options":["Over 2,000","271 words","Only 27"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"Lincoln's Gettysburg Address was only 271 words long and ten sentences."}}]},{"icon":"🧑","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Harriet Tubman escaped slavery."},{"t":"fact","text":"Then she made some 13 trips to rescue about 70 enslaved people."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"About how many people did Harriet Tubman rescue?","options":["Just 7","About 70","Nearly 7,000"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. The answer is there.","explain":"Then she made some 13 trips to rescue about 70 enslaved people."}}]},{"icon":"👤","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Harriet Tubman guided a raid at Combahee Ferry."},{"t":"fact","text":"It freed more than 700 enslaved people."}]},{"icon":"⏳","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The Emancipation Proclamation changed the lives of more than 3.5 million enslaved people."},{"t":"fact","text":"It made them free."}]},{"icon":"🔎","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"An election in 1860. A surrender at Appomattox. A short address. Harriet Tubman and her trips. A raid at a ferry."},{"t":"p","text":"Freedom came to many."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Lincoln","Elected president in 1860"],["Lee","Surrendered to General Grant"],["Gettysburg Address","Only 271 words long"],["Tubman","Rescued about 70 enslaved people"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"Who guided a raid at Combahee Ferry?","options":["Abraham Lincoln","Harriet Tubman","General Grant"],"answer":1,"explain":"Harriet Tubman guided a raid at Combahee Ferry."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How long did the speaker before Lincoln talk?","options":["Ten minutes","Two hours","A whole week"],"answer":1,"explain":"The speaker before him talked for two hours and used 13,607 words."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where did General Lee surrender to General Grant?","options":["Gettysburg battlefield","Appomattox Court House","Independence Hall"],"answer":1,"explain":"General Lee surrendered to General Grant at Appomattox Court House."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many enslaved people did the Emancipation Proclamation change the lives of?","options":["Fewer than 350,000 people","More than 3.5 million","Around 35,000 people in all"],"answer":1,"explain":"The Emancipation Proclamation changed the lives of more than 3.5 million enslaved people."}]},{"id":"america-gold","course":"america","kind":"facts","section":"gold","title":"Gold & Trails","glyph":"⛏️","n":6,"idea":"Gold was found in California in 1848, and many people headed west by wagon trail and Pony Express.","pages":[{"icon":"⛏️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The California gold rush began on January 24, 1848."},{"t":"term","word":"gold rush","text":"A rush of many people to a place where gold was found."},{"t":"fact","text":"Gold was found by James W. Marshall."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"When did the California gold rush begin?","options":["February 14, 1858","January 24, 1848","September 4, 1749"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"The California gold rush began on January 24, 1848."}}]},{"icon":"📏","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The news of gold brought about 300,000 people to California."},{"t":"fact","text":"They came from the rest of the United States and from other countries."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"About how many people came to California for gold?","options":["30,000","300,000","3 million"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"The news of gold brought about 300,000 people to California."}}]},{"icon":"📐","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"San Francisco grew from a small settlement of about 200 people in 1846."},{"t":"fact","text":"By 1852 it was a boomtown of about 36,000 people."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many people lived in San Francisco by 1852?","options":["Only 360","About 36,000","Nearly 3,600"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"By 1852 it was a boomtown of about 36,000 people."}}]},{"icon":"⚖️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The Oregon Trail was a wagon route 2,170 mi (3,490 km) long."},{"t":"fact","text":"It connected the Missouri River to valleys in Oregon Territory."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What was the length of the Oregon Trail?","options":["About 217 miles","2,170 mi (3,490 km)","Over 21,700 miles"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"The Oregon Trail was a wagon route 2,170 mi (3,490 km) long."}}]},{"icon":"📅","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Use of the Oregon Trail declined after the first transcontinental railroad was completed in 1869."},{"t":"fact","text":"The railroad made the trip west faster, cheaper and safer."}]},{"icon":"🏠","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Pony Express riders could not weigh more than 125 lb (57 kg)."},{"t":"fact","text":"The riders changed about every 75 to 100 mi (120 to 160 km), and they rode day and night."}]},{"icon":"🔎","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Gold found in 1848. People coming to California. A boomtown. A long wagon trail. A railroad. Riders day and night."},{"t":"p","text":"The West grew fast."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Marshall","Found gold in California in 1848"],["San Francisco","Became a boomtown of about 36,000"],["Oregon Trail","A wagon route 2,170 mi long"],["Pony Express","Riders could not weigh more than 125 lb"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"Who found gold and began the California gold rush?","options":["George Washington","James W. Marshall","Theodore Roosevelt"],"answer":1,"explain":"The California gold rush began when gold was found by James W. Marshall."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did the railroad make the trip west?","options":["Slower and more costly","Faster, cheaper and safer","Longer and much harder"],"answer":1,"explain":"The railroad made the trip west faster, cheaper and safer."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How much could Pony Express riders weigh at most?","options":["Under 12 lb","125 lb (57 kg)","Over 1,250 lb"],"answer":1,"explain":"Pony Express riders could not weigh more than 125 lb (57 kg)."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"When did Pony Express riders change?","options":["Once every day at the same noon hour","Every 75 to 100 mi (120 to 160 km)","Just once at the end of each month"],"answer":1,"explain":"The riders changed about every 75 to 100 mi (120 to 160 km), and they rode day and night."}]},{"id":"america-coming","course":"america","kind":"facts","section":"coming","title":"Coming to America","glyph":"🗽","n":6,"idea":"Ships and an island brought new people to America: the Pilgrims on the Mayflower, and millions through Ellis Island.","pages":[{"icon":"🗽","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Ellis Island is a place where immigrants were processed."},{"t":"term","word":"immigrants","text":"People who arrived at the Port of New York to live in a new country."},{"t":"fact","text":"From 1892 to 1954, about 12 million immigrants who arrived at the Port of New York and New Jersey were processed there."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many immigrants were processed at Ellis Island?","options":["Only about 12,000 people","About 12 million","Nearly 1,200 people"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. The answer is there.","explain":"From 1892 to 1954, about 12 million immigrants who arrived at the Port of New York and New Jersey were processed there."}}]},{"icon":"🧑","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Ellis Island is named after Samuel Ellis."},{"t":"fact","text":"He was a Welshman who bought the island in 1774."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Who is Ellis Island named after?","options":["Samuel Adams","Samuel Ellis","George Washington"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"Ellis Island is named after Samuel Ellis."}}]},{"icon":"📅","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The Statue of Liberty was dedicated on October 28, 1886."},{"t":"fact","text":"In her left hand she carries a tablet marked July 4, 1776, in Roman numerals."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"When was the Statue of Liberty dedicated?","options":["April 12, 1861","October 28, 1886","March 8, 1688"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"The Statue of Liberty was dedicated on October 28, 1886."}}]},{"icon":"🧭","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The Mayflower was an English sailing ship."},{"t":"fact","text":"It was 110 ft (34 m) long and 25 ft (7.5 m) wide. In 1620 she carried a group of English families, known today as the Pilgrims, to the New World."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What were the English families on the Mayflower known as?","options":["The Patriots","The Pilgrims","The Pioneers"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"In 1620 she carried a group of English families, known today as the Pilgrims, to the New World."}}]},{"icon":"🗺️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"After 10 weeks at sea, the Mayflower reached what is now the United States."},{"t":"fact","text":"It carried 102 passengers and a crew of about 30. It dropped anchor near the tip of Cape Cod in November 1620."}]},{"icon":"⏳","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The first inspection station on Ellis Island opened in 1892."},{"t":"fact","text":"A fire destroyed it in 1897."}]},{"icon":"🔎","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Millions of immigrants. A Welshman and an island. A statue with a tablet. A ship called the Mayflower. Ten weeks at sea."},{"t":"p","text":"People came to a new land."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Ellis Island","Where about 12 million immigrants were processed"],["Samuel Ellis","A Welshman who bought the island"],["Statue of Liberty","Dedicated on October 28, 1886"],["Mayflower","Carried the Pilgrims to the New World"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many passengers did the Mayflower carry?","options":["12","102","1,020"],"answer":1,"explain":"It carried 102 passengers and a crew of about 30."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where did the Mayflower drop anchor in November 1620?","options":["Inside the Port of New York","Near the tip of Cape Cod","Along the shores of Florida"],"answer":1,"explain":"It dropped anchor near the tip of Cape Cod in November 1620."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What destroyed the first inspection station on Ellis Island?","options":["A flood","A fire","A storm"],"answer":1,"explain":"A fire destroyed it in 1897."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many weeks was the Mayflower at sea?","options":["Only 2","10 weeks","Nearly 20"],"answer":1,"explain":"After 10 weeks at sea, the Mayflower reached what is now the United States."}]},{"id":"america-parks","course":"america","kind":"facts","section":"parks","title":"National Parks","glyph":"🏞️","n":6,"idea":"A canyon over a mile deep, a geyser that erupts about every 92 minutes, and the first public park.","pages":[{"icon":"🏞️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"In 1872, the United States set up Yellowstone National Park."},{"t":"term","word":"national park","text":"A park set up by the country for the benefit of its people."},{"t":"fact","text":"It was the first public park for the benefit and enjoyment of the people."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"In what year was Yellowstone National Park established?","options":["1827","1872","1782"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"In 1872, the United States set up Yellowstone National Park."}}]},{"icon":"📏","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Yellowstone has a bison herd."},{"t":"fact","text":"It is the oldest and largest public bison herd in the United States."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which animal herd in Yellowstone is the oldest and largest public herd?","options":["Elk","Bison","Moose"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"Yellowstone has a bison herd."}}]},{"icon":"📐","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The Grand Canyon is 277 mi (446 km) long and up to 18 mi (29 km) wide."},{"t":"fact","text":"It is over a mile deep, at 6,093 ft (1,857 m)."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is the length of the Grand Canyon?","options":["About 27 miles","277 mi (446 km)","Over 2,700 miles"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"The Grand Canyon is 277 mi (446 km) long and up to 18 mi (29 km) wide."}}]},{"icon":"⚙️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The North Rim of the Grand Canyon is cooler than the South Rim, because it is higher."},{"t":"fact","text":"It averages 8,000 ft (2,400 m) above sea level."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which rim of the Grand Canyon is higher?","options":["The South Rim","The North Rim","The Inner Rim"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"The North Rim of the Grand Canyon is cooler than the South Rim, because it is higher."}}]},{"icon":"💨","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Old Faithful erupts on average every 92 minutes."},{"t":"fact","text":"The time between eruptions can be as short as 35 minutes or as long as 120 minutes."}]},{"icon":"⚖️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"An eruption of Old Faithful can shoot 3,700 to 8,400 gallons of boiling water."},{"t":"fact","text":"The water reaches a height of 106 to 185 ft (32 to 56 m)."}]},{"icon":"🔎","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A park for the people. A herd of bison. A deep canyon. A cooler rim. A geyser that erupts."},{"t":"p","text":"Parks are for everyone."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Yellowstone","First public park for the people"],["Bison","Oldest and largest public herd"],["Grand Canyon","Over a mile deep"],["Old Faithful","Erupts on average every 92 minutes"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"How often does Old Faithful erupt on average?","options":["Once each hour on the hour","Every 92 minutes","About twice in a whole year"],"answer":1,"explain":"Old Faithful erupts on average every 92 minutes."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does the North Rim average above sea level?","options":["About 80 ft","8,000 ft (2,400 m)","Nearly 80,000 feet"],"answer":1,"explain":"It averages 8,000 ft (2,400 m) above sea level."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is shot out in an eruption of Old Faithful?","options":["Melted snow","Boiling water","Cold mud"],"answer":1,"explain":"An eruption of Old Faithful can shoot 3,700 to 8,400 gallons of boiling water."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How tall is the water when Old Faithful erupts?","options":["Only 10 to 18 ft","106 to 185 ft (32 to 56 m)","Nearly 1,060 ft"],"answer":1,"explain":"The water reaches a height of 106 to 185 ft (32 to 56 m)."}]},{"id":"utah-peoples","course":"utah","kind":"facts","section":"peoples","title":"The First Peoples","glyph":"🪶","n":6,"idea":"A canyon wall is covered in life-size painted figures, made long ago by people whose names no one knows. Meet the people who lived in Utah first, and who live here today.","pages":[{"icon":"🎨","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"In Horseshoe Canyon, in southeastern Utah, a long sandstone wall called the Great Gallery is covered with painted figures."},{"t":"fact","text":"The life-size figures are tapered, with no arms or legs. Many have detailed designs. They were painted long ago by people who hunted and gathered their food. No one knows their names."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is the Great Gallery?","options":["A cave filled with old stone tools","A wall covered with painted figures","A tall tower made of piled-up stone"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. It says what the Great Gallery is.","explain":"The Great Gallery is a long sandstone wall in Horseshoe Canyon, covered with painted figures."}}]},{"icon":"🏚️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"High on a cliff in Capitol Reef National Park, a small stone storehouse still stands. The Fremont people built small granaries like it."},{"t":"term","word":"granary","text":"A small storehouse for food and seed."},{"t":"fact","text":"They used granaries to store food and seed. The Fremont grew corn, beans and squash."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did the Fremont store in their granaries?","options":["Water and fish","Food and seed","Tools and blankets"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. It says what they stored.","explain":"The Fremont stored food and seed in granaries."}}]},{"icon":"🪶","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The word Utah comes from the Ute people. The Ute lived in the mountains and valleys of this land and moved with the seasons, hunting, fishing and gathering."},{"t":"fact","text":"Spanish travelers wrote their name as Yuta, and English speakers later turned it into Utah. The Ute call themselves Nuche, which means the People."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where does the name Utah come from?","options":["From a Spanish king","From the Ute people","From a mountain range"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the first paragraph. It names where Utah comes from.","explain":"The word Utah comes from the Ute people."}}]},{"icon":"🌲","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The Goshute lived in the dry western desert near the Great Salt Lake, where water is scarce. They moved with the seasons to find food and water."},{"t":"fact","text":"They gathered pine nuts, seeds and roots, and they hunted rabbits. In autumn, families went to the mountains to harvest pine nuts from the piñon pines and stored them for winter."}]},{"icon":"🧶","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"In the red-rock country of southeastern Utah live the Navajo, who call themselves Diné. Monument Valley sits on the border of Utah and Arizona, inside the Navajo Nation."},{"t":"fact","text":"Navajo weavers are famous for rugs woven on upright looms. A rug can take months to weave."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What do Navajo weavers make rugs on?","options":["Flat wooden tables","Upright looms","Spinning wheels"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. It names what the rugs are woven on.","explain":"Navajo weavers make their rugs on upright looms, and a rug can take months."}}]},{"icon":"🏜️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Utah is home to eight federally recognized tribes. They include the Ute, Paiute, Goshute, Northwestern Shoshone and Navajo peoples."},{"t":"fact","text":"They have their own governments, languages and ceremonies, and they are living in Utah today. Many of the names on a Utah map began as words from these languages."}]},{"icon":"🪶","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A painted wall. A stone storehouse. The Ute, the Goshute and the Navajo."},{"t":"p","text":"People have lived in Utah for a very long time, and they are here today."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Great Gallery","Wall of life-size painted figures"],["Fremont","Built stone granaries in cliffs"],["Ute","The people Utah is named for"],["Goshute","Gathered pine nuts in the desert"],["Navajo","Weave rugs on upright looms"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"Who painted the figures in the Great Gallery?","options":["Spanish friars who kept a diary","Hunters and gatherers, long ago","Pioneers who drove covered wagons"],"answer":1,"explain":"They were painted long ago by people who hunted and gathered their food."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does Nuche mean?","options":["The Mountains","The Desert","The People"],"answer":2,"explain":"The Ute call themselves Nuche, which means the People."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did the Goshute gather in autumn?","options":["Pine nuts","Corn kernels","Wild apples"],"answer":0,"explain":"In autumn, families went to the mountains to harvest pine nuts from the piñon pines."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many federally recognized tribes live in Utah today?","options":["Two","Twenty","Eight"],"answer":2,"explain":"Utah is home to eight federally recognized tribes."}],"picgame":{"type":"tapall","title":"First Peoples","goal":"Tap what the first peoples of Utah used.","items":[{"icon":"🌽","name":"corn","good":true},{"icon":"🌰","name":"pine nuts","good":true},{"icon":"🐇","name":"rabbit","good":true},{"icon":"🧶","name":"wool yarn","good":true},{"icon":"📱","name":"phone","good":false},{"icon":"🚗","name":"car","good":false},{"icon":"🎮","name":"video game","good":false},{"icon":"🍦","name":"ice cream","good":false}]}},{"id":"utah-explorers","course":"utah","kind":"facts","section":"explorers","title":"Explorers & Trappers","glyph":"🧭","n":6,"idea":"Two friars set out for California and never got there, but they wrote everything down. Then a trapper found a lake so salty he thought it was an arm of the ocean.","pages":[{"icon":"🧭","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"In July 1776, two Spanish friars, Domínguez and Escalante, left Santa Fe to find a route to California. They never got there."},{"t":"fact","text":"They wandered through Utah and wrote a daily diary. They came home after about 1,700 miles (2,700 km) in five months. When winter closed in, they cast lots to decide whether to keep going, and the lots said to go home."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How long were the friars gone?","options":["About five days","About five months","About five years"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. It tells how long the trip took.","explain":"They came home after about 1,700 miles in five months."}}]},{"icon":"🐟","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"On their way, the friars came down into a valley with a big freshwater lake, now called Utah Lake."},{"t":"fact","text":"The people there, called the Timpanogots, fished the lake. They traded dried fish to the friars. The friars were the first Europeans known to visit the valley."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did the Timpanogots do at the lake?","options":["They sold beaver furs","They fished in it","They sailed across it"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. It says what the Timpanogots did at the lake.","explain":"The Timpanogots fished the lake and traded dried fish to the friars."}}]},{"icon":"🧂","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Late in 1824 or early in 1825, a fur trapper tasted the water of a huge lake. It was salty, so he thought he had found an arm of the ocean."},{"t":"term","word":"trapper","text":"Someone who catches animals for their fur, like the one who tasted the lake."},{"t":"fact","text":"Most accounts name Jim Bridger, and the lake was the Great Salt Lake. Later trappers went all the way around it and found no outlet to the sea."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Who do most accounts name as the trapper?","options":["Jedediah Smith","Jim Bridger","Brigham Young"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. It names the trapper.","explain":"Most accounts name Jim Bridger, and the lake was the Great Salt Lake."}}]},{"icon":"🏕️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Each summer, trappers met at a rendezvous to trade beaver furs for supplies. Bear Lake, on the Utah and Idaho border, hosted the rendezvous in 1827 and 1828."},{"t":"fact","text":"A thousand or more trappers and Native Americans gathered there. One observer said their campfires looked like a lighted city."}]},{"icon":"🏜️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"In 1827, a trapper named Jedediah Smith and two companions crossed Utah's western desert on their way back from California. There was very little water or game."},{"t":"fact","text":"The lack of water made the trip very hard. Smith may have been the first non-Native traveler to cross that desert. He went on to the rendezvous at Bear Lake."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What was hard to find in that desert?","options":["Sand","Water","Rocks"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the first paragraph. It says what there was very little of.","explain":"There was very little water, and that made the trip very hard."}}]},{"icon":"🗺️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"In the 1840s, the explorer John C. Frémont mapped much of this region. He found that its rivers never reach any ocean."},{"t":"term","word":"basin","text":"A place where water cannot get out to the sea: it ends in lakes or sinks into the sand."},{"t":"fact","text":"They end in lakes or sink into the sand. He named the region the Great Basin, because water that falls here never flows to the sea."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did Frémont name the region?","options":["Salt Plain","Great Basin","Red Desert"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. It gives the name he chose.","explain":"He named it the Great Basin, because water that falls there never flows to the sea."}}]},{"icon":"🧭","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Two friars with a diary. A lake that tasted of salt. A summer fair for trappers. A crossing of a dry desert. A map of a basin with no way to the sea."},{"t":"p","text":"Each one found out a little more about the land."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Domínguez and Escalante","Friars who kept a diary"],["Utah Lake","Lake where the Timpanogots fished"],["Jim Bridger","Trapper said to taste the salty lake"],["Bear Lake","Where trappers held a summer fair"],["John C. Frémont","Mapped the Great Basin"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did the friars do when winter closed in?","options":["Built a fort and stayed","Cast lots and went home","Sailed across the lake"],"answer":1,"explain":"They cast lots to decide whether to keep going, and the lots said to go home."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why did the trapper think he had found an arm of the ocean?","options":["The shore was sandy","The water was salty","The lake had tides"],"answer":1,"explain":"The water was salty, so he thought he had found an arm of the ocean."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where was the rendezvous held in 1827 and 1828?","options":["Utah Lake","Great Salt Lake","Bear Lake"],"answer":2,"explain":"Bear Lake, on the Utah and Idaho border, hosted the rendezvous in 1827 and 1828."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is special about water that falls in the Great Basin?","options":["It always freezes in winter","It never flows to the sea","It flows north to the ocean"],"answer":1,"explain":"The rivers there end in lakes or sink into the sand, so the water never reaches the sea."}],"picgame":{"type":"catch","title":"Trapper Gear","goal":"Catch what a trapper in the wilds might need.","items":[{"icon":"🦫","name":"beaver","good":true},{"icon":"🧭","name":"compass","good":true},{"icon":"🐴","name":"horse","good":true},{"icon":"🗺️","name":"map","good":true},{"icon":"⛺","name":"tent","good":true},{"icon":"📱","name":"phone","good":false},{"icon":"🚗","name":"car","good":false},{"icon":"🚀","name":"rocket","good":false}]}},{"id":"utah-land","course":"utah","kind":"facts","section":"land","title":"Lake, Desert & Peak","glyph":"🏜️","n":6,"idea":"A lake with no way out, a plain of salt where cars race, and a mountain range that runs east to west. Meet the land of Utah.","pages":[{"icon":"🧂","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The Great Salt Lake is so salty that swimmers float like corks. In some parts it is several times saltier than the ocean."},{"t":"fact","text":"No fish can live in it, but brine shrimp and brine flies thrive there. The flies number in the billions. Millions of migrating birds stop to feed on them."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What lives in the lake?","options":["Trout, bass and catfish","Brine shrimp and flies","Crabs, clams and oysters"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. It names what lives in the lake.","explain":"Brine shrimp and brine flies thrive in the lake, but no fish can live there."}}]},{"icon":"💧","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"No river flows out of the Great Salt Lake. Rivers flow in, bringing a little salt each day."},{"t":"term","word":"evaporate","text":"To turn from water into air you cannot see."},{"t":"fact","text":"Water can leave only by rising into the air. The water evaporates and the salt stays behind. That is why the lake stays salty."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why does the lake stay salty?","options":["Ships bring in new salt daily","Water leaves but the salt stays","Ocean tides carry the salt in"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. It says what happens to the water and the salt.","explain":"Water leaves by evaporating into the air, and the salt stays behind."}}]},{"icon":"🩷","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A railroad causeway built of rock divides the lake in two. The south arm gets fresh river water."},{"t":"fact","text":"The north arm does not, so it is much saltier, and salt-loving microbes turn its water pink or red. From the air the two arms are two different colors."}]},{"icon":"🏁","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"West of the lake lie the Bonneville Salt Flats, a flat white plain of hard salt."},{"t":"fact","text":"It is so smooth that people have raced cars there to set land speed records since 1914. The salt crust is less than an inch thick in places and about six feet thick in others."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What do people set on the salt flats?","options":["Swimming records","Land speed records","High jump records"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. It tells what people set there.","explain":"The salt is so smooth that people race cars there to set land speed records."}}]},{"icon":"⛰️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The Uinta Mountains are the largest mountain range in the lower 48 states that runs east to west."},{"t":"fact","text":"Utah's tallest peak, Kings Peak, rises there to 13,528 feet (4,123 m). The Uinta Mountains stretch across northeastern Utah."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which way do the Uinta Mountains run?","options":["North to south","East to west","Round in a circle"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the first paragraph. It says which way the range runs.","explain":"The Uinta Mountains run east to west."}}]},{"icon":"❄️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Utah is one of the driest states in the country. On average it gets only a little over a foot of rain and snow a year."},{"t":"fact","text":"But the mountains catch heavy winter snow, and the melting snow runs down in streams. Most of Utah's water begins as mountain snow. That is why Utahns watch the snowpack all winter long."}]},{"icon":"🏜️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A salty lake. A pink arm. A white plain. A range that runs east to west. A dry land that gets its water from snow."},{"t":"p","text":"Each one makes Utah look like no other place."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Great Salt Lake","Salty enough to float on"],["Brine shrimp","Tiny animals that feed birds"],["Salt Flats","White plain where cars race"],["Uinta Mountains","Range that runs east to west"],["Kings Peak","Highest point in Utah"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"How salty can the Great Salt Lake be?","options":["Just as salty as a pond","Several times saltier than the ocean","A little less salty than tap water"],"answer":1,"explain":"In some parts it is several times saltier than the ocean."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What color is the north arm of the lake?","options":["Bright blue","Pink or red","Pure white"],"answer":1,"explain":"Salt-loving microbes turn the water of the north arm pink or red."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How tall is Kings Peak?","options":["Only 3,528 feet","About 13,528 feet","Over 23,000 feet"],"answer":1,"explain":"Kings Peak rises to 13,528 feet (4,123 m), the tallest point in Utah."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How much rain and snow does Utah get in a year?","options":["About ten feet","A little over a foot","Almost none at all"],"answer":1,"explain":"On average Utah gets only a little over a foot of rain and snow a year."}],"picgame":{"type":"sort","title":"Lake or Mountain","goal":"Put each one at the salt lake or in the mountains.","bins":[{"icon":"🧂","label":"salt lake"},{"icon":"⛰️","label":"mountains"}],"items":[{"icon":"🦐","name":"brine shrimp","bin":0},{"icon":"⛵","name":"sailboat","bin":0},{"icon":"🏊","name":"swimmer","bin":0},{"icon":"❄️","name":"snow","bin":1},{"icon":"🌲","name":"pine tree","bin":1},{"icon":"🐐","name":"mountain goat","bin":1}]}},{"id":"utah-pioneers","course":"utah","kind":"facts","section":"pioneers","title":"The Pioneer Trail","glyph":"🐂","n":6,"idea":"A sick leader came over the mountains, and a pioneer later remembered him saying it was the right place. See how the pioneers got there, and what happened when the crickets came.","pages":[{"icon":"🐂","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"On July 24, 1847, Brigham Young reached the Salt Lake Valley. He had been sick with a fever, so the main company came in two days before him."},{"t":"fact","text":"Years later, a pioneer named Wilford Woodruff remembered him saying, \"This is the right place.\" July 24 is still a state holiday in Utah. It is called Pioneer Day."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is July 24 called in Utah?","options":["Gull Day","Pioneer Day","Harvest Day"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. It names the holiday.","explain":"July 24 is called Pioneer Day, and it is still a state holiday in Utah."}}]},{"icon":"🚶","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The pioneers were members of The Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints, looking for a place of their own. The first company was small: about 150 people and 73 wagons."},{"t":"fact","text":"They left Winter Quarters, in what is now Nebraska, in April and traveled more than 1,000 miles (1,600 km). The trip took more than three months."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many people were in the first company?","options":["About 15 people","About 150 people","About 1,500 people"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the first paragraph. It gives the number of people.","explain":"The first company was small: about 150 people and 73 wagons."}}]},{"icon":"⚙️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"To count the miles, Orson Pratt and William Clayton designed a roadometer, and Appleton Harmon built it."},{"t":"term","word":"roadometer","text":"A wooden device on a wagon wheel that counts each turn."},{"t":"fact","text":"Its gears counted the turns of the wheel. It took 360 turns to make one mile. Clayton wrote the distances in a guidebook."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did the roadometer count?","options":["Steps of the oxen","Turns of a wheel","Days on the trail"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. It says what the gears counted.","explain":"The gears counted the turns of the wagon wheel."}}]},{"icon":"🥔","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"When the pioneers arrived, they went to work at once."},{"t":"fact","text":"They sent water from a nearby creek into ditches to water the dry ground. They planted their first potatoes and turnips in a field near what is now Third South and State Street."},{"t":"p","text":"The settlers shared the water as one community."}]},{"icon":"🛒","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"From 1856 to 1860, about 3,000 pioneers crossed the plains pushing and pulling handcarts, two-wheeled wooden carts."},{"t":"fact","text":"Handcarts cost much less than wagons and oxen. Eight of the ten companies arrived safely. Two arrived late in 1856 and were caught by early snow until rescuers reached them."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why did they use handcarts?","options":["Handcarts were faster than wagons","Handcarts cost much less","Oxen were not allowed on the trail"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. It gives the reason.","explain":"Handcarts cost much less than wagons and oxen."}}]},{"icon":"🐦","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"In 1848, swarms of crickets began eating the settlers' crops. People fought them with fire and ditches, but nothing worked."},{"t":"fact","text":"Then flocks of California gulls came and ate the crickets. A monument to the seagulls stands in Salt Lake City today. The California gull is now Utah's state bird."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What ate the crickets?","options":["Hungry coyotes","California gulls","Barn owls"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. It names who ate them.","explain":"Flocks of California gulls came and ate the crickets."}}]},{"icon":"🐂","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A leader with a fever. A wooden roadometer. Potatoes in dry ground. Handcarts on the plains. Gulls on the crickets."},{"t":"p","text":"The pioneers worked together to make a home."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Pioneer Day","July 24, a Utah holiday"],["Roadometer","Counted the turns of a wheel"],["Handcart","Two-wheeled cart pushed by hand"],["California gull","Ate the crickets"],["Brigham Young","Leader of the first company"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did the first pioneers do soon after arriving?","options":["Built a high stone fort for safety","Dug ditches and planted potatoes","Dug a mine looking for silver ore"],"answer":1,"explain":"They sent creek water into ditches and planted potatoes and turnips."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"About how far did the first company travel?","options":["Under 100 miles","Over 1,000 miles","Over 10,000 miles"],"answer":1,"explain":"They traveled more than 1,000 miles (1,600 km) from Winter Quarters."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is the state bird of Utah?","options":["The soaring bald eagle","The California gull","The little blue jay"],"answer":1,"explain":"The California gull saved the crops, and it is now Utah's state bird."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What was the roadometer made of?","options":["Iron and steam","Wood and gears","Rope and stone"],"answer":1,"explain":"The roadometer was a wooden device with gears that counted the turns of the wheel."}],"picgame":{"type":"tapall","title":"On the Trail","goal":"Tap what the pioneers used on the trail.","items":[{"icon":"🐂","name":"ox","good":true},{"icon":"🛒","name":"handcart","good":true},{"icon":"🥔","name":"potato","good":true},{"icon":"🔥","name":"campfire","good":true},{"icon":"📱","name":"phone","good":false},{"icon":"✈️","name":"airplane","good":false},{"icon":"🚀","name":"rocket","good":false},{"icon":"🎮","name":"video game","good":false}]}},{"id":"utah-building","course":"utah","kind":"facts","section":"building","title":"Building Towns","glyph":"🐝","n":6,"idea":"Streets 132 feet wide, a beehive on the seal, and a temple that took 40 years. Find out how the pioneers built their towns.","pages":[{"icon":"🛣️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Salt Lake City was laid out in a grid with very wide streets, 132 feet (40 m) from edge to edge."},{"t":"fact","text":"The plan came from the Plat of Zion, a city plan Joseph Smith wrote down years before. A story says the streets were wide so oxen could turn around, but nobody has found proof."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How wide were the streets?","options":["Only 32 feet, like a lane","132 feet from edge to edge","Over 232 feet, wider than a field"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the first paragraph. It gives the width of the streets.","explain":"The streets are 132 feet (40 m) from edge to edge."}}]},{"icon":"🔢","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Addresses in Salt Lake City count out from the middle of town. The corner of Main Street and South Temple is the zero point."},{"t":"fact","text":"Each 100 numbers is one block. An address like 400 South 300 West means four blocks south and three blocks west of that corner."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where do the address numbers start?","options":["The shore of the Great Salt Lake","Main Street and South Temple","The steps of the State Capitol"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the first paragraph. It names the zero point.","explain":"The numbers count out from the corner of Main Street and South Temple."}}]},{"icon":"🐝","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The beehive became the symbol of Utah. Bees work together and keep busy, and the beehive stood for hard work and thrift."},{"t":"term","word":"industry","text":"Steady, hard work."},{"t":"fact","text":"Utah is the Beehive State, its motto is Industry, and a beehive is on its seal."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is Utah's motto?","options":["Freedom","Industry","Honor"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. It gives the motto.","explain":"Utah's motto is Industry, which means steady, hard work."}}]},{"icon":"⛪","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The Salt Lake Temple took 40 years to build, from 1853 to 1893."},{"t":"fact","text":"The granite-like stone came from Little Cottonwood Canyon, about 20 miles (32 km) away. At first, oxen pulled each block on a wagon, and one round trip took three to four days. Later a railroad carried the stone."}]},{"icon":"📜","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"In 1849 the settlers asked for a state called Deseret, a word from their scripture that means honeybee."},{"t":"fact","text":"In 1850, Congress made it a territory instead and gave it the name Utah, after the Ute people. The Territory of Utah was much bigger than the state is today."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does Deseret mean?","options":["Red rock","Honeybee","Salt lake"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. It says what the word means.","explain":"Deseret means honeybee."}}]},{"icon":"🍑","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Within a few years settlers built dozens of towns, some hundreds of miles from Salt Lake City: Provo, Ogden, Manti and Cedar City."},{"t":"fact","text":"In 1861 families were called to the hot southwest corner to grow cotton. They built St. George, still known as Utah's Dixie. Winters there are mild. The cotton never paid well enough, so farmers gave it up, but the town stayed."}]},{"icon":"🐝","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Wide streets. A grid of numbers. A beehive. A temple of stone. A name that means honeybee. A town in the warm southwest."},{"t":"p","text":"The settlers built a home together."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Beehive","Symbol of steady, hard work"],["Deseret","Settlers' name meaning honeybee"],["Zero point","Corner of Main Street and South Temple"],["Salt Lake Temple","Took 40 years to build"],["St. George","Town settled to grow cotton"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What was the Plat of Zion?","options":["A map of the railroad","A plan for a city","A guidebook for the trail"],"answer":1,"explain":"The Plat of Zion was a city plan that Joseph Smith wrote down, and Salt Lake City followed it."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How long did the Salt Lake Temple take to build?","options":["Only 4 years","About 40 years","More than 100 years"],"answer":1,"explain":"The temple took 40 years to build, from 1853 to 1893."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did the families called to St. George grow?","options":["Rice","Cotton","Tea"],"answer":1,"explain":"In 1861 families were called to the hot southwest corner to grow cotton."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is Utah's nickname?","options":["The Eagle State","The Beehive State","The Sunflower State"],"answer":1,"explain":"Utah is the Beehive State, and a beehive is on its seal."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does Industry mean on Utah's seal?","options":["A day of rest","Steady, hard work","Big factories only"],"answer":1,"explain":"Industry means steady, hard work, like the bees in a beehive."}],"picgame":{"type":"catch","title":"Town Builders","goal":"Catch what belongs in a settler town.","items":[{"icon":"🐝","name":"bee","good":true},{"icon":"🏠","name":"house","good":true},{"icon":"⛪","name":"temple","good":true},{"icon":"🌽","name":"corn","good":true},{"icon":"🧵","name":"cotton thread","good":true},{"icon":"🚗","name":"car","good":false},{"icon":"📱","name":"phone","good":false},{"icon":"🚀","name":"rocket","good":false}]}},{"id":"utah-rails","course":"utah","kind":"facts","section":"rails","title":"Rails & Mines","glyph":"🚂","n":6,"idea":"A golden spike joined two railroads at Promontory Summit. Then follow the rails to copper, silver and uranium.","pages":[{"icon":"🚂","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"On May 10, 1869, at Promontory Summit in northern Utah, two railroads met. A golden spike was set in a special tie."},{"t":"fact","text":"Telegraph wires carried the news across the country in one word: \"Done.\" The two steam engines stood nose to nose. A trip across the country that once took months now took days."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What word did the telegraph send?","options":["Finished","Done","Hurrah"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. It gives the one word.","explain":"The telegraph sent one word across the country: \"Done.\""}}]},{"icon":"🧱","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"About 10,000 Chinese workers built the Central Pacific line through mountains and desert."},{"t":"fact","text":"On April 28, 1869, in Utah, crews laid 10 miles of track in a single day. No crew has equaled that record since. Central Pacific boss Charles Crocker wanted to beat the Union Pacific's record of 8.5 miles in a day."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many miles of track were laid in one day?","options":["2 miles","10 miles","50 miles"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. It gives the number of miles.","explain":"Crews laid 10 miles of track in a single day."}}]},{"icon":"🌉","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"In 1904, a railroad shortcut called the Lucin Cutoff opened across the Great Salt Lake."},{"t":"term","word":"trestle","text":"A wooden frame, built on piles, that carries a railroad across a lake."},{"t":"fact","text":"Trains rode on a wooden trestle nearly 12 miles (19 km) long, built on piles driven into the lake bottom. In 1959 a rock causeway replaced it, and trains still cross the lake there."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What held the trestle up?","options":["Floating barrels tied together","Piles driven into the lake bottom","Ropes pulled tight to the shore"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. It says what the trestle was built on.","explain":"The trestle was built on piles driven into the lake bottom."}}]},{"icon":"⛏️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Bingham Canyon Mine, near Salt Lake City, is a copper mine dug as a giant open pit. Open-pit mining began there in 1906."},{"t":"fact","text":"The pit is about 3,650 feet (1.1 km) deep and 2.75 miles (4.4 km) wide. It is often called the deepest open-pit mine in the world. Giant haul trucks have tires about 12 feet (3.7 m) tall."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What metal comes out of Bingham Canyon Mine?","options":["Iron ore","Copper","Coal dust"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the first paragraph. It names the metal.","explain":"Bingham Canyon Mine is a copper mine."}}]},{"icon":"🪙","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Silver was found in the mountains near Park City in 1868, and a mining camp grew up."},{"t":"fact","text":"Miners dug tunnels deep into the mountains for silver ore. About a hundred years later, the old mining company started a ski area there. Today those same mountains hold ski runs."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What do those mountains hold today?","options":["Wheat farms","Ski runs","Railroad yards"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. It tells what the mountains hold now.","explain":"Today the same mountains hold ski runs."}}]},{"icon":"☢️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"In 1952, a prospector named Charlie Steen found a rich uranium deposit near Moab."},{"t":"fact","text":"Most uranium prospectors carried Geiger counters, but Steen did not own one. His find set off a rush, and Moab grew to five times its size. A Geiger counter clicks faster near radioactive rock such as uranium."}]},{"icon":"🚂","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A spike of gold. Ten miles of track in a day. A trestle across a lake. A copper pit. A silver camp. A uranium rush."},{"t":"p","text":"Each one started with a railroad or a mine."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Promontory Summit","Where two railroads met"],["Lucin Cutoff","Trestle across the Great Salt Lake"],["Bingham Canyon","Giant open-pit copper mine"],["Park City","Silver camp turned ski town"],["Charlie Steen","Found uranium near Moab"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where did the two railroads meet?","options":["Bear Lake, on the border","Promontory Summit","Moab, near the red rocks"],"answer":1,"explain":"The two railroads met at Promontory Summit in northern Utah."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How long was the Lucin Cutoff trestle?","options":["About one mile","Nearly 12 miles","Over 100 miles"],"answer":1,"explain":"The wooden trestle was nearly 12 miles (19 km) long."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How deep is Bingham Canyon Mine?","options":["Only about ten feet deep, like a pool","About 3,650 feet","More than ten miles straight down"],"answer":1,"explain":"The pit is about 3,650 feet (1.1 km) deep."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does a Geiger counter help people find?","options":["Silver","Uranium","Salt"],"answer":1,"explain":"A Geiger counter clicks faster near radioactive rock such as uranium."}],"picgame":{"type":"sort","title":"Rail or Mine","goal":"Put each one on the railroad or in the mine.","bins":[{"icon":"🚂","label":"railroad"},{"icon":"⛏️","label":"mine"}],"items":[{"icon":"🛤️","name":"track","bin":0},{"icon":"🚃","name":"train car","bin":0},{"icon":"🌉","name":"bridge","bin":0},{"icon":"🪨","name":"rock","bin":1},{"icon":"🔦","name":"lamp","bin":1},{"icon":"🪙","name":"silver","bin":1}]}},{"id":"utah-statehood","course":"utah","kind":"facts","section":"statehood","title":"Becoming a State","glyph":"⭐","n":6,"idea":"Bells rang when Utah became the 45th state. Meet the first woman to vote, a doctor who beat her own husband, and a state with its own cooking pot.","pages":[{"icon":"⭐","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"On January 4, 1896, President Grover Cleveland proclaimed Utah the 45th state."},{"t":"fact","text":"Bells rang and whistles blew in Salt Lake City, and towns across Utah held parades. Utah had asked to become a state many times before it got a yes."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which number state is Utah?","options":["The 5th","The 45th","The 50th"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the first paragraph. It gives the number.","explain":"Utah became the 45th state on January 4, 1896."}}]},{"icon":"🗳️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"On February 14, 1870, a young woman named Seraph Young cast her vote in Salt Lake City. She is usually counted as the first woman in the United States to vote under an equal-suffrage law."},{"t":"fact","text":"Congress took the vote away in 1887, and Utah's new constitution gave it back in 1896."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did Seraph Young do?","options":["Signed a law","Cast a vote","Built a school"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the first paragraph. It says what she did in 1870.","explain":"Seraph Young cast her vote in Salt Lake City on February 14, 1870."}}]},{"icon":"🌷","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Utah's state seal shows an eagle on a shield, the word Industry, a beehive, and sego lilies on both sides of the beehive. An American flag stands on each side."},{"t":"term","word":"sego lily","text":"The state flower of Utah, with a bulb that can be eaten."},{"t":"fact","text":"The sego lily is the state flower. Pioneers dug up sego lily bulbs to eat when food ran short."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did pioneers eat from the sego lily?","options":["Petals","Bulbs","Seeds"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. It says what they dug up.","explain":"Pioneers dug up sego lily bulbs to eat when food ran short."}}]},{"icon":"🍲","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Utah has some unusual state symbols. The state bird is the California gull. The state animal is the Rocky Mountain elk."},{"t":"fact","text":"The state cooking pot is the Dutch oven. The state fossil is Allosaurus, a meat-eating dinosaur. Utah named the Dutch oven in 1997 and Allosaurus in 1988."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is Utah's state cooking pot?","options":["Frying pan","Dutch oven","Tea kettle"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. It names the state cooking pot.","explain":"Utah's state cooking pot is the Dutch oven."}}]},{"icon":"👩","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Martha Hughes Cannon, a doctor, ran for the Utah state senate in 1896 against her own husband. She won."},{"t":"fact","text":"She became the first woman state senator in the United States. She also introduced a bill to create a state board of health, and it passed."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Whom did Martha Hughes Cannon run against?","options":["Her sister","Her own husband","A famous judge"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the first paragraph. It says who she ran against.","explain":"She ran against her own husband, and she won."}}]},{"icon":"🗺️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"In 1850, the Territory of Utah was much larger than the state is now. It stretched from California to the Rocky Mountains and included the land that became Nevada."},{"t":"fact","text":"Over the next years Congress cut off pieces to make other territories and states. In 1868 the last big piece went to Wyoming Territory, and Utah took the shape it has today."}]},{"icon":"⭐","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A bell for statehood. A vote cast by a young woman. A beehive and a lily. A Dutch oven. A senator who beat her husband."},{"t":"p","text":"Utah has many firsts."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["January 4, 1896","The day Utah became a state"],["Seraph Young","Voted in 1870"],["Sego lily","The state flower"],["Dutch oven","Utah's state cooking pot"],["Martha Hughes Cannon","First woman state senator"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"In which year did Utah become a state?","options":["1847","1896","1950"],"answer":1,"explain":"Utah became a state on January 4, 1896."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did Congress do in 1887?","options":["Gave Utah a new state seal","Took the vote away from women","Built a railroad across the lake"],"answer":1,"explain":"Congress took the vote away in 1887, and Utah's new constitution gave it back in 1896."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is Utah's state fossil?","options":["Stegosaurus","Allosaurus","Triceratops"],"answer":1,"explain":"The state fossil is Allosaurus, a meat-eating dinosaur."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is Utah's state animal?","options":["The giant grizzly bear","The Rocky Mountain elk","The howling gray wolf"],"answer":1,"explain":"The state animal is the Rocky Mountain elk."}],"picgame":{"type":"tapall","title":"Utah Symbols","goal":"Tap what belongs to Utah.","items":[{"icon":"🐝","name":"bee","good":true},{"icon":"🌷","name":"lily","good":true},{"icon":"🦅","name":"eagle","good":true},{"icon":"🚩","name":"flag","good":true},{"icon":"🍲","name":"cooking pot","good":true},{"icon":"🐧","name":"penguin","good":false},{"icon":"🌴","name":"palm tree","good":false},{"icon":"🐘","name":"elephant","good":false}]}},{"id":"utah-wonders","course":"utah","kind":"facts","section":"wonders","title":"Wonders of Stone","glyph":"🏞️","n":6,"idea":"Five national parks, a wall of dinosaur bones and the Greatest Snow on Earth. See the wonders of Utah's red-rock country.","pages":[{"icon":"🏞️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Utah has five national parks, called the Mighty Five: Zion, Bryce Canyon, Capitol Reef, Arches and Canyonlands."},{"t":"fact","text":"Each one shows a different side of the red-rock desert. Together they cover more than a thousand square miles of canyons and cliffs."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many national parks does Utah have?","options":["Three","Five","Ten"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the first paragraph. It gives the number.","explain":"Utah has five national parks, called the Mighty Five."}}]},{"icon":"🗿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Bryce Canyon is full of hoodoos, tall thin spires of rock that stand in rows like a crowd."},{"t":"term","word":"hoodoo","text":"A tall, thin spire of rock."},{"t":"fact","text":"Bryce has the largest collection of hoodoos in the world. Frost may help shape them: about 170 days a year, the temperature there goes both above and below freezing."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What are hoodoos?","options":["Deep caves in a cliff","Tall thin spires of rock","Flat rocks like tables"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. It says what hoodoos are.","explain":"Hoodoos are tall, thin spires of rock."}}]},{"icon":"🌊","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"In Zion Canyon the Virgin River has carved a slot so narrow that hikers wade in the river itself."},{"t":"fact","text":"In the Narrows, the walls rise up to 1,000 feet (300 m), and in places the gap is only about 30 feet (9 m) wide. After a storm upstream, the river can rise fast."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How tall are the walls of the Narrows?","options":["Only about 10 feet","As much as 1,000 feet","Roughly 100 feet"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. It gives the height of the walls.","explain":"In the Narrows the walls rise up to 1,000 feet (300 m)."}}]},{"icon":"🌉","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Delicate Arch stands alone, and the opening under it is 46 feet (14 m) high. A picture of it has been on Utah's license plates."},{"t":"fact","text":"Arches National Park holds more than 2,000 natural stone arches, the densest collection in the world. Rain soaks into the rock and ice pries it apart, slowly shaping the arches."}]},{"icon":"🦕","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"In Dinosaur National Monument in northeastern Utah, a whole wall of rock is full of dinosaur bones. There are about 1,500 of them, left where they were found so visitors can see them."},{"t":"fact","text":"Earl Douglass found the first huge bones there in 1909. There are a few places where visitors can touch real dinosaur bones."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Who found the first huge bones?","options":["Jim Bridger","Earl Douglass","Brigham Young"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. It names the man who found them.","explain":"Earl Douglass found the first huge bones there in 1909."}}]},{"icon":"⛷️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"In 2002, Salt Lake City hosted the Winter Olympics. Utah's snow falls light and dry, and Utah calls it the Greatest Snow on Earth."},{"t":"fact","text":"Some mountain resorts get more than 500 inches (12.7 m) of snow in a year. Alta, a ski area near Salt Lake City, averages more than 540 inches (13.7 m)."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"In which year did Salt Lake City host the Winter Olympics?","options":["1896","2002","1869"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the first paragraph. It gives the year.","explain":"Salt Lake City hosted the Winter Olympics in 2002."}}]},{"icon":"🏞️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Five parks. A crowd of hoodoos. A slot with a river in it. An arch on a license plate. A wall of bones. A mountain of dry, light snow."},{"t":"p","text":"Utah is full of stone and snow."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Mighty Five","Utah's five national parks"],["Hoodoo","Tall thin spire of rock"],["The Narrows","Slot where hikers wade a river"],["Delicate Arch","On Utah's license plates"],["Earl Douglass","Found the huge dinosaur bones"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which river carved Zion Canyon?","options":["The Green River","The Virgin River","The Snake River"],"answer":1,"explain":"The Virgin River has carved a slot so narrow that hikers wade in it."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"About how many dinosaur bones are on the wall at Dinosaur National Monument?","options":["About 15","About 1,500","About 150,000"],"answer":1,"explain":"A whole wall of rock holds about 1,500 dinosaur bones, left where they were found."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How high is the opening under Delicate Arch?","options":["4 feet","46 feet","460 feet"],"answer":1,"explain":"The opening under Delicate Arch is 46 feet (14 m) high."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does Utah call its snow?","options":["The Wettest Snow in the World","The Greatest Snow on Earth","The Whitest Rain on Earth"],"answer":1,"explain":"Utah's snow falls light and dry, and Utah calls it the Greatest Snow on Earth."}],"picgame":{"type":"catch","title":"Red Rock Park","goal":"Catch what you can find in Utah.","items":[{"icon":"🏜️","name":"red rock desert","good":true},{"icon":"🦕","name":"dinosaur","good":true},{"icon":"🌵","name":"cactus","good":true},{"icon":"⛷️","name":"skier","good":true},{"icon":"🏞️","name":"canyon park","good":true},{"icon":"🐧","name":"penguin","good":false},{"icon":"🐋","name":"whale","good":false},{"icon":"🧊","name":"ice cube","good":false}]}},{"id":"utah-cities","course":"utah","kind":"facts","section":"cities","title":"Cities & Towns","glyph":"🏙️","n":6,"idea":"Utah's cities began with settlers led by Brigham Young, and they now form a long line along the Wasatch Front.","pages":[{"icon":"🏙️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Salt Lake City was founded in 1847."},{"t":"term","word":"settlers","text":"The people led by Brigham Young who founded Salt Lake City."},{"t":"fact","text":"Settlers led by Brigham Young founded it."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Who led the settlers who founded Salt Lake City?","options":["Philo Farnsworth","Brigham Young","Butch Cassidy"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. The answer is there.","explain":"Settlers led by Brigham Young founded it."}}]},{"icon":"📏","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Salt Lake City is part of a long line of cities along the Wasatch Front."},{"t":"fact","text":"This urban corridor is a 120-mile (190 km) stretch, with about 2.8 million people."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"About how many people live in the urban corridor along the Wasatch Front?","options":["Only 28,000","About 2.8 million","Nearly 280 million"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. The answer is there.","explain":"This urban corridor is a 120-mile (190 km) stretch, with about 2.8 million people."}}]},{"icon":"📅","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Four days after arriving in the Salt Lake Valley, Brigham Young marked the building site for the Salt Lake Temple."},{"t":"fact","text":"It took 40 years to finish."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many days after arriving did Young mark the temple site?","options":["Ten days","Four days","Forty days"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"Four days after arriving in the Salt Lake Valley, Brigham Young marked the building site for the Salt Lake Temple."}}]},{"icon":"⏳","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Ogden is the closest big city to the Golden Spike site at Promontory Summit, Utah."},{"t":"fact","text":"That is where the first railroad across the country was joined in 1869."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What famous spike site is closest to Ogden?","options":["The Silver Nail site","The Golden Spike site","The Copper Rail site"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"Ogden is the closest big city to the Golden Spike site at Promontory Summit, Utah."}}]},{"icon":"🧑","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Fort Utah was renamed Provo in 1850."},{"t":"fact","text":"It was named for Étienne Provost, a French-Canadian trapper who arrived in the region in 1825."}]},{"icon":"🤔","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The highest temperature ever recorded in Utah was 118°F (48°C)."},{"t":"fact","text":"It was recorded in south St. George."}]},{"icon":"🔎","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Settlers on the Wasatch Front. A temple site marked in four days. A golden spike near Ogden. A fort renamed Provo. A hot day in St. George."},{"t":"p","text":"Utah has cities."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Salt Lake City","Founded in 1847 by Brigham Young"],["Wasatch Front","A long line of cities in Utah"],["Ogden","Closest sizable city to the Golden Spike site"],["Provo","Named for Étienne Provost"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What year was Salt Lake City founded?","options":["1874","1847","1748"],"answer":1,"explain":"Salt Lake City was founded in 1847."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What was Fort Utah renamed in 1850?","options":["Ogden","Provo","Alta"],"answer":1,"explain":"Fort Utah was renamed Provo in 1850."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How hot was the highest temperature ever recorded in Utah?","options":["Only 81°F","118°F","Nearly 181°F"],"answer":1,"explain":"The highest temperature ever recorded in Utah was 118°F (48°C)."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where was the highest temperature in Utah recorded?","options":["Central Provo","South St. George","Northern Ogden"],"answer":1,"explain":"It was recorded in south St. George."}]},{"id":"utah-people","course":"utah","kind":"facts","section":"people","title":"Famous Utahns","glyph":"🌟","n":6,"idea":"A television inventor, a gun maker with 128 patents and Butch Cassidy all have roots in Utah.","pages":[{"icon":"🌟","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Philo Farnsworth was an American inventor."},{"t":"term","word":"patent","text":"A right the government grants to an inventor, like the first one for the television."},{"t":"fact","text":"He was granted the first patent for the television."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Who was granted the first patent for the television?","options":["John Moses Browning","Philo Farnsworth","Robert LeRoy Parker"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"Philo Farnsworth was an American inventor."}}]},{"icon":"🧑","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Philo Farnsworth was born on August 19, 1906, in a small log cabin near Beaver, Utah."},{"t":"fact","text":"He was the oldest of five children."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where was Philo Farnsworth born?","options":["Near Ogden, Utah","Near Beaver, Utah","Near Provo, Utah"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"Philo Farnsworth was born on August 19, 1906, in a small log cabin near Beaver, Utah."}}]},{"icon":"📅","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"On September 7, 1927, Philo Farnsworth's camera tube sent its first image, a simple straight line."},{"t":"fact","text":"It went to a receiver in another room of his laboratory."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What was the first image Farnsworth's camera tube sent?","options":["A smiling face","A straight line","A tall tower"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"On September 7, 1927, Philo Farnsworth's camera tube sent its first image, a simple straight line."}}]},{"icon":"👤","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"John Browning made his first gun at age 13, in his father's gun shop."},{"t":"fact","text":"He received the first of his 128 patents at age 24."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"At what age did John Browning make his first gun?","options":["At age 3","At age 13","At age 30"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"John Browning made his first gun at age 13, in his father's gun shop."}}]},{"icon":"👑","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"John Browning's father, Jonathan, was one of the pioneers who came to Utah."},{"t":"fact","text":"In 1852 he set up a gunsmith shop in Ogden."}]},{"icon":"🧑‍🔬","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Butch Cassidy was born Robert LeRoy Parker on April 13, 1866, in Beaver, Utah Territory."},{"t":"fact","text":"He was the first of thirteen children."}]},{"icon":"🔎","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A television patent. A log cabin in Beaver. A straight line sent to a receiver. A first gun at age 13. A shop in Ogden."},{"t":"p","text":"Utah people made things."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Farnsworth","Granted the first patent for television"],["Browning","Made his first gun at age 13"],["Jonathan Browning","Set up a gunsmith shop in Ogden"],["Cassidy","Born Robert LeRoy Parker"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where did the first image from Farnsworth's camera tube go?","options":["Onto a big screen at a theater","To a receiver in another room","Into a camera on the street"],"answer":1,"explain":"It went to a receiver in another room of his laboratory."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many patents did John Browning receive?","options":["12","128","1,280"],"answer":1,"explain":"He received the first of his 128 patents at age 24."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where did Jonathan Browning set up a gunsmith shop in 1852?","options":["In Beaver","In Ogden","In Provo"],"answer":1,"explain":"In 1852 he set up a gunsmith shop in Ogden."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many children was Butch Cassidy the first of?","options":["Three children","Thirteen children","Thirty children"],"answer":1,"explain":"He was the first of thirteen children."}]},{"id":"utah-animals","course":"utah","kind":"facts","section":"animals","title":"Utah Animals","glyph":"🦌","n":6,"idea":"Elk that move down the mountain in winter, prairie dogs, a sheep with heavy horns and a tortoise that lives 70 to 80 years.","pages":[{"icon":"🦌","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A Rocky Mountain elk weighs 500 to 750 lb (230 to 340 kg)."},{"t":"fact","text":"Males are about 7.9 ft (2.4 m) long and females about 6.6 ft (2 m)."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How much does a Rocky Mountain elk weigh?","options":["Only 50 to 75 pounds","500 to 750 lb","Over 5,000 pounds"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"A Rocky Mountain elk weighs 500 to 750 lb (230 to 340 kg)."}}]},{"icon":"🏡","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"In the mountains, Rocky Mountain elk often stay in higher places in the warmer months."},{"t":"fact","text":"They move down lower in the winter."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"When do mountain elk move down lower?","options":["During summer","In the winter","Every spring"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"They move down lower in the winter."}}]},{"icon":"📏","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Male Utah prairie dogs weigh 0.66 to 1.98 lb (0.3 to 0.9 kg) in the spring."},{"t":"fact","text":"In the summer they weigh 1.1 to 3.3 lb (0.5 to 1.5 kg)."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How much does a male Utah prairie dog weigh in the summer?","options":["Up to 33 pounds","1.1 to 3.3 lb","Only 0.11 pounds"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"Male Utah prairie dogs weigh 0.66 to 1.98 lb (0.3 to 0.9 kg) in the spring."}}]},{"icon":"🐣","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A Utah prairie dog mother has only one litter a year, usually one to eight pups."},{"t":"term","word":"litter","text":"The group of pups a mother has at one time."},{"t":"fact","text":"She carries the pups for about 28 days before they are born."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many days does a Utah prairie dog mother carry her pups?","options":["Just 2","About 28 days","Nearly 280 days"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. The answer is there.","explain":"She carries the pups for about 28 days before they are born."}}]},{"icon":"📐","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A male bighorn sheep's horns can weigh up to 30 lb (14 kg)."},{"t":"fact","text":"That is as much as all the bones in his body."}]},{"icon":"🍼","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A desert tortoise lives 70 to 80 years on average."},{"t":"fact","text":"It grows slowly, and it has few young."}]},{"icon":"🔎","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"An elk down in winter. A prairie dog with pups. Horns that weigh as much as bones. A tortoise that lives long."},{"t":"p","text":"Utah has many animals."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Elk","Moves down lower in the winter"],["Prairie dog","Has one litter a year"],["Bighorn sheep","Horns weigh up to 30 lb"],["Desert tortoise","Lives 70 to 80 years"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many pups does a Utah prairie dog mother usually have in a litter?","options":["Ten to twenty","One to eight","Twenty to thirty"],"answer":1,"explain":"A Utah prairie dog mother has only one litter a year, usually one to eight pups."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How long does a desert tortoise live on average?","options":["Only 7 or 8","70 to 80 years","Nearly 700 years"],"answer":1,"explain":"A desert tortoise lives 70 to 80 years on average."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"A bighorn sheep's horns can weigh as much as what?","options":["A whole herd of prairie dogs","All the bones in his body","The weight of one small feather"],"answer":1,"explain":"That is as much as all the bones in his body."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where do Rocky Mountain elk stay in the warmer months?","options":["In lower valleys","In higher places","In deep caves"],"answer":1,"explain":"Rocky Mountain elk often stay in higher places in the warmer months."}]},{"id":"utah-dinos","course":"utah","kind":"facts","section":"dinos","title":"Dinosaur Country","glyph":"🦕","n":6,"idea":"Thousands of dinosaur bones have been dug up in Utah, and one raptor had a big claw.","pages":[{"icon":"🦕","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"More than 16,200 bones have been dug up at the Cleveland-Lloyd Dinosaur Quarry in Utah."},{"t":"term","word":"quarry","text":"A place where bones are dug up, like the Cleveland-Lloyd Dinosaur Quarry."},{"t":"fact","text":"Many thousands more are waiting to be dug up and studied."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many bones have been dug up at the Cleveland-Lloyd Dinosaur Quarry?","options":["Fewer than 1,620","More than 16,200","About 162,000"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"More than 16,200 bones have been dug up at the Cleveland-Lloyd Dinosaur Quarry in Utah."}}]},{"icon":"🏺","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Skeletons made from Cleveland-Lloyd bones are on show."},{"t":"fact","text":"They are in more than 65 museums around the world."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"In how many museums can you see skeletons made from Cleveland-Lloyd bones?","options":["Fewer than 6","More than 65","About 650"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. The answer is there.","explain":"They are in more than 65 museums around the world."}}]},{"icon":"📏","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Utahraptor is the largest known member of its dinosaur family."},{"t":"fact","text":"It was about 20 to 23 ft (6 to 7 m) long and weighed around 1,100 lb (500 kg)."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What was the length of a Utahraptor?","options":["About 2 to 3 feet in all","20 to 23 ft (6 to 7 m)","Over 200 feet from end to end"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. The answer is there.","explain":"It was about 20 to 23 ft (6 to 7 m) long and weighed around 1,100 lb (500 kg)."}}]},{"icon":"🤔","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Utahraptor had a big claw that may have been about 9.4 in (24 cm) long."},{"t":"fact","text":"Its feet seem to have had a very tight grip."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What length may a Utahraptor's big claw have been?","options":["Only about 0.94 inches","About 9.4 in (24 cm)","Nearly 94 inches"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"Utahraptor had a big claw that may have been about 9.4 in (24 cm) long."}}]},{"icon":"📐","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Allosaurus averaged about 25 to 26 ft (7.5 to 8 m) long."},{"t":"fact","text":"The largest ones were estimated at about 30 ft (9 m) long."}]},{"icon":"🏆","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Earl Douglass and his team dug up over 700,000 lb (320,000 kg) of fossils."},{"t":"fact","text":"They dug them from a single quarry at what became Dinosaur National Monument."}]},{"icon":"🔎","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A quarry with thousands of bones. Skeletons in museums. A raptor with a big claw. An Allosaurus. Fossils from one quarry."},{"t":"p","text":"Utah has dinosaur bones."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Cleveland-Lloyd","Quarry with over 16,200 bones dug up"],["Utahraptor","Largest known member of its dinosaur family"],["Allosaurus","Averaged about 25 to 26 ft long"],["Earl Douglass","Dug up over 700,000 lb of fossils"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"How heavy was a Utahraptor?","options":["Only a few pounds","Around 1,100 lb","Over ten tons"],"answer":1,"explain":"It was about 20 to 23 ft (6 to 7 m) long and weighed around 1,100 lb (500 kg)."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did Earl Douglass and his team dig up?","options":["A few boxes of old coins","Over 700,000 lb of fossils","Some jars of dried seeds"],"answer":1,"explain":"Earl Douglass and his team dug up over 700,000 lb (320,000 kg) of fossils."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"About how long were the largest Allosaurus ones estimated to be?","options":["Only 3 ft","About 30 ft (9 m)","Nearly 300 feet"],"answer":1,"explain":"The largest ones were estimated at about 30 ft (9 m) long."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What seem to have had a very tight grip?","options":["Its tail","Its feet","Its teeth"],"answer":1,"explain":"Its feet seem to have had a very tight grip."}]},{"id":"utah-lakes","course":"utah","kind":"facts","section":"lakes","title":"Lakes of Utah","glyph":"🏖️","n":6,"idea":"Lakes in Utah: a big lake shared with Idaho, a lake made by a dam, and a shallow lake.","pages":[{"icon":"🏖️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Bear Lake covers about 109 sq mi (280 km2)."},{"t":"fact","text":"It is split about equally between Utah and Idaho. The Utah part is the second-largest natural freshwater lake in Utah, after Utah Lake."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What area does Bear Lake cover?","options":["Only 10 sq mi","About 109 sq mi (280 km2)","Over 1,900 square miles"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"Bear Lake covers about 109 sq mi (280 km2)."}}]},{"icon":"📅","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Jedediah Smith arrived at Bear Lake in June 1827."},{"t":"term","word":"overland","text":"By land, like the first overland round trip to California."},{"t":"fact","text":"That marked the completion of the first overland round trip to California from the United States."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did Smith's arrival in June 1827 complete?","options":["A boat race down the Mississippi River","The first overland round trip to California","Another long railroad across the West"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. The answer is there.","explain":"That marked the completion of the first overland round trip to California from the United States."}}]},{"icon":"⏳","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The south end of Bear Lake, near Laketown, was the site of two annual Rocky Mountain Rendezvous."},{"t":"fact","text":"They were held in the summers of 1827 and 1828."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which town is near the rendezvous site at Bear Lake?","options":["Park City","Laketown","Salt Lake City"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"The south end of Bear Lake, near Laketown, was the site of two annual Rocky Mountain Rendezvous."}}]},{"icon":"📏","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The Glen Canyon Dam that makes Lake Powell is 710 ft (216 m) high."},{"t":"fact","text":"When the lake is full, its surface is about 3,700 ft (1,100 m) above sea level."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is the height of the dam that holds back Lake Powell?","options":["About 71 feet","710 ft (216 m)","Over 7,100 feet"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"The Glen Canyon Dam that makes Lake Powell is 710 ft (216 m) high."}}]},{"icon":"🕰️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Lake Powell took sixteen years to fill to the 3,700 ft (1,100 m) level."},{"t":"fact","text":"It got there on June 22, 1980."}]},{"icon":"📐","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Utah Lake is shallow."},{"t":"fact","text":"Its deepest spot is just under 14 ft (4.3 m), and its average depth is about 10.5 ft (3.2 m)."}]},{"icon":"🔎","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A lake shared with Idaho. A trip to California. A rendezvous near Laketown. A dam on Lake Powell. A shallow lake."},{"t":"p","text":"Utah has lakes."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Bear Lake","Split between Utah and Idaho"],["Laketown","Near the Rocky Mountain Rendezvous site"],["Glen Canyon Dam","The dam that makes Lake Powell"],["Utah Lake","Shallow with an average depth of about 10.5 ft"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many years did Lake Powell take to fill?","options":["Sixty years","Sixteen years","Six years"],"answer":1,"explain":"Lake Powell took sixteen years to fill to the 3,700 ft (1,100 m) level."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"On what date did Lake Powell reach its 3,700 ft level?","options":["August 2, 1890","June 22, 1980","May 22, 1908"],"answer":1,"explain":"It got there on June 22, 1980."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How deep is the deepest spot of Utah Lake?","options":["Well over 140 feet deep","Just under 14 ft (4.3 m)","Nearly 1.4 feet deep"],"answer":1,"explain":"Its deepest spot is just under 14 ft (4.3 m)."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which two states is Bear Lake split between?","options":["Nevada and Oregon","Utah and Idaho","Arizona and Colorado"],"answer":1,"explain":"It is split about equally between Utah and Idaho."}]},{"id":"utah-redrock","course":"utah","kind":"facts","section":"redrock","title":"Red Rock Country","glyph":"🪨","n":6,"idea":"Tall red buttes, hoodoos, stone bridges and the darkest sky ever assessed by the Park Service.","pages":[{"icon":"🪨","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Monument Valley is famous for its buttes."},{"t":"term","word":"butte","text":"A rock formation, like the famous butte formations of Monument Valley."},{"t":"fact","text":"The most famous butte formations are along the Utah–Arizona state line."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Along what line are the famous Monument Valley buttes?","options":["A line of old railroad tracks","The Utah–Arizona state line","The edge of the Great Salt Lake"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. The answer is there.","explain":"The most famous butte formations are along the Utah–Arizona state line."}}]},{"icon":"🧑","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Director John Ford filmed a number of Westerns in Monument Valley."},{"t":"fact","text":"A critic wrote that its five square miles (13 km2) define the American West for moviegoers."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which director filmed Westerns in Monument Valley?","options":["Steven Spielberg","John Ford","Walt Disney"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"Director John Ford filmed a number of Westerns in Monument Valley."}}]},{"icon":"📏","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Goblin Valley State Park has hoodoos."},{"t":"fact","text":"It and Bryce Canyon National Park have some of the largest groups of hoodoos in the world."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does Goblin Valley State Park have many of?","options":["Geysers","Hoodoos","Glaciers"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"Goblin Valley State Park has hoodoos."}}]},{"icon":"🏗️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Natural Bridges has three bridges."},{"t":"fact","text":"Their names are Kachina, Owachomo and Sipapu. Sipapu is the largest. All three are Hopi names."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which of the three bridges is the largest?","options":["Kachina","Sipapu","Owachomo"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. The answer is there.","explain":"Their names are Kachina, Owachomo and Sipapu."}}]},{"icon":"👀","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Natural Bridges has the darkest sky ever assessed by the National Park Service Night Sky Team."},{"t":"fact","text":"It rates a Class 2 on the Bortle Dark-Sky Scale."}]},{"icon":"📐","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Rainbow Bridge has a span of 275 ft (84 m) and a height of 290 ft (88 m)."},{"t":"fact","text":"It is one of the largest natural arches in the world."}]},{"icon":"🔎","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Buttes on the state line. Westerns filmed by a director. Groups of hoodoos. Three bridges. A dark sky. A big arch."},{"t":"p","text":"Red rock country is big."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Monument Valley","Famous buttes along the state line"],["John Ford","Filmed Westerns in Monument Valley"],["Hoodoos","Large groups at Goblin Valley"],["Sipapu","Largest of the three bridges"],["Rainbow Bridge","A span of 275 ft"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What kind of names are the three bridge names at Natural Bridges?","options":["Navajo names","Hopi names","Spanish names"],"answer":1,"explain":"All three are Hopi names."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How tall is Rainbow Bridge?","options":["About 29 feet","290 ft (88 m)","Nearly 2,900 feet"],"answer":1,"explain":"Rainbow Bridge has a span of 275 ft (84 m) and a height of 290 ft (88 m)."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What class does the sky at Natural Bridges rate on the Bortle Dark-Sky Scale?","options":["Level 9","Class 2","Grade 5"],"answer":1,"explain":"It rates a Class 2 on the Bortle Dark-Sky Scale."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which park has some of the largest groups of hoodoos, along with Goblin Valley?","options":["Yellowstone National Park","Bryce Canyon National Park","Everglades National Park"],"answer":1,"explain":"It and Bryce Canyon National Park have some of the largest groups of hoodoos in the world."}]},{"id":"utah-ski","course":"utah","kind":"facts","section":"ski","title":"Skiing & Snow","glyph":"🎿","n":6,"idea":"Skiing in Utah: a resort with lots of snow, the largest ski area in the U.S., and the 2002 Winter Olympics.","pages":[{"icon":"🎿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Alta opened its first lift on January 15, 1939."},{"t":"term","word":"resort","text":"A place for skiing, like Alta, one of the oldest ski resorts in the country."},{"t":"fact","text":"It is one of the oldest ski resorts in the country."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"When did Alta open its first lift?","options":["November 5, 1993","January 15, 1939","March 15, 1989"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"Alta opened its first lift on January 15, 1939."}}]},{"icon":"📏","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Alta gets more snow than most Utah resorts."},{"t":"fact","text":"Its average annual snowfall is 543 inches (13.8 m)."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How much snow does Alta get in an average year?","options":["Only 54 inches","543 inches (13.8 m)","Over 5,000 inches"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. The answer is there.","explain":"Its average annual snowfall is 543 inches (13.8 m)."}}]},{"icon":"🤔","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Alta does not allow snowboarders."},{"t":"fact","text":"It is one of just three ski areas in the U.S. that do not."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many U.S. ski areas do not allow snowboarders?","options":["Thirty","Three","Thirteen"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. The answer is there.","explain":"It is one of just three ski areas in the U.S."}}]},{"icon":"📐","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Park City lies mostly higher than 7,000 ft (2,100 m), so it is usually cooler than Salt Lake City."},{"t":"fact","text":"Salt Lake City sits at about 4,300 ft (1,300 m)."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is the height of most of Park City?","options":["About 700 feet above the sea","Higher than 7,000 ft","Under 70 feet above the sea"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"Park City lies mostly higher than 7,000 ft (2,100 m), so it is usually cooler than Salt Lake City."}}]},{"icon":"📅","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"In 2015, Park City Ski Resort and Canyons resorts merged."},{"t":"fact","text":"That created the largest ski area in the U.S."}]},{"icon":"⚖️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The 2002 Winter Olympics in Salt Lake City had 2,399 athletes from 78 nations."},{"t":"fact","text":"They took part in 78 events in 15 disciplines."}]},{"icon":"🔎","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A lift from 1939. Lots of snow. No snowboards. A cool town up high. A large ski area. A Winter Olympics."},{"t":"p","text":"Utah is great for skiing."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Alta","Opened its first lift in 1939"],["Park City","Lies mostly higher than 7,000 ft"],["Snowboarders","Not allowed at Alta"],["Olympics","Held in Salt Lake City in 2002"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many athletes took part in the 2002 Winter Olympics in Salt Lake City?","options":["239","2,399","23,990"],"answer":1,"explain":"The 2002 Winter Olympics in Salt Lake City had 2,399 athletes from 78 nations."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many nations did the athletes come from?","options":["8","78","780"],"answer":1,"explain":"The 2002 Winter Olympics in Salt Lake City had 2,399 athletes from 78 nations."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What happened to Park City Ski Resort and Canyons resorts in 2015?","options":["They closed","They merged","They moved"],"answer":1,"explain":"In 2015, Park City Ski Resort and Canyons resorts merged."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many disciplines were in the 2002 Winter Olympics?","options":["5","15","150"],"answer":1,"explain":"They took part in 78 events in 15 disciplines."}]},{"id":"world-middle","course":"world","kind":"facts","section":"middle","title":"The Middle Ages","glyph":"🏰","n":6,"idea":"Castles had arrow slits that were narrow outside but wide inside. Then see how a 404-foot spire stands on foundations only 4 feet deep, and how monks, guilds, and a king's survey ran medieval life.","pages":[{"icon":"🏰","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Castles had arrow slits that were narrow on the outside but opened wide on the inside."},{"t":"fact","text":"This shape lets an archer swing his aim across a whole field through a tiny gap that attackers can barely hit."},{"t":"try","text":"If you ever visit a castle, put your arm through a slit and feel how it works."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why is the slit wide on the inside?","options":["So guards can drop stones on climbers through it","The archer can swing his aim across a whole field through a tiny gap","So the stone around the gap stays strong and does not crack under attack"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It explains why the inside is wide.","explain":"The wide inside lets the archer move freely while the narrow outside makes it hard to hit."}}]},{"icon":"⛪","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The spire of Salisbury Cathedral soars 404 feet into the sky, but its foundations are only 4 feet deep in wet gravel."},{"t":"fact","text":"It leans, it has been braced repeatedly, and there is a medieval iron rod inside that somebody added when they saw something was going wrong. Yet, it has not fallen down."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How deep are the spire's foundations?","options":["About 10 feet","Only 4 feet","About 20 feet"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the first sentence. It gives the exact depth.","explain":"The foundations are only 4 feet deep in wet gravel, but the spire still stands."}}]},{"icon":"📜","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Before printing, every book was copied by hand. A monk worked in a scriptorium, the room where books were copied. He worked in daylight only, with no candles near the parchment, the animal-skin pages. One flame or drop of hot wax could ruin months of work."},{"t":"fact","text":"A good copyist produced just a few pages a day. Copying a Bible could take years of work."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why did monks copy books in daylight only?","options":["No candles were allowed near the parchment","To save on candle wax","Because the parchment was too thick to see through"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the second sentence. It explains why no candles were used.","explain":"No candles were allowed near the parchment to protect it from damage."}}]},{"icon":"🐷","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"In 1086, the King of England ordered a big survey to find out who owned what. It went down to every plow, mill, and even pig."},{"t":"fact","text":"The survey filled two volumes written in abbreviated Latin that you can still read today at the National Archives."},{"t":"p","text":"People called it Domesday, after the day of judgment. There was no appealing against what it said you owned."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did the survey count?","options":["Only the number of castles, towers, and walls","Only the length of every road and river","Who owned what, down to plows, mills and pigs"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the second sentence. It lists some of the things counted.","explain":"The survey went down to every plow, mill, and pig."}}]},{"icon":"🔨","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"In medieval towns, if you wanted to sell your work, you had to join the guild for your trade."},{"t":"fact","text":"A boy would be apprenticed for around seven years. Then he worked for wages. He became a master only by producing one piece good enough to be judged. That piece was his masterpiece."},{"t":"term","word":"masterpiece","text":"The qualifying test piece that earned you master status in the guild."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did masterpiece originally mean?","options":["The finest work of art that a student ever made","A special gift made for the king by the best workers","A qualifying test piece that earned you master status"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the term box. It defines what a masterpiece was.","explain":"A masterpiece was the qualifying test piece that allowed someone to become a master in their trade."}}]},{"icon":"🧵","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The Bayeux Tapestry is 230 feet long, and it is not a tapestry at all."},{"t":"fact","text":"It is embroidery — wool thread stitched onto linen, in eight colors. You can walk beside it along the wall of a museum in Normandy."},{"t":"p","text":"There is even a comet stitched into the sky on it. It is Halley's Comet, which people saw in 1066."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is the Bayeux Tapestry really?","options":["Embroidery, wool thread stitched onto linen","A tapestry woven with colorful threads","A painting on a long piece of linen"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It tells you what the Bayeux Tapestry actually is.","explain":"The Bayeux Tapestry is embroidery — wool thread stitched onto linen."}}]},{"icon":"🏰","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A slit for a shot. A spire pointing up. A pen on parchment. A survey of the land. A guild test. A long embroidery that is not a tapestry."},{"t":"p","text":"Each one shows how people lived and worked in the Middle Ages."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Arrow slit","Narrow on the outside, wide on the inside"],["Salisbury Cathedral","Spire stands despite 4 feet deep foundations"],["Scriptorium","Monks copied books in daylight only"],["Domesday","Survey counted everything down to pigs"],["Guild","Required a masterpiece to become a master"],["Bayeux Tapestry","Embroidery, not a tapestry at all"]]},"picgame":{"type":"tapall","title":"Monk's Desk","goal":"Tap what a monk needs to copy a book.","items":[{"icon":"🖋️","name":"pen","good":true},{"icon":"📜","name":"parchment","good":true},{"icon":"☀️","name":"daylight","good":true},{"icon":"📖","name":"book","good":true},{"icon":"🕯️","name":"candle","good":false},{"icon":"🔥","name":"flame","good":false},{"icon":"🌙","name":"night","good":false}]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"In 1086, the King of England ordered a survey so detailed people nicknamed it after Judgment Day. What did it record?","options":["Who owned what, down to individual plows, mills, and pigs","Only the largest estates and castles","How much tax each town owed"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Domesday Book fills two volumes — there was no appealing what it said you owned."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where does the word \"masterpiece\" actually come from?","options":["A medieval guild apprentice's qualifying piece of work, judged before he could become a master craftsman","A term for the greatest artwork of an era","A piece commissioned by a king"],"answer":0,"explain":"It originally meant a qualifying test, not a great work of art."},{"type":"tf","prompt":"The Bayeux Tapestry is actually woven cloth, exactly like other tapestries of its time.","answer":1,"explain":"It's embroidery — wool thread stitched onto linen in eight colors, running 230 feet long."}]},{"id":"world-voyages","course":"world","kind":"facts","section":"voyages","title":"Great Voyages","glyph":"🗺️","n":6,"idea":"Ships set out to sail around the world, but only one returned. Find out who finished the voyage and what gifts explorers brought back.","pages":[{"icon":"⛵","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Five ships set out to sail around the world. Only one ship finished the trip, and Ferdinand Magellan was not on it."},{"t":"fact","text":"Magellan was killed in the Philippines. The man who finished the voyage was Juan Sebastián Elcano, and his coat of arms showed a globe with the words \"you went round me first.\""},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many ships finished the trip around the world?","options":["Two","One","Five"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the second sentence on the page.","explain":"Only one ship, the Victoria, finished the trip around the world."}}]},{"icon":"🌟","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Polynesian navigators crossed thousands of miles of open Pacific with no instruments."},{"t":"term","word":"swells","text":"The big waves that move across the ocean."},{"t":"fact","text":"They steered by the stars, read the direction of the swells, watched cloud shapes over islands they could not yet see, and followed the flight paths of birds."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How did Polynesian navigators find their way?","options":["By iron compasses, paper maps, and clocks","By following whales and sharks at sea","By the stars, swells, clouds, and birds"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the fact box on the page.","explain":"They used the stars, the direction of the swells, cloud shapes, and bird flights to navigate."}}]},{"icon":"🦒","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Chinese fleets under Zheng He sailed as far as East Africa and brought back some unusual gifts."},{"t":"fact","text":"One of those gifts was a giraffe for the emperor. The size of his largest ships is disputed, but a stone pillar lists where he went."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did Zheng He bring back for the emperor?","options":["A giraffe","A polar bear","A penguin"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the fact box.","explain":"One of the gifts brought back was a giraffe."}}]},{"icon":"⚓","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The warship Vasa sank after sailing only about 4,000 feet on her first voyage in 1628, right in front of Stockholm."},{"t":"fact","text":"She went down because she was too tall and too narrow."},{"t":"p","text":"The cold, barely salty Baltic water had no wood-eating shipworms, so it preserved her. In 1961, they raised her and put her in a museum built around her."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why did the Vasa sink?","options":["She was too tall and too narrow","Her sails were torn by the wind","She hit a rock"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It tells you why she sank.","explain":"She was too tall and too narrow, which made her unstable in the water."}}]},{"icon":"🍊","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"In 1747, a naval surgeon had an idea to test what might cure scurvy, the deadly sickness sailors got after months at sea without fresh fruit."},{"t":"fact","text":"He took twelve sick sailors, split them into six pairs, and gave each pair a different remedy. The two given oranges and lemons recovered within days."},{"t":"p","text":"It was one of the first fair tests of its kind: each pair got a different remedy, so the results could be compared. But the navy took over forty years to act on it."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which remedy worked?","options":["Vinegar by the spoon","Oranges and lemons","Sea water by the cup"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It names what helped the sailors recover quickly.","explain":"The pair given oranges and lemons recovered within days."}}]},{"icon":"📕","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Marco Polo did not write his account of China down himself."},{"t":"fact","text":"He dictated it to a writer of adventure tales he shared a prison cell with. That is one reason people have argued about it for centuries."},{"t":"p","text":"He describes paper money accurately, something Europe had never seen before, and black stones that people burned as fuel."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Who wrote down Marco Polo's account?","options":["A royal scribe sent by the Pope to write it down","A priest he shared a long sea voyage with","A writer of adventure tales he shared a prison cell with"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the first sentence in the fact box. It names who wrote it down.","explain":"The adventure writer took down what Marco Polo told him in prison."}}]},{"icon":"⛵","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Ships lost at sea. Stars read like a map. A giraffe in a city. A warship that sank on her first trip. Lemons helped sailors. A book dictated in a prison cell."},{"t":"p","text":"All of it part of the voyages that reshaped how people saw the world."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Elcano","Finished the round-the-world voyage"],["swells","The big waves that move across the ocean"],["Zheng He","Brought a giraffe for the emperor"],["Vasa","Sank because she was too tall and too narrow"],["oranges and lemons","Cured scurvy in days"],["Marco Polo","Dictated his account in prison"]]},"picgame":{"type":"catch","title":"Star Sailors","goal":"Catch what the Polynesian sailors watched.","items":[{"icon":"⭐","name":"star","good":true},{"icon":"🌊","name":"wave","good":true},{"icon":"☁️","name":"cloud","good":true},{"icon":"🐦","name":"bird","good":true},{"icon":"🧭","name":"compass","good":false},{"icon":"🗺️","name":"map","good":false},{"icon":"⏰","name":"clock","good":false}]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many of Ferdinand Magellan's roughly 270 original crew actually completed the voyage around the world?","options":["Eighteen, on one ship, without Magellan himself","All five ships returned safely","About half the original crew"],"answer":0,"explain":"Magellan was killed in the Philippines — Juan Sebastián Elcano finished the voyage in his place."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How did Polynesian navigators cross thousands of miles of open Pacific Ocean with no instruments?","options":["By reading stars, swells, cloud shapes, and bird flight paths","By following established sea routes marked with buoys","By using early compasses made from magnetized stone"],"answer":0,"explain":"In 1976 a crew successfully sailed this way from Hawaii to Tahiti, though they had to bring in a navigator from Micronesia since the knowledge had been lost in Hawaii."},{"type":"tf","prompt":"A naval surgeon in 1747 proved citrus fruit cured scurvy by testing different remedies on groups of sick sailors.","answer":0,"explain":"It's one of the first properly controlled trials ever run — though the navy took over forty years to act on the results."}]},{"id":"world-printing","course":"world","kind":"facts","section":"printing","title":"Printing & Books","glyph":"📖","n":6,"idea":"Printing fixed English spelling while pronunciation was still changing. Find out how that made spelling so tricky, and how printing began.","pages":[{"icon":"📖","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Gutenberg's great Bible has 42 lines to the column, and about 180 copies were made. Forty-nine survive today."},{"t":"fact","text":"The pages are so even that many people at first took them for handwriting. His real invention was a mold. He poured melted metal into it to cast thousands of identical metal letters quickly."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What was Gutenberg's real invention?","options":["A machine that pressed whole pages onto paper","A mold for casting identical metal letters","A special paper made from plant fiber and glue"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It says his mold let him cast thousands of identical metal letters quickly.","explain":"His mold made it possible to cast thousands of identical metal letters, which was key to the printing process."}}]},{"icon":"🏮","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The oldest dated printed book is not European. It is a Buddhist scroll printed from carved wooden blocks in AD 868."},{"t":"fact","text":"This scroll was found sealed in a cave in western China and is now kept in the British Library."},{"t":"term","word":"Buddhist","text":"Having to do with Buddhism, a religion that teaches people to find peace through meditation and good actions."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where was the oldest dated printed book found?","options":["Found inside an old library in Europe","Under the floor of a temple in India","Sealed in a cave in western China"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It says it was found sealed in a cave in western China.","explain":"The scroll was discovered sealed in a cave in western China."}}]},{"icon":"📄","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Paper was invented in China and took about a thousand years to reach Europe, traveling west along the Silk Road through Samarkand and Baghdad."},{"t":"fact","text":"Before paper arrived in Europe, most books were written on animal skin. A single large book could take the hides of a whole flock."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What were most books written on before paper arrived in Europe?","options":["Animal skin","Stone tablets","Wooden blocks"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the second sentence. It tells you what Europe used to write on before paper.","explain":"Before paper, Europe wrote on animal skin, and a large book could use hides from an entire flock."}}]},{"icon":"🖤","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Ordinary ink runs right off metal type, so printers needed something new."},{"t":"fact","text":"Printers invented a sticky paint made of soot ground into boiled linseed oil. It was more like artists' paint than anything in an inkwell."},{"t":"try","text":"Try writing on a metal spoon with a pen that has watery ink, then with a smear of oil paint. Only the paint stays put."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What was printer's ink made of?","options":["Colored clay mixed into thick tree sap","Soot ground into boiled linseed oil","Crushed berries stirred into beeswax"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It tells you exactly what printer's ink was made of.","explain":"Printer's ink was a sticky paint made from soot mixed with boiled linseed oil."}}]},{"icon":"🍇","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The press used in printing was not brand new either."},{"t":"fact","text":"Wine and olive growers had been using big screw presses for centuries, and the printing press is just that machine with a flat plate on the bottom of the screw."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What machine did the printing press come from?","options":["The screw presses of wine and olive growers","The big stone mills of grain and flour makers","The wooden looms of cloth and rug weavers"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It tells you exactly what the printing press was based on.","explain":"The printing press borrowed its design from the screw presses used by wine and olive growers for centuries."}}]},{"icon":"🔤","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Once printing arrived, spelling started to settle down. Every copy of a word looked the same in thousands of books, so one spelling won and others faded."},{"t":"fact","text":"English spelling froze at an awkward moment. Words like knight and through were once said roughly as they look. The spelling was fixed in print just as the pronunciation was still changing.","label":"Worked out"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why is English spelling so strange?","options":["It was made up by one king who liked unusual letters","It was decided by a vote of all the people in England","It was fixed in print while pronunciation was still changing"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the last sentence in the fact box. It says when the spelling was fixed.","explain":"The spelling was fixed in print just as the pronunciation was still moving, which is why English spelling can seem so strange now."}}]},{"icon":"📚","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Cast letters. Carved blocks. Paper from China. Ink from soot. An old wine press for printing."},{"t":"p","text":"Each part helped change how words were spread."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Gutenberg's great Bible","Has 42 lines per column"],["Buddhist scroll","Found sealed in a cave in western China"],["Animal skin","Used for writing before paper"],["Printer's ink","Made from soot and linseed oil"],["Screw presses","Base of the printing press"],["English spelling","Fixed while pronunciation changed"]]},"picgame":{"type":"tapall","title":"Printer's Tools","goal":"Tap what printers used to print.","items":[{"icon":"🔤","name":"letters","good":true},{"icon":"🖤","name":"ink","good":true},{"icon":"📄","name":"paper","good":true},{"icon":"🪵","name":"wood block","good":true},{"icon":"🚗","name":"car","good":false},{"icon":"🎮","name":"game","good":false},{"icon":"🍦","name":"ice cream","good":false},{"icon":"🧸","name":"teddy","good":false}]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What was Gutenberg's real breakthrough invention — more important than the printing press itself?","options":["A mold that could quickly cast thousands of identical metal letters","A new type of paper","A faster way to bind pages into books"],"answer":0,"explain":"The metal alloy was soft enough to cast and hard enough to print — pages came out so even, people doubted they weren't handwritten."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where was paper actually invented, long before it reached Europe?","options":["China","Egypt","Baghdad"],"answer":0,"explain":"It took about a thousand years to travel west along the Silk Road — before that, Europeans wrote on animal skin."},{"type":"tf","prompt":"The printing press was an entirely new invention, built from scratch with no earlier machine to base it on.","answer":1,"explain":"The press itself was borrowed from big screw presses that wine and olive growers had used for centuries."}]},{"id":"world-discovery","course":"world","kind":"facts","section":"discovery","title":"Age of Discovery","glyph":"🔭","n":6,"idea":"Galileo saw moons orbiting Jupiter with his homemade telescope. Then meet the people who weighed the air, kept time at sea, named every living thing, and paid to print Newton's book.","pages":[{"icon":"🔭","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"In the winter of 1610, Galileo pointed his homemade telescope at Jupiter and saw something odd."},{"t":"fact","text":"Four little stars beside it that moved from night to night. He drew them every night, and those drawings still exist today. They are moons going round something that is not the Earth."},{"t":"try","text":"You can try this too. Brace binoculars against a wall and look at Jupiter two nights in a row."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did Galileo's four little stars turn out to be?","options":["Moons going round Jupiter","Planets going round the sun","Stars that moved closer each night"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the first fact on the page. It tells you what they turned out to be.","explain":"They are moons, going around something that is not Earth."}}]},{"icon":"🌡️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Evangelista Torricelli took a glass tube, filled it with mercury, and turned it upside down in a dish."},{"t":"fact","text":"The column of mercury dropped to about 30 inches and then stopped. Something was holding it up: the weight of the air. He had just built the first barometer. The empty space at the top of the tube was a vacuum."},{"t":"term","word":"vacuum","text":"A space with nothing in it at all, not even air."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What held the mercury column up?","options":["The bottom of the dish","The glass tube","The weight of the air"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It tells you what is holding the mercury up.","explain":"The weight of the air holds up the column of mercury in the barometer."}}]},{"icon":"⌚","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"John Harrison spent decades building a clock that could keep time at sea. The one that finally passed the tests is a watch about 5 inches (13 cm) across, and it is kept in Greenwich."},{"t":"fact","text":"A clock that loses just a few seconds a day piles up miles of error on a long voyage. Sailors find how far east or west they are by comparing noon where they are with the time at home. Every four seconds wrong is a mile out of position."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How far out of position is a clock four seconds wrong?","options":["Half a mile","A mile","Two miles"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It gives the number of seconds and the distance.","explain":"Every four seconds wrong on a clock means a mile out of position."}}]},{"icon":"🏷️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Carl Linnaeus had an idea to make talking about living things easier."},{"t":"fact","text":"He decided every living thing should have exactly two Latin names. One name is the group, and the other is the kind. That's why a wolf is Canis lupus everywhere on Earth, no matter what language you speak."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why does every living thing get two names?","options":["The first name is for the male and the second is for the female, so no one mixes them up","One name is its group and the other is its kind, so everyone can say which one they mean","Each name belongs to a different stage, young or grown, so people know which stage they mean"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It explains why two names are used.","explain":"The first name tells you the group, and the second name tells you the specific kind within that group."}}]},{"icon":"🧪","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The Royal Society, a group of scientists in London, had a motto in Latin that means take nobody's word for it."},{"t":"fact","text":"They did experiments in front of witnesses and then published what happened, including times when things did not work out as planned."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did the Royal Society do with its experiments?","options":["Did them in front of witnesses and published the results, failures too","Ran them in private rooms and shared only the results that worked out well","Locked each result in a vault until the king gave permission to share it"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It explains how they made sure everyone knew what happened.","explain":"They did not hide anything, even if things did not work out as expected."}}]},{"icon":"📚","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Isaac Newton's great book, the Principia, took about eighteen months to write, all in Latin."},{"t":"fact","text":"The Royal Society could not help pay for printing it. They had already spent their whole book budget on a history of fishes that nobody bought."},{"t":"p","text":"A friend, Edmond Halley, paid to print the book out of his own pocket."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Who paid to print Newton's book?","options":["His teacher Isaac Barrow","The king of England himself","His friend Edmond Halley"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the last paragraph. It names the friend who paid.","explain":"Edmond Halley put up the money, not the Royal Society or Newton."}}]},{"icon":"🔭","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A telescope that sees more. A tube of mercury to weigh the air. A watch that tells time on the move. Two names that mean something, and a motto in Latin."},{"t":"p","text":"Each one came from looking closely, testing, and writing down what really happened."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Galileo","Saw moons orbiting Jupiter"],["Torricelli","Built the first barometer"],["John Harrison","Made a clock that keeps time at sea"],["Carl Linnaeus","Gave every living thing two Latin names"],["Royal Society","Took nobody's word for it, did experiments"],["Edmond Halley","Paid to print Newton's book"]]},"picgame":{"type":"tapall","title":"Thinker Tools","goal":"Tap what the old thinkers used.","items":[{"icon":"🔭","name":"telescope","good":true},{"icon":"⌚","name":"watch","good":true},{"icon":"📖","name":"book","good":true},{"icon":"🧪","name":"glass tube","good":true},{"icon":"📱","name":"phone","good":false},{"icon":"🚀","name":"rocket","good":false},{"icon":"💻","name":"laptop","good":false},{"icon":"🎮","name":"game","good":false}]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did Evangelista Torricelli accidentally invent by turning a mercury-filled tube upside down in a dish?","options":["The first barometer, by discovering that air itself has weight","The first clock accurate enough for sea travel","A way to measure temperature"],"answer":0,"explain":"The mercury column dropped and stopped at a fixed height — something was holding it up, and that something was the weight of the air."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What was the Royal Society's core principle, captured in its Latin motto?","options":["Take nobody's word for it — do the experiment in front of witnesses, and publish even the failures","Knowledge should be kept among scholars only","Only royalty could fund scientific research"],"answer":0,"explain":"Publishing failures honestly is the part later ages have found hardest to keep up."},{"type":"tf","prompt":"John Harrison spent decades trying to build a clock accurate enough to use at sea, since a clock that lost even a few seconds a day could put a ship miles off course.","answer":0,"explain":"Every four seconds of error meant being a mile out of position — his winning design was a watch about 5 inches across."}]},{"id":"world-builders","course":"world","kind":"facts","section":"builders","title":"Great Builders","glyph":"🏗️","n":6,"idea":"The Great Wall isn’t visible from space. Find out how builders at Machu Picchu, Petra, and Hagia Sophia made structures that survive earthquakes, and how the Dutch keep the sea out.","pages":[{"icon":"🧱","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Have you ever heard that you can see the Great Wall of China from space? It is actually not true."},{"t":"fact","text":"The Great Wall is long, but it is only about 20 ft (6 m) wide. Much of it is made of the same earth and stone as the ground beside it. Astronauts have said so repeatedly, and photographs back them up."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Can you see the Great Wall from space with the naked eye?","options":["No, it blends in too much","Yes, it stands out clearly","Only at night when it is lit up"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It tells you why the wall is hard to see.","explain":"The Great Wall blends in with the ground too much to be seen from space without a camera."}}]},{"icon":"🧗","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"At Machu Picchu, the stones fit so exactly that you cannot slide a knife blade between them. They have no mortar holding them together."},{"t":"term","word":"mortar","text":"A sticky paste that holds bricks or stones together."},{"t":"fact","text":"In an earthquake, these blocks jostle and settle back into place. Spanish colonial buildings in the same region fell down, while these did not. Nothing but their exact fit holds them together."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What holds the Machu Picchu stones together?","options":["Wooden pegs hidden inside the stone blocks","A sticky tree sap spread between each stone","Nothing but their exact fit, no mortar"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It tells you what holds the stones together.","explain":"Because the blocks fit so exactly, they jostle and settle back into place in an earthquake instead of breaking apart."}}]},{"icon":"🏜️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"In Petra, the carvers did not build up, they carved down. They started at the top of a solid sandstone cliff and worked their way down."},{"t":"fact","text":"The great fronts were carved straight into the cliff from the top down. They needed little or no scaffolding because they cut away the rock beneath their own feet as they went."},{"t":"p","text":"Unfinished tombs still show where the work stopped partway down, revealing how the method was done."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why did the Petra carvers need no scaffolding?","options":["They stood on tall wooden platforms that were later taken away","They started at the top and cut away the rock under their own feet","They hung from long ropes tied to the top and carved from there"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It explains where they started and what they cut away.","explain":"By starting at the top and cutting down, they always had a solid surface to stand on as they worked."}}]},{"icon":"🕌","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"In Istanbul, the dome of Hagia Sophia is like a crown with forty windows around its base."},{"t":"fact","text":"From below, the roof looks as though it is resting on a ring of light because there are forty windows around the base of the dome."},{"t":"p","text":"The dome partly fell in earthquakes more than once and was rebuilt each time. The first rebuilding, in the 500s, made it taller and stronger."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many windows ring the dome of Hagia Sophia?","options":["Twenty","Forty","Sixty"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It gives the exact number of windows.","explain":"The dome has forty windows around its base, creating a ring of light from below."}}]},{"icon":"🌴","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Angkor Wat is the largest religious building in the world by area, with a moat that goes over 3 miles all the way around."},{"t":"fact","text":"Aircraft have surveyed the forest with lasers, and they found an entire city laid out around the temple, complete with roads and reservoirs."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did the laser survey find?","options":["A long network of tunnels and caves under the temple","A secret vault full of gold behind the temple walls","A whole city of roads and reservoirs around the temple"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the second sentence. It gives what the lasers found.","explain":"The lasers fired through the forest canopy revealed streets and structures that had been hidden for centuries."}}]},{"icon":"🌷","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"About a quarter of the Netherlands sits below sea level, but it stays dry."},{"t":"fact","text":"Dikes, long walls of earth, and pumps keep the water out. The legal system for maintaining them is older than the country's parliament. Water boards were electing officials in the Middle Ages."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What keeps the low Netherlands dry?","options":["Dikes and pumps, kept up by a very old legal system","Deep tunnels that carry the sea away under the ground","Tall cliffs and rocky hills along the whole coast"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It tells you what keeps it dry and who looks after it.","explain":"Dikes and pumps are crucial, but an old legal system helps keep them maintained and working properly."}}]},{"icon":"🏗️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A wall that blends into the land. Stones held together by their exact fit. A cliff carved from the top down. A ring of light under a dome. A city hidden under a forest. A country kept dry by dikes and pumps."},{"t":"p","text":"Each one is a way that people built something to last."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Great Wall","Long but blends in too much"],["Machu Picchu","Stones fit without mortar"],["Petra","Carved into cliffs from the top down"],["Hagia Sophia","Dome with forty windows"],["Angkor Wat","Largest religious building with a surrounding city"],["Netherlands","Dikes and pumps keep it dry"]]},"picgame":{"type":"tapall","title":"Hidden City","goal":"Tap what the lasers found hiding in the forest.","items":[{"icon":"🛣️","name":"road","good":true},{"icon":"🏙️","name":"city","good":true},{"icon":"💧","name":"water","good":true},{"icon":"🪙","name":"gold","good":false},{"icon":"🕳️","name":"tunnel","good":false},{"icon":"💎","name":"jewel","good":false},{"icon":"🚀","name":"rocket","good":false},{"icon":"🐉","name":"dragon","good":false}]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"Can the Great Wall of China actually be seen from space with the naked eye, as often claimed?","options":["No — astronauts have repeatedly said it can't, and photographs back them up","Yes, it's the only human structure visible from orbit","Only from very low orbit, not from the moon"],"answer":0,"explain":"Highways and airport runways are actually much easier to spot, since they're wider and a different color than the ground around them."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How were the great carved facades at Petra actually built into the sandstone cliff?","options":["Carvers started at the top and worked downward, needing no scaffolding","Blocks were carved separately and assembled against the cliff","Workers tunneled in from behind the cliff face"],"answer":0,"explain":"They cut away the rock beneath their own feet as they went — unfinished tombs still show where work stopped partway down."},{"type":"tf","prompt":"Laser surveys of the jungle around Angkor Wat revealed an entire forgotten city with roads and reservoirs.","answer":0,"explain":"The lasers fired through gaps in the forest canopy, mapping streets nobody had walked on for centuries."}]},{"id":"world-faroff","course":"world","kind":"facts","section":"faroff","title":"Far-Off Kingdoms","glyph":"🐘","n":6,"idea":"Librarians hid books in rice sacks. Then see how the Aztecs farmed on a lake, how the Inca kept records in knots, and how Benin artists cast metal.","pages":[{"icon":"📚","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Timbuktu, in the African country of Mali, was a university city full of knowledge. Families kept hundreds of thousands of handwritten manuscripts on astronomy, medicine, law, and mathematics."},{"t":"fact","text":"In 2012, when armed fighters took over the city, librarians smuggled these precious books out in rice sacks and fishing boats."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How were the manuscripts saved?","options":["Carried out under piles of firewood on wagons","Smuggled out in rice sacks and fishing boats","Locked in a steel vault and flown out by plane"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It gives the method used.","explain":"The librarians smuggled the manuscripts out in rice sacks and fishing boats to keep them safe."}}]},{"icon":"🐫","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The Silk Road was not a road at all, but a shifting web of routes across deserts and mountains."},{"t":"fact","text":"Almost nobody traveled the whole length. Goods, paper, gunpowder, printing, and diseases were handed on town to town."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Did anyone travel the whole Silk Road?","options":["Almost nobody did","Everyone did","Only traders did"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the first sentence in the fact box.","explain":"Very few people traveled the entire length of the Silk Road. Goods and ideas were passed from one town to another along the way."}}]},{"icon":"🏯","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Himeji Castle in Japan is made of wood and white plaster on top of a stone base."},{"t":"fact","text":"Inside, it is a deliberate maze with narrow twisting passages, some dead ends, and steps of different heights, which are said to trip up anyone rushing. An attacker cannot move fast or safely."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why are the steps different heights?","options":["So the builders could save stone on the way up","So the castle looks taller from the outside","So an attacker cannot move fast or safely"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the second sentence in the fact box.","explain":"Steps of different heights make it hard for an attacker to move quickly and safely."}}]},{"icon":"🌽","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The Aztecs farmed on the lake itself by staking out rectangles in the shallows."},{"t":"fact","text":"They filled these plots with mud and reeds until they rose above the water. Willows planted at the corners held the whole thing together with their roots, and several crops a year were harvested."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What held the Aztec garden plots together?","options":["Heavy stones piled up at each corner","Large baskets of clay sunk beneath the plots","The roots of willow trees at the corners"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the second sentence in the fact box.","explain":"The willows planted at the corners kept everything from falling apart."}}]},{"icon":"🧶","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The Inca kept records without writing, using knotted cords called quipu."},{"t":"term","word":"quipu","text":"A set of knotted cords used by the Inca to record information."},{"t":"fact","text":"The color of the cord, the kind of knot, and its position all carried meaning. Numbers can be read from surviving quipu with confidence, but whether they also recorded words is not settled."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What three things carried meaning in a quipu?","options":["The color, the kind of knot, and its position","The sound of the knots, the age of the weaver, and the weather","The smell of the cord, the day of the week, and the weight"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It lists three specific things that carried meaning.","explain":"The color, the kind of knot, and its position all held information in a quipu."}}]},{"icon":"🗿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The bronze plaques and heads made in the old Kingdom of Benin, in what is now Nigeria, started as wax packed inside clay."},{"t":"fact","text":"The wax was melted out, and then metal was poured into the empty space it left behind. Some are cast so thin that they show skill equal to the best European foundries of the time."},{"t":"p","text":"Thousands of these pieces were taken by British soldiers in 1897 and ended up in museums across Europe and America. Many are now being returned."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What was melted out of the clay to make space for the metal?","options":["The clay","The wax","The bronze"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It tells you what was melted out.","explain":"The wax was melted out, leaving a space for the metal to be poured in."}}]},{"icon":"👑","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Rice sacks of books. A web of roads. A maze of wood. Bronze plaques and heads. Wax packed inside clay."},{"t":"p","text":"Each one shows how clever people solved a problem with what they had."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Timbuktu","Kept hundreds of thousands of manuscripts"],["Silk Road","A shifting web of routes"],["Himeji Castle","Made of wood and white plaster on a stone base"],["Aztecs","Farmed on lake plots held by willow roots"],["quipu","Knotted cords used to record information"],["Benin plaques","Made by melting out wax inside clay"]]},"picgame":{"type":"tapall","title":"Inca Records","goal":"Tap what the Inca used instead of writing.","items":[{"icon":"🧶","name":"cord","good":true},{"icon":"🪢","name":"knot","good":true},{"icon":"🎨","name":"colors","good":true},{"icon":"✏️","name":"pencil","good":false},{"icon":"📝","name":"notes","good":false},{"icon":"📖","name":"book","good":false},{"icon":"📜","name":"scroll","good":false},{"icon":"📓","name":"notebook","good":false}]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"How were Timbuktu's hundreds of thousands of manuscripts saved when the city was threatened?","options":["Librarians smuggled them out in rice sacks and fishing boats","They were buried underground for protection","They were copied and sent to European museums"],"answer":0,"explain":"The manuscripts cover astronomy, medicine, law, and mathematics — kept by families for generations."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What was the Silk Road actually like, despite its name suggesting a single path?","options":["A shifting web of routes where goods were handed town to town, with almost nobody traveling the whole length","One well-defined road maintained by multiple empires","A sea route primarily used for trading silk"],"answer":0,"explain":"Paper, gunpowder, printing, and disease all spread this way too — very little silk itself reached the far end."},{"type":"tf","prompt":"The Inca kept detailed numerical records without any system of writing, using knotted cords called quipu.","answer":0,"explain":"The color, knot type, and position on the cord all carried meaning — whether they recorded words too is still not settled."}]},{"id":"world-medicine","course":"world","kind":"facts","section":"medicine","title":"Medicine","glyph":"💊","n":6,"idea":"Surgery used to be done on awake patients. Then see how X-rays, antiseptic spray, insulin, a circular chart, and vaccines changed medicine.","pages":[{"icon":"😴","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Before 1846, surgery was done on people who were awake and held down, so the surgeon had to work as fast as possible."},{"t":"fact","text":"In October 1846, a dentist in Boston gave ether to a patient and the surgeon removed a tumor from his neck while he lay still. The surgeon said afterwards: gentlemen, this is no humbug, which means no trick."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did the dentist give the patient?","options":["Ether","Morphine","Alcohol"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the second sentence on the page. It mentions what the dentist gave the patient.","explain":"The dentist gave ether to the patient so the surgery could be done while the patient was unconscious."}}]},{"icon":"🩻","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"In 1895, Wilhelm Röntgen found a new kind of ray coming from his equipment. He called them X-rays, because X means unknown."},{"t":"term","word":"photographed","text":"To make a picture using light or other rays."},{"t":"fact","text":"He used it to take a picture of his wife's hand. The bones and her wedding ring showed up clearly."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Whose hand did Röntgen photograph?","options":["His own","His wife's","A patient's"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the second sentence on the page. It mentions whose hand was photographed.","explain":"Röntgen photographed his wife’s hand to show how the new rays worked."}}]},{"icon":"🧴","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Joseph Lister read that souring, the way milk and wine go bad, was caused by tiny living things in the air. He wondered if wounds might go bad the same way."},{"t":"fact","text":"He began spraying carbolic acid over the operating table and washing his instruments in it too. Deaths after surgery on his ward fell sharply."},{"t":"p","text":"He published the numbers before and after to show how much safer his methods were."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did Lister spray over the operating table?","options":["Alcohol","Mercury","Carbolic acid"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the second sentence. It mentions what he sprayed over the operating table.","explain":"Lister sprayed carbolic acid over the operating table to keep the living things in the air from causing infections."}}]},{"icon":"💉","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"In 1921, a surgeon and a student spent their summer in a borrowed lab in Toronto."},{"t":"fact","text":"They worked out how to take a substance from the pancreas that lowered blood sugar. It is called insulin. After tests on dogs, a purified version made from cattle pancreas was given to a fourteen-year-old boy. He was dying of diabetes, a sickness in which the body cannot control its blood sugar. He got better."},{"t":"p","text":"Then they sold their patent, the legal right to be the only makers of insulin, to the university for one dollar each."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How much did they sell the patent for?","options":["Ten dollars","Free, no money at all","One dollar each"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the last sentence on the page. It gives the amount.","explain":"They sold the patent for one dollar each so that no company could keep insulin for itself."}}]},{"icon":"📊","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Florence Nightingale's lasting weapon was not the lamp. It was a diagram."},{"t":"fact","text":"She made a circular chart that laid out deaths month by month. It showed that far more soldiers were dying of disease in the hospital than of wounds."},{"t":"p","text":"She put this chart in front of people who could make changes to save lives."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What was Florence Nightingale's lasting weapon?","options":["A diagram, a circular chart","A lamp, a small brass lantern","A song, a hymn sung to soldiers"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It describes what her lasting weapon was.","explain":"Her circular chart showed that more soldiers died of disease than wounds, and it convinced people to make changes."}}]},{"icon":"🛡️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Smallpox is the only human disease that has ever been wiped out."},{"t":"fact","text":"The last natural case was in Somalia in 1977, and it was declared gone in 1980. The method was finding each case fast and vaccinating the ring of people around it."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How was smallpox wiped out?","options":["By giving every person in every country a shot in the same year","By finding each case fast and vaccinating the ring around it","By killing the animals that carry the sickness from town to town"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the second sentence of the fact box. It explains the method used.","explain":"The key was finding each case quickly and vaccinating those nearby to stop the spread."}}]},{"icon":"⚕️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Ether to ease pain. Rays to see bones. Spray to stop infection. Insulin for diabetes. A circular chart. Smallpox wiped out."},{"t":"p","text":"Six ways to help sick people, and each one a step in healing."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Ether","The dentist gave it to the patient"],["Röntgen","Found new rays from equipment"],["Lister","Sprayed carbolic acid over tables"],["Carbolic acid","Washed instruments in it too"],["Florence Nightingale","Used a circular chart"],["Smallpox","Wiped out by fast case finding"]]},"picgame":{"type":"tapall","title":"Germ Fighters","goal":"Tap what helps stop germs from making people sick.","items":[{"icon":"🧴","name":"spray","good":true},{"icon":"🧪","name":"carbolic acid","good":true},{"icon":"💉","name":"vaccine","good":true},{"icon":"🧼","name":"soap","good":true},{"icon":"🍬","name":"candy","good":false},{"icon":"🎈","name":"balloon","good":false},{"icon":"🧸","name":"teddy","good":false},{"icon":"🚗","name":"car","good":false}]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"Before 1846, how was surgery typically performed?","options":["On patients who were awake and held down, as fast as the surgeon could work","With early forms of anesthesia already in common use","Only as a last resort, rarely attempted"],"answer":0,"explain":"That October, a dentist gave a patient ether in Boston, and the surgeon afterward said, 'gentlemen, this is no humbug.'"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What made Florence Nightingale's work during the war so effective at changing military medical policy?","options":["She invented a diagram showing far more soldiers died of disease than of wounds","She personally treated more patients than any other nurse","She wrote detailed letters home describing conditions"],"answer":0,"explain":"Her circular chart laid deaths out month by month, and she put it directly in front of the people who could act on it."},{"type":"tf","prompt":"Smallpox is the only human disease ever completely wiped out worldwide.","answer":0,"explain":"The last natural case was in Somalia in 1977 — it worked because the virus only lives in people, and the rash makes it easy to spot and contain."}]},{"id":"world-modern","course":"world","kind":"facts","section":"modern","title":"The Modern World","glyph":"🌐","n":6,"idea":"The sun appears on both sides of the courtyard due to hours-long exposure. Then follow the Atlantic cable, a radio signal across the ocean, the shipping container, the first mouse, and satellite clocks.","pages":[{"icon":"📷","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The world's first photograph is a view from an upstairs window in France, made on a pewter plate, a flat sheet of soft gray metal."},{"t":"fact","text":"The exposure, the time the plate sat soaking up light, took hours, which is why the sun appears to be shining on both sides of the courtyard at once."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why does the sun seem to shine on both sides in the first photograph?","options":["The camera was broken","The exposure took hours","The photographer used a flash"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It explains why the sun appears on both sides.","explain":"The long exposure time allowed the sun to move across the sky, capturing its light on both sides of the courtyard."}}]},{"icon":"🌊","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The first telegraph cable laid across the Atlantic worked for about three weeks in 1858, sending messages just a few words an hour."},{"t":"term","word":"telegraph","text":"A machine that sends messages along a wire as short and long electric signals."},{"t":"fact","text":"After those three weeks, it died. It took eight more years, a bigger ship called the Great Eastern, and a stronger cable to get a permanent one working."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How long did the first Atlantic telegraph cable work?","options":["Three weeks","Eight years","One day"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the first sentence on the page. It gives the number of weeks.","explain":"The first cable worked for only three weeks before it stopped functioning."}}]},{"icon":"📻","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"In 1901, Marconi claimed to have heard three faint dots — the letter S — sent from Cornwall to Newfoundland."},{"t":"fact","text":"Physicists said it was impossible because radio waves travel in straight lines and the Earth curves. He had no recording and no witnesses outside his own team. Later tests showed that radio waves can bounce off the upper air and travel far around the curve of the Earth."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why did physicists say it was impossible for Marconi to hear a message across the Atlantic?","options":["The radio machines were too weak to send signals that far","Storms over the ocean would drown out any signal sent across it","Radio waves travel in straight lines and the Earth curves"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the second sentence on the page. It explains why physicists thought it was impossible.","explain":"Physicists said radio waves travel in straight lines, so they could not curve around the Earth to reach Newfoundland."}}]},{"icon":"📦","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A steel box of fixed size can go straight from ship to truck to train without being unpacked. That cut the cost of loading a ship from about $6 a ton to about 16 cents a ton."},{"t":"fact","text":"The first container voyage was in 1956, with 58 boxes on a converted tanker.","label":"First voyage"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many boxes were on the first container voyage?","options":["28","58","108"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It gives the number of boxes on the first voyage.","explain":"The first container voyage in 1956 carried exactly 58 boxes."}}]},{"icon":"🖱️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The first computer mouse was not a small plastic shape with a ball underneath."},{"t":"fact","text":"It was a small wooden box with two wheels underneath and a button on top, shown off in 1968. It got its name from the cord coming out of the back."},{"t":"p","text":"The same demonstration also showed windows on a screen, video calls, and clickable links. But nobody could buy any of it for more than ten years."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What was the first computer mouse made of?","options":["A rubber ball","Plastic and metal","A wooden box"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It tells you what the first mouse was made of.","explain":"The first mouse was a wooden box with two wheels and a button."}}]},{"icon":"🛰️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The satellites up in space that tell your phone where you are have clocks inside them."},{"t":"fact","text":"Time runs at a very slightly different rate up there, because the satellites move fast and are far from Earth's pull, so those clocks need to be corrected constantly. Left uncorrected, your position would drift by about 6 miles a day."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What would happen if the satellite clocks were not corrected?","options":["Your position would drift about 6 miles a day","Your location would drift about 20 feet a day","The satellites would fall from the sky within weeks"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It tells you how far your position would drift each day.","explain":"Left uncorrected, the position would drift by about 6 miles a day."}}]},{"icon":"💡","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Hours of light on a plate. A cable under the sea. Three dots heard across an ocean. A steel box. A wooden mouse. Clocks in the satellites."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["pewter plate","The material used for the first photograph"],["telegraph","Sends messages along a wire as electric signals"],["Marconi","Claimed to hear three dots across the Atlantic"],["58 boxes","Number on the first container voyage"],["wooden box","Material of the first computer mouse"],["satellite clocks","Corrected constantly because time runs slightly different up there"]]},"picgame":{"type":"tapall","title":"Container Rides","goal":"Tap what a big steel shipping box rides on.","items":[{"icon":"🚢","name":"ship","good":true},{"icon":"🚚","name":"truck","good":true},{"icon":"🚂","name":"train","good":true},{"icon":"🚲","name":"bike","good":false},{"icon":"🛴","name":"scooter","good":false},{"icon":"🐎","name":"horse","good":false},{"icon":"🛹","name":"skateboard","good":false},{"icon":"🛶","name":"canoe","good":false}]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"When Marconi claimed to receive the first transatlantic radio signal in 1901, why were physicists skeptical?","options":["Radio waves were thought to travel in straight lines, and the curve of the Earth should have blocked the signal","They didn't believe radio waves existed at all","The distance was thought to be too far for any signal"],"answer":0,"explain":"He turned out to be right for a reason nobody knew yet — the waves bounce off a layer high in the atmosphere."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What invention made shipping goods across the ocean dramatically cheaper, starting in 1956?","options":["The standardized steel shipping container","Larger cargo ships with bigger holds","Faster-loading cranes at ports"],"answer":0,"explain":"Loading a ship by hand once cost several dollars a ton — by container it fell to a few cents."},{"type":"tf","prompt":"GPS satellites need their onboard clocks specially corrected, because time actually runs at a slightly different rate in orbit.","answer":0,"explain":"Left uncorrected, your phone's calculated position would drift by about 6 miles a day."}]},{"id":"world-vikings","course":"world","kind":"facts","section":"vikings","title":"Vikings","glyph":"⚓","n":6,"idea":"The Vikings sailed in longships far from home, and some of them even reached North America.","pages":[{"icon":"⚓","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The Vikings sailed far from home."},{"t":"term","word":"voyaged","text":"Went as far as the Mediterranean by ship, like the Vikings."},{"t":"fact","text":"They voyaged as far as the Mediterranean, North Africa, the Middle East, Greenland, and Vinland, in present-day Newfoundland."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which land did the Vikings call Vinland?","options":["Greenland","Newfoundland","North Africa"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. The answer is there.","explain":"They voyaged as far as the Mediterranean, North Africa, the Middle East, Greenland, and Vinland, in present-day Newfoundland."}}]},{"icon":"📅","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The Vikings were the first Europeans to reach North America."},{"t":"fact","text":"They briefly settled in Newfoundland, which they called Vinland."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Who were the first Europeans to reach North America?","options":["The Romans","The Vikings","The Greeks"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"The Vikings were the first Europeans to reach North America."}}]},{"icon":"🏗️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Longships were long, slender ships built for speed."},{"t":"fact","text":"They could carry a large crew of warriors."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What were longships built for?","options":["Storage","Speed","Fishing"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"Longships were long, slender ships built for speed."}}]},{"icon":"🧑","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Leif Erikson bought a ship from Bjarni and gathered a crew of thirty-five men."},{"t":"fact","text":"Then he set off to find the lands Bjarni had described."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many men were in Leif's crew?","options":["Fifteen men","Thirty-five men","Fifty-five men"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"Leif Erikson bought a ship from Bjarni and gathered a crew of thirty-five men."}}]},{"icon":"👤","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Erik the Red, the father of Leif Erikson, founded the first Norse settlement in Greenland."},{"t":"fact","text":"Leif was raised there."}]},{"icon":"🍽️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"After wintering in Vinland, Leif returned to Greenland in the spring."},{"t":"fact","text":"He brought back a cargo of grapes and timber."}]},{"icon":"🔎","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Ships that sailed far. A land called Vinland. Longships built for speed. A crew of thirty-five. A cargo of grapes and timber."},{"t":"p","text":"The Vikings were bold."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Vikings","First Europeans to reach North America"],["Vinland","Where the Vikings briefly settled in Newfoundland"],["Longships","Long slender ships built for speed"],["Leif Erikson","Gathered a crew of thirty-five men"],["Erik the Red","Founded the first Norse settlement in Greenland"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did Leif bring back from Vinland?","options":["Gold and silver","Grapes and timber","Wool and cloth"],"answer":1,"explain":"He brought back a cargo of grapes and timber."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Who founded the first Norse settlement in Greenland?","options":["Leif Erikson","Erik the Red","Bjarni"],"answer":1,"explain":"Erik the Red founded the first Norse settlement in Greenland."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"When did Leif return to Greenland?","options":["In the autumn","In the spring","In midwinter"],"answer":1,"explain":"After wintering in Vinland, Leif returned to Greenland in the spring."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What kind of ships could carry a large crew of warriors?","options":["Rowboats","Longships","Barges"],"answer":1,"explain":"Longships could carry a large crew of warriors."}]},{"id":"world-castles","course":"world","kind":"facts","section":"castles","title":"Castles & Knights","glyph":"🏰","n":6,"idea":"Castles had walls, arrowslits and gates, and knights wore armor made to fit them.","pages":[{"icon":"🏰","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A castle was a fortified building made in the Middle Ages."},{"t":"fact","text":"It was built mostly by nobles or royalty, and by military orders."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Who built most castles?","options":["Farmers and fishers","Nobles and royalty","Monks and merchants"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. The answer is there.","explain":"It was built mostly by nobles or royalty, and by military orders."}}]},{"icon":"🏗️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Castles took many forms in the Middle Ages."},{"t":"fact","text":"Some parts, such as curtain walls, arrowslits and portcullises, were common. A portcullis was a gate that could be lowered."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which castle part was a gate that could be lowered?","options":["A curtain wall","A portcullis","An arrowslit"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. The answer is there.","explain":"A portcullis was a gate that could be lowered."}}]},{"icon":"📅","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Between 1066 and 1087, King William set up 36 castles, such as Warwick Castle."},{"t":"fact","text":"He used them to guard against rebellion in the English Midlands."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many castles did King William set up between 1066 and 1087?","options":["3","36","360"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"Between 1066 and 1087, King William set up 36 castles, such as Warwick Castle."}}]},{"icon":"🧑","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Knights were mounted warriors."},{"t":"term","word":"vassal","text":"A person who served a lord."},{"t":"fact","text":"Often a knight was a vassal who served a lord as an elite fighter or a bodyguard. His payment was land."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How was a knight often paid?","options":["With gold coins","With land","With fine horses"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. The answer is there.","explain":"His payment was land."}}]},{"icon":"⏳","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Plate armor was developed in Europe."},{"t":"fact","text":"By about 1420, complete suits of plate armor had been developed."}]},{"icon":"🧱","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Armorers fitted armor to the person who would wear it, like a tailor."},{"t":"fact","text":"They made parts that needed to bend from smaller individual plates called lames."}]},{"icon":"🔎","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A fortified building. A gate that could be lowered. Castles for a king. A knight paid with land. Plate armor made by armorers."},{"t":"p","text":"Castles and knights."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Portcullis","A gate that could be lowered"],["Knight","A mounted warrior who served a lord"],["King William","Set up 36 castles"],["Armorers","Fitted armor to the person who would wear it"],["Lames","Smaller individual plates of armor"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did King William use his castles to guard against?","options":["Bad weather","Rebellion","Heavy floods"],"answer":1,"explain":"He used them to guard against rebellion in the English Midlands."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What were the smaller individual plates of armor called?","options":["Helms","Lames","Greaves"],"answer":1,"explain":"They made parts that needed to bend from smaller individual plates called lames."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did armorers fit armor to?","options":["The horse that would carry it","The person who would wear it","The castle that would hold it"],"answer":1,"explain":"Armorers fitted armor to the person who would wear it, like a tailor."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What was a knight, often, to a lord?","options":["A farmer","A vassal","A baker"],"answer":1,"explain":"Often a knight was a vassal who served a lord."}]},{"id":"world-silkroad","course":"world","kind":"facts","section":"silkroad","title":"The Silk Road","glyph":"🐫","n":6,"idea":"The Silk Road got its name from trade in silk. Meet Marco Polo, and a camel that can drink up to 53 gallons in a day.","pages":[{"icon":"🐫","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The Silk Road got its name from the valuable trade in silk."},{"t":"term","word":"silk","text":"A valuable cloth that was first developed in China."},{"t":"fact","text":"Silk was first developed in China."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What gave the Silk Road its name?","options":["The color of the road","The trade in silk","The shape of the mountains"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"The Silk Road got its name from the valuable trade in silk."}}]},{"icon":"🧑","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The name Silk Road was made popular in 1877 by Ferdinand von Richthofen."},{"t":"fact","text":"He made seven expeditions to China from 1868 to 1872."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Who made the name Silk Road popular in 1877?","options":["Marco Polo of Venice","Ferdinand von Richthofen","Leif Erikson of Greenland"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"The name Silk Road was made popular in 1877 by Ferdinand von Richthofen."}}]},{"icon":"👤","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Marco Polo traveled widely inside China."},{"t":"fact","text":"He lived in the emperor's lands for 17 years and saw many things that Europeans had never known."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many years did Marco Polo live in the emperor's lands?","options":["7 years","17 years","70 years"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. The answer is there.","explain":"He lived in the emperor's lands for 17 years and saw many things that Europeans had never known."}}]},{"icon":"👑","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"In 1269, Niccolò and Maffeo Polo came home to Venice and met young Marco for the first time."},{"t":"fact","text":"By then Marco was 21 years old."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How old was Marco Polo when he met Niccolò and Maffeo for the first time?","options":["12 years old","21 years old","31 years old"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. The answer is there.","explain":"By then Marco was 21 years old."}}]},{"icon":"📏","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The Bactrian camel is the largest mammal in its native range."},{"t":"fact","text":"At the top of the humps, its average height is 6.99 ft (213 cm)."}]},{"icon":"👀","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A thirsty camel can drink in a hot, dry season."},{"t":"fact","text":"It can drink up to 53 US gallons (200 L) of water in one day."}]},{"icon":"🔎","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A name from silk. A man who made it popular. Marco Polo in China. A camel with humps. A thirsty camel."},{"t":"p","text":"The Silk Road was long."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Silk Road","Named for the trade in silk"],["Richthofen","Made the name Silk Road popular"],["Marco Polo","Lived 17 years in the emperor's lands"],["Bactrian camel","The largest mammal in its native range"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"In what year did Ferdinand von Richthofen make the name Silk Road popular?","options":["1787","1877","1778"],"answer":1,"explain":"The name Silk Road was made popular in 1877 by Ferdinand von Richthofen."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How tall is a Bactrian camel at the top of the humps, on average?","options":["About 2 ft","6.99 ft (213 cm)","Nearly 12 feet"],"answer":1,"explain":"At the top of the humps, its average height is 6.99 ft (213 cm)."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How much water can a thirsty camel drink in one day?","options":["Only about 5 gallons","Up to 53 US gallons (200 L)","Over 530 gallons in total"],"answer":1,"explain":"It can drink up to 53 US gallons (200 L) of water in one day."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many expeditions did Richthofen make to China from 1868 to 1872?","options":["Three","Seven","Twelve"],"answer":1,"explain":"He made seven expeditions to China from 1868 to 1872."}]},{"id":"world-china","course":"world","kind":"facts","section":"china","title":"Ancient China","glyph":"🐉","n":6,"idea":"The Great Wall, the Terracotta Army and the first paper money.","pages":[{"icon":"🐉","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The Great Wall of China is a series of fortifications in China."},{"t":"term","word":"fortifications","text":"A series of walls and towers built to protect, like the Great Wall."},{"t":"fact","text":"They were built across the northern borders of ancient Chinese states, to protect against nomadic groups."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What was the Great Wall built to protect against?","options":["Rising floods","Nomadic groups","Strong winds"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. The answer is there.","explain":"They were built across the northern borders of ancient Chinese states, to protect against nomadic groups."}}]},{"icon":"🏗️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The Great Wall had watchtowers, troop barracks and garrison stations."},{"t":"fact","text":"It could send signals with smoke or fire."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did the Great Wall use to send signals?","options":["Flags or drums","Smoke or fire","Birds or mirrors"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. The answer is there.","explain":"It could send signals with smoke or fire."}}]},{"icon":"📏","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The three pits of the Terracotta Army hold more than 8,000 soldiers."},{"t":"fact","text":"There are also 130 chariots with 520 horses, and 150 cavalry horses."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many soldiers do the pits of the Terracotta Army hold?","options":["Fewer than 800","More than 8,000","About 80,000"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"The three pits of the Terracotta Army hold more than 8,000 soldiers."}}]},{"icon":"🏺","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The tallest figures in the Terracotta Army are the generals."},{"t":"fact","text":"The figures vary in height according to their rank."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which figures in the Terracotta Army are the tallest?","options":["The soldiers","The generals","The horses"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"The tallest figures in the Terracotta Army are the generals."}}]},{"icon":"📅","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the Song dynasty."},{"t":"fact","text":"It was the first government to issue paper currency."}]},{"icon":"🧱","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A Song dynasty book describes a magnetic navigation device."},{"t":"fact","text":"It was an iron south-pointing fish floating in a bowl of water."}]},{"icon":"🔎","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A long wall. Smoke signals. Soldiers in pits. Tall generals. Paper money. A fish in water."},{"t":"p","text":"China had big ideas."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Great Wall","A series of fortifications in China"],["Terracotta Army","More than 8,000 soldiers in three pits"],["Generals","The tallest Terracotta Army figures"],["Song dynasty","First government to issue paper currency"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many chariots are there at the Terracotta Army?","options":["13","130","1,300"],"answer":1,"explain":"There are also 130 chariots with 520 horses, and 150 cavalry horses."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did the Song dynasty issue first?","options":["Silver coins","Paper currency","Bronze bells"],"answer":1,"explain":"It was the first government to issue paper currency."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What was the south-pointing fish made of?","options":["Wood","Iron","Clay"],"answer":1,"explain":"It was an iron south-pointing fish floating in a bowl of water."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did the Great Wall have?","options":["Windmills","Watchtowers","Lighthouses"],"answer":1,"explain":"The Great Wall had watchtowers, troop barracks and garrison stations."}]},{"id":"world-renaissance","course":"world","kind":"facts","section":"renaissance","title":"The Renaissance","glyph":"🎨","n":6,"idea":"An artist who filled notebooks, a sculptor, and a man who built a telescope.","pages":[{"icon":"🎨","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Leonardo da Vinci was a painter."},{"t":"term","word":"notebooks","text":"Books where Leonardo drew and wrote about what he saw."},{"t":"fact","text":"He also kept notebooks. In them he drew and wrote about the body, the stars, plants and maps."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is Leonardo da Vinci known for besides painting?","options":["His sculptures","His notebooks","His violins"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. The answer is there.","explain":"He also kept notebooks."}}]},{"icon":"🏺","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Leonardo painted the Mona Lisa."},{"t":"fact","text":"It is regarded as the world's most famous painting. His Last Supper is the most reproduced religious painting."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is the world's most famous painting?","options":["The Last Supper","The Mona Lisa","The Sistine Ceiling"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"Leonardo painted the Mona Lisa."}}]},{"icon":"🧑","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Leonardo became an apprentice by the age of 17."},{"t":"fact","text":"He remained in training for seven years."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"At what age did Leonardo become an apprentice?","options":["Age 7","Age 17","Age 27"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"Leonardo became an apprentice by the age of 17."}}]},{"icon":"👤","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Michelangelo was a sculptor."},{"t":"fact","text":"Two of his best-known works, the Pietà and David, were sculpted before he was 30."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which two famous sculptures did Michelangelo make before he was 30?","options":["The Mona Lisa and Last Supper","The Pietà and David","The telescope and the notebooks"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. The answer is there.","explain":"Two of his best-known works, the Pietà and David, were sculpted before he was 30."}}]},{"icon":"🪙","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Michelangelo did not think of himself as a painter."},{"t":"fact","text":"Still, he painted scenes from Genesis on the ceiling of the Sistine Chapel in Rome, and The Last Judgment on its altar wall."}]},{"icon":"👑","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Galileo built a telescope."},{"t":"fact","text":"He saw the stars of the Milky Way, the phases of Venus and the four largest moons of Jupiter. He also saw Saturn's rings and sunspots."}]},{"icon":"🔎","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Notebooks full of drawings. The Mona Lisa. An apprentice of 17. The Pietà and David. A ceiling in Rome. A telescope."},{"t":"p","text":"Artists and thinkers."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Leonardo","Painted the Mona Lisa"],["Michelangelo","Sculpted the Pietà and David"],["Galileo","Built a telescope"],["Apprentice","Leonardo was one by the age of 17"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did Galileo build?","options":["A printing press","A telescope","A sailing ship"],"answer":1,"explain":"Galileo built a telescope."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where is the Sistine Chapel?","options":["In Paris","In Rome","In Venice"],"answer":1,"explain":"He painted scenes from Genesis on the ceiling of the Sistine Chapel in Rome."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did Michelangelo paint on the altar wall?","options":["The Mona Lisa and more","The Last Judgment","A scene of the Milky Way"],"answer":1,"explain":"He painted The Last Judgment on the altar wall."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"For how many years did Leonardo remain in training?","options":["Two years","Seven years","Seventeen years"],"answer":1,"explain":"He remained in training for seven years."}]},{"id":"world-factories","course":"world","kind":"facts","section":"factories","title":"Factories & Steam","glyph":"🏭","n":6,"idea":"Machines and steam engines changed how things were made, starting in Great Britain around 1760.","pages":[{"icon":"🏭","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The Industrial Revolution began in Great Britain around 1760."},{"t":"term","word":"revolution","text":"A time of big change, like the Industrial Revolution that began in Great Britain."},{"t":"fact","text":"By about 1840 it had spread to Europe and the United States."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where did the Industrial Revolution begin?","options":["The United States","Great Britain","Northern Europe"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"The Industrial Revolution began in Great Britain around 1760."}}]},{"icon":"📅","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The textile industry was the first to use modern production methods."},{"t":"fact","text":"Textiles became the main industry in jobs, in value of output and in money invested."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which industry was the first to use modern production methods?","options":["The shipping industry","The textile industry","The farming industry"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"The textile industry was the first to use modern production methods."}}]},{"icon":"📏","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Mechanized cotton spinning was powered by water and later by steam."},{"t":"fact","text":"It increased output per worker by a factor of around 500."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"By about how much did cotton spinning machines increase output per worker?","options":["Only twice as much","A factor of around 500","Nearly a million times"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. The answer is there.","explain":"It increased output per worker by a factor of around 500."}}]},{"icon":"⏳","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"In 1712, Thomas Newcomen built an engine that sold well."},{"t":"fact","text":"It could give steady power to a machine."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Who built an engine that sold well in 1712?","options":["Richard Arkwright","Thomas Newcomen","George Stephenson"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"In 1712, Thomas Newcomen built an engine that sold well."}}]},{"icon":"🧑","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"James Watt saw that engines of his day wasted a lot of energy."},{"t":"fact","text":"They cooled and reheated the cylinder again and again. His separate condenser avoided this waste."}]},{"icon":"👤","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"James Watt developed the idea of horsepower."},{"t":"fact","text":"The unit of power called the watt is named after him."}]},{"icon":"🔎","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A revolution that began in Great Britain. Textiles and cotton spinning. An engine that sold well. A condenser that saved energy. Horsepower and the watt."},{"t":"p","text":"Machines changed work."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Great Britain","Where the Industrial Revolution began"],["Textiles","The first industry to use modern methods"],["Newcomen","Built an engine that sold well"],["James Watt","Developed the idea of horsepower"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What powered mechanized cotton spinning?","options":["Wind and later oil","Water and later steam","Horses and later wind"],"answer":1,"explain":"Mechanized cotton spinning was powered by water and later by steam."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What unit of power is named after James Watt?","options":["The pound","The watt","The volt"],"answer":1,"explain":"The unit of power called the watt is named after him."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did Watt's separate condenser avoid?","options":["Noise","Waste","Smoke"],"answer":1,"explain":"His separate condenser avoided this waste."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"In what year did Thomas Newcomen build an engine that sold well?","options":["1721","1712","1172"],"answer":1,"explain":"In 1712, Thomas Newcomen built an engine that sold well."}]},{"id":"world-africa","course":"world","kind":"facts","section":"africa","title":"Kingdoms of Africa","glyph":"🌍","n":6,"idea":"Gold, a king's journey to Mecca, a rich city of books, and a great stone wall in Africa.","pages":[{"icon":"🌍","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"At its peak, Mali was the largest empire in West Africa."},{"t":"term","word":"empire","text":"A large area with one ruler, like the largest empire in West Africa."},{"t":"fact","text":"It spread its language, laws and customs across the region."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What was the largest empire in West Africa at its peak?","options":["Egypt","Mali","Persia"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"At its peak, Mali was the largest empire in West Africa."}}]},{"icon":"🏆","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"About two thirds of the gold that moved around the Mediterranean in medieval times came from West Africa."},{"t":"fact","text":"That was almost half of the Old World's gold."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"About how much of the Mediterranean's gold came from West Africa?","options":["About one tenth","About two thirds","Almost all of it"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"About two thirds of the gold that moved around the Mediterranean in medieval times came from West Africa."}}]},{"icon":"🧑","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Mansa Musa made a famous pilgrimage to Mecca from 1324 to 1326."},{"t":"fact","text":"He gave away so much gold that prices went up in Egypt."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where did Mansa Musa make a famous pilgrimage?","options":["Rome","Mecca","Athens"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"Mansa Musa made a famous pilgrimage to Mecca from 1324 to 1326."}}]},{"icon":"📅","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"After the visit by Mansa Musa around 1325, Timbuktu flourished."},{"t":"fact","text":"Its trade in salt, gold and ivory made it rich."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did Timbuktu trade in?","options":["Wool, silk and cotton","Salt, gold and ivory","Tea, sugar and rice"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. The answer is there.","explain":"Its trade in salt, gold and ivory made it rich."}}]},{"icon":"🏗️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"In its golden age, Timbuktu had a big book trade."},{"t":"fact","text":"It also had a university, the Sankoré Madrasah."}]},{"icon":"📏","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The Great Enclosure at Great Zimbabwe has walls as high as 36 ft (11 m)."},{"t":"fact","text":"They extend about 820 ft (250 m)."}]},{"icon":"🔎","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"An empire in West Africa. Gold from the south. A pilgrimage to Mecca. Timbuktu with books and salt. A wall at Great Zimbabwe."},{"t":"p","text":"Africa had rich kingdoms."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Mali","Largest empire in West Africa"],["Mansa Musa","Made a famous pilgrimage to Mecca"],["Timbuktu","Traded in salt, gold and ivory"],["Great Zimbabwe","Has walls as high as 36 ft"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did Mansa Musa give away so much of that prices went up in Egypt?","options":["Salt","Gold","Ivory"],"answer":1,"explain":"He gave away so much gold that prices went up in Egypt."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What was the name of the university in Timbuktu?","options":["The Great Enclosure","The Sankoré Madrasah","The Old World School"],"answer":1,"explain":"It also had a university, the Sankoré Madrasah."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How high are the walls of the Great Enclosure at Great Zimbabwe?","options":["Only about 3 ft (1 m) tall","As high as 36 ft (11 m)","Close to 360 ft (110 m)"],"answer":1,"explain":"The Great Enclosure at Great Zimbabwe has walls as high as 36 ft (11 m)."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did Mali spread across the region?","options":["Its ships, bridges and roads","Its language, laws and customs","Its gold, salt and ivory"],"answer":1,"explain":"It spread its language, laws and customs across the region."}]},{"id":"economics-needs","course":"economics","kind":"facts","section":"needs","title":"Needs & Wants","glyph":"🍞","n":6,"idea":"A person needs water but only wants cake. Learn what a need is, what a want is, and what economics studies.","pages":[{"icon":"🍞","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A need is something a living thing must have to be able to live."},{"t":"fact","text":"Plants and animals have needs too.","label":"Not only people"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What do we call something a living thing must have to be able to live?","options":["A want","A game","A need"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. It says what a need is.","explain":"A need is something a living thing must have to be able to live."}}]},{"icon":"🧥","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"People need water, food, clothing and shelter to live."},{"t":"fact","text":"Every person has the same basic needs for the body.","label":"The same for everyone"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What four things do people need to live?","options":["Water, food, clothing and shelter","Toys, games, books and music","Cake, candy, juice and cookies"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. It lists four things people need.","explain":"People need water, food, clothing and shelter to live."}}]},{"icon":"💧","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The need for water is the most important, because a person will die very quickly without drinking water."},{"t":"fact","text":"In a warm place, food is the next most important need.","label":"Next in line"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Without which need will a person die very quickly?","options":["Food","Water","Clothing"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. It says what a person cannot go without for long.","explain":"A person will die very quickly without drinking water."}}]},{"icon":"🎂","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A want is a wish or a desire for something."},{"t":"term","word":"want","text":"A wish for something that a person can live without."},{"t":"fact","text":"A person could choose not to have a want, and still be able to live.","label":"Living without it"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What do we call something a person wishes for but can live without?","options":["A need","A rule","A want"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. It says what a want is.","explain":"A want is a wish for something that a person can live without."}}]},{"icon":"🍰","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A person can need to eat food and can want to eat cake."},{"t":"fact","text":"If there is no cake, the person may eat bread. The person is no longer hungry, but the want for cake may still be there.","label":"No cake?"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"A person can need to eat food and can want to eat cake. Which one is the need?","options":["To eat food","To eat cake","To eat candy"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. It says which one a person needs.","explain":"A person needs to eat food, but only wants to eat cake."}}]},{"icon":"🧠","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Economics is the study of how people make choices to get what they want."},{"t":"fact","text":"Economics has been called the study of scarcity and choice. Scarcity is when there is not a lot of something.","label":"Scarcity and choice"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does economics study?","options":["How rocks are formed","How people make choices","How plants grow"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. It says what economics studies.","explain":"Economics is the study of how people make choices to get what they want."}}]},{"icon":"🔎","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A need to live. Water, food, clothing and shelter. A want you can live without. Cake or bread. Choices."},{"t":"p","text":"Needs come first."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Need","Something a living thing must have to live"],["Want","A wish a person can live without"],["Water","The most important of all the needs"],["Economics","The study of how people make choices"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which of these does a person need to live?","options":["Water","Cake","A new toy"],"answer":0,"explain":"People have bodily needs of water, food, clothing and shelter to live."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What do plants and animals have, just like people?","options":["Wants","Needs","Money"],"answer":1,"explain":"A need is something that a living being, plant or animal must have to be able to live."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Can a person live without something that is only a want?","options":["No","Only in winter","Yes"],"answer":2,"explain":"A want is something a person is able to live without."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why is water the most important need?","options":["A person dies very quickly without it","It is the easiest thing to carry around","It is always sold in the biggest stores"],"answer":0,"explain":"A person will die very quickly without drinking water."}]},{"id":"economics-choices","course":"economics","kind":"facts","section":"choices","title":"Scarcity & Choices","glyph":"⚖️","n":6,"idea":"There is never enough of everything, so every choice gives something up. Learn what scarcity and opportunity cost mean.","pages":[{"icon":"📉","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Scarcity is when there is not a lot of something."},{"t":"fact","text":"Drought, floods, storms and fire can make things scarce.","label":"Things that cause it"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is scarcity?","options":["Having far more than anyone needs","Getting something for free","When there is not a lot of something"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. It says what scarcity is.","explain":"Scarcity is when there is not a lot of something."}}]},{"icon":"🔁","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Scarcity happens because people always want something new, but resources are limited."},{"t":"fact","text":"There are never enough resources for every want and need.","label":"Never enough"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why does scarcity happen?","options":["Resources are limited","Nobody wants anything","Everything is free"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. It says why scarcity happens.","explain":"Scarcity happens because people always want something new, but resources are limited."}}]},{"icon":"⚠️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Scarcity is called the basic problem of economics. It never goes away."},{"t":"fact","text":"Economics is about the choices people make.","label":"What follows"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is scarcity called, because it never goes away?","options":["The easiest rule of money","The basic problem of economics","The last page of a book"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. It says what scarcity is called.","explain":"Scarcity is called the basic problem of economics, because it never goes away."}}]},{"icon":"🥇","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Scarce things have higher prices. Gold costs much more than iron, because gold is more scarce."},{"t":"term","word":"scarce","text":"Not much of it is around, so it costs more, like gold."},{"t":"fact","text":"Gold is used less than iron, but the price of gold is much higher.","label":"Gold and iron"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why does gold cost much more than iron?","options":["Gold is easier to find","Gold is cheaper to make","Gold is more scarce"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. It says why gold costs more.","explain":"Gold costs much more than iron, because gold is more scarce."}}]},{"icon":"⚖️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Opportunity cost is the value of the next best thing you give up whenever you make a decision."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is opportunity cost?","options":["The next best thing you give up","The price tag on something you buy","The money you get as a birthday gift"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. It says what opportunity cost is.","explain":"Opportunity cost is the value of the next best thing you give up whenever you make a decision."}}]},{"icon":"⏳","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Going to work means more income but less free time. Staying at home means more free time but less income."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does a person give up by going to work instead of staying at home?","options":["A place to live","Free time","Their income"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. It says what a person has less of at work.","explain":"Going to work means more income but less free time."}}]},{"icon":"🔎","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Not a lot of something. Limited resources. A problem that never goes away. Gold and iron. The next best thing. Free time."},{"t":"p","text":"Every choice gives something up."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Scarcity","When there is not a lot of something"],["Gold","Costs more than iron because it is scarce"],["Opportunity cost","The next best thing you give up"],["Free time","What a person gives up by going to work"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which of these can make things scarce?","options":["A drought","A birthday","A song"],"answer":0,"explain":"Scarcity exists due to the effects of nature, such as drought, floods, storms and fire."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which costs more because it is more scarce?","options":["Iron","Air","Gold"],"answer":2,"explain":"Gold is used less than iron, but the price of gold is much higher, because gold is more scarce."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does staying at home give a person, compared with going to work?","options":["More income","More free time","A new job"],"answer":1,"explain":"Staying at home means more free time but less income."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is economics about, when there is never enough?","options":["The choices people make","The names of the planets","The shapes of the clouds"],"answer":0,"explain":"Economics is about the choices people make."}]},{"id":"economics-goods","course":"economics","kind":"facts","section":"goods","title":"Goods & Services","glyph":"📦","n":6,"idea":"Some things you can hold and some things people do for you. Meet goods, services, producers and consumers.","pages":[{"icon":"📦","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A good is a physical thing that can be handed to a buyer."},{"t":"term","word":"service","text":"An action that helps someone, not a thing."},{"t":"fact","text":"A service is not a thing. It is an action that helps someone.","label":"What about services?"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is a service?","options":["A thing that people can hold and carry","A place where people go to shop","An action that helps someone"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the box. It says what a service is.","explain":"A service is not a thing. It is an action that helps someone."}}]},{"icon":"🚲","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A bicycle is a good. The work of a doctor is a service."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Is the work of a doctor a good or a service?","options":["A good","A service","Neither one"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. It says what a doctor's work is.","explain":"The work of a doctor is a service."}}]},{"icon":"🧑‍🏭","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"People who make things are called producers. People who buy things are called consumers."},{"t":"fact","text":"Everyone buys things, so everyone is a consumer.","label":"Everyone"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What are people who make things called?","options":["Producers","Consumers","Teachers"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. It names the people who make things.","explain":"People who make things are called producers."}}]},{"icon":"🛒","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Without people who want to buy, producers have no reason to make things."},{"t":"fact","text":"A producer makes things mainly to sell them to consumers.","label":"Why producers make things"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Who do producers mainly make things to sell to?","options":["Other producers","Their own families","Consumers"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the box. It says who producers sell things to.","explain":"A producer makes things mainly to sell them to consumers."}}]},{"icon":"🧰","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Making things takes four kinds of help."},{"t":"fact","text":"Labor (work), land, capital and enterprise. Capital means machines and buildings. Enterprise means people who make the decisions.","label":"The four kinds"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does capital mean when it comes to making things?","options":["Cash in a wallet","Machines and buildings","The people who work"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. It says what capital means.","explain":"Capital means machines and buildings."}}]},{"icon":"🏗️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Capital goods are goods that are needed to produce other goods."},{"t":"fact","text":"Buildings, equipment and machines are capital goods.","label":"Examples"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What are capital goods needed for?","options":["To produce other goods","To pay the taxes","To feed the animals"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. It says what capital goods are needed for.","explain":"Capital goods are goods that are needed to produce other goods."}}]},{"icon":"🔎","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A good you can hand over. A service that helps. Producers and consumers. Labor, land and capital. Machines and buildings."},{"t":"p","text":"Goods and services are made and bought."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Good","A physical thing that can be handed to a buyer"],["Service","An action that helps someone"],["Producer","A person who makes things"],["Consumer","A person who buys things"],["Capital","Machines and buildings used to make things"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which of these is a good?","options":["The work of a doctor","A haircut","A bicycle"],"answer":2,"explain":"A bicycle is a good."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which of these is a service?","options":["A bicycle","The work of a doctor","A house"],"answer":1,"explain":"The work of a doctor is a service."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Who is a consumer?","options":["Everyone who buys things","Only rich people","Only people who make things"],"answer":0,"explain":"Everyone buys things, so everyone is a consumer."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which of these is capital?","options":["A loaf of bread","A machine in a factory","A haircut"],"answer":1,"explain":"Capital means things like machines and buildings."}]},{"id":"economics-money","course":"economics","kind":"facts","section":"money","title":"What Money Is","glyph":"🪙","n":6,"idea":"Before there was money, people traded cows for wheat. Find out why that got tricky and what money fixed.","pages":[{"icon":"🪙","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Money is anything that people use to buy goods and services."},{"t":"fact","text":"There are lots of different kinds of money in the world. Most countries have their own kind of money.","label":"Many kinds"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is money?","options":["Something that only kings may own","Anything that people use to buy things","A thing that grows on a tree"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. It says what money is.","explain":"Money is anything that people use to buy goods and services."}}]},{"icon":"🥚","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Barter means trading things directly. It works when each person wants what the other person has."},{"t":"term","word":"barter","text":"Trading things directly, without using money."},{"t":"fact","text":"Bob needs shoes and John has shoes, but Bob has eggs and John needs milk. Money fixes that, because anything can be traded for a set amount of money.","label":"A problem"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What fixes the problem when two people do not want each other's things?","options":["Money","Barter","A bigger farm"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the box. It says what fixes the problem.","explain":"Money fixes that, because anything can be traded for a set amount of money."}}]},{"icon":"🐄","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Money is easier to divide than cows."},{"t":"fact","text":"A person who wants only half a cow's worth of wheat does not want to cut a cow in half.","label":"Half a cow?"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why is money easier to use than a cow?","options":["It can give milk and cream","It eats a lot less grass","It is easier to divide"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. It says why money is easier to use than a cow.","explain":"Money is easier to divide than cows."}}]},{"icon":"⏳","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Cows die and wheat rots, but money lasts longer than most trade goods."},{"t":"fact","text":"A person who sells a cow for money can save the money until it is needed.","label":"Saving it"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What lasts longer than cows and wheat?","options":["Money","Fresh eggs","Fresh milk"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. It says what lasts longer than cows and wheat.","explain":"Money lasts longer than cows and wheat."}}]},{"icon":"🦁","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The first metal coins were made in a country called Lydia, in the western part of what is now Turkey."},{"t":"fact","text":"The coins were stamped with a picture of a lion.","label":"A lion"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What picture was stamped on the first Lydian coins?","options":["A king","A ship","A lion"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the box. It says what picture was on the coins.","explain":"The coins were stamped with a picture of a lion."}}]},{"icon":"📜","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Carrying a lot of silver or gold was hard and dangerous, so banknotes were made."},{"t":"fact","text":"A banknote was a promise to give an amount of precious metal to anyone who brought the paper.","label":"A promise"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did the first banknotes promise?","options":["A free meal every single day","An amount of precious metal","A house in the middle of town"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. It says what a banknote promised.","explain":"A banknote was a promise to give an amount of precious metal to anyone who brought the paper."}}]},{"icon":"🔎","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Money for goods and services. Eggs, milk and shoes. A cow cut in half. A coin with a lion. A paper promise."},{"t":"p","text":"Money lasts."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Money","Anything people use to buy goods and services"],["Barter","Trading things without using money"],["Lydia","Where the first metal coins were made"],["Banknote","A paper promise to give precious metal"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What do people not use when they barter?","options":["Money","Goods","Services"],"answer":0,"explain":"Barter is trading goods or services directly, without using money."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where were the first metal coins made?","options":["Egypt","Lydia","Rome"],"answer":1,"explain":"The first country in the world to make metal coins was called Lydia."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Money is easier to divide than what?","options":["A coin","A banknote","A cow"],"answer":2,"explain":"Money is easier to divide than cows."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why did people start to use banknotes?","options":["Heavy metal was hard to carry","Paper was more beautiful","Coins were against the law"],"answer":0,"explain":"Carrying around a lot of precious metal was hard and dangerous, so banknotes were made."}]},{"id":"economics-trade","course":"economics","kind":"facts","section":"trade","title":"Trading & Markets","glyph":"🤝","n":6,"idea":"Every trade works because both people think they come out ahead. Learn about markets and about trading between countries.","pages":[{"icon":"🤝","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"To trade is to willingly give things or services and get other things or services in return."},{"t":"fact","text":"Exchange and swap mean the same thing.","label":"Other names"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What do we call it when people willingly give things and get other things in return?","options":["A gift","A trade","A loan"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. It says what a trade is.","explain":"To trade is to willingly give things or services and get other things or services in return."}}]},{"icon":"🏪","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A place where trading takes place is called a market."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is a place where trading takes place called?","options":["A market","A garden","A library"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. It names the place where trading takes place.","explain":"A place where trading takes place is called a market."}}]},{"icon":"💵","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"When money is part of a trade, the person who gives the money is buying, and the person who gets the money is selling."},{"t":"fact","text":"When there is no money in a trade, the trade is called barter.","label":"Without money"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"In a trade with money, what is the person who gets the money doing?","options":["Buying","Borrowing","Selling"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. It says what the person who gets the money is doing.","explain":"The person who gets the money is selling."}}]},{"icon":"🚢","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"International trade is trade between countries."},{"t":"term","word":"exports","text":"Goods that a business sells to another country."},{"t":"fact","text":"A business in one country can sell goods to other countries (exports) or buy goods from them (imports).","label":"Imports and exports"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What are goods called that a business sells to another country?","options":["Imports","Exports","Markets"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. It names the goods a business sells to another country.","explain":"A business in one country can sell goods to other countries. Those goods are called exports."}}]},{"icon":"🏷️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Governments sometimes take some of the money or goods in a trade between countries."},{"t":"fact","text":"This kind of tax is called a tariff.","label":"What it is called"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is a tax on trade between countries called?","options":["A market","A wage","A tariff"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the box. It names the tax.","explain":"A tax on trade between countries is called a tariff."}}]},{"icon":"📦","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A shipping container is a large steel box."},{"t":"fact","text":"A shipping container can go from a ship to a train to a truck without anyone unloading and reloading the cargo.","label":"One box, many rides"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"A shipping container can ride on ships, and on what two other kinds of transport?","options":["Trains and trucks","Planes and balloons","Bikes and horses"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the box. It says what a container can go from a ship to.","explain":"A shipping container can go from a ship to a train to a truck without being unloaded."}}]},{"icon":"🔎","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A trade. A market. Buying and selling. Exports and imports. A tariff. A box on a ship, a train and a truck."},{"t":"p","text":"Every trade has two sides."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Trade","Giving things and getting things in return"],["Market","A place where trading takes place"],["Export","Goods a business sells to another country"],["Import","Goods a business buys from another country"],["Tariff","A tax on trade between countries"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is it called when a business buys goods from another country?","options":["Exporting","Importing","Bartering"],"answer":1,"explain":"A business in one country can buy goods from other countries, which are called imports."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"When no money is part of a trade, what is the trade called?","options":["A purchase","A tariff","Barter"],"answer":2,"explain":"When there is no money in a trade, the trade is called barter."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Who gives the money in a trade that uses money?","options":["The buyer","The seller","The market"],"answer":0,"explain":"The person who gives the money is buying."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which of these can a shipping container travel on without being unloaded?","options":["A bike, a kite and a boat","A ship, a train and a truck","A horse, a cart and a sled"],"answer":1,"explain":"A shipping container can go from a ship to a train to a truck without anyone unloading and reloading the cargo."}]},{"id":"economics-work","course":"economics","kind":"facts","section":"work","title":"Jobs & Pay","glyph":"🛠️","n":6,"idea":"People trade their time and skills for pay. See what a wage is, and why sharing the work makes more.","pages":[{"icon":"🛠️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A job is work that a person does to earn money."},{"t":"fact","text":"A job can also mean some work that has to be done, like washing up or mending things that are broken.","label":"Jobs at home"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What do we call work that a person does to earn money?","options":["A job","A hobby","A holiday"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. It says what a job is.","explain":"A job is work that a person does to earn money."}}]},{"icon":"🚒","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A teacher's job is to teach. A taxi driver's job is to drive people. A firefighter rescues people from burning buildings and puts out fires."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does a firefighter do for people?","options":["Teaches them to read and write","Drives them around in a taxi","Rescues them from burning buildings"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. It says what a firefighter does.","explain":"A firefighter rescues people from burning buildings and puts out fires."}}]},{"icon":"💰","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A wage is money that is paid by an employer to an employee in exchange for work."},{"t":"term","word":"wage","text":"Money that an employer pays an employee for work."},{"t":"fact","text":"A salary is an arranged amount that an employer pays at regular times, such as every week or month.","label":"Another kind of pay"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is a wage?","options":["Cash that a person finds on the street","Money paid by an employer for work","Coins that a bank keeps safe for you"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. It says what a wage is.","explain":"A wage is money that is paid by an employer to an employee in exchange for work."}}]},{"icon":"⏰","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"In many countries, laws set a minimum wage that all employers must pay their workers."},{"t":"fact","text":"Wages are usually paid by the hour, which is called an hourly wage.","label":"Paid by the hour"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What do we call the lowest wage that a law says employers must pay?","options":["A tip from a customer","A gift from the boss","The minimum wage"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. It names the lowest wage a law allows.","explain":"In many countries, laws set a minimum wage that all employers must pay their workers."}}]},{"icon":"👞","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Xenophon wrote about large cities, where a single worker may earn a living by mending shoes."},{"t":"fact","text":"A person who does only one very specialised task will do it best.","label":"Xenophon's idea"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Who does a very specialised task well, according to Xenophon?","options":["Someone who tries many things","A person who does only one task","Whoever works the longest hours"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. It says who does a specialised task well.","explain":"A person who does only one very specialised task will do it best."}}]},{"icon":"📌","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Adam Smith wrote about a small pin factory where ten workers shared the job of making pins."},{"t":"fact","text":"Together they could make upwards of forty-eight thousand pins in a day. Working separately, each could not make twenty pins in a day, perhaps not even one.","label":"The result"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"When each worker did one small step, how many pins could ten workers make in a day?","options":["Upwards of forty-eight thousand","A few dozen, working all day long","About two hundred if they hurried"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the box. It says how many pins they could make.","explain":"Together the ten workers could make upwards of forty-eight thousand pins in a day."}}]},{"icon":"🔎","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A job. A wage. An hourly wage. A minimum wage. One person mending shoes. Ten workers making pins."},{"t":"p","text":"Work can be shared."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Job","Work a person does to earn money"],["Wage","Money paid by an employer for work"],["Minimum wage","The lowest wage a law allows"],["Specialised","Doing only one kind of task very well"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"A wage is money that an employer pays an employee for what?","options":["Gifts","Rent","Work"],"answer":2,"explain":"A wage is money that is paid by an employer to an employee in exchange for work."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is an hourly wage?","options":["Pay for each hour worked","Pay for a whole year","A free gift from the boss"],"answer":0,"explain":"Wages are usually paid by the hour, which is called an hourly wage."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What do laws in many countries set for workers?","options":["A maximum age","A minimum wage","A bedtime"],"answer":1,"explain":"In many countries, laws set a minimum wage that all employers must pay their workers."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many workers shared the job in the pin factory that Adam Smith wrote about?","options":["Two","A hundred","Ten"],"answer":2,"explain":"Adam Smith wrote about a small pin factory where ten workers shared the job of making pins."}]},{"id":"economics-business","course":"economics","kind":"facts","section":"business","title":"Starting a Business","glyph":"🏭","n":6,"idea":"A lemonade stand is a business: spend a little, sell for more and keep the difference. Learn about entrepreneurs, profit and risk.","pages":[{"icon":"🏭","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A business is a place where people work together to make and sell products or services."},{"t":"fact","text":"Other people buy the products and services.","label":"Who buys"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What do people in a business work to make and sell?","options":["Weather and seasons","Roads and bridges","Products or services"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. It says what people in a business make and sell.","explain":"People in a business work together to make and sell products or services."}}]},{"icon":"🧑‍💼","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"An entrepreneur is a person who starts a business instead of working for someone else."},{"t":"fact","text":"The entrepreneur makes the main decisions for the business.","label":"Making decisions"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What do we call a person who starts a business instead of working for someone else?","options":["An employee at a store","An entrepreneur","A customer in a shop"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. It says what that person is called.","explain":"An entrepreneur is a person who starts a business instead of working for someone else."}}]},{"icon":"⚠️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"An entrepreneur takes on the risks and the rewards of a business."},{"t":"fact","text":"Many new businesses fail, lose money, and close.","label":"Risk"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does an entrepreneur take on besides the rewards?","options":["The risks","The taxes","The wages"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. It says what an entrepreneur takes on.","explain":"An entrepreneur takes on the risks and the rewards of a business."}}]},{"icon":"🧮","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Profit is what a business makes."},{"t":"term","word":"profit","text":"The money brought in minus the money spent."},{"t":"fact","text":"Profit is found by subtracting how much money was spent from how much money was brought in.","label":"How to find it"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How is profit found?","options":["By adding up the prices of everything","By counting all of the customers each day","By subtracting how much money was spent"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the box. It says how profit is found.","explain":"Profit is found by subtracting how much money was spent from how much money was brought in."}}]},{"icon":"🍦","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"John spends $15 on ice cream cones and sells them for $20."},{"t":"fact","text":"John has made a profit of $5.","label":"His profit"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"John spends $15 on ice cream cones and sells them for $20. How much profit does he make?","options":["$5","$15","$20"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the box. It says how much profit John made.","explain":"John spent $15 and sold the cones for $20, so he made a profit of $5."}}]},{"icon":"🏬","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A business can be small or big. One person can open a small barber-shop."},{"t":"fact","text":"A big business can employ thousands of people all over the world.","label":"Big ones"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many people can a big business employ?","options":["Only one person","Thousands of people","Never more than ten"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. It says how many people a big business can employ.","explain":"A big business can employ thousands of people all over the world."}}]},{"icon":"🔎","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"People who make and sell. An entrepreneur. Risks and rewards. Profit from ice cream cones. A small shop and a big business."},{"t":"p","text":"Businesses make and sell."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Business","A place where people make and sell things"],["Entrepreneur","A person who starts a business"],["Profit","Money brought in minus money spent"],["Risk","What an entrepreneur takes on with the rewards"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is profit?","options":["Money brought in minus money spent","All the money a bank holds","The price of one ice cream cone"],"answer":0,"explain":"Profit is found by subtracting how much money was spent from how much money was brought in."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"A business brings in $20 and spends $15. What is its profit?","options":["$35","$15","$5"],"answer":2,"explain":"$20 brought in, minus $15 spent, leaves a profit of $5."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What happens to many new businesses?","options":["They never sell anything","They fail and close","They always make a profit"],"answer":1,"explain":"Many new businesses fail, lose money, and close."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which person starts a business to make use of an opportunity?","options":["A consumer","An employee","An entrepreneur"],"answer":2,"explain":"An entrepreneur is a person who starts a business instead of working for someone else."}]},{"id":"economics-supply","course":"economics","kind":"facts","section":"supply","title":"Prices & Supply","glyph":"📈","n":6,"idea":"Why does a rare card cost so much? Prices go up and down with how much there is and how many people want it.","pages":[{"icon":"🏷️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Price is the value of a product or service."},{"t":"fact","text":"A price can be expressed in other goods, in services, or in money.","label":"In money or goods"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is a price?","options":["The weight of a heavy shipping box","The color on a label or a sign","The value of a product or service"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. It says what a price is.","explain":"Price is the value of a product or service."}}]},{"icon":"🐑","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Peter has wool and Sally has eggs. Peter will not trade his wool for eggs if he does not need eggs."},{"t":"fact","text":"So Sally sells her eggs for money and gives Peter money for the wool. How much money Peter wants for his wool is the price.","label":"The price"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How much money Peter wants for his wool is called what?","options":["The profit","The price","The wage"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. It says what Peter's price is.","explain":"How much money Peter wants for his wool is the price."}}]},{"icon":"⚖️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Two things make a price go up or down."},{"t":"term","word":"demand","text":"The number of things that people want to buy."},{"t":"fact","text":"One is the number of things people can buy (supply). The other is the number of things people want to buy (demand).","label":"Supply and demand"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What do we call the number of things that people want to buy?","options":["Demand","Supply","Profit"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the box. It names the number of things people want to buy.","explain":"The number of things people want to buy is called demand."}}]},{"icon":"📈","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"If more people want to buy something but the supply stays the same, there will be a shortage. Then the price will go up."},{"t":"fact","text":"Many people want the same thing, but there is not enough of it.","label":"Why"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What happens to the price when more people want something and the supply stays the same?","options":["Sellers must give things away","The price will go up","Nothing changes at all"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. It says what happens to the price.","explain":"When more people want something and the supply stays the same, the price will go up."}}]},{"icon":"📉","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"If the supply goes up and the demand stays the same, there will be a surplus. Then the price will go down."},{"t":"fact","text":"People do not want to pay more for things that they can easily buy somewhere else.","label":"Why the price falls"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What happens to the price when the supply goes up and the demand stays the same?","options":["Sellers can charge much more","Nothing changes at all","The price will go down"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. It says what happens to the price.","explain":"When the supply goes up and the demand stays the same, the price will go down."}}]},{"icon":"🍞","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Inflation means that prices are going up. More money is needed to pay for goods, like a loaf of bread."},{"t":"fact","text":"The opposite of inflation is deflation.","label":"The opposite"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does inflation mean?","options":["Prices are going up","Everything is on sale forever","Shops are closing early"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. It says what inflation means.","explain":"Inflation means that prices are going up."}}]},{"icon":"🔎","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A price. Wool and eggs. Supply and demand. A shortage and a surplus. Prices going up."},{"t":"p","text":"Prices change."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Price","The value of a product or service"],["Supply","The number of things people can buy"],["Demand","The number of things people want to buy"],["Shortage","Too little supply for what people want"],["Inflation","Prices going up in general"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What are the two things that make a price go up or down?","options":["Profit and wages","Supply and demand","Cost and rent"],"answer":1,"explain":"Two things make a price go up or down: supply and demand."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is there when more people want something than there is supply?","options":["A shortage","A surplus","A tariff"],"answer":0,"explain":"If the demand increases and the supply stays the same, there will be a shortage."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does a surplus do to the price?","options":["It makes the price go up","It stops all trading","It makes the price go down"],"answer":2,"explain":"If the supply increases and the demand stays the same, there will be a surplus, and the price will go down."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is the opposite of inflation?","options":["Interest","Deflation","Barter"],"answer":1,"explain":"The opposite of inflation is deflation."}]},{"id":"economics-saving","course":"economics","kind":"facts","section":"saving","title":"Saving & Banks","glyph":"🐷","n":6,"idea":"Saving is money you do not spend. See what a bank does, what interest is, and how a budget helps.","pages":[{"icon":"🐷","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Saving is money that is not spent."},{"t":"fact","text":"One way to save is to put money in a savings account.","label":"One way to save"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is saving?","options":["Cash handed out to friends","A loan from the bank","Money that is not spent"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. It says what saving is.","explain":"Saving is money that is not spent."}}]},{"icon":"🏦","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A bank is a place where customers can save or borrow money."},{"t":"fact","text":"Customers can put their money in the bank for safekeeping.","label":"Safekeeping"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What can customers do at a bank?","options":["Plant seeds or crops","Save or borrow money","Fix cars or bicycles"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. It says what customers can do at a bank.","explain":"Customers can save or borrow money at a bank."}}]},{"icon":"💹","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Interest is the cost for borrowing money or the payment for lending money."},{"t":"term","word":"interest","text":"The cost of borrowing money."},{"t":"fact","text":"Interest is usually a percentage of the money borrowed.","label":"How it is set"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is interest?","options":["The cost for borrowing money","The color of a shiny coin or note","A special kind of savings box"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. It says what interest is.","explain":"Interest is the cost for borrowing money or the payment for lending money."}}]},{"icon":"💲","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"If the interest on $100 is 1 percent per year, then every year the borrower must pay $1."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"The interest on $100 is 1 percent per year. How much interest must the borrower pay each year?","options":["$10","$100","$1"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. It says how much the borrower pays each year.","explain":"On $100 at 1 percent per year, the borrower must pay $1 every year."}}]},{"icon":"📋","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A budget is a plan for the money a person or group will spend."},{"t":"fact","text":"A budget lists what to spend money on and how much to spend on each.","label":"What it lists"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does a budget say about each thing to spend money on?","options":["How tall each one is","How much to spend on each","Where each one was made"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. It says what a budget lists.","explain":"A budget lists what to spend money on and how much to spend on each."}}]},{"icon":"🤝","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A loan is a type of debt."},{"t":"fact","text":"The borrower agrees to pay the lender back at a particular time.","label":"Paying back"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Who agrees to pay the money back in a loan?","options":["The borrower","The lender","The banker"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the box. It says who pays the lender back.","explain":"In a loan, the borrower agrees to pay the lender back at a particular time."}}]},{"icon":"🔎","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Money put aside. A bank for safekeeping. Interest on a loan. A plan for spending. A borrower and a lender."},{"t":"p","text":"Saving is planning."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Saving","Money that is not spent"],["Bank","A place to save or borrow money"],["Interest","The cost of borrowing money"],["Budget","A plan for the money a person will spend"],["Loan","A debt that the borrower pays back"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which of these is a way to save?","options":["Spending it on a toy right away","Putting money in a savings account","Giving it all to a friend"],"answer":1,"explain":"One way to save is to put money in a savings account."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why might a customer put money in a bank?","options":["To make it heavier","To change its color","For safekeeping"],"answer":2,"explain":"Customers can put their money in the bank for safekeeping."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What do we call a plan for the money a person will spend?","options":["A budget","A market","A tariff"],"answer":0,"explain":"A budget is a plan for the money a person or group will spend."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"In a loan, who pays the money back?","options":["The lender","The bank teller","The borrower"],"answer":2,"explain":"The borrower agrees to pay the lender back at a particular time."}]},{"id":"economics-taxes","course":"economics","kind":"facts","section":"taxes","title":"Taxes & Shared Things","glyph":"🏛️","n":6,"idea":"Roads, police and schools are paid for by everyone together. That is what taxes are for.","pages":[{"icon":"🏛️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Tax is money that people have to pay to the government."},{"t":"fact","text":"The government uses the money it gets from taxes to pay for things.","label":"What it is used for"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Who do people pay taxes to?","options":["A bank in the city","The government","A shop on the corner"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. It says who people pay taxes to.","explain":"Tax is money that people have to pay to the government."}}]},{"icon":"🛣️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Taxes pay for people who work for the government, such as the military and police."},{"t":"fact","text":"Taxes also pay for services such as education and health care, and for roads, bridges and sewers.","label":"And more"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What can taxes be used to build or fix?","options":["Toys, games and cards","Cakes, pies and bread","Roads, bridges and sewers"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the box. It says what taxes also pay for.","explain":"Taxes also pay for roads, bridges and sewers."}}]},{"icon":"🛒","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A sales tax is based on how much a person buys."},{"t":"term","word":"property tax","text":"A tax based on how much a person owns, like a house."},{"t":"fact","text":"An income tax is based on how much a person earns. A property tax is based on how much a person owns.","label":"Two more kinds"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is a tax based on how much a person buys called?","options":["A sales tax","An income tax","A property tax"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. It names the tax based on what a person buys.","explain":"A sales tax is based on how much a person buys."}}]},{"icon":"🏺","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The earliest known taxes were those of the Egyptian Old Kingdom."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where were the earliest known taxes?","options":["The Roman Empire","The Egyptian Old Kingdom","The American colonies"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. It says where the earliest known taxes were.","explain":"The earliest known taxes were those of the Egyptian Old Kingdom."}}]},{"icon":"💡","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Governments use some tax money to pay for public goods."},{"t":"fact","text":"Public goods, like street lights, are available for everyone.","label":"Public goods"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What are things like street lights, available for everyone, called?","options":["Private toys","Sales items","Public goods"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. It names the things that governments pay for with tax money.","explain":"Public goods, like street lights, are available for everyone."}}]},{"icon":"🇺🇸","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"In the United States, the national government has an income tax."},{"t":"fact","text":"Most states have an income tax or a sales tax, or both. Cities and towns may have a sales tax or a property tax.","label":"Other levels"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"In the United States, which kind of government has an income tax?","options":["The national government","Only the neighborhood club","No government at all"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. It says which government has an income tax.","explain":"In the United States, the national government has an income tax."}}]},{"icon":"🔎","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Money for the government. Roads, police and schools. A sales tax and an income tax. Street lights for everyone."},{"t":"p","text":"Taxes are shared."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Tax","Money people have to pay to the government"],["Sales tax","A tax based on how much a person buys"],["Income tax","A tax based on how much a person earns"],["Public goods","Things like street lights for everyone"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which of these is paid for with taxes?","options":["A birthday party","A video game","The police"],"answer":2,"explain":"Taxes pay for people who work for the government, such as the military and police."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is an income tax based on?","options":["How much a person earns","How tall a person is","How old a person is"],"answer":0,"explain":"An income tax is based on how much a person earns."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is a tax based on how much a person owns called?","options":["A sales tax","A property tax","An income tax"],"answer":1,"explain":"A property tax is based on how much a person owns."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What are street lights an example of?","options":["Private toys","Sales items","Public goods"],"answer":2,"explain":"Public goods, like street lights, are available for everyone."}]},{"id":"body-brain","course":"body","kind":"facts","section":"brain","title":"Brain & Nerves","glyph":"🧠","n":15,"idea":"Your brain fills in gaps so well you never see your blind spot. It also runs on about 20 watts and floats in fluid that makes it almost weightless.","pages":[{"icon":"👁️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Close one eye and there is a hole in what you see. Each eye has a spot where the nerve leaves it and nothing is sensed. Your brain paints over that gap, so smoothly that you never notice."},{"t":"try","text":"Draw a cross, and a dot 6 inches (15 cm) to the left of it. Hold the paper at arm's length, close your right eye, and stare at the cross with your left. Slowly move the paper toward your face. The dot will vanish — that is your blind spot, where the nerve leaves the eye."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does your brain do with the gap where the nerve leaves the eye?","options":["It sends a bright flash to warn you of the gap","It leaves a small black spot in your sight all day","It paints over it so smoothly you never notice"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the box that tells you what to do. It explains what happens when you move the paper.","explain":"The dot vanishes because your brain fills in the gap so smoothly that you do not notice it."}}]},{"icon":"⚡","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Signals race along your nerves at up to about 270 mph (430 km/h) — faster than a Formula 1 car."},{"t":"fact","text":"Not all nerves are quick. The fibers that carry a sharp, sudden pain are fast. The ones carrying dull ache crawl along at about walking pace. Your spinal cord also acts on the fast signal by itself, so your hand jerks back before the message even reaches your brain."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why do you snatch your hand from something hot before it hurts?","options":["Because your eyes see the danger before your skin can feel it","Because the fibers carrying sharp pain are fast","Because your muscles feel the heat before your skin does"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It explains the speed of different nerve fibers.","explain":"The fast fibers for sharp pain make you react quickly, while the slow ones for dull ache take longer to send their signal."}}]},{"icon":"🤯","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Your brain is about the size of two clenched fists held together."},{"t":"fact","text":"About 60 percent of its dry weight is fat, which makes it your fattiest organ. That fat forms the insulating sheath around your nerve fibers and the membranes of your brain cells."},{"t":"fact","text":"Your brain weighs about 2% of your total body weight but burns about 20% of your energy — that’s roughly 20 watts, about half of a 40-watt light bulb."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"About how much of your energy does the brain burn?","options":["About 5 percent","About 20 percent","About 50 percent"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the last fact box. It gives the exact percentage.","explain":"The brain burns about 20% of your energy, even though it makes up only 2% of your weight."}}]},{"icon":"🧮","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"For a long time, nobody had actually counted the cells in a human brain."},{"t":"fact","text":"Suzana Herculano-Houzel dissolved brains into a smooth soup and counted the nuclei floating in it. Each cell has one nucleus, so counting nuclei counts cells. The answer came out near 86 billion neurons, not the 100 billion that textbooks used to say."},{"t":"p","text":"The idea that you use only 10% of your brain is simply wrong. Scans show activity all over it, and there is no spare, unused portion sitting idle."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How were the brain's cells counted?","options":["The brain was dissolved into soup and the nuclei were counted","A robot counted each cell one by one under a microscope","Its total weight was divided by the weight of one cell"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It explains how the counting was done.","explain":"Suzana Herculano-Houzel dissolved brains into a smooth soup and counted the nuclei floating in it to get the number of neurons."}}]},{"icon":"🍦","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Brain freeze is not your brain getting cold — your brain never cools that fast."},{"t":"fact","text":"When something very cold hits the roof of your mouth, a blood vessel there narrows and then widens again quickly. Nearby nerves report this change.","label":"Worked out, not seen directly"},{"t":"p","text":"Your brain misreads where that signal came from, so you feel it in your forehead instead of the roof of your mouth."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where does brain freeze really start?","options":["In the muscles that move your jaw","In the nerves of your tongue and front teeth","In a blood vessel in the roof of your mouth"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the second sentence. It explains where the signal starts.","explain":"The signal starts in a blood vessel in the roof of your mouth, and your brain misreads its location."}}]},{"icon":"🦶","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Sit down, lift your right foot off the floor and circle it clockwise."},{"t":"try","text":"Keep circling your foot, and now draw a big number 6 in the air with your right hand. The foot will flip direction."},{"t":"fact","text":"Both jobs are run from the same side of your brain, and the hand wins."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which one wins, the foot or the hand?","options":["The hand","The foot","They both stop"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It says which one wins.","explain":"The hand wins because both jobs are run from the same side of your brain."}}]},{"icon":"🧷","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Have you ever felt a fizzing sensation in your arm or leg after sitting in an awkward position? That's what we call pins and needles."},{"t":"fact","text":"Pins and needles happen when you squash a nerve, not because your foot \"falls asleep\". When the pressure lifts, the nerve fires off a burst of confused messages that feel like fizzing."},{"t":"p","text":"Another strange feeling is the jolt you sometimes get just as you are falling asleep. It's called a hypnic jerk. Nobody is sure why it happens; one idea is that your brain mistakes your muscles relaxing for a fall."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What causes pins and needles?","options":["Your foot falling asleep","A squashed nerve","A sudden muscle spasm"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It explains what causes pins and needles.","explain":"When you squash a nerve, it sends confused messages that feel like fizzing."}}]},{"icon":"🎈","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Stand in a doorway and push the backs of both hands outward against the frame for 45 seconds, as if you were trying to widen the doorway."},{"t":"fact","text":"Step forward and relax, and your arms will drift up on their own. The muscles are still firing even after your brain stopped asking them to."}]},{"icon":"🚖","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"London taxi drivers need to know 25,000 streets before they can get their license."},{"t":"term","word":"hippocampus","text":"A part deep in the brain that helps you remember places and events."},{"t":"fact","text":"Scans show the back of their hippocampus — the brain's map room — is measurably larger than other drivers', and it grows while they learn."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What grows larger in a taxi driver's brain?","options":["The back of the hippocampus, the brain's map room","The cerebellum, the part that keeps you balanced","The visual cortex, the brain's picture screen"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It tells you which part of the hippocampus grows.","explain":"The back of the hippocampus grows larger as they learn the streets."}}]},{"icon":"🌊","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Your brain weighs about 3 pounds (1.4 kg), yet it feels like almost nothing inside your head."},{"t":"fact","text":"Your brain sits in clear fluid that is almost exactly as dense as brain tissue itself. This makes its effective weight drop from about 3 lb (1.4 kg) to roughly 2 oz (50 g). It is like the difference between resting on bone and hanging weightlessly."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Blind spot","Where your brain hides the gap"],["Suzana Herculano-Houzel","Dissolved brains into soup"],["86 billion","Neurons in a human brain"],["Pins and needles","A squashed nerve sends confused messages"],["Hypnic jerk","The jolt you feel when falling asleep"],["London taxi drivers","Need to know 25,000 streets"]]},"picgame":{"type":"tapall","title":"Brain Freeze","goal":"Tap the very cold things that give you brain freeze.","items":[{"icon":"🍦","name":"ice cream","good":true},{"icon":"🧊","name":"ice","good":true},{"icon":"🍧","name":"snow cone","good":true},{"icon":"🍨","name":"sundae","good":true},{"icon":"☕","name":"hot tea","good":false},{"icon":"🍲","name":"hot soup","good":false},{"icon":"🥞","name":"pancakes","good":false},{"icon":"🍞","name":"bread","good":false}]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What's actually causing \"brain freeze\" when you eat something very cold too fast?","options":["A blood vessel in the roof of your mouth overreacts, and your brain misreads where the signal comes from","Your brain itself is briefly cooled down","Nerves in your teeth send a false pain signal"],"answer":0,"explain":"Your brain never actually cools that fast — that's why you feel it in your forehead instead of the roof of your mouth."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why do London taxi drivers who pass their famously hard 25,000-street test have a measurably larger hippocampus?","options":["The brain region they use most while learning actually grows with the work","They're born with larger brains to begin with","It's an illusion from how brain scans are measured"],"answer":0,"explain":"Trainees who studied for years but failed the test showed no such growth — the parts you work hardest are the parts that get built up."},{"type":"tf","prompt":"People only use about 10 percent of their brain, with the rest sitting unused.","answer":1,"explain":"Brain scans show activity all over it — damage to almost any small patch costs something, a word, a memory, or a movement."},{"type":"tf","prompt":"For decades, textbooks claimed the human brain has 100 billion neurons, but a careful recount found a different number.","answer":0,"explain":"Suzana Herculano-Houzel dissolved brains into a soup and counted the nuclei — the real number came out near 86 billion."}]},{"id":"body-heart","course":"body","kind":"facts","section":"heart","title":"Heart & Blood","glyph":"❤️","n":13,"idea":"Your heart beats about 100,000 times a day and keeps going because it rests between every beat. It moves about 5 quarts of blood a minute and sets its own rhythm.","pages":[{"icon":"🫰","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"You can find your own heartbeat in about five seconds. Put two fingers — not your thumb, it has its own pulse — on the thumb side of your wrist."},{"t":"try","text":"Count the beats for 15 seconds and multiply by four to get beats per minute."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How do you turn a 15-second count into beats a minute?","options":["Divide by four","Add thirty","Multiply by four"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the box that tells you what to do. It tells you what to do with the count.","explain":"Count the beats for 15 seconds and then multiply that number by four."}}]},{"icon":"👊","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Your heart is about the size of your closed fist and grows as you do."},{"t":"term","word":"aorta","text":"The pipe leaving the heart, about an inch across."},{"t":"fact","text":"The aorta, the pipe leaving the heart, is about 1 inch (2.5 cm) across, almost like a garden hose."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is the aorta?","options":["The valve inside the heart","The pipe leaving the heart, about an inch across","A vein that brings blood back to the heart"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the term box. It says what the aorta is.","explain":"The aorta is the pipe leaving the heart and it is about an inch across."}}]},{"icon":"🥁","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Your heart beats about 100,000 times a day and has never once taken a day off."},{"t":"fact","text":"It manages that because it rests between every single beat. Add all those tiny pauses together and the heart is actually \"off duty\" for more than half of every day."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How can the heart keep going all day?","options":["It rests between every single beat","It gets an hour-long break in the middle","Each beat is so gentle that it uses almost no energy"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It explains how the heart takes tiny breaks.","explain":"The heart rests between each beat, adding up to a lot of rest time throughout the day."}}]},{"icon":"🧭","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A grown-up's heart moves about 5 quarts of blood every single minute. That is all the blood in the body, over and over again."},{"t":"fact","text":"In just one day, your heart pumps roughly 2,000 gallons of blood through you."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"About how much blood does the heart move in a minute?","options":["About 5 quarts, all of it","About 2 quarts, just a part","About 12 gallons, all of it"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the first sentence. It gives the number of quarts.","explain":"Your heart moves about 5 quarts each minute, which is your entire blood supply."}}]},{"icon":"🔄","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"In 1628, William Harvey figured out that blood circulates through your body."},{"t":"fact","text":"He used arithmetic to show it. He worked out how much blood one heartbeat pushes, multiplied it by the number of beats in half an hour, and got far more blood than a whole body holds."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How did Harvey show that blood goes round in a loop?","options":["By finding tiny capillaries with a strong microscope","By watching the blood move through a glass window in the skin","By arithmetic, which gave more blood than a body holds"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It explains how he used numbers to figure it out.","explain":"Harvey's numbers showed there was too much blood for it to just be used up, so it had to go round in a loop."}}]},{"icon":"🩸","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Your veins look blue under your skin, but that is just an illusion."},{"t":"fact","text":"Blood that has given up its oxygen is dark red. Veins only appear blue because of how light travels through your skin and bounces back to your eye."},{"t":"p","text":"Red blood cells carry oxygen using iron, which is why blood tastes faintly metallic."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What color is blood that has given up its oxygen?","options":["Bright red","Dark red","Blue"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the first sentence of the fact box. It tells you what color blood is without oxygen.","explain":"Blood that has given up its oxygen is dark red."}}]},{"icon":"🧵","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Nobody has ever laid one person's blood vessels end to end, but we can work out how long they are."},{"t":"fact","text":"Count the capillaries in a small piece of tissue and multiply up. You get somewhere between ten and sixty thousand miles, and scientists still argue about the exact number.","label":"How we know"},{"t":"p","text":"Almost all of that length is capillaries — tiny vessels so narrow that red blood cells have to squeeze through in single file."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How do we know how long the blood vessels are?","options":["By pulling them out of a body and laying them in a line","By following one blood drop with a stopwatch and a fast camera","By counting capillaries in a small piece and multiplying up"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the How we know box. It tells you how scientists work it out.","explain":"Scientists count capillaries in a small piece of tissue and then multiply up to get the total length."}}]},{"icon":"⏱️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"You can watch your blood refill part of your body in under two seconds."},{"t":"fact","text":"Press hard on a fingernail for five seconds, then let go. The nail will turn white, then color floods back — normally in under two seconds."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How fast should the color come back?","options":["In under two seconds","In over five seconds","It should stay white"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It gives the time the color should return.","explain":"The color should flood back in under two seconds. This is how paramedics check that blood is reaching someone's hands and feet."}}]},{"icon":"🎛️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Your heart does not wait for instructions. A patch of cells at the top sets the beat."},{"t":"fact","text":"Even single heart cells taken out and kept in a dish keep beating on their own. That is why a donated heart starts up again in a new chest."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What sets the heartbeat?","options":["The brain sends a signal every time","A patch of cells in the heart itself","The lungs tell it when to beat"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the second sentence. It names what sets the beat.","explain":"A patch of cells in the heart itself sets the beat, and even single heart cells kept in a dish continue to beat on their own."}}]},{"icon":"🚪","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The lub-dub sound in your heart is not from the squeeze of the heartbeat."},{"t":"fact","text":"It is four valves snapping shut, two at a time: first the two between the upper and lower chambers (lub), then the two leaving the heart (dub). Each valve opens one way only, so blood goes forward and can never wash back."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Aorta","The pipe leaving the heart, about an inch across"],["Harvey","Used arithmetic to show blood circulates"],["Capillaries","Counted in small pieces to work out total length"],["Veins","Appear blue due to light travel"],["Red blood cells","Carry oxygen using iron"],["Lub-dub sound","Four valves snapping shut"]]},"picgame":{"type":"tapall","title":"Heart Size","goal":"Tap what is about as big as your heart.","items":[{"icon":"🍎","name":"apple","good":true},{"icon":"🍊","name":"orange","good":true},{"icon":"🎾","name":"tennis ball","good":true},{"icon":"🍑","name":"peach","good":true},{"icon":"🍉","name":"watermelon","good":false},{"icon":"🏀","name":"basketball","good":false},{"icon":"🐘","name":"elephant","good":false},{"icon":"🏠","name":"house","good":false}]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"How did William Harvey prove in 1628 that blood circulates in a loop, without ever seeing a capillary?","options":["He calculated that the heart pushes far more blood in half an hour than the whole body could possibly hold","He dissected hundreds of hearts to trace the vessels directly","He used an early microscope to watch blood flow"],"answer":0,"explain":"Before Harvey, blood was thought to be made fresh in the liver and used up at the edges — his arithmetic left no other answer."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why do veins look blue through your skin, even though blood is never actually blue?","options":["Blue light bounces back off skin fibers, while red light gets absorbed deep by the dark blood","Veins contain a different, bluer type of blood than arteries","Oxygen turns blood blue once it leaves the lungs"],"answer":0,"explain":"The vein itself is red — it's the picture reaching your eye that's blue."},{"type":"tf","prompt":"Your heart needs signals from your brain to keep beating in rhythm.","answer":1,"explain":"A patch of cells at the top of the heart sets its own beat — even single heart cells in a dish will keep beating on their own, which is why a donated heart starts up again in a new chest."}]},{"id":"body-bones","course":"body","kind":"facts","section":"bones","title":"Bones & Muscles","glyph":"🦴","n":14,"idea":"Your body starts with 300 bones but ends up with 206. The smallest bone sits in your ear, and one bone in your throat touches no other bone.","pages":[{"icon":"🍼","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"You were born with about 300 bones. By the time you are grown you have 206. Some of your bones fused together as you grew."},{"t":"fact","text":"A baby's skull is made up of several separate plates with gaps between them. This lets the head flex during birth and keep growing after."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many bones does an adult have?","options":["300","206","150"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the second sentence on the page. It gives the number you have now.","explain":"The number dropped from about 300 at birth to 206 as some bones fused together."}}]},{"icon":"🦴","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Bone is light, yet it holds up your whole body and takes hard knocks without breaking."},{"t":"fact","text":"Bone manages that by being two things at once. Hard mineral makes it stiff. Stretchy collagen fibers make it tough. Pure mineral would shatter. Pure collagen would simply bend."},{"t":"term","word":"collagen","text":"The stretchy fibers in your bones that keep them from breaking easily."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What makes bone tough instead of brittle?","options":["Stretchy collagen fibers","Layers of dried marrow","Thin, smooth muscle fibers"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It explains how hard mineral and stretchy collagen work together.","explain":"Stretchy collagen fibers make bone tough instead of brittle."}}]},{"icon":"👂","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Your smallest bone is inside your ear."},{"t":"fact","text":"It is called the stapes, or stirrup, because that is exactly what it looks like. Three tiny bones in a chain amplify the wobble of your eardrum and pass it inward."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where is your smallest bone?","options":["In your foot","In your hand","Inside your ear"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the first sentence. It tells you where your smallest bone is.","explain":"Your smallest bone, the stapes or stirrup, is inside your ear."}}]},{"icon":"🖐️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Over half the bones in your body are tucked into your hands and feet."},{"t":"fact","text":"Each hand has twenty-seven bones, and each foot has twenty-six bones. All those small bones let your hand wrap around a ball and your foot roll through a step."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many bones are in each hand?","options":["Twenty-seven","Twenty","Thirty"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It gives the exact number of bones in each hand.","explain":"Each hand has twenty-seven bones, making it easy for you to grip and move with precision."}}]},{"icon":"🦒","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A giraffe's neck may look very different from yours, but it is built with the same number of bones."},{"t":"fact","text":"A giraffe has exactly seven neck bones, just like you do. Almost every mammal, from a mouse to a whale, has seven neck bones too."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many neck bones does a giraffe have?","options":["Five","Seven","Ten"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It tells you that a giraffe has the same number of neck bones as you do.","explain":"Both a giraffe and you have exactly seven neck bones, even though the giraffe's are much longer."}}]},{"icon":"🫳","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"There's one bone in your body that doesn't touch any other bone."},{"t":"fact","text":"It’s called the hyoid, a small horseshoe-shaped bone in your throat. It hangs from muscles and ligaments, has no joint to any other bone, and anchors your tongue."},{"t":"try","text":"Rest a finger above your voice box and swallow. You'll feel it jump up and down under your finger."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which bone touches no other bone?","options":["The jawbone in your face","The kneecap in your leg","The hyoid in your throat"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It tells you about the small horseshoe-shaped bone in your throat.","explain":"The hyoid is the bone that hangs in place without touching any other bone."}}]},{"icon":"💪","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Your jaw muscle is one of the strongest muscles in your body for its size."},{"t":"try","text":"Put your fingers on your temples and bite down gently. Feel that bulge? That is your temporalis muscle — it runs up the side of your skull and pulls your jaw shut, so it bulges when you chew."},{"t":"fact","text":"Your jaw muscles can press your back teeth together with a force of well over 100 pounds."}]},{"icon":"🦵","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Your thigh bone is the longest bone in your body, and it is close to a quarter of your height."},{"t":"fact","text":"Take your height, divide by four, and you have roughly the length of your thigh bone."},{"t":"p","text":"Your arm is joined to the rest of your skeleton by just one bone. Trace the ridge from the notch at the base of your throat out to the point of your shoulder: that collarbone is the only bony link."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How long is your thigh bone compared with your height?","options":["About half of it","About a quarter of it","About three-quarters of it"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It tells you to divide your height by four.","explain":"Your thigh bone is about a quarter of your total height."}}]},{"icon":"🦷","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Teeth are not bones. Enamel, the hard outer layer of your teeth, is actually the hardest material your body makes."},{"t":"fact","text":"Unlike bone, enamel cannot grow back once it is worn away, which is why brushing your teeth is so important."},{"t":"p","text":"Cracking your knuckles does not give you arthritis. Donald Unger cracked the knuckles of his left hand at least twice a day for over 60 years and never did it with his right hand. After all that time, neither hand had arthritis."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Does cracking your knuckles give you arthritis?","options":["Yes, it wears down the joints","It only happens if you do it too much","No, Donald Unger found no difference"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the last two sentences of the paragraph. They tell what Donald Unger did and what he found.","explain":"Donald Unger cracked his left hand knuckles for over 60 years and saw no difference compared to his right hand."}}]},{"icon":"🌀","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Your backbone is not a straight column."},{"t":"fact","text":"It curves in at the neck, out at the chest, and in again at the waist. This S-shape turns every footfall into a spring, instead of sending jarring shocks up to your skull."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Stapes","Inside your ear, looks like a stirrup"],["Hyoid","In your throat, shaped like a horseshoe"],["Collagen","Keeps bones tough and flexible"],["Thigh bone","About a quarter of your height"],["Donald Unger","Cracked knuckles for over 60 years without arthritis"],["Enamel","The hardest material in your body, can’t grow back"]]},"picgame":{"type":"sort","title":"Bones or None","goal":"Does it have bones inside, or not?","bins":[{"icon":"🦴","label":"has bones"},{"icon":"🚫","label":"no bones"}],"items":[{"icon":"🖐️","name":"hand","bin":0},{"icon":"🦶","name":"foot","bin":0},{"icon":"🦒","name":"giraffe","bin":0},{"icon":"🍎","name":"apple","bin":1},{"icon":"🌳","name":"tree","bin":1},{"icon":"☁️","name":"cloud","bin":1},{"icon":"🍌","name":"banana","bin":1}]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"You were born with about 300 bones but now have only 206. What happened to the rest?","options":["Some of them fused together as you grew","You lost them through natural wear","They dissolved and were absorbed by your body"],"answer":0,"explain":"A baby's skull starts as several separate plates, which is what lets the head flex during birth, then knits together over the first couple of years."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did Donald Unger prove by cracking only his left hand's knuckles for over 60 years?","options":["Cracking knuckles does not cause arthritis","Cracking knuckles makes joints weaker over time","Knuckle cracking sound comes from bone friction"],"answer":0,"explain":"X-rays of both his hands showed no difference — he won an Ig Nobel Prize for the experiment in 2009."},{"type":"tf","prompt":"Bone is a fixed, unchanging material once you stop growing.","answer":1,"explain":"Bone is constantly being torn down and rebuilt — over about ten years, your entire skeleton gets replaced."}]},{"id":"body-lungs","course":"body","kind":"facts","section":"lungs","title":"Lungs & Breathing","glyph":"🫁","n":12,"idea":"Your lungs, spread out flat, would cover about a quarter of a tennis court. Learn why you take big sighs without even knowing it.","pages":[{"icon":"🤐","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Have you ever tried to hum with your nose pinched shut?"},{"t":"try","text":"Pinch your nose shut and try to hum. You will see that it stops the sound instantly."},{"t":"fact","text":"Humming needs air moving out through your nose, so pinching it shut stops the sound right away. Also try humming while swallowing — that is also impossible."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why can you not hum with your nose pinched shut?","options":["Your pinched nose squeezes your vocal cords shut","Humming needs air to move out through your nose","Your throat closes up whenever you pinch your nose"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the first sentence of the fact box. It tells you what humming needs.","explain":"When you pinch your nose, it stops the air from moving out through your nose, so you cannot hum."}}]},{"icon":"🎾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"If you could unfold the inside of your lungs flat, they would cover about a quarter of a tennis court."},{"t":"term","word":"sacs","text":"Tiny air pockets where oxygen moves into your blood."},{"t":"fact","text":"All that area is folded into your chest as hundreds of millions of tiny air sacs. The wall of each one is thinner than a sheet of tissue paper, so oxygen slips straight through into the blood."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How thin is the wall of each air sac?","options":["About as thick as a slice of bread","Thicker than a sheet of stiff cardboard","Thinner than a sheet of tissue paper"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the last sentence on the page. It gives the thickness.","explain":"The wall of each air sac is thinner than a sheet of tissue paper, which lets oxygen move into your blood easily."}}]},{"icon":"❤️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Your left lung is smaller than your right because your heart takes up some of that space."},{"t":"fact","text":"The left lung has two lobes and the right has three. The spot where your heart sits in the left lung is called the cardiac notch."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why is the left lung smaller?","options":["Your heart sits in the space","It needs more room for air","It helps you breathe faster"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the first sentence on the page. It tells you why the left lung is smaller.","explain":"Your heart takes up some of the space where your left lung would otherwise be."}}]},{"icon":"😮","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Every five minutes or so, you take a big sigh without even deciding to do it."},{"t":"fact","text":"A sigh is a double-sized breath. Its job is popping open air sacs in your lungs that have collapsed shut."},{"t":"p","text":"Yawning is not about getting more oxygen. Robert Provine tested this by having people breathe pure oxygen and air with extra carbon dioxide. Neither changed how much they yawned."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does a sigh do?","options":["Grabs extra oxygen and stores it for later","Pops open air sacs that have collapsed shut","Warms up the air before it reaches your lungs"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It tells you what a sigh does.","explain":"A sigh helps pop open air sacs that have collapsed shut in your lungs."}}]},{"icon":"🪞","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"You are mostly breathing through just one nostril right now."},{"t":"fact","text":"About three quarters of the air goes through the dominant nostril, and your nose swaps which one that is every couple of hours, all day long."},{"t":"try","text":"Hold a cold mirror under your nose and breathe out. One patch of mist will be bigger than the other."}]},{"icon":"🌬️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Your breath leaves at one temperature but can feel hot or cold depending on how you blow."},{"t":"fact","text":"Hold your palm a few inches from your mouth. Breathe out slowly with your mouth wide open — it feels warm. Now purse your lips and blow hard at the same spot — it feels cold. The fast jet drags cool room air along with it, and that is most of what lands on your hand."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why does fast-blown breath feel cold?","options":["The fast jet drags cool room air along with it","Blowing hard makes your lungs breathe out colder air","Your lips squeeze the breath and chill it as it goes"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the last sentence of the fact box. It explains why the fast jet feels cold.","explain":"When you blow hard with pursed lips, the fast jet of air drags in cool room air, making it feel cold on your hand."}}]},{"icon":"😛","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Have you ever had a hiccup?"},{"t":"fact","text":"A hiccup happens when your diaphragm, the big muscle under your lungs, twitches and makes you gulp air suddenly. About a thirty-fifth of a second later, your vocal cords snap shut, making the \"hic\" sound."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What makes the hic sound?","options":["Your tongue snapping back fast","Your lungs bursting open fast","Your vocal cords snapping shut"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It explains the timing and what snaps shut.","explain":"When your vocal cords snap shut a thirty-fifth of a second after the twitch, that is what makes the \"hic\" sound."}}]},{"icon":"🐦","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"You breathe in and out through the same tubes, but a bird's air flows in one direction, in a loop."},{"t":"fact","text":"In a bird, fresh air keeps moving across its lungs even while it breathes out. This helps some birds fly at heights where a person would black out."}]},{"icon":"🧹","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"You have tiny hairs in your airways that row mucus upward, and you swallow around 3 pints of it every day without even noticing."},{"t":"fact","text":"These tiny hairs carry trapped dust with the mucus and move it upward at about half an inch a minute."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How fast do the airway hairs move mucus?","options":["About half an inch a minute","About one inch a second","About one foot a minute"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the second sentence. It gives the speed of the movement.","explain":"The tiny hairs move mucus upward at about half an inch a minute, carrying dust with it."}}]},{"icon":"🫧","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Imagine the tiny air sacs deep inside your lungs."},{"t":"fact","text":"These air sacs are wet inside, and wet surfaces stick shut. Each one is coated in a soapy film that keeps them open, so every sac reopens on every breath."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Humming","Stops when your nose is pinched shut"],["sacs","Tiny air pockets where oxygen moves into your blood"],["cardiac notch","The spot where your heart sits in the left lung"],["sigh","A double-sized breath to pop open collapsed air sacs"],["dominant nostril","Takes about three quarters of the air"],["soapy film","Keeps the air sacs open on every breath"]]},"picgame":{"type":"catch","title":"Breathing Helpers","goal":"Catch the parts that help you breathe.","items":[{"icon":"🫁","name":"lungs","good":true},{"icon":"👃","name":"nose","good":true},{"icon":"👄","name":"mouth","good":true},{"icon":"🦶","name":"foot","good":false},{"icon":"👁️","name":"eye","good":false},{"icon":"👂","name":"ear","good":false},{"icon":"🦵","name":"leg","good":false}]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"If you could unfold the inside surface of your lungs flat, how much space would it cover?","options":["A good chunk of a tennis court","About the size of a bedsheet","Roughly the size of your own skin"],"answer":0,"explain":"All that area is folded into your chest as hundreds of millions of tiny air sacs, each thinner than tissue paper."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"A 1959 researcher named Mary Ellen Avery discovered why premature babies sometimes struggle to breathe. What was the cause?","options":["Their lungs' air sacs hadn't yet made the soapy film that keeps them from sticking shut when wet","Their lungs were simply too small to hold enough air","Their diaphragm muscles hadn't developed yet"],"answer":0,"explain":"That coating can now be given to premature babies as medicine."},{"type":"tf","prompt":"A 1980s study found that breathing pure oxygen made people yawn less, proving yawning is about getting more oxygen.","answer":1,"explain":"Researcher Robert Provine found that neither pure oxygen nor extra carbon dioxide changed how much people yawned — why we yawn is still an open question."}]},{"id":"body-senses","course":"body","kind":"facts","section":"senses","title":"The Five Senses","glyph":"👂","n":14,"idea":"Every part of your tongue can taste everything, and the old taste map was a mistake. Most of what you call taste is really smell.","pages":[{"icon":"🫢","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Pinch your nose shut and eat a jelly bean without looking. You will get sweet, and almost nothing else."},{"t":"fact","text":"Most of what you call taste is actually smell. Let go of your nose mid-chew and the flavor arrives all at once."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What makes most of the flavor you taste?","options":["The texture of the food","The sound it makes when you bite it","Smell"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It says that letting go of your nose makes the flavor arrive all at once.","explain":"When you let go of your nose, the smell hits your senses and brings the full flavor with it."}}]},{"icon":"👅","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Remember those old maps showing sweet at the tip and bitter at the back? They are wrong."},{"t":"fact","text":"Every part of your tongue can detect every taste. The map came from a mistranslated German study and got copied for decades."},{"t":"term","word":"mistranslated","text":"Changed from one language into another by mistake."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Is there a map of tastes on your tongue?","options":["No, every part can taste everything","Yes, sweet at the tip and bitter at the back","Only salty is at the sides"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It says that every part of your tongue can detect every taste.","explain":"The old maps were based on a mistake, but every part of your tongue can taste all flavors."}}]},{"icon":"🌡️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Your sense of temperature is relative, and you can fool it in under a minute."},{"t":"fact","text":"Put one hand in cold water and one in warm for 30 seconds, then plunge both into lukewarm water. The same water feels warm to one hand and cool to the other."},{"t":"try","text":"Fill three bowls with cold, lukewarm, and comfortably warm water (never hot)."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why does lukewarm water feel warm to one hand and cool to the other?","options":["One hand has more nerves for feeling heat than the other","Temperature sense is relative to what it felt before","The water really is warmer next to one hand than the other"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It explains how your hands can feel different temperatures in the same bowl.","explain":"Your sense of temperature depends on what you felt before, so one hand thinks it's warm and the other cool in the same water."}}]},{"icon":"👁️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Your sight seems sharp all over, but there's a trick to it."},{"t":"fact","text":"The genuinely sharp part of your sight is about the size of your thumbnail held at arm's length."},{"t":"try","text":"Hold a book at arm's length and stare hard at one word. Do not let your eyes drift. Now try to read the words on either side of it. You cannot."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How big is the sharp part of your sight?","options":["Roughly as wide as a dinner plate held at arm's length","About the size of your thumbnail held at arm's length","Nearly as large as a whole book page held at arm's length"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It says how big the sharp part is.","explain":"Only a patch about the size of your thumbnail at arm's length is sharp. Everything outside it is rough, and your eyes hide that by darting around constantly."}}]},{"icon":"🟥","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Stare at a strong color for 30 seconds and then look at a white wall. Your eye will print the opposite color there."},{"t":"fact","text":"The cells in your eye that handle the first color get worn out for a moment, so you see the opposite one."},{"t":"try","text":"Stare at something red for 30 seconds without moving your eyes, then look at a white wall and watch what happens."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why do you see the opposite color on the white wall?","options":["The wall itself changes color when you stare at it","The light from the picture bounces back from the wall","The cells for the first color are worn out for a moment"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It says what happens to the cells that handle the first color.","explain":"The cells that handle the first color get worn out, so you see the opposite one."}}]},{"icon":"🎨","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"How many colors do you think you can see?"},{"t":"fact","text":"By measuring the smallest change your eye can just catch and multiplying it up, scientists work out that you can probably see about a million shades.","label":"Worked out, not counted"},{"t":"p","text":"About 1 in 12 boys and 1 in 200 girls cannot tell certain reds and greens apart. The instructions for those color detectors sit on the X chromosome, and boys have only one X."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How do we know about a million colors?","options":["By measuring the smallest change you can catch and multiplying up","By having a computer print out every shade and counting them","By asking thousands of people to name every color they can see"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It says how scientists work it out.","explain":"Scientists measure the smallest change your eye can just catch and multiply it up to get a rough number."}}]},{"icon":"👃","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Your nose has about 400 different types of smell detector, but you can tell apart far more smells than that. Each smell must be a pattern across many detectors at once."},{"t":"fact","text":"Your nose stops reporting any smell that does not change. That is why you cannot smell your own house, but other people can."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why can't you smell your own house?","options":["Your nose has too few smell detectors to catch a familiar smell","The smells in your house are too faint for any nose to catch","Your nose stops reporting any smell that does not change"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It explains why smells that do not change fade away.","explain":"Your nose stops reporting any smell that stays the same, so you stop noticing it."}}]},{"icon":"🎧","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"You can hear higher notes than most of the grown-ups around you."},{"t":"fact","text":"By 50, most people have lost everything above about 12,000 vibrations a second. That is the top of their hearing range."},{"t":"p","text":"Some shops have played a 17,000-per-second tone to move teenagers along. Most grown-ups will not hear it at all."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Who can hear the 17,000-per-second tone?","options":["Most grown-ups","Teenagers and young people","People over 50"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the last paragraph. It says who the tone is meant to move along.","explain":"Teenagers and young people can still hear higher frequencies that older folks cannot."}}]},{"icon":"🚗","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Have you ever felt sick while reading in a car?"},{"t":"fact","text":"Reading in a moving car gives your brain two different stories. Your eyes report a still page, but your inner ear reports the bouncing.","label":"Worked out, not seen directly"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why does reading in a car make you feel ill?","options":["Your eyes and ears give your brain contradictory stories","The strong smell inside the car upsets your stomach as you read","The car's speed squeezes the blood away from your stomach"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It explains what your eyes and ears report differently.","explain":"Your eyes see a still page, but your inner ear feels the motion. Your brain gets confused by these conflicting signals."}}]},{"icon":"🎹","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Inside your ear is a coiled tube laid out like a piano keyboard."},{"t":"fact","text":"High notes shake the hairs at one end, low notes at the far end. Each spot along it has its own line reporting to the brain."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Smell","Most of what you call taste"],["Lukewarm water","Feels warm to a cold hand"],["Thumbnail","Sharp sight area size"],["Opposite color","After staring at a strong color"],["Million shades","Colors you can see"],["No change","Why you can't smell your own house"]]},"picgame":{"type":"sort","title":"Eyes or Ears","goal":"Do you see it or hear it?","bins":[{"icon":"👁️","label":"eyes"},{"icon":"👂","label":"ears"}],"items":[{"icon":"🎨","name":"colors","bin":0},{"icon":"🟥","name":"red","bin":0},{"icon":"📖","name":"book","bin":0},{"icon":"🎧","name":"headphones","bin":1},{"icon":"🎹","name":"piano","bin":1},{"icon":"🎵","name":"music note","bin":1}]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"The classic \"tongue map\" showing sweet at the tip and bitter at the back — is it accurate?","options":["No — every part of the tongue can actually detect every taste","Yes — it's confirmed by modern taste research","It's accurate only for bitter and sour tastes"],"answer":0,"explain":"The diagram came from a mistranslation of a German study and got copied into textbooks for decades."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What's the real explanation for motion sickness in a moving car?","options":["Your eyes report stillness while your inner ear reports movement, and your brain can't resolve the conflict","Your stomach is upset by the car's vibrations","Your eyes simply can't process fast-moving scenery"],"answer":0,"explain":"Reading in a moving car creates exactly this contradiction, which is why it makes motion sickness worse."},{"type":"tf","prompt":"Your nose has enough different smell detectors — one for every smell you can recognize.","answer":1,"explain":"Your nose has only about 400 types of detector, far fewer than the smells you can tell apart — each smell is actually a pattern across many detectors at once."}]},{"id":"body-gut","course":"body","kind":"facts","section":"gut","title":"Eating & Digesting","glyph":"🍎","n":13,"idea":"Your stomach acid is strong enough to damage metal. Learn why it doesn't digest your stomach in the process.","pages":[{"icon":"🍪","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Chew a plain cracker slowly and hold it in your mouth for a whole minute without swallowing. It turns sweet because an enzyme in your saliva, a chemical that breaks food apart, cuts the starch into sugar."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why does the cracker turn sweet?","options":["The cracker is flavored with sugar to begin with","An enzyme in your saliva cuts the starch into sugar","Your teeth grind out the sweetness"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the second sentence. It tells you what the enzyme in your saliva does.","explain":"The enzyme in your saliva starts cutting the starch into sugar right away, which makes the cracker taste sweet after a minute."}}]},{"icon":"🎢","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Swallowing is not just gravity pulling food down."},{"t":"fact","text":"Muscles squeeze food along in a wave, so you could swallow upside down. This wave runs the whole length of your gut."},{"t":"term","word":"peristalsis","text":"The wave of squeezing muscles that moves food through your gut."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What moves food down your gut?","options":["A wave of squeezing muscles","Gravity alone","The teeth grinding it down"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the term definition. It names what moves the food.","explain":"Muscles squeeze food along in a wave, and this wave is called peristalsis."}}]},{"icon":"🔥","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Your stomach acid is strong enough to damage metal. But it does not digest your stomach."},{"t":"fact","text":"Your stomach relines itself with a layer of mucus and fresh cells, replacing the lining continuously."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why does your stomach not digest itself?","options":["Its acid is too weak to hurt a living stomach wall","The food inside always coats and protects the lining","A layer of mucus and constant new lining cells"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It says what keeps the acid from digesting the stomach wall.","explain":"A layer of mucus and a fresh supply of cells replace the lining continuously, protecting the stomach from its own acid."}}]},{"icon":"🧪","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"In 1822, Alexis St Martin got shot in the side and the wound left a hole into his stomach that never closed."},{"t":"fact","text":"His doctor, William Beaumont, lowered food on a string into the hole more than 200 times to see how much of it had been digested. These experiments showed that the stomach works mostly by chemistry, with juices breaking the food down."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did these experiments show?","options":["That the stomach only stores food until it moves on","That the stomach never needs to rest","That the stomach works by chemistry"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It names what the experiments proved about how the stomach works.","explain":"The experiments showed that the stomach works by chemistry, not grinding."}}]},{"icon":"🌽","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"You can time your own digestion with corn."},{"t":"fact","text":"Eat a good helping of corn and write down the time. The yellow skins are made of a fiber you cannot break down, so they come out looking exactly the same. For most people, they show up around a day to a day and a half later."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"About how long does corn take to pass through?","options":["Only an hour to two hours","A day to a day and a half","A week to a week and a half"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It gives the time range.","explain":"Most people see the corn skins around a day to a day and a half later."}}]},{"icon":"📏","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Your small intestine is not actually small. Stretched out, it is about 20 feet (6 m) long."},{"t":"fact","text":"It is called \"small\" because it is narrow, not short. Its inner surface is folded and fringed into millions of tiny projections."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why is it called the small intestine?","options":["It is narrow","It is short","It is the smallest organ in your belly"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It explains why it's called small.","explain":"The term \"small\" refers to its width, not its length."}}]},{"icon":"🔊","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Your stomach does not rumble because it wants food."},{"t":"fact","text":"About every 90 minutes, when there is nothing to digest, a slow wave sweeps down your gut clearing out leftovers. The noise you hear is gas and liquid being shoved along ahead of it."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why does your stomach rumble when empty?","options":["Your stomach is calling for more food","A slow wave is clearing out leftovers","It is shrinking to squeeze out extra water"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It describes what happens every 90 minutes.","explain":"The rumbling sound is from a slow wave pushing gas and liquid through your gut."}}]},{"icon":"🧠","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Your gut has its own nerve network, made of about 500 million cells."},{"t":"fact","text":"Cut the gut off from the brain, and it carries on digesting just fine. It has enough nerves to work on its own."},{"t":"p","text":"Inside your gut are trillions of bacteria that help break down food your body can't handle and make vitamins for you."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"About how many cells are in the gut's nerve network?","options":["About 50 million","About 500 million","About 5 billion"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the first sentence on the page. It gives the number of cells.","explain":"The gut's nerve network has about 500 million cells."}}]},{"icon":"🫀","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Your liver does hundreds of jobs, from cleaning your blood to making bile, which helps you digest fat."},{"t":"fact","text":"The liver is the one large organ inside you that grows back. Remove two thirds of it and the remaining part will grow back to full size."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which large organ grows back to full size after being partly removed?","options":["The heart","The lungs","The liver"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It names the organ that regrows.","explain":"The liver is unique in that it can grow back to its full size even after two thirds of it are removed."}}]},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The appendix is often called a spare part, but it may have a real job."},{"t":"fact","text":"The appendix is a narrow dead-end pocket off the main gut tube. Researchers think that when illness flushes out the gut, useful bacteria hiding in that pocket are not swept away and can restock the gut after recovery.","label":"Worked out, not seen directly"}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Alexis St Martin","Got shot and had an open stomach wound"],["corn","Helps you time your own digestion"],["small intestine","Narrow, not short"],["liver","Grows back to full size"],["appendix","May hide good bacteria and restock the gut"],["William Beaumont","Lowered food on a string into the wound"]]},"picgame":{"type":"catch","title":"Food Breakers","goal":"Catch what helps break food down.","items":[{"icon":"💧","name":"spit","good":true},{"icon":"🧪","name":"stomach acid","good":true},{"icon":"🦠","name":"bacteria","good":true},{"icon":"🧸","name":"teddy","good":false},{"icon":"🎈","name":"balloon","good":false},{"icon":"🪑","name":"chair","good":false},{"icon":"🧱","name":"brick","good":false}]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"How did a doctor named William Beaumont first learn how digestion actually works, in the 1820s?","options":["He lowered food on a string into a hole in a patient's stomach that never fully healed after a gunshot wound","He studied cadavers to observe stomach tissue","He experimented on animals and compared results to humans"],"answer":0,"explain":"Over 250 experiments on Alexis St. Martin showed that a stomach works by chemistry, not grinding."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why can astronauts eat and drink normally while floating in zero gravity?","options":["Swallowing works by muscles squeezing food along in a wave, not by gravity pulling it down","Food is specially processed to float directly into the stomach","Their stomachs adapt to function without gravity"],"answer":0,"explain":"That wave, called peristalsis, means you could technically swallow upside down."},{"type":"tf","prompt":"A rumbling stomach is a signal from your body that it urgently needs food.","answer":1,"explain":"About every 90 minutes, a cleanup wave sweeps through your gut clearing out leftovers — the rumble is just gas and liquid being pushed along, whether or not you're hungry."}]},{"id":"body-skin","course":"body","kind":"facts","section":"skin","title":"Skin, Hair & Nails","glyph":"✋","n":13,"idea":"Your skin is waterproof in both directions, and your fingers wrinkle in water, maybe to grip better. The skin over your elbow barely notices a pinch.","pages":[{"icon":"📎","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Your skin is not equally sensitive everywhere."},{"t":"try","text":"Bend a paperclip into a U with the ends about half an inch (1 cm) apart. Touch both ends to your fingertip — you should feel two points. Now do the same on your forearm or back and it feels like one point because fewer touch sensors are packed in there."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why does it feel like one point on your forearm?","options":["Fewer touch sensors are packed in there","Your skin there is too thick to feel it well","Nerves in your arm carry signals more slowly"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the box that tells you what to do. Its last sentence says why the forearm feels only one point.","explain":"Fingertips have far more touch sensors packed in, so they can feel each point clearly."}}]},{"icon":"🏠","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Skin is your largest organ."},{"t":"fact","text":"You shed skin cells constantly — some of the dust in your home is made up of these cells. The whole outer layer of your skin is replaced about once a month."},{"t":"term","word":"organ","text":"A part of your body that does a specific job, like your skin protecting you."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"About how often is your outer skin replaced?","options":["About every two days","About once a month","Roughly once a year"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It gives the replacement time.","explain":"Your outer layer of skin is replaced about once a month."}}]},{"icon":"🛁","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Your fingers wrinkle when they stay in the bath for a while."},{"t":"fact","text":"Your nervous system triggers tiny blood vessels under the skin to narrow, pulling the surface into ridges.","label":"Worked out, not seen directly"},{"t":"p","text":"These wrinkles are like the tread on a tire. They may help you grip wet things better."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What makes your fingers wrinkle in the bath?","options":["Water soaks into the outer layer and swells it into folds","Bath soap dries the oils out and shrinks your skin tight","Nerves narrow tiny blood vessels and pull the skin into ridges"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It explains what happens under the skin.","explain":"Your nervous system triggers the narrowing of tiny blood vessels, pulling the surface into gripping ridges."}}]},{"icon":"🐈","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"When you get goosebumps, your tiny muscles pull each hair straight up."},{"t":"fact","text":"The same reflex puffs a cat's coat right up, trapping warm air and making the cat look bigger.","label":"Worked out, not seen directly"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does each tiny goosebump muscle pull?","options":["A hair upright","The skin tight","The blood vessels narrow"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the first sentence on this page. It tells you what happens to the hair.","explain":"Each muscle pulls a hair upright, and that makes a small bump in the skin."}}]},{"icon":"😖","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Itching has its own special nerve pathway in your body, separate from the one that feels pain."},{"t":"fact","text":"Scratching works, but only briefly. It floods the spot with a different sensation, drowning out the itch signal for a moment."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why does scratching help only briefly?","options":["It switches off the nerve that carries the itch so it cannot send signals for a while","It floods the spot with a different feeling that drowns out the itch for a moment","It heals the skin underneath right away and takes away what caused the itch"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It explains what happens when you scratch.","explain":"Scratching brings a different feeling that temporarily blocks the itch."}}]},{"icon":"🪒","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Many people believe that shaving makes hair grow back thicker, darker and faster."},{"t":"fact","text":"Shaving does not make hair grow back thicker, darker or faster. Mildred Trotter tested it in 1928, and every careful study since has found the same result."},{"t":"p","text":"An uncut hair tapers to a fine soft point, but shaving cuts it flat. So what you feel next is the blunt end, which feels stubbly and looks darker because it catches the light differently."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Does shaving make hair grow back thicker?","options":["Yes, it makes it darker too","Yes, it grows faster","No"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the first sentence in the fact box. It clearly states what happens when you shave.","explain":"Shaving does not change how thick or dark the hair grows. It just cuts off the soft tip, leaving a blunt end that feels stubbly."}}]},{"icon":"🧢","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"You do not lose most of your heat through your head. Your head is about 7 percent of your skin and loses roughly that share of your heat."},{"t":"fact","text":"In an old cold-weather test, volunteers wore arctic suits with just their heads left bare. Of course the head lost more heat — it was the only part uncovered."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why did the head seem to lose most of the heat in the old test?","options":["It is larger than a tenth of your skin","It has special heat-loss properties","It was the only part left uncovered"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It explains what happened in the test.","explain":"The head seemed to lose more heat because it was the only part left bare in the arctic suits."}}]},{"icon":"😅","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Sweat itself smells of almost nothing. The smell actually comes from bacteria living on your skin."},{"t":"fact","text":"These bacteria feed on what the sweat glands release and give off the smelly chemicals themselves."}]},{"icon":"🦴","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"There is a spot on your body where a hard pinch hurts much less."},{"t":"fact","text":"Pinch the loose skin over the point of your elbow firmly, but not hard enough to hurt, then pinch the same amount of skin on your forearm. The elbow skin is thicker, fattier and much shorter on pain endings, so a pinch there hurts much less."},{"t":"try","text":"Pinch the skin over your elbow and then your forearm to feel the difference."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why does a hard pinch on your elbow skin hurt so little?","options":["The skin there is thicker and has fewer pain endings","The skin there is thinner than anywhere else on your arm","There are no nerves under the skin at your elbow"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It says what the elbow skin is like.","explain":"The elbow skin is thicker, fattier and has fewer pain endings, so a pinch there hurts much less."}}]},{"icon":"🧱","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Your skin is nearly waterproof both ways at once."},{"t":"fact","text":"Bath water stays out, and your own moisture stays in. The outer layer is dead flat cells stacked like bricks in greasy mortar.","label":"How it works"}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Finger wrinkle","Helps you grip wet things better"],["Tiny blood vessels","Narrow to make your fingers wrinkle"],["Sweat bacteria","Feed on what the sweat glands release"],["Elbow skin","Thicker, fattier and has fewer pain endings"],["Head heat loss","About 7 percent of your total heat loss"],["Outer skin layer","Dead flat cells stacked like bricks in greasy mortar"]]},"picgame":{"type":"sort","title":"Waterproof Skin","goal":"Does your skin keep it out or keep it in?","bins":[{"icon":"🛡️","label":"keeps out"},{"icon":"🔒","label":"keeps in"}],"items":[{"icon":"🛁","name":"bath water","bin":0},{"icon":"🌧️","name":"rain","bin":0},{"icon":"🌊","name":"sea","bin":0},{"icon":"💧","name":"moisture","bin":1},{"icon":"🩸","name":"blood","bin":1},{"icon":"🫀","name":"heart","bin":1}]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why do your fingers actually wrinkle in the bath?","options":["Your nervous system deliberately narrows blood vessels to pull the skin into ridges that grip better wet","Water physically soaks into and swells the skin","Soap chemically reacts with the outer skin layer"],"answer":0,"explain":"Nobody has caught the ridges doing their job directly, but the tire-tread pattern is strong evidence they help with grip."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"A 1928 study, and every careful one since, tested a common claim about shaving. What did they find?","options":["Shaving does not make hair grow back thicker, darker, or faster","Shaving actually slows hair growth over time","Shaving makes hair grow back finer"],"answer":0,"explain":"A shaved hair just gets cut flat instead of tapering to a point — that blunt end feels and looks different, but it's the same hair."},{"type":"tf","prompt":"You lose most of your body heat through your head, which is why it's especially important to cover it in cold weather.","answer":1,"explain":"Your head is about a tenth of your skin and loses about a tenth of your heat — the myth traces back to a test where only volunteers' heads were left uncovered."}]},{"id":"body-defence","course":"body","kind":"facts","section":"defence","title":"Germ Fighters","glyph":"🛡️","n":12,"idea":"Your body deliberately turns up the heat when you have a fever. Find out why your immune system raises your temperature and how it fights off germs.","pages":[{"icon":"🌡️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"When you have a fever, your body does it on purpose."},{"t":"fact","text":"A fever is not the illness itself. It is your body deliberately turning up the heat."},{"t":"p","text":"Many germs breed best at your normal temperature, and your immune cells work faster when it gets warmer."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why does your body turn up the heat?","options":["Heat dries out the germs completely and your blood cells need warmth to carry oxygen","Many germs breed best at normal temperature and immune cells work faster when warm","Extra warmth helps your muscles heal faster and lets you sweat the germs out"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the last sentence. It explains why your body raises the temperature.","explain":"Your body raises the temperature to slow down germ breeding and speed up immune cell activity."}}]},{"icon":"🛡️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"White blood cells are like tiny hunters in your body."},{"t":"term","word":"phagocytosis","text":"The process where a cell flows around an invader, engulfs it, and breaks it apart inside. It literally means \"cell eating.\""},{"t":"fact","text":"Some white blood cells chase bacteria down and swallow them whole by flowing around them. This is called phagocytosis."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does phagocytosis mean?","options":["Cell splitting","Cell copying","Cell eating"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the term definition. It explains what phagocytosis means.","explain":"Phagocytosis literally means \"cell eating.\" The cell flows around an invader, engulfs it, and breaks it apart inside."}}]},{"icon":"💧","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Pus is mostly made of white blood cells that died doing their job, mixed with the germs they killed and the bits of tissue damaged in the fight."},{"t":"fact","text":"Green snot does not mean you need antibiotics. The color comes from an enzyme inside your own white blood cells."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where does the green color of snot come from?","options":["An enzyme in your own white blood cells","Germs that must be killed with antibiotics","Green dye left over from the food you eat"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the fact box. Its last sentence tells you where the green color comes from.","explain":"The green color is from an enzyme inside your white blood cells, not from bacteria that need antibiotics."}}]},{"icon":"🩹","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A scab is like a temporary lid that your body builds to close up a cut."},{"t":"fact","text":"Tiny cells called platelets clump at the tear and help lay down a mesh of fibers. Blood cells get caught in it, and the whole plug dries hard while new skin builds underneath."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What builds a scab?","options":["Platelets clumping and laying down a mesh of fibers","White blood cells swarming in and drying out the wound","Sweat drying and hardening over the cut like a crust"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It tells you what platelets do.","explain":"Platelets clump at the tear, lay down fibers, and catch blood cells to form a hard plug."}}]},{"icon":"🤧","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Most people cannot keep their eyes open when they sneeze."},{"t":"fact","text":"The eye-closing is a reflex bundled with the sneeze. Nobody has found a good reason for it — your eyes would not pop out, despite the playground legend."},{"t":"p","text":"Your immune system fights germs, but sometimes it mistakes harmless things like pollen or peanuts for threats."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is an allergy?","options":["A germ that lives in the air and makes people sneeze","The immune system attacking something harmless by mistake","A sickness you catch from touching a sick animal's fur"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the last paragraph. It explains what an allergy is.","explain":"An allergy happens when your immune system mistakes something harmless like pollen or peanuts for a threat and overreacts."}}]},{"icon":"🤢","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Throwing up is a defense. It is your body's way of getting poison out."},{"t":"fact","text":"A small patch at the base of your brain samples your blood for poisons. If it finds one, it empties your stomach without asking you first."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What samples your blood for poisons?","options":["A thin valve at the bottom of your stomach","A pair of small glands above your kidneys","A small patch at the base of your brain"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It explains where the sample happens.","explain":"The small patch at the base of your brain checks your blood for poisons and makes you sick if it finds one."}}]},{"icon":"🫳","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"You can feel your immune system's inspection points right under your jaw."},{"t":"fact","text":"Press gently under your jaw, just behind the corner, and roll your fingers around. Small firm beads move under the skin: lymph nodes. Fluid from your head passes through them to be checked, and they swell when you are fighting something off."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What are the small firm beads under your jaw?","options":["Lymph nodes","Hair roots","Muscles"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It explains what lymph nodes are.","explain":"The small firm beads you feel are lymph nodes, which check fluid from your head and swell when you are fighting off something."}}]},{"icon":"👃","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Alexander Fleming had a cold one day, and he sneezed into a dish of bacteria."},{"t":"fact","text":"Where the drop from his nose sat, the bacteria dissolved. He had stumbled on a germ-killer that your tears, spit, and snot make all the time. He named it lysozyme in 1922."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What killed the bacteria Fleming saw?","options":["Mold blown in from outside","Lysozyme from his own nose","Strong soap from his own sink"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It names the germ-killer.","explain":"Lysozyme, a germ-killer in your tears, spit, and snot, is what dissolved the bacteria."}}]},{"icon":"💉","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Milkmaids who had caught cowpox seemed never to catch smallpox."},{"t":"fact","text":"In 1796, Edward Jenner scratched cowpox fluid into the arm of an eight-year-old named James Phipps. Later, he exposed Phipps to smallpox, and Phipps did not get ill. The word vaccine comes from vacca, the Latin for cow."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where does the word vaccine come from?","options":["The name of the doctor who invented it","The name of the first patient","Vacca, the Latin for cow"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the last sentence on the page. It tells you where the word comes from.","explain":"The word vaccine comes from vacca, which is Latin for cow."}}]},{"icon":"🚪","blocks":[{"t":"try","text":"Open wide in front of a mirror and look at the two lumps at the back on either side."},{"t":"fact","text":"Air and food both enter through the same doorway, and a ring of immune tissue is stationed exactly there as guard posts. Your tonsils are part of it, constantly sampling what you breathe and swallow."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Fever","Body's way to slow down germs"],["Phagocytosis","White blood cells swallowing invaders"],["Pus","Dead white blood cells mixed with germs"],["Scab","Platelets forming a hard plug"],["Lysozyme","Germ-killer in your snot"],["Lymph nodes","Small firm beads under your jaw"]]},"picgame":{"type":"sort","title":"Body or Doctor","goal":"Does your body do it, or a doctor?","bins":[{"icon":"🛡️","label":"your body"},{"icon":"🏥","label":"doctor"}],"items":[{"icon":"🌡️","name":"fever","bin":0},{"icon":"💧","name":"tears","bin":0},{"icon":"🤢","name":"throw up","bin":0},{"icon":"👃","name":"snot","bin":0},{"icon":"💉","name":"vaccine","bin":1},{"icon":"💊","name":"pill","bin":1}]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why does your body deliberately raise its own temperature when you get a fever?","options":["Many germs grow best at normal body temperature, and your immune cells work faster when it's warmer","It's an uncontrolled side effect of fighting infection","Fever helps you sweat out the infection faster"],"answer":0,"explain":"A fever is your body turning up the heat on purpose, not damage being done."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does green snot actually indicate about an illness?","options":["Nothing specific — the color comes from an enzyme in white blood cells, whether it's a virus or bacteria","That antibiotics are definitely needed","That the infection has become more serious"],"answer":0,"explain":"Green snot shows up regardless of whether the cause is viral or bacterial."},{"type":"tf","prompt":"Alexander Fleming discovered a germ-killer in his own tears years before he discovered penicillin.","answer":0,"explain":"He named it lysozyme in 1922, six years before the mold that led to penicillin — he had volunteers squeeze lemon peel to make their eyes water for testing."}]},{"id":"body-sleep","course":"body","kind":"facts","section":"sleep","title":"Sleep & Dreams","glyph":"😴","n":9,"idea":"Michel Siffre's day in the cave was slightly longer than 24 hours. Find out how your body clock uses light to keep time.","pages":[{"icon":"🕳️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Michel Siffre spent two months alone in a cave with no clock and no sunlight."},{"t":"fact","text":"His sleep-and-wake rhythm settled into a day slightly longer than 24 hours."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How long was the day his body settled into?","options":["Exactly 24 hours","Slightly longer than 24 hours","Much shorter than 24 hours"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It tells you how his day changed.","explain":"His rhythm settled into a day slightly longer than 24 hours."}}]},{"icon":"☀️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Light sets your body clock, but not through the cells you see with."},{"t":"fact","text":"A separate set of cells sits at the back of your eye. Their main job is to report how bright it is. They report straight to a small clump of cells in the middle of your brain that keeps the schedule."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which cells tell your body clock about light?","options":["The same cells that you use to see colors and shapes in daytime","Small cells in your skin that feel the warmth of the sunlight","A separate set at the back of the eye that only report brightness"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It tells you where those cells are.","explain":"Those cells at the back of your eye only report how bright it is to your body clock."}}]},{"icon":"🧸","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Newborns sleep a lot, but not all at once."},{"t":"fact","text":"A newborn sleeps 14 to 17 hours out of every 24, in scraps of two or three hours at a time. By the time you are a teenager, you need 8 to 10 hours in one block."},{"t":"p","text":"The amount of sleep you need drops as you grow, and most adults settle at about 7 to 9 hours."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How much sleep does a teenager need?","options":["14 to 17 hours in scraps","6 to 8 hours in scraps","8 to 10 hours in one block"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the fact box. Its last sentence gives the number of hours a teenager needs.","explain":"A teenager needs 8 to 10 hours all at once, not in small pieces like a newborn does."}}]},{"icon":"🌙","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Melatonin is the hormone that tells your body it is night."},{"t":"term","word":"hormone","text":"A chemical messenger that travels in your blood and tells parts of your body what to do."},{"t":"fact","text":"In a teenager, melatonin's evening rise arrives one to two hours later than in a younger child. So a teenager who is still wide awake at eleven is following a clock, not dodging one."}]},{"icon":"🧠","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Scientists have looked for a single reason why we sleep, but they do not have one yet."},{"t":"fact","text":"The strongest evidence points to memory. The patterns your brain fired while learning get replayed overnight, and people tested after a night's sleep beat people tested after the same gap awake.","label":"The strongest evidence"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does the strongest evidence say sleep is for?","options":["Memory","Digesting food","Staying warm"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It talks about replaying patterns from the day.","explain":"The strongest evidence points to memory. Your brain replays what you learned during the day while you sleep."}}]},{"icon":"🧼","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Overnight, your brain seems to clean itself."},{"t":"fact","text":"In mice, brain cells shrink back, the gaps between them widen, and fluid washes through carrying off the day's waste. The flow is far stronger when you are asleep than it ever is when you are awake.","label":"Worked out, not seen directly"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did scientists see happening in the brains of sleeping mice?","options":["New brain cells grow in and replace the tired ones","Fluid washes through and carries off the day's waste","Blood stops flowing and lets the brain rest completely"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It describes how the fluid washes through the wider gaps.","explain":"The fluid washes through and carries off the day's waste, which is far stronger when you are asleep."}}]},{"icon":"👀","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Roughly every 90 minutes, your eyes start darting about under closed lids."},{"t":"fact","text":"During these times, your brain runs almost as hard as when you are awake. Most dreaming happens then, about two hours a night, and your muscles are switched off so you cannot act it out."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"About how often do your eyes start darting in sleep?","options":["Roughly every 90 minutes","Roughly every 20 minutes","Only once, just before dawn"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the first sentence on the page. It gives the time.","explain":"Your eyes start darting roughly every 90 minutes during sleep."}}]},{"icon":"🥄","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Falling asleep for just fifteen seconds can help you solve a problem you could not crack awake."},{"t":"fact","text":"Researchers gave volunteers a math puzzle with a hidden shortcut. Those who dozed into shallow sleep spotted the shortcut 83% of the time, while those who stayed awake only managed 30%."},{"t":"try","text":"Try the spoon nap. Sit in a chair holding a spoon over a metal tray and let yourself doze off. The moment you drop off, your grip will go and the clatter will wake you."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How often did the shallow sleepers spot the shortcut?","options":["30 percent of the time","83 percent of the time","50 percent of the time"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the second sentence in the fact box. It gives the percentage.","explain":"The shallow sleepers spotted the shortcut 83% of the time."}}]},{"icon":"📖","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Your body needs rest every night, and it will not let you skip it for long."},{"t":"fact","text":"The first week in Genesis has six days of work and a seventh set aside for rest.","label":"The written account says"}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Michel Siffre","Spent two months alone in a cave"],["newborn","Sleeps 14 to 17 hours out of every 24"],["teenager","Needs 8 to 10 hours in one block"],["melatonin","The hormone that tells your body it is night"],["memory","The strongest evidence for why we sleep"]]},"picgame":{"type":"sort","title":"Awake or Asleep","goal":"Does it happen awake or asleep?","bins":[{"icon":"☀️","label":"awake"},{"icon":"🌙","label":"asleep"}],"items":[{"icon":"📚","name":"books","bin":0},{"icon":"🏃","name":"running","bin":0},{"icon":"⚽","name":"ball","bin":0},{"icon":"💭","name":"dream","bin":1},{"icon":"👀","name":"darting eyes","bin":1},{"icon":"🧼","name":"cleaning","bin":1}]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did Michel Siffre discover about his own body clock after spending two months alone in a cave with no daylight?","options":["His sleep-wake rhythm settled into a day slightly longer than 24 hours, but his sense of time drifted badly","His body clock stopped working entirely without sunlight","He needed far less sleep than normal"],"answer":0,"explain":"When told the two months were up, he thought about a month less had passed — his body kept decent time, but his mind lost track completely."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why does a teenager often feel wide awake at 11pm when a younger child would already be asleep?","options":["Melatonin, the hormone signaling nighttime, actually rises one to two hours later in a teenager's body","Teenagers simply have more energy than younger children","It's purely a habit, not a biological difference"],"answer":0,"explain":"A teenager still awake late is following their own body clock, not fighting it."},{"type":"tf","prompt":"Overnight, your brain actually flushes out waste that builds up during the day, more strongly than it does while you're awake.","answer":0,"explain":"This was first traced in mice using dye — among the waste cleared out are proteins linked to dementia when they build up."}]},{"id":"body-cells","course":"body","kind":"facts","section":"cells","title":"Cells & DNA","glyph":"🧬","n":9,"idea":"You are made up of trillions of cells, mostly red blood cells. Find out how long the DNA inside each cell is and why some cells never get replaced.","pages":[{"icon":"🧮","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Nobody has ever counted all the cells in a human body. But scientists worked out an estimate."},{"t":"fact","text":"The best estimate measures the size and packing of about 1,200 kinds of cell across 60 tissues and adds it up. Roughly 36 trillion cells in an adult man, 28 trillion in an adult woman, and about 17 trillion in a ten-year-old.","label":"How we know"},{"t":"p","text":"Most of those cells are red blood cells, which are tiny."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How was the number of cells found?","options":["By measuring the size and packing of the kinds of cell in each tissue and adding it all up","By putting a whole person in a giant scanner that counts every cell one at a time","By reading the number of cells written in a person's DNA"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It explains how the estimate was made.","explain":"The estimate measures the size and packing of about 1,200 kinds of cell across 60 tissues, then adds it all up."}}]},{"icon":"🧵","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Inside almost every one of your cells is something very long. If you stretched it out, it would be about 6 feet (2 m) long."},{"t":"fact","text":"This DNA is spelled out in an alphabet of four letters and is about six billion letters long, three billion from each parent. All of it is folded into a nucleus that is far too small to see with the naked eye."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"About how long is the DNA in one cell?","options":["About 1 foot","About 6 feet","About 60 yards"],"answer":1,"hint":"The length is given right at the start of the page.","explain":"It's about 6 feet of DNA, all coiled up inside each cell."}}]},{"icon":"📏","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Your longest cell runs from the base of your spine all the way down to your big toe. That's over 3 feet of one single cell."},{"t":"fact","text":"At the other end of the scale, you could line about ten red blood cells side by side across the width of one hair from your head."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How long is your longest cell?","options":["About 1 foot","Over 3 feet","About 1 inch"],"answer":1,"hint":"The length is given right at the start of the page.","explain":"It runs over 3 feet from the base of your spine to your big toe."}}]},{"icon":"♻️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Every day, you throw away and rebuild around 330 billion cells."},{"t":"fact","text":"The lining of your gut is the quickest to swap out, changing its whole surface every four or five days."},{"t":"p","text":"Red blood cells stick around for about four months before they need replacing."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which swaps faster, the gut lining or red blood cells?","options":["Red blood cells","Both at the same rate","The gut lining"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It gives how often the gut lining changes.","explain":"The gut lining swaps its whole surface every four or five days, much faster than red blood cells which last about four months."}}]},{"icon":"💧","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"You are made up of about 60 percent water. Your brain and heart are 73 percent water, your lungs are 83 percent, your skin is 64 percent, and even your bones are 31 percent water."},{"t":"fact","text":"Babies are the wettest of all, coming in at roughly 78 percent water at birth. By their first birthday, they dry out to about 65 percent."},{"t":"p","text":"Small children use up and replace a bigger share of their body water each day than adults do, so they need to drink often."}]},{"icon":"👁️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Some of your cells go their whole lives without being replaced."},{"t":"fact","text":"The cells in the middle of each eye's lens formed before you were born and are never swapped out. They have to stay perfectly see-through for a whole lifetime."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why are the cells in the middle of your eye's lens never swapped out?","options":["They have to stay perfectly see-through for a whole lifetime","They are made too tough for anything in the body to break them down","They are kept safe by the bone around the eye and never get hurt"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It explains why these cells must remain unchanged.","explain":"These cells need to stay clear so you can see clearly."}}]},{"icon":"🔋","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Inside most of your cells are hundreds of tiny power plants. They are called mitochondria."},{"t":"term","word":"mitochondria","text":"The little power plants inside your cells that turn food and oxygen into energy."},{"t":"fact","text":"A heart muscle cell crams in thousands of them, filling about a third of the cell. They never get a night off."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What do mitochondria do?","options":["Store extra water and salt that the cell can use","Send out messages and warnings that other cells can read","Turn food and oxygen into energy the cell can use"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the term box. It tells you what mitochondria do.","explain":"Mitochondria turn food and oxygen into energy that the cell can use."}}]},{"icon":"😤","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Hold your breath for a moment. You will soon feel a desperate need to gasp."},{"t":"fact","text":"That urge is mostly from carbon dioxide piling up in your blood, not from running out of oxygen."},{"t":"try","text":"Breathe in and hold it, counting how long you can last (no deep breathing first, and stop if you feel dizzy). Then breathe all the way out and hold that, counting again. The empty one is far shorter because your lungs have less air to spare, so the carbon dioxide builds up faster."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why do you feel the need to gasp when you hold your breath?","options":["Waste gas is piling up","You are running out of oxygen","Your lungs are getting tired"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the first sentence of the fact box. It tells you why you need to gasp.","explain":"The urge to gasp comes from waste gas building up in your blood."}}]},{"icon":"📖","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Almost every cell in your body holds a full instruction book."},{"t":"fact","text":"Each cell reads only its own chapter. A skin cell has the directions for building a heart but never opens them. Tiny machines inside the cell copy the code and check its spelling.","label":"How it works"}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["36 trillion","The estimated number of cells in an adult man"],["6 feet","The length of DNA coiled up in one cell"],["mitochondria","Turn food and oxygen into energy"],["78 percent","A baby's water content at birth"],["lens","Has cells that form before birth and are never replaced"],["gut lining","Swaps its whole surface every four or five days"]]},"picgame":{"type":"tapall","title":"Cell Power","goal":"Tap what your cells use to make energy.","items":[{"icon":"🍎","name":"apple","good":true},{"icon":"🥕","name":"carrot","good":true},{"icon":"🍞","name":"bread","good":true},{"icon":"💨","name":"air","good":true},{"icon":"🪨","name":"stone","good":false},{"icon":"🧱","name":"brick","good":false},{"icon":"🪵","name":"wood","good":false},{"icon":"🧸","name":"teddy","good":false}]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why does a skin cell hold the complete instructions for building a heart, but never use them?","options":["Every cell carries the whole genetic instruction book but only reads its own relevant chapter","Only certain specialized cells carry the full instructions","Heart-building instructions are deleted from skin cells before birth"],"answer":0,"explain":"A code that stores, copies, and checks its own spelling reads like something written, not something that happened by accident."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which of your cells is actually the longest, stretching over 3 feet?","options":["A single nerve cell running from the base of your spine to your big toe","A blood vessel cell in your leg","A muscle fiber in your thigh"],"answer":0,"explain":"At the other extreme, you could line up about ten red blood cells across the width of a single hair."},{"type":"tf","prompt":"When you hold your breath, the urge to gasp comes mainly from your body running out of oxygen.","answer":1,"explain":"It's mostly your body reacting to waste gas building up, not oxygen running low."}]},{"id":"body-voice","course":"body","kind":"facts","section":"voice","title":"Voice & Sound","glyph":"🗣️","n":9,"idea":"You can whisper without using your voice. Newborns cry in the tune of the language they heard before they were born.","pages":[{"icon":"🤫","blocks":[{"t":"fact","text":"A whisper does not use your voice at all. The vocal flaps in your throat hold still, and you shape the hiss of moving air instead."},{"t":"try","text":"Rest two fingers lightly on the front of your throat and say your name out loud. You will feel a clear buzz. Now whisper it."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What stops when you whisper?","options":["The movement of your lips","The buzz of your vocal flaps","The air coming out of your mouth"],"answer":1,"hint":"Try the finger test on your throat and notice what you feel when you whisper.","explain":"Your vocal flaps hold still when you whisper, so there is no buzz."}}]},{"icon":"🎺","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Your voice is two small flaps in your throat slapping together. For a child, they slap about 285 times a second."},{"t":"term","word":"slaps","text":"The quick movements of the flaps in your throat that make sound."},{"t":"fact","text":"A woman's voice runs around 250 slaps per second, while a man's is around 145. The lower the voice, the slower the flaps."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What makes a voice sound lower?","options":["Its flaps slap more slowly","Its flaps are smaller and lighter","Its flaps are closer together"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It tells you that lower voices have slower slaps.","explain":"The slower the flaps slap, the lower the sound."}}]},{"icon":"📈","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"During puberty, your vocal flaps change a lot."},{"t":"fact","text":"A boy's vocal flaps can grow up to 60% longer and thicker. A girl's grow by about a third. Bigger, heavier flaps swing more slowly, so the voice drops."},{"t":"p","text":"The cracking in a boy's voice is probably because the equipment changes size faster than the brain can adjust its aim."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why does the voice drop in puberty?","options":["The smaller, tighter flaps swing more slowly","The flaps move up and down much more quickly","The bigger, heavier flaps swing more slowly"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It explains that bigger, heavier flaps swing more slowly.","explain":"Bigger and heavier flaps cause the voice to drop because they move more slowly."}}]},{"icon":"👶","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Newborns cry with an accent, even before they can speak."},{"t":"fact","text":"Recordings made in the first days show that French babies' cries mostly rise in pitch, while German babies' cries mostly fall. Each matches the tune of the language they heard in the womb."},{"t":"p","text":"Researchers recorded 1,254 cries from thirty French and thirty German newborns. None of them could babble yet, but the tune of their language was already there."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How do French newborns' cries mostly move?","options":["They stay at one pitch","They mostly rise in pitch","They sound like high-pitched laughter"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It explains that French babies' cries mostly rise in pitch.","explain":"French babies' cries mostly rise in pitch to match the tune of the language they heard from inside."}}]},{"icon":"😴","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Yawning is catching, but nobody has settled why."},{"t":"fact","text":"Cooling the brain, sharpening attention, and keeping a group in step have all been argued for, but none has enough evidence to close the case.","label":"Still argued"},{"t":"try","text":"Yawn deliberately in front of someone who is reading, and count how long until they yawn back. It works better on people who know you well."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Do we know why yawning is catching?","options":["No, it is not settled","Yes, it sharpens attention","Yes, it cools the brain"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the Still argued fact box. It says none of the arguments have enough evidence to close the case.","explain":"Nobody has settled why yawning is catching. The reasons are still being argued about."}}]},{"icon":"😂","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Laughing is mostly not about jokes. Researchers recorded real conversations and counted."},{"t":"fact","text":"People were around 30 times likelier to laugh with company than alone, and only about one line in eight that got a laugh was even remotely funny."},{"t":"p","text":"The speaker usually laughs more than the audience does, dropping laughs into their own sentences like punctuation."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Who laughs more, the speaker or the audience?","options":["The audience","A dog watching the conversation","Nobody laughs","The speaker"],"answer":3,"hint":"Look at the last paragraph. It says the speaker drops laughs into their own sentences.","explain":"The speaker usually laughs more than the audience."}}]},{"icon":"📢","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The loudest shout Guinness has measured is 122.4 decibels, set by Joseph McGrail-Bateup shouting the word 'now'."},{"t":"fact","text":"It left him hoarse for days. The loudest scream on record is even louder at 129 decibels, and that belongs to Jill Drake."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What word did Joseph McGrail-Bateup shout to set the record?","options":["Quiet","Loud","Now"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the first sentence. It says he shouted one word.","explain":"He shouted 'now' to reach 122.4 decibels."}}]},{"icon":"🐷","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Have you ever had a case of hiccups that just wouldn't stop? One way to stop them is to make your diaphragm pull hard while you swallow."},{"t":"fact","text":"A stiff straw built to force exactly that stopped hiccups for nearly 92% of the people who tried it in one survey of its users."},{"t":"p","text":"An American farmer named Charles Osborne started hiccupping while trying to lift a hog. He did not stop for 68 years."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How long did Charles Osborne hiccup?","options":["1 day","68 years","1 week"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the last sentence on the page. It tells you how long he hiccupped.","explain":"Charles Osborne hiccupped for 68 years, which is the longest bout on record."}}]},{"icon":"🗣️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"In Genesis, one of a man's early jobs involves using his voice."},{"t":"fact","text":"In Genesis, after the man is put in the garden to work it and keep it, he is given the job of naming the animals one at a time. Speech turns up very early in that account.","label":"The written account says"}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Whisper","Does not use vocal flaps"],["Puberty","Voice drops as flaps grow bigger"],["French newborns","Cries mostly rise in pitch"],["Yawning","Still a mystery why it's catching"],["Speaker","Laughs more than the audience"],["Joseph McGrail-Bateup","Shouted 'now' to set the record"]]},"picgame":{"type":"catch","title":"Buzzing Throat","goal":"Catch what makes your throat buzz.","items":[{"icon":"😂","name":"laughing","good":true},{"icon":"👶","name":"crying baby","good":true},{"icon":"📢","name":"shouting","good":true},{"icon":"🗣️","name":"talking","good":true},{"icon":"🤫","name":"whisper","good":false},{"icon":"👂","name":"ear","good":false},{"icon":"🦶","name":"foot","good":false},{"icon":"👁️","name":"eye","good":false}]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What actually produces the sound of your voice?","options":["Two small flaps in your throat slapping together as air passes through","Air vibrating directly against your teeth and lips","A single vocal cord stretching and contracting"],"answer":0,"explain":"A child's flaps slap about 285 times a second; an adult man's only about 145 — longer, heavier flaps swing more slowly, making a deeper voice."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"A study recording newborn babies' cries in their first days found something surprising. What was it?","options":["French and German babies' cries followed different pitch patterns, matching the language they'd heard in the womb","All babies cry with the exact same pitch pattern worldwide","Babies' cries have no pitch pattern until they start babbling"],"answer":0,"explain":"French babies' cries mostly rose in pitch; German babies' mostly fell — the tune of language arrived before any words could be spoken."},{"type":"tf","prompt":"A whisper is just a very quiet version of your normal voice, using the same vocal flaps.","answer":1,"explain":"In a whisper, the vocal flaps actually hold still — you're shaping the hiss of moving air instead."}]},{"id":"body-hands","course":"body","kind":"facts","section":"hands","title":"Hands & Grip","glyph":"✋","n":9,"idea":"Your ring finger is tied to your middle finger's tendon. Find out how much your little finger adds to your grip strength.","pages":[{"icon":"🤏","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"There are no muscles inside your fingers."},{"t":"fact","text":"Every bend and straighten is worked from a distance, by muscles in your palm and forearm hauling on cords called tendons that run down inside each finger."},{"t":"try","text":"Wrap your left hand around your right forearm just below the elbow and wiggle your right fingers. You can feel the muscles doing the job several inches away from the fingers that are actually moving."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where are the muscles that move your fingers?","options":["In your palm and forearm","Inside each of your fingers","In your shoulder and chest"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at what you can feel when you wrap your hand around your forearm and wiggle your fingers.","explain":"The muscles in your palm and forearm do all the work."}}]},{"icon":"🔒","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"One of your fingers cannot move on its own, and it is not the one you would guess."},{"t":"fact","text":"Put your palm flat on a table and fold your middle finger under, knuckle down on the wood. Now lift each finger in turn. The thumb, index, and little finger all come up easily. But the ring finger will not budge because its tendon is tied to the middle finger's."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which finger will not lift when your middle finger is folded down?","options":["The thumb","The index finger","The ring finger"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at what happens when you try to lift each finger after folding the middle finger down.","explain":"The ring finger's tendon is tied to the middle finger's, so it will not budge."}}]},{"icon":"🖐️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Your little finger is not just for looks. It actually adds a lot to your grip strength."},{"t":"fact","text":"Measured on a grip meter, leaving out the little finger costs about a third of your grip strength."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does leaving out your little finger cost?","options":["About a tenth of your grip strength","About a third of your grip strength","About two-thirds of your grip strength"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the first sentence in the fact box.","explain":"Your little finger adds quite a bit to your grip strength. Leaving it out costs about a third."}}]},{"icon":"👆","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Your fingerprints were finished before you were born."},{"t":"fact","text":"They buckle into place between about the tenth and sixteenth week in the womb. Once they form, those ridges never change. Even identical twins, with the same DNA, have different prints."},{"t":"try","text":"Scribble a thick patch of pencil on paper, rub your fingertip in it, press clear tape onto the finger, and stick the tape down on white paper. You have just lifted your own print."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"When do fingerprint ridges form?","options":["In the womb, between about the tenth and sixteenth week","Right after birth, during your first few days of life","Slowly over the first few years of your childhood"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the first sentence in the fact box.","explain":"Your fingerprints form in the womb, between about the tenth and sixteenth week."}}]},{"icon":"💧","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Put your fingers in warm water for a while, and they get all wrinkly."},{"t":"fact","text":"A finger whose nerve has been cut will not wrinkle in water. Doctors use that as a test: warm water for half an hour, and any fingertip that stays smooth has a nerve problem."},{"t":"p","text":"Photograph a wrinkled finger, dry it off, and soak it again a day later. The loops and ridges land in the same places each time."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does a finger that stays smooth in water tell a doctor?","options":["There is a bone problem","There is a nerve problem","There is a skin problem"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the second sentence in the fact box.","explain":"A finger that stays smooth in warm water for half an hour suggests there is a nerve problem."}}]},{"icon":"🗺️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Your brain has a map of your body, but it is wildly out of scale."},{"t":"fact","text":"The hands take up more room on that map than your whole trunk and both legs together. It is drawn by how much control a part needs, not by how big the part is."},{"t":"p","text":"A surgeon named Wilder Penfield mapped it during operations with awake patients. He touched the surface of the brain with a fine wire and asked what they felt or watched which part of them twitched."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What takes up more of the brain's body map, the hands or the trunk and legs together?","options":["The trunk and legs together","The feet","The back and belly","The hands"],"answer":3,"hint":"Look at the first sentence in the fact box.","explain":"The hands take up more room on the brain's map because they need more control."}}]},{"icon":"🔨","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Most ways you hold something are one of two grips. A surgeon, John Napier, worked that out in 1956."},{"t":"term","word":"precision grip","text":"When you pinch something between your thumb and fingertips, like holding a pencil."},{"t":"fact","text":"In a power grip, your fingers wrap around and your palm joins in, like using a hammer. In a precision grip, you pinch with your thumb and fingertips, like holding a pencil."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which grip do you use for a pencil?","options":["A power grip","Both grips","A precision grip"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the term box. It explains what a precision grip is.","explain":"You use a precision grip to hold a pencil, pinching it between your thumb and fingertips."}}]},{"icon":"💅","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Fingernails grow about 0.14 inches a month, roughly twice as fast as toenails, which grow about 0.06 inches a month."},{"t":"fact","text":"Your little fingernail is the slowest of the ten."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which fingernail grows the slowest?","options":["The ring finger's","The little finger's","The middle finger's"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the second sentence. It tells you which nail grows the slowest.","explain":"Your little fingernail is the slowest of the ten."}}]},{"icon":"👍","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Your thumb can reach around and touch the tip of every other finger."},{"t":"fact","text":"Because of that one trick, your hand can grip tools instead of just pushing things around. It lets you thread a needle, hold a pen, or fasten a button."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Precision grip","Pinch something between thumb and fingertips"],["Power grip","Fingers wrap around like using a hammer"],["Wilder Penfield","Mapped the brain’s body map"],["Fingerprints","Form between tenth and sixteenth week in womb"],["Little fingernail","The slowest growing nail"],["Ring finger","Tied to middle finger's tendon"]]},"picgame":{"type":"sort","title":"Pinch or Wrap","goal":"Does your hand pinch it or wrap it?","bins":[{"icon":"🤏","label":"pinch"},{"icon":"✊","label":"wrap"}],"items":[{"icon":"✏️","name":"pencil","bin":0},{"icon":"🪡","name":"needle","bin":0},{"icon":"🖊️","name":"pen","bin":0},{"icon":"🔨","name":"hammer","bin":1},{"icon":"🪓","name":"axe","bin":1},{"icon":"🧹","name":"broom","bin":1}]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"How do your fingers bend and straighten, even though there are no muscles inside the fingers themselves?","options":["Muscles in your palm and forearm pull on tendons that run down inside each finger","Small joints inside each finger bend on their own","Blood pressure changes push the fingers into position"],"answer":0,"explain":"Every finger movement is worked from a distance, by muscles located elsewhere in your hand and arm."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why do identical twins, who share the same DNA, still have different fingerprints?","options":["The ridges form from how skin buckles randomly in the womb, not purely from DNA instructions","Their fingerprints actually do match exactly","Fingerprints change too much after birth to compare"],"answer":0,"explain":"The ridges buckle into place between about weeks 10 and 16 in the womb, and never change after that."},{"type":"tf","prompt":"Leaving your little finger out of your grip barely affects your overall hand strength.","answer":1,"explain":"Measured on a grip meter, leaving out the little finger actually costs about a third of your total grip strength."}]},{"id":"body-heat","course":"body","kind":"facts","section":"heat","title":"Hot & Cold","glyph":"🌡️","n":9,"idea":"You shiver at the start of a fever because your body's thermostat has reset higher. Find out how much of your skin is actually your head and why sweating cools you down.","pages":[{"icon":"🌡️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Have you ever heard that your body temperature should be 98.6°F (37°C)?"},{"t":"fact","text":"98.6°F (37°C) came from one German doctor's readings in 1851. Today, the average is closer to 97.9°F (36.6°C), and your temperature can shift by about a degree over the course of a day.","label":"Where did it come from?"},{"t":"try","text":"Take your temperature first thing in the morning and again around six in the evening. The evening reading is usually higher."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where did the number 98.6 come from?","options":["A big study across many countries","An ancient Chinese text","One German doctor's readings in 1851"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It tells you where it came from.","explain":"The number 98.6°F (37°C) came from one German doctor's readings in 1851."}}]},{"icon":"💦","blocks":[{"t":"fact","text":"Sweat cools you only when it turns into vapor. On a muggy day the air is already full of water, so your sweat has a harder time turning into vapor and cooling you off."},{"t":"try","text":"Wet the back of one hand and leave the other dry, then blow steadily across both. The wet one will feel noticeably colder as the water steals heat from your skin to turn into vapor."},{"t":"term","word":"vapor","text":"The gas form of water, like steam."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"When does sweat cool you?","options":["As it turns into vapor","While it sits on your skin","Just before you start sweating"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the first sentence. It tells you when the cooling happens.","explain":"The cooling happens as sweat turns into vapor and leaves your skin."}}]},{"icon":"🧢","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"You may have heard that you lose 40 percent of your body heat through your head. That is not right."},{"t":"fact","text":"The idea comes from a US Army manual in the 1950s, after tests where people wore arctic suits with only their heads bare. Your head is about 7% of your skin and loses roughly that share."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"About how much of your skin is your head?","options":["Almost all of it","About 7 percent","Less than 1 percent"],"answer":1,"hint":"The fact box gives the percentage of your skin that is your head.","explain":"Your head is about 7% of your skin, so it loses roughly that share of body heat."}}]},{"icon":"🥶","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"When you shiver, your muscles are firing against each other on purpose."},{"t":"fact","text":"Shivering makes heat instead of movement and can lift your heat production to about five times its resting level."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does shivering make?","options":["Sweating","Heat instead of movement","Colder body temperature"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It explains what shivering does.","explain":"Shivering makes heat by firing muscles against each other, not movement."}}]},{"icon":"🤒","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"At the start of a fever you can shiver even though your temperature is going up."},{"t":"fact","text":"The best explanation is that your thermostat has been reset upward, so your ordinary temperature now reads as far too cold. You feel frozen while your body is deliberately running hot.","label":"Worked out, not seen directly"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why do you shiver at the start of a fever?","options":["Your body has stopped making heat and is cooling off too quickly","You have sweated out too much water and salt through your skin","Your thermostat has been reset upward so normal feels too cold"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the worked out fact box. It explains how your thermostat changes.","explain":"Your body's thermostat resets higher, so your normal temperature now feels too cold."}}]},{"icon":"🪶","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Almost every hair on your body has a tiny muscle that yanks it upright, making a goosebump."},{"t":"fact","text":"The same muscle fires for cold, fright, and even music. In mice, it also holds a nerve against the cells that grow new hair."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does the goosebump muscle also hold in place?","options":["A nerve, against the cells that grow new hair","A tiny vein, against the fat under the skin","A sweat gland, against the bone beneath the skin"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It gives the exact function of the muscle.","explain":"In mice, the muscle holds a nerve against the cells that grow new hair."}}]},{"icon":"🧊","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The range of temperatures a person can survive is wider than you might think."},{"t":"fact","text":"Anna Bagenholm was pulled from under river ice with her body temperature at 56.7°F (13.7°C) and no heartbeat, yet she lived. Willie Jones survived heatstroke when his body hit 115.7°F (46.5°C)."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Who was pulled from under river ice and survived?","options":["Willie Jones","Anna Bagenholm","A person in the desert"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the first fact. It names who was found under the ice.","explain":"Anna Bagenholm's body temperature was very low, but she survived."}}]},{"icon":"👶","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Newborns have a trick for staying warm. They do not shiver much, the way adults do."},{"t":"fact","text":"Instead, they carry brown fat across their back and shoulders. This special fat is stuffed with power plants and burns purely to make heat."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What do newborns burn to make heat?","options":["White fat","Muscle","Brown fat"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It mentions what kind of fat newborns have.","explain":"Newborns burn brown fat to keep warm. Most of this fat fades as a child grows."}}]},{"icon":"🌞","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Your sweat glands and thermostat let you keep working through the middle of a hot day, while most animals have to find shade."},{"t":"fact","text":"Genesis puts people in a garden to work it and keep it.","label":"The written account says"}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["German doctor","Readings came from one German doctor"],["vapor","The gas form of water"],["7 percent","About how much of your skin is your head"],["Anna Bagenholm","Pulled from under river ice and survived"],["brown fat","Newborns burn this to make heat"],["goosebump muscle","Holds a nerve against the cells that grow new hair"]]},"picgame":{"type":"sort","title":"Warm or Cool","goal":"Does it make heat or cool you?","bins":[{"icon":"🔥","label":"makes heat"},{"icon":"⛄","label":"cools you"}],"items":[{"icon":"🥶","name":"shivering","bin":0},{"icon":"🤒","name":"fever","bin":0},{"icon":"👶","name":"baby","bin":0},{"icon":"💦","name":"sweat","bin":1},{"icon":"🧊","name":"ice","bin":1},{"icon":"❄️","name":"snow","bin":1}]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"The famous \"98.6°F\" body temperature — is that actually accurate for most people today?","options":["Not quite — that figure came from one 1851 doctor's readings, and the average today measures closer to 97.9°F","Yes, it's still exactly accurate for everyone","It was always wrong and has never matched anyone"],"answer":0,"explain":"Your own temperature also naturally shifts by about a degree over the course of a day."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why doesn't sweat cool you down while it's just sitting on your skin?","options":["The cooling only happens as sweat evaporates into vapor","Sweat needs to soak fully into the skin first","Cooling happens immediately, but too slowly to notice"],"answer":0,"explain":"That's exactly why a muggy, humid day feels so much worse than a dry one — sweat can't evaporate as easily."},{"type":"tf","prompt":"A person has actually survived with a measured core body temperature below 57°F, with no heartbeat.","answer":0,"explain":"Anna Bagenholm was pulled from under river ice at 56.7°F with no heartbeat — and lived."}]},{"id":"body-growing","course":"body","kind":"facts","section":"growing","title":"Growing Up","glyph":"📈","n":13,"idea":"The tallest person ever was over 8 feet tall. Learn why your height changes throughout the day and how bones grow.","pages":[{"icon":"🌙","blocks":[{"t":"fact","text":"Your body makes most of its growth hormone during deep sleep, so sleep matters for growing."},{"t":"fact","text":"You are also measurably taller in the morning than at bedtime. The discs in your spine decompress overnight."},{"t":"try","text":"Measure your height first thing in the morning, then again just before bed. Most people lose up to about half an inch (1 cm) over the day as the cushions between the bones of the spine squash down."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"When are you tallest?","options":["At the end of a long day","First thing in the morning","Right after you eat dinner"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the second fact box. It says when you are measurably taller.","explain":"The discs in your spine decompress overnight, making you taller in the morning."}}]},{"icon":"🦵","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Babies are born without kneecaps, at least not the bony kind."},{"t":"fact","text":"At birth, their kneecaps start as cartilage — the same bendy stuff as the tip of your nose — and harden into bone over several years."},{"t":"term","word":"cartilage","text":"The bendy, flexible material that makes up parts of your body like the tip of your nose."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What are a baby's kneecaps made of at first?","options":["Bone","Skin","Cartilage"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It says they start as cartilage.","explain":"At birth, a baby’s kneecaps are made of cartilage and harden into bone over several years."}}]},{"icon":"🦷","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Take an x-ray of a six-year-old's jaw and you see two sets of teeth, one in place and one still forming."},{"t":"fact","text":"The baby ones are in place, and the adult ones are stacked behind and below, waiting their turn."},{"t":"p","text":"A baby tooth does not get pushed out with its roots intact. The roots dissolve away first, which is why a wobbly tooth comes out as a small hollow cap."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What happens to a baby tooth's roots?","options":["They dissolve away first","They get pushed out whole","They grow longer and stronger"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the second sentence in the last paragraph. It explains how the roots dissolve away.","explain":"The roots of a baby tooth dissolve away first, which is why a wobbly tooth comes out as a small hollow cap."}}]},{"icon":"👟","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"During a growth spurt, the ends grow first. Hands and feet reach their full adult size before the arms and legs."},{"t":"fact","text":"That's why shoes stop fitting before pants do."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which parts of your body finish growing first during a growth spurt?","options":["Hands and feet","Arms and legs","Chest and back"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the first paragraph. It says which parts reach full size first.","explain":"Hands and feet reach their full size before the arms and legs do, so shoes stop fitting first."}}]},{"icon":"🧠","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A newborn's head is about a quarter of its whole length."},{"t":"fact","text":"Yours is nearer an eighth. The head changes least because the brain does most of its growing earliest, and the rest of the body spends years catching up."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How much of a newborn's length is its head?","options":["About an eighth","Half","About a quarter"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the first sentence on this page.","explain":"A newborn's head is about a quarter of its whole length."}}]},{"icon":"😢","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A newborn can cry hard without any tears."},{"t":"fact","text":"At first, the glands make just enough to keep the eyes wet. The flood that runs down cheeks usually does not arrive for several weeks."}]},{"icon":"📏","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Your arm span comes out very close to your height."},{"t":"fact","text":"For most people, the difference is only an inch or two (2-5 cm). Children usually measure a shade narrower than they are tall."},{"t":"try","text":"Stand against a wall with your arms straight out to the sides and have someone mark where your fingertips reach. Measure between those marks and then measure your height."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How does your arm span compare with your height?","options":["It is usually much longer than your height","It is within an inch or so of each other","It is usually much shorter than your height"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the first sentence of the fact box. It tells you how close they are.","explain":"Most people's arm span and height are very close, usually within an inch or two."}}]},{"icon":"😴","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"How much sleep you need changes as you grow."},{"t":"fact","text":"Nine to twelve hours of sleep is right for a nine-year-old. It was worked out by testing how children think and feel with more or less sleep.","label":"Worked out"},{"t":"p","text":"As you become a teenager, your body clock shifts later. The urge to stay up late isn't laziness. Your body just wants to sleep at a different time."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How much sleep does a nine-year-old need?","options":["Six to eight hours","Nine to twelve hours","Fourteen to sixteen hours"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It gives the exact range for nine-year-olds.","explain":"Nine to twelve hours is just right for a nine-year-old to think and feel their best."}}]},{"icon":"📐","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The tallest person ever measured was Robert Wadlow."},{"t":"fact","text":"He reached 8 feet 11.1 inches (2.72 m) by June 1940. His growing never switched off because a gland under his brain kept releasing growth hormone."}]},{"icon":"🌱","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Your long bones grow in a special way."},{"t":"fact","text":"Each long bone has a thin plate of soft cartilage near its end. New bone is added there, so the shaft gets longer without being rebuilt. When you finish growing, the plate seals over."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Robert Wadlow","Tallest person ever measured"],["Growth hormone","Helps bones grow longer"],["Cartilage","The flexible material that turns into bone"],["Arm span","Usually within an inch of your height"],["Nine to twelve hours","Sleep needed for a nine-year-old"],["Kneecaps","Start as cartilage and harden over years"]]},"picgame":{"type":"tapall","title":"Growing Signs","goal":"Tap what shows you are growing.","items":[{"icon":"🦷","name":"tooth","good":true},{"icon":"👟","name":"shoe","good":true},{"icon":"📏","name":"ruler","good":true},{"icon":"👖","name":"pants","good":true},{"icon":"🧸","name":"teddy","good":false},{"icon":"🎈","name":"balloon","good":false},{"icon":"🍌","name":"banana","good":false},{"icon":"🪁","name":"kite","good":false}]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why are you measurably taller in the morning than right before bed?","options":["The discs in your spine decompress and expand overnight","Your bones actually grow a tiny amount every night","Morning measurements are simply less accurate"],"answer":0,"explain":"Most growth hormone is also released during deep sleep, which is why you grow fastest while asleep."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"In a growth spurt, which parts of your body typically reach their adult size first?","options":["Hands and feet, before arms, legs, and the rest of the body catch up","The head and face, before the rest of the body","The torso, before the arms and legs"],"answer":0,"explain":"That's exactly why shoes stop fitting before pants do during a growth spurt."},{"type":"tf","prompt":"A baby is born with hard, bony kneecaps, just like an adult's.","answer":1,"explain":"Babies' kneecaps start as soft cartilage, the same bendy material as the tip of your nose, and harden into bone over several years."}]},{"id":"body-eyes","course":"body","kind":"facts","section":"eyes","title":"Eyes & Seeing","glyph":"👁️","n":6,"idea":"Your eyes have cells for dim light and cells for color, and a spot where you cannot see at all.","pages":[{"icon":"👁️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Your eyeball grows fast."},{"t":"fact","text":"It is about 0.63 to 0.67 in (16 to 17 mm) wide at birth. By age three it is 0.89 to 0.91 in (22.5 to 23 mm), and it reaches full size by age 12."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"At what age does the eyeball reach full size?","options":["Age 6","Age 12","Age 20"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. The answer is there.","explain":"By age three it is 0.89 to 0.91 in (22.5 to 23 mm), and it reaches full size by age 12."}}]},{"icon":"⚙️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The retina is the innermost, light-sensitive layer of the eye."},{"t":"term","word":"retina","text":"The light-sensitive inner layer of the eye, like film in a camera."},{"t":"fact","text":"It does a job much like the film or image sensor in a camera."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is the light-sensitive layer of the eye called?","options":["The lens","The retina","The pupil"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"The retina is the innermost, light-sensitive layer of the eye."}}]},{"icon":"🔧","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Rods work mainly in dim light and give you black-and-white vision."},{"t":"fact","text":"Cones work in bright light. They let you see color, and they give the sharp vision you use for reading."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which cells let you see color?","options":["Rods","Cones","Lenses"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. The answer is there.","explain":"Cones work in bright light."}}]},{"icon":"👀","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"One part of the retina has no light-sensing cells. That part gives rise to the blind spot."},{"t":"fact","text":"Rods and cones are the cells that sense light."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does the part of the retina with no light-sensing cells cause?","options":["The black pupil","The blind spot","The tear duct"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"That part gives rise to the blind spot."}}]},{"icon":"👂","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The light you can see ranges from about 380 to 740 nanometers."},{"t":"fact","text":"Violet is at the short end, 380 to 450 nm. Red is at the long end, 625 to 740 nm."}]},{"icon":"🧰","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The eyes sit in bony cavities in the skull."},{"t":"fact","text":"These cavities are called the orbits."}]},{"icon":"🔎","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"An eyeball that grows. A layer like film. Rods and cones. A blind spot. Colors of light. Orbits in the skull."},{"t":"p","text":"Your eyes do a lot."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Retina","The light-sensitive layer of the eye"],["Rods","Work mainly in dim light"],["Cones","Let you see color"],["Orbits","Bony cavities that hold the eyes"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which cells work mainly in dim light?","options":["Cones","Rods","Lenses"],"answer":1,"explain":"Rods work mainly in dim light and give you black-and-white vision."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What are the bony cavities that hold the eyes called?","options":["The valves","The orbits","The ridges"],"answer":1,"explain":"These cavities are called the orbits."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What do cones give you the sharp vision to do?","options":["Sleeping","Reading","Swimming"],"answer":1,"explain":"They give the sharp vision you use for reading."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which color is at the short end of the light you can see?","options":["Red","Violet","Green"],"answer":1,"explain":"Violet is at the short end, 380 to 450 nm."}]},{"id":"body-ears","course":"body","kind":"facts","section":"ears","title":"Ears & Hearing","glyph":"👂","n":6,"idea":"Your ear hears and keeps your balance, and tiny bones inside it make sound stronger.","pages":[{"icon":"👂","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"In mammals, the ear lets you hear."},{"t":"fact","text":"It also helps your body keep its balance."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What two jobs does the ear do in mammals?","options":["Smelling and tasting","Hearing and balance","Seeing and touching"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. The answer is there.","explain":"It also helps your body keep its balance."}}]},{"icon":"🔧","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The middle ear holds the three smallest bones in your body, called the ossicles."},{"t":"term","word":"ossicles","text":"The three smallest bones in your body, in the middle ear."},{"t":"fact","text":"They are the malleus, incus and stapes, also called the hammer, anvil and stirrup."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What are the three smallest bones in your body called?","options":["The vertebrae","The ossicles","The ribs"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"The middle ear holds the three smallest bones in your body, called the ossicles."}}]},{"icon":"⚙️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The ossicles make sound waves stronger."},{"t":"fact","text":"They make them nearly 15 to 20 times stronger."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How much do the ossicles make sound waves stronger?","options":["Only about twice","Nearly 15 to 20 times","Almost a thousand times"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. The answer is there.","explain":"They make them nearly 15 to 20 times stronger."}}]},{"icon":"🧩","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The ear canal stretches for about 1 inch (2.5 cm)."},{"t":"fact","text":"It is cleaned by earwax, which naturally moves out toward the outer ear."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is the length of the ear canal?","options":["About 10 inches","About 1 inch","Nearly 1 foot"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"The ear canal stretches for about 1 inch (2.5 cm)."}}]},{"icon":"🧰","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The cochlea is the part of the inner ear involved in hearing."},{"t":"fact","text":"It is a spiral-shaped cavity that makes 2.75 turns in humans."}]},{"icon":"💡","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Your brain compares when a sound reaches each ear and how loud it is in each ear."},{"t":"fact","text":"That helps it work out where the sound came from."}]},{"icon":"🔎","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"An ear for hearing and balance. Three small bones. Sound waves made stronger. An ear canal with earwax. A spiral cochlea."},{"t":"p","text":"Your ears help you."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Ossicles","The three smallest bones in the body"],["Cochlea","A spiral-shaped part of the inner ear"],["Earwax","Cleans the ear canal"],["Ear","Lets you hear and keep balance"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What cleans the ear canal?","options":["Fresh water","Earwax","Plain soap"],"answer":1,"explain":"It is cleaned by earwax, which naturally moves out toward the outer ear."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What shape is the cochlea?","options":["Square","Spiral","Flat"],"answer":1,"explain":"It is a spiral-shaped cavity that makes 2.75 turns in humans."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many turns does the cochlea make in humans?","options":["27.5 turns","2.75 turns","0.25 turns"],"answer":1,"explain":"It is a spiral-shaped cavity that makes 2.75 turns in humans."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many bones are the ossicles?","options":["Two","Three","Twelve"],"answer":1,"explain":"The middle ear holds the three smallest bones in your body, called the ossicles."}]},{"id":"body-teeth","course":"body","kind":"facts","section":"teeth","title":"Teeth","glyph":"🦷","n":6,"idea":"Your teeth are covered in the hardest substance in your body, but sugar can still harm them.","pages":[{"icon":"🦷","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A baby's first set of teeth is called baby teeth or milk teeth."},{"t":"term","word":"milk teeth","text":"A baby's first set of teeth."},{"t":"fact","text":"They normally start to appear at about six months of age."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"At about what age do baby teeth start to appear?","options":["About six years","About six months","About sixty days"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. The answer is there.","explain":"They normally start to appear at about six months of age."}}]},{"icon":"📏","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Enamel is the hardest substance in the human body."},{"t":"fact","text":"It is 96% mineral. The rest is water and organic material."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is the hardest substance in the human body?","options":["Bone","Enamel","Dentin"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"Enamel is the hardest substance in the human body."}}]},{"icon":"⚙️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The large amount of mineral in enamel makes it strong."},{"t":"fact","text":"It also makes it brittle."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does the mineral in enamel make it besides strong?","options":["Soft","Brittle","Stretchy"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. The answer is there.","explain":"It also makes it brittle."}}]},{"icon":"🔧","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Under the enamel is dentin."},{"t":"fact","text":"It is less mineralized and less brittle, and it supports the enamel."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What layer supports the enamel?","options":["Plaque","Dentin","Gum"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"Under the enamel is dentin."}}]},{"icon":"🍽️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Bacteria in the mouth use the simple sugars in food for energy."},{"t":"fact","text":"A diet high in simple sugar raises the risk of tooth decay."}]},{"icon":"📐","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"As of 2016, about 3.6 billion people had cavities in their permanent teeth."},{"t":"fact","text":"That is 48% of all the people in the world."}]},{"icon":"🔎","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Baby teeth at six months. Enamel, the hardest layer. Dentin under it. Bacteria and sugar. Billions with cavities."},{"t":"p","text":"Teeth need care."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Enamel","The hardest substance in the human body"],["Dentin","Supports the enamel"],["Baby teeth","The first set of teeth"],["Bacteria","Use simple sugars in food for energy"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"How much of enamel is mineral?","options":["9.6%","96%","66%"],"answer":1,"explain":"It is 96% mineral."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What do bacteria in the mouth use for energy?","options":["Plain water and air","Simple sugars in food","Clean salt and milk"],"answer":1,"explain":"Bacteria in the mouth use the simple sugars in food for energy."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"About how many people had cavities in their permanent teeth as of 2016?","options":["About 36 million","About 3.6 billion","About 360 thousand"],"answer":1,"explain":"As of 2016, about 3.6 billion people had cavities in their permanent teeth."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does a diet high in simple sugar raise the risk of?","options":["Hair loss","Tooth decay","Bad eyesight"],"answer":1,"explain":"A diet high in simple sugar raises the risk of tooth decay."}]},{"id":"body-blood","course":"body","kind":"facts","section":"blood","title":"Blood","glyph":"🩸","n":6,"idea":"Blood is about 7% of your body weight, and its watery plasma and its cells each have a job.","pages":[{"icon":"🩸","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Think about the blood in a person's body."},{"t":"fact","text":"Blood is about 7% of a person's body weight."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What share of body weight is blood?","options":["About 70%","About 7%","Nearly half"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. The answer is there.","explain":"Blood is about 7% of a person's body weight."}}]},{"icon":"⚙️","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Plasma makes up 55% of blood."},{"t":"term","word":"plasma","text":"The liquid part of blood, mostly water."},{"t":"fact","text":"It is mostly water, 92% by volume. It also holds proteins, glucose, mineral ions and hormones."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is plasma mostly made of?","options":["Protein","Water","Sugar"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. The answer is there.","explain":"It is mostly water, 92% by volume."}}]},{"icon":"🔧","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Platelets are also called thrombocytes."},{"t":"fact","text":"They take part in blood clotting. Platelets help your blood clot."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What do platelets help your blood do?","options":["Boil","Clot","Freeze"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. The answer is there.","explain":"Platelets help your blood clot."}}]},{"icon":"💨","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Platelets circulate in your blood."},{"t":"fact","text":"Their average life span is 8 to 9 days."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How long do platelets live?","options":["8 to 9 years","8 to 9 days","80 to 90 days"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. The answer is there.","explain":"Their average life span is 8 to 9 days."}}]},{"icon":"🏃","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Red blood cells travel all around the body."},{"t":"fact","text":"A red blood cell takes about 60 seconds to go once around the body."}]},{"icon":"🧩","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Red blood cells take up oxygen in the lungs."},{"t":"fact","text":"They release it into the tissues while squeezing through the body's capillaries."}]},{"icon":"🔎","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Blood in your body. Plasma made of water. Platelets that clot. A red blood cell around the body. Oxygen from the lungs."},{"t":"p","text":"Blood is in your body."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Plasma","Makes up 55% of blood"],["Platelets","Take part in blood clotting"],["Red blood cells","Take up oxygen in the lungs"],["Capillaries","Where red blood cells squeeze through the body"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What share of blood does plasma make up?","options":["5.5%","55%","95%"],"answer":1,"explain":"Plasma makes up 55% of blood."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where do red blood cells take up oxygen?","options":["In the skin","In the lungs","In the feet"],"answer":1,"explain":"Red blood cells take up oxygen in the lungs."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"About how long does a red blood cell take to go once around the body?","options":["About 60 minutes","About 60 seconds","About 6 days"],"answer":1,"explain":"A red blood cell takes about 60 seconds to go once around the body."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What are platelets also called?","options":["Lymphocytes","Thrombocytes","Erythrocytes"],"answer":1,"explain":"Platelets are also called thrombocytes."}]},{"id":"animals-mammals-1","course":"animals","kind":"species","group":"mammals","title":"Cheetah, Snow leopard & Tiger","names":["Cheetah","Snow leopard","Tiger","Lion","Fennec fox"],"glyph":"🦁","tint":"#ffc94a","items":["cheetah","snow-leopard","tiger","lion","fennec-fox"],"idea":"Five animals, each built for one job. Find out who sprints at 65 miles an hour, who vanishes on bare rock, and who hears dinner under the sand.","pages":[{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Five animals are waiting to meet you: a cheetah, a snow leopard, a tiger, a lion and a fennec fox."},{"t":"p","text":"Each one is built for a different job."}]},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Start with the fastest runner on land. The fastest cheetah ever timed ran 100 meters in 5.95 seconds. The human world record is 9.58."},{"t":"fact","text":"Her name was Sarah and she lived at the Cincinnati Zoo. She chased a fluffy lure on a rope and hit a peak of 61 mph (98 km/h).","label":"The race"},{"t":"p","text":"Cheetah speeds get exaggerated all the time. The highest reliably measured is 65 mph (104 km/h). A famous figure of 71 mph came from a faulty way of measuring and was thrown out."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is the highest speed ever reliably measured for a cheetah?","options":["71 mph","35 mph","65 mph"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the last two sentences. One number was thrown out.","explain":"65 mph. The famous 71 came from a faulty way of measuring."}}],"img":"cheetah"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Now climb. The snow leopard lives higher up the mountains than almost any other large animal, between about 9,800 and 14,800 feet (3,000 to 4,500 m)."},{"t":"p","text":"Its tail is up to 3 feet (1 m) long, nearly as long as the rest of the cat. On a ledge barely wider than a paw, it works as a balance bar."},{"t":"term","word":"rosette","text":"A dark smudge shaped like a ring. A snow leopard's rosettes break up its outline, so on bare rock people cannot find the cat."},{"t":"fact","text":"Researchers can work in snow leopard country for months and see nothing but tracks. Almost everything we know comes from camera traps and GPS collars, not from watching.","label":"The ghost of the mountains"}],"img":"snow-leopard"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"No cat alive is bigger than the tiger. A large male weighs around 485 lb (220 kg), about as much as three grown men."},{"t":"fact","text":"Shave a tiger and the stripes are still there, because the pattern is in the skin itself. Every tiger's pattern is its own. Even the left and right sides of one tiger do not match.","label":"Stripes to the skin"},{"t":"p","text":"Orange looks loud to you, but deer cannot see orange. The best read is that in dry grass the stripes look like broken lines of shadow."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How can researchers tell one tiger from another?","options":["By the sound of its roar","By its stripes","By how much it weighs"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box about the stripes. It says whose pattern is whose.","explain":"By its stripes. The pattern is in the skin and no two tigers share one."}}],"img":"tiger"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The lion is the only cat that lives in a family group, called a pride."},{"t":"fact","text":"A pride is a permanent group of mostly related females. Grandmothers, mothers, sisters and daughters stay together for life.","label":"The pride"},{"t":"fact","text":"A lion's roar carries about 5 miles (8 km) across open country. The low pitch is the trick, because deep sound bends around trees and rocks.","label":"The roar"},{"t":"p","text":"And a lion may rest 20 hours out of every 24."}],"img":"lion"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The smallest fox on earth has the biggest ears for its size. A fennec weighs about 3 lb (1.3 kg), and its ears are nearly 4 inches (10 cm) tall."},{"t":"fact","text":"A fennec stops dead and swivels its head. Then it digs straight down into open sand and comes up with a beetle or a lizard. Its ears pick up small movements underground.","label":"Hunting by listening"},{"t":"p","text":"It can live where there is nothing to drink, because it takes its water from the animals it eats."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How does a fennec fox find food hidden in the sand?","options":["It listens for it","It smells it from far away","It follows tracks"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the box. It says what the fox does with its ears.","explain":"It listens. Its ears pick up small movements underground, and it digs to the exact spot."}}],"img":"fennec-fox"},{"icon":"📊","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Now put the five side by side. The tiger weighs about 485 lb and the lion about 419. The cheetah and the snow leopard each weigh about 99 lb. The fennec fox weighs about 3."},{"t":"fact","text":"The cheetah's top speed is 65 mph. The tiger and the lion both manage about 35 mph, and only for a short sprint.","label":"Speed"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which of the five is the heaviest?","options":["The lion","The cheetah","The tiger"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the first paragraph and find the biggest number.","explain":"The tiger, at about 485 lb."}}]},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A cheetah that sprints, a snow leopard that hides, a tiger with stripes in its skin, a lion with a family and a fox that listens."},{"t":"p","text":"Five animals, five different jobs, and every one of them built for its own."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Cheetah","The fastest runner on land"],["Snow leopard","The ghost of the mountains"],["Tiger","The largest cat in the world"],["Lion","The only cat that lives in a pride"],["Fennec fox","The smallest fox, with enormous ears"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which animal is the fastest runner on land?","options":["The tiger","The cheetah","The lion"],"answer":1,"explain":"The cheetah, with a highest measured speed of 65 mph."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does a snow leopard use its long tail for?","options":["To warn other cats of danger","To balance on narrow ledges","To swim across mountain rivers"],"answer":1,"explain":"The tail works as a balance bar on ledges barely wider than a paw."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where are a tiger's stripes?","options":["Only in the fur, never in the skin","Along its back but not its legs","In its skin as well as its fur"],"answer":2,"explain":"Shave a tiger and the stripes are still there."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is a family group of lions called?","options":["A pride","A herd","A pack"],"answer":0,"explain":"A pride is a permanent group of mostly related females and their young."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How does a fennec fox find food under the sand?","options":["It follows tracks","It smells it from far away","It listens with its huge ears"],"answer":2,"explain":"Its ears pick up small movements underground, and it digs to the exact spot."}]},{"id":"animals-mammals-2","course":"animals","kind":"species","group":"mammals","title":"Arctic fox, Gray wolf & more","names":["Arctic fox","Gray wolf","Polar bear","Grizzly bear","Giant panda"],"glyph":"🦁","tint":"#ffc94a","items":["arctic-fox","grey-wolf","polar-bear","grizzly-bear","giant-panda"],"idea":"Meet five animals, each suited to its own cold, wild place. Find out what the extra thumb on a panda's paw is for.","pages":[{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Five animals are waiting to meet you: an arctic fox, a gray wolf, a polar bear, a grizzly bear, and a giant panda."},{"t":"p","text":"Each one lives in its own world."}]},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the arctic fox, a small fox with deep fur that lives farther north than almost any other land animal."},{"t":"fact","text":"Turn one paw over and even the soles are furred, pads and all. Its scientific name, Vulpes lagopus, means \"hare-foot\" — no other wild dog has this.","label":"Extra-furry feet"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is special about the arctic fox's paws?","options":["They have webbed toes","They are completely bare","They have fur on the soles"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the second sentence of the page.","explain":"The arctic fox's paws have fur even on the soles, which helps it grip ice and keeps its feet warm."}}],"img":"arctic-fox"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the gray wolf, the largest wild member of the dog family. It has long legs and hunts in a family group."},{"t":"term","word":"carnassials","text":"Sharp teeth shaped like blades that cut through hide, tendon, and bone."},{"t":"fact","text":"A wolf pack is made up of parents and pups from this year and last. The idea of an \"alpha\" wolf fighting for dominance comes from watching zoo wolves, not those in the wild.","label":"The pack is a family"},{"t":"p","text":"Wolves hunt with their noses. Researchers have seen them head straight for a moose 1.5 miles (2.4 km) away, downwind and out of sight."}],"img":"grey-wolf"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Next up is the polar bear, the largest bear on earth. It's a pale, thick-furred hunter that lives out on Arctic sea ice and hunts seals."},{"t":"fact","text":"One collared female swam for nine days straight across 426 miles (687 km) of open Arctic sea. She lost about a fifth of her body weight doing it.","label":"The bear that swims far"},{"t":"p","text":"Polar bear fur isn't white. Each hair is actually a clear, hollow tube that scatters sunlight in all directions, making the fur look white."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What makes polar bear fur appear white?","options":["Black skin under the fur","Clear, hollow tubes that scatter sunlight","Thick fur padding on the soles of its feet"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the last paragraph. It says what each hair is like inside.","explain":"The hollow core of each hair scatters sunlight in all directions, making the fur look white."}}],"img":"polar-bear"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Next, meet the grizzly bear, a large brown bear of North America's mountains and salmon rivers. It has a hump of muscle on its shoulders and claws built for digging."},{"t":"fact","text":"The hump over its shoulders is solid muscle, powering the front legs to dig through ground you would need a pickaxe for. Its claws are 2-3.9 in (5-10 cm) long and almost straight, perfect for turning over rocks and hauling out roots and ground squirrels.","label":"The digging champion"},{"t":"p","text":"By late summer, a grizzly goes into hyperphagia — \"over-eating.\" It feeds up to 20 hours a day."},{"t":"p","text":"During this time, it can put on 3-7 lb (1.5-3 kg) of fat every single day. This is crucial for surviving the long winter in its den."}],"img":"grizzly-bear"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Next up is the giant panda, a black-and-white bear from the cold mountain forests of China that eats almost nothing but bamboo."},{"t":"fact","text":"A panda has an extra \"thumb\" — not a finger at all but an enlarged wrist bone jutting out under its paw. This helps it hold and strip bamboo stems like you hold a pencil.","label":"The spare thumb"},{"t":"p","text":"An adult eats 26-84 lb (12-38 kg) of bamboo a day, spending up to 15 hours munching."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What extra feature does the panda have that helps it hold bamboo?","options":["An extra thumb-like bone","A long, sticky tongue","Webbed skin on each paw"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the box about the spare thumb. It names the bone.","explain":"The panda has an enlarged wrist bone, which acts like an extra thumb to help hold bamboo."}}],"img":"giant-panda"},{"icon":"📊","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The polar bear weighs about 992 lb, the grizzly bear about 551 lb, and the giant panda about 220 lb. The gray wolf is much lighter at about 88 lb, and the arctic fox is the smallest, at just 8 lb."},{"t":"fact","text":"The polar bear runs at 25 mph, while the gray wolf and grizzly bear both reach up to 35 mph for short sprints.","label":"Speed"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which of the five is the heaviest?","options":["The grizzly bear","The giant panda","The polar bear"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the first paragraph and find the biggest number.","explain":"The polar bear, at about 992 lb."}}]},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A polar bear that swims for days, a grizzly bear with a muscle hump for digging, a giant panda with a spare thumb."},{"t":"p","text":"A gray wolf that hunts with its nose, and an arctic fox with furry feet."},{"t":"p","text":"Five animals, each built for their own world, and all so different."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Arctic fox","Lives in the far north with furry feet"],["Gray wolf","Hunts in a family group with sharp teeth"],["Polar bear","Swims long distances across the Arctic sea"],["Grizzly bear","Has a hump of muscle for digging"],["Giant panda","Eats bamboo and has an extra thumb-like bone"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"How do wolves find their prey?","options":["By feeling the ground shake through their paws","By sight, from high ground","With their noses"],"answer":2,"explain":"Wolves have been seen heading straight for a moose 1.5 miles away, downwind and out of sight."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What are carnassials?","options":["Flat, wide teeth shaped like plates that mash up leaves, roots, and seeds","Long hollow fangs that squirt out poison to stun and slow down prey","Sharp teeth shaped like blades that cut through hide, tendon, and bone"],"answer":2,"explain":"Carnassials help wolves tear into their prey efficiently."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How far did the collared polar bear swim without stopping?","options":["126 miles","426 miles","926 miles"],"answer":1,"explain":"She swam nine days straight across 426 miles of open Arctic sea."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How much bamboo does an adult giant panda eat each day?","options":["10-25 lb (4.5-11 kg)","26-84 lb (12-38 kg)","100-120 lb (45-54 kg)"],"answer":1,"explain":"An adult giant panda eats 26-84 lb (12-38 kg) of bamboo a day and spends up to 15 hours eating it."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is the hump on a grizzly's shoulders made of?","options":["Fat","Bone","Solid muscle"],"answer":2,"explain":"The hump is solid muscle that powers the front legs for digging."}]},{"id":"animals-mammals-3","course":"animals","kind":"species","group":"mammals","title":"Three-toed sloth & more","names":["Three-toed sloth","Naked mole-rat","Pangolin","Platypus","Echidna"],"glyph":"🦁","tint":"#ffc94a","items":["sloth","naked-mole-rat","pangolin","platypus","echidna"],"idea":"Five unusual mammals, each odd in its own way. Find out who climbs down to poop once a week, who lives like an ant, and who lays eggs.","pages":[{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Five unusual animals are waiting to meet you: a three-toed sloth, a naked mole-rat, a pangolin, a platypus, and an echidna."},{"t":"p","text":"Each one lives in its own odd way."}]},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the three-toed sloth, a shaggy leaf-eater that lives in the rainforest canopy and spends almost its entire life hanging upside down."},{"t":"fact","text":"Top speed on the ground is about 0.15 mph (0.24 km/h). That is roughly 13 feet (4 m) in a minute. You cover that in about three seconds.","label":"The slowest"},{"t":"p","text":"Its fur can turn green with algae, making it nearly invisible in the trees. The claws are as long as your finger and perfect for hanging. A sloth climbs all the way down to the forest floor to poop only about once a week."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How often does a sloth poop?","options":["Once a week","Once a month","Once a day"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the last sentence of the paragraph about its fur and claws.","explain":"A sloth climbs all the way down to the forest floor to poop, about once a week."}}],"img":"sloth"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Next, meet the naked mole-rat, a nearly hairless rodent from East Africa that spends its whole life underground in a colony like an ant nest."},{"t":"p","text":"It is about as long as your finger and weighs about 1.2 oz (35 g), about the same as a large strawberry. The queen of the colony can grow much bigger, up to 2.8 oz (80 g)."},{"t":"term","word":"colony","text":"A group of animals that live together and work as one big family."},{"t":"fact","text":"A naked mole-rat colony has one queen who is the only one to have babies. Everyone else digs, cleans, feeds, and defends the nest. Colonies can grow up to 300 animals.","label":"The underground life"}],"img":"naked-mole-rat"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Next up is the pangolin, a shy night-time insect eater covered head to tail in hard scales."},{"t":"fact","text":"Threatened, a pangolin rolls into a ball so tight that even a big cat cannot get in. Predators have been filmed batting one around and eventually giving up.","label":"The armored ball"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does a pangolin do when threatened?","options":["It runs away quickly","It rolls into a tight ball","It stabs attackers with sharp horns"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the fact box about the armored ball.","explain":"When threatened, a pangolin rolls into a tight ball, making it difficult for predators to get in."}}],"img":"pangolin"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the platypus, an Australian river mammal with a soft duck-like bill, webbed feet, and a beaver’s tail. Only five kinds of mammal in the world lay eggs, and this is one of them."},{"t":"fact","text":"A platypus lays eggs, usually two small, leather-like ones. The mother curls around them in a burrow for about ten days until they hatch.","label":"The egg-laying mammal"},{"t":"p","text":"Its bill is packed with sensors that pick up the faint electricity of a shrimp's muscle movements. This is called electroreception and helps it hunt in muddy rivers with its eyes, ears, and nostrils all shut."},{"t":"fact","text":"Platypus mothers have no nipples. Milk seeps out through the skin of the belly, pools in grooves in the fur, and the babies lap it up from there.","label":"Milk delivery"}],"img":"platypus"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the echidna, an ant-eating, spine-covered Australian mammal that lays a single egg into a pouch on its belly."},{"t":"fact","text":"An echidna lays one leathery egg straight into a pouch on her belly. Ten days later, a tiny blind and hairless baby called a puggle hatches inside the pouch. It stays there for two to three months.","label":"The egg-laying pouch"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How long does it take for an echidna's puggle to hatch?","options":["2 weeks","2 months","10 days"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the box. It says how many days after the egg is laid the baby hatches.","explain":"It takes 10 days for the puggle to hatch inside the pouch."}}],"img":"echidna"},{"icon":"📊","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The pangolin is about 2.6 ft long, the three-toed sloth about 1.6 ft, the platypus 1.5 ft and the echidna 1.3 ft. The naked mole-rat is just 0.3 ft."},{"t":"fact","text":"The three-toed sloth weighs about 9 lb, the same as an echidna. A platypus is lighter at 3 lb, and a naked mole-rat weighs only about 1.2 oz.","label":"Weight"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which of the five is the longest?","options":["The three-toed sloth","The naked mole-rat","The pangolin"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the first paragraph and find the longest length.","explain":"The pangolin is the longest at 2.6 ft."}}]},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A three-toed sloth that moves slowly, a naked mole-rat that lives underground, a pangolin that curls into a ball, a platypus that lays eggs, and an echidna that has spines."},{"t":"p","text":"Five animals, each with its own way of living."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Three-toed sloth","Moves at 0.15 mph"],["Naked mole-rat","Lives in colonies like ants"],["Pangolin","Rolls into a tight ball"],["Platypus","Finds shrimp with a bill that senses electricity"],["Echidna","Has an egg-laying pouch"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"How fast can a three-toed sloth move on the ground?","options":["1 mph","0.15 mph","5 mph"],"answer":1,"explain":"A three-toed sloth moves at about 0.15 mph on the ground, roughly 13 feet in a minute."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does a naked mole-rat do that's like an ant colony?","options":["Build tall mounds","Have one queen who has babies","Sting intruders with venom"],"answer":1,"explain":"Just like ants, they have a special queen!"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How does a platypus find food in muddy rivers?","options":["By spotting prey with its sharp eyes","By sensing electricity with its bill","By sniffing out prey with its nose"],"answer":1,"explain":"Its bill picks up the faint electricity of a shrimp's muscle movements."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What unique feature do both the platypus and echidna share?","options":["They can fly","They lay eggs","They have fur and scales"],"answer":1,"explain":"Laying eggs is really unusual for mammals."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How does a pangolin protect itself from predators?","options":["It runs away quickly","It rolls into a tight ball","It stabs attackers with sharp horns"],"answer":1,"explain":"Rolling into a ball keeps it safe from predators."}]},{"id":"animals-mammals-4","course":"animals","kind":"species","group":"mammals","title":"Red kangaroo, Koala & Wombat","names":["Red kangaroo","Koala","Wombat","Tasmanian devil","African elephant"],"glyph":"🦁","tint":"#ffc94a","items":["red-kangaroo","koala","wombat","tasmanian-devil","african-elephant"],"idea":"Five animals, each with a body part that does something odd. Find out whose legs work like springs, whose droppings come out as cubes, and whose trunk has no bones.","pages":[{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Five animals are waiting to meet you: a red kangaroo, a koala, a wombat, a Tasmanian devil, and an African elephant."},{"t":"p","text":"Each one is so different! They all have their own ways of living."}]},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Start with the red kangaroo, the largest marsupial alive. A big male stands about 5 ft (1.5 m) tall and weighs about 146 lb (66 kg)."},{"t":"fact","text":"In a short burst, a red kangaroo can hit about 35 mph (56 km/h), faster than any human sprinter who has ever lived.","label":"The speed"},{"t":"p","text":"Its legs are springs. Huge Achilles tendons in the lower legs stretch on landing and snap back on the next hop, so most of the energy from one hop gets recycled into the next. That is why it does not breathe harder as it speeds up."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What special feature helps a red kangaroo recycle energy while hopping?","options":["Its pouch stores up energy to use on each new hop","Its legs are springs with enormous Achilles tendons","Its tail is a spring that pushes it into each hop"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the second paragraph. It explains how the tendons work.","explain":"The kangaroo's legs have enormous Achilles tendons that stretch when it lands and snap back to propel the next hop, recycling energy efficiently."}}],"img":"red-kangaroo"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Next, meet the koala, a thick-furred tree-living marsupial from eastern Australia. It eats almost nothing but eucalyptus leaves."},{"t":"p","text":"A koala's liver takes apart the toxins in eucalyptus leaves and flushes them out in its urine. Eucalyptus leaves are also tough, watery, and low in energy. So a koala spends up to 20 hours a day dozing or sitting perfectly still."},{"t":"term","word":"cecum","text":"A pouch off the gut where millions of microbes live and break down leaf fiber."},{"t":"fact","text":"A joey's first solid food is pap, a soft green paste from its mother’s gut. Pap comes from the cecum, a pouch off the gut, and is full of living microbes that help the joey digest eucalyptus leaves.","label":"The gut bacteria"}],"img":"koala"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the wombat, a barrel-shaped marsupial from Australia that digs long tunnel systems with its strong front legs and comes out at night to graze."},{"t":"p","text":"Wombat droppings come out as cubes. Eighty to a hundred of them a night, and the wombat balances them on rocks and logs where other wombats will find them."},{"t":"fact","text":"Scientists studied wombat intestines and found that stiff patches and soft patches in the last stretch squeeze the drying pellets into flat sides. A cube stays put on a rock. A ball rolls off.","label":"Cubes in the wild"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why do wombats produce cube-shaped droppings?","options":["To keep them from rolling away","To keep them warm inside the burrow","To make them easier to eat"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the last two sentences.","explain":"A cube stays put on a rock or a log, where other wombats will find it. A ball would roll off."}}],"img":"wombat"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the Tasmanian devil, the largest meat-eating marsupial alive. It’s a stocky, black, night-roaming scavenger found only in Tasmania."},{"t":"fact","text":"For its size, no living mammal bites harder than the Tasmanian devil. Pound for pound, its jaws out-bite a lion's and it can crunch straight through leg bone.","label":"The strongest bite"},{"t":"p","text":"Devils eat everything — skin, fur, organs, and bones. A dead wallaby can vanish overnight, leaving barely a stain on the grass."},{"t":"p","text":"When a devil is excited or upset, its ears turn red. Blood floods the thin bare skin there, and its mood becomes visible from across a clearing."}],"img":"tasmanian-devil"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the African elephant, the largest animal that walks on land. It has a trunk that works as both a nose and a hand."},{"t":"fact","text":"The trunk is made of tens of thousands of muscle bundles with no bone at all. It can lift a fallen branch or pick up a single blade of grass.","label":"The powerful trunk"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is an elephant's trunk made of?","options":["Mostly bone and gristle, wrapped in thick skin","Hundreds of tiny bones joined by rubbery cords","Tens of thousands of muscle bundles and no bone"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the box about the trunk. It says what the trunk is made of.","explain":"The trunk has tens of thousands of muscle bundles with no bone, making it strong enough to lift branches and delicate enough to pick up small objects."}}],"img":"african-elephant"},{"icon":"📊","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Now put the five side by side. The African elephant weighs about 12,125 lb and is 19.7 ft long. The red kangaroo weighs about 146 lb and is 4.6 ft long. The koala weighs about 18 lb and is 2.3 ft long. The wombat weighs about 57 lb and is 3.3 ft long. The Tasmanian devil weighs about 18 lb and is 2.1 ft long."},{"t":"fact","text":"The red kangaroo can run at 35 mph (56 km/h) in a short burst, and the African elephant at about 16 mph (26 km/h).","label":"Speed"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which of the five is the heaviest?","options":["The African elephant","The big red kangaroo","The common wombat"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the first paragraph and find the biggest number.","explain":"The African elephant, at about 12,125 lb."}}]},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"An African elephant weighing 12,125 lb. A red kangaroo at 35 mph. A koala in trees. A wombat weighing 57 lb. A Tasmanian devil with the strongest bite for its size."},{"t":"p","text":"Five animals, each one different, each one built for its own way of life."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Red kangaroo","Its legs are springs with enormous Achilles tendons"],["Koala","Eats almost nothing but eucalyptus leaves"],["Wombat","Drops cube-shaped droppings"],["Tasmanian devil","Has the strongest bite for its size"],["African elephant","Trunk has tens of thousands of muscle bundles"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"How does a red kangaroo recycle energy while hopping?","options":["Its pouch stores up energy to use on each new hop","Its legs are springs with enormous Achilles tendons","Its tail is a spring that pushes it into each hop"],"answer":1,"explain":"The kangaroo's legs have special tendons that help it hop efficiently."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does a joey eat first to help digest eucalyptus leaves?","options":["Dirt, scooped from the ground","Grubs, picked out of tree bark","Pap, a paste from its mother"],"answer":2,"explain":"Pap contains helpful microbes for the joey's digestion."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What special feature do wombats have that helps them dig long tunnel systems?","options":["A hard plate on its nose","Strong front legs","A tail like a shovel"],"answer":1,"explain":"Wombats use their strong front legs to dig tunnels."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How does the Tasmanian devil's bite compare with other mammals of its size?","options":["Pound for pound, a lion bites much harder","For its size, no living mammal bites harder","Most mammals of its size bite about the same"],"answer":1,"explain":"Pound for pound, no living mammal bites harder than the Tasmanian devil."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many muscle bundles are in an African elephant's trunk?","options":["About 500","Tens of thousands","Fewer than 100"],"answer":1,"explain":"The trunk has tens of thousands of muscle bundles with no bone."}]},{"id":"animals-mammals-5","course":"animals","kind":"species","group":"mammals","title":"Giraffe, Hippopotamus & more","names":["Giraffe","Hippopotamus","White rhinoceros","Dromedary camel","Reindeer"],"glyph":"🦁","tint":"#ffc94a","items":["giraffe","hippopotamus","rhinoceros","camel","reindeer"],"idea":"A giraffe's neck has only seven bones, just like yours. Find out whose eyes turn blue in winter, whose hump is fat instead of water, and whose horn grows back.","pages":[{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Five animals are waiting to meet you: a giraffe, a hippopotamus, a white rhinoceros, a dromedary camel, and a reindeer."}]},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Start with the tallest animal alive: a giraffe. A big bull stands about 18 ft (5.5 m) tall. Stand him beside a basketball hoop and his head is still about 8 ft (2.4 m) clear above the rim."},{"t":"fact","text":"Your neck has seven bones in it. So does a giraffe's. But a single bone can be longer than your whole forearm. One giraffe neck vertebra can measure over 11 in (28 cm).","label":"The long neck"},{"t":"p","text":"A giraffe's blood pressure is roughly twice yours. It has to be — the heart is pushing blood about 6 ft (2 m) straight uphill to reach the brain. Then the head swings down to drink. A mesh of fine vessels at the base of the skull, called the rete mirabile, soaks up the surge."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What helps prevent a giraffe's brain from getting flooded when it lowers its head to drink?","options":["Extra-thick skin on its neck","The rete mirabile","Its long neck vertebrae"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the last paragraph. It explains how the mesh of vessels works.","explain":"The rete mirabile is a mesh of fine vessels at the base of the skull that soaks up the surge when the head lowers to drink."}}],"img":"giraffe"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Next is the hippopotamus, a river giant that spends its days submerged in water and its nights grazing on land."},{"t":"fact","text":"Hippo skin oozes a thick red liquid called blood sweat — it's neither blood nor sweat. It acts as a natural sunscreen and antiseptic, because its pigments soak up ultraviolet light.","label":"The red sunscreen"},{"t":"term","word":"pigments","text":"Substances that give color to something. In this case, hippo skin produces pigments that protect against UV light."},{"t":"p","text":"A hippo’s lower canine teeth can grow up to 20 in (50 cm) long — arm's length — and they never stop growing. They sharpen themselves every time the jaws close."}],"img":"hippopotamus"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Next up is the white rhinoceros, the heaviest land animal after the elephants. It's a grass-mowing giant with thick gray hide and two horns made of keratin, the same stuff as your fingernails and hair."},{"t":"fact","text":"The horn keeps growing back, just like your nails. Rangers sometimes cut it off to protect the rhino from poachers, but it regrows from its base.","label":"The never-ending horn"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is the material that makes up a rhinoceros's horn?","options":["Bone","Ivory","Keratin"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the first paragraph. It names the material that makes up the horns.","explain":"The horn is made of keratin, just like your fingernails and hair."}}],"img":"rhinoceros"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the dromedary camel, the one-humped giant of hot deserts, built to carry loads across country with no water in sight."},{"t":"fact","text":"The hump is not a water tank. It's fat — up to about 80 lb (36 kg) of it — that the camel carries around on its own back. When hungry, the hump shrinks and flops over; feed it up and the hump firms up again.","label":"The hump's secret"},{"t":"p","text":"A thirsty camel can drink more than 26 gallons (100 liters) in about ten minutes. That is fifty big water bottles straight down without stopping."}],"img":"camel"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Next, meet the reindeer, the deer of the frozen north. A bull stands about waist-high to a grown-up, but it is heavy for its height, up to 330 lb (150 kg)."},{"t":"fact","text":"In summer, a reindeer's eyes are gold. Through the long winter dark, they turn deep blue. The mirror at the back of the eye squeezes closer together and turns blue, so the eye catches more light.","label":"Eyes that change color"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What color are a reindeer's eyes in the winter?","options":["Deep blue","Gold","Green"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the last sentence of the fact box.","explain":"In winter, a reindeer's eyes turn deep blue to catch more light."}}],"img":"reindeer"},{"icon":"📊","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Put these five animals side by side. The white rhinoceros weighs about 4,409 lb, the hippopotamus about 3,307 lb, and the giraffe about 2,646 lb. The dromedary camel weighs around 1,213 lb, and the reindeer about 287 lb."},{"t":"fact","text":"The white rhinoceros can run up to 31 mph, the hippopotamus up to 19 mph, and the giraffe up to 37 mph. The dromedary camel can reach 25 mph, while the reindeer also runs at 37 mph.","label":"Speed"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which of the five is the heaviest?","options":["The river hippopotamus","The white rhinoceros","The tall male giraffe"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the first paragraph and find the biggest number.","explain":"The white rhinoceros, at about 4,409 lb."}}]},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A white rhinoceros running up to 31 mph and weighing 4,409 lb, a hippopotamus up to 19 mph and 3,307 lb, and a giraffe reaching 37 mph and 2,646 lb. A dromedary camel at 25 mph and 1,213 lb, and a reindeer also running 37 mph and weighing 287 lb."},{"t":"p","text":"Five animals, each built for their own way of life."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Giraffe","Tallest animal standing at 18 ft"],["Hippopotamus","Oozes red liquid as natural sunscreen"],["White Rhinoceros","Heaviest land animal after the elephants, horn regrows like nails"],["Dromedary Camel","Hump is fat, not water"],["Reindeer","Eyes turn deep blue in winter"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does the rete mirabile do for the giraffe?","options":["Pumps blood to its long legs when it starts to run","Soaks up blood pressure when it lowers its head","Helps it hear sounds from far across the plains"],"answer":1,"explain":"The rete mirabile is a mesh of vessels that helps prevent the giraffe's brain from getting flooded when it lowers its head to drink."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does the red liquid on a hippo's skin do?","options":["Helps it grip slippery rocks","Works as sunscreen and antiseptic","Cools it down like regular sweat"],"answer":1,"explain":"The red liquid, called blood sweat, protects the hippo’s skin from UV light and keeps it free from infections."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What happens to a rhinoceros's horn if it is cut off by rangers?","options":["It never grows back again","It regrows from its base","It turns soft and crumbles"],"answer":1,"explain":"The rhinoceros's horn, made of keratin, will regrow from its base if it is cut off."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How much water can a thirsty camel drink in ten minutes?","options":["13 gallons","26 gallons","5 gallons"],"answer":1,"explain":"A thirsty camel can drink more than 26 gallons of water in about ten minutes."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is the top speed of a reindeer?","options":["25 mph","37 mph","31 mph"],"answer":1,"explain":"Reindeer are fast runners, capable of reaching speeds up to 37 mph."}]},{"id":"animals-mammals-6","course":"animals","kind":"species","group":"mammals","title":"Moose, Pronghorn & Mountain goat","names":["Moose","Pronghorn","Mountain goat","Chimpanzee","Orangutan"],"glyph":"🦁","tint":"#ffc94a","items":["moose","pronghorn","mountain-goat","chimpanzee","orangutan"],"idea":"A moose can feed underwater for half a minute. Find out who runs 4 miles at 35 mph, who brakes with dewclaws, and who sways trees to cross gaps.","pages":[{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Five animals are waiting to meet you: a moose, a pronghorn, a mountain goat, a chimpanzee, and an orangutan."},{"t":"p","text":"Each one is special in its own way."}]},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the biggest deer on Earth, the moose. It wades through northern ponds pulling up water plants."},{"t":"fact","text":"A moose can seal its nostrils shut and feed off the pond floor for half a minute at a time. The widest moose antlers ever recorded spanned close to 7 feet (2.1 m) tip to tip, wider than a standard doorway is tall.","label":"The underwater eater"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How long can a moose stay underwater while feeding?","options":["10 seconds","30 seconds","5 minutes"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the first sentence of the fact box.","explain":"A moose can seal its nostrils shut and feed off the pond floor for half a minute at a time."}}],"img":"moose"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the pronghorn, a tan-and-white speedster of the North American plains. It’s not an antelope — it has its own family."},{"t":"fact","text":"A pronghorn hits about 55 mph (88 km/h) and can keep running at 35 mph (56 km/h) for four miles (6.4 km). That's a different kind of fast from the cheetah — no stop button.","label":"The long-distance runner"},{"t":"term","word":"keratin","text":"The same stuff as your fingernails and hair. A pronghorn's horns are made of it, and the outer sheath of each horn slides off every year and grows back."},{"t":"p","text":"Pronghorns can spot movement from very far away, thanks to their huge eyes that see almost 320 degrees around them without turning their heads."}],"img":"pronghorn"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the mountain goat, a shaggy white climber of North America's highest cliffs. Its home is on ledges barely wider than a book."},{"t":"fact","text":"Each hoof splits into two toes that spread wide for balance, with a rubbery pad in the middle that grips bare rock like a climbing shoe. Behind each hoof, sharp dewclaws dig in as brakes on the way down.","label":"The perfect climber"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What do mountain goats use as brakes when coming down from high ledges?","options":["Sharp dewclaws","Their horns","Their tails"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the last sentence of the fact box.","explain":"Sharp dewclaws act like brakes on the way down."}}],"img":"mountain-goat"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Next up is the chimpanzee, a powerful, noisy, tool-making ape of African forests. It lives in big groups where everybody knows everybody."},{"t":"fact","text":"In the Congo, chimps punch holes into termite nests with a stout stick and fish out termites with a thinner stem, its tip chewed into a brush. Different communities make different designs and pass them to their young.","label":"Tool kits"},{"t":"p","text":"Chimps also use stones as hammers and anvils to crack rock-hard nuts. It takes years of practice for a young chimp to get it right. Mothers help by setting up nuts and stones correctly for their children."}],"img":"chimpanzee"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Next up is the orangutan, a huge, ginger, unhurried ape that spends almost its entire life up in the trees of Borneo and Sumatra."},{"t":"fact","text":"Imagine your arms longer than you are tall. A male's arm span reaches about 6 feet 7 inches (2 m), while he stands only around 4.5 feet (1.4 m). The orangutan has four gripping hands, with hips loose enough to point its legs in any direction. To cross a gap between trees, it grips a trunk and sways it back and forth until the top leans over to the next tree.","label":"Arms and hands"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does an orangutan use to cross a gap between trees?","options":["Leaping","Swaying the tree","Dropping down to walk across"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box about arms and hands. It says what the orangutan does to reach the next tree.","explain":"They sway the tree back and forth until it leans far enough to reach the next one."}}],"img":"orangutan"},{"icon":"📊","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Now put the five side by side. The moose weighs about 992 lb and is 8.9 ft long. The pronghorn weighs about 110 lb and is 4.6 ft long. The mountain goat weighs about 198 lb and is 4.9 ft long. The chimpanzee weighs about 88 lb. The orangutan weighs about 132 lb."},{"t":"fact","text":"The moose can run at 35 mph, while the pronghorn is faster at 55 mph.","label":"Speed"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which of the five is the heaviest?","options":["The mountain goat","The moose","The orangutan"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the first paragraph and find the biggest number.","explain":"The moose, at about 992 lb."}}]},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A moose running 35 mph, a pronghorn at 55 mph, a mountain goat climbing cliffs, a chimpanzee weighing 88 lb, an orangutan weighing 132 lb."},{"t":"p","text":"Five animals, each one built for its own way of life."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Moose","Can stay underwater for 30 seconds"],["Pronghorn","Hits about 55 mph"],["Mountain goat","Sharp dewclaws act like brakes"],["Chimpanzee","Uses sticks to fish out termites"],["Orangutan","Arm span reaches about 6 feet 7 inches"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What material makes up the pronghorn's horns?","options":["Bone","Keratin","Muscle"],"answer":1,"explain":"Keratin is the same stuff as your fingernails."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How do mountain goats maintain balance on narrow ledges?","options":["Swinging their short tails sideways","Spreading their split hooves wide","Digging in with their curved horns"],"answer":1,"explain":"The split hooves spread wide for balance on narrow ledges."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What tool do chimps use to fish out termites?","options":["A rock with a sharp flat edge","A thin stem with a chewed tip","A large leaf folded into a scoop"],"answer":1,"explain":"Chimps chew the tip of the stem into a brush to fish out termites."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How long is an adult male orangutan's arm span?","options":["4.5 feet","6 feet 7 inches","8 feet"],"answer":1,"explain":"An adult male orangutan has an arm span that reaches about 6 feet 7 inches."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What speed can the pronghorn maintain for four miles?","options":["25 mph","35 mph","45 mph"],"answer":1,"explain":"The pronghorn can keep running at 35 mph for four miles."}]},{"id":"animals-mammals-7","course":"animals","kind":"species","group":"mammals","title":"Mandrill, Ring-tailed lemur & more","names":["Mandrill","Ring-tailed lemur","Tarsier","Vampire bat","Flying fox"],"glyph":"🦁","tint":"#ffc94a","items":["mandrill","ring-tailed-lemur","tarsier","vampire-bat","flying-fox"],"idea":"A male mandrill's face colors brighten when he rises in rank. Find out which animal fights with smell, which has eyes as big as its brain, and which bat hunts by heat.","pages":[{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Five animals are waiting to meet you: a mandrill, a ring-tailed lemur, a tarsier, a vampire bat, and a flying fox."},{"t":"p","text":"Each one is built for a different life."}]},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the mandrill, the biggest monkey in the world. A big male weighs around 55 lb (25 kg) — about as heavy as a nine-year-old kid."},{"t":"fact","text":"A male's scarlet nose stripe and blue cheek ridges brighten when he climbs in rank, and fade when he loses the top spot. Rivals read his color like a scoreboard.","label":"The big red nose"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What happens to a male mandrill's face colors when he moves up in rank?","options":["They stay the same whatever his rank","They fade when he moves up in rank","They brighten when he moves up in rank"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the box about the nose. It says what happens to a male's colors when his rank changes.","explain":"The colors of a male mandrill's face brighten when he gains status and fade when he loses it. This is how other mandrills read his rank."}}],"img":"mandrill"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the ring-tailed lemur. This cat-sized primate from Madagascar is known by its long striped tail that it carries straight up like a flag."},{"t":"fact","text":"Males settle arguments by smell. A male drags his tail across scent glands on his wrists and shoulders, arcs it over his head, and wafts it at his rival. This is called a stink fight.","label":"The stink fight"},{"t":"term","word":"waft","text":"To move something gently through the air, often to spread a smell."},{"t":"p","text":"The troop keeps different alarm calls for different dangers. One call means a hawk overhead, and another means a fossa, a cat-like hunter, on the ground."}],"img":"ring-tailed-lemur"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the tarsier, a tiny night-hunting primate from islands of Southeast Asia. Its eyes are so big they cannot move in their sockets."},{"t":"fact","text":"Each eyeball is about 0.63 inches (16 mm) across — as big as its own brain. Scaled up to your size, you would have eyes as big as grapefruit.","label":"The big eyes"},{"t":"p","text":"The tarsier can turn its head almost 180 degrees each way, enough to look back over its own spine. Its ears move separately and track a single insect through the dark by sound alone."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is one surprising fact about the tarsier's eyes?","options":["They are as big as its brain.","They are smaller than its brain.","They change color in winter."],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the box about the big eyes. It compares each eyeball to something else.","explain":"The tarsier's eyes are as big as its brain."}}],"img":"tarsier"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the vampire bat, a small bat from Mexico, Central, and South America. It lives on one food: the blood of sleeping animals."},{"t":"fact","text":"Pits around its nose read heat. Held close to an animal, they show it exactly where a blood vessel runs nearest the skin — like seeing a heat map.","label":"Heat-sensing nose"},{"t":"p","text":"It does not suck blood; instead, razor-edged front teeth open a shallow cut and it laps up the blood with grooves along its tongue."},{"t":"fact","text":"Its saliva contains an anticoagulant, a chemical that stops blood from clotting, so the tiny wound keeps flowing while it feeds.","label":"Feeding"}],"img":"vampire-bat"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the flying fox, the largest kind of bat on Earth. Its wings open wider than you are tall — about 5 ft (1.5 m) in the biggest kinds."},{"t":"fact","text":"Unlike other bats, flying foxes do not use echolocation. They find their food with big forward-facing eyes and an excellent nose, just like you would find a bakery.","label":"No sonar"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What do flying foxes use to locate fruit trees?","options":["Echoes and very sharp ears","Big eyes and a good nose","Heat pits and long whiskers"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. It tells you how they find food.","explain":"Flying foxes rely on their big eyes and excellent sense of smell to locate fruit trees, just like you might use your nose to find a bakery."}}],"img":"flying-fox"},{"icon":"📊","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Now put the five side by side. The mandrill is about 2.6 ft long. The ring-tailed lemur is around 1.4 ft. The tarsier is just 0.4 ft, and the vampire bat is even shorter at 0.3 ft. The flying fox is about 1 ft."},{"t":"fact","text":"The mandrill weighs about 55 lb, while the ring-tailed lemur weighs around 5 lb. The tarsier and vampire bat each weigh only a few ounces. The flying fox is around 2 lb.","label":"Weights"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which of the five is the longest?","options":["The mandrill","The ring-tailed lemur","The flying fox"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the first paragraph and find the longest length.","explain":"The mandrill, at about 2.6 ft long, is the longest of the five."}}]},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A mandrill with a colorful face. A ring-tailed lemur with a long striped tail. A tarsier with eyes as big as its brain. A vampire bat that feeds on blood. A flying fox with big wings."},{"t":"p","text":"Five animals, five different jobs, each one made for its own life."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Mandrill","Face colors show status"],["Ring-tailed lemur","Uses a stink fight with its tail"],["Tarsier","Eyes as big as its brain"],["Vampire bat","Feeds on blood using heat-sensing nose"],["Flying fox","Biggest bats that use eyes and nose to find fruit"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What do male ring-tailed lemurs use to settle arguments?","options":["A slap fight with their long striped tails","A stink fight with their scented tails","A loud shouting match from the treetops"],"answer":1,"explain":"Ring-tailed lemurs use their sense of smell to resolve conflicts."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How big are a tarsier's eyes compared to its brain?","options":["Smaller than its brain","As big as its brain","Twice the size of its brain"],"answer":1,"explain":"Tarsiers have eyes that are as big as their brains, which helps them see well at night."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What helps vampire bats find blood vessels on an animal?","options":["Sharp claws on their feet","Heat pits on their nose","Night vision in their eyes"],"answer":1,"explain":"Vampire bats use heat-sensing pits to locate blood vessels on their prey."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How do flying foxes navigate at night?","options":["Using sonar like other bats","With big eyes and a good sense of smell","By following the sound of ripening fruit"],"answer":1,"explain":"Flying foxes rely on their large eyes and strong sense of smell to find food."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"About how much does a big male mandrill weigh?","options":["Around 5 lb","About 55 lb","Around 2 lb"],"answer":1,"explain":"The mandrill is much heavier than the other animals, weighing around 55 lb."}]},{"id":"animals-mammals-8","course":"animals","kind":"species","group":"mammals","title":"Blue whale, Humpback whale & more","names":["Blue whale","Humpback whale","Sperm whale","Narwhal","Orca"],"glyph":"🦁","tint":"#ffc94a","items":["blue-whale","humpback-whale","sperm-whale","narwhal","orca"],"idea":"A blue whale's heart alone weighs about 400 pounds. Find out whose flippers are the longest, whose clicks are the loudest, and whose tusk is really a tooth.","pages":[{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Five animals are waiting to meet you: a blue whale, a humpback whale, a sperm whale, a narwhal, and an orca."},{"t":"p","text":"Each one has a unique life under the sea."}]},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the blue whale, the largest animal on Earth. It can grow to about 82 ft (25 m) long and weighs around 265,000 lb (120 metric tons)."},{"t":"fact","text":"Its heart alone weighs about 400 pounds (180 kg), the biggest of any living animal. One blue whale heart, from a whale that washed ashore in Newfoundland, Canada, was preserved by a museum. It is still the only one on display.","label":"The heart"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How much does a blue whale's heart weigh?","options":["200 pounds","400 pounds","600 pounds"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It gives the exact weight.","explain":"The blue whale's heart weighs about 400 pounds."}}],"img":"blue-whale"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Next up, meet the humpback whale. It's about 49 ft (15 m) long and has enormous white flippers with bumps on the edge, called tubercles."},{"t":"fact","text":"Its flippers are the longest of any animal — up to 16 feet (5 m), about a third of its whole body. Its scientific name, Megaptera, means big wing.","label":"The longest flippers"},{"t":"term","word":"tubercles","text":"Bumps on the edge of a humpback whale's flipper that help it swim better."},{"t":"p","text":"Humpbacks have a habit of leaping clear of the water and singing long songs. Only the males sing these songs, which can run from a few minutes to half an hour."}],"img":"humpback-whale"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Next, meet the sperm whale. It’s about 52 ft (16 m) long and has a huge blunt head built for diving deep to catch squid."},{"t":"fact","text":"The sperm whale makes the loudest sounds of any animal — sharp clicks that carry for miles underwater. It listens for the echoes to find squid in the dark depths where no light reaches.","label":"The loudest animal"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does a sperm whale use to find its prey in total darkness?","options":["Sound clicks","Its eyes","Its flippers"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the box. It says what the whale listens for in the dark.","explain":"The sperm whale uses sound clicks to echolocate and find its prey in the dark depths of the ocean."}}],"img":"sperm-whale"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Next up is the narwhal — an Arctic whale with a long spiral tusk growing straight out of its head."},{"t":"fact","text":"The tusk is a tooth — the narwhal's left canine, grown straight out through its upper lip. It can reach 10 feet (3 m) and weigh over 20 pounds (9 kg). Researchers found it is porous with nerve channels, feeling the sea the whale swims through.","label":"The tooth that feels"},{"t":"p","text":"Almost every tusk belongs to a male, though a few females grow one and the odd narwhal grows two. The tusk keeps growing for most of the whale's life. Narwhals have no other working teeth — their mouths are otherwise nearly bare."}],"img":"narwhal"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the orca, often called a killer whale, though it is actually the largest dolphin."},{"t":"fact","text":"A large male orca can reach 32 feet (9.8 m) long and weigh as much as two elephants, about 11 tons.","label":"The big dolphin"},{"t":"p","text":"Every family of orcas has its own dialect — a set of calls that only they use. Researchers can tell which family is swimming by their sounds alone."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What makes each orca family unique?","options":["Their size","Their color","Their dialect"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the paragraph about families and their calls.","explain":"Each family has its own set of calls, or a dialect."}}],"img":"orca"},{"icon":"📊","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Now put the five side by side. The blue whale weighs about 264,554 lb, the sperm whale about 88,185 lb and the humpback whale about 66,139 lb. A typical orca weighs about 8,818 lb and the narwhal about 2,205 lb."},{"t":"fact","text":"The orca can swim up to 30 mph. The blue whale and sperm whale both swim at 20 mph.","label":"Speed"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which of the five is the heaviest?","options":["The sperm whale","The humpback whale","The blue whale"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the first paragraph and find the biggest number.","explain":"The blue whale, at about 264,554 lb."}}]},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A blue whale with a 400-lb heart. A humpback whale that sings. A sperm whale with the loudest voice in the sea. A narwhal with a spiraled tusk. An orca whose family has its own dialect."},{"t":"p","text":"Five mammals, all different, and each one doing what it does best."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Blue whale","Weighs around 265,000 lb"],["Humpback whale","Flippers are up to 16 feet"],["Sperm whale","Loudest sounds of any animal"],["Narwhal","Has a long spiral tusk"],["Orca","Swims up to 30 mph"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is the longest a narwhal's tusk can reach?","options":["5 feet","10 feet","8 feet"],"answer":1,"explain":"The narwhal's tusk can grow up to an impressive 10 feet."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is the humpback whale's scientific name?","options":["Balaenoptera","Megaptera","Orcinus"],"answer":1,"explain":"Megaptera means big wing, fitting for the humpback's long flippers."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How fast can an orca swim?","options":["25 mph","30 mph","20 mph"],"answer":1,"explain":"Orcas are speedy swimmers, reaching up to 30 mph."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is special about the sperm whale's sound?","options":["It whistles","It's the loudest","It hums"],"answer":1,"explain":"The sperm whale makes the loudest sounds of any animal underwater."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which humpback whales sing the long songs?","options":["Only the young calves","Only the females","Only the males"],"answer":2,"explain":"Only male humpbacks sing these long songs that can last from a few minutes to half an hour."}]},{"id":"animals-mammals-9","course":"animals","kind":"species","group":"mammals","title":"Bottlenose dolphin & more","names":["Bottlenose dolphin","Sea otter","Walrus","Star-nosed mole","Capybara"],"glyph":"🦁","tint":"#ffc94a","items":["bottlenose-dolphin","sea-otter","walrus","star-nosed-mole","capybara"],"idea":"A star-nosed mole can handle a worm in a quarter of a second. Find out who finds fish with echoes, who has a million hairs in a patch of fur, and who feels for clams with whiskers.","pages":[{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Five animals are waiting to meet you: a bottlenose dolphin, a sea otter, a walrus, a star-nosed mole, and a capybara."},{"t":"p","text":"Each one has its own way of getting a meal."}]},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Start with a bottlenose dolphin, a fast, clever sea mammal that breathes air and travels in groups."},{"t":"p","text":"Click. Listen. Click again. A dolphin fires sound out of its forehead and listens to the echoes coming back. That is echolocation. It tells the dolphin the fish's size, shape and direction without ever seeing it."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does a bottlenose dolphin use to locate fish?","options":["It smells the fish underwater","It fires sound and listens to the echoes","It uses its tail to feel vibrations"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the second paragraph. It explains how a dolphin uses sound.","explain":"It fires sound and listens to the echoes. This is called echolocation."}}],"img":"bottlenose-dolphin"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the sea otter, a dog-sized mammal of the cold North Pacific. It floats on its back and cracks shellfish open with a rock."},{"t":"term","word":"blubber","text":"A thick layer of fat under the skin that keeps animals warm in cold water. Sea otters don't have this."},{"t":"fact","text":"No blubber at all. Instead, up to a million hairs in a patch as big as a postage stamp — the densest fur on Earth. The fur traps air against its skin, like wearing a coat of bubbles.","label":"The coat"}],"img":"sea-otter"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the walrus, an enormous Arctic sea mammal with two ivory tusks and a bristling mustache. It hauls out on sea ice and vacuums clams from the seafloor."},{"t":"fact","text":"Count the whiskers: 400 to 700 of them in thirteen to fifteen neat rows across the snout. In pitch-black water, a walrus finds clams buried in mud by feel alone.","label":"The whiskers"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many whiskers does a walrus have?","options":["200 to 300","400 to 700","100 to 200"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It gives the exact number range.","explain":"A walrus has between 400 and 700 whiskers, arranged neatly in rows on its snout."}}],"img":"walrus"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the star-nosed mole, a small, dark animal with twenty-two pink tentacles ringing its nose. It is the fastest eater ever measured among mammals."},{"t":"p","text":"Those pink tentacles are not for smelling; they are for touching. The star carries more than 100,000 nerve fibers — about six times what your entire hand has."},{"t":"fact","text":"From first touch to swallowed, a star-nosed mole handles a worm in just a quarter of a second. Its brain decides if something is food in about 8 thousandths of a second.","label":"Fast eating"}],"img":"star-nosed-mole"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the capybara, the largest rodent in the world. It is a calm, barrel-shaped animal that lives with one foot in the river."},{"t":"p","text":"The capybara's teeth never stop growing. It grinds them down all day on tough grass to keep them from overgrowing."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How does the capybara keep its teeth from overgrowing?","options":["By crunching on small river stones","By soaking them in cool river water","By grinding them down on tough grass"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. It explains how the capybara grinds down its teeth.","explain":"The capybara grinds down its teeth on tough grass to keep them from overgrowing."}}],"img":"capybara"},{"icon":"📊","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Put these animals side by side. The walrus weighs about 2,646 lb and the bottlenose dolphin about 551 lb. The capybara weighs about 110 lb, the sea otter about 65 lb and the star-nosed mole only about 2 oz."},{"t":"fact","text":"The capybara can run at 22 mph on land, while the bottlenose dolphin can swim at up to 18 mph.","label":"Speed"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which of the five is the heaviest?","options":["The walrus","The bottlenose dolphin","The capybara"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the first paragraph and find the biggest number.","explain":"The walrus, at about 2,646 lb."}}]},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A bottlenose dolphin that finds fish with echoes. A sea otter with its rock and clam. A walrus that weighs 2,646 lb. A star-nosed mole that hunts in the dark. A capybara that runs at 22 mph."},{"t":"p","text":"Five animals, each different, and each built for their own world."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Bottlenose dolphin","Uses sound echoes to find fish"],["Sea otter","Floats on its back cracking shellfish with a rock"],["Walrus","Has 400 to 700 whiskers for finding clams"],["Star-nosed mole","Hunts using 100,000 nerve fibers around its nose"],["Capybara","Grinds down never-stopping teeth on tough grass"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many hairs can be found in a small patch of a sea otter's fur?","options":["About 50,000 hairs","Up to a million hairs","Nearly 200,000 hairs"],"answer":1,"explain":"Sea otters have the densest fur on Earth to keep warm in cold water."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How does a walrus find clams buried in mud?","options":["By raking up the mud with its long tusks","By feeling with 400 to 700 whiskers","By spotting them with its sharp eyes"],"answer":1,"explain":"A walrus uses its whiskers to feel for clams in the dark ocean floor."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What helps the star-nosed mole decide if something is food?","options":["Its keen sense of smell, which detects food from far away","Its star-shaped nose with over 100,000 nerve fibers","Its large eyes, which can see small worms in the dark"],"answer":1,"explain":"The star-nosed mole's unique nose helps it make quick decisions about what to eat."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What keeps the capybara's teeth from overgrowing?","options":["It crunches small stones and gravel","It grinds them down on tough grass","It soaks them in cool river water"],"answer":1,"explain":"Grinding down its teeth on tough grass prevents overgrowth for the capybara."}]},{"id":"animals-mammals-10","course":"animals","kind":"species","group":"mammals","title":"Beaver, Porcupine & more","names":["Beaver","Porcupine","Striped skunk","Nine-banded armadillo","Meerkat"],"glyph":"🦁","tint":"#ffc94a","items":["beaver","porcupine","skunk","armadillo","meerkat"],"idea":"A beaver's teeth never stop growing. Find out whose quills only come out when touched, which animal has only five or six shots of spray, and which one always keeps a lookout.","pages":[{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Five animals are waiting to meet you: a beaver, a porcupine, a striped skunk, a nine-banded armadillo, and a meerkat."},{"t":"p","text":"Each one has its own special way of living."}]},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the beaver, a thickset, buck-toothed rodent that cuts down trees with its teeth and builds dams to create ponds."},{"t":"fact","text":"A beaver's four front teeth never stop growing. It has to gnaw every day or they would grow right past its jaw.","label":"The never-stopping teeth"},{"t":"p","text":"Every bite grinds the tooth back into a fresh chisel edge. The front face is hard enamel, the back softer dentine. The soft side wears away faster, so the teeth sharpen themselves on the job."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What happens if a beaver doesn't gnaw every day?","options":["Its teeth turn soft and fall out","Its teeth split in two and break","Its teeth grow right past its jaw"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the box. It says what happens if the beaver does not gnaw every day.","explain":"If a beaver doesn't gnaw every day, its teeth would grow right past its jaw."}}],"img":"beaver"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Next, meet the porcupine — a slow, shaggy rodent that climbs trees and is covered with about thirty thousand sharp quills."},{"t":"term","word":"barbs","text":"Tiny hooks that make something hard to pull out. Porcupine quills have these to stay stuck in anything they touch."},{"t":"fact","text":"A porcupine cannot throw its quills. It turns its back, hunches up, and reverses at you — the quills, which have tiny barbs, release only when touched.","label":"The myth"}],"img":"porcupine"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Next up is the striped skunk — a cat-sized black-and-white forager that can spray a foul-smelling chemical from two nozzles under its tail."},{"t":"p","text":"The spray is a last resort. First, the skunk stamps its front feet, hisses, arches its back, fluffs up, and hoists its tail like a flag."},{"t":"fact","text":"A skunk carries only about five or six shots — roughly a tablespoon in total. Once the glands run dry, it can take up to ten days to refill them.","label":"The spray"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many shots does a skunk carry?","options":["Two or three","About five or six","Ten or more"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the first sentence of the fact box.","explain":"A skunk carries only about five or six shots."}}],"img":"skunk"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Next up is the nine-banded armadillo — a digging, insect-hunting mammal built inside a jointed shell of bony plates."},{"t":"fact","text":"Every litter is identical quadruplets. Four babies, all the same sex, all grown from one egg that splits into four.","label":"The quadruplets"},{"t":"p","text":"An armadillo gulps air to swim or walks across the bottom of a creek holding its breath for up to six minutes."}],"img":"armadillo"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Next up is the meerkat — a small, sharp-eyed African mongoose that lives in big underground families."},{"t":"fact","text":"Somebody is always watching. One meerkat climbs the highest thing around and scans the sky while the rest dig, then swaps out for the next volunteer.","label":"The lookout"}],"img":"meerkat"},{"icon":"📊","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Now put the five side by side. The beaver weighs about 44 lb, the porcupine about 18 lb, the nine-banded armadillo about 11 lb and the striped skunk about 7 lb. The meerkat weighs only about 2 lb."},{"t":"fact","text":"A beaver swims at about 5 mph, steering with its flat tail and pushing with its webbed back feet.","label":"Speed"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which of the five is the heaviest?","options":["The beaver","The porcupine","The striped skunk"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the first paragraph and find the biggest number.","explain":"The beaver, at about 44 lb."}}]},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A beaver that builds dams, a porcupine that climbs trees, a striped skunk that sprays, an armadillo that digs, and a meerkat that watches."},{"t":"p","text":"Five animals, each with their own way to live."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Beaver","Builds dams with its never-stopping teeth"],["Porcupine","Covered with thirty thousand sharp quills"],["Striped skunk","Sprays a foul-smelling chemical as a last resort"],["Nine-banded armadillo","Digs and hunts insects inside a jointed shell"],["Meerkat","Lives in big underground families with a lookout always watching"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does a beaver use to cut down trees?","options":["Claws","Teeth","Paws"],"answer":1,"explain":"Beavers are known for their strong teeth that help them cut down trees."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How do porcupine quills get released?","options":["Thrown by the porcupine","By touching something","By shaking its body like a wet dog"],"answer":1,"explain":"Porcupine quills release when they touch something, not by being thrown."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does a skunk do first before spraying?","options":["Digs a hole to hide in","Stamps its front feet","Runs in wide circles"],"answer":1,"explain":"Skunks give warning signs like stamping their feet before they spray."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How long can an armadillo hold its breath underwater?","options":["2 minutes","6 minutes","10 minutes"],"answer":1,"explain":"Armadillos can hold their breath for up to six minutes underwater."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does a meerkat do to watch for danger?","options":["Hide deep and stay very still","Climb high and scan the sky","Race around in wide circles"],"answer":1,"explain":"Meerkats take turns climbing high to keep an eye out for danger."}]},{"id":"animals-birds-1","course":"animals","kind":"species","group":"birds","title":"Peregrine falcon & more","names":["Peregrine falcon","Bald eagle","Barn owl","Snowy owl","Wandering albatross"],"glyph":"🦅","tint":"#4fd6e8","items":["peregrine-falcon","bald-eagle","barn-owl","snowy-owl","wandering-albatross"],"idea":"A peregrine falcon dives faster than 200 mph. Find out whose wings make no sound, whose toes wear feathered boots, and who glides 600 miles without flapping.","pages":[{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Five birds are waiting to meet you: a peregrine falcon, a bald eagle, a barn owl, a snowy owl, and a wandering albatross."},{"t":"p","text":"Each one has its own special skill."}]},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the peregrine falcon, a crow-sized bird that hunts other birds from mid-air. It dives faster than any other animal on Earth."},{"t":"fact","text":"In a stoop — a folded-wing drop — it hits 200 mph (320 km/h). Level flight isn't so fast though; flapping along, it's no quicker than many ordinary birds.","label":"The dive"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is the peregrine falcon's top diving speed?","options":["150 mph","200 mph","250 mph"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It mentions the exact speed in a stoop.","explain":"The peregrine falcon can reach 200 mph during a dive, faster than any other animal."}}],"img":"peregrine-falcon"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Next up is the bald eagle, a huge fish-hunting sea eagle with a snow-white head, found only in North America."},{"t":"fact","text":"Stretch its wings out and they span up to 7 feet 7 inches (2.3 m) — wider than a tall man standing up.","label":"The wingspan"},{"t":"term","word":"spicules","text":"Rough spikes on the soles of an eagle's toes that help it grip slippery fish."},{"t":"p","text":"The bald eagle's feathers weigh more than twice as much as its hollow bones. Rough spikes on the soles of its toes, called spicules, help it grip slippery fish."}],"img":"bald-eagle"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Next up is the barn owl. It has a pale face and catches mice in total darkness using only its hearing."},{"t":"fact","text":"A barn owl flies without making a sound. Comb-like teeth along its flight feathers break the air apart before it can turn into noise. The back edge of the wing is fringed like a soft brush, and the whole surface is velvety, soaking up sound.","label":"The silent hunter"}],"img":"barn-owl"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Next is the snowy owl, a huge white owl from the Arctic that hunts in broad daylight."},{"t":"fact","text":"A snowy owl's toes are buried under feathers that average an inch and a third (33 mm) long. That’s like walking on built-in boots.","label":"The feathered boot"},{"t":"p","text":"Most owls hunt at night, but snowy owls hunt in full daylight because the sun never sets during Arctic summers."}],"img":"snowy-owl"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the wandering albatross, a giant seabird of the Southern Ocean with the longest wings of any living bird. Its wings can span up to 11 feet (3.5 m) tip to tip."},{"t":"fact","text":"The albatross can cover 600 miles (1,000 km) in a day without flapping once. It falls forwards through the wind over and over, using a trick called dynamic soaring.","label":"The flight"},{"t":"p","text":"A sheet of tendon locks its shoulder when the wing is fully out, so the wing stays open with no muscle working at all. Gliding costs it almost nothing."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How does the albatross cover such long distances without flapping?","options":["It falls forwards through the wind over and over, using dynamic soaring.","It uses its long, narrow wings to glide on rising currents of warm air.","It flaps its huge wings slowly just once every few hours to stay up."],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It tells you how the albatross covers long distances without flapping.","explain":"The albatross uses dynamic soaring by falling forwards through the wind over and over."}}],"img":"wandering-albatross"},{"icon":"📊","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Now put the five birds side by side. The wandering albatross weighs about 19 lb, the bald eagle about 10 lb and the snowy owl about 4 lb. The peregrine falcon weighs about 2 lb and the barn owl only about 1 lb."},{"t":"fact","text":"The peregrine falcon can reach a speed of 200 mph, much faster than the wandering albatross's 50 mph.","label":"Speed"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which of the five is the heaviest?","options":["The American bald eagle","The pale-faced barn owl","The wandering albatross"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the first paragraph and find the biggest number.","explain":"The wandering albatross, at about 19 lb."}}]},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A peregrine falcon diving at 200 mph. A bald eagle with a 7-foot-7-inch wingspan. A barn owl hunting in the dark. A snowy owl with feathered boots. A wandering albatross gliding for miles."},{"t":"p","text":"Five birds, each with its own unique way of flying and living."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Peregrine falcon","Dives faster than any other animal"],["Bald eagle","Has a wingspan wider than a tall man"],["Barn owl","Hunts silently using only its hearing"],["Snowy owl","Has long feathers on its toes like boots"],["Wandering albatross","Uses dynamic soaring to cover long distances without flapping"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What helps the barn owl fly silently?","options":["Long, smooth feathers on its back","Comb-like teeth along its flight feathers","Extra-long tail feathers that steer it"],"answer":1,"explain":"The comb-like teeth break the air to stop noise."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How wide can a bald eagle spread its wings?","options":["5 feet 3 inches (1.6 m)","7 feet 7 inches (2.3 m)","8 feet 9 inches (2.7 m)"],"answer":1,"explain":"That's wider than a tall man standing up."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is special about the snowy owl’s toes?","options":["They are covered in thick fur","They are buried under long feathers","They have sharp talons for digging"],"answer":1,"explain":"The feathers act like built-in boots to keep its feet warm."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How does the peregrine falcon dive so fast?","options":["By flapping its wings very quickly","By folding its wings and dropping","By gliding on air currents"],"answer":1,"explain":"When it folds its wings, it can hit 200 mph in a dive."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why do snowy owls hunt during the day?","options":["They prefer warmer temperatures","The sun never sets in Arctic summers","They cannot see well in the dark"],"answer":1,"explain":"The sun never sets in the Arctic summer, so there is no night to hunt in."}]},{"id":"animals-birds-2","course":"animals","kind":"species","group":"birds","title":"Bar-tailed godwit & more","names":["Bar-tailed godwit","Arctic tern","Emperor penguin","Ostrich","Emu"],"glyph":"🦅","tint":"#4fd6e8","items":["bar-tailed-godwit","arctic-tern","emperor-penguin","ostrich","emu"],"idea":"A bird flew 8,435 miles without stopping. Find out who runs 43 mph, who follows summer around the world, and whose father sits on dark green eggs for eight weeks.","pages":[{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Five birds are waiting to meet you: a bar-tailed godwit, an Arctic tern, an emperor penguin, an ostrich, and an emu."},{"t":"p","text":"Each one does something different."}]},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Start with the bar-tailed godwit, a long-billed wading bird that holds the record for the longest non-stop flight of any animal."},{"t":"term","word":"wading bird","text":"A long-legged bird that hunts for food in shallow water."},{"t":"fact","text":"One godwit flew from Alaska to Tasmania without stopping — 8,435 miles (13,575 km) in 11 days. It was tracked by a tiny satellite tag and was making the journey for the first time.","label":"The journey"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What was the distance flown by the bar-tailed godwit?","options":["5,000 miles","8,435 miles","12,000 miles"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. It gives the exact number.","explain":"The godwit flew 8,435 miles (13,575 km) without stopping."}}],"img":"bar-tailed-godwit"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the Arctic tern, a small silver seabird that follows summer from one end of the planet to the other, every single year."},{"t":"fact","text":"Tracked birds cover about 44,000 miles (71,000 km) in a year, following a looping path rather than a straight line. Over a 30-year life, that adds up to nearly three round trips to the Moon.","label":"The journey"},{"t":"p","text":"Chasing summer at both ends of the world, an Arctic tern sees more daylight in a year than any other creature alive."}],"img":"arctic-tern"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the emperor penguin, the largest penguin alive and the only bird that breeds through the Antarctic winter."},{"t":"fact","text":"The father holds the single egg on his feet under a fold of skin for about two months of Antarctic winter. He does not eat the whole time.","label":"The father's job"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How long does the father emperor penguin hold the egg?","options":["4 weeks","2 months","6 weeks"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the last two sentences. The exact number is given.","explain":"The father holds the egg for about 2 months of Antarctic winter without eating."}}],"img":"emperor-penguin"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the ostrich, the biggest bird on Earth and the fastest on two legs. It cannot fly but is built entirely for running."},{"t":"fact","text":"An ostrich can run at 43 mph (70 km/h) and hold about 30 mph (48 km/h) for half an hour. No other two-legged animal comes close.","label":"The sprinter"},{"t":"p","text":"Its eye is about 2 in (5 cm) across, the largest eye of any land animal, and bigger than its own brain."}],"img":"ostrich"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Next up is the emu, Australia’s tallest bird. It’s shaggy and flightless, but built for wandering."},{"t":"fact","text":"The father does all the work. He sits on dark green eggs for about eight weeks without eating much, and raises the chicks for up to 18 months.","label":"The father"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How long can an emu father sit on the eggs without eating properly?","options":["Eight weeks","Two weeks","Four weeks"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the box about the father. It says how long he sits on the eggs.","explain":"The father sits on the eggs for about eight weeks without eating much."}}],"img":"emu"},{"icon":"📊","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Now put the five side by side. The ostrich weighs about 287 lb, the emperor penguin about 84 lb and the emu about 121 lb. The bar-tailed godwit weighs about 1 lb and the Arctic tern about 0.24 lb."},{"t":"fact","text":"The ostrich can run at 43 mph, while the bar-tailed godwit flies at 35 mph and the emu runs at 31 mph.","label":"Speed"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which of the five is the heaviest?","options":["The ostrich","The emu","The emperor penguin"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the first paragraph and find the biggest number.","explain":"The ostrich, at about 287 lb."}}]},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"An ostrich that runs at 43 mph. An Arctic tern that flies for miles. A bar-tailed godwit that soars at 35 mph. An emu that dashes at 31 mph. An emperor penguin that holds its egg through the winter."},{"t":"p","text":"Five birds, each with their own way to move."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Bar-tailed godwit","Flew 8,435 miles non-stop"],["Arctic tern","Follows summer around the world"],["Emperor penguin","Breeds through Antarctic winter"],["Ostrich","Runs at 43 mph"],["Emu","Father sits on dark green eggs for eight weeks"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is the top speed of an ostrich?","options":["30 mph","43 mph","50 mph"],"answer":1,"explain":"An ostrich can sprint at 43 mph (70 km/h)."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does the Arctic tern do every year?","options":["Flies to the tropics for good","Follows summer around the world","Stays near the same Arctic beach"],"answer":1,"explain":"The Arctic tern chases the sun to get more daylight."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is the longest an emu father keeps raising the chicks?","options":["6 months","Up to 18 months","3 months"],"answer":1,"explain":"The emu dad is very involved in raising the young ones!"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is special about the ostrich's eye?","options":["It can see in complete darkness","It is bigger than its own brain","It glows in the dark"],"answer":1,"explain":"An ostrich's eye is about 2 in (5 cm) across, bigger than its own brain."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How far does the bar-tailed godwit fly without stopping?","options":["7,000 miles","8,435 miles","10,000 miles"],"answer":1,"explain":"The bar-tailed godwit can fly for thousands of miles non-stop!"}]},{"id":"animals-birds-3","course":"animals","kind":"species","group":"birds","title":"Kiwi, Bee hummingbird & more","names":["Kiwi","Bee hummingbird","Ruby-throated hummingbird","Kea","New Caledonian crow"],"glyph":"🦅","tint":"#4fd6e8","items":["kiwi","bee-hummingbird","ruby-throated-hummingbird","kea","new-caledonian-crow"],"idea":"The kea picks rubber seals off cars for fun. Find out which bird sniffs out worms, which flies 500 miles over open water, and which carves a hook from a twig.","pages":[{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Five birds are waiting to meet you: a kiwi, a bee hummingbird, a ruby-throated hummingbird, a kea, and a New Caledonian crow."},{"t":"p","text":"Each has a special story to tell."}]},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The kiwi is a flightless bird from New Zealand, about the size of a chicken. It hunts at night by smell, poking its long beak into the ground."},{"t":"fact","text":"The kiwi has nostrils at the very tip of its beak. This lets it sniff the ground and dig straight down after worms.","label":"The nose at the tip"}],"img":"kiwi"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the smallest bird in the world — the bee hummingbird. It’s so tiny, people often mistake it for a big bee."},{"t":"fact","text":"A male bee hummingbird weighs about 0.07 oz (1.95 g) — less than a US penny — and is only 2 inches (5.5 cm) long.","label":"The tiniest bird"},{"t":"term","word":"cobweb","text":"A very thin spider web used by the female bee hummingbird to build its nest."},{"t":"p","text":"The female bee hummingbird builds a tiny nest using materials like cobwebs, bark, and lichen. The nest is about 1 inch (2.5 cm) across — small enough to sit on a coin."}],"img":"bee-hummingbird"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the ruby-throated hummingbird. It’s the little green hummingbird you might see at a garden feeder in eastern North America."},{"t":"p","text":"Before flying over the Gulf of Mexico, it eats a lot to double its fat reserves. This gives it enough fuel for a long flight."},{"t":"fact","text":"The ruby-throated hummingbird can fly over 500 miles (800 km) of open water without stopping. The trip takes about 18 to 22 hours.","label":"The flight"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How does the ruby-throated hummingbird prepare for its long flight over the Gulf of Mexico?","options":["By flying south in a big flock","By growing extra wing feathers","By eating a lot to double its fat"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the second paragraph and find how it prepares.","explain":"It doubles its fat reserves by eating a lot before flying."}}],"img":"ruby-throated-hummingbird"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the kea, a big olive-green parrot that lives in New Zealand's snowy mountains. It’s the world's only alpine parrot."},{"t":"fact","text":"The kea takes cars apart for fun. It picks rubber seals out of windshields and strips wiper blades, just to see what happens.","label":"The curious kea"}],"img":"kea"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the New Caledonian crow, a glossy black bird from a Pacific island. It makes hooked tools out of twigs — something very few animals besides humans do."},{"t":"fact","text":"This crow snips a forked twig off a branch, strips the leaves, and carves it into a neat little hook. Then it uses the hook to fish for beetle grubs inside rotting wood.","label":"The clever hook"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does the New Caledonian crow use its hooked twig tool to catch?","options":["Small birds nesting inside tree holes","Fat beetle grubs jammed deep in wood","Ripe fruit hanging high in the trees"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the second sentence. It tells you what the crow catches with its hook.","explain":"The crow uses its hooked twig to catch fat beetle grubs jammed deep in wood."}}],"img":"new-caledonian-crow"},{"icon":"📊","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The kea weighs about 2 lb and measures 1.6 ft long. The bee hummingbird is the lightest at about 0.07 oz (2 g) and just 0.2 ft long."},{"t":"fact","text":"The ruby-throated hummingbird can fly up to 30 mph, while the bee hummingbird reaches about 26 mph.","label":"Speed"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which of the five birds is the lightest?","options":["The bee hummingbird","The alpine kea parrot","The brown kiwi bird"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the first paragraph. One bird is named the lightest.","explain":"The bee hummingbird is the lightest, at about 0.07 oz (2 g)."}}]},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A kiwi that sniffs out worms. A bee hummingbird weighing almost nothing. A ruby-throated hummingbird zipping at 30 mph. A kea that takes cars apart. A New Caledonian crow using sticks to find food."},{"t":"p","text":"Five birds, each so different, and every one built for its own world."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Kiwi","Hunts at night by smell"],["Bee hummingbird","Weighs less than a penny"],["Ruby-throated hummingbird","Flies 500 miles over the Gulf of Mexico"],["Kea","Takes apart cars for fun"],["New Caledonian crow","Uses hooked tools to catch beetle grubs"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does the kiwi use its beak for?","options":["Spearing fish and frogs in streams","Sniffing and digging for worms","Pecking holes in tree trunks"],"answer":1,"explain":"The kiwi's special beak helps it hunt at night."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How much does a male bee hummingbird weigh?","options":["About as heavy as a nickel","Less than a US penny","About as heavy as a grape"],"answer":1,"explain":"A male bee hummingbird weighs about 0.07 oz (1.95 g), less than a US penny."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does the kea do for fun?","options":["Play with leaves","Take cars apart","Sing songs"],"answer":1,"explain":"The kea loves to play and explore, even if that means taking things apart."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How does the New Caledonian crow make its tool?","options":["By bending a straight twig","By carving a forked twig into a hook","By using its beak to shape a rock"],"answer":1,"explain":"The crow is very clever with its tools."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where can you find the ruby-throated hummingbird?","options":["In the dry deserts of southern Africa","At garden feeders in eastern North America","On the icy shores of northern Greenland"],"answer":1,"explain":"This little green bird loves to visit gardens for nectar."}]},{"id":"animals-birds-4","course":"animals","kind":"species","group":"birds","title":"African gray parrot & more","names":["African gray parrot","Superb lyrebird","Indian peafowl","Greater flamingo","Toco toucan"],"glyph":"🦅","tint":"#4fd6e8","items":["african-grey-parrot","lyrebird","peacock","flamingo","toucan"],"idea":"A parrot named Alex learned the idea of \"none\". Find out which bird plays back the forest, which has eyespots in its tail, and which has a bill that is mostly air.","pages":[{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Five birds are waiting to meet you: an African gray parrot, a superb lyrebird, an Indian peafowl, a greater flamingo, and a toco toucan."},{"t":"p","text":"Each one has something unique about it."}]},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the African gray parrot, a dust-gray bird from the forests of central Africa with a scarlet tail and a mind that solves problems."},{"t":"fact","text":"A gray parrot named Alex learned over a hundred English words and could answer questions he had never been taught.","label":"Alex's words"},{"t":"p","text":"In one test, Alex said \"none\" when asked about an amount that was not there. Nobody had taught him a word for zero, so he used a word he already knew."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did Alex say when asked about an amount that was not present?","options":["Said \"many\"","Said \"none\"","Said \"all\""],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the first sentence of the paragraph under the box. It tells you the word Alex said when there was nothing to count.","explain":"Alex said \"none\" when there was no amount to count."}}],"img":"african-grey-parrot"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the Superb lyrebird, a chicken-sized Australian songbird with a tail longer than its body and a voice that copies every bird in the forest."},{"t":"fact","text":"Seven or eight out of every ten sounds a male lyrebird makes are copied from other birds. He is not really singing his own song; he is playing back the forest.","label":"The copycat"},{"t":"term","word":"filaments","text":"Thin, string-like parts that make up the tail feathers."},{"t":"p","text":"The male's lyre-shaped tail has sixteen feathers, including two curved outer ones and twelve fine silvery filaments in between."}],"img":"lyrebird"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the Indian peafowl, a blue-and-green bird whose male grows a fan of shimmering eyespots taller than a doorway."},{"t":"fact","text":"No dye in that train. The color is built into the shape of each feather, like a soap bubble or an oil slick on water.","label":"The feather trick"}],"img":"peacock"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Next up is the greater flamingo, a tall pink wading bird that eats with its head held upside down in water too salty for almost anything else."},{"t":"fact","text":"It feeds with its head upside down. The bill is a strainer. The fat tongue pumps like a piston, and comb-like ridges called lamellae hold back the shrimp and algae. In almost every animal, the lower jaw moves, but a flamingo's upper jaw swings instead.","label":"The upside-down eater"}],"img":"flamingo"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the Toco toucan, a crow-sized South American fruit-eater with a huge orange bill that takes up a third of its whole length."},{"t":"fact","text":"The bill is about 8 inches (20 cm) long and weighs almost nothing. It's mostly air pockets inside a thin shell of keratin, like your fingernails.","label":"The light yet strong bill"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is the main material inside the toucan's bill?","options":["Air pockets","Fat","Muscle"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the box. It says what fills the inside of the bill.","explain":"The bill is mostly made of air pockets inside a thin shell of keratin, which keeps it light and strong."}}],"img":"toucan"},{"icon":"📊","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The Indian peafowl is the longest at 7.2 ft, while the African gray parrot is the shortest at 1.1 ft."},{"t":"fact","text":"The Indian peafowl weighs about 10 lb, the greater flamingo about 7 lb and the toco toucan about 1 lb.","label":"Weight"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which of the five is the longest?","options":["The Toco toucan","The African gray parrot","The Indian peafowl"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the first sentence. It gives the length of the Indian peafowl and the African gray parrot.","explain":"The Indian peafowl, at 7.2 ft, is the longest among them."}}]},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"An African gray parrot that talks. A superb lyrebird that mimics. An Indian peafowl with a long train. A greater flamingo that eats upside down. A toco toucan with a big beak."},{"t":"p","text":"Five birds, each with their own unique way of living and thriving."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["African gray parrot","Learned over a hundred English words"],["Superb lyrebird","Copies sounds from other birds"],["Indian peafowl","Shimmering eyespots in its tail feathers"],["Greater flamingo","Feeds with its head upside down"],["Toco toucan","Has a bill that is mostly air pockets"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did Alex figure out on his own?","options":["He counted to ten.","He used the word \"none\" for zero.","He sang songs."],"answer":1,"explain":"Alex showed he could think for himself."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How does a male lyrebird mostly communicate?","options":["By tapping its beak on tree trunks.","By copying other birds' sounds.","By clapping its wings loudly."],"answer":1,"explain":"The lyrebird is a great mimic of bird sounds."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What creates the colorful shimmer in peacock feathers?","options":["A coat of bright dye.","The shape of each feather.","The food that the bird eats."],"answer":1,"explain":"There is no dye. The color is built into the shape of each feather."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How does a flamingo eat its food?","options":["By diving far down under the water.","With its head upside down in water.","By spearing fish with its sharp beak."],"answer":1,"explain":"Flamingos have a unique way of feeding."}]},{"id":"animals-birds-5","course":"animals","kind":"species","group":"birds","title":"Pileated woodpecker & more","names":["Pileated woodpecker","Sociable weaver","Satin bowerbird","Magnificent frigatebird","Brown pelican","Atlantic puffin"],"glyph":"🦅","tint":"#4fd6e8","items":["woodpecker","sociable-weaver","bowerbird","frigatebird","pelican","puffin"],"idea":"A puffin can hold over a dozen fish in its bill. Find out who builds a grass nest shared by over a hundred families, who collects blue treasures, and who dives from 70 ft.","pages":[{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Six birds are waiting to meet you: a pileated woodpecker, a sociable weaver, a satin bowerbird, a magnificent frigatebird, a brown pelican, and an Atlantic puffin."},{"t":"p","text":"Each one does something surprising."}]},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the pileated woodpecker, a crow-sized black bird with a flaming red crest. It chisels rectangular holes into dead trees to hunt ants."},{"t":"fact","text":"This woodpecker gouges rectangles deep enough to snap a small tree in half. The rectangular hole is its signature, so you can spot it without ever seeing the bird.","label":"The miner"},{"t":"p","text":"Its tongue is too long to fit inside its head the ordinary way. The bones that hold it curl up around the back of the skull, so the tongue can shoot far out and pull ants from their tunnels."}],"img":"woodpecker"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Next, meet the sociable weaver, a small brown bird about the size of a sparrow, from the dry plains of southern Africa. It builds the largest nest of any bird on earth — a giant grass haystack shared by over a hundred families."},{"t":"fact","text":"The sociable weaver's nest can grow so heavy that it tears branches loose or brings down the whole tree.","label":"The nest"},{"t":"p","text":"Each pair gets a private room with its own entrance tunnel. Sharp straw spikes around each doorway keep snakes out."}],"img":"sociable-weaver"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the satin bowerbird, an Australian forest bird known for its decorated stage of sticks."},{"t":"fact","text":"The male collects blue objects — feathers, flowers, bottle caps, and more — to decorate his stage. He spends his life gathering these blue treasures.","label":"The blue collection"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does the male satin bowerbird collect to decorate his stage?","options":["Blue objects","Smooth white stones","Live beetles"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the box about the blue collection. It says what the male gathers.","explain":"The male satin bowerbird collects blue objects, like feathers, flowers, and bottle caps."}}],"img":"bowerbird"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Next, meet the magnificent frigatebird, a huge black seabird with an eight-foot wingspan and a scarlet throat balloon."},{"t":"term","word":"wingspan","text":"The distance from one wing tip to the other when the wings are stretched out."},{"t":"fact","text":"The wings span up to 8 ft (2.44 m) — wider than a doorway is tall. The whole bird weighs about 3 lb (1.4 kg), roughly the weight of a big bottle of water. Enormous wing, almost no body.","label":"The weight-to-wing ratio"}],"img":"frigatebird"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Next up, the brown pelican. It’s a big gray-brown bird that hunts by folding its wings and smashing into the ocean from high above."},{"t":"fact","text":"From 60-70 ft (18-21 m) high, it dives bill-first, hitting the water hard enough to stun fish just below the surface.","label":"The dive"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which part of the pelican hits the water first when it dives?","options":["Its wings","Its bill","Its feet"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It talks about how the pelican dives with its bill.","explain":"It dives bill-first, hitting the water hard enough to stun fish just below the surface."}}],"img":"pelican"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Next up is the Atlantic puffin, a small black-and-white seabird with a colorful striped bill."},{"t":"fact","text":"A puffin can hold more than a dozen small fish crosswise in its bill at once. It uses backward-pointing spines on its tongue and roof of its mouth to keep the fish from falling out while it catches more.","label":"The fish trick"}],"img":"puffin"},{"icon":"📊","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Measured from bill to tail, the brown pelican is the longest at 4.1 ft, and the sociable weaver is the shortest at just 0.5 ft."},{"t":"fact","text":"The Atlantic puffin can fly up to 50 mph, beating its wings about 400 times a minute.","label":"Speed"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which of the six is the longest, measured from bill to tail?","options":["The magnificent frigatebird","The pileated woodpecker","The Atlantic puffin","The brown pelican"],"answer":3,"hint":"Look at the first sentence and find the largest number.","explain":"The Brown pelican, at 4.1 ft, is the longest of the six birds."}}]},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A pileated woodpecker that drills into trees. A sociable weaver that builds nests like apartment blocks. A magnificent frigatebird with an eight-foot wingspan. A brown pelican diving for fish. An Atlantic puffin that flies up to 50 mph."},{"t":"p","text":"Six birds, each with its own way of life."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Pileated woodpecker","Drills rectangular holes in trees"],["Sociable weaver","Builds giant communal nests"],["Satin bowerbird","Collects blue objects for decoration"],["Magnificent frigatebird","Has an 8 ft wingspan"],["Brown pelican","Dives from 60-70 ft to catch fish"],["Atlantic puffin","Can hold over a dozen fish at once"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does the pileated woodpecker use to pull ants out of the wood?","options":["Its claws","Its tongue","Its beak"],"answer":1,"explain":"The pileated woodpecker has a super long tongue that helps it get ants."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How does the sociable weaver keep snakes out of its nest?","options":["Thick clay walls","Sharp straw spikes","A hidden trap door"],"answer":1,"explain":"The sociable weaver uses sharp straw spikes to keep snakes away from its nest."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What special feature does the magnificent frigatebird have?","options":["A bright blue tail","A scarlet throat balloon","Feathers of different colors"],"answer":1,"explain":"The magnificent frigatebird has a bright red throat that it puffs up like a balloon."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How high does the brown pelican dive from?","options":["30 ft","60-70 ft","100 ft"],"answer":1,"explain":"The brown pelican dives from very high up to catch fish in the water."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What helps the Atlantic puffin hold more than a dozen small fish in its bill?","options":["Sticky glue all over its tongue and bill","Backward-pointing spines in its mouth","Two big stretchy pouches under its bill"],"answer":1,"explain":"The Atlantic puffin has special backward-pointing spines that help it hold lots of fish."}]},{"id":"animals-reptiles-1","course":"animals","kind":"species","group":"reptiles","title":"King cobra & more","names":["King cobra","Reticulated python","Gaboon viper","Western diamondback rattlesnake","Komodo dragon"],"glyph":"🦎","tint":"#9ee85f","items":["king-cobra","reticulated-python","gaboon-viper","rattlesnake","komodo-dragon"],"idea":"A king cobra eats other snakes. A python is longer than a classroom. A Komodo dragon can eat 80 percent of its own weight in one meal. Meet five giant reptiles.","pages":[{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Five giant reptiles are waiting for you: a king cobra, a reticulated python, a gaboon viper, a western diamondback rattlesnake and a Komodo dragon."},{"t":"p","text":"Each one has its own special trick. Which one would you most like to meet first?"}]},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the king cobra, the longest venomous snake in the world. This tall, sharp-eyed forest snake hunts and eats other snakes."},{"t":"fact","text":"Most adults run 10 to 13 feet (3.2 to 4 m) long, and the record reached 19 feet (5.85 m). Its scientific name, Ophiophagus, means snake-eater.","label":"Snake-eater"},{"t":"p","text":"It is the only snake in the world that builds a nest. The mother rakes dead leaves into a mound with her own coils and lays her eggs inside."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does a mother king cobra build?","options":["A burrow in the sand","A nest of dead leaves","A web of silk"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the last paragraph. It says what the mother builds for her eggs.","explain":"The king cobra is the only snake that builds a nest. She rakes dead leaves into a mound and lays her eggs inside."}}],"img":"king-cobra"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the reticulated python, the longest snake in the world. This heavy, net-patterned constrictor lives in the forests and rivers of Southeast Asia."},{"t":"fact","text":"A wild one caught in Borneo and measured with a tape came to 22 feet 10 inches (6.95 m), long enough to stretch across a classroom.","label":"The longest"},{"t":"term","word":"reticulated","text":"Marked with a pattern like a net."},{"t":"p","text":"A python does not crush bones. It squeezes hard enough to stop the blood moving, and its prey goes unconscious in seconds. Rows of pits along its lips sense heat, so it can find a warm rat in the dark."}],"img":"reticulated-python"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the gaboon viper. This huge, heavy African viper lies motionless in dead leaves, wearing the best camouflage of any snake in the world."},{"t":"fact","text":"It has the longest fangs of any snake alive, up to 2 inches (5 cm). They lie flat against the roof of the mouth and swing forward when it strikes.","label":"The fangs"},{"t":"p","text":"Lying in leaf litter, it is hardly there at all. Its pale and dark pattern matches dead leaves so well that people walk over one without seeing it."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How long can a gaboon viper's fangs be?","options":["About a quarter inch long","Longer than a pencil","Up to 2 inches (5 cm)"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the box. It gives the length of the longest fangs.","explain":"The gaboon viper has the longest fangs of any snake alive, up to 2 inches (5 cm)."}}],"img":"gaboon-viper"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the western diamondback rattlesnake. This thick-bodied American desert snake has diamonds down its back and a warning rattle on its tail."},{"t":"fact","text":"Every time it sheds its skin, it adds a new segment to the rattle. But you cannot count the segments to find its age. That part is a myth.","label":"The rattle"},{"t":"p","text":"A small pit between each eye and nostril is a heat detector. It can register a temperature change of three thousandths of a degree."},{"t":"p","text":"The young are born alive, about a dozen at a time, each up to a foot (30 cm) long and already venomous."}],"img":"rattlesnake"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the Komodo dragon, the biggest, heaviest lizard alive. This slow-walking hunter lives on a handful of Indonesian islands and brings down animals as large as deer."},{"t":"fact","text":"The largest one ever properly measured stretched 10 feet 3 inches (3.13 m) and weighed 366 pounds (166 kg).","label":"The giant"},{"t":"p","text":"It smells with its tongue. The yellow fork flicks out, catches scent from the air, and wipes it onto the roof of its mouth. One meal can be 80 percent of its own body weight."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does a Komodo dragon smell with?","options":["Its short snout","Its forked tongue","Its large ears"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the last paragraph. It says what flicks out to catch scent.","explain":"A Komodo dragon smells with its forked tongue, which catches scent from the air."}}],"img":"komodo-dragon"},{"icon":"📊","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Five reptiles, from the longest venomous snake to the heaviest lizard. The king cobra eats other snakes. The python is the longest snake. The gaboon viper hides in leaves. The rattlesnake warns. The Komodo dragon smells with its tongue."},{"t":"fact","text":"A king cobra can reach 19 feet (5.85 m). A reticulated python reached 22 feet 10 inches (6.95 m). A gaboon viper is about 4.6 ft (1.4 m) long. A Komodo dragon reached 10 feet 3 inches (3.13 m).","label":"Sizes"}]},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A king cobra that eats snakes. A python longer than a classroom. A viper lying in leaves. A rattlesnake with its warning rattle. A Komodo dragon with a forked tongue."},{"t":"p","text":"Five giant reptiles, each with its own special trick."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["King cobra","Longest venomous snake, eats other snakes"],["Reticulated python","Longest snake in the world"],["Gaboon viper","Longest fangs, hides in dead leaves"],["Western diamondback rattlesnake","Desert snake with a warning rattle"],["Komodo dragon","Heaviest lizard alive"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does the name Ophiophagus mean?","options":["Tall hood","Forest king","Snake-eater"],"answer":2,"explain":"The king cobra's scientific name means snake-eater, and that is exactly its job."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How does a python kill its prey?","options":["It crushes every bone in the body","It bites with a strong, deadly venom","It squeezes until the blood stops moving"],"answer":2,"explain":"A python does not crush bones. It squeezes hard enough to stop the blood moving."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How does a gaboon viper hide?","options":["It buries itself deep in the sand","Its pattern matches dead leaves","It climbs high up into the trees"],"answer":1,"explain":"The pale and dark pattern matches dead leaves so well that people walk over one without seeing it."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Can you tell a rattlesnake's age by counting its rattle segments?","options":["Yes, one segment for each year","No, that is a myth","Yes, one segment for each month"],"answer":1,"explain":"A snake may shed several times in a good year, so the number of segments does not give its age."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How much of its own weight can one Komodo dragon meal be?","options":["About 8 percent","About 20 percent","About 80 percent"],"answer":2,"explain":"One meal can be 80 percent of its own body weight."}]},{"id":"animals-reptiles-2","course":"animals","kind":"species","group":"reptiles","title":"Veiled chameleon & more","names":["Veiled chameleon","Thorny devil","Tokay gecko","Common basilisk","Frilled-neck lizard"],"glyph":"🦎","tint":"#9ee85f","items":["veiled-chameleon","thorny-devil","gecko","basilisk-lizard","frilled-lizard"],"idea":"A frilled-neck lizard opens a frill wider than a dinner plate. Find out which lizard drinks with its skin, which one sticks to glass, and which one runs across water.","pages":[{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Five reptiles are waiting to meet you: a veiled chameleon, a thorny devil, a tokay gecko, a common basilisk, and a frilled-neck lizard."},{"t":"p","text":"Each one has something special that makes it stand out."}]},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the veiled chameleon. It’s a tall-helmeted tree lizard from Yemen’s mountains. Its eyes swivel independently, and its tongue fires like a spear."},{"t":"p","text":"The casque, the tall helmet on its head, is about 2 inches (5 cm) tall. It may help catch night fog, which drips down to the chameleon's mouth. It also helps the chameleon show off to rivals."},{"t":"fact","text":"The tongue tip is coated in mucus 400 times thicker than your spit. It grabs prey and pulls it back.","label":"The sticky tongue"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does the veiled chameleon use its casque for?","options":["Catching night fog and showing off","Hearing far sounds and staying warm","Digging burrows and holding food"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the second paragraph. It mentions two jobs the casque does.","explain":"The veiled chameleon uses its casque to catch night fog and for displays."}}],"img":"veiled-chameleon"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Next up is the thorny devil. It’s a small, spiky lizard from Australia’s desert that eats ants."},{"t":"p","text":"This lizard drinks by pulling water across its skin. Microscopic grooves between its scales draw water to its mouth."},{"t":"term","word":"capillary action","text":"The way water moves up a narrow tube without needing to be pushed."},{"t":"fact","text":"Stand the thorny devil in a shallow puddle and the water climbs its legs, crosses its back, and reaches its mouth.","label":"Drinking with its feet"}],"img":"thorny-devil"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Next up is the tokay gecko — a blue-and-orange lizard from Southeast Asia. It walks up glass and across ceilings."},{"t":"fact","text":"Each foot has half a million tiny hairs called setae, and each hair splits into hundreds of even tinier pads. These pads use a weak pull between molecules, called van der Waals attraction, to stick to surfaces.","label":"The secret grip"}],"img":"gecko"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Next up is the common basilisk, a crested lizard from Central American riverbanks. It escapes danger by sprinting across water on its back legs."},{"t":"fact","text":"It runs up on its back legs, sprinting across the surface of a river. People call it the Jesus lizard because of this ability. A basilisk can cross about 15 feet (4.5 m) of open water before sinking in and swimming the rest.","label":"The Jesus lizard"},{"t":"p","text":"Each footfall on water is three moves: slap, stroke, recover. The slap traps air, the stroke pushes against that pocket, and then it lifts clear."}],"img":"basilisk-lizard"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the frilled-neck lizard, a gray tree dweller from Australia. Its neck has a huge collar of skin that can suddenly snap open."},{"t":"fact","text":"Corner it and the show starts. The collar of skin opens wider than a dinner plate — over 12 in (30 cm) across — while its mouth gapes bright pink and it hisses.","label":"The surprise"},{"t":"p","text":"The frill is not a weapon. It makes the lizard look much bigger to scare off predators."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why does the frilled-neck lizard open its frill?","options":["To trap insects for food","To look bigger and scare away threats","To hide in trees more effectively"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph that starts with \"The frill is not a weapon.\"","explain":"The frill opens wide to make the lizard look bigger and scare away any threats."}}],"img":"frilled-lizard"},{"icon":"📊","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Now put the five side by side. The frilled-neck lizard is about 3 ft long and weighs about 1 lb. The veiled chameleon is about 2 ft long and the common basilisk about 2.5 ft. The thorny devil is just 0.7 ft long."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which of the five is the longest?","options":["The common basilisk","The veiled chameleon","The frilled-neck lizard"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the first paragraph and find the biggest length.","explain":"The frilled-neck lizard, at about 3 ft long."}}]},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A frilled-neck lizard with a neck frill that opens wide. A thorny devil with spikes all over. A tokay gecko sticking to walls. A common basilisk running on water. A veiled chameleon with a helmet that catches fog."},{"t":"p","text":"Five reptiles, each one unique in its own way."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Veiled chameleon","Catching night fog with its casque"],["Thorny devil","Drinks water using microscopic grooves"],["Tokay gecko","Walks up glass with tiny hairs"],["Common basilisk","Sprints across water on back legs"],["Frilled-neck lizard","Opens a frill to look bigger"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What special ability does the thorny devil have?","options":["It can store water in a large hump on its back.","It drinks water through capillary action on its skin.","It can dig deep tunnels to find water far below."],"answer":1,"explain":"The thorny devil uses microscopic grooves on its scales to pull water to its mouth."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How does the tokay gecko stick to surfaces?","options":["With a layer of sticky glue on its toes.","Each foot has half a million tiny hairs.","By using its tail to hold on."],"answer":1,"explain":"These hairs use van der Waals attraction to help the gecko stick to any surface."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is unique about the common basilisk's movement?","options":["It can burrow underground in just minutes.","It can run across water on its back legs.","It can leap very far from tree to tree."],"answer":1,"explain":"The basilisk uses a special three-move sequence to stay on top of the water, earning it the nickname \"Jesus lizard.\""},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is the main purpose of the frilled-neck lizard's frill?","options":["To catch insects by spreading out like a net.","To scare away predators by looking bigger.","To hide in the trees by looking like bark."],"answer":1,"explain":"The frill opens up wide to intimidate any threats and make the lizard appear larger."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How does the veiled chameleon use its tongue to catch food?","options":["It spits a thick web that wraps up its prey.","Its sticky tongue shoots out and pulls prey back.","It pokes prey with a sharp spike on its tongue."],"answer":1,"explain":"The veiled chameleon's tongue is coated in thick mucus, helping it grab prey and pull it back."}]},{"id":"animals-reptiles-3","course":"animals","kind":"species","group":"reptiles","title":"Galápagos tortoise & more","names":["Galápagos tortoise","Leatherback sea turtle","Saltwater crocodile","American alligator","Texas horned lizard"],"glyph":"🦎","tint":"#9ee85f","items":["galapagos-tortoise","leatherback-turtle","saltwater-crocodile","alligator","horned-lizard"],"idea":"A Texas horned lizard can shoot blood out of its eyes. Find out which reptile can go up to a year without food or water.","pages":[{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Five reptiles are waiting to meet you: a Galápagos tortoise, a leatherback sea turtle, a saltwater crocodile, an American alligator, and a Texas horned lizard."},{"t":"p","text":"Each one is special in its own way."}]},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the Galápagos tortoise, the largest tortoise on Earth. It lives on volcanic islands in the Pacific Ocean."},{"t":"fact","text":"The heaviest one ever weighed came in at 919 lb (417 kg) — heavier than five grown men put together.","label":"The giant"},{"t":"p","text":"These tortoises are built to last. They can go more than six months without a drink and up to a whole year with no food and no water at all."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is the longest a Galápagos tortoise can go with no food and no water at all?","options":["3 months","6 months","1 year"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the last sentence. It gives the exact time.","explain":"A Galápagos tortoise can go up to a whole year with no food and no water."}}],"img":"galapagos-tortoise"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the leatherback sea turtle, the biggest sea turtle in the world. Its back is thick rubbery skin over tiny bones called osteoderms."},{"t":"term","word":"osteoderms","text":"Tiny bones under the skin that make it flexible and strong."},{"t":"fact","text":"One leatherback was recorded diving to 4,200 ft (1,280 m). That is more than three Empire State Buildings stacked end to end.","label":"The deep diver"}],"img":"leatherback-turtle"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the saltwater crocodile, the largest living reptile. It lives in rivers and the sea."},{"t":"fact","text":"A 15.1 ft (4.6 m) crocodile bit down with about 3,700 pounds of force. No living animal has a stronger bite.","label":"The big bite"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is the saltwater crocodile's bite force?","options":["2,000 pounds","3,700 pounds","5,000 pounds"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box that talks about the big bite.","explain":"The saltwater crocodile can bite with 3,700 pounds of force."}}],"img":"saltwater-crocodile"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the American alligator, a big reptile that lives in swamps and rivers. It has a broad snout and rules the southeastern United States."},{"t":"fact","text":"A large alligator can bite with about 3,000 pounds of force. That is a little less than the saltwater crocodile.","label":"The strong bite"},{"t":"p","text":"The muscles that close its jaws are huge, but the ones that open them are weak. Because those opening muscles are so weak, a few wraps of tape are enough to hold an alligator's mouth shut."}],"img":"alligator"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the Texas horned lizard, a flat, spiky little desert lizard that lives almost entirely on stinging ants."},{"t":"fact","text":"Push it too far and it shoots blood out of its own eyes up to 5 feet (1.5 m). It aims for the face to make predators back off.","label":"The blood cannon"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does the Texas horned lizard shoot out of its eyes?","options":["Blood","Water","Ants"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It says what comes out of its eyes.","explain":"Blood. It shoots blood from its eyes to scare away predators."}}],"img":"horned-lizard"},{"icon":"📊","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The saltwater crocodile is the longest at 14.8 ft. The American alligator is next at about 11 ft and the leatherback sea turtle is about 6.6 ft. The Texas horned lizard is the shortest at 0.4 ft."},{"t":"fact","text":"The leatherback sea turtle can swim up to 22 mph. The saltwater crocodile runs up to 15 mph on land.","label":"Speed"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which of the five is the longest?","options":["The saltwater crocodile","The leatherback sea turtle","The American alligator"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the first paragraph. It gives the lengths.","explain":"The saltwater crocodile, at 14.8 ft long."}}]},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A Galápagos tortoise that can go a whole year without food or water. A leatherback sea turtle swimming up to 22 mph. A saltwater crocodile that grows up to 14.8 ft long. An American alligator with powerful jaws. A Texas horned lizard that is only 0.4 ft long."},{"t":"p","text":"Five reptiles, each built differently for their own world."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Galápagos tortoise","Survives a year without food or water"],["Leatherback sea turtle","Dives deeper than three Empire State Buildings"],["Saltwater crocodile","Strongest bite at 3,700 pounds"],["American alligator","Weak muscles to open its jaws"],["Texas horned lizard","Shoots blood from its eyes"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"How deep can a leatherback sea turtle dive?","options":["2,000 ft","4,200 ft","3,000 ft"],"answer":1,"explain":"A leatherback has been recorded diving to 4,200 ft, more than three Empire State Buildings stacked end to end."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What can a Galápagos tortoise do for up to a year?","options":["Stay awake without sleeping","Go without food and water","Swim across the ocean without stopping"],"answer":1,"explain":"This tortoise is built to last, even in tough conditions."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How fast can the leatherback sea turtle swim?","options":["10 mph","22 mph","15 mph"],"answer":1,"explain":"The leatherback sea turtle is a speedy swimmer."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What do Texas horned lizards eat mostly?","options":["Snails and slugs","Stinging ants","Leaves and seeds"],"answer":1,"explain":"These lizards have quite the appetite for stinging ants."}]},{"id":"animals-amphibians-1","course":"animals","kind":"species","group":"amphibians","title":"Axolotl, Glass frog & more","names":["Axolotl","Glass frog","Golden poison frog","Goliath frog","Wood frog"],"glyph":"🐸","tint":"#7fe3a0","items":["axolotl","glass-frog","poison-dart-frog","goliath-frog","wood-frog"],"idea":"A wood frog freezes solid and comes back to life. Find out which amphibian regrows its limbs, which one you can see through, and which one carries enough poison for ten adults.","pages":[{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Five amphibians are waiting to meet you: an axolotl, a glass frog, a golden poison frog, a goliath frog, and a wood frog."},{"t":"p","text":"Each one is special in its own way."}]},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the axolotl, a pink Mexican salamander with feathery gills sticking out of its head. It lives in water and can regrow almost any part of itself."},{"t":"fact","text":"Those pink feathers on its head are actually gills that pull oxygen directly from the water.","label":"The gills"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What do the axolotl's pink gills do?","options":["Sense food floating nearby","Pull oxygen from the water","Store fat for the cold months"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. It says what the pink feathers on its head are.","explain":"The pink gills on an axolotl's head pull oxygen directly from the water."}}],"img":"axolotl"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the glass frog, a small green rainforest frog with see-through skin on its underside. Turn one over and you can watch its beating heart."},{"t":"fact","text":"The underside of a glass frog is translucent. You can see its heart beating, liver sitting there, and food moving through its gut.","label":"The see-through skin"},{"t":"term","word":"translucent","text":"See-through but not clear. Light passes through, but you can't see everything clearly."},{"t":"p","text":"Asleep, the glass frog pulls almost all its red blood cells out of circulation and parks them inside its liver. This makes it much harder to see."}],"img":"glass-frog"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the golden poison frog, about as long as your thumb and colored like a highlighter pen. Its skin holds enough poison to kill about ten grown adults."},{"t":"fact","text":"The frog's skin is packed with poison that it gets from what it eats. In the wild, its diet of tiny insects and mites gives it this deadly protection. Zoo-bred frogs are harmless because they don't eat those insects.","label":"The bright warning"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How does the golden poison frog get its poison?","options":["It makes it in its body","It takes it from the plants it climbs on","It collects it from its diet"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the first sentence of the box. It says where the poison comes from.","explain":"The golden poison frog gets its poison from its diet of tiny insects and mites in the wild."}}],"img":"poison-dart-frog"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the Goliath frog, the largest frog on Earth. It's longer than a dinner plate is wide and heavy enough that you would want both hands to lift it."},{"t":"fact","text":"For all its bulk, this frog is a serious jumper. One leap covers somewhere between 4 and 10 feet (1.2 to 3 m), from a standing start with no run-up at all.","label":"The giant leap"},{"t":"p","text":"A big Goliath frog measures about 13 inches (32 cm) just in body length. Stretch its legs out, and the whole animal passes 30 inches (80 cm). The heaviest one ever properly weighed came in at 7.3 lb (3.3 kg)."}],"img":"goliath-frog"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the wood frog. It’s a small brown forest frog with a dark mask across its eyes."},{"t":"p","text":"In winter this frog freezes solid. Ice fills the spaces around its organs, its heart stops, and its brain goes quiet. Warm it up and within hours it comes back to life."},{"t":"fact","text":"Before freezing, the wood frog’s liver floods its body with glucose — sugar. This works as antifreeze inside every cell, so ice can only form around cells, not in them.","label":"The secret to surviving"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What protects a wood frog's cells while it is frozen?","options":["Thick layers of leaf litter","A coat of mucus that traps heat","Sugar that works as antifreeze"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the box. It says what the liver does before the freeze.","explain":"Before freezing, the liver floods its body with sugar, which works as antifreeze inside every cell."}}],"img":"wood-frog"},{"icon":"📊","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The Goliath frog is the heaviest at 7 lb and the longest at about 1 ft. The axolotl is about 0.8 ft long. The glass frog is the smallest, only 0.1 ft long."},{"t":"fact","text":"A Goliath frog can leap 4 to 10 feet from a standing start. At the top of that range it is ten times its own 1-ft body length.","label":"Jumping"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which of the five is the longest?","options":["The axolotl, at 0.8 ft","The glass frog, at 0.1 ft","The Goliath frog, at 1 ft"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the first paragraph and find the longest length.","explain":"The Goliath frog, at 1 ft long."}}]},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"An axolotl that regrows its limbs. A glass frog you can see through. A golden poison frog as bright as a warning. A Goliath frog big enough to hold. A wood frog frozen solid in winter."},{"t":"p","text":"Five amphibians, each one built to survive in its own way."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Axolotl","Can regrow almost any part of itself"],["Glass frog","See-through skin reveals its beating heart"],["Golden poison frog","Colored like a highlighter pen and deadly"],["Goliath frog","Largest frog on Earth, jumps up to 10 feet"],["Wood frog","Freezes solid in winter, then thaws in spring"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What can the wood frog do in winter?","options":["Swim under the pond ice","Freeze solid","Migrate to warmer areas"],"answer":1,"explain":"The wood frog has a unique way of surviving the cold."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where does the axolotl live?","options":["In a desert","In water","On a tree"],"answer":1,"explain":"The axolotl is a water-dwelling salamander."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What makes the golden poison frog deadly?","options":["Its bright color","Its poisonous skin","Its loud croak"],"answer":1,"explain":"The golden poison frog's skin holds enough poison to be dangerous."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How far can a Goliath frog jump?","options":["Only about 1 to 3 feet","Between 4 and 10 feet","Between 12 and 16 feet"],"answer":1,"explain":"Despite its size, the Goliath frog is quite a jumper."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What can you see through a glass frog's underside?","options":["Its brain","Its beating heart","Its tail"],"answer":1,"explain":"The glass frog's skin is translucent, allowing you to see inside it."}]},{"id":"animals-amphibians-2","course":"animals","kind":"species","group":"amphibians","title":"Surinam toad & more","names":["Surinam toad","Red-eyed tree frog","Hellbender","Olm"],"glyph":"🐸","tint":"#7fe3a0","items":["surinam-toad","red-eyed-tree-frog","hellbender","olm"],"idea":"A Surinam toad carries its babies in pockets on its back. Find out which amphibian flashes red eyes, which breathes through its skin, and which can go ten years without eating.","pages":[{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Four amphibians are waiting to meet you: a Surinam toad, a red-eyed tree frog, a hellbender, and an olm."},{"t":"p","text":"Each one lives in a different world."}]},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the Surinam toad. It looks exactly like a dead leaf and lives on the bottom of muddy South American ponds."},{"t":"fact","text":"The babies grow inside the mother's back. After mating, her skin swells up over the fertilized eggs and seals each one into its own pocket with a lid on top.","label":"The nursery"},{"t":"p","text":"For three to five months, she carries them, her back a honeycomb of bumps. Then the lids open and tiny toads push their way out."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How are the babies protected while growing?","options":["Inside pouches in the mud","Inside pockets with lids on top","Inside the mother's stomach"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box about the nursery. It says what seals each egg into its pocket.","explain":"The pockets with lids on top protect the babies as they grow inside the mother's back."}}],"img":"surinam-toad"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the red-eyed tree frog. It's a small neon-green rainforest frog with big red eyes, orange feet, and blue-and-yellow striped sides."},{"t":"fact","text":"When it's asleep, it looks like a green leaf. But touch it, and its eyes snap open — two red discs — while orange feet and blue-and-yellow flanks flash out. Biologists call this a deimatic display: a sudden burst of color meant to startle.","label":"The flash of color"},{"t":"term","word":"deimatic","text":"A sudden burst of bright color to startle predators."},{"t":"p","text":"The frog uses this quick flash to surprise any attacker, giving it the split-second it needs to escape."}],"img":"red-eyed-tree-frog"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the hellbender. It's a giant, flat-headed, wrinkled salamander that spends its whole life under rocks in cold, fast rivers."},{"t":"fact","text":"A typical hellbender is about 2 feet (60 cm) long. The largest ever measured was 29 inches (74 cm). It breathes through its skin and rarely uses its lungs.","label":"The skin breather"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How does a hellbender breathe?","options":["Through its skin","Through its gills","By using its lungs"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the box. It says how a hellbender gets its oxygen.","explain":"A hellbender breathes through its skin and rarely uses its lungs."}}],"img":"hellbender"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Next up is the olm, a pale, blind salamander that lives in the dark limestone caves of southeastern Europe."},{"t":"fact","text":"An olm can go ten years without eating — about as long as you have been alive. Food is scarce in its cave home, so it slows down and survives on fat and sugar stored in its liver.","label":"The long wait"}],"img":"olm"},{"icon":"📊","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The hellbender is the longest at 2 ft. The olm is about 1 ft and the Surinam toad about 0.6 ft. The red-eyed tree frog is the smallest at just 0.2 ft."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which of the four is the longest?","options":["The Surinam toad","The red-eyed tree frog","The hellbender"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the first paragraph and find the biggest number.","explain":"The hellbender, at 2 ft."}}]},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A hellbender that grows as long as 2 ft. A red-eyed tree frog so tiny at just 0.2 ft. A Surinam toad with eggs embedded in its skin. An olm that lives in caves and never sees the light."},{"t":"p","text":"Four amphibians, each with their own unique way of life."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Surinam toad","Babies grow in pockets on its back"],["Red-eyed tree frog","Uses sudden color flash to escape"],["Hellbender","Breathes through its skin"],["Olm","Can go ten years without eating"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does the red-eyed tree frog use to startle predators?","options":["Its green leaf-like appearance","A sudden flash of bright colors","Its loud croaking sound"],"answer":1,"explain":"The red-eyed tree frog uses its bright colors to surprise and escape from danger."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where does the Surinam toad carry its babies?","options":["In her mouth, behind a flap of skin","In a nest of leaves by the river","On her back in pockets with lids"],"answer":2,"explain":"The Surinam toad's back becomes a nursery for her young."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How long can an olm go without eating?","options":["1 year","10 years","5 years"],"answer":1,"explain":"Food is scarce in its cave, so an olm slows down and lives on fat and sugar stored in its liver."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What helps the hellbender breathe underwater?","options":["Its gills","Its skin","Its lungs"],"answer":1,"explain":"The hellbender's skin is specially adapted to absorb oxygen from water."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What happens when the Surinam toad's babies are ready to come out?","options":["They hatch from eggs left on the muddy ground","They wriggle out through their mother's mouth","They push through the lids on their mother's back"],"answer":2,"explain":"The baby toads emerge by popping open the lids on their mother's back."}]},{"id":"animals-fish-1","course":"animals","kind":"species","group":"fish","title":"Great white shark & more","names":["Great white shark","Whale shark","Great hammerhead","Greenland shark","Giant oceanic manta ray"],"glyph":"🐠","tint":"#6fb3ff","items":["great-white-shark","whale-shark","hammerhead-shark","greenland-shark","manta-ray"],"idea":"The Greenland shark can live for almost 400 years. Discover which fish has the largest brain and how the manta ray filters food.","pages":[{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Five big fish are waiting to meet you: a great white shark, a whale shark, a great hammerhead, a Greenland shark, and a giant oceanic manta ray."},{"t":"p","text":"Each one is built for a different life in the sea."}]},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the great white shark, a huge, warm-bodied hunter that patrols cool coastal seas and takes seals with one strike from below."},{"t":"fact","text":"A big female is about 15 ft (4.6 m) long. The longest one measured was 19.8 ft (6 m), and the heaviest weighed 5,580 lb (2,530 kg).","label":"The size"},{"t":"p","text":"It cruises at barely walking pace but can sprint at about 15 mph (24 km/h) for the final rush of an attack."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How fast does a great white shark sprint during its final attack?","options":["25 mph","15 mph","5 mph"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the last sentence. It gives the speed.","explain":"The great white shark sprints at about 15 mph for the final rush of an attack."}}],"img":"great-white-shark"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Next up is the biggest fish in the world, the whale shark. It swims with its mouth open, eating some of the smallest food in the sea."},{"t":"fact","text":"The largest one ever properly measured was 61.7 ft (18.8 m) from nose to tail.","label":"The length"},{"t":"term","word":"denticles","text":"Tiny tooth-shaped scales that cover parts of the shark's body, even its eyes."},{"t":"p","text":"Each eyeball has about 2,900 tiny tooth-shaped scales called denticles. These act like armor for the eye."}],"img":"whale-shark"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the great hammerhead shark, the largest of its kind. It has a head shaped like a blade and a dorsal fin taller than your arm."},{"t":"fact","text":"The biggest great hammerhead ever measured was 20 ft (6.1 m) long. A record holder weighed 1,280 lb (580 kg), but length is the fairer way to measure these sharks.","label":"The size"},{"t":"p","text":"Its head, called a cephalofoil, sweeps side to side like a metal detector. Hundreds of pores on the underside sense electricity from hidden prey."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does the great hammerhead use to find hidden prey?","options":["Pores that read electricity","Whiskers that feel the seafloor","Sound pulses that bounce back"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the last paragraph. It mentions pores that read electricity.","explain":"The great hammerhead uses pores on the underside of its head to sense the electricity of hidden prey."}}],"img":"hammerhead-shark"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the Greenland shark, a huge, slow, nearly blind shark that lives in the freezing Arctic deep."},{"t":"fact","text":"This is the longest-living animal with a backbone anyone has ever measured. The oldest one studied had been swimming for close to four hundred years.","label":"The record"},{"t":"p","text":"Most Greenland sharks swim with a worm-like parasite dangling off each eyeball, chewing at the surface and leaving them nearly blind."},{"t":"p","text":"Its flesh is poisonous when fresh. It is loaded with a chemical that leaves anyone who eats it staggering as though drunk. In Iceland, the meat is buried, fermented for weeks, and hung to dry for months until it's safe to eat — a dish called hákarl."}],"img":"greenland-shark"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Next up is the giant oceanic manta ray, the largest ray in the ocean. It can reach up to 23 ft (7 m) wingtip to wingtip."},{"t":"fact","text":"It has the largest brain of any fish, and a special net of blood vessels keeps it warm even in cold water.","label":"The brain"},{"t":"p","text":"When feeding, it swims with its mouth open, letting seawater pour through. Its filter plates catch plankton while the water goes right through. The plankton do not stick or clog the plates. They ricochet off them and are swept toward the throat."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How does the manta ray's filter system work?","options":["It sticks tiny plankton to its skin with a layer of slime.","It crushes plankton between its tiny teeth before swallowing.","Plankton ricochet off the filter plates and are captured."],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the second paragraph. It explains how the filter works.","explain":"Plankton ricochet off the filter plates and are captured, which keeps the filter from clogging."}}],"img":"manta-ray"},{"icon":"📊","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The whale shark weighs about 33,069 lb, making it the heaviest. The Greenland shark is the longest-lived, at close to 400 years."},{"t":"fact","text":"The great white shark can swim up to 15 mph, while the Greenland shark moves at only 0.8 mph.","label":"Speed"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which of the five is the longest-lived?","options":["The whale shark","The Greenland shark","The great white shark"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the first paragraph. It gives the number of years for the Greenland shark.","explain":"The Greenland shark lives close to 400 years, which is the longest."}}]},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A great white shark that sprints at 15 mph. A whale shark that filters plankton. A great hammerhead with its wide head. A Greenland shark that lives for centuries in the cold depths. A giant oceanic manta ray gliding through the water."},{"t":"p","text":"Five fish, each one unique, swimming in their own world."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Great white shark","Sprints at about 15 mph"],["Whale shark","Largest fish in the world"],["Great hammerhead","Uses pores to sense electricity"],["Greenland shark","Lives for almost 400 years"],["Giant oceanic manta ray","Has the largest brain of any fish"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"How long is the largest whale shark measured?","options":["40 ft (12 m)","61.7 ft (18.8 m)","50 ft (15 m)"],"answer":1,"explain":"The largest whale shark ever properly measured was 61.7 ft (18.8 m) from nose to tail."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does a great hammerhead use to sense hidden prey?","options":["Vibrations","Pores on its head","Smell"],"answer":1,"explain":"Pores on the underside of its head sense the electricity of hidden prey."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How does a great hammerhead sweep the seafloor for hidden prey?","options":["Straight ahead with its head held perfectly still","Its head sweeps side to side like a metal detector","In tight circles with its tail leading"],"answer":1,"explain":"The wide head sweeps side to side like a metal detector, and pores on its underside read the electricity of hidden prey."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does the giant oceanic manta ray eat?","options":["Fish","Plankton","Coral"],"answer":1,"explain":"Manta rays filter tiny plankton from the water."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How fast can a Greenland shark swim?","options":["5 mph (8 km/h)","0.8 mph (1.3 km/h)","2 mph (3.2 km/h)"],"answer":1,"explain":"The Greenland shark is one of the slowest swimmers in the ocean."}]},{"id":"animals-fish-2","course":"animals","kind":"species","group":"fish","title":"Electric eel, Anglerfish & more","names":["Electric eel","Anglerfish","Seahorse","Clownfish","Archerfish"],"glyph":"🐠","tint":"#6fb3ff","items":["electric-eel","anglerfish","seahorse","clownfish","archerfish"],"idea":"An electric eel can make a shock of 860 volts, about seven times a wall socket. Find out which fish dangles a glowing lure, which dad gives birth, and which one shoots down insects with water.","pages":[{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Five fish are waiting to meet you: an electric eel, an anglerfish, a seahorse, a clownfish, and an archerfish."},{"t":"p","text":"Each one is special in its own way."}]},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Start with the electric eel, a long river fish from South America. It can flood the water around it with the strongest electric shock any animal is known to make."},{"t":"fact","text":"A wall socket in an American house carries 120 volts. One kind of electric eel has been measured at 860 volts — about seven times that much. Most electric eels top out at about 600 volts, still five wall sockets' worth.","label":"The power surge"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many volts can the strongest electric eel produce?","options":["500 volts","860 volts","120 volts"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It lists the highest measured voltage.","explain":"The strongest electric eel can produce 860 volts."}}],"img":"electric-eel"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the anglerfish, a deep-sea fish that hunts in total darkness. It dangles a glowing lure in front of its own mouth and waits for something to come and look."},{"t":"fact","text":"The glowing bulb over an anglerfish's mouth hangs from a spine on its back called the illicium, and the lit bait is called the esca. In one species, the rod stretches nearly five times the length of the fish's own body.","label":"The glowing lure"},{"t":"term","word":"bioluminescent","text":"Living things that can make their own light."},{"t":"p","text":"The anglerfish does not make its own light. Billions of bioluminescent bacteria live inside the lure and glow on its behalf. The fish gives them a safe home and food in return."}],"img":"anglerfish"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Next up, the seahorse: a small upright fish with a horse-shaped head and a curling tail. It has bony armor instead of scales."},{"t":"fact","text":"The male gets pregnant. The female lays her eggs in a pouch on his belly. He fertilizes them. Then he carries them for 9 to 45 days before giving birth.","label":"The father carries them"},{"t":"p","text":"When the time comes, a single seahorse father can release between 100 and 1,000 babies."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many babies can a seahorse father release in a single birth?","options":["10 to 100","100 to 1,000","10,000 to 15,000"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the last sentence. It gives the number range.","explain":"A seahorse father can release between 100 and 1,000 babies in a single birth."}}],"img":"seahorse"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the clownfish: a small orange-and-white reef fish that lives inside a stinging sea anemone."},{"t":"fact","text":"The clownfish lives inside an animal that stings, but it wears a thick coat of mucus that does not trigger the anemone’s stinging cells. So the clownfish is safe and the anemone gets a defender.","label":"The fortress"},{"t":"p","text":"The clownfish drives off anemone-eating fish, clears away scraps, and fans fresh water through the tentacles all night. Both sides gain from this arrangement."}],"img":"clownfish"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the archerfish: a mangrove fish that shoots insects out of the air with a jet of water."},{"t":"fact","text":"It presses its tongue up against a groove in the roof of its mouth to make a barrel, then slams its gill covers shut to force water down the barrel. The jet is not a steady stream. The back of the jet travels faster than the front, so it bunches into one heavy blob. All the water lands at once, and it hits hard enough to knock the insect off its perch.","label":"The shot"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How does the archerfish ensure the water hits as one heavy blob?","options":["It spits out a steady stream of water.","The back of the jet travels faster than the front.","It squirts the water straight up into the air."],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. It says which part of the jet moves faster.","explain":"The archerfish makes the back of the jet travel faster than the front, so all the water lands at once and delivers a strong punch."}}],"img":"archerfish"},{"icon":"📊","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The electric eel weighs about 44 lb and is 6.6 ft long. The seahorse is just 0.5 ft long and lives for about 4 years. The clownfish is even shorter at 0.4 ft but can live up to 20 years."},{"t":"fact","text":"The electric eel is by far the longest of the five, at 6.6 ft. The archerfish is 0.7 ft, the seahorse 0.5 ft and the clownfish 0.4 ft.","label":"Length"}]},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"An electric eel that shocks. An anglerfish with a glowing lure. A seahorse that swims upright. A clownfish that hides in anemones. An archerfish that shoots water."},{"t":"p","text":"Five fish, each with their own special way of living."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Electric eel","Can produce 860 volts"],["Anglerfish","Has a glowing lure called the esca"],["Seahorse","Male carries and gives birth to babies"],["Clownfish","Lives inside a stinging anemone"],["Archerfish","Shoots insects with a jet of water"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does the anglerfish use to attract prey?","options":["its colorful scales","a glowing lure","its loud noises"],"answer":1,"explain":"The anglerfish uses light to catch its food in the dark ocean."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How long can seahorse babies stay inside the father's pouch?","options":["1 to 3 days","9 to 45 days","60 to 90 days"],"answer":1,"explain":"A seahorse father carries the eggs in his pouch for 9 to 45 days before giving birth."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does the clownfish live inside?","options":["a spiny black sea urchin","a stinging sea anemone","a hollow sea sponge"],"answer":1,"explain":"The clownfish and the anemone help each other out."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How does the archerfish shoot water?","options":["by squeezing water out of a pouch in its belly","by pressing its tongue against the roof of its mouth","by flicking its tail to splash the insect"],"answer":1,"explain":"The archerfish uses its mouth like a little gun to catch insects."}]},{"id":"animals-fish-3","course":"animals","kind":"species","group":"fish","title":"Pufferfish, Flying fish & more","names":["Pufferfish","Flying fish","Mudskipper","Sailfish","Ocean sunfish"],"glyph":"🐠","tint":"#6fb3ff","items":["pufferfish","flying-fish","mudskipper","sailfish","ocean-sunfish"],"idea":"A pufferfish can swell into a spiky ball. Find out which fish glides for 45 seconds, which one walks on mud, and which one carries 300 million eggs.","pages":[{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Five fish are waiting to meet you: a pufferfish, a flying fish, a mudskipper, a sailfish, and an ocean sunfish."},{"t":"p","text":"Each one does something different and surprising."}]},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the pufferfish, a slow and round fish with big eyes that swells into a spiky ball when threatened. It also carries one of the deadliest poisons in the ocean."},{"t":"fact","text":"Underwater, a puffer gulps seawater into its stomach, which stretches like a balloon until it's too wide for a predator to eat.","label":"Inflating defense"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does a pufferfish use to inflate itself underwater?","options":["Air","Freshwater","Seawater"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It mentions what the puffer gulps into its stomach.","explain":"The pufferfish uses seawater to inflate itself underwater."}}],"img":"pufferfish"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the flying fish, a slim silver ocean fish with pectoral fins as long as wings. It can launch clear of the sea and glide above the waves to escape predators."},{"t":"fact","text":"The longest flight ever filmed lasted 45 seconds. A TV crew off southern Japan recorded it. That fish stayed up longer than most people can hold their breath.","label":"The longest flight"},{"t":"term","word":"biplane","text":"A flying machine with two sets of wings, one on top of the other."},{"t":"p","text":"Some species have two pairs of wings — long pectoral fins in front and enlarged pelvic fins behind, like a fish-shaped biplane."}],"img":"flying-fish"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the mudskipper, a fish that walks on tropical mudflats. It props itself up on its front fins with eyes bulging off the top of its head."},{"t":"fact","text":"Its front fins are jointed and set far forward. It plants them and hauls itself along, a movement called crutching.","label":"How it moves"},{"t":"p","text":"A mudskipper breathes through its skin as long as it stays damp. Oxygen soaks in through the skin and the lining of its mouth and throat."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How does a mudskipper breathe?","options":["Through its skin and mouth lining","Through a blowhole on its head","Through its fins"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the second paragraph. It gives how the mudskipper breathes.","explain":"A mudskipper breathes through its skin and the lining of its mouth and throat, as long as it stays damp."}}],"img":"mudskipper"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Next up, the sailfish: a long blue hunter from the open ocean with a spear-like bill and a dorsal fin that opens like a sail."},{"t":"fact","text":"You might hear that sailfish hit 68 mph (110 km/h), but careful measurements show their top speed is nearer 20-35 mph (35-55 km/h). The higher number came from an old fishing test, not a lab.","label":"The real speed"},{"t":"p","text":"When hunting, the sailfish raises its dorsal fin to steady its head and keep the bill almost still as it closes in on a school of sardines."}],"img":"sailfish"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the ocean sunfish, a huge flat gray fish shaped like a head with two fins and no proper tail. It drifts through the open ocean eating almost anything small enough to swallow."},{"t":"fact","text":"One female was estimated to carry 300 million eggs at once. That's more than any other known backboned animal.","label":"The egg champion"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many eggs can one female ocean sunfish carry at once?","options":["30 million","300 million","3 billion"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It gives the exact number.","explain":"One female can carry 300 million eggs at once."}}],"img":"ocean-sunfish"},{"icon":"📊","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The ocean sunfish weighs about 2,205 lb and is 10.8 ft long, the longest of the five. The sailfish is 9.8 ft long. The pufferfish, flying fish and mudskipper all weigh less than 1 lb."},{"t":"fact","text":"The flying fish can glide at up to 43 mph, faster than the sailfish at 22 mph and the ocean sunfish at just 2 mph.","label":"Speed"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which of the five is the longest?","options":["The sailfish","The ocean sunfish","The pufferfish"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the first paragraph and find the longest length.","explain":"The ocean sunfish, at 10.8 ft, is the longest."}}]},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A flying fish that glides. An ocean sunfish that floats. A pufferfish that blows up. A sailfish with a high fin. A mudskipper that walks on land."},{"t":"p","text":"Five fish, each one different, and all so surprising."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Pufferfish","Swells into a spiky ball"],["Flying fish","Glides above waves for 45 seconds"],["Mudskipper","Walks on mudflats with crutching"],["Sailfish","Hunts with a high dorsal fin"],["Ocean sunfish","Carries 300 million eggs at once"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does a sailfish use its dorsal fin for when hunting?","options":["To keep itself warm","To steady its head","To warn other fish"],"answer":1,"explain":"The dorsal fin helps the sailfish keep its bill still as it hunts."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How long was the longest recorded flight of a flying fish?","options":["30 seconds","45 seconds","60 seconds"],"answer":1,"explain":"That's longer than most people can hold their breath!"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What kind of fins does a mudskipper use to move on land?","options":["Its tail fin","Its front fins","Its back fins"],"answer":1,"explain":"Mudskippers use their jointed front fins to haul themselves along."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How fast can the sailfish swim, according to careful measurements?","options":["68 mph","20-35 mph","45 mph"],"answer":1,"explain":"The higher speed was from an old fishing test, not a lab measurement."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What do flying fish look like when they glide over water?","options":["A kite","A biplane","A rocket"],"answer":1,"explain":"They have two sets of wings, like a fish-shaped biplane."}]},{"id":"animals-fish-4","course":"animals","kind":"species","group":"fish","title":"Sockeye salmon & more","names":["Sockeye salmon","Red-bellied piranha","Lungfish"],"glyph":"🐠","tint":"#6fb3ff","items":["salmon","piranha","lungfish"],"idea":"A lungfish breathes air and survives in dried mud. Find out how a salmon finds its home river and how a piranha replaces its teeth.","pages":[{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Three fish are ready for you to meet: a sockeye salmon, a red-bellied piranha, and a lungfish."},{"t":"p","text":"Each one has its own surprising story."}]},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the sockeye salmon. At sea, it’s plain silver-blue. But when it comes home to spawn, it turns fire-engine red."},{"t":"p","text":"The sockeye finds its way back to the exact river it hatched in by smell. It memorizes the scent of its home water as a young fish."},{"t":"fact","text":"Sockeye salmon swim over 870 miles (1,400 km) up rivers and climb 6,500 ft (2,000 m) to reach their spawning grounds.","label":"Homecoming"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How does a sockeye salmon find its way back to the exact river it hatched in?","options":["By remembering the scent of its home","By following the strongest current","By using the position of the sun"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the second paragraph. It tells you how the salmon remembers the scent.","explain":"The sockeye salmon remembers the chemical scent of its home water from when it was young."}}],"img":"salmon"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the red-bellied piranha, a flat, deep-bodied fish from South American rivers with a red belly and sharp teeth."},{"t":"fact","text":"A piranha does not lose its teeth one by one. It sheds a whole half-jaw of them at once and replaces the lot in one go. So one side is always fully armed while the other regrows.","label":"The tooth swap"},{"t":"p","text":"Mostly, piranhas eat insects, worms, snails, small fish, and fruit and seeds that drop into the river. They bark to communicate, making different sounds for different situations."}],"img":"piranha"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the African lungfish, a freshwater fish that breathes air with real lungs and can survive months sealed inside a cocoon of dried mud."},{"t":"fact","text":"A lungfish has real lungs joined to its throat. It swims to the surface and gulps air just like you do. Most lungfish have a pair of lungs; the Australian kind has only one.","label":"The air breather"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How does an adult African lungfish get most of its oxygen?","options":["Through its gills","By absorbing it through its skin","By breathing air from the surface"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the box. It says what a lungfish gulps at the surface.","explain":"A lungfish has real lungs, and it swims to the surface to gulp air."}}],"img":"lungfish"},{"icon":"📊","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The lungfish is about 3.3 ft long, the sockeye salmon about 2.5 ft, and the red-bellied piranha just 0.9 ft."},{"t":"term","word":"spawn","text":"To lay eggs, the way fish and frogs do."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which of the three is the longest?","options":["The sockeye salmon","The lungfish","The red-bellied piranha"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the first paragraph and find the biggest number.","explain":"The lungfish is about 3.3 ft long, making it the longest among these three."}}]},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A lungfish that can breathe air. A red-bellied piranha with sharp teeth. A sockeye salmon swimming upstream to spawn."},{"t":"p","text":"Three fish, each with its own way of living, and all so different from one another."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Sockeye salmon","Turns red to spawn"],["Red-bellied piranha","Sharp teeth, sheds half-jaw at once"],["Lungfish","Breathes air with real lungs"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"How far do sockeye salmon swim to reach their spawning grounds?","options":["500 miles","870 miles","200 miles"],"answer":1,"explain":"Sockeye salmon swim over 870 miles (1,400 km) up rivers and climb 6,500 ft (2,000 m) to reach their spawning grounds."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does a piranha do with its teeth?","options":["Loses one tooth at a time and grows it back","Sheds and replaces a whole half-jaw at once","Keeps the same teeth for its whole long life"],"answer":1,"explain":"A piranha sheds a whole half-jaw of teeth at once, so one side is always fully armed."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How does a lungfish survive in dried-up rivers?","options":["By hiding under big rocks until rain","By breathing air with real lungs","By growing legs and walking to water"],"answer":1,"explain":"Real lungs let a lungfish breathe air, so it can survive sealed in dried mud."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What do red-bellied piranhas mostly eat?","options":["Cattle, deer, and wild pigs","Insects, worms, and fruit","Mud, sand, and small pebbles"],"answer":1,"explain":"They're not as scary as you might think!"}]},{"id":"animals-insects-1","course":"animals","kind":"species","group":"insects","title":"Honeybee, Leafcutter ant & more","names":["Honeybee","Leafcutter ant","Bullet ant","Army ant","Monarch butterfly"],"glyph":"🐝","tint":"#ffb04a","items":["honeybee","leafcutter-ant","bullet-ant","army-ant","monarch-butterfly"],"idea":"A honeybee dances to tell others where flowers are. Find out which ant farms a fungus, which ant has the worst sting ever measured, and which butterfly steers by the sun with its antennae.","pages":[{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Five bugs are waiting to meet you: a honeybee, a leafcutter ant, a bullet ant, an army ant, and a monarch butterfly."},{"t":"p","text":"Each one has a special job."}]},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the honeybee, a small furry insect that lives in colonies of tens of thousands."},{"t":"p","text":"A bee that finds flowers comes home and dances. The angle of her waggle tells the others which way to fly. The length tells them how far."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does the angle of a bee's waggle dance indicate?","options":["How far to fly","The amount of nectar in the flowers","Which direction to fly"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the second paragraph and find what the angle represents.","explain":"The angle of the waggle dance tells other bees which direction to fly."}}],"img":"honeybee"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the leafcutter ant. It cuts leaves but does not eat them. It feeds the leaves to a fungus it farms underground."},{"t":"fact","text":"Leafcutter ants grow fungus in spongy gray gardens and harvest its swollen tips to feed their young. They use antibiotics made by bacteria on their bodies to keep weed-mold out of the gardens.","label":"The farmers"},{"t":"term","word":"fungus","text":"A type of organism that grows like a network of tiny threads, often forming mushrooms or molds."},{"t":"p","text":"A carrier ant hoists a leaf several times heavier than she is over her head and jogs home with it."}],"img":"leafcutter-ant"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the bullet ant, one of the biggest ants alive — up to 1.2 in (3 cm) long."},{"t":"fact","text":"It carries the most painful sting ever measured in an insect. An entomologist named Justin Schmidt ranked it as 4.0+, describing it as walking over flaming charcoal with a three-inch nail in your heel.","label":"The pain scale"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is the bullet ant's pain rating on Justin Schmidt's scale?","options":["3.0","2.5","4.0+"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the box. It gives the rating.","explain":"The bullet ant's sting ranks as 4.0+ on Justin Schmidt's pain scale."}}],"img":"bullet-ant"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the army ant, a wandering ant that builds no permanent nest. It marches and hunts as one big swarm."},{"t":"fact","text":"When a gap opens in their trail, ants throw themselves across to form a living bridge. The column runs over their backs, then the bridge gets up and follows.","label":"The living bridge"},{"t":"p","text":"At the end of a day’s march, the colony builds its shelter out of itself. Workers link legs into a hanging ball, bigger than a basketball, with the queen sealed inside. It's called a bivouac."}],"img":"army-ant"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the monarch butterfly, an orange-and-black flyer that eats poison milkweed as a caterpillar. One generation flies most of the way across a continent."},{"t":"fact","text":"Some monarchs fly from Canada to central Mexico — up to 2,800 miles (4,500 km). Not one of them has ever been there before, and no butterfly alive remembers the way.","label":"The long journey"},{"t":"p","text":"A monarch uses its antennae as a clock to steer by the sun. Researchers painted the antennae with black enamel and the butterflies lost their heading."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What part of the monarch works as a clock for steering by the sun?","options":["Their wings","Their eyes","Their legs","Their antennae"],"answer":3,"hint":"Look at the last paragraph. It tells you what part gets painted.","explain":"The antennae help the monarch steer by the sun."}}],"img":"monarch-butterfly"},{"icon":"📊","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The honeybee flies at 15 mph, while the monarch butterfly cruises at 6 mph. The bullet ant is about 1.2 in long, and a honeybee is about half an inch."},{"t":"fact","text":"All five are insects. They differ in size and speed, but each has six legs and three body parts: the head, the middle (thorax), and the back end (abdomen).","label":"Common traits"}]},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A honeybee buzzing at 15 mph. A monarch butterfly fluttering at 6 mph. A bullet ant about 1.2 in long. A leafcutter ant marching in a line. An army ant swarming together."},{"t":"p","text":"Five insects, all with six legs and three body parts, each built for its own job."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Honeybee","Dances to signal the location of flowers"],["Leafcutter ant","Grows fungus in underground gardens"],["Bullet ant","Has the most painful sting measured"],["Army ant","Forms living bridges with their bodies"],["Monarch butterfly","Uses antennae as a clock to steer by the sun"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What do leafcutter ants feed their young with?","options":["Ripe fruit they gather at night","Swollen tips of fungus they grow","Seeds and grain they store below"],"answer":1,"explain":"Leafcutter ants use the fungus they farm to feed their young."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How does a monarch butterfly steer by the sun?","options":["Using its wings to sense direction","With its antennae as a clock","By following the path of other butterflies"],"answer":1,"explain":"Monarchs use their antennae like a clock to navigate by the sun."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What do army ants build at the end of a day’s march?","options":["A nest made of leaves","A bivouac made of themselves","A shelter under the ground"],"answer":1,"explain":"Army ants form a hanging ball with their bodies, called a bivouac, to make their shelter."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How long is a bullet ant?","options":["1.5 in (3.8 cm)","1.2 in (3 cm)","0.6 in (1.5 cm)"],"answer":1,"explain":"The bullet ant can grow up to 1.2 inches long."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many legs and body parts does every insect have?","options":["Four legs and two body parts","Six legs and three body parts","Eight legs and four body parts"],"answer":1,"explain":"Every insect has six legs and three body parts: head, thorax and abdomen."}]},{"id":"animals-insects-2","course":"animals","kind":"species","group":"insects","title":"Atlas moth, Dung beetle & more","names":["Atlas moth","Dung beetle","Bombardier beetle","Hercules beetle","Firefly"],"glyph":"🐝","tint":"#ffb04a","items":["atlas-moth","dung-beetle","bombardier-beetle","hercules-beetle","firefly"],"idea":"An adult Atlas moth never eats a single meal. Find out which beetle steers by the Milky Way, which one fires a boiling-hot spray, and which one makes its own light.","pages":[{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Five bugs are waiting to meet you: an Atlas moth, a dung beetle, a bombardier beetle, a Hercules beetle, and a firefly."},{"t":"p","text":"Each one has its own special trick."}]},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the Atlas moth, one of the largest moths on earth. Its wings can be wider than your spread hand, tip to tip."},{"t":"term","word":"caterpillar","text":"The young, wormlike stage of a moth or butterfly."},{"t":"fact","text":"An adult atlas moth has no mouth — it never eats a single meal in its life. It lives about a week or two, using fat stored from when it was a caterpillar.","label":"The mouthless moth"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does an adult Atlas moth live on?","options":["Fat stored from when it was a caterpillar","Nectar sipped from flowers in the forest","Sap licked from the bark of old trees"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the last sentence of the box. It says what fuels the moth.","explain":"An adult Atlas moth has no mouth, so it lives for a week or two on fat stored from when it was a caterpillar."}}],"img":"atlas-moth"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the dung beetle. This beetle finds fresh animal droppings, carves out a piece, and shapes it into a ball to roll away."},{"t":"fact","text":"A dung beetle rolls its ball in a dead straight line by reading the Milky Way. On moonless nights, the African species Scarabaeus satyrus uses the pale band of the Milky Way to stay on course.","label":"The star gazer"}],"img":"dung-beetle"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the bombardier beetle. This small ground beetle defends itself by firing a boiling-hot chemical spray out of its back end."},{"t":"fact","text":"The spray leaves its body at about 212°F (100°C) — the temperature of boiling water. It fires in pulses around 500 times every second.","label":"The hot spray"},{"t":"p","text":"Toads that swallow a bombardier beetle sometimes vomit it back up, alive and unbothered. In one case, the beetle came back out more than an hour later."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What happens when a toad swallows a bombardier beetle?","options":["The toad digests the beetle quickly.","The beetle always dies inside the toad.","The toad sometimes vomits it back up, alive."],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the second paragraph. It describes what happens to the beetle in the toad's stomach.","explain":"The toad sometimes vomits the beetle back up, alive and unbothered."}}],"img":"bombardier-beetle"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Next up is the Hercules beetle — the longest beetle in the world. Males have a horn longer than their body."},{"t":"fact","text":"A big male stretches about 6.7 inches (17 cm) long. More than half of that length is the horn, which works like a pair of pincers.","label":"The giant horn"},{"t":"p","text":"The male's two horns close like a nutcracker. He uses them to grip and lift rivals off branches during fights over sap runs on tree trunks."},{"t":"fact","text":"Its wing cases change color depending on the humidity. In dry air they are olive-yellow, but in humid air they darken to black.","label":"The color change"}],"img":"hercules-beetle"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the firefly, a beetle that makes its own light. It uses that light to talk with flashing codes."},{"t":"fact","text":"The light comes from the underside of its abdomen, the back section of its body. A chemical called luciferin meets oxygen and a helper called luciferase to make light.","label":"The lamp"},{"t":"p","text":"A firefly controls its flash by controlling how much oxygen reaches its light-making cells."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How does a firefly control its light?","options":["It changes colors","It controls oxygen","It moves its wings"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph under the box. It says what the firefly controls to make its flash.","explain":"By controlling oxygen, the firefly can turn its light on and off."}}],"img":"firefly"},{"icon":"📊","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Five bugs with a lot in common and a lot that sets each apart. The Atlas moth is as big as your hand. The dung beetle rolls its food. The bombardier beetle has a chemical spray. The Hercules beetle grips a rival between its two horns and lifts him off a branch. The firefly flashes to talk."},{"t":"fact","text":"The Hercules beetle grows up to 6.7 inches long, horn included. The Atlas moth is as wide as your hand, and the dung beetle, bombardier beetle and firefly are each about as long as your thumb or shorter.","label":"Sizes"}]},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"An Atlas moth as big as your hand. A dung beetle rolling its food. A bombardier beetle with a chemical spray. A Hercules beetle lifting a rival off a branch. A firefly flashing to talk."},{"t":"p","text":"Five bugs, each built for their own special job."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Atlas moth","Adult has no mouth and never eats"],["Dung beetle","Rolls dung balls using the Milky Way for guidance"],["Bombardier beetle","Fires boiling-hot chemical spray"],["Hercules beetle","Horn longer than its body, used like pincers"],["Firefly","Controls light by controlling oxygen"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What do dung beetles use to stay on course on a moonless night?","options":["The sun's position","The Milky Way","The moon's glow"],"answer":1,"explain":"Dung beetles are clever and use the stars to guide them."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How long does an adult Atlas moth typically live?","options":["About six or seven months","About one or two weeks","About two or three years"],"answer":1,"explain":"The Atlas moth lives for just a short time as an adult."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What happens when the air is humid for a Hercules beetle?","options":["The wing cases go soft and wet","Its wing cases darken to black","Its horns grow a bit shorter"],"answer":1,"explain":"The humidity makes the Hercules beetle's wing cases change color."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What do bombardier beetles defend themselves with?","options":["A coat of thick, hard armor","A boiling-hot chemical spray","A pair of strong, sharp jaws"],"answer":1,"explain":"The bombardier beetle's spray is both hot and effective."}]},{"id":"animals-insects-3","course":"animals","kind":"species","group":"insects","title":"Dragonfly, Praying mantis & more","names":["Dragonfly","Praying mantis","Orchid mantis","Stick insect","Periodical cicada"],"glyph":"🐝","tint":"#ffb04a","items":["dragonfly","praying-mantis","orchid-mantis","stick-insect","cicada"],"idea":"A dragonfly can catch 95 of every 100 flies it chases. An orchid mantis looks like a flower. A cicada waits underground for 13 or 17 years. Meet five bugs with a trick.","pages":[{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Five bugs are waiting for you: a dragonfly, a praying mantis, an orchid mantis, a stick insect and a periodical cicada."},{"t":"p","text":"Each one has its own special trick. Which one would you most like to meet first?"}]},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the dragonfly. This long-bodied hunting insect has four glassy wings. It can flap them one at a time, which makes it one of the finest fliers alive."},{"t":"fact","text":"Four wings, four separate controls. A dragonfly can beat each wing on its own, which is how it hovers, flies backwards, and stops dead in mid-air.","label":"Four controls"},{"t":"p","text":"In laboratory tests, hunting dragonflies caught up to 95 out of every 100 flies they went after. No lion or shark comes anywhere near that strike rate."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many of every 100 flies did hunting dragonflies catch in the lab tests?","options":["Only 25 of every 100","Up to 95 of every 100","About 50 of every 100"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the last paragraph. It gives the number of flies caught.","explain":"Hunting dragonflies caught up to 95 out of every 100 flies they went after."}}],"img":"dragonfly"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the praying mantis, a long-necked ambush hunter. It sits perfectly still with its spined front legs folded, then grabs whatever walks past."},{"t":"fact","text":"The strike is over in less than a tenth of a second. You cannot blink that fast, and the mantis can still change its aim halfway through.","label":"The lightning strike"},{"t":"term","word":"raptorial","text":"Built for catching prey, as a mantis's folded front legs are."},{"t":"p","text":"A mantis hears with a single ear, sunk in a groove down the middle of its chest. It is tuned to a sound you cannot hear: the hunting calls of bats."}],"img":"praying-mantis"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the orchid mantis. This hunting insect lives in the rainforests of Southeast Asia. Its whole body is shaped and colored like a flower, and the flower is the trap."},{"t":"fact","text":"Each of its four walking legs is flattened into a wide, curved lobe, the exact shape of a petal. The mantis is not sitting on a flower. It is the flower.","label":"Petal legs"},{"t":"p","text":"Researchers set orchid mantises out in the rainforest, beside real flowers, and counted the visitors. More pollinating insects flew to the mantis than to the flowers."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did the researchers find when they counted visitors?","options":["The real flowers won easily","The mantis got more visitors","No insects came near at all"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the last paragraph. It tells what the researchers counted.","explain":"More pollinating insects flew to the mantis than to the real flowers."}}],"img":"orchid-mantis"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the stick insect, a long, slow, leaf-eating insect shaped exactly like the twig it stands on."},{"t":"fact","text":"The longest insect ever measured is a stick insect. Front foot to back foot, it reached 24.6 in (62.4 cm), longer than two school rulers laid end to end.","label":"The record"},{"t":"p","text":"The disguise is not only the shape. It is the stillness. A stick insect can hold one position for hours, and a hunting bird's eye is built to catch movement."},{"t":"p","text":"Many stick insects need no male at all. A female on her own lays eggs that hatch into daughters."}],"img":"stick-insect"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the periodical cicada. This big, red-eyed insect spends 13 or 17 years underground, drinking from tree roots. Then it climbs out in millions, all at once, to sing."},{"t":"fact","text":"More than 1.5 million cicadas per acre have been counted, around 370 in every square meter of forest floor.","label":"Big numbers"},{"t":"p","text":"The chorus can reach 100 decibels, as loud as a chainsaw or a motorbike going past. Only the males sing."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How long does a periodical cicada spend underground?","options":["3 or 7 years","13 or 17 years","30 or 70 years"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the first paragraph. It says how long the cicada waits underground.","explain":"A periodical cicada spends 13 or 17 years underground, feeding on fluid from tree roots."}}],"img":"cicada"},{"icon":"📊","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Five insects, and every one has its own trick. The dragonfly flies with four separate wings. The mantis strikes in a flash. The orchid mantis looks like a flower. The stick insect looks like a twig. The cicada waits for years."},{"t":"fact","text":"A dragonfly's body is about as long as your finger, and the biggest spread about 6 in (16 cm) of wing. A big mantis is over 6 in (15 cm). A stick insect can stretch to 24.6 in (62.4 cm). A cicada is about 1.2 in (3 cm) long.","label":"Sizes"}]},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A dragonfly with four wings. A mantis striking. An orchid mantis like a flower. A stick insect like a twig. A cicada climbing out after years underground."},{"t":"p","text":"Five insects, each with its own trick."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Dragonfly","Beats each wing on its own"],["Praying mantis","Strikes in under a tenth of a second"],["Orchid mantis","Looks like a flower to catch bees"],["Stick insect","Looks like a twig and holds still"],["Periodical cicada","Waits underground for 13 or 17 years"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"How does a dragonfly beat its wings?","options":["Both wings on a side together","Only when it is hovering","Each wing on its own"],"answer":2,"explain":"A dragonfly can beat each wing on its own, which is how it hovers and flies backwards."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What sound is a mantis's ear tuned to?","options":["The hunting calls of bats","The loud buzzing of bees","The chirping of crickets"],"answer":0,"explain":"A mantis hears with one ear in its chest, tuned to the hunting calls of bats."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why do bees fly to an orchid mantis?","options":["It hums like a bee","It looks like a flower","It carries a sweet drink"],"answer":1,"explain":"Its whole body is shaped and colored like a flower, and the flower is the trap."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How long can a stick insect hold one position?","options":["A few seconds","A whole year","For hours"],"answer":2,"explain":"A stick insect can hold one position for hours, and a hunting bird's eye is built to catch movement."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How loud can a cicada chorus get?","options":["As quiet as a whisper","As soft as falling rain","As loud as a chainsaw"],"answer":2,"explain":"The chorus can reach 100 decibels, as loud as a chainsaw or a motorbike going past."}]},{"id":"animals-insects-4","course":"animals","kind":"species","group":"insects","title":"Termite, Flea & Water strider","names":["Termite","Flea","Water strider"],"glyph":"🐝","tint":"#ffb04a","items":["termite","flea","water-strider"],"idea":"A flea can jump 60 times its own body length. A termite queen lays an egg every four seconds. One bug stands on a pond and never sinks. Meet all three.","pages":[{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Three small bugs are waiting for you: a termite, a flea, and a water strider."},{"t":"p","text":"Each one has its own special trick. Which one would you most like to meet first?"}]},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the termite. It is a small, pale, blind insect that eats wood and lives in a colony of millions."},{"t":"fact","text":"A worker termite is tiny. The queen of the same colony can reach 4 in (11 cm), longer than your index finger. She cannot walk, so workers bring her food and carry off her eggs.","label":"The giant queen"},{"t":"p","text":"A big queen can lay more than 20,000 eggs in a single day. That is about one egg every four seconds, all day and all night."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How often can a big termite queen lay an egg?","options":["Just one egg per day","About four eggs each hour","Once every four seconds"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. It tells how fast a big queen lays eggs.","explain":"A big queen lays about one egg every four seconds, all day and all night."}}],"img":"termite"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the flea. This tiny, wingless insect drinks blood. For its size, it jumps farther than almost anything alive."},{"t":"fact","text":"A flea can jump 7 in (18 cm) straight up. That is about 60 times its own body length. Sideways it can leap 13 in (33 cm).","label":"The jump"},{"t":"term","word":"resilin","text":"A rubbery protein that stores energy and gives it all back at once. A flea squeezes energy into a pad of it, then lets go."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How high can a flea jump straight up?","options":["Over 7 ft (2 m) straight up","About 7 in (18 cm) straight up","Less than 1 in (2.5 cm) up"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. It gives the height of the jump.","explain":"A flea can jump about 7 in (18 cm) straight up, around 60 times its own body length."}}],"img":"flea"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the water strider. This long-legged insect spends its whole life standing on top of the water without sinking."},{"t":"fact","text":"It is not swimming. It stands on the water, held up by surface tension, a thin, stretchy film across the top of the pond. Its feet dent the film but never break through.","label":"The pond's skin"},{"t":"p","text":"Its legs are furred with water-repelling hairs. More than a thousand are crammed into a single millimeter. They trap a layer of air, so the legs stay dry."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What holds a water strider up on the pond?","options":["Tiny wings that beat very fast","A thin, stretchy film on the pond","Big webbed feet like a duck's"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. It tells what the strider stands on.","explain":"Surface tension makes a thin, stretchy film across the pond, and the strider stands on it."}}],"img":"water-strider"},{"icon":"📊","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Three tiny insects, and each has its own tool. The queen termite lays eggs without stopping. The flea springs from a rubbery pad. The water strider stands on the skin of the pond."},{"t":"fact","text":"A termite queen reaches 4 in (11 cm). A flea is only 0.06 to 0.13 in (1.5 to 3.3 mm) long. A water strider has a tiny body, but its legs spread far wider than the body itself.","label":"Sizes"}]},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A termite queen laying an egg every four seconds. A flea springing from a rubbery pad. A water strider standing on the skin of the pond."},{"t":"p","text":"Three small bugs, each with its own special trick."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Termite","Lives in a colony of millions and eats wood"],["Flea","Jumps 60 times its own body length"],["Water strider","Stands on the skin of the pond"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"How long can a termite queen grow?","options":["About 4 inches (11 cm)","About 4 feet (1.2 m)","About half an inch (1.3 cm)"],"answer":0,"explain":"The queen of a termite colony can reach 4 in (11 cm), longer than your index finger."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does a flea use to make its jump?","options":["Strong wings that flap very fast","Long, powerful muscles in its back","A rubbery pad that stores energy"],"answer":2,"explain":"A flea squeezes energy into a pad of resilin, then lets it go all at once."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What keeps a water strider's legs dry?","options":["A thick coat of waxy oil on top","Hairs that trap a layer of air","Tiny sponges growing on each foot"],"answer":1,"explain":"More than a thousand water-repelling hairs per millimeter hold a layer of air against the leg."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How does a water strider sense a fly that has fallen in?","options":["Ripples on the water reach its legs","It smells the fly from far away","It hears the splash with its ears"],"answer":0,"explain":"The ripples reach its legs, and the strider reads direction and distance from the waves."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What do termites need in their gut to digest wood?","options":["Strong stones they have swallowed","A special sticky juice from the queen","Tiny microbes that break the wood down"],"answer":2,"explain":"A termite cannot break down wood alone. Its gut is full of microbes that do the job for it."}]},{"id":"animals-spiders-1","course":"animals","kind":"species","group":"spiders","title":"Goliath birdeater & more","names":["Goliath birdeater","Jumping spider","Trapdoor spider","Diving bell spider","Golden orb-weaver","Emperor scorpion","Horseshoe crab"],"glyph":"🕷️","tint":"#b088ff","items":["goliath-birdeater","jumping-spider","trapdoor-spider","diving-bell-spider","golden-orb-weaver","emperor-scorpion","horseshoe-crab"],"idea":"The Goliath birdeater is as big as a dinner plate. The diving bell spider lives in a bubble of air. The horseshoe crab has blue blood. Meet seven spiders and their kin.","pages":[{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Seven animals are waiting for you: a Goliath birdeater, a jumping spider, a trapdoor spider, a diving bell spider, a golden orb-weaver, an emperor scorpion and a horseshoe crab."},{"t":"p","text":"Each one has its own special trick. Which one would you most like to meet first?"}]},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the Goliath birdeater, the heaviest spider on Earth. It is a rainforest tarantula big enough to cover a dinner plate."},{"t":"fact","text":"Its legs reach 12 in (30 cm) across, and it can weigh 6 oz (175 g), about the same as a large apple.","label":"The big spider"},{"t":"p","text":"It hardly ever eats birds. The real menu is earthworms, beetles, frogs, toads, lizards and now and then a small snake."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does the Goliath birdeater mostly eat?","options":["Mostly hummingbirds and finches","Earthworms, beetles and frogs","Seeds and fallen fruit"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the last paragraph. It lists what the spider really eats.","explain":"Despite its name, it hardly ever eats birds. It mostly eats earthworms, beetles and frogs."}}],"img":"goliath-birdeater"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the jumping spider. This small, bright-eyed spider stalks its prey on foot and then pounces, instead of waiting in a web."},{"t":"fact","text":"Two huge front eyes and six smaller ones around the sides give it the sharpest eyesight of any animal near its size.","label":"The headlights"},{"t":"p","text":"The big front eyes cannot move, so the retinas inside them do. The dark spot behind each lens swivels and sweeps like a searchlight."},{"t":"term","word":"retina","text":"The part inside the eye that senses the picture behind the lens."}],"img":"jumping-spider"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the trapdoor spider. This stout, burrowing spider spends its whole life in a silk-lined tunnel behind a hinged door she built herself."},{"t":"fact","text":"The door is a thick plug of soil, plant fiber and silk that fits the burrow mouth exactly. She finishes the outside with the same moss, grit and dead leaf as the ground, so the burrow vanishes into the floor.","label":"The hidden door"},{"t":"p","text":"She never chases anything. When a beetle walks over, the door flicks open and her legs and fangs shoot out. She always strikes upward from beneath."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How does a trapdoor spider catch a beetle?","options":["She chases it across the forest floor","The door flicks open and she strikes","She spins a sticky web in the trees"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the last paragraph. It says what happens when a beetle walks over the door.","explain":"The door flicks open and the spider strikes upward from beneath, all in a fraction of a second."}}],"img":"trapdoor-spider"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the diving bell spider. It is the only spider in the world that lives its whole life underwater, inside a bubble of air it builds itself."},{"t":"fact","text":"It spins a silk dome between underwater plants. Then it ferries bubbles down from the surface one at a time until the dome inflates. The result looks like a small silver balloon.","label":"The air house"},{"t":"p","text":"It is about as long as your thumbnail. It hunts by feel: when something bumps a silk thread, the spider feels the tug and shoots out to grab it."}],"img":"diving-bell-spider"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the golden orb-weaver, a big tropical spider that spins a wheel-shaped web of silk the color of real gold."},{"t":"fact","text":"The gold is real color. Her silk carries a yellow pigment that she lays down as she spins. Her web can be about 5 ft (1.5 m) across.","label":"The gold silk"},{"t":"p","text":"Weight for weight, her dragline silk is stronger than steel. Small birds and bats have been found tangled in golden orb webs."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How wide can a golden orb-weaver's web be?","options":["No wider than a dinner plate","Over 50 ft (15 m) across","About 5 ft (1.5 m) across"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the box. It gives the width of the web.","explain":"The web can be about 5 ft (1.5 m) across, wider than a child is tall."}}],"img":"golden-orb-weaver"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the emperor scorpion, one of the largest scorpions in the world. It is a glossy black, heavy-clawed burrower from West Africa."},{"t":"fact","text":"Shine ultraviolet light on one at night and it glows an eerie blue-green. It runs about 8 in (20 cm) long and weighs around 1 oz (30 g).","label":"The glow"},{"t":"p","text":"The rule with scorpions is fat claws, mild venom. The emperor has enormous claws, and its sting is roughly a bee sting."}],"img":"emperor-scorpion"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the horseshoe crab. It is a helmet-shaped sea animal with a spike for a tail. Despite the name, it is not a crab."},{"t":"fact","text":"Its blood is blue. A horseshoe crab carries oxygen with copper, and copper turns blue when it grabs oxygen. Hospitals use the blood to check that vaccines are clean.","label":"The blue blood"},{"t":"p","text":"A big female runs about 2 ft (60 cm) from front to tail tip. Its two big side eyes are built from about a thousand separate units."}],"img":"horseshoe-crab"},{"icon":"📊","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Seven animals, all close kin of the spiders, and no two alike. The birdeater is the heaviest. The jumping spider sees the sharpest. The trapdoor spider hides behind a door. The diving bell spider lives in a bubble. The orb-weaver spins gold. The scorpion glows. The horseshoe crab has blue blood."},{"t":"fact","text":"The Goliath birdeater's legs span 12 in (30 cm). The emperor scorpion is about 8 in (20 cm) long. The horseshoe crab is about 2 ft (60 cm). The trapdoor spider is about 1.2 in (3 cm) long.","label":"Sizes"}]},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A birdeater as big as a dinner plate. A jumping spider with the sharpest eyesight. A trapdoor spider behind its door. A diving bell spider in its bubble. A golden orb-weaver with gold silk. A scorpion that glows. A horseshoe crab with blue blood."},{"t":"p","text":"Seven animals, each with its own special trick."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Goliath birdeater","Heaviest spider on Earth"],["Jumping spider","Has the sharpest eyesight for its size"],["Trapdoor spider","Hides behind a door she built"],["Diving bell spider","Lives underwater in a bubble of air"],["Golden orb-weaver","Spins a web of golden silk"],["Horseshoe crab","Has blue blood and a spike for a tail"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"How much can a Goliath birdeater weigh?","options":["About the same as a small grape","About the same as a big pumpkin","About the same as a large apple"],"answer":2,"explain":"It can weigh 6 oz (175 g), about the same as a large apple."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does a jumping spider glue down before every leap?","options":["A silk safety line","A sticky ball of mud","A tiny seed"],"answer":0,"explain":"If it misses, it swings on that line and then climbs calmly back up."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does a diving bell spider breathe from underwater?","options":["Gills like a fish on its legs","A hollow reed that it holds","A bubble of air it builds"],"answer":2,"explain":"The air bubble works like a gill, letting oxygen seep in from the water."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What color does an emperor scorpion glow under ultraviolet light?","options":["Bright red","Blue-green","Pale yellow"],"answer":1,"explain":"Shine ultraviolet light on one at night and it glows an eerie blue-green."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why is a horseshoe crab's blood useful in hospitals?","options":["It cures people of a bad cold fast","It helps check that vaccines are clean","It makes medicine taste a bit sweeter"],"answer":1,"explain":"The blood clots around bacterial poison, which shows whether a vaccine is clean."}]},{"id":"animals-sea-1","course":"animals","kind":"species","group":"sea","title":"Giant Pacific octopus & more","names":["Giant Pacific octopus","Mimic octopus","Colossal squid","Vampire squid","Common cuttlefish"],"glyph":"🐙","tint":"#4fd6e8","items":["giant-pacific-octopus","mimic-octopus","colossal-squid","vampire-squid","cuttlefish"],"idea":"A colossal squid can weigh over 1,000 lb. Find out which animal redraws its skin in less than a second, which one turns itself inside out, and which one fits through a gap two fingers wide.","pages":[{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Five sea creatures are waiting to meet you: a giant Pacific octopus, a mimic octopus, a colossal squid, a vampire squid, and a common cuttlefish."},{"t":"p","text":"Each one has its own secrets and tricks."}]},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Start with the largest octopus in the world. The giant Pacific octopus is soft, boneless, and can change colors."},{"t":"fact","text":"Its only truly hard part is a beak like a parrot's. Wherever the beak fits, the rest of the body can follow, even through a gap only two fingers wide.","label":"The master of escape"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is the only truly hard part of the giant Pacific octopus?","options":["Its eyes","Its skin","Its arms","Its beak"],"answer":3,"hint":"Look at the box about escaping. It names the one hard part.","explain":"The beak, like a parrot's, is the only truly hard part."}}],"img":"giant-pacific-octopus"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the mimic octopus, a small creature that lives on bare open sand and survives by pretending to be other animals, a trick called impersonation."},{"t":"fact","text":"It flattens its body and glides like a flatfish, or fans out its arms to look like a lionfish with venomous spines. Even when attacked by damselfish, it waves two arms like a sea snake to scare them away.","label":"The master of disguise"},{"t":"term","word":"impersonation","text":"Pretending to be someone else to trick others."}],"img":"mimic-octopus"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The colossal squid is the heaviest animal on Earth without a backbone. It lives in the deep waters around Antarctica."},{"t":"fact","text":"Its eye is the largest known on any animal — about 10.5 inches (27 cm) across when measured, and probably wider still in life.","label":"The giant eye"},{"t":"p","text":"One colossal squid hauled up off Antarctica weighed 1,091 lb (495 kg). It was caught mid-meal, still gripping a Patagonian toothfish."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How much did the colossal squid weigh when it was hauled up?","options":["700 lb","500 lb","1,091 lb"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the second paragraph and find the exact weight.","explain":"The colossal squid weighed 1,091 lb."}}],"img":"colossal-squid"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the vampire squid, a small, soft, red deep-sea animal that is neither a squid nor an octopus. It wears its eight arms like a hooded cloak."},{"t":"fact","text":"Its scientific name means \"vampire squid from hell.\" It drinks no blood and chases nothing; it eats mostly falling scraps.","label":"The cloak"},{"t":"p","text":"Threatened, it turns itself inside out. The cloak flips up over its head, and what was soft webbing becomes a ball of spines pointing outward in every direction. Remote submarine cameras have caught the animal holding this \"pineapple\" pose under a bright light."}],"img":"vampire-squid"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Next up, the common cuttlefish. It can redraw the pattern on its skin in less than a second."},{"t":"fact","text":"In one square millimeter of skin — a pinhead — there can be 200 color sacs. Each is wired directly to the brain, ready to stretch wide or shrink to an invisible speck.","label":"The skin that thinks"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many color sacs are in a square millimeter of cuttlefish skin?","options":["50","100","200"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It gives the exact number.","explain":"In one square millimeter of skin there can be 200 color sacs, and each one is wired directly to the brain."}}],"img":"cuttlefish"},{"icon":"📊","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The heaviest is the colossal squid at 1,091 lb, and the vampire squid is the smallest, only 1 ft long."},{"t":"fact","text":"The giant Pacific octopus is about 14 ft (4.3 m) long. The colossal squid is about 13.8 ft (4.2 m) long, yet far heavier, because its body is thick and barrel-shaped.","label":"Length"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which of the five is the heaviest?","options":["Colossal squid","Common cuttlefish","Giant Pacific octopus"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the first paragraph. One animal is named the heaviest.","explain":"The colossal squid, at 1,091 lb."}}]},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"An octopus that squeezes through a gap two fingers wide. An octopus that mimics other creatures. A squid that can grow to 1,091 lb. Another squid that turns itself inside out. A cuttlefish that changes color."},{"t":"p","text":"Five sea creatures, each with its own way of surviving in the ocean."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Giant Pacific octopus","Can fit through gaps as small as two fingers wide"],["Mimic octopus","Flattens its body and glides like a flatfish"],["Colossal squid","Weighed 1,091 lb when hauled up"],["Vampire squid","Turns itself inside out to show spines"],["Common cuttlefish","Can redraw its skin pattern in less than a second"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does a mimic octopus do when attacked by damselfish?","options":["Squirts a cloud of ink and swims off","Waves two arms like a sea snake","Hides under the sand"],"answer":1,"explain":"The mimic octopus uses clever tricks to survive."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is unique about the colossal squid's eye?","options":["It can see in complete darkness","It is the largest eye of any animal, about 10.5 inches across","It glows in the dark"],"answer":1,"explain":"The colossal squid has a giant eye to help it see in deep waters."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How does the vampire squid defend itself when threatened?","options":["Hides in a hole in the rock","Turns itself inside out","Sprays ink like an octopus"],"answer":1,"explain":"The vampire squid turns itself into a spiny ball to protect itself."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is special about the common cuttlefish's skin?","options":["Its color sacs turn on when the water gets warm","Each color sac is wired directly to the brain","It grows a thick layer of tough, shiny scales"],"answer":1,"explain":"The cuttlefish's skin changes colors quickly thanks to its direct brain connection."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How does the giant Pacific octopus escape through small gaps?","options":["Its bones fold flat so it slips through","Its soft body follows its beak through","Its arms push the rocks out of the way"],"answer":1,"explain":"The octopus uses its small beak to fit through tight spots."}]},{"id":"animals-sea-2","course":"animals","kind":"species","group":"sea","title":"Chambered nautilus & more","names":["Chambered nautilus","Box jellyfish","Lion's mane jellyfish","Immortal jellyfish","Sea star"],"glyph":"🐙","tint":"#4fd6e8","items":["nautilus","box-jellyfish","lions-mane-jellyfish","immortal-jellyfish","sea-star"],"idea":"A nautilus lives in a coiled shell. A box jellyfish has twenty-four eyes and no brain. An immortal jellyfish can start its life over. Meet five sea animals.","pages":[{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Five sea animals are waiting for you: a chambered nautilus, a box jellyfish, a lion's mane jellyfish, an immortal jellyfish and a sea star."},{"t":"p","text":"Each one has its own special trick. Which one would you most like to meet first?"}]},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the chambered nautilus, a cephalopod that lives inside a coiled shell. About ninety small tentacles and two pinhole eyes peer out of it."},{"t":"fact","text":"Every other cephalopod alive, such as octopus, squid and cuttlefish, keeps its shell on the inside or has none at all. The nautilus is the only one that lives inside one.","label":"The only one"},{"t":"p","text":"The shell is a spiral of sealed chambers, around thirty in a grown animal. The nautilus lives only in the outermost room, and the rest are empty and full of gas."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where does the nautilus live inside its shell?","options":["In the innermost room","In the outermost room","In every single room"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the last paragraph. It says which room the nautilus lives in.","explain":"The nautilus lives only in the outermost room. The rest are empty and full of gas."}}],"img":"nautilus"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the box jellyfish. This cube-shaped jellyfish has tentacles trailing from each corner. It swims after its prey instead of drifting."},{"t":"fact","text":"It has twenty-four eyes, in four clusters of six, and no brain at all. A ring of nerve around the rim of the bell coordinates every pulse and every turn.","label":"Twenty-four eyes"},{"t":"term","word":"stinging cell","text":"A tiny coiled harpoon, held under pressure, that fires in less than a thousandth of a second."},{"t":"p","text":"Every tentacle is carpeted in stinging cells. The jellyfish beats its bell and reaches about 1.5 meters a second (5.4 km/h, 3.4 mph), a brisk walking pace."}],"img":"box-jellyfish"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the lion's mane jellyfish, the largest jellyfish in the ocean. A shaggy curtain of stinging tentacles hangs beneath its bell, and it drifts in cold water."},{"t":"fact","text":"The biggest bells measured were over 7 ft (2 m) across, wider than a door is tall. The tentacles trailed more than 100 ft (30 m) behind.","label":"A giant"},{"t":"p","text":"Around 1,200 tentacles hang in eight thick bunches, like eight mops under one umbrella. About 95% of a jellyfish is water."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How are a lion's mane's tentacles grouped?","options":["In one long single row","In eight thick bunches","In two very long braids"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the last paragraph. It says how the tentacles hang.","explain":"Around 1,200 tentacles hang in eight thick bunches, like eight mops under one umbrella."}}],"img":"lions-mane-jellyfish"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the immortal jellyfish. It is smaller than your fingernail. When it is injured or starving, it rewinds itself back into a baby and starts its life over."},{"t":"fact","text":"A full-grown adult is about 0.18 in (4.5 mm) across. Injure it, starve it, or chill the water, and instead of dying it sinks and turns into its own young stage.","label":"Turning back"},{"t":"p","text":"The shrunken blob settles on the seafloor and grows into a polyp, a tiny stalk. That polyp then buds off brand-new jellyfish."},{"t":"p","text":"It is see-through except for one bright red stomach, glowing in the middle of the bell like a warning light."}],"img":"immortal-jellyfish"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the sea star. This five-armed animal walks on hundreds of tiny water-powered feet. It eats by pushing its stomach out through its own mouth."},{"t":"fact","text":"It has no fins, no scales, no backbone, no bones at all. Sea stars are echinoderms, a word that means spiny skin.","label":"Not a fish"},{"t":"p","text":"It eats by turning its stomach inside out. The stomach slides out of the mouth, wraps around the meal, digests it outside the body, then folds back in."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How does a sea star eat?","options":["It crushes food with teeth","It pushes its stomach out","It sucks food in like a straw"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the last paragraph. It says what the stomach does.","explain":"The stomach slides out of the mouth, wraps around the meal and digests it outside the body."}}],"img":"sea-star"},{"icon":"📊","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Five sea animals, each built in its own odd way. The nautilus lives in a coiled shell. The box jellyfish has twenty-four eyes and no brain. The lion's mane is the largest jellyfish in the ocean. The immortal jellyfish can start over. The sea star turns its stomach inside out."},{"t":"fact","text":"A nautilus shell is about as wide as a side plate. A lion's mane bell can reach 6.6 ft (2 m) across. The immortal jellyfish is about 0.18 in (4.5 mm) across. A box jellyfish's tentacles trail up to 9.8 ft (3 m).","label":"Sizes"}]},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A nautilus in its coiled shell. A box jellyfish with its many eyes. A lion's mane with its long tentacles. A tiny jellyfish starting over. A sea star with its stomach outside."},{"t":"p","text":"Five sea animals, each with its own special trick."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Chambered nautilus","Lives inside a coiled shell"],["Box jellyfish","Cube-shaped and swims after prey"],["Lion's mane jellyfish","Largest jellyfish in the ocean"],["Immortal jellyfish","Turns back into a baby"],["Sea star","Five arms and tube feet"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which cephalopod lives inside its shell?","options":["The octopus","The squid","The nautilus"],"answer":2,"explain":"Octopus, squid and cuttlefish keep their shells inside or have none. The nautilus lives inside one."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How does a box jellyfish move?","options":["It drifts wherever the water goes","It swims after its prey","It crawls along the seabed"],"answer":1,"explain":"Most jellyfish go wherever the water goes. A box jellyfish beats its bell and hunts."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How big can a lion's mane jellyfish bell get?","options":["As big as a dinner plate","About 7 in (18 cm) across","Over 7 ft (2 m) across"],"answer":2,"explain":"The biggest bells measured were over 7 ft (2 m) across, wider than a door is tall."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does the immortal jellyfish do when it is injured?","options":["It turns back into a baby","It sinks and dies","It floats up to the surface"],"answer":0,"explain":"Instead of dying, it sinks and turns into its own young stage, then starts its life over."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many arms does a sea star have?","options":["Three","Five","Eight"],"answer":1,"explain":"A sea star is a five-armed animal that walks on hundreds of tiny water-powered feet."}]},{"id":"animals-sea-3","course":"animals","kind":"species","group":"sea","title":"Sea urchin, Sea cucumber & more","names":["Sea urchin","Sea cucumber","Giant clam","Cone snail","Coral","Sea slug"],"glyph":"🐙","tint":"#4fd6e8","items":["sea-urchin","sea-cucumber","giant-clam","cone-snail","coral","sea-slug"],"idea":"A sea cucumber can fire its own guts to escape. Find out which animal borrows its prey's stingers, which one farms algae inside its flesh, and which one builds a stone city.","pages":[{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Six sea creatures are ready to meet you: a sea urchin, a sea cucumber, a giant clam, a cone snail, coral, and a sea slug."},{"t":"p","text":"Each one is special in its own way."}]},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"First up is a sea urchin — like a living pincushion that walks on sticky tube feet."},{"t":"fact","text":"Turn one over and there is a mouth in the middle with five teeth meeting in a point. The whole jaw has a name of its own: Aristotle's lantern.","label":"The jaw"},{"t":"p","text":"These teeth never go blunt. As the tip wears away, it breaks off along a set line, leaving a fresh edge behind — so the jaw sharpens itself by being used."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How does the sea urchin keep its teeth sharp?","options":["It uses its spines to sharpen them.","The jaw sharpens itself as the tips wear away.","It has a special organ that sharpens the teeth."],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the last two sentences. They explain how the jaw sharpens itself.","explain":"The jaw sharpens itself as the tips wear away, leaving fresh edges behind."}}],"img":"sea-urchin"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Next up is a sea cucumber — a soft, leathery animal shaped like a fat sausage that vacuums the seafloor clean."},{"t":"fact","text":"Squeeze one hard and it may fire its own guts out through its back end. The attacker gets a mouthful of stomach; the sea cucumber crawls off and grows a new one. This process is called evisceration.","label":"The surprise defense"},{"t":"term","word":"evisceration","text":"When an animal throws out its insides to escape danger."},{"t":"p","text":"It breathes through its bottom. Water goes in through the anus and into branching tubes inside the body called respiratory trees, which pull the oxygen out of it."}],"img":"sea-cucumber"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the giant clam. It grows up to 3.3 ft (1 m) wide and can weigh up to 441 lb (200 kg)."},{"t":"fact","text":"Inside its flesh, millions of tiny algae live and provide the clam with food. The algae catch sunlight and give the clam sugar. One adult can release up to 500 million eggs in a single spawning.","label":"The farmer"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many eggs can one adult giant clam release in a single spawning?","options":["50 million","500 million","100 million"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the last sentence of the box about the farmer.","explain":"One adult can release up to 500 million eggs in a single spawning."}}],"img":"giant-clam"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the cone snail. It's a beautiful sea snail that hunts fish with a venom-loaded harpoon it fires from its mouth."},{"t":"fact","text":"The cone snail uses a tooth in its mouth as a harpoon. It fires this tooth into a fish, and then reels the catch right back in.","label":"The hunter's harpoon"},{"t":"p","text":"Its venom is so powerful that one ingredient turned out to be helpful for people in severe pain. A single ingredient from the magician's cone became a medicine called ziconotide, which helps with pain that morphine can't touch."}],"img":"cone-snail"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Coral isn't a rock or a plant. It's a colony of tiny soft animals that build a stone city and live inside it."},{"t":"p","text":"Each polyp is a soft sac, usually less than a quarter of an inch (a few millimeters) across. They build themselves a limestone cup to live in, cup by cup. The color you see on a reef comes from the tiny algae living inside each polyp, not from the polyp itself."},{"t":"fact","text":"The Great Barrier Reef runs over 1,243 miles (2,000 km) along Australia's coast and you can see it from space.","label":"The biggest structure"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What gives colorful coral most of its color?","options":["The algae inside the polyp","The polyp's own color","The limestone cup around it"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the last sentence of the second paragraph.","explain":"The algae living inside the polyp give coral its color."}}],"img":"coral"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the sea slug, a sea snail with no shell, soft and colorful. It's also a thief, taking weapons from its meals."},{"t":"fact","text":"Some sea slugs eat stinging hydroids and store the sting cells in their frilly tips. The stolen cells are called kleptocnidae — literally \"stolen stingers.\"","label":"The stolen stingers"},{"t":"p","text":"The blue dragon eats Portuguese man o' war and stores its stings, making its tiny body even more dangerous."}],"img":"sea-slug"},{"icon":"📊","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"All six are sea creatures, but they're very different. The giant clam is the biggest at 3.3 feet long and can live up to 100 years. Not counting a single coral polyp, the sea slug is the smallest at just 0.2 feet long, and it lives only a year."},{"t":"fact","text":"The sea cucumber and sea slug are soft all over. The giant clam has a shell, the sea urchin has spines and a stone-hard jaw, and coral builds a cup of limestone.","label":"Soft and hard"}]},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A giant clam that can grow to 3.3 feet long and live for a century. A sea slug that's just 0.2 feet long but lives for only a year. A coral that builds a stone city. A cone snail with a venom-loaded harpoon. A sea cucumber that fires its guts to escape. A sea urchin covered in spines."},{"t":"p","text":"Six sea creatures, each unique in size, lifespan, and way of life."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Sea urchin","Covered in spines"],["Sea cucumber","Fires its guts to escape"],["Giant clam","Grows up to 3.3 ft wide"],["Cone snail","Hunts fish with venomous harpoon"],["Coral","Builds a stone city"],["Sea slug","Steals stinging cells from its meals"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does a sea cucumber do when squeezed hard?","options":["It releases ink to confuse the attacker.","It fires its guts out through its back end.","It inflates and becomes too big to swallow."],"answer":1,"explain":"This surprising defense helps it escape predators."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How does the giant clam get food?","options":["It hunts small fish with a harpoon.","It has algae inside that provide sugar.","It scrapes algae off rocks with its shell."],"answer":1,"explain":"The algae help the clam by turning sunlight into food."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is the Great Barrier Reef known for?","options":["Being the deepest part of any ocean in the world.","Running over 1,243 miles along Australia's coast.","Having the biggest sharks found anywhere on Earth."],"answer":1,"explain":"It's so big you can see it from space!"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does a cone snail use to catch its prey?","options":["A sticky net of silk across its mouth.","A venom-loaded harpoon in its mouth.","A long stinging tentacle on its head."],"answer":1,"explain":"The cone snail is a clever hunter with a powerful weapon."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What do some sea slugs store after eating stinging hydroids?","options":["The hydroid's long stinging tentacles.","Stolen sting cells called kleptocnidae.","Bits of the hydroid's hard outer skeleton."],"answer":1,"explain":"These stolen stingers help protect the sea slug."}]},{"id":"animals-crustaceans-1","course":"animals","kind":"species","group":"crustaceans","title":"Mantis shrimp & more","names":["Mantis shrimp","Pistol shrimp","Coconut crab","Japanese spider crab","Hermit crab"],"glyph":"🦀","tint":"#ff9f7a","items":["mantis-shrimp","pistol-shrimp","coconut-crab","japanese-spider-crab","hermit-crab"],"idea":"The mantis shrimp has the fastest punch in the ocean. Find out which of these five crabs and shrimps can span up to 12 ft.","pages":[{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Five animals are waiting to meet you: a mantis shrimp, a pistol shrimp, a coconut crab, a Japanese spider crab, and a hermit crab."},{"t":"p","text":"Each one is special in its own way."}]},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"First up, meet the mantis shrimp. It’s not really a shrimp but a reef crustacean with the fastest punch in the ocean."},{"t":"fact","text":"The punch lands with about 1,500 newtons of force, roughly 340 lb of push. That is more than a thousand times its own body weight.","label":"The punch"},{"t":"p","text":"Its punch moves so fast it creates a bubble of vapor that collapses an instant later, adding a second hit to the same spot."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many times its own body weight does the mantis shrimp's punch land with?","options":["Only about 200 times","More than 1,000 times","Less than 50 times"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box about the punch. It gives the number of times.","explain":"The mantis shrimp's punch lands with more than 1,000 times its own body weight."}}],"img":"mantis-shrimp"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Next, meet the pistol shrimp. It’s a thumb-sized shrimp with one oversized claw that kills by snapping a bubble at things."},{"t":"fact","text":"Its weapon isn't the claw — it's the bubble. When the claw snaps, water splits into a vapor pocket, and when that collapses (cavitation), it hits prey hard enough to stun it.","label":"The snap"},{"t":"term","word":"cavitation","text":"A bubble of empty space in water that forms when water is pushed apart really fast."},{"t":"p","text":"The shrimp never has to touch its dinner. The shockwave from the snapping claw stops a small fish or worm from moving, and the shrimp strolls out to collect it."}],"img":"pistol-shrimp"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Next up is the coconut crab — the largest crab that walks on land. Leg tip to leg tip, it can span 3 feet (1 meter) — the width of a doorway."},{"t":"fact","text":"The strongest coconut crab tested squeezed with 1,765 newtons (about 397 lb of push) — like balancing two adults on a single point, and it does not let go quickly.","label":"The pinch"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is the force measured in newtons for the strongest coconut crab pinch?","options":["100 newtons","1,765 newtons","300 newtons"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box about the pinch. It gives the number of newtons.","explain":"The strongest coconut crab tested squeezed with 1,765 newtons."}}],"img":"coconut-crab"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Next up is the Japanese spider crab — the legs of this deep-water crab stretch further than any other animal with a shell on the outside."},{"t":"fact","text":"Claw tip to claw tip, it can reach up to 12 ft (3.7 m) — that's two grown adults lying head to toe.","label":"The span"},{"t":"p","text":"The body is the surprise. Nearly all of its 12 ft (3.7 m) span is leg — the body shell, called the carapace, is only about 15.7 in (40 cm) across."}],"img":"japanese-spider-crab"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Next is the hermit crab — a crab with a soft, curled tail that borrows an empty snail shell to live in."},{"t":"fact","text":"Its back half is bare. Under the borrowed shell, the abdomen is soft, pale and twisted into a spiral, curled to fit the inside of a snail shell exactly.","label":"The soft underbelly"},{"t":"p","text":"They queue for houses. A crab that finds a shell too big waits beside it. As others arrive they line up largest to smallest. Then all of them swap at once, down the line."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What do hermit crabs do when they find a shell that's too big?","options":["Wait beside the shell","Carry it away to a hiding place","Climb into the shell"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the last paragraph. It describes how they line up and swap shells.","explain":"Hermit crabs wait beside a shell that is too big and queue up to swap with others."}}],"img":"hermit-crab"},{"icon":"📊","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The Japanese spider crab is the longest at 12.1 ft. The pistol shrimp is the shortest at just 0.2 ft."},{"t":"fact","text":"The mantis shrimp's punch lands with about 1,500 newtons, and the coconut crab's pinch squeezes with 1,765 newtons.","label":"Strikes"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which of the five is the longest?","options":["The Japanese spider crab","The giant coconut crab","The peacock mantis shrimp"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the first paragraph. It gives the exact lengths.","explain":"The Japanese spider crab is the longest, measuring 12.1 ft."}}]},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A mantis shrimp with a powerful punch. A pistol shrimp that snaps its claws. A coconut crab with a pinch of 1,765 newtons. A Japanese spider crab with legs that stretch 12.1 ft. A hermit crab that moves into new homes."},{"t":"p","text":"Five crustaceans, each one built differently, and all with surprising abilities."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Mantis shrimp","Fastest punch in the ocean"],["Pistol shrimp","Snaps a bubble to stun prey"],["Coconut crab","Largest crab that walks on land"],["Japanese spider crab","Legs stretch up to 12 ft"],["Hermit crab","Lives in borrowed snail shells"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does the pistol shrimp use to stun its prey?","options":["a puff of dark ink","a collapsing bubble","a sting from its tail"],"answer":1,"explain":"The pistol shrimp uses a bubble to create a shockwave."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How does the mantis shrimp's punch create a second hit?","options":["by springing back off the shell of its prey","by creating a vapor bubble that collapses","by spinning its body around in the water"],"answer":1,"explain":"The mantis shrimp’s punch creates a vapor bubble that adds another hit when it collapses."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is the carapace of the Japanese spider crab like in size?","options":["nearly 31.5 inches (80 cm) across","only about 15.7 inches (40 cm) across","roughly 4.5 feet (137 cm) across"],"answer":1,"explain":"The carapace of the Japanese spider crab is much smaller than you might think."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What special ability does the coconut crab have?","options":["it can breathe underwater for hours","it can squeeze with 1,765 newtons","it can fire a jet of water at prey"],"answer":1,"explain":"The coconut crab has a powerful pinch that can squeeze very hard."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How do hermit crabs line up to swap shells?","options":["from smallest to largest","largest to smallest","randomly"],"answer":1,"explain":"Hermit crabs line up from largest to smallest to swap shells."}]},{"id":"animals-crustaceans-2","course":"animals","kind":"species","group":"crustaceans","title":"Fiddler crab & more","names":["Fiddler crab","American lobster","Barnacle","Antarctic krill"],"glyph":"🦀","tint":"#ff9f7a","items":["fiddler-crab","american-lobster","barnacle","krill"],"idea":"A male fiddler crab carries a claw that weighs half of him. A lobster chews with its stomach. A barnacle glues its head to a rock. Meet four small sea animals.","pages":[{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Four small sea animals are waiting for you: a fiddler crab, an American lobster, a barnacle, and an Antarctic krill."},{"t":"p","text":"Each one has its own special tool. Which one would you most like to meet first?"}]},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the fiddler crab. This small mudflat crab is only about 1.2 in (3 cm) across. The male has one ordinary claw and one huge one."},{"t":"fact","text":"In a male, the big claw can reach half his total weight. He carries it everywhere and waves it like a flag.","label":"The giant claw"},{"t":"p","text":"Females have two small claws, and both work as spoons. A male can feed with only one."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How heavy can a male fiddler crab's big claw get?","options":["About the weight of a pebble","Half of his whole weight","Only a few grains of sand"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. It says how heavy the big claw can get.","explain":"A male's big claw can reach half his total weight, and he carries it everywhere."}}],"img":"fiddler-crab"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the American lobster. This armored crawler walks the cold Atlantic seabed, hunting anything it can crack open."},{"t":"fact","text":"The claws do not match. One is a fat crusher with blunt knobs for smashing shells. The other is a thin ripper with a jagged edge, like a steak knife.","label":"Two different claws"},{"t":"term","word":"gastric mill","text":"Three hard teeth in a lobster's stomach that grind up food after it is swallowed."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where does a lobster chew its food?","options":["In its tail","In its stomach","In its claws"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box with the new word. It tells where the teeth are.","explain":"A lobster chews with its stomach, where three hard teeth grind up the food."}}],"img":"american-lobster"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the barnacle, a cousin of crabs and shrimp. When it is young, it glues its own head to a rock and stays there for life."},{"t":"fact","text":"Barnacle glue sticks on a wet, dirty, moving surface, even underwater. Engineers copied it to make a paste that seals a bleeding wound in about fifteen seconds.","label":"The glue"},{"t":"p","text":"A barnacle lies on its back inside its shell, head cemented down. Its feathery legs sweep out and haul food back inside."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did engineers copy from barnacles?","options":["A shell that glows in the dark","A leg that grows back each year","A glue that sticks when wet"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the box. It tells what engineers copied.","explain":"Engineers copied barnacle glue, which sticks on wet, dirty surfaces, to seal wounds fast."}}],"img":"barnacle"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the Antarctic krill. This small, almost see-through animal swims in the freezing Southern Ocean. It is about as long as your little finger."},{"t":"fact","text":"At their thickest, 10,000 to 30,000 krill pack into one cubic meter of water. Whales, seals, penguins, squid and fish all hunt in the swarm.","label":"The swarm"},{"t":"p","text":"Krill make their own light. Glowing organs beneath the eyes shine yellow-green for two or three seconds at a time."}],"img":"krill"},{"icon":"📊","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Four small sea animals, and four very different tools. The fiddler crab waves a giant claw. The lobster grinds food with teeth in its stomach. The barnacle glues itself to a rock. The krill swarms by the thousands and glows."},{"t":"fact","text":"A fiddler crab is about 1.2 in (3 cm) across. A barnacle is smaller than your thumbnail. A krill is about 2.4 in (6 cm) long. A common lobster is about as long as your forearm.","label":"Sizes"}]},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A fiddler crab waving a giant claw. A lobster with teeth in its stomach. A barnacle glued to a rock. A krill swarm glowing in the dark."},{"t":"p","text":"Four small sea animals, each with its own special tool."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Fiddler crab","Waves one giant claw like a flag"],["American lobster","Has teeth in its stomach"],["Barnacle","Glues its head to a rock"],["Antarctic krill","Swarms and makes its own light"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is odd about a lobster's two claws?","options":["They are both the same size","They are two different shapes","They fall off every day"],"answer":1,"explain":"One claw is a fat crusher and the other is a thin ripper, so they do not match."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How does a fiddler crab stay safe at high tide?","options":["It swims far out into the sea","It climbs up onto a tall rock","It seals its burrow with mud"],"answer":2,"explain":"At high tide the crab plugs its burrow with mud and breathes from a pocket of air."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How does a barnacle feed?","options":["Feathery legs comb food from the water","It chases small fish through the sea","It digs for worms in the soft mud"],"answer":0,"explain":"A barnacle sweeps its feathery legs out and hauls plankton back inside."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What do krill do each night?","options":["Dive down deep to sleep","Climb up to feed","Walk across the ice"],"answer":1,"explain":"Krill drop deep by day, where it is dark and safer, then climb back up at night to feed."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What makes krill glow?","options":["Glow-worms they have swallowed","Sunlight stored in their shells","Light organs they can aim"],"answer":2,"explain":"Krill have light organs that glow yellow-green, and they can rotate them to aim the light."}]},{"id":"animals-worms-1","course":"animals","kind":"species","group":"worms","title":"Earthworm, Medicinal leech & more","names":["Earthworm","Medicinal leech","Planarian","Velvet worm","Banana slug","Garden snail"],"glyph":"🪱","tint":"#c9a06f","items":["earthworm","leech","planarian","velvet-worm","banana-slug","garden-snail"],"idea":"A planarian can regrow from a scrap one 279th of its size. Find out which animal shoots glue at its prey, which one bites with three jaws, and which one has over ten thousand teeth on its tongue.","pages":[{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Six animals are ready to meet you: an earthworm, a medicinal leech, a planarian, a velvet worm, a banana slug, and a garden snail."},{"t":"p","text":"Each has its own surprising way of living."}]},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the earthworm, a soft, ringed animal that eats its way through soil. It swallows dirt at one end and leaves it richer at the other."},{"t":"fact","text":"An earthworm has no lungs. It breathes straight through its skin, which is why it has to stay damp — a dried-out worm suffocates.","label":"Breathing skin"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How does an earthworm breathe?","options":["Through its skin","Through gills","Using its hearts"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It tells you how an earthworm breathes.","explain":"An earthworm breathes through its skin because it has no lungs."}}],"img":"earthworm"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the medicinal leech, a soft, muscular freshwater worm with a sucker at each end. It feeds on blood and is still kept in hospital tanks today."},{"t":"fact","text":"A leech has tiny teeth arranged in three jaws shaped like a Y. The bite leaves a mark shaped like a three-pointed star and keeps bleeding for hours after the leech lets go.","label":"The bite that bleeds"},{"t":"p","text":"Hospitals still use leeches because their spit has hirudin, a chemical that stops blood from clotting. This helps in surgeries where blood needs to flow freely."}],"img":"leech"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the planarian, a soft, flat little worm that glides along the underside of stones in a cold stream."},{"t":"fact","text":"Cut a planarian in half and you get two planarians. Even a tiny scrap can grow into a whole new worm. A piece as small as one two-hundred-and-seventy-ninth of the worm can regrow everything it needs, including the head, brain, and eyes.","label":"The rebuild"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How small a piece can regrow into a complete planarian?","options":["One four-hundred-and-ninetieth","One six-hundred-and-thirtieth","One two-hundred-and-seventy-ninth"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It gives the exact number.","explain":"A piece as small as one two-hundred-and-seventy-ninth of the worm can regrow into a complete planarian."}}],"img":"planarian"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the velvet worm, a soft night hunter of damp forest floors that catches its dinner by shooting glue at it."},{"t":"fact","text":"It hunts with glue. Two nozzles either side of its mouth fire jets of sticky slime that stiffen in seconds, leaving the prey wrapped in a net it cannot break out of. The jets leave the nozzles at 10 to 16 ft per second (3 to 5 m/s) — faster than you could snatch your hand back.","label":"The sticky attack"},{"t":"p","text":"Velvet worms eat their own dried glue and recycle the protein once the meal is over. Refilling an emptied slime store takes about 24 days."}],"img":"velvet-worm"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Next up is the banana slug, a big, bright yellow creature found in Pacific coast forests. It eats fallen leaves and turns them into soil."},{"t":"fact","text":"Its slime can numb the mouth of an attacker. The mucus is so sticky that the attacker often gives up mid-meal.","label":"The sticky defense"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does the banana slug's slime do to an attacker?","options":["Makes it run faster","Numbs its mouth","Stains its fur"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the first sentence in the fact box.","explain":"The banana slug's slime numbs the mouth of an attacker and is so sticky that the attacker often gives up."}}],"img":"banana-slug"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Next up is the garden snail, a common brown snail you might find under a flowerpot after rain. It has its own house on its back."},{"t":"term","word":"radula","text":"A ribbon covered in tiny teeth that a snail uses to scrape up its food."},{"t":"fact","text":"Its tongue is a ribbon with over ten thousand tiny teeth. The ribbon is called a radula. A snail rasps leaves with this toothed belt, like sandpaper.","label":"The toothy tongue"}],"img":"garden-snail"},{"icon":"📊","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Side by side, all six are soft and squishy, but each has its own look and life. The earthworm is about 0.8 ft long and lives for 2 years. The medicinal leech is shorter at 0.7 ft. A planarian is only about 0.1 ft long, about an inch."},{"t":"p","text":"The velvet worm is about 0.2 ft long, while the banana slug stretches to 0.6 ft and can live up to 4 years. The garden snail is also just 0.1 ft long, and it moves at a slow speed of 0.005 mph, about 26 feet in an hour."}]},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"An earthworm that digs. A medicinal leech that sticks. A planarian that splits in two. A velvet worm that squirts slime. A banana slug that slides slowly. A garden snail that carries its home."},{"t":"p","text":"Six soft bodies, six different ways to live."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Earthworm","Breathes through its damp skin"],["Medicinal leech","Bites and leaves a star-shaped mark"],["Planarian","Regrows from one two-hundred-and-seventy-ninth of its body"],["Velvet worm","Shoots glue to catch prey"],["Banana slug","Slime numbs attackers"],["Garden snail","Has over ten thousand tiny teeth"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does a leech use to prevent blood from clotting?","options":["Acid from its stomach","Hirudin in its spit","Slime on its skin"],"answer":1,"explain":"Leeches have a special chemical in their spit that keeps blood flowing freely."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How long does it take for a velvet worm to refill its slime store?","options":["3 days","24 days","1 week"],"answer":1,"explain":"Velvet worms need time to make more slime after they use it up."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does the garden snail's tongue have on it?","options":["Rows of sticky, curved hairs","Over ten thousand tiny teeth","A pair of hard, sharp fangs"],"answer":1,"explain":"The garden snail's tongue has a lot of tiny teeth to help it eat leaves."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How long is a banana slug?","options":["0.2 ft","0.6 ft","0.1 ft"],"answer":1,"explain":"Banana slugs are longer than you might think!"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does an earthworm do as it moves through the soil?","options":["It packs the soil down very hard","It eats soil and leaves it richer","It drinks the water out of the soil"],"answer":1,"explain":"Earthworms help make the soil better as they move through it."}]},{"id":"animals-tiny-1","course":"animals","kind":"species","group":"tiny","title":"Tardigrade & more","names":["Tardigrade","Bdelloid rotifer","Amoeba","Paramecium","Diatom","Dust mite"],"glyph":"🔬","tint":"#ff7a6b","items":["tardigrade","rotifer","amoeba","paramecium","diatom","dust-mite"],"idea":"A tardigrade can survive being dried out for years. Find out which animal shrinks to a speck of dust, which one builds a shell of glass, and which one lives in your mattress.","pages":[{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Six tiny wonders are waiting to meet you: a tardigrade, a bdelloid rotifer, an amoeba, a paramecium, a diatom, and a dust mite."},{"t":"p","text":"Each one is special in its own way."}]},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the tardigrade, a microscopic eight-legged animal that can live in a film of water on a scrap of moss."},{"t":"fact","text":"A dried-out tardigrade folds into a barrel shape called a tun and stops moving or feeding. It can stay like this for years and come back to life when water returns.","label":"The tiny but tough"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What shape does a tardigrade fold into when it dries out?","options":["A ball","A tun","A spiral"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the last sentence of the fact box.","explain":"A tun. This shape helps the tardigrade survive without water for long periods."}}],"img":"tardigrade"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Next up is the bdelloid rotifer, an animal so tiny you need a microscope to see it. It has a brain, jaws, muscles, and all the parts of a real animal packed into a body smaller than the dot on the letter \"i\"."},{"t":"p","text":"The head of a bdelloid rotifer ends in two rings of beating hairs called the corona. Under a microscope, these look like spinning wheels."},{"t":"fact","text":"Dry one out completely, and it shrivels into a speck of dust. But add a drop of water, and it uncurls and comes back to life. This ability is called anhydrobiosis. Rotifers have been brought back to life after being dried out for nine years.","label":"The tough survivor"}],"img":"rotifer"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the amoeba, a single living cell that has no fixed shape at all. It flows into whatever shape it needs next."},{"t":"fact","text":"An amoeba has no front, back or fixed shape. It moves by pushing out a bulge called a pseudopod and pouring its insides into it. Watch one for a minute and it's always changing.","label":"The shape shifter"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is the name of the bulge an amoeba pushes out to move?","options":["Pseudopod","Tentacle","Flagellum"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the second sentence in the fact box.","explain":"A pseudopod is the bulge an amoeba uses to move. It means \"false foot\" in Greek."}}],"img":"amoeba"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Next up is the paramecium, shaped like a slipper and covered with tiny hairs called cilia that row it through water."},{"t":"term","word":"cilia","text":"Tiny hairs on a cell that beat together to push it through water."},{"t":"fact","text":"It crosses about 0.04 in (1 mm) every second, which is four of its own body lengths. Imagine you swimming four of your body lengths each second!","label":"The swimmer"},{"t":"p","text":"Bump a paramecium into an obstacle and it does something clever: it backs up, turns slightly, and tries forward again. This is called the avoidance reaction."}],"img":"paramecium"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Next up is the diatom, a single-celled drifter that makes food from sunlight and lives inside a shell made of real glass."},{"t":"fact","text":"A diatom builds its shell out of silica — the same mineral as sand and window glass. It pulls dissolved silicon from water to create a finely patterned glass house called a frustule. No two kinds look alike, with ribs, pores, spikes, stars, triangles, and spirals.","label":"The glass house"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does a diatom use to make its shell?","options":["Limestone","Wood","Silica"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the fact about the shell.","explain":"A diatom uses silica from water to build its glass shell."}}],"img":"diatom"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the dust mite, a tiny eight-legged cousin of spiders that lives in your mattress and eats the flakes of dead skin you shed."},{"t":"fact","text":"Dust mites are smaller than the period at the end of this sentence. You could line up eighty of them across the width of your thumbnail.","label":"The invisible diners"},{"t":"p","text":"They live in almost every house with people, feasting on the dead skin you leave behind in your bed, pillow, carpet, and sofa. Their mouthparts are built for scraping and chewing dry crumbs, not biting you."}],"img":"dust-mite"},{"icon":"📊","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"All of these creatures are so small they can hide on the tip of a pin. A dust mite lives for about two and a half months."},{"t":"fact","text":"Tardigrades, bdelloid rotifers, amoebas, paramecia, diatoms, and dust mites are all smaller than the tip of a pin. Even the biggest of them, a tardigrade, is about the size of a grain of salt.","label":"Sizes"}]},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Tiny creatures that hide on a pin tip. A tardigrade with eight legs. A bdelloid rotifer swimming in a drop of water. An amoeba stretching its body. A paramecium with cilia. Diatoms like tiny glass jewels. A dust mite that eats the skin flakes you shed."},{"t":"p","text":"Six tiny wonders, each smaller than the tip of a pin, and each one unique."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Tardigrade","Can survive years without water"],["Bdelloid rotifer","Shrinks into a speck of dust"],["Amoeba","Changes shape constantly"],["Paramecium","Rows through water with cilia"],["Diatom","Builds its shell out of silica"],["Dust mite","Smaller than a period"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is the longest a dried-out rotifer has been brought back to life from?","options":["1 year","9 years","3 months"],"answer":1,"explain":"Rotifers have an amazing ability to come back to life after being dried out for a very long time."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does the paramecium use to row itself through water?","options":["Flagella","Cilia","Pseudopods"],"answer":1,"explain":"The tiny hairs on the paramecium help it move through the water."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is a diatom's glass shell called?","options":["Carapace","Capsule","Frustule"],"answer":2,"explain":"Each diatom has a unique glass house with special patterns."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many legs does a dust mite have?","options":["4","8","6"],"answer":1,"explain":"Dust mites are like tiny spiders with eight legs."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does an amoeba use to move?","options":["Flagellum","Pseudopod","Cilia"],"answer":1,"explain":"The amoeba moves by pushing out a bulge called a pseudopod."}]},{"id":"animals-dinosaurs-1","course":"animals","kind":"species","group":"dinosaurs","title":"Tyrannosaurus rex & more","names":["Tyrannosaurus rex","Triceratops","Stegosaurus","Brachiosaurus","Apatosaurus"],"glyph":"🦕","tint":"#ffa04a","items":["tyrannosaurus","triceratops","stegosaurus","brachiosaurus","apatosaurus"],"idea":"A T. rex tooth could be a foot long, root and all. See how each dinosaur's body was built for its own world.","pages":[{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Five dinosaurs are waiting to meet you: a Tyrannosaurus rex, a Triceratops, a Stegosaurus, a Brachiosaurus, and an Apatosaurus."},{"t":"p","text":"Each one was built for a different world long ago."}]},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Start with the biggest meat-eating dinosaur from North America. Tyrannosaurus rex was about 40 ft (12 m) nose to tail, and 12 ft (3.7 m) tall at the hips."},{"t":"fact","text":"Scotty, a skeleton found in Canada, may be the biggest T. rex ever found, weighing about 19,500 lb (8,870 kg) and measuring around 43 ft (13 m) long.","label":"The heavy one"},{"t":"p","text":"T. rex teeth were thick pegs for crunching bone, not slicing flesh. The largest tooth ever found is 12 in (30.5 cm) long, with only about 4 in showing above the gum."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How long is the largest T. rex tooth ever found?","options":["6 inches","12 inches","18 inches"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the last sentence of the paragraph before the check.","explain":"The largest T. rex tooth ever found is 12 in (30.5 cm) long."}}],"img":"tyrannosaurus"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Next, meet Triceratops, a three-horned plant-eater with a solid bone frill."},{"t":"fact","text":"About 28 ft (8.5 m) long, nearly a third of its body is its huge skull.","label":"The big head"},{"t":"term","word":"rostral","text":"A special bone that only horned dinosaurs have. It makes their beak."},{"t":"fact","text":"One Triceratops skull shows tooth marks from a tyrannosaur attack. The bone grew over the wounds, so this one survived and lived on for years.","label":"Survivor"}],"img":"triceratops"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Next up, Stegosaurus, a plant-eater with spiked plates on its back and a dog-sized brain."},{"t":"p","text":"Those plates were once thought to be radiators for shedding heat, but now they are seen as a way to look bigger to rivals or enemies."},{"t":"fact","text":"The four tail spikes are 2 to 3 ft (60–90 cm) long and stick out sideways. Around one in ten spike tips found are broken and then healed, showing they were actually used.","label":"The spikes"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is the best read today of what the plates were for?","options":["Radiators for shedding heat","Digging up roots to eat","Looking bigger to rivals or enemies"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the second paragraph. It tells you what the main job is.","explain":"The best read is that the plates were for show: seen side-on, they make the animal look taller and broader to a rival or an enemy."}}],"img":"stegosaurus"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet Brachiosaurus, a long-necked plant-eater built like a giraffe with front legs longer than its back legs."},{"t":"p","text":"Its upper arm bone is 6 ft 8 in (204 cm) long. That’s the bone from shoulder to elbow — imagine how tall the whole leg was!"},{"t":"fact","text":"The shoulders were much higher than the hips, so its back sloped downhill from front to rear like a giraffe's. Nearly every other big long-neck had high hips and low shoulders.","label":"The tilted back"}],"img":"brachiosaurus"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet Apatosaurus, a heavy long-necked plant-eater that most people still call Brontosaurus. It was as long as 72 feet (22 m) and as heavy as three or four elephants."},{"t":"fact","text":"It has fifteen neck bones, and none of them are solid — they’re hollowed out just like a bird’s bones.","label":"The hollow neck"}],"img":"apatosaurus"},{"icon":"📊","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Now put the five side by side. The heaviest is Brachiosaurus at 70,000 lb. The lightest is Stegosaurus at 7,700 lb."},{"t":"fact","text":"Brachiosaurus is 70 ft long and Apatosaurus is 72 ft long. Both are giants, but T. rex is only 40 ft long.","label":"Size"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which of the five is the heaviest?","options":["The Brachiosaurus","The Apatosaurus","The Stegosaurus"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the first paragraph and find the biggest number.","explain":"The Brachiosaurus, at 70,000 lb."}}]},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A T. rex that hunts, a Triceratops with horns, a Stegosaurus with plates, a Brachiosaurus reaching high, an Apatosaurus long and heavy."},{"t":"p","text":"Five giants, each one built for its own world."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Tyrannosaurus rex","A tooth a foot long, root and all"],["Triceratops","Three-horned with a solid bone frill"],["Stegosaurus","Has spiked plates and a dog-sized brain"],["Brachiosaurus","Built like a giraffe with long front legs"],["Apatosaurus","Called Brontosaurus, as heavy as three elephants"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"How long was Brachiosaurus's upper arm bone?","options":["4 ft 6 in (137 cm)","6 ft 8 in (204 cm)","5 ft 2 in (157 cm)"],"answer":1,"explain":"That’s a huge bone, taller than most grown-ups!"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did scientists once think Stegosaurus's plates were used for?","options":["Looking bigger to rivals","Shedding heat","Digging up roots to eat"],"answer":1,"explain":"They thought the plates helped cool off the big dinosaur."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How long is T. rex nose to tail?","options":["30 ft (9 m)","40 ft (12 m)","50 ft (15 m)"],"answer":1,"explain":"T. rex was huge, but not as big as some other dinosaurs."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What special bone do horned dinosaurs have?","options":["A jawbone","A rostral bone","A rib bone"],"answer":1,"explain":"The rostral bone makes their beak, perfect for munching plants."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"About how much does Scotty, the biggest T. rex found, weigh?","options":["12,000 lb (5,443 kg)","19,500 lb (8,870 kg)","25,000 lb (11,340 kg)"],"answer":1,"explain":"Scotty may be the biggest T. rex found, at about 19,500 lb (8,870 kg)."}]},{"id":"animals-dinosaurs-2","course":"animals","kind":"species","group":"dinosaurs","title":"Diplodocus, Velociraptor & more","names":["Diplodocus","Velociraptor","Spinosaurus","Allosaurus","Ankylosaurus"],"glyph":"🦕","tint":"#ffa04a","items":["diplodocus","velociraptor","spinosaurus","allosaurus","ankylosaurus"],"idea":"Ankylosaurus swung a heavy club of bone on its tail. Find out which dinosaur had a tail fin for swimming, which was found mid-fight, and which was covered in healed injuries.","pages":[{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Five dinosaurs are here to meet you: a Diplodocus, a Velociraptor, a Spinosaurus, an Allosaurus, and an Ankylosaurus."},{"t":"p","text":"Each one was built for a different job in their world."}]},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"First up, meet the Diplodocus. It's one of the longest animals ever to walk on land, stretching about 85 ft (26 m) nose to tail."},{"t":"fact","text":"Skin impressions from a close cousin show that Diplodocus likely had narrow, pointed spines along the whip end of its tail. The biggest spine was maybe 7 in (18 cm) long.","label":"The surprising tail"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did skin impressions reveal about Diplodocus's tail?","options":["It had thick, bony armor all along the whip end","It had wide, flat plates along its whole length","It had narrow, pointed spines along the whip end"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It mentions what the skin impressions showed.","explain":"Skin impressions from a close cousin showed narrow, pointed spines along the whip end of the tail."}}],"img":"diplodocus"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Next up, meet the Velociraptor. It's a turkey-sized hunter from the sand dunes of Mongolia, with one oversized sickle claw on each foot."},{"t":"term","word":"fossil","text":"The hardened remains of a plant or animal, or its footprint, kept in rock."},{"t":"fact","text":"The Fighting Dinosaurs is a fossil found in 1971. It shows a Velociraptor and a Protoceratops buried mid-fight. Sand poured over both animals fast, preserving the moment.","label":"The Fighting Dinosaurs"},{"t":"fact","text":"The second toe of each foot had a curved claw over 2.6 in (6.5 cm) around its outer edge. It was probably used to pin prey to the ground, not slash.","label":"The claw"}],"img":"velociraptor"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the longest meat-eating dinosaur anyone has found: Spinosaurus. It had a crocodile-like snout and a tall sail of bone down its back."},{"t":"fact","text":"Its tail bones form a flat fin, much like a newt's. A model of this tail was tested in a water tank and produced over eight times the push of an ordinary meat-eater's tail.","label":"The surprising tail"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What feature of Spinosaurus surprised researchers when they tested it in a water tank?","options":["Its tall sail","Its tail fin","Its crocodile-like snout"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the last sentence. It talks about what was tested.","explain":"The tail fin. When tested, it produced over eight times the push of an ordinary meat-eater's tail."}}],"img":"spinosaurus"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Next up is Allosaurus, the most common big meat-eater found in the western United States. It was a two-legged hunter with saw-edged teeth and blunt horns above its eyes."},{"t":"fact","text":"Big Al, found in Wyoming, is about 95% complete and has 19 broken or infected bones, including ribs, backbones, foot bones, and more. One of its toes was swollen from an infection that lasted months.","label":"The injured hunter"}],"img":"allosaurus"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet Ankylosaurus, a low, wide plant eater covered in armor from head to tail. The most surprising part is its tail club, which is like a big bone knob under a hard shell."},{"t":"fact","text":"The first Ankylosaurus tail club was found in 1910 and measured 24 inches (60 cm) long. 19 inches (49 cm) across. 7.5 inches (19 cm) thick. It could deliver a powerful blow.","label":"The tail club"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is the length of the Ankylosaurus tail club?","options":["19 inches","7.5 inches","24 inches"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the first sentence in the fact box.","explain":"The tail club is 24 inches long."}}],"img":"ankylosaurus"},{"icon":"📊","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The heaviest is Diplodocus, weighing in at 28,000 lb, while the lightest is Velociraptor, only 40 lb."},{"t":"fact","text":"Diplodocus stretches to 85 ft long. Spinosaurus is next at 46 ft, then Allosaurus at 28 ft, Ankylosaurus at 26 ft, and Velociraptor at 6.8 ft.","label":"Length"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which of the five is the longest?","options":["Velociraptor","Spinosaurus","Allosaurus","Diplodocus"],"answer":3,"hint":"Look at the fact box for the lengths.","explain":"Diplodocus is the longest at 85 ft."}}]},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Diplodocus stretching 85 ft long. Velociraptor weighing just 40 lb. Spinosaurus with a sail on its back. Allosaurus hunting with sharp teeth. Ankylosaurus armored like a tank."},{"t":"p","text":"Five dinosaurs, five different shapes, and each one built for its own world."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Diplodocus","One of the longest animals to walk on land"],["Velociraptor","Turkey-sized hunter with an oversized sickle claw"],["Spinosaurus","Had a crocodile-like snout and a tall sail of bone"],["Allosaurus","Two-legged hunter with saw-edged teeth"],["Ankylosaurus","Covered in armor, with a powerful tail club"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"How long was Diplodocus from nose to tail?","options":["50 ft (15 m)","85 ft (26 m)","100 ft (30 m)"],"answer":1,"explain":"Diplodocus stretched about 85 ft (26 m) nose to tail, one of the longest dinosaurs."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did the Fighting Dinosaurs fossil show?","options":["A Velociraptor and a Stegosaurus","A Velociraptor and a Protoceratops","A Velociraptor and a Triceratops"],"answer":1,"explain":"The fossil captured a moment in time when these two dinosaurs were fighting."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What feature did researchers test in Spinosaurus?","options":["Its tall sail","Its tail fin","Its snout"],"answer":1,"explain":"A model of the tail fin gave over eight times the push of an ordinary meat-eater's tail in a water tank."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many broken or infected bones did Big Al have?","options":["10","19","5"],"answer":1,"explain":"Big Al had 19 broken or infected bones, including ribs, backbones and foot bones."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is the thickness of Ankylosaurus's tail club?","options":["5 inches (13 cm)","7.5 inches (19 cm)","10 inches (25 cm)"],"answer":1,"explain":"The tail club was thick enough to deliver a powerful blow."}]},{"id":"animals-dinosaurs-3","course":"animals","kind":"species","group":"dinosaurs","title":"Parasaurolophus & more","names":["Parasaurolophus","Pachycephalosaurus","Iguanodon","Argentinosaurus","Giganotosaurus"],"glyph":"🦕","tint":"#ffa04a","items":["parasaurolophus","pachycephalosaurus","iguanodon","argentinosaurus","giganotosaurus"],"idea":"A Parasaurolophus crest was a hollow tube of bone. Find out whose skull roof was thicker than a brick is long, and whose thumb spike was once stuck on its nose.","pages":[{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Five dinosaurs are waiting to meet you: a Parasaurolophus, a Pachycephalosaurus, an Iguanodon, an Argentinosaurus, and a Giganotosaurus."},{"t":"p","text":"Each one is unique and full of surprises."}]},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the Parasaurolophus, a plant-eating dinosaur with a long hollow tube of bone curving off its skull."},{"t":"fact","text":"The crest is hollow, with tubes running from each nostril all the way up to the tip and back down into the skull. Air moving through those tubes could have made a deep, trumpet-like sound.","label":"The wind instrument"}],"img":"parasaurolophus"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Next is the Pachycephalosaurus, a two-legged plant-eater with a skull roof that is a thick lump of solid bone."},{"t":"fact","text":"The dome on top of its head is solid bone up to 8.7 inches (22 cm) thick — thicker than a brick is long.","label":"The thick head"},{"t":"p","text":"Almost everything known about this dinosaur is its head. Complete skeletons are rare, likely because the thick dome survived better in rivers than thin body bones did."}],"img":"pachycephalosaurus"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Next is the Iguanodon, one of the very first dinosaurs ever named. It is a big plant-eater with a spike on each thumb."},{"t":"fact","text":"Gideon Mantell found a cone-shaped spike bone but had no idea where it belonged. So he stuck it on the nose like a rhino's horn. It is actually a thumb.","label":"The spike mistake"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did Gideon Mantell mistakenly place on the Iguanodon's nose?","options":["A tooth","A rib","The thumb spike"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the last sentence of the fact block.","explain":"Gideon Mantell mistakenly placed the thumb spike on the Iguanodon's nose, thinking it was a horn."}}],"img":"iguanodon"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Next is the Argentinosaurus, a truly enormous long-necked plant-eater from Argentina. It is known from only a handful of gigantic bones."},{"t":"fact","text":"What was actually dug up includes seven back bones (vertebrae), part of a hip block with some ribs, and a lower leg bone. No skull or neck bones were found.","label":"The few bones"},{"t":"p","text":"One of the back vertebrae is about 5 ft 3 in (159 cm) tall and 4 ft 3 in (129 cm) wide. The lower leg bone is 5 ft 1 in (155 cm) long — taller than you are!"},{"t":"term","word":"vertebra","text":"One of the small bones that line up to make a backbone."}],"img":"argentinosaurus"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Next up, the Giganotosaurus, a huge meat-eating dinosaur from Argentina. It was found by Rubén Carolini while he was out driving a dune buggy."},{"t":"p","text":"The first skeleton had roughly 70 out of every 100 bones. That is a lot for a big predator."},{"t":"fact","text":"A second Giganotosaurus is known from a lower jaw bone that is about 8 percent bigger than the same bone in the first skeleton.","label":"The bigger jaw"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How much bigger was the jaw bone of the second Giganotosaurus compared to the first one?","options":["10%","8%","5%"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the last sentence. It gives the exact percentage.","explain":"The jaw bone of the second Giganotosaurus was about 8% bigger."}}],"img":"giganotosaurus"},{"icon":"📊","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Put them side by side. The Argentinosaurus weighed an estimated 165,000 lb, the Giganotosaurus about 15,000 lb and the Iguanodon about 7,000 lb. The Pachycephalosaurus weighs only about 900 lb."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which of the five is the heaviest?","options":["The Argentinosaurus","The Iguanodon","The Giganotosaurus"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the first paragraph and find the biggest number.","explain":"The Argentinosaurus, at about 165,000 lb."}}]},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A long crest on the head of a Parasaurolophus. A dome on top of a Pachycephalosaurus. Iguanodon with thumb spikes. Argentinosaurus, known from only a few giant bones. Giganotosaurus, found by a man in a dune buggy."},{"t":"p","text":"Five dinosaurs, each built for its own world, and all so different from anything alive today."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Parasaurolophus","Has a hollow bone tube for a crest"],["Pachycephalosaurus","Dome is 8.7 inches thick"],["Iguanodon","Thumb spike mistaken for a nose horn"],["Argentinosaurus","Known from only a few gigantic bones"],["Giganotosaurus","Found by Rubén Carolini while driving"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What was actually dug up from the Argentinosaurus?","options":["A full skull, a long neck, and several sharp teeth","Seven back bones, a hip block with ribs, and a leg bone","A long tail, two front legs, and a set of claws"],"answer":1,"explain":"The bones found tell us a lot about this giant dinosaur."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is special about the Parasaurolophus's crest?","options":["It is a solid block of bone","It is hollow, with tubes inside","It is covered in sharp spikes"],"answer":1,"explain":"The crest of the Parasaurolophus was like a wind instrument."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is unusual about the Pachycephalosaurus's head?","options":["A hollow crest of bone about 2 feet tall","A solid dome of bone 8.7 inches thick","A pair of sharp horns, each 20 inches long"],"answer":1,"explain":"The Pachycephalosaurus had an incredibly thick skull."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How was the first Giganotosaurus found?","options":["By a team digging inside a museum","By a man driving a dune buggy","By a girl walking on a beach"],"answer":1,"explain":"Rubén Carolini spotted it while he was out driving a dune buggy."}]},{"id":"animals-dinosaurs-4","course":"animals","kind":"species","group":"dinosaurs","title":"Carnotaurus & more","names":["Carnotaurus","Therizinosaurus","Deinonychus","Compsognathus","Protoceratops"],"glyph":"🦕","tint":"#ffa04a","items":["carnotaurus","therizinosaurus","deinonychus","compsognathus","protoceratops"],"idea":"A Carnotaurus skeleton came out of the rock with its skin print. Find out whose hand claws were as long as a nine-year-old's arm and which dinosaur was found locked in a fight.","pages":[{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Five dinosaurs are waiting to meet you: a Carnotaurus, a Therizinosaurus, a Deinonychus, a Compsognathus, and a Protoceratops."},{"t":"p","text":"Each one has something surprising about it."}]},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the Carnotaurus, a meat-eating bull from Patagonia. It had two thick horns above its eyes and a skeleton that came out of the ground with its skin still printed in the rock."},{"t":"fact","text":"The skin was covered with rows of big cone-shaped studs and small pebbly scales. This pattern is the most complete ever found on a meat-eating dinosaur.","label":"The skin"},{"t":"p","text":"Its arms were almost silly, with the forearm only a quarter the length of the upper arm and just four stubby fingers."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What was printed in the rock beside the Carnotaurus skeleton?","options":["Its eggs","Its skin pattern","Its last meal"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the first paragraph. It says what was printed in the rock.","explain":"The skeleton came out of the ground with its skin still printed in the rock."}}],"img":"carnotaurus"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Next up is the Therizinosaurus, a huge, pot-bellied, beaked plant-eater with the longest hand claws of any land animal ever found."},{"t":"fact","text":"The largest known claw bone measures about 20 in (52 cm) around its curve — about as long as a nine-year-old's whole arm.","label":"The claws"},{"t":"term","word":"cheloniformis","text":"turtle-shaped. When the first Therizinosaurus claws were described, they were thought to belong to a giant turtle."},{"t":"p","text":"The claws did not belong to a hunter. This dinosaur had a horny beak and a wide barrel of a gut for fermenting greenery. The best idea is that those claws were hooks for pulling branches down to the beak."}],"img":"therizinosaurus"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet Deinonychus, an 11-ft hunter with a switchblade claw on each second toe. Its name means \"terrible claw.\" The movie 'velociraptors' were really built from this animal."},{"t":"fact","text":"The sickle claw on its second toe is over 4.7 in (12 cm) measured round the outer curve. This claw rode up off the ground, out of the dirt, staying sharp.","label":"The claw"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does the name Deinonychus mean?","options":["Swift runner","Sharp teeth","Terrible claw"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the first paragraph. It says what the name means.","explain":"The name Deinonychus means \"terrible claw\"."}}],"img":"deinonychus"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet Compsognathus, a small meat-eater known from just two skeletons — one chicken-sized and one bigger."},{"t":"fact","text":"Inside the German skeleton's ribs is a lizard, in several pieces. It shows how Compsognathus tore its food apart and swallowed chunks of it.","label":"The meal inside"},{"t":"p","text":"The German skeleton is about 2 ft 6 in (75 cm) long and weighed around a pound. The French one is bigger, roughly 4 ft (1.25 m) and 6 lb."}],"img":"compsognathus"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet Protoceratops, a sheep-sized plant-eater known for its bony frill."},{"t":"fact","text":"Protoceratops is famous for being found locked in battle with a Velociraptor. They are called the \"Fighting Dinosaurs.\"","label":"The fighting fossil"},{"t":"p","text":"Protoceratops fossils are not rare. Many have been found, from hatchlings to adults."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"In the \"Fighting Dinosaurs\" fossil, what was found buried with the Protoceratops?","options":["A Velociraptor","Another Protoceratops","An Oviraptor"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the fact box about the \"Fighting Dinosaurs.\"","explain":"The \"Fighting Dinosaurs\" are a Protoceratops and a Velociraptor buried together in the middle of a fight."}}],"img":"protoceratops"},{"icon":"📊","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Let’s put these dinosaurs side by side. Therizinosaurus weighs about 11,000 lb, Carnotaurus about 3,300 lb and Protoceratops about 180 lb. Compsognathus weighs just 6 lb."},{"t":"fact","text":"Carnotaurus is 26 ft long, while Therizinosaurus is longer at 32 ft.","label":"Length"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which of the five is the heaviest?","options":["Carnotaurus","Therizinosaurus","Protoceratops"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the first paragraph and find the biggest number.","explain":"Therizinosaurus weighs about 11,000 lb."}}]},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Carnotaurus with its horns, Deinonychus with its claws, Therizinosaurus with the longest hand claws, Compsognathus so small, Protoceratops with a frill."},{"t":"p","text":"Five dinosaurs, each different, and all from long ago."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Carnotaurus","Found with its skin and horns"],["Therizinosaurus","Has the longest hand claws ever"],["Deinonychus","Known for its sickle claw"],["Compsognathus","Known from just two skeletons"],["Protoceratops","Found locked in battle with a Velociraptor"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What unusual feature did scientists find inside the German Compsognathus skeleton?","options":["A stone","A lizard","A bone"],"answer":1,"explain":"The German Compsognathus skeleton had a lizard inside, showing how it ate."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How long is the sickle claw on Deinonychus's second toe, measured round the outer curve?","options":["3 in (7.5 cm)","4.7 in (12 cm)","6 in (15 cm)"],"answer":1,"explain":"The sickle claw was over 4.7 inches long."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is Protoceratops famous for being found with?","options":["Another Protoceratops","A Velociraptor","An Oviraptor"],"answer":1,"explain":"Protoceratops and a Velociraptor were found locked in battle."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What was the skin of the Carnotaurus covered with?","options":["Smooth plates like a turtle's shell","Cone-shaped studs and pebbly scales","Slick, smooth skin like a frog's"],"answer":1,"explain":"The skin had rows of cone-shaped studs and small pebbly scales."}]},{"id":"animals-dinosaurs-5","course":"animals","kind":"species","group":"dinosaurs","title":"Oviraptor, Gallimimus & more","names":["Oviraptor","Gallimimus","Dilophosaurus","Styracosaurus","Edmontosaurus"],"glyph":"🦕","tint":"#ffa04a","items":["oviraptor","gallimimus","dilophosaurus","styracosaurus","edmontosaurus"],"idea":"An Oviraptor skeleton was found lying on a nest of eggs. Find out whose skeleton kept its skin, whose crests were too thin to fight with, and which dinosaur had more than 1,000 teeth.","pages":[{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Five dinosaurs are waiting to meet you: an Oviraptor, a Gallimimus, a Dilophosaurus, a Styracosaurus, and an Edmontosaurus."},{"t":"p","text":"Each one was built for a different job."}]},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the Oviraptor, a parrot-beaked dinosaur about 6 ft (2 m) long. It weighed around 80 lb (36 kg), far lighter than an ostrich."},{"t":"p","text":"The only known Oviraptor skeleton was found lying on a nest of about fifteen eggs, each egg roughly 5.5 in (14 cm) long."},{"t":"fact","text":"Its name means \"egg thief,\" because people once thought it died raiding another dinosaur's nest. Then, in 1993, an expedition found an egg of the same kind in the same Mongolian desert with an unhatched baby, an embryo, still inside. The eggs were its own, and it was probably sitting on them.","label":"The true identity"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What was found inside the egg the 1993 expedition dug up?","options":["A lizard","An embryo","A smaller egg"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the last fact box. It mentions what was found inside an egg.","explain":"An embryo. The egg held an unhatched baby, which showed the eggs were the Oviraptor's own."}}],"img":"oviraptor"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Next up is the Gallimimus, a long-legged runner built like an ostrich with a tail, and one of the largest ostrich-mimic dinosaurs."},{"t":"p","text":"Gallimimus means \"chicken mimic,\" but it was chosen because the neck bones reminded scientists of a chicken's neck, not its size."},{"t":"fact","text":"One skull kept part of its horny beak with fine vertical ridges inside, like the comb of plates in a duck's bill.","label":"The preserved beak"}],"img":"gallimimus"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Next up, Dilophosaurus — a meat-eater that reached 20 feet long and had two thin bony crests along the top of its skull."},{"t":"fact","text":"The thin plates stood up along the snout, arched like a pair of sails. They were too fragile to use in a fight.","label":"The thin plates"},{"t":"p","text":"There is a notch in the upper jaw just behind the front teeth, called the subnarial gap. It makes this skull easy to recognize at a glance."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is the notch behind the front teeth of a Dilophosaurus called?","options":["The crest gap","The nasal frill","The subnarial gap"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the last sentence on the page.","explain":"The subnarial gap is the notch behind the front teeth."}}],"img":"dilophosaurus"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the Styracosaurus — a rhino-sized horned dinosaur with one long nose horn and a bony neck frill rimmed with spikes."},{"t":"fact","text":"The frill is not solid bone; it has two large windows that make it lighter. The spikes point up and back, away from anything in front of the animal.","label":"The spiked collar"},{"t":"p","text":"The best read is that the spikes and frill were for show, not for fighting, since a frill with two large windows is too thin to take a blow."}],"img":"styracosaurus"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Next up, the Edmontosaurus — a big duck-billed plant-eater. It is one of the very few dinosaurs known from fossils that kept their skin."},{"t":"fact","text":"In 1908, Charles H. Sternberg and his sons found a fossil near Lusk, Wyoming, that still had its skin. It’s now at the American Museum of Natural History in New York.","label":"The mummy"},{"t":"p","text":"One of the most surprising facts is about its teeth. The jaws hold roughly a hundred columns of teeth on each side of the head. Each column has up to six teeth stacked, making more than 1,000 teeth total."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many teeth does an Edmontosaurus have in total?","options":["About 500","More than 1,000","Less than 300"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the last sentence of the second paragraph.","explain":"The jaws hold roughly a hundred columns of teeth on each side of the head, with up to six teeth stacked in every column, making more than 1,000 teeth total."}}],"img":"edmontosaurus"},{"icon":"📊","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Now put the five side by side. The heaviest is the Edmontosaurus at about 12,000 lb. The lightest is the Oviraptor at about 80 lb."},{"t":"fact","text":"Gallimimus and Dilophosaurus both have a similar length of 20 ft, but Gallimimus weighs 1,000 lb while Dilophosaurus weighs 620 lb.","label":"Length and weight"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which of the five is the heaviest?","options":["The Edmontosaurus","The Styracosaurus","The Gallimimus"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the first paragraph and find the biggest number.","explain":"The Edmontosaurus, at about 12,000 lb."}}]},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"An Oviraptor that weighs 80 lb. A Gallimimus with a horny beak and fine ridges inside it. A Dilophosaurus, 20 ft long and 620 lb. A Styracosaurus with its spikes. An Edmontosaurus, the heaviest of all."},{"t":"term","word":"specimen","text":"One real example of an animal or plant that is kept so people can study it."},{"t":"p","text":"Five dinosaurs, each built for their own world."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Oviraptor","Found lying on a nest of eggs"],["Gallimimus","Long-legged runner built like an ostrich"],["Dilophosaurus","Has two thin bony crests along its skull"],["Styracosaurus","Has a frill with spikes for decoration"],["Edmontosaurus","Has more than 1,000 teeth total"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did one Gallimimus skull keep that most skulls lose?","options":["A full row of tiny, sharp teeth in the jaw","A horny beak with fine vertical ridges","A small crest of soft skin on the head"],"answer":1,"explain":"The skull kept part of its horny beak, much like a duck's bill."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why is Gallimimus called the \"chicken mimic\"?","options":["It was only about as big as a chicken","Its neck bones looked like a chicken's neck","It made clucking sounds just like a chicken"],"answer":1,"explain":"The name was chosen because the neck bones reminded researchers of a chicken's neck, not because of its size."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is the notch in the upper jaw of a Dilophosaurus called?","options":["A crest notch","A subnarial gap","A nasal frill"],"answer":1,"explain":"The notch behind the front teeth makes this skull easy to recognize at a glance."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What was special about the Edmontosaurus specimen found in 1908?","options":["It had a complete set of spikes","It still had its skin","It weighed more than 12,000 lb"],"answer":1,"explain":"This specimen, found by Charles H. Sternberg and his sons, is known for keeping its skin."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is the best read on the Styracosaurus frill and spikes?","options":["For fighting off predators","Decoration to impress others","To help it run faster"],"answer":1,"explain":"The best read is that the spikes and frill were for show, not for fighting."}]},{"id":"animals-dinosaurs-6","course":"animals","kind":"species","group":"dinosaurs","title":"Pteranodon, Quetzalcoatlus & more","names":["Pteranodon","Quetzalcoatlus","Plesiosaurus","Mosasaurus","Ichthyosaurus"],"glyph":"🦕","tint":"#ffa04a","items":["pteranodon","quetzalcoatlus","plesiosaurus","mosasaurus","ichthyosaurus"],"idea":"Five giants of the air and sea, and not one was a dinosaur. One had a neck with about 40 bones, and one stood as tall as a basketball hoop.","pages":[{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Five giants of the air and the sea are waiting: the Pteranodon, the Quetzalcoatlus, the Plesiosaurus, the Mosasaurus and the Ichthyosaurus."},{"t":"p","text":"Not one of them was a dinosaur. Which one would you most like to meet?"}]},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the Pteranodon. This large flying reptile had a toothless beak and a long, backswept crest. It was not a dinosaur, and not a bird."},{"t":"fact","text":"Its wings were about 20 ft (6 m) from tip to tip. The body was only about 6 ft (1.8 m) from beak to tail.","label":"The wings"},{"t":"p","text":"The name means wing without tooth. The beak was completely empty: two long, pointed jaws with nothing in them."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does the name Pteranodon mean?","options":["Giant bird of the chalk","Lizard with long legs","Wing without tooth"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. It explains the meaning of the name.","explain":"Pteranodon means wing without tooth, because its long beak had no teeth at all."}}],"img":"pteranodon"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the Quetzalcoatlus. It is the largest flying animal ever found. It was a toothless flying reptile that stalked river shallows on all fours."},{"t":"fact","text":"Its wings spanned about 33 to 36 ft (10 to 11 m), the wingspan of a small airplane. Standing on the ground, it held its head more than 13 ft (4 m) up.","label":"The giant wings"},{"t":"p","text":"To get into the air, it jumped. Researchers worked out that it leaped at least 8 ft (2.4 m) straight up, then swept its wings down."},{"t":"term","word":"wingspan","text":"How far a pair of wings reaches, from one tip to the other."}],"img":"quetzalcoatlus"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the Plesiosaurus. This swimming reptile had a long neck, a short tail and four big flippers. It was not a dinosaur."},{"t":"fact","text":"The neck alone held about 40 separate bones. A giraffe manages its whole neck with seven.","label":"The neck"},{"t":"p","text":"It was about 10 ft (3 m) long from nose to tail, and roughly a third of that was neck."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"About how many bones were in its neck?","options":["Over 400 bones","Just 7 bones, like a giraffe","Around 40 bones"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the box. It counts the bones in the neck.","explain":"The neck alone held about 40 separate bones, far more than a giraffe's seven."}}],"img":"plesiosaurus"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the Mosasaurus. This enormous sea-going lizard had paddles instead of feet and a shark-like tail."},{"t":"fact","text":"About 40 ft (12 m) long, worked out from the skull. The skull alone measures over 4 ft 9 in (145 cm).","label":"The size"},{"t":"p","text":"The name means lizard of the Meuse, after a river. The jaws carry 14 to 16 teeth along each upper edge."}],"img":"mosasaurus"},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the Ichthyosaurus. This dolphin-shaped reptile swam off the Dorset coast. It was shaped like a fish, but it breathed air."},{"t":"fact","text":"About 6 to 7 ft (2 m) long. The largest one ever found reached nearly 11 ft (3.3 m).","label":"The size"},{"t":"p","text":"It had the same shape as a dolphin: a pointed snout, a back fin and a crescent tail. Its huge eye held a ring of bony plates."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which animal alive today has the same shape as an Ichthyosaurus?","options":["A crocodile","A dolphin","A giant turtle"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. It says which animal has the same shape.","explain":"Ichthyosaurus had the same shape as a dolphin, with a pointed snout, a back fin and a crescent tail."}}],"img":"ichthyosaurus"},{"icon":"📊","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Five reptiles, and not one was a dinosaur. Two flew on wings of skin. Three swam, and all three came up to breathe air."},{"t":"fact","text":"Pteranodon wings reached about 20 ft (6 m). Quetzalcoatlus spread 33 to 36 ft (10 to 11 m). Plesiosaurus was about 10 ft (3 m) long, Mosasaurus about 40 ft (12 m), and Ichthyosaurus 6 to 7 ft (2 m).","label":"Sizes"}]},{"icon":"🐾","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A Pteranodon with a toothless beak. A Quetzalcoatlus jumping into the air. A Plesiosaurus with a long neck. A Mosasaurus with paddles. An Ichthyosaurus shaped like a dolphin."},{"t":"p","text":"Five giants of the air and the sea, and not one was a dinosaur."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Pteranodon","Flying reptile with a toothless beak"],["Quetzalcoatlus","Largest flying animal ever found"],["Plesiosaurus","Long neck, short tail, four flippers"],["Mosasaurus","Sea lizard with paddles and a shark-like tail"],["Ichthyosaurus","Dolphin-shaped swimming reptile"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What was special about the Pteranodon's beak?","options":["It was full of sharp teeth","It could crack open shells","It had no teeth at all"],"answer":2,"explain":"The name means wing without tooth, and the long beak was completely empty."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How did the Quetzalcoatlus get into the air?","options":["It jumped first, then swept its wings down","It ran along the ground, then took off","It dropped from a tall tree and glided"],"answer":0,"explain":"Researchers worked out that it leaped at least 8 ft (2.4 m) straight up, then swept its wings down."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Who dug up the first complete Plesiosaurus?","options":["A farmer in Kansas","Georges Cuvier","Mary Anning"],"answer":2,"explain":"Mary Anning dug the first complete Plesiosaurus out of the cliffs at Lyme Regis in 1823."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does the name Mosasaurus mean?","options":["Lizard of the sea","Lizard of the Meuse","Giant sea snake"],"answer":1,"explain":"It is named after the Meuse, the river at Maastricht, where the first skull was found."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How did Ichthyosaurus babies come into the world?","options":["They hatched from eggs on a beach","They dropped down from the air","They were born alive in the water"],"answer":2,"explain":"Ichthyosaurs gave birth in the water, and they bore live young instead of laying eggs."}]},{"id":"plants-trees-1","course":"plants","kind":"species","group":"trees","title":"Giant sequoia & more","names":["Giant sequoia","Coast redwood","Bristlecone pine","Baobab","English oak"],"glyph":"🌳","tint":"#7fe3a0","items":["giant-sequoia","coast-redwood","bristlecone-pine","baobab","oak"],"idea":"A giant sequoia can weigh millions of pounds. Five trees show how heavy, how tall, how old, and how much water a tree can hold.","pages":[{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Five trees are waiting to meet you: a giant sequoia, a coast redwood, a bristlecone pine, a baobab, and an English oak."},{"t":"p","text":"Each one lives in its own way."}]},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The giant sequoia is the bulkiest tree on Earth. General Sherman, in Sequoia National Park, stands 275 ft (83.8 m) tall and is 102.6 ft around at the ground."},{"t":"fact","text":"Its trunk alone holds 52,508 cubic feet of wood. A 1938 estimate put its weight at about 4.2 million lb.","label":"The heaviest tree"},{"t":"p","text":"In January 2006, a branch over 6.6 ft (2 m) thick and more than 98 ft (30 m) long tore off the General Sherman tree. It smashed the perimeter fence and the sidewalk below."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What happened to the General Sherman tree in January 2006?","options":["A lightning strike split the trunk and burned off the top.","The tree was cut down and its rings counted by loggers.","A branch over 6.6 ft thick and more than 98 ft long tore off."],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the last paragraph. It describes what happened to the branch.","explain":"A branch over 6.6 ft thick and more than 98 ft long tore off, smashing the perimeter fence and the sidewalk below."}}],"img":"giant-sequoia"},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the coast redwood, the tallest tree on Earth. Hyperion, one of these giants, stands at 381.3 ft (116.22 m). That is taller than the Statue of Liberty, which is 305 ft (93 m) from the ground to the tip of the torch."},{"t":"fact","text":"Chris Atkins and Michael Taylor found Hyperion in Redwood National Park on August 25, 2006. It continues to grow taller every year.","label":"The tallest tree"},{"t":"term","word":"fog drip","text":"When fog condenses on needles high in the tree's crown and drips down, supplying water to the roots."},{"t":"p","text":"Fog drip can provide about 30 percent of the water a coast redwood needs each year. The tree combs water out of the air and waters its own roots with it."}],"img":"coast-redwood"},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the bristlecone pine, a short, twisted tree that grows on bare mountain rock where almost nothing else will."},{"t":"fact","text":"In 1957 Edmund Schulman counted the rings of a bristlecone in California's White Mountains. The count came to 4,789. He named it Methuselah.","label":"The oldest tree"},{"t":"p","text":"Another bristlecone, cut down in 1964, had at least 4,862 rings. It was later named Prometheus."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many rings did the oldest bristlecone pine ever counted have?","options":["3,000","4,862","2,500"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the last two sentences.","explain":"The tree that was cut down had at least 4,862 rings."}}],"img":"bristlecone-pine"},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Next up, the baobab. This African tree is built like a water tank with a trunk you could park a car in and branches that look like roots waving in the air."},{"t":"fact","text":"A large baobab can hold as much as 36,000 gallons (136,400 liters) of water in its trunk. That's about the same as two backyard swimming pools stored inside the wood.","label":"The water tank"},{"t":"p","text":"The trunk shrinks a little during the dry season, by about 1 inch (2–3 cm) around and losing roughly a tenth of its stored water. When the rain returns, it swells back out again."}],"img":"baobab"},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Next is the English oak. It's a big, heavy tree that grows in Europe and supports more living things than any other tree in Britain."},{"t":"fact","text":"A count put 2,300 different species living on or with English oak in the UK — insects, mosses, lichens, birds, mammals, and more.","label":"The living library"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many species live on or with the English oak in the UK?","options":["400","2,300","500"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It gives the exact number.","explain":"The number is 2,300 species living on or with the English oak in the UK."}}],"img":"oak"},{"icon":"📊","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The giant sequoia was estimated in 1938 to weigh about 4,210,000 lb, and it can live for 3,000 years. The coast redwood lives up to 2,000 years. The bristlecone pine lives longest, at least 4,862 years. The baobab can live more than 1,000 years, and the English oak about 500 to 1,000."},{"t":"fact","text":"All of them are long-lived trees. The bristlecone pine lives longest of the five, and the giant sequoia and the coast redwood can reach thousands of years too.","label":"Long lives"}]},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A giant sequoia weighing millions of pounds. A coast redwood living up to 2,000 years. Bristlecone pine rings counting time. Baobab trunks holding water. An English oak standing strong for 500 years."},{"t":"p","text":"Five trees, each in their own way, reaching through time and growth."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Giant sequoia","Weighs millions of pounds"],["Coast redwood","Tallest at 381.3 ft"],["Bristlecone pine","Oldest at 4,862 rings"],["Baobab","Holds about 36,000 gallons of water"],["English oak","Supports 2,300 species"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"How tall is Hyperion, the tallest coast redwood?","options":["354.7 ft (108.11 m)","381.3 ft (116.22 m)","412.6 ft (125.76 m)"],"answer":1,"explain":"That's taller than the Statue of Liberty."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does fog drip provide to a coast redwood?","options":["50% of its water needs","30% of its water needs","20% of its water needs"],"answer":1,"explain":"Fog drip supplies about 30 percent of the water a coast redwood needs each year."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How much water can a large baobab hold in its trunk?","options":["13,200 gallons (50,000 liters)","36,000 gallons (136,400 liters)","6,600 gallons (25,000 liters)"],"answer":1,"explain":"That's about two backyard swimming pools stored inside the wood."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is the English oak known for supporting?","options":["1,000 species","2,300 species","1,500 species"],"answer":1,"explain":"About 2,300 species live on or with the English oak in the UK, more than on any other tree in Britain."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is the longest a bristlecone pine is known to have lived?","options":["3,000 years","4,862 years","2,000 years"],"answer":1,"explain":"One bristlecone cut down in 1964 had at least 4,862 rings."}]},{"id":"plants-trees-2","course":"plants","kind":"species","group":"trees","title":"Banyan, Red mangrove & more","names":["Banyan","Red mangrove","Quaking aspen","Dragon's blood tree","Joshua tree"],"glyph":"🌳","tint":"#7fe3a0","items":["banyan","mangrove","aspen","dragon-blood-tree","joshua-tree"],"idea":"A banyan can spread over three and a half football fields. Meet five trees with odd ways of living, from a forest that is one plant to a seed that sprouts before it falls.","pages":[{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Five trees are waiting to meet you: a banyan, a red mangrove, a quaking aspen, a dragon's blood tree, and a Joshua tree."},{"t":"p","text":"Each one has secrets hidden in its shape or where it lives."}]},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Start with the banyan, a fig that starts high in another tree's branches and grows down to the ground."},{"t":"fact","text":"The biggest banyan's canopy covers 4.67 acres, about three and a half football fields. The Great Banyan near Kolkata stands on 3,772 prop roots.","label":"The spread"},{"t":"term","word":"prop root","text":"A root that grows from a branch or trunk, reaches the ground, and holds the tree up."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many prop roots does the Great Banyan have?","options":["2,000","5,000","3,772"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It gives the number of prop roots.","explain":"The Great Banyan has 3,772 prop roots."}}],"img":"banyan"},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Next is the red mangrove, a tree that stands in salt water on stilts and sends its babies out already grown."},{"t":"fact","text":"The prop roots arch out of the trunk and branches and stab down into the mud, holding the tree above the water on a tangle of stilts. From below at low tide it looks like the tree is standing on its hands.","label":"The stilt roots"},{"t":"term","word":"vivipary","text":"When a seed germinates while still attached to the parent and grows into a finished green seedling before dropping off."},{"t":"fact","text":"A red mangrove seed does not wait to fall before it sprouts, which is called vivipary. It germinates while still attached to the parent. It grows into a finished green seedling 8 to 10 in (20–25 cm) long, a living dart, before it drops off.","label":"The ready-grown seedlings"}],"img":"mangrove"},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the quaking aspen, a tree with white bark and leaves that shake in the faintest breeze. Often, what looks like a forest of aspens is actually one single plant."},{"t":"fact","text":"Pando, in Utah's Fishlake National Forest, is about 47,000 aspen stems spread over 106 acres (42.89 hectares). Every stem shares one root system and one set of DNA. Together they weigh roughly 13.2 million lb (6,000 metric tons), the heaviest known single organism.","label":"The one plant"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many aspen stems make up Pando?","options":["1,000","47,000","470"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the first sentence of the fact box.","explain":"Pando consists of about 47,000 aspen stems."}}],"img":"aspen"},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the Socotra dragon's blood tree, a tree shaped like an open umbrella that oozes dark red when cut. It grows wild only on one island group in the Arabian Sea."},{"t":"fact","text":"The crown is up to 39 ft (12 m) across — wider than it is tall. Each branch splits into two, then each of those splits again.","label":"The umbrella shape"}],"img":"dragon-blood-tree"},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the Joshua tree, a giant yucca of the Mojave Desert. It is not really a tree or a cactus."},{"t":"fact","text":"The yucca moth Tegeticula synthetica carries pollen from flower to flower while laying its eggs inside them. Its grubs eat some of the seeds and leave the rest. The tree has no other pollinator, so without the moth it sets no seed.","label":"The one moth"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What happens if the yucca moth does not show up?","options":["The tree grows faster","The tree flowers more often","The tree's roots spread wider","The tree sets no seed"],"answer":3,"hint":"Look at the last sentence of the fact box.","explain":"Without the yucca moth, the Joshua tree sets no seed."}}],"img":"joshua-tree"},{"icon":"📊","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Side by side, the banyan, red mangrove, quaking aspen, dragon's blood tree, and Joshua tree all grow differently. The banyan spreads with its prop roots. The red mangrove thrives in saltwater. The quaking aspen grows in groves connected by one root system. The dragon's blood tree has a unique umbrella shape. The Joshua tree needs a moth to make seeds."},{"t":"fact","text":"All five trees have a trick that suits the place they live. Whether it’s aerial roots, saltwater tolerance, interconnected groves, distinctive shapes, or a moth partner, each tree is built for its own world.","label":"Shared trait"}]},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A banyan spreading wide with its aerial roots. A red mangrove standing in salty water. Aspen trees in groves connected underground. A dragon's blood tree shaped like an umbrella. A Joshua tree that needs a moth to make seeds."},{"t":"p","text":"Five trees, each unique, each built for its own world."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Banyan","Spreads over 4.67 acres"],["Red mangrove","Sends ready-grown seedlings into the water"],["Quaking aspen","Weighs 13.2 million lb as one organism"],["Dragon's blood tree","Shaped like an open umbrella"],["Joshua tree","Depends on the yucca moth for seeds"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"How much ground does the biggest banyan's canopy cover?","options":["2 acres","4.67 acres","6 acres"],"answer":1,"explain":"The biggest banyan's canopy covers 4.67 acres, about three and a half football fields."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is the widest the crown of a dragon's blood tree can be?","options":["15 ft (4.5 m)","39 ft (12 m)","25 ft (7.5 m)"],"answer":1,"explain":"The crown can be up to 39 ft (12 m) across, wider than the tree is tall."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does a red mangrove seed grow into before it drops off the parent tree?","options":["A tough, hard brown seed","A finished green seedling","A dry pod full of seeds"],"answer":1,"explain":"The seed sprouts while still on the parent tree and becomes a green seedling shaped like a dart."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How heavy is Pando, the quaking aspen grove in Utah?","options":["5 million lb (2,270 metric tons)","13.2 million lb (6,000 metric tons)","8 million lb (3,630 metric tons)"],"answer":1,"explain":"Pando is the heaviest known single organism."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where does the dragon's blood tree grow wild?","options":["A dry plain in central Australia","One island group in the Arabian Sea","One river valley in southern Chile"],"answer":1,"explain":"It grows wild only on one island group in the Arabian Sea."}]},{"id":"plants-trees-3","course":"plants","kind":"species","group":"trees","title":"Cacao, Rubber tree & more","names":["Cacao","Rubber tree","Rainbow eucalyptus","Date palm","Cedar of Lebanon"],"glyph":"🌳","tint":"#7fe3a0","items":["cacao","rubber-tree","eucalyptus","date-palm","cedar-of-lebanon"],"idea":"A cacao tree grows its fruit directly out of its trunk. A rainbow eucalyptus shows how old each patch of its bark is by its color.","pages":[{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Five trees are ready to meet you: a cacao tree, a rubber tree, a rainbow eucalyptus, a date palm, and a cedar of Lebanon."},{"t":"p","text":"Each one has something special."}]},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Start with the cacao tree. It grows its fruit straight out of its trunk, and is the only source of chocolate."},{"t":"fact","text":"Cacao flowers and fruits grow straight out of the trunk and old thick branches. This is called cauliflory. A cacao tree looks like someone glued the fruit on by hand.","label":"Fruit placement"},{"t":"p","text":"The flowers are tiny, less than an inch across, and pollinated by tiny midges only about one tenth of an inch (2–3 mm) long."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is the name for flowers and fruits growing straight out of the trunk?","options":["Cauliflory","Branching","Leafing"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the second sentence of the fact box.","explain":"Cauliflory. That's the term for when flowers and fruits grow directly from the trunk or old branches."}}],"img":"cacao"},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Next up is the rubber tree. This Amazon tree has milky sap that comes from its bark and can be turned into tires, gloves, and erasers."},{"t":"term","word":"latex","text":"The milky sap inside a rubber tree that is used to make rubber products."},{"t":"fact","text":"The latex does not travel through the wood. It moves in its own vessels inside the bark. These vessels wind up the trunk in a slow spiral. A tapper cuts a thin, slanted groove across them so the latex drips out.","label":"The hidden network"}],"img":"rubber-tree"},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the rainbow eucalyptus, a rainforest tree whose bark peels off in strips, leaving its trunk streaked with green, blue, orange, and purple."},{"t":"fact","text":"The colors are not paint or disease; they show when each strip of bark peeled off. Freshly exposed bark is bright green, then ages through blue, purple, orange, and reddish brown.","label":"The colorful calendar"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What color is the bark when it first peels off?","options":["Reddish brown","Blue","Bright green"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It describes the colors as they age.","explain":"The freshly exposed bark is bright green."}}],"img":"eucalyptus"},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the date palm, a desert tree that has fed people in dry lands for as long as we have records."},{"t":"fact","text":"Date palms are either male or female, and only the females make dates. A grower climbs up and pollinates each female palm by hand. This job has been done for thousands of years.","label":"The hand-pollinated harvest"}],"img":"date-palm"},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the Cedar of Lebanon, a giant tree that can grow up to 130 ft (40 m) tall on a trunk as thick as 8 ft (2.5 m)."},{"t":"fact","text":"The wood is fragrant and resists rot and insects, making it highly prized for building temples, palaces, and ships.","label":"The valuable wood"},{"t":"p","text":"Cedars of Lebanon are so special that they appear on the national flag of Lebanon and its coat of arms. Very few countries have a specific tree on their flag."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What makes the wood of the Cedar of Lebanon so valuable?","options":["It is colorful and decorative","It resists rot and insects","It is very flexible"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It explains why the wood was highly valued.","explain":"The cedar's wood resists both rot and insects, making it perfect for long-lasting buildings."}}],"img":"cedar-of-lebanon"},{"icon":"📊","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Four of these trees give people something to use: chocolate, rubber, dates, and timber. The date palm lives about 100 years, while the cedar of Lebanon can live more than 1,000 years."},{"t":"fact","text":"A rubber tree is usually tapped for its latex for about 30 years.","label":"Lifespan"}]},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A cacao tree that gives us chocolate. A rubber tree whose milky sap becomes tires and gloves. A rainbow eucalyptus whose bark shows its age in stripes of color. A date palm that lives for a century. A cedar of Lebanon that stands for a thousand years."},{"t":"p","text":"Five trees, each one different."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Cacao","Fruit grows straight from trunk"],["Rubber tree","Milky sap used for rubber products"],["Rainbow eucalyptus","Bark peels off in colorful strips"],["Date palm","Hand-pollinated by growers"],["Cedar of Lebanon","Valuable wood resists rot and insects"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What do cacao flowers and fruits grow directly from?","options":["roots","trunk and old branches","leaves"],"answer":1,"explain":"Cacao flowers and fruits grow straight from the trunk and old, thick branches, which is called cauliflory."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is the milky sap inside a rubber tree called?","options":["resin","latex","sapwood"],"answer":1,"explain":"Latex is what we use to make things like tires and erasers."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How do growers pollinate date palms?","options":["by wind","by hand","by bees"],"answer":1,"explain":"Growers have been climbing up and pollinating each female palm by hand for thousands of years."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why is the wood of the Cedar of Lebanon highly prized?","options":["it is colorful","it resists rot and insects","it is very flexible"],"answer":1,"explain":"This makes the wood perfect for building long-lasting structures like temples and palaces."}]},{"id":"plants-trees-4","course":"plants","kind":"species","group":"trees","title":"Olive tree, Common fig & more","names":["Olive tree","Common fig","Apple tree","Weeping willow","Silver birch"],"glyph":"🌳","tint":"#7fe3a0","items":["olive","fig","apple","willow","birch"],"idea":"An olive is too bitter to eat fresh. A fig is a bag of flowers turned inside out. An apple never grows true from seed. Meet five trees that are not what they seem.","pages":[{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Five trees are waiting for you: an olive tree, a common fig, an apple tree, a weeping willow and a silver birch."},{"t":"p","text":"Each one hides a surprise. Which one would you most like to meet first?"}]},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the olive tree. This slow, gnarled tree of the Mediterranean grows a fruit you cannot eat straight off the branch."},{"t":"fact","text":"A young olive is savagely bitter. To make it edible it must be cured, for a few days in lye or a few months in brine.","label":"Too bitter"},{"t":"p","text":"The written account of the flood says that Noah's dove came back with a fresh olive leaf in her beak."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What has to be done to a fresh olive before it can be eaten?","options":["Roast it over a fire","Cure it in lye or brine","Rinse it in fresh milk"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. It tells how the bitter fruit is made edible.","explain":"A fresh olive is savagely bitter, so it has to be cured in lye or brine first."}}],"img":"olive"},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the common fig. This tree of the Mediterranean grows a fruit that is not a fruit at all, but a bag of flowers turned inside out."},{"t":"fact","text":"Cut one in half and look. A fig is a hollow, fleshy pouch lined on the inside with hundreds of tiny flowers, all facing inward.","label":"Inside a fig"},{"t":"term","word":"syconium","text":"A hollow, fleshy pouch with tiny flowers lined up on the inside."},{"t":"p","text":"There is one way in, a pinhole at the tip. The fig wasp squeezes through to reach the flowers, and she generally loses her wings on the way."}],"img":"fig"},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the apple tree. It refuses to copy itself from seed. Every apple variety you have eaten is a cutting off one original plant."},{"t":"fact","text":"Plant a pip from a Gala and you do not get a Gala. The seedling comes out different from both parents, usually small and sour.","label":"No copies"},{"t":"p","text":"So every Granny Smith is a cutting. A shoot from the original plant is grafted onto a rootstock. There are more than 7,500 named apple varieties."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What do you get if you plant a pip from a Gala apple?","options":["A Gala tree just like its parent","A tree with different apples","A tree that grows pears"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. It says what comes out of a planted pip.","explain":"Apple seedlings come out different from both parents, so a Gala pip does not give a Gala."}}],"img":"apple"},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the weeping willow, the drooping tree by the water. Its branchlets hang straight down to the ground like a curtain."},{"t":"fact","text":"In 1763 the Reverend Edward Stone reported that powdered white willow bark cured shivering fevers. In 1899 Bayer began selling Aspirin.","label":"The medicine"},{"t":"p","text":"There is no aspirin in a willow. Aspirin is a made compound that does not occur in the bark at all."},{"t":"p","text":"Push a cut willow stick into wet ground and it roots. Willows are among the easiest trees on Earth to grow from a piece."}],"img":"willow"},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the silver birch. This white-barked tree gets to burned ground first. Its bark works as a waterproof sheet."},{"t":"fact","text":"The outer bark is up to 20 percent betulin, the white powder that comes off on your fingers. Betulin is waxy and water-repellent.","label":"The white powder"},{"t":"p","text":"Birch is a pioneer, one of the first trees to come up on bare or burned land. But it cannot stand shade, so slower trees grow up past it."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What makes birch bark waterproof?","options":["A thick layer of sap","Tiny hairs that trap air","Betulin, a waxy powder"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the box. It names the white powder on the bark.","explain":"The bark is up to 20 percent betulin, a waxy, water-repellent powder."}}],"img":"birch"},{"icon":"📊","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Five trees, five surprises. The olive makes a fruit too bitter to eat. The fig hides its flowers inside. The apple never copies itself from seed. The willow gave us the idea behind aspirin. The birch is first to a burned field."},{"t":"fact","text":"A birch grows 49 to 82 ft (15 to 25 m) tall. A weeping willow reaches 66 to 82 ft (20 to 25 m). A common fig reaches 23 to 33 ft (7 to 10 m). One olive trunk measures 82 ft (25 m) around, and it would take a dozen children with their arms out to circle it.","label":"Sizes"}]},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"An olive too bitter to eat. A fig full of flowers. An apple that never copies itself. A willow by the water. A birch on burned ground."},{"t":"p","text":"Five trees, each with a surprise."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Olive tree","Fruit too bitter to eat fresh"],["Common fig","Fruit is really a bag of flowers"],["Apple tree","Never grows true from seed"],["Weeping willow","Bark gave us the idea for aspirin"],["Silver birch","First tree on bare or burned ground"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is a fig really?","options":["A seed pod full of nuts","A bag of tiny flowers","A cluster of berries"],"answer":1,"explain":"A fig is a hollow pouch lined with hundreds of tiny flowers, all facing inward."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How is a Granny Smith apple tree made?","options":["Its seeds are planted in long rows","A branch is dipped in sticky glue","A shoot is grafted onto a rootstock"],"answer":2,"explain":"Apple seeds never repeat their parent, so every Granny Smith is a cutting of the original plant."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did the Reverend Edward Stone give people for fevers?","options":["Crushed leaves from an olive tree","Powdered white willow bark","Warm tea made from fig leaves"],"answer":1,"explain":"In 1763 he reported that powdered white willow bark cured shivering fevers."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where does a birch tree grow first?","options":["Deep inside an old forest","In the shade of big trees","On bare or burned ground"],"answer":2,"explain":"Birch is a pioneer, one of the first trees to come up on bare or burned land."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many children would it take to circle the biggest olive trunk?","options":["Only one child","About a dozen children","More than a hundred children"],"answer":1,"explain":"The trunk measures 82 ft (25 m) around, so a dozen children with their arms out could just circle it."}]},{"id":"plants-flowers-1","course":"plants","kind":"species","group":"flowers","title":"Sunflower, Corpse flower & more","names":["Sunflower","Corpse flower","Rafflesia","Bee orchid","Sacred lotus"],"glyph":"🌸","tint":"#ff9ec7","items":["sunflower","corpse-flower","rafflesia","orchid-bee","lotus"],"idea":"A sunflower is hundreds of tiny flowers. A corpse flower smells of rotting meat. A rafflesia has no leaves, stem or roots. Meet five flowers with a trick.","pages":[{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Five flowers are waiting for you: a sunflower, a corpse flower, a rafflesia, a bee orchid and a sacred lotus."},{"t":"p","text":"Each one has its own special trick. Which one would you most like to meet first?"}]},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the sunflower. What looks like a big flower is really a packed disc of hundreds of tiny separate flowers on a stalk."},{"t":"fact","text":"The yellow outer blades are ray florets. The whole brown center is packed with disc florets. Each disc floret is a complete little flower, and each one makes exactly one seed.","label":"Pull it apart"},{"t":"p","text":"A garden sunflower stands about 10 ft (3 m) tall, with a head the width of a dinner plate. The record holder reached 35 ft 9 in (10.9 m)."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many seeds does each tiny disc floret make?","options":["Two seeds each","Exactly one seed","A whole handful of seeds"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. It says how many seeds each little flower makes.","explain":"Each disc floret is a complete little flower, and each one makes exactly one seed."}}],"img":"sunflower"},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the corpse flower. This Sumatran plant banks food underground for years, then spends the lot on one enormous bloom that smells of rotting meat."},{"t":"fact","text":"The underground store can top 200 lb (90 kg), the largest of any flowering plant. One grown in Edinburgh reached 339 lb (153.9 kg) after seven years.","label":"The giant corm"},{"t":"term","word":"corm","text":"An underground food store, a swollen stem rather than a root."},{"t":"p","text":"The spike heats itself to about 99°F (37°C), which is human body temperature. Put a hand near it and you can feel the warmth."}],"img":"corpse-flower"},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the rafflesia, the largest single flower there is. It grows straight out of a jungle vine with no leaves, no stem and no roots of its own."},{"t":"fact","text":"A bloom measured in West Sumatra in January 2020 came to 3 ft 7.7 in (111 cm) across, the widest reliably recorded. That is about as wide as a car tire laid flat.","label":"The giant bloom"},{"t":"p","text":"It has no chlorophyll, so there is nothing green anywhere on it. Everything you can see of the plant is the flower. The rest lives as threads inside a jungle vine."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why is there nothing green on a rafflesia?","options":["Its leaves are underground","It has no chlorophyll","Rain washes the green away"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the last paragraph. It says what the plant does not have.","explain":"A rafflesia has no chlorophyll, the green machinery that turns sunlight into sugar, so nothing on it is green."}}],"img":"rafflesia"},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the bee orchid. This small European orchid has a furry, brown lower lip shaped so much like a bee that people look twice."},{"t":"fact","text":"The lip is velvety chestnut brown with a yellow-edged pattern. Two narrow inner petals stand up above it like antennae, and three broad pink sepals spread out behind like a propeller.","label":"The look-alike"},{"t":"p","text":"In Britain, its pollinating bee is scarce, so the orchid mostly pollinates itself. Its pollen sacs hang on long, flexible stalks that gradually bend down until they touch the flower's own sticky part."}],"img":"orchid-bee"},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the sacred lotus, a water plant whose leaves shed dirt completely. Paint and fabric makers copied the surface."},{"t":"fact","text":"Water will not wet a lotus leaf. It balls up and rolls straight off, picking up dust on the way.","label":"The lotus effect"},{"t":"p","text":"The flower runs a heater. It holds itself between 86 and 95°F (30 to 35°C) inside for about two days, even when the air around it falls to 50°F (10°C)."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What happens to a drop of water on a lotus leaf?","options":["It soaks straight into the leaf","It balls up and rolls off","It spreads out in a thin film"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. It tells what water does on the leaf.","explain":"Water balls up and rolls straight off the leaf, picking up dust on the way."}}],"img":"lotus"},{"icon":"📊","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Five flowers, and five very different tricks. The sunflower is hundreds of flowers in one. The corpse flower smells of rotting meat. The rafflesia is the largest single flower. The bee orchid looks like a bee. The lotus leaf sheds dirt."},{"t":"fact","text":"A garden sunflower stands about 10 ft (3 m) tall. A rafflesia is about 3 ft 3 in (1 m) across. A bee orchid is 6 to 20 in (15 to 50 cm) tall. A lotus leaf is 2 ft 7 in to 3 ft 3 in (80 to 100 cm) across.","label":"Sizes"}]},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A sunflower of hundreds of tiny flowers. A corpse flower that smells of rotting meat. A rafflesia, the largest single flower. A bee orchid that looks like a bee. A lotus leaf that sheds dirt."},{"t":"p","text":"Five flowers, each with its own special trick."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Sunflower","Hundreds of tiny flowers on one stalk"],["Corpse flower","Smells of rotting meat and heats itself"],["Rafflesia","Largest single flower, no leaves or roots"],["Bee orchid","Has a lip shaped like a bee"],["Sacred lotus","Leaves that shed dirt"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is a corm?","options":["A kind of petal on the bloom","An underground food store","A sweet nectar that bees drink"],"answer":1,"explain":"The corm is an underground store of food, and a corpse flower's can top 200 lb (90 kg)."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How wide is a big rafflesia bloom?","options":["As wide as a dinner plate","About as wide as a car tire","As wide as a football pitch"],"answer":1,"explain":"The widest bloom reliably recorded was 3 ft 7.7 in (111 cm) across."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How does a bee orchid get pollinated in Britain?","options":["A moth carries its pollen","The wind blows it away","It pollinates itself"],"answer":2,"explain":"In Britain its pollinating bee is scarce, so the orchid mostly pollinates itself."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why is a lotus leaf self-cleaning?","options":["Morning dew washes it clean each day","Water rolls off and takes dust with it","It sheds its dirty skin every night"],"answer":1,"explain":"Water balls up and rolls straight off, picking up dust on the way."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How warm does a corpse flower's spike get?","options":["Cold as a glass of ice","Hot as a boiling kettle","Warm as your own body"],"answer":2,"explain":"At its peak the tip reaches about 99°F (37°C), which is human body temperature."}]},{"id":"plants-flowers-2","course":"plants","kind":"species","group":"flowers","title":"Rose, Tulip & Dandelion","names":["Rose","Tulip","Dandelion","Common poppy","Lavender"],"glyph":"🌸","tint":"#ff9ec7","items":["rose","tulip","dandelion","poppy","lavender"],"idea":"Garden roses live up to about 35 years. Find out why a rose has prickles, not thorns, how a dandelion seed flies, and how poppy seeds wait in the soil for decades.","pages":[{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Five flowers are waiting to meet you: a rose, a tulip, a dandelion, a common poppy, and lavender."},{"t":"p","text":"Each one looks different and does its own job."}]},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"First, meet the rose. It's a prickly shrub with 140 wild species and around 30,000 garden versions."},{"t":"p","text":"Those spikes on a rose are not thorns but prickles. A prickle is just an outgrowth of the skin. You can snap one off a rose stem without hurting the plant."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What are the spikes on a rose called?","options":["Prickles, an outgrowth of the skin","Thorns, which are twigs that turned hard","Needles, which are leaves that turned hard"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the second paragraph and find the description.","explain":"The spikes on a rose are prickles, which are just an outgrowth of the skin."}}],"img":"rose"},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Next up is the tulip. It’s a mountain plant from Central Asia that became so popular in Holland, it is said that a single rare bulb once sold for more than a house."},{"t":"term","word":"bulb","text":"A part of a plant that grows underground and stores food to help the plant grow."},{"t":"fact","text":"A bulb grows small copies of itself, so one rare tulip can be multiplied into many that are exactly the same.","label":"The bulb's secret"}],"img":"tulip"},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Next up is the dandelion, a lawn weed whose seeds fly on the wind."},{"t":"fact","text":"The yellow head is not one flower. It's 40 to more than 100 little flowers packed side by side, each making a single seed.","label":"The seed head"},{"t":"p","text":"Each seed hangs beneath a pappus, a fluffy parachute of about 100 thin bristles. When they fly, a ring of spinning air sits above the bristles, keeping the seed steady."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What keeps the dandelion seed steady while flying?","options":["The stem","The leaves","A ring of spinning air above the bristles"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the last sentence of the last paragraph. It says what sits above the bristles.","explain":"A ring of spinning air that forms above the bristles keeps the seed steady as it flies."}}],"img":"dandelion"},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Next, meet the common poppy, a bright red flower that shows up where the ground has been disturbed."},{"t":"fact","text":"The seeds can sit in the soil for decades, waiting for the ground to be turned over by a plow or even an exploding artillery shell. Then they come up everywhere at once.","label":"The seed bank"},{"t":"p","text":"The petals are crumpled inside the bud and push out flat within a day of the bud splitting open."},{"t":"fact","text":"The seed capsule works like a pepper shaker with small holes under a flat lid, shaking out seeds little by little when the wind rocks the dry stalk.","label":"Seed capsule"}],"img":"poppy"},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet lavender, a gray-green shrub in the mint family. It's grown for the oil that sits in tiny glands on its surface."},{"t":"fact","text":"Roll a lavender stem between finger and thumb and you'll notice it’s square, not round. Nearly every plant in the mint family has this square stem.","label":"The square stem"},{"t":"p","text":"The smell is not in the petals but in small glands all over the outside of the plant. Brushing past a plant bursts them, which is why you can smell lavender from the path without touching a single flower."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where is the smell of lavender stored?","options":["In the petals","In the glands on the leaves and stems","In the soil"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the last paragraph. It talks about where the smell comes from.","explain":"The smell is stored in the glands on the leaves and stems."}}],"img":"lavender"},{"icon":"📊","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A garden rose lives up to about 35 years, the dandelion about 10, and lavender about 15. The common poppy lasts just one year."},{"t":"fact","text":"Each of these flowers has its own trick: prickles, a bulb that splits, seeds that fly, seeds that wait for years, and smell glands that burst when brushed.","label":"What they share"}]},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A garden rose that lives up to about 35 years. A dandelion with seeds spread by the wind. Lavender that grows for about 15 years. A common poppy that lasts just one year. Tulip bulbs that split off copies of themselves."},{"t":"p","text":"Five flowers, each built for its own way of growing and spreading seeds."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Rose","Garden bushes live up to about 35 years"],["Tulip","A bulb plant from Central Asia"],["Dandelion","Seeds fly with bristles"],["Common poppy","Grows where ground is disturbed"],["Lavender","Smell stored in glands on leaves and stems"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"About how long can a garden rose bush live?","options":["10 years","35 years","1 year"],"answer":1,"explain":"Garden roses can live up to about 35 years, though a few very old roses have lasted for centuries."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How does a tulip bulb make more tulips that are exactly like it?","options":["It turns into a seed.","It splits off copies of itself.","It sends out runners above the ground."],"answer":1,"explain":"The bulb of a tulip can split into new bulbs, which is why they spread so easily."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many little flowers make up the dandelion's yellow head?","options":["5 to 30","40 to more than 100","1 to 10"],"answer":1,"explain":"The dandelion's yellow head has between 40 and over 100 small flowers."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How long does one common poppy plant live, from seed to seed?","options":["5 years","1 year","10 years"],"answer":1,"explain":"The common poppy plant lasts just one year, although its seeds can wait in the soil for decades."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What shape is the stem of lavender?","options":["Round","Square","Oval"],"answer":1,"explain":"Nearly every plant in the mint family, like lavender, has a square stem."}]},{"id":"plants-flowers-3","course":"plants","kind":"species","group":"flowers","title":"Bird of paradise flower & more","names":["Bird of paradise flower","Passionflower","Snapdragon","Giant water lily","Edelweiss"],"glyph":"🌸","tint":"#ff9ec7","items":["bird-of-paradise","passionflower","snapdragon","water-lily","edelweiss"],"idea":"A bird of paradise flower splits open under a bird's weight. A giant water lily leaf can hold a child. A snapdragon's seed pod looks like a skull. Meet five flowers with a trick.","pages":[{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Five flowers are waiting for you: a bird of paradise flower, a passionflower, a snapdragon, a giant water lily and an edelweiss."},{"t":"p","text":"Each one has its own special trick. Which one would you most like to meet first?"}]},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the bird of paradise flower. This South African plant's flowers open sideways out of a hard beak and look like the head of a crested bird."},{"t":"fact","text":"Each flower has three orange sepals standing up like a crest and three blue-purple petals below. Two of the blue petals are fused into an arrow shape, with the pollen hidden inside.","label":"The crest"},{"t":"p","text":"A bird landing on the blue arrow makes it split open along its length, and the pollen presses onto the bird's feet. Almost nothing else in the plant world moves pollen by foot."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What splits the blue arrow open?","options":["A gust of wind","A bird landing on it","A bee buzzing near it"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the last paragraph. It says what makes the arrow split open.","explain":"A bird landing on the blue arrow makes it split open, and the pollen presses onto the bird's feet."}}],"img":"bird-of-paradise"},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the passionflower, a climbing vine with a flower built in rings. Missionaries gave the plant its name, because they saw the story of the cross in its parts."},{"t":"fact","text":"The ring of colored threads inside the flower is called the corona. Some species stack as many as eight rings of it in a single flower.","label":"The corona"},{"t":"term","word":"corona","text":"The ring of colored threads inside a passionflower."},{"t":"p","text":"The vine makes nectar outside its flowers too, from small glands on the leaf stalks. Ants find the sugar and stay on the plant."}],"img":"passionflower"},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the snapdragon, a Mediterranean garden flower with a mouth that snaps shut and a seed pod that dries into a tiny skull."},{"t":"fact","text":"Pinch the sides of the flower and it gapes open like a jaw. Let go and it snaps shut. The seed capsule later dries into a skull, with two hollow eye sockets and an open mouth.","label":"The snap"},{"t":"p","text":"The flower is shut. A bumblebee has to land on the lower lip, force it down and climb inside to reach the nectar, collecting pollen on its back as it goes."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does a snapdragon's dried seed pod look like?","options":["A small star","A tiny skull","A little bell"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the first paragraph. It says what the seed pod dries into.","explain":"The seed capsule dries into the shape of a skull, with two hollow eye sockets and an open mouth."}}],"img":"snapdragon"},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the giant water lily. This Amazon water plant has leaves 10 ft (3 m) across, strong enough to hold a child standing up."},{"t":"fact","text":"A leaf tested at Ghent measured 8 ft 6 in (2.6 m) across and weighed 13 lb (6.1 kg). Loaded with weights, it carried 498 lb (226 kg) before it gave way, about 38 times its own weight.","label":"Strong leaf"},{"t":"p","text":"Turn one over. Thick ribs radiate from the center with cross-braces between them, and every rib is covered in sharp spines."},{"t":"p","text":"The flower opens white on the first night, then closes with beetles shut inside. The next night it reopens, and the petals have turned pink."}],"img":"water-lily"},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the edelweiss, a wooly Alpine flower of high limestone rock. Its hairs soak up ultraviolet light."},{"t":"fact","text":"The white star is not petals. Those are bracts, which are modified leaves, arranged in a double star around five or six small yellow flower heads. Every white surface is packed with dense white hairs.","label":"White hairs"},{"t":"p","text":"Researchers shone light through a pad of the white hairs. The hairs absorb near-ultraviolet light before it reaches the living tissue underneath. They do not simply bounce it away."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What do the white hairs do with ultraviolet light?","options":["Bounce it all away","Soak it up","Turn it into nectar"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the last paragraph. It says what the hairs do with ultraviolet light.","explain":"The hairs absorb near-ultraviolet light before it reaches the living tissue underneath."}}],"img":"edelweiss"},{"icon":"📊","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Five flowers, five ways of working. The bird of paradise opens for a bird's weight. The passionflower stacks rings of threads. The snapdragon snaps its mouth shut. The giant water lily carries a child. The edelweiss soaks up ultraviolet light."},{"t":"fact","text":"The bird of paradise plant reaches about 6 ft 6 in (2 m). A passionflower vine climbs 20 to 30 ft (6 to 9 m). A snapdragon stands 1 ft 8 in to 3 ft 3 in (0.5 to 1 m) tall. An edelweiss is 1 to 8 in (3 to 20 cm) tall in the wild.","label":"Sizes"}]},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A bird of paradise that splits for a bird's weight. A passionflower with rings of threads. A snapdragon that snaps shut. A water lily leaf holding a child. A wooly edelweiss."},{"t":"p","text":"Five flowers, each with its own way of working."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Bird of paradise flower","Splits open under a bird's weight"],["Passionflower","Flower built in rings of threads"],["Snapdragon","Mouth snaps shut, seed pod looks like a skull"],["Giant water lily","Leaf strong enough to hold a child"],["Edelweiss","Wooly flower that soaks up ultraviolet"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What colors are the bird of paradise flower's crest and arrow?","options":["Bright red crest, deep green arrow","Pale yellow crest, snow white arrow","Orange crest, blue-purple arrow"],"answer":2,"explain":"Three orange sepals stand up like a crest, and the blue-purple petals form the arrow."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many rings of threads can some passionflowers stack?","options":["Just a single ring","As many as eight","Exactly three rings"],"answer":1,"explain":"Some species stack as many as eight rings of the corona in a single flower."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which insect has to climb inside a snapdragon to reach the nectar?","options":["A tiny fly","A bumblebee","A small ant"],"answer":1,"explain":"The flower is shut, so a bumblebee has to land, force the lip down and climb inside."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How much weight did a giant water lily leaf carry before it gave way?","options":["About the weight of a coin","Only its own weight","About 38 times its own weight"],"answer":2,"explain":"A leaf that weighed 13 lb (6.1 kg) carried 498 lb (226 kg) before it gave way."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where does edelweiss grow?","options":["In a warm, wet swamp","On sandy sea beaches","On high limestone rock"],"answer":2,"explain":"Edelweiss is a wooly Alpine flower of high limestone rock."}]},{"id":"plants-flowers-4","course":"plants","kind":"species","group":"flowers","title":"Hibiscus, Foxglove & Bluebell","names":["Hibiscus","Foxglove","Bluebell"],"glyph":"🌸","tint":"#ff9ec7","items":["hibiscus","foxglove","bluebell"],"idea":"A hibiscus flower lasts about one day. A foxglove can poison or heal. A bluebell takes five years to bloom. Meet three flowers with very different lives.","pages":[{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Three flowers are waiting for you: a hibiscus, a foxglove and a bluebell."},{"t":"p","text":"Each one lives its life in a different way. Which one would you most like to meet first?"}]},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the hibiscus. This tropical shrub has big trumpet flowers that open in the morning and are finished by evening."},{"t":"fact","text":"A hibiscus flower lasts about one day. It opens in the morning and is limp by late afternoon. One species is even called flower-of-an-hour.","label":"A single day"},{"t":"p","text":"The flowers run from 1.5 to 7 in (4 to 18 cm) across. The big ones are wider than an adult's outstretched hand."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How long does a hibiscus flower last?","options":["About one month","About one day","About one week"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. It tells how long the flower lasts.","explain":"A hibiscus flower lasts about one day. It opens in the morning and is limp by late afternoon."}}],"img":"hibiscus"},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the foxglove. It is a tall spike of speckled purple bells. It is poisonous to eat, yet measured out carefully it steadies a failing heart."},{"t":"fact","text":"It is a biennial. The first year is a flat rosette of leaves and nothing else. The second year it throws up the spike, flowers, sets seed and dies.","label":"Two years"},{"t":"term","word":"biennial","text":"A plant that grows only leaves in its first year, then flowers and dies in its second."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does a foxglove grow in its first year?","options":["A tall spike of purple bells","A flat rosette of leaves","Seeds ready to scatter"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. It tells what the plant grows in its first year.","explain":"In its first year a foxglove is just a flat rosette of leaves. The spike comes in the second year."}}],"img":"foxglove"},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the bluebell. This woodland bulb turns British woods solid blue for a few weeks in spring, then disappears underground for the rest of the year."},{"t":"fact","text":"A bluebell seed takes at least five years to build a bulb big enough to flower. A wood does not gain a bluebell carpet in a hurry.","label":"Slow growers"},{"t":"p","text":"The British Isles hold roughly half of all the native bluebells in the world. The bulbs are poisonous, which is one reason deer and rabbits leave them alone."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How long does a bluebell seed take to grow a bulb big enough to flower?","options":["About five days","At least five years","About five weeks"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. It says how long the seed takes to build a bulb.","explain":"A bluebell seed takes at least five years to build a bulb big enough to flower."}}],"img":"bluebell"},{"icon":"📊","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Three flowers, three very different lives. The hibiscus lives for one day. The foxglove waits two years. The bluebell waits five. All three are lovely, and two are poisonous."},{"t":"fact","text":"A hibiscus flower is 1.5 to 7 in (4 to 18 cm) across. A foxglove stands 3 ft 3 in to 6 ft 6 in (1 to 2 m) tall in its second year. A bluebell reaches about 18 in (45 cm).","label":"Sizes"}]},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A hibiscus lasting one day. A foxglove waiting two years. A bluebell waiting five."},{"t":"p","text":"Three flowers, each very different."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Hibiscus","Flowers that last just one day"],["Foxglove","Poisonous bells that can steady a heart"],["Bluebell","Turns woods blue for a few weeks"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"How wide can the biggest hibiscus flowers get?","options":["About 1 in (2.5 cm)","About 7 in (18 cm)","About 2 ft (60 cm)"],"answer":1,"explain":"The flowers run from 1.5 to 7 in (4 to 18 cm) across, wider than an adult's outstretched hand."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"In which year does a foxglove flower?","options":["The first year","The second year","Every single month"],"answer":1,"explain":"The first year is only leaves. In the second year it throws up the spike and flowers."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What can foxglove help with, if it is measured out carefully?","options":["A broken bone in the arm","A bad toothache or sore gum","A weak, failing heart"],"answer":2,"explain":"Foxglove is poisonous to eat, but measured out carefully it steadies a failing heart."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why do deer and rabbits leave bluebells alone?","options":["They taste too sweet","The bulbs are poisonous","The flowers smell like smoke"],"answer":1,"explain":"The bulbs are poisonous, which is one reason a bluebell wood is not grazed flat."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where do roughly half of the world's native bluebells grow?","options":["Tropical Africa","Northern Canada","The British Isles"],"answer":2,"explain":"The British Isles hold roughly half of all the native bluebells in the world."}]},{"id":"plants-carnivores-1","course":"plants","kind":"species","group":"carnivores","title":"Venus flytrap & more","names":["Venus flytrap","Tropical pitcher plant","Sundew","Bladderwort","Cobra lily","Butterwort"],"glyph":"🪤","tint":"#9ee85f","items":["venus-flytrap","pitcher-plant","sundew","bladderwort","cobra-lily","butterwort"],"idea":"A Venus flytrap counts to two before it shuts. A sundew makes fake dew. A bladderwort traps a water flea faster than you can blink. Meet six plants that eat animals.","pages":[{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Six plants that eat animals are waiting for you: a Venus flytrap, a tropical pitcher plant, a sundew, a bladderwort, a cobra lily and a butterwort."},{"t":"p","text":"Each one has its own kind of trap. Which one would you most like to meet first?"}]},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the Venus flytrap. This bog plant has hinged leaves that snap shut on insects, and it will not close for a raindrop."},{"t":"fact","text":"Each half of a trap has three stiff hairs. Touch one and nothing happens. Touch a second one within 20 seconds, and the trap shuts. One touch is a raindrop. Two is something walking.","label":"The trigger"},{"t":"p","text":"The snap takes about one tenth of a second. Digestion takes about ten days, and then the trap opens and the empty husk blows away."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many touches does it take to shut a Venus flytrap?","options":["One light touch of a leaf","Two touches within 20 seconds","Ten touches in a single row"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. It tells how many touches the trigger hairs need.","explain":"One touch is a raindrop. Two touches within 20 seconds mean something is walking, so the trap shuts."}}],"img":"venus-flytrap"},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the tropical pitcher plant. This climbing vine grows a lidded jug at the tip of every leaf and lets things fall in."},{"t":"fact","text":"The rim is the trap. Wet it and the surface turns into a water slide, so insects slip over the edge. The record pitcher stood 16 in (41 cm) tall.","label":"The slide"},{"t":"p","text":"One mountain kind gets help from a tree shrew. The shrew perches on the lid, licks something sweet, and leaves its droppings in the jug."},{"t":"term","word":"pitcher","text":"A leaf tip that has grown into a lidded jug."}],"img":"pitcher-plant"},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the sundew. Every stalk on its leaf ends in a bead of clear glue that looks like dew and does not dry out in the sun."},{"t":"fact","text":"Once an insect sticks to the glue, the neighboring stalks bend over and press onto it, adding their own glue.","label":"Sticky leaf"},{"t":"p","text":"Darwin tested what the stalks answer to. Bits of glass, hair and sand got a weak reaction or none. Meat and insects got a strong one."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did Darwin find the sundew's stalks answer to?","options":["Bits of glass and sand","Meat and insects","Drops of rain"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. It tells what got a strong reaction.","explain":"Glass and sand got a weak reaction or none. Meat and insects got a strong one."}}],"img":"sundew"},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the bladderwort. This rootless water plant is strung with tiny bladders that swallow a water flea faster than anything else in the plant world."},{"t":"fact","text":"A bladderwort trap opens in about 0.5 milliseconds. It is the fastest movement known in any plant.","label":"Faster than a blink"},{"t":"p","text":"The plant pumps water out of the sealed bladder until the walls cave inward. Brush a trigger hair and the door lets go, and water rushes in with the prey."},{"t":"p","text":"After a catch, the bladder pumps the water back out over roughly 15 to 30 minutes and is armed again."}],"img":"bladderwort"},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the cobra lily. This plant is shaped like a rearing snake, with a hood full of skylights. It grows in northern California and Oregon."},{"t":"fact","text":"The hood is peppered with see-through patches, like windows. They fool the insect inside: it flies at the bright spots, hits leaf and drops back. The real exit is a small hole under the hood, in shadow.","label":"The skylights"},{"t":"p","text":"Below the opening the tube is lined with hairs that all point down. Walking in is easy. Walking back up is not."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What do the see-through patches in the hood do?","options":["They collect the rainwater","They fool the insect inside","They let the plant breathe"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. It says what the bright patches do to an insect.","explain":"The patches fool the insect, which flies at the bright spots and drops back instead of finding the real exit."}}],"img":"cobra-lily"},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the butterwort. This flat rosette of leaves feels buttery to the touch, and that greasy film is the trap."},{"t":"fact","text":"The leaf is covered in tiny stalked glands, each carrying a droplet of clear slime. A small fly lands and cannot get a grip, because the whole surface is glue.","label":"Greasy slime"},{"t":"p","text":"In Norway and Sweden, the leaves were used to set milk. Pour warm milk over butterwort leaves and it thickens."}],"img":"butterwort"},{"icon":"📊","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Six plants that eat animals, and each has its own trap. The Venus flytrap snaps shut. The pitcher plant is a slippery jug. The sundew is covered in glue. The bladderwort sucks its prey in. The cobra lily has false windows. The butterwort is a greasy leaf."},{"t":"fact","text":"A flytrap trap is about 1 in (2.5 cm) long. A bladderwort trap runs from about 1/100 in to 1/2 in (0.2 to 12 mm). The record pitcher stood 16 in (41 cm) tall. A tall cobra lily stands about 3 ft (1 m).","label":"Sizes"}]},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A trap that snaps shut. A jug with a slippery rim. A leaf covered in glue. A bladder that swallows a water flea. A hood full of windows. A leaf as greasy as butter."},{"t":"p","text":"Six plants that eat animals, each with its own trap."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Venus flytrap","Snaps shut after two touches"],["Tropical pitcher plant","Lidded jug with a slippery rim"],["Sundew","Leaf covered in beads of glue"],["Bladderwort","Rootless water plant with tiny bladders"],["Cobra lily","Hood with see-through windows"],["Butterwort","Greasy leaf that thickens milk"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"How long does a Venus flytrap take to digest a meal?","options":["About ten minutes","About ten days","About ten weeks"],"answer":1,"explain":"Digestion takes about ten days, and then the trap opens and the empty husk blows away."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How does a pitcher plant's rim trap insects?","options":["It snaps shut like a jaw","It shoots out sticky threads","It turns slippery when wet"],"answer":2,"explain":"Wet the rim and it becomes a water slide, so insects slip over the edge and fall in."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is the fastest movement known in any plant?","options":["A bladderwort trap opening","A sunflower turning","A leaf curling in the cold"],"answer":0,"explain":"A bladderwort trap opens in about 0.5 milliseconds."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where does a cobra lily grow?","options":["The hot rainforests of Brazil","Northern California and Oregon","The dry deserts of North Africa"],"answer":1,"explain":"The cobra lily grows only in northern California and southwest Oregon."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does a sundew's glue look like?","options":["Drops of rain","Tiny drops of nectar","Beads of dew"],"answer":2,"explain":"Each bead catches the light like a dewdrop, but unlike dew it is still there at noon in full sun."}]},{"id":"plants-desert-1","course":"plants","kind":"species","group":"desert","title":"Saguaro cactus, Welwitschia & more","names":["Saguaro cactus","Welwitschia","Living stones","Resurrection plant","Century plant"],"glyph":"🌵","tint":"#ffc94a","items":["saguaro","welwitschia","living-stones","resurrection-plant","agave"],"idea":"A welwitschia lives for 1,500 years but only has two leaves. Find out how these desert plants survive.","pages":[{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Five desert plants are waiting to meet you: a saguaro cactus, a welwitschia, living stones, a resurrection plant, and a century plant."},{"t":"p","text":"Each one has its own way of coping with the harsh desert."}]},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the saguaro cactus, the tall branching giant of the Sonoran Desert. It grows wild nowhere else on Earth."},{"t":"fact","text":"A big one stands 40 to 52 ft (12–16 m) tall and can weigh 4,800 lb (2,200 kg) full of water.","label":"The slow grower"},{"t":"p","text":"Saguaros start slowly and stay slow. It takes about 75 years before the plant grows its first arm."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How long does it take for a saguaro cactus to grow its first arm?","options":["10 years","50 years","75 years"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the last sentence on the page.","explain":"It takes about 75 years for a saguaro cactus to grow its first arm."}}],"img":"saguaro"},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Next up is the Welwitschia, a plant that grows exactly two leaves when it sprouts and never grows another one."},{"t":"fact","text":"The largest known welwitschia stands about 6 ft (1.8 m) high and sprawls 28 ft (8.7 m) wide — all of it made up of those same two leaves.","label":"The leaf keeper"},{"t":"p","text":"Afrikaans speakers call it tweeblaarkanniedood — 'two leaves, cannot die'. The leaves never stop growing and are never replaced. They split and fray over time but keep pushing out new growth from the base."}],"img":"welwitschia"},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the living stones, a plant that looks exactly like the pebbles around it. It's two fat leaves with a window on top."},{"t":"fact","text":"Most of the plant is underground, and the green tissue for photosynthesis lines the inside walls of the buried leaves. The leaves are translucent to let light in.","label":"The hidden green"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What part of the living stone does its photosynthesis?","options":["The top surface","The inner wall","The outer surface"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the fact box called The hidden green.","explain":"The inner wall. The green tissue is inside the buried leaves and not on the outside."}}],"img":"living-stones"},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the resurrection plant, a Chihuahuan Desert plant that curls into a dead-looking brown ball and waits for rain."},{"t":"term","word":"dormant","text":"Resting and not growing, almost like a deep sleep, until the right conditions come back."},{"t":"fact","text":"It can lose about 95 percent of its water and live. Dried right down, it goes dormant, simply stopping for years at a stretch, with no damage done.","label":"The hidden life"},{"t":"p","text":"Put a dry ball in a saucer of water and it starts opening within a few hours. The green comes back and it goes back to making sugar from light."}],"img":"resurrection-plant"},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Next up is the century plant. It makes a rosette of gray spiked leaves that spend their whole life saving up for one enormous flower stalk. Then it dies."},{"t":"fact","text":"The flower stalk has been measured growing 6 to 9 inches (15–23 cm) in a day, sometimes close to a foot.","label":"The fast growth"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How much can the flower stalk grow in a single day?","options":["2 to 3 feet","6 to 9 inches","3 to 4 feet"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It gives the exact measurement.","explain":"The flower stalk grows 6 to 9 inches in a single day, sometimes close to a foot."}}],"img":"agave"},{"icon":"📊","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The saguaro cactus can live up to 175 years and weighs about 4,800 lb. The welwitschia can live for about 1,500 years."},{"t":"fact","text":"Despite its name, the century plant lives only about 30 years. Then it sends up one huge flower stalk and dies.","label":"The lives of plants"}]},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A saguaro cactus that lives 175 years. A welwitschia that lasts 1,500 years. A century plant with a fast-growing flower stalk. Living stones and a resurrection plant that survive the desert."},{"t":"p","text":"Each plant, in its own way, built to endure."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Saguaro cactus","Grows its first arm at 75 years"],["Welwitschia","Only grows two leaves"],["Living stones","Looks like pebbles"],["Resurrection plant","Can lose 95% of water"],["Century plant","Flower stalk grows 6 to 9 inches a day"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"How tall can a big saguaro cactus grow?","options":["20 to 30 ft (6–9 m) tall","40 to 52 ft (12–16 m) tall","60 to 70 ft (18–21 m) tall"],"answer":1,"explain":"Saguaros can grow really tall, but it takes them a long time."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How wide does the largest known Welwitschia sprawl?","options":["15 ft (4.6 m) wide","28 ft (8.7 m) wide","20 ft (6.1 m) wide"],"answer":1,"explain":"The welwitschia is a big plant with just two leaves that keep growing."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is hidden underground in a living stone?","options":["Only the window on top","Most of the plant","Just the roots"],"answer":1,"explain":"Most of a living stone is buried, and only the window on top shows."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is the largest share of its water the resurrection plant can lose and still live?","options":["75 percent of its water","95 percent of its water","80 percent of its water"],"answer":1,"explain":"The resurrection plant is really tough and can dry out almost completely."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"About how long do the oldest welwitschias live?","options":["500 years","1,500 years","1,000 years"],"answer":1,"explain":"Welwitschia plants are some of the longest-lived plants in the world."}]},{"id":"plants-desert-2","course":"plants","kind":"species","group":"desert","title":"Barrel cactus, Prickly pear & more","names":["Barrel cactus","Prickly pear","Aloe vera"],"glyph":"🌵","tint":"#ffc94a","items":["barrel-cactus","prickly-pear","aloe"],"idea":"A barrel cactus leans south like a compass. A prickly pear is a pile of flat stems. An aloe is not a cactus at all. Meet three desert survivors.","pages":[{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Three desert survivors are waiting for you: a barrel cactus, a prickly pear and an aloe vera."},{"t":"p","text":"Each one has its own way to live with little water. Which one would you most like to meet first?"}]},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the barrel cactus. This squat, ribbed cactus is armed with hooked spines, and it leans toward the sun as it ages."},{"t":"fact","text":"The spines are thick, stiff and curved back on themselves, like fish hooks. That is where the fishhook barrel gets its name.","label":"Fishhooks"},{"t":"p","text":"Mature barrels lean toward the south or southwest, and desert hikers call it the compass barrel. The best reading is that the side facing the hot afternoon sun grows more slowly."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which way does a mature fishhook barrel cactus lean?","options":["South or southwest","North or northeast","Straight up and tall"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the last paragraph. It says which way the cactus leans.","explain":"Mature barrels lean toward the south or southwest, which is why hikers call it the compass barrel."}}],"img":"barrel-cactus"},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the prickly pear. This cactus is built out of flat pads, with edible fruit and edible pads, and fine hairs that get into your skin and stay there."},{"t":"fact","text":"The flat green pads are not leaves. They are stems, the plant's trunk and branches flattened out. Young pads, called nopales, are eaten in tacos and with eggs.","label":"Not leaves"},{"t":"term","word":"glochids","text":"Fine, barbed hairs around the spines that come off the plant and lodge in skin."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What are the flat green pads of a prickly pear?","options":["Leaves, folded flat","Its trunk and branches","Fruit, grown very large"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. It tells what the pads really are.","explain":"The pads are not leaves. They are the plant's trunk and branches, flattened out."}}],"img":"prickly-pear"},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the aloe vera. This succulent has thick, spiky leaves full of clear gel. It grows on windowsills worldwide and wild in almost nowhere."},{"t":"fact","text":"Aloe vera is not a cactus. It is a succulent, closer kin to a daylily than to anything with spines. Cacti are almost all American. Aloes are African and Arabian.","label":"Not a cactus"},{"t":"p","text":"Cut a leaf across and you get two things: the clear gel in the middle, and a thin layer of bitter yellow sap just under the green skin."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is just under the green skin of an aloe leaf?","options":["A layer of clear gel","A row of tiny seeds","A bitter yellow sap"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. It says what lies just under the skin.","explain":"Under the green skin is a thin layer of bitter yellow sap. The clear gel is in the middle."}}],"img":"aloe"},{"icon":"📊","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Three desert survivors, and no two are alike. The barrel cactus leans south. The prickly pear is a pile of flat stems. The aloe is not a cactus at all."},{"t":"fact","text":"A fishhook barrel usually stands 3 to 6 ft (0.9 to 1.8 m) tall. An aloe vera plant stands about 2 to 3 ft (60 to 100 cm) tall. A prickly pear pad runs from hand-sized to dinner-plate size.","label":"Sizes"}]},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A barrel cactus leaning south. A prickly pear of flat stems. An aloe with gel in its leaves."},{"t":"p","text":"Three desert survivors, each different."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Barrel cactus","Leans south and carries hooked spines"],["Prickly pear","Flat pads with fine barbed hairs"],["Aloe vera","Succulent with clear gel in its leaves"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why is the fishhook barrel named that?","options":["It grows beside rivers full of fish","Its spines are curved like fish hooks","It is shaped like a fishing reel"],"answer":1,"explain":"The spines are thick, stiff and curved back on themselves, like fish hooks."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What are glochids?","options":["Sweet red fruits that people eat","Fine, barbed hairs that lodge in skin","Flat green pads that hold water"],"answer":1,"explain":"Glochids are fine barbed hairs that come off the plant and stay in skin."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What do people call young prickly pear pads that they eat?","options":["Nopales","Tortillas","Glochids"],"answer":0,"explain":"Young pads are called nopales, and they are eaten in tacos and with eggs."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is the aloe closer kin to?","options":["A barrel cactus","A prickly pear","A daylily"],"answer":2,"explain":"Aloe is a succulent, closer kin to a daylily than to anything with spines."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How do desert hikers use a mature barrel cactus?","options":["As a shade from the sun","As a rough compass","As a jug of water"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lean is steady enough to take a rough bearing from, so hikers call it the compass barrel."}]},{"id":"plants-crops-1","course":"plants","kind":"species","group":"crops","title":"Wheat, Rice & Maize","names":["Wheat","Rice","Maize","Potato","Tomato"],"glyph":"🌾","tint":"#ffb04a","items":["wheat","rice","maize","potato","tomato"],"idea":"Five crops feed billions of people, and each one has a surprising way of growing.","pages":[{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Five plants are here to meet you: wheat, rice, maize, potato, and tomato."},{"t":"p","text":"Each one has its own way of growing and helping people."}]},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet wheat, a grass grown for its seeds. It covers more land than any other food crop."},{"t":"fact","text":"In 2024, the world grew about 880 million U.S. tons (about 800 million metric tons) of wheat on 542 million acres (219.5 million hectares). Russia sold the most wheat to other countries, about 32.7 million tons.","label":"The world's top crop"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which country sold the most wheat to other countries in 2024?","options":["Russia","India","China"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the second sentence of the fact box.","explain":"Russia sold the most wheat to other countries, about 32.7 million tons."}}],"img":"wheat"},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet rice, a grass that grows standing in water. It's the main food for more than half of the people alive."},{"t":"fact","text":"Rice has hollow stems that pipe air down to its roots. Most plants would drown in flooded mud, but rice thrives.","label":"Water wonder"}],"img":"rice"},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Next up is maize, which Americans call corn, a giant grass that grows taller than an adult and carries its seeds on a cob."},{"t":"fact","text":"The silk on a cob is not just decoration. There is exactly one silk thread for each kernel, and a grain of pollen has to land on that thread and grow down it. A cob with gaps in the rows means some threads got missed.","label":"Silk surprise"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does the silk thread do on a maize cob?","options":["It's just decoration.","It holds the kernels together.","It guides pollen to each kernel."],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It explains what the silk threads are for.","explain":"The silk thread guides pollen to each kernel."}}],"img":"maize"},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the potato, a swollen underground stem from the Andes and the most important food crop in the world that isn't a grain."},{"t":"fact","text":"Each eye on a potato is a bud, and if you leave a potato for a few weeks, shoots will come out of those eyes.","label":"The eyes have it"},{"t":"p","text":"Potatoes grow at the tips of stolons, which are underground stems running sideways from the base of the plant. One plant can make several potatoes, which is why you often find a whole nest of them under a single plant."},{"t":"term","word":"stolon","text":"An underground stem that runs sideways from a plant and grows new plants at its tip."}],"img":"potato"},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the tomato, a berry from South America that most people call a vegetable."},{"t":"fact","text":"In 1893, the U.S. Supreme Court said tomatoes are vegetables, even though they are really fruits. A law of that time charged a tax on vegetables brought in from other countries, but not on fruits. The court said tomatoes are eaten like vegetables, so the tax applied.","label":"The Supreme Court ruling"},{"t":"p","text":"The fruit is juicy and packed with seeds that can survive being eaten by an animal."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why did the Supreme Court say tomatoes are vegetables?","options":["They grow underground like potatoes","They are eaten like vegetables","They have no seeds inside"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the last sentence of the fact box. It says how the court said tomatoes are eaten.","explain":"The court said tomatoes are eaten like vegetables, so the vegetable tax applied, even though botanically they are fruits."}}],"img":"tomato"},{"icon":"📊","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Farmers plant all five fresh every year: wheat, rice, maize, potato, and tomato."},{"t":"fact","text":"Farmers plant all five fresh each year as annuals, which are plants that finish their whole life in one growing season. Potatoes and tomatoes could live longer in warm places, but farmers start them over every year.","label":"Common ground"}]},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Wheat in a field. Rice growing tall. Maize with yellow ears. Potatoes underground. Tomatoes on a vine."},{"t":"p","text":"Five plants, and each one grows in a way that surprises people."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Wheat","Covers more land than any other food crop"],["Rice","Has hollow stems to pipe air to its roots"],["Maize","Each silk thread guides pollen to a kernel"],["Potato","Eyes are buds that can grow shoots"],["Tomato","Botanically a fruit but taxed as a vegetable"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is the main reason rice can grow in flooded mud?","options":["Rice has waxy leaves that shed the water like a coat.","Rice has hollow stems that pipe air to its roots.","Rice has long roots that reach down to dry ground."],"answer":1,"explain":"This special feature lets rice thrive where other plants would drown."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many silks are there on a maize cob for each kernel?","options":["Only one silk thread for every two kernels","Exactly one silk thread for each kernel","Several silk threads for each single kernel"],"answer":1,"explain":"Each silk guides pollen to its corresponding kernel, ensuring the cob fills out fully."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What do the eyes on a potato actually do?","options":["They are marks left by insect bites.","They are buds from which shoots can grow.","They take in sunlight to feed the potato."],"answer":1,"explain":"These buds can sprout new plants when conditions are right."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How long is the life cycle of an annual crop like wheat, rice, maize, potato, or tomato?","options":["Two years","One year","Five years"],"answer":1,"explain":"All these plants are annuals, finishing their entire life cycle in just one growing season."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where do potatoes grow on the plant?","options":["On branches high above the ground","At the tips of underground stems","At the very ends of the long roots"],"answer":1,"explain":"Potatoes form at the ends of stolons, which spread out below the soil."}]},{"id":"plants-crops-2","course":"plants","kind":"species","group":"crops","title":"Banana, Coffee & Vanilla orchid","names":["Banana","Coffee","Vanilla orchid","Pineapple","Strawberry"],"glyph":"🌾","tint":"#ffb04a","items":["banana","coffee","vanilla","pineapple","strawberry"],"idea":"The vanilla orchid relies on a twelve-year-old boy’s discovery to be pollinated. Banana, coffee, pineapple, and strawberry each hide a surprise about what we are really eating.","pages":[{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Five plants are waiting to meet you: a banana, coffee, vanilla orchid, pineapple, and strawberry."},{"t":"p","text":"Each one has something special about it."}]},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the banana. It’s not a tree or a bush. It's the largest herb on Earth, which means a plant with no wood in its stem. The bananas we eat are berries with no seeds."},{"t":"fact","text":"What looks like a trunk is a pseudostem made of tightly wrapped leaf stalks. There’s no wood in it at all — you can cut one through with a knife.","label":"The giant herb"},{"t":"term","word":"pseudostem","text":"A structure that looks like a stem but is made of tightly wrapped leaf stalks, not wood."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What makes the banana plant so unusual?","options":["It's a tall palm with a trunk of hard wood","It's a woody vine that climbs up trees","It's a giant herb with no wood inside"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the third sentence of the paragraph and at the fact box.","explain":"The banana plant is a giant herb with no wood inside."}}],"img":"banana"},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the coffee plant, a tropical shrub grown for its seeds inside small red fruits called cherries."},{"t":"fact","text":"Each cherry usually holds two flat-sided seeds, but about 5 to 10 percent hold just one round seed called a peaberry.","label":"The seeds inside"},{"t":"p","text":"Coffee leaves and seeds contain caffeine, a bitter chemical that keeps many insects from eating the plant. It is the same caffeine that helps people feel awake in the morning."}],"img":"coffee"},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the vanilla orchid, the best-known orchid grown for food. It is a climbing vine that can grow up to 50 ft (15 m) long."},{"t":"fact","text":"A vanilla flower lasts about one day. If you miss it, the flower drops off and no pod grows.","label":"The flower's brief life"},{"t":"p","text":"Outside Mexico, the insect that pollinates vanilla does not live there. In 1841, a twelve-year-old boy named Edmond Albius figured out how to pollinate vanilla flowers by hand on the island of Reunion."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Who worked out how to pollinate vanilla by hand?","options":["Edmond Albius","George Washington Carver","An orchid bee"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the last paragraph. It mentions the boy's name and what he did.","explain":"Edmond Albius, a twelve-year-old boy, figured out how to pollinate vanilla flowers by hand."}}],"img":"vanilla"},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the pineapple. It's not one fruit but up to two hundred flowers grown into one lump."},{"t":"fact","text":"Every diamond-shaped eye on a pineapple is where one flower used to be. They all fused together to make the fruit.","label":"The flower fusion"}],"img":"pineapple"},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the strawberry. The red part you eat is not the fruit, and those specks on top are not seeds."},{"t":"fact","text":"Each speck on a strawberry is an achene, a dry fruit with one seed inside it. The red flesh is the swollen tip of the flower stalk.","label":"The tiny fruits"},{"t":"p","text":"A medium strawberry carries around 200 achenes — tiny one-seeded fruits — on its surface. So a strawberry is about two hundred fruits sitting on a red cushion."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many achenes does a medium strawberry carry?","options":["50","200","500"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the last paragraph. It gives the number of tiny fruits.","explain":"A medium strawberry carries around 200 achenes on its surface."}}],"img":"strawberry"},{"icon":"📊","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Each of these five plants gives us a different part to eat: a fruit, a seed, a pod, a fused cluster of flowers, or a swollen stalk tip."},{"t":"fact","text":"A banana gives us its peelable fruit. Coffee its seeds ground into a drink. Vanilla its orchid pod. Pineapple its fused flowers. The strawberry its red cushion of achenes.","label":"Unique parts"}]},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A banana's peelable fruit. Coffee seeds ground into a drink. Vanilla's orchid pod. A pineapple's spiky fruit. A strawberry's red cushion of achenes."},{"t":"p","text":"Five plants, each giving us something unique to eat."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Banana","Largest herb with no wood inside"],["Coffee","Grown for seeds inside red cherries"],["Vanilla orchid","Edmond Albius figured out hand pollination"],["Pineapple","Up to two hundred flowers fused into one lump"],["Strawberry","200 achenes on a red cushion"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is a vanilla flower's lifespan?","options":["Two days","One day","Three days"],"answer":1,"explain":"A vanilla flower only blooms for one day, so you have to be quick."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What part of the coffee plant do we use to make coffee?","options":["The leaves","The seeds","The stem"],"answer":1,"explain":"Coffee is made from the seeds inside the small red fruits called cherries."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is the longest a vanilla orchid vine can grow?","options":["20 ft (6 m)","50 ft (15 m)","30 ft (9 m)"],"answer":1,"explain":"The vanilla orchid vine can grow up to 50 feet long, climbing other plants or structures."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does each diamond-shaped eye on a pineapple represent?","options":["A leaf","A fused flower","A seed"],"answer":1,"explain":"Each eye on a pineapple is where one flower used to be before they all fused together."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What looks like the trunk of a banana plant but isn't made of wood?","options":["The fruit","The pseudostem","The roots"],"answer":1,"explain":"The banana plant’s “trunk” is actually a pseudostem made of tightly wrapped leaf stalks, not wood."}]},{"id":"plants-crops-3","course":"plants","kind":"species","group":"crops","title":"Pumpkin, Sugarcane & Peanut","names":["Pumpkin","Sugarcane","Peanut","Chilli pepper","Grapevine"],"glyph":"🌾","tint":"#ffb04a","items":["pumpkin","sugarcane","peanut","chilli","grape"],"idea":"A pumpkin can weigh over 2,800 pounds. Find out how one plant buries its own fruit, how a chili pepper tricks your nerves, and how a vine climbs without eyes.","pages":[{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Five plants are waiting to meet you: a pumpkin, sugarcane, peanuts, chili peppers, and grapevines."},{"t":"p","text":"Each one has a secret that makes them special."}]},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Start with the pumpkin, a sprawling vine that grows the heaviest fruit on Earth. The record pumpkin weighed 2,819 lb (1,278.8 kg), nicknamed Muggle."},{"t":"fact","text":"Grown by twin brothers Ian and Stuart Paton, it was weighed at Wargrave in Berkshire, England, on October 6, 2025.","label":"The giant"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How much did the heaviest pumpkin weigh?","options":["2,907.85 lb","1,000 lb","2,819 lb"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the first paragraph. It gives the exact weight.","explain":"The heaviest pumpkin weighed 2,819 lb."}}],"img":"pumpkin"},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the sugarcane, a towering grass that grows up to 20 ft (6 m) tall with sugar stored in its stem."},{"t":"fact","text":"Sugarcane is the largest crop in the world by weight: 1.9 billion metric tons in 2020. In 2022, Brazil grew about 40 percent of the world's sugarcane, 724.4 million metric tons, with India next at 439.4 million.","label":"The giant crop"},{"t":"term","word":"ratoon","text":"New growth from a plant’s stump after the main stalk is cut. Sugarcane regrows this way and can be harvested several times."}],"img":"sugarcane"},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the peanut, a low bushy plant about 1 to 2 ft (30-60 cm) high. It's not a nut but a legume."},{"t":"fact","text":"The flower does its work above ground and then sends the job underground. A stalk called a peg turns downward, grows into the soil, and pushes the pod beneath it.","label":"The hidden pod"},{"t":"p","text":"This process of burying its own fruit is called geocarpy. Very few plants do this."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is the word for a plant burying its own fruit underground?","options":["Pea pod","Geocarpy","Peg"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the last paragraph. It explains the term.","explain":"The term is geocarpy."}}],"img":"peanut"},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the chili pepper, a bushy plant about 2 to 3 ft (60-90 cm) high whose fruits can be tiny or very long, depending on the kind."},{"t":"fact","text":"The burn comes from capsaicin, which tricks your nerves into feeling heat when nothing is actually hot. It's mostly in the pale ribs inside, not in the seeds.","label":"The spicy trick"},{"t":"p","text":"Birds eat chilies without feeling the burn, but mammals feel it strongly."}],"img":"chilli"},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the grapevine, a woody climber that hauls itself up trees using coiled tendrils and carries its fruit in bunches of berries."},{"t":"fact","text":"A tendril grows out, touches something, wraps around it, then coils itself into a spring. It does all this without eyes.","label":"The climbing trick"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How does a grapevine tendril find and grip a branch?","options":["It grows out, touches something, wraps around it, and coils into a spring","It uses its leaves to feel around","It has tiny eyes to spot branches"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the fact box. It describes how the tendril works.","explain":"The tendril grows out, touches something, wraps around it, and coils into a spring."}}],"img":"grape"},{"icon":"📊","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The record pumpkin weighed 2,819 lb, the heaviest fruit ever grown."},{"t":"fact","text":"The peanut and pumpkin last one year. The chili pepper lives for three years. Grapevines can live much longer, with some reaching more than 100 years and a few more than 400.","label":"Lifespans"}]},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A pumpkin that can grow up to 2,819 lb. Sugarcane that regrows after it is cut. Peanuts that last a year. Chili peppers that live for three years. Grapevines living more than 100 years."},{"t":"p","text":"Five plants, each built for its own life span and growth."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Pumpkin","Weighs up to 2,819 lb"],["Sugarcane","Grows up to 20 feet tall"],["Peanut","Buries its fruit underground (geocarpy)"],["Chili pepper","Tricks your nerves into feeling heat"],["Grapevine","Uses coiled tendrils to climb"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is the greatest height sugarcane reaches?","options":["10 feet","30 feet","20 feet"],"answer":2,"explain":"Sugarcane is a towering grass that grows up to 20 feet tall."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is capsaicin?","options":["A type of seed in chili peppers","The part of a chili pepper that makes it sweet","The substance that tricks your nerves into feeling heat"],"answer":2,"explain":"Capsaicin is what gives chili peppers their spicy kick."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"About how long do the oldest grapevines live?","options":["10 years","50 years","More than 100 years"],"answer":2,"explain":"Some grapevines are more than 100 years old."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What term describes peanuts burying their fruit underground?","options":["Pea pod","Peg","Geocarpy"],"answer":2,"explain":"Very few plants do this unique process of geocarpy."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many metric tons of sugarcane did Brazil grow in 2022?","options":["500 million metric tons","600 million metric tons","724.4 million metric tons"],"answer":2,"explain":"Brazil leads the world in sugarcane production."}]},{"id":"plants-water-1","course":"plants","kind":"species","group":"water","title":"Giant kelp, Duckweed & Papyrus","names":["Giant kelp","Duckweed","Papyrus","Seagrass"],"glyph":"🌊","tint":"#4fd6e8","items":["giant-kelp","duckweed","papyrus","seagrass"],"idea":"Giant kelp can grow two feet in one day. Duckweed is the smallest flowering plant. Papyrus became the first paper. Meet four plants of the water, and one that is not a plant.","pages":[{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Four plants of the water are waiting for you: a giant kelp, a duckweed, a papyrus and a seagrass."},{"t":"p","text":"One of them is not a plant at all. Which one would you most like to meet first?"}]},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the giant kelp. This brown seaweed grows in underwater forests off rocky coasts. It runs from the seabed to the surface, and it is not a plant."},{"t":"fact","text":"Giant kelp can grow two feet (60 cm) in a single day, among the fastest-growing living things anywhere.","label":"Fast growing"},{"t":"p","text":"Divers have measured it at more than 150 ft (45 m) from the rock it grips to the surface. Its holdfast, the knobbly tangle at the bottom, is only for grip."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How much can giant kelp grow in one day?","options":["About two inches (5 cm)","About two feet (60 cm)","About two yards (180 cm)"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. It tells how much kelp grows in a day.","explain":"Giant kelp can grow about two feet (60 cm) in a single day."}}],"img":"giant-kelp"},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the duckweed. These are the smallest flowering plants there are. They are green specks that cover a still pond so completely it looks like spilled paint."},{"t":"fact","text":"One watermeal, the smallest duckweed, is about 0.6 mm long, small enough to pass through the eye of a sewing needle. One kind doubles itself in 29.3 hours, the fastest growth measured in any flowering plant.","label":"Tiny and fast"},{"t":"term","word":"watermeal","text":"The smallest kind of duckweed, a floating green crumb with no roots at all."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"About how long does it take one kind of duckweed to double itself?","options":["About 29 days","About 29 hours","About 29 minutes"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. It tells how fast the duckweed doubles.","explain":"One duckweed doubles itself in 29.3 hours, the fastest growth measured in any flowering plant."}}],"img":"duckweed"},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the papyrus. This giant African wetland sedge had its stem sliced, layered and pressed into the world's first paper."},{"t":"fact","text":"Roll a papyrus stem between your fingers and you feel three flat sides. It is a triangle, not a tube. Stems of 13 to 16 ft (4 to 5 m) are ordinary.","label":"Three sides"},{"t":"p","text":"To make a sheet, strips of the white pith are laid side by side, with a second layer across them at right angles, and hammered together."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many flat sides does a papyrus stem have?","options":["Four flat sides","No sides, it is round","Three flat sides"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the box. It describes the shape of the stem.","explain":"A papyrus stem is a triangle, not a tube, so it has three flat sides."}}],"img":"papyrus"},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the seagrass. This true flowering plant lives underwater in the sea. It flowers, sheds pollen and sets seed without ever coming up for air."},{"t":"fact","text":"Seagrass is not seaweed. It has roots, a creeping underground stem, veined leaves, flowers, pollen and seeds.","label":"Not a seaweed"},{"t":"p","text":"One seagrass meadow in Shark Bay, Western Australia, covers about 77 square miles (200 km2). Shoots 110 miles (180 km) apart turned out to be the same plant."}],"img":"seagrass"},{"icon":"📊","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Four water plants, and one is not a plant at all. The giant kelp is an alga. The duckweed is the smallest flowering plant. The papyrus became paper. The seagrass flowers underwater."},{"t":"fact","text":"Giant kelp can reach more than 150 ft (45 m). Papyrus stems are 13 to 16 ft (4 to 5 m). A watermeal is about 0.6 mm long. A seagrass meadow can cover 77 square miles (200 km2).","label":"Sizes"}]},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A kelp forest reaching for the light. A duckweed smaller than a needle's eye. A papyrus stem pressed into paper. A seagrass meadow flowering under the sea."},{"t":"p","text":"Four plants of the water, each different."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Giant kelp","Brown seaweed that is not a plant"],["Duckweed","Smallest flowering plant, a floating green speck"],["Papyrus","Stem pressed into the first paper"],["Seagrass","Flowering plant that lives underwater"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is the holdfast of giant kelp for?","options":["Taking in water like a root","Only for grip","Making its flowers"],"answer":1,"explain":"A holdfast absorbs nothing. Its only job is to grip the rock."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How small is a watermeal?","options":["It is as big as a small coin","It fits through a needle's eye","It is as big as your whole hand"],"answer":1,"explain":"A watermeal is about 0.6 mm long, small enough to pass through the eye of a sewing needle."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What was papyrus pressed into?","options":["Bricks for building houses","Cloth for making clothes","The world's first paper"],"answer":2,"explain":"The stem was sliced, layered and pressed into the world's first paper."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why is seagrass not a seaweed?","options":["It is a flowering plant with roots and seeds","It lives only on dry sandy beaches","It is a kind of alga with no roots"],"answer":0,"explain":"Seagrass has roots, veined leaves, flowers, pollen and seeds, the full kit of a flowering plant."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is the largest known living thing on Earth by area?","options":["A kelp forest","A duckweed pond","A seagrass meadow"],"answer":2,"explain":"One seagrass in Shark Bay, Western Australia, covers about 77 square miles (200 km2)."}]},{"id":"plants-moss-1","course":"plants","kind":"species","group":"moss","title":"Sphagnum moss, Lichen & more","names":["Sphagnum moss","Lichen","Common moss"],"glyph":"🍃","tint":"#8fd18f","items":["sphagnum","lichen","moss"],"idea":"Sphagnum can hold 16 to 26 times its own weight in water. A lichen is two living things in one body. A moss has no roots at all. Meet three small green things.","pages":[{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Three small green things are waiting for you: a sphagnum moss, a lichen and a common moss."},{"t":"p","text":"Each one is built in its own way. Which one would you most like to meet first?"}]},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the sphagnum moss. This bog moss is built like a sponge. It soaks up water, turns the ground sour, and slowly turns into peat."},{"t":"fact","text":"Sphagnum can hold 16 to 26 times its own dry weight in water. Lift a handful out of a bog and it runs like a squeezed sponge.","label":"A living sponge"},{"t":"p","text":"Most of a leaf is dead on purpose. Big empty cells hold the water, and the living green cells make the food. Peat is mostly dead sphagnum that never rotted."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How much water can sphagnum hold?","options":["Only half of its dry weight","16 to 26 times its dry weight","A few drops of water at most"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. It gives the number of times its own weight.","explain":"Sphagnum can hold 16 to 26 times its own dry weight in water."}}],"img":"sphagnum"},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the lichen. It is not a plant at all. A fungus and a green partner live together as one organism, growing on bare rock where nothing else will start."},{"t":"fact","text":"The fungus makes the structure and catches the water. The alga inside it makes the food. Lichens grow on bare rock, roof tiles and tree bark.","label":"Two in one"},{"t":"term","word":"alga","text":"The green partner in a lichen, which makes the food."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does the fungus do in a lichen?","options":["Makes the food from the sunlight","Makes the structure, catches water","Grows the flowers and the seeds"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. It says what the fungus does for the lichen.","explain":"The fungus makes the structure and catches the water. The alga inside it makes the food."}}],"img":"lichen"},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the common moss. This plant has no flowers, no seeds and no proper roots. It covers damp ground, walls and rock in a low green mat."},{"t":"fact","text":"The hairs holding it down are called rhizoids, and they are only for grip. Water and food go in through the leaves, across the whole surface at once.","label":"No roots"},{"t":"p","text":"Moss makes spores, not seeds. The green cushion sends up a bare stalk with a capsule on top. A moss can dry to a brown crisp and stay that way for months. Water it and it turns green within hours."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does a moss make instead of seeds?","options":["Fruit with pips","Spores in a capsule","Flowers with petals"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the last paragraph. It says what the moss makes.","explain":"Moss makes spores in a capsule on a bare stalk, not seeds."}}],"img":"moss"},{"icon":"📊","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Three small green things, and each is different. Sphagnum holds water like a sponge. A lichen is a fungus and a green partner living as one. Moss is a true plant with no roots, flowers or seeds."},{"t":"fact","text":"Most mosses stand between a tenth of an inch and 4 in (0.2 to 10 cm) tall. The tallest of all reaches about 20 in (50 cm). A crust lichen spreads by 1 to 2 mm a year.","label":"Sizes"}]},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A sphagnum sponge in a bog. A lichen on bare rock. A moss mat on damp ground."},{"t":"p","text":"Three small green things, each different."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Sphagnum moss","Soaks up water like a sponge"],["Lichen","A fungus and a green partner in one body"],["Common moss","Has no flowers, seeds or proper roots"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is peat mostly made of?","options":["Crushed rock mixed with sand","Old burned wood from forests","Dead sphagnum that never rotted"],"answer":2,"explain":"Peat is mostly dead sphagnum that never rotted."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What two living things make one lichen?","options":["A fungus and an alga","A moss and a fern","A seed and a root"],"answer":0,"explain":"A lichen is a fungus and an alga living inside one body."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where can lichens grow?","options":["Only in rich garden soil","On bare rock and tree bark","Only underwater"],"answer":1,"explain":"Lichens grow on bare rock, roof tiles and tree bark."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What do a moss's rhizoids do?","options":["Take in water for it","Only grip the ground","Make the moss spores"],"answer":1,"explain":"The hairs that hold a moss down are only for grip. Water and food go in through the leaves."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What happens when you water a dried-out moss?","options":["It crumbles away into brown dust","It grows bright flowers right away","It turns green again within hours"],"answer":2,"explain":"A moss can dry to a brown crisp for months, then turn green and start working again within hours."}]},{"id":"plants-ferns-1","course":"plants","kind":"species","group":"ferns","title":"Tree fern, Bracken & Horsetail","names":["Tree fern","Bracken","Horsetail"],"glyph":"🌿","tint":"#6fbf73","items":["tree-fern","bracken","horsetail"],"idea":"A tree fern's trunk has no wood in it. Bracken spreads under a whole hillside. Horsetail is stiff with real grit. Meet three ferns and fern kin that never make a flower.","pages":[{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Three plants with no flowers are waiting for you: a tree fern, a bracken and a horsetail."},{"t":"p","text":"Each one has its own odd habit. Which one would you most like to meet first?"}]},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the tree fern. This fern stands on a trunk like a tree, but the trunk is not wood. It is an upright stem wrapped in its own roots."},{"t":"fact","text":"There is no wood in the trunk at all. What holds it up is stiff cell walls and a thick felt of tiny roots grown down around it.","label":"Not wood"},{"t":"p","text":"The Norfolk Island tree fern reaches about 66 ft (20 m), with fronds 16 ft (5 m) long. It is probably the largest fern in the world."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What holds a tree fern's trunk up?","options":["A core of solid wood","Stiff cells and tiny roots","Hard, thick bark outside"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. It tells what holds the trunk up.","explain":"There is no wood in the trunk. Stiff cell walls and a thick felt of tiny roots hold it up."}}],"img":"tree-fern"},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the bracken. This coarse fern spreads by underground stems and takes over whole hillsides. It is one of the most widespread plants there is."},{"t":"fact","text":"The fronds are the small end of it. Its underground stem is about 2 in (5 cm) thick and runs up to 50 ft (15 m) sideways. Bracken is poisonous to cattle and sheep that eat enough of it.","label":"Underground"},{"t":"term","word":"rhizome","text":"An underground stem that runs sideways below the ground."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How far sideways can bracken's underground stem run?","options":["Only about 5 in (13 cm)","As far as 50 ft (15 m)","More than 500 ft (150 m)"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. It gives how far the underground stem runs.","explain":"The underground stem is about 2 in (5 cm) thick and runs up to 50 ft (15 m) sideways."}}],"img":"bracken"},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the horsetail. This jointed plant has hollow stems stiffened with real grit. People used it for centuries to scour pots and polish wood."},{"t":"fact","text":"Rub a horsetail stem and it is rough, like fine sandpaper. The plant loads silica, the stuff of sand and glass, into its stem.","label":"Gritty stems"},{"t":"p","text":"The stems are hollow and jointed, and they pull apart at the joints with a small pop."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What makes a horsetail stem rough like sandpaper?","options":["Tiny thorns along each stem","Silica, the stuff of glass","A coat of dried-up salt"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. It names what the plant loads into its stem.","explain":"The plant loads silica, the stuff of sand and glass, into its stem, so it feels like fine sandpaper."}}],"img":"horsetail"},{"icon":"📊","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Three green plants with no flowers and no seeds. The tree fern stands on a trunk with no wood in it. The bracken spreads far under the ground. The horsetail is stiff with grit. The tree fern and the horsetail make spores instead of seeds."},{"t":"fact","text":"The Norfolk Island tree fern reaches about 66 ft (20 m). Bracken fronds stand 1 to 3.5 ft (0.3 to 1 m) tall. Most horsetails are knee-high.","label":"Sizes"}]},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A tree fern on its felt of roots. A bracken taking a hillside. A gritty horsetail."},{"t":"p","text":"Three green plants with no flowers, each with its own odd habit."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Tree fern","Stands on a trunk that is not wood"],["Bracken","Spreads far underground over hillsides"],["Horsetail","Jointed stems stiff with grit"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is a tree fern's trunk made of?","options":["Solid, hard wood like an oak tree","Leaves rolled tightly into a tube","An upright stem wrapped in roots"],"answer":2,"explain":"The trunk is not wood. It is an upright stem wrapped in a thick felt of its own roots."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How do tree ferns and horsetails spread, since they have no seeds?","options":["By tiny spores","By flower pollen","By juicy fruit"],"answer":0,"explain":"They have no flowers, seeds or fruit. They make spores instead."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What happens to cattle and sheep that eat a lot of bracken?","options":["They grow much stronger","Nothing happens at all","They get seriously ill"],"answer":2,"explain":"Bracken is poisonous to cattle and sheep that eat enough of it."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What was horsetail used for?","options":["Making sweet syrup for pancakes","Weaving cloth and thick rope","Scouring pots and polishing wood"],"answer":2,"explain":"The silica grit in its stems made it good for scouring pots and polishing wood."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How tall is the Norfolk Island tree fern?","options":["About 6 ft (2 m) tall","About 66 ft (20 m) tall","About 600 ft (180 m) tall"],"answer":1,"explain":"The Norfolk Island tree fern reaches about 66 ft (20 m), probably the largest fern in the world."}]},{"id":"plants-fungi-1","course":"plants","kind":"species","group":"fungi","title":"Fly agaric, Honey fungus & more","names":["Fly agaric","Honey fungus","Stinkhorn","Giant puffball","Bracket fungus","Truffle"],"glyph":"🍄","tint":"#ff7a6b","items":["fly-agaric","honey-fungus","stinkhorn","puffball","bracket-fungus","truffle"],"idea":"A fly agaric is not a plant. A honey fungus covers 3.5 square miles of forest. A stinkhorn smells like rotting meat on purpose. Meet six fungi.","pages":[{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Six fungi are waiting for you: a fly agaric, a honey fungus, a stinkhorn, a giant puffball, a bracket fungus and a truffle."},{"t":"p","text":"Not one of them is a plant. Which one would you most like to meet first?"}]},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the fly agaric. This is the storybook toadstool, with a scarlet cap and white flecks, growing under birches and pines. It is not a plant at all."},{"t":"fact","text":"A fungus has no leaves and no green, and it never makes food from sunlight. What you see is only the fruiting body, the spore-making part.","label":"Not a plant"},{"t":"p","text":"The white flecks are not painted on. A young fly agaric comes up wrapped in a white skin, and as the cap swells the skin tears into patches. It is poisonous, so never taste one."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where do the white flecks on a fly agaric come from?","options":["Drops of white paint","A white skin that tears","Bits of chalky stone"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the last paragraph. It tells what the flecks are.","explain":"A young fly agaric comes up wrapped in a white skin, and the skin tears into patches as the cap swells."}}],"img":"fly-agaric"},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the honey fungus. This tree-killing fungus spreads underground on black bootlaces. In Oregon, one has grown into one of the largest single living things known."},{"t":"fact","text":"In the Malheur National Forest in eastern Oregon, one honey fungus covers 3.5 square miles (9.1 km²). That is about 1,600 football fields.","label":"A giant"},{"t":"term","word":"rhizomorph","text":"A tough black cord of fungus, like a bootlace, that runs under bark and through soil."},{"t":"p","text":"The honey-colored mushrooms at the foot of a tree are only the fruit. The fungus itself is a web of fine threads, hidden in the wood and soil."}],"img":"honey-fungus"},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the stinkhorn. This fungus smells like rotting meat on purpose, because it sends its spores out by fly instead of by wind."},{"t":"fact","text":"It starts as a white egg buried in leaf litter. The stalk then pushes up about 5 mm a minute, a fifth of an inch, quick enough to see against a marked stick.","label":"Fast growing"},{"t":"p","text":"The slime on the cap smells of dead animal and dung. Flies land, feed, and leave carrying the spores on their feet."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why does a stinkhorn smell like rotting meat?","options":["To scare off deer","To attract flies","To keep rain away"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the last paragraph. It tells who visits the smelly slime.","explain":"The smell brings flies. They land, feed and carry the spores away on their feet."}}],"img":"stinkhorn"},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the giant puffball. This smooth white ball sits in a meadow and, when it ripens, turns entirely into spore dust."},{"t":"fact","text":"They run from 4 to 20 in (10 to 50 cm) across. One found at Thunder Bay in Ontario weighed 51 lb (23 kg).","label":"Big ones"},{"t":"p","text":"A ripe one falls apart at a touch. A kick sends up a cloud like a bag of soot, and that cloud is the spores leaving."}],"img":"puffball"},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the bracket fungus. These are shelves of fungus growing straight out of tree trunks, some as hard as the wood itself, adding a fresh layer every year."},{"t":"fact","text":"Turn one over. Instead of gills there are pores, thousands of tiny tube mouths packed together like a honeycomb, and the spores fall out of the tubes.","label":"Pores"},{"t":"p","text":"Some brackets live on year after year, adding a fresh layer of tubes each season. The layers show as rings, and can be counted like tree rings."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What can you count on an old bracket fungus?","options":["Leaves along the top","Seeds in the pores","The rings of its layers"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the last paragraph. It says what shows on top of the layers.","explain":"Each season adds a fresh layer, and the layers show as rings that can be counted like tree rings."}}],"img":"bracket-fungus"},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the truffle. This fungus fruits underground, smells strongly enough to be dug up, and is the most expensive food on earth by weight."},{"t":"fact","text":"Pigs were the old method, but a pig that finds a truffle wants to eat it. Italy banned truffle pigs in 1985, so dogs are trained instead.","label":"Found by nose"},{"t":"p","text":"Voles, squirrels, deer and wild boar dig truffles up and eat them, and the spores come out somewhere else."}],"img":"truffle"},{"icon":"📊","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Six fungi, and not one is a plant. The fly agaric wears white flecks. The honey fungus is huge. The stinkhorn smells to call flies. The puffball turns to dust. The bracket makes shelves. The truffle hides underground."},{"t":"fact","text":"A fly agaric cap is 2 to 12 in (5 to 30 cm) across. A giant puffball is 4 to 20 in (10 to 50 cm) across. An artist's conk can reach 30 in (75 cm). One truffle weighed 4 lb 3 oz (1.89 kg).","label":"Sizes"}]},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A scarlet toadstool with white flecks. A honey fungus spreading through a forest. A stinkhorn calling the flies. A puffball full of dust. A bracket shelf on a trunk. A truffle underground."},{"t":"p","text":"Six fungi, and not one of them is a plant."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Fly agaric","Scarlet toadstool with white flecks"],["Honey fungus","Tree killer that spreads on black cords"],["Stinkhorn","Smells like rotting meat to call flies"],["Giant puffball","White ball that turns to spore dust"],["Bracket fungus","Shelves growing out of tree trunks"],["Truffle","Fungus that fruits underground"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why is a fly agaric not a plant?","options":["It has no stem or cap at all","It grows only in the winter months","It never makes food from sunlight"],"answer":2,"explain":"A fungus has no green and never makes a scrap of food from sunlight."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How much forest does the honey fungus in Oregon cover?","options":["About 3.5 square miles","About 3.5 square feet","About 3.5 acres"],"answer":0,"explain":"One honey fungus in the Malheur National Forest covers 3.5 square miles (9.1 km²)."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How does a stinkhorn spread its spores?","options":["The wind blows them off","Flies carry them away","Rain washes them off"],"answer":1,"explain":"Flies land, feed and leave carrying the spores on their feet."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What happens when you kick a ripe giant puffball?","options":["It bounces like a ball","A cloud of spores puffs out","It splits into thin slices"],"answer":1,"explain":"A ripe puffball is brown powder inside, and a kick sends up a cloud like soot."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How are truffles usually found?","options":["By their bright color","By their loud noise","By trained dogs sniffing"],"answer":2,"explain":"Dogs are trained to find truffles by smell, because pigs wreck the fungus digging."}]},{"id":"plants-odd-1","course":"plants","kind":"species","group":"odd","title":"Sensitive plant, Bamboo & more","names":["Sensitive plant","Bamboo","Strangler fig","Ghost plant","Himalayan balsam"],"glyph":"🌀","tint":"#b088ff","items":["mimosa","bamboo","strangler-fig","corpse-lily","touch-me-not"],"idea":"The ghost plant has given up on sunlight and eats fungus instead. Four more plants do things that sound made up, like growing 36 inches in a day or throwing seeds 23 ft.","pages":[{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Five plants are waiting to meet you: a sensitive plant, bamboo, a strangler fig, a ghost plant, and a Himalayan balsam."},{"t":"p","text":"Each one does something surprising."}]},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Start with the sensitive plant. Its leaves fold shut the instant you touch them. They open again a few minutes later."},{"t":"fact","text":"Brush a leaf and the leaflets snap shut in pairs, starting from where you touched. The whole leaf stalk drops in just a second or two.","label":"The surprising speed"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What happens when you touch a sensitive plant's leaf?","options":["It stays open for hours","It turns pink","It folds shut quickly"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the first paragraph. It tells you exactly what happens.","explain":"The leaves fold shut quickly after you touch them."}}],"img":"mimosa"},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Bamboo is a grass that can stand 151 ft (46 m) tall. It is so big you might think it’s a tree, but it isn’t."},{"t":"term","word":"segment","text":"A part of the bamboo stem between two joints. The stem is hollow inside each segment, with a solid wall at every joint."},{"t":"fact","text":"The fastest-growing bamboo grows up to 36 in (91 cm) in just 24 hours. That's nearly 1.5 in (40 mm) every hour.","label":"The surprising growth"}],"img":"bamboo"},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the strangler fig, a fig that starts life high up in another tree and ends up standing where its host used to be."},{"t":"fact","text":"A strangler fig begins as a seed in a bird dropping, forty feet up in another tree. It sprouts there with no soil underneath it at all, living on rainwater and trapped leaf mold.","label":"The surprising start"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How does the strangler fig start its life?","options":["In the ground","High up in another tree","In the middle of a forest"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the second sentence. It tells you where the seed starts.","explain":"The strangler fig starts high up in another tree, from a seed in a bird dropping."}}],"img":"strangler-fig"},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the ghost plant, a flowering plant with no green anywhere. It has given up sunlight and takes its food from a fungus instead."},{"t":"fact","text":"The ghost plant is white, not pale green — completely white, waxy, and slightly see-through. There is no chlorophyll in it anywhere, so it cannot make food from sunlight.","label":"No green at all"}],"img":"corpse-lily"},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Next up is the Himalayan balsam, a tall pink-flowered plant from the Himalayas. When its seed pods are ripe, they explode at a touch and throw seeds clear across a riverbank."},{"t":"fact","text":"A ripe pod can fire seeds as far as 23 ft (7 m) when touched by anything — a finger. A raindrop. Or even a passing sheep.","label":"The explosive pods"},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is the farthest the Himalayan balsam's seed pods can throw seeds?","options":["10 ft","15 ft","23 ft"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look at the first sentence of the fact box.","explain":"The pods can throw seeds up to 23 ft (7 m)."}}],"img":"touch-me-not"},{"icon":"📊","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The sensitive plant folds its leaves when touched. Bamboo can grow 36 inches in a day. A strangler fig starts high in another tree. The ghost plant has no green and feeds on a fungus. Himalayan balsam throws its seeds as far as 23 ft."},{"t":"fact","text":"Each plant breaks a rule. One moves when touched. One is a grass as tall as a tree. One starts life in the air. One is a flower with no green, and one has a pod that explodes.","label":"Rule breakers"}]},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A sensitive plant that curls up when touched. Bamboo growing 36 inches in a day. Strangler figs growing around trees. Colorless ghost plants. Himalayan balsam seed pods exploding."},{"t":"p","text":"Five plants, each with a unique trick, showing how different they can be."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Sensitive plant","Leaves fold shut quickly when touched"],["Bamboo","Grows up to 36 inches in a day"],["Strangler fig","Starts as a seed in another tree"],["Ghost plant","Lives off a fungus, not sunlight"],["Himalayan balsam","Seed pods explode, throwing seeds 23 ft"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"How much can the fastest-growing bamboo grow in 24 hours?","options":["About 4 inches (10 cm)","Up to 36 inches (91 cm)","About 10 inches (25 cm)"],"answer":1,"explain":"Bamboo can grow up to 36 in (91 cm) in a single day."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is unique about the strangler fig’s starting point?","options":["It starts from a seed planted by farmers","It begins as a small plant on the forest floor","It begins as a seed in a bird dropping high up in another tree"],"answer":2,"explain":"The strangler fig's journey starts way up in the canopy."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why is the ghost plant completely white?","options":["Because it grows in the dark","Because it has no chlorophyll and relies on a fungus for food","Because it is covered in snow"],"answer":1,"explain":"Without chlorophyll, the ghost plant looks very different from other plants."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What special feature does the sensitive plant have?","options":["It produces a loud noise when touched","Its leaves fold shut quickly when touched","It changes color when touched"],"answer":1,"explain":"The sensitive plant reacts quickly to touch by folding its leaves."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How does bamboo's stem structure differ from that of trees?","options":["It has soft, spongy wood all the way through","It has solid walls at every joint","It is covered in thorns"],"answer":1,"explain":"Bamboo's segments are divided by solid walls at every joint."}]},{"id":"plants-odd-2","course":"plants","kind":"species","group":"odd","title":"Telegraph plant & more","names":["Telegraph plant","Squirting cucumber","Tumbleweed"],"glyph":"🌀","tint":"#b088ff","items":["telegraph-plant","squirting-cucumber","tumbleweed"],"idea":"A telegraph plant's leaflets swing in circles. A squirting cucumber fires its seeds like a jet. A tumbleweed dies on purpose and rolls away. Meet three plants that move in odd ways.","pages":[{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Three odd plants are waiting for you: a telegraph plant, a squirting cucumber and a tumbleweed."},{"t":"p","text":"Each one moves in its own strange way. Which one would you most like to meet first?"}]},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the telegraph plant. This tropical shrub has two small leaflets on every leaf that swing round in circles all day, fast enough to watch."},{"t":"fact","text":"The small leaflets rotate in jerky steps along an oval path. One full circuit takes about three to five minutes.","label":"Round and round"},{"t":"p","text":"It only moves in daylight, and only when the air is above 72°F (22°C). Cold stops it. Night stops it."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"When do the telegraph plant's leaflets move?","options":["Only at night, when it is cold","In daylight, when it is warm","Only when someone touches it"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the paragraph. It says when the leaflets move and when they stop.","explain":"The leaflets move only in daylight and only when the air is above 72°F (22°C)."}}],"img":"telegraph-plant"},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the squirting cucumber. This Mediterranean weed has a fruit that builds up pressure for days, blows itself off its own stalk and fires its seeds out in a jet."},{"t":"fact","text":"When the fruit is ripe it snaps off its stalk, and the hole becomes a nozzle. It fires a jet of slime and seeds as far as 23 to 26 ft (7 to 8 m).","label":"The jet"},{"t":"term","word":"elaterium","text":"The dried juice of this fruit, once a strong medicine, no longer used because it is too dangerous."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How far can a squirting cucumber fire its seeds?","options":["Only a few inches","Up to 26 ft (8 m)","About half a mile"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the box. It tells how far the jet of seeds goes.","explain":"The fruit fires a jet of slime and seeds as far as 23 to 26 ft (7 to 8 m)."}}],"img":"squirting-cucumber"},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Meet the tumbleweed. This plant dies on purpose, snaps itself off at the ground, and rolls across the desert scattering seed the whole way."},{"t":"fact","text":"Once the seed is ripe, the stem gives way at one spot just above the root. The root stays in the ground and the whole round bush leaves.","label":"Built to break"},{"t":"p","text":"One large Russian thistle, the classic tumbleweed, carries about 200,000 seeds. The ball shakes a few seeds loose with every bounce."},{"t":"check","q":{"type":"choice","prompt":"How does a tumbleweed spread its seeds?","options":["All of the seeds fall out at once","A few seeds shake loose each bounce","The seeds stay inside the dead bush"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at the last paragraph. It says what happens with every bounce.","explain":"The ball shakes a few seeds loose with every bounce, so one plant seeds a long line of ground."}}],"img":"tumbleweed"},{"icon":"📊","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Three plants that move in odd ways. The telegraph plant swings its leaflets in circles. The squirting cucumber fires its seeds in a jet. The tumbleweed rolls away across the desert."},{"t":"fact","text":"A squirting cucumber fruit is about 2 in (5 cm) long. A big tumbleweed is a tangled ball more than 3 ft (1 m) across. A telegraph plant leaflet makes a full circle in about three to five minutes.","label":"Sizes"}]},{"icon":"🌿","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A telegraph plant swinging its leaflets. A squirting cucumber firing its seeds. A tumbleweed rolling across the desert."},{"t":"p","text":"Three plants that move in odd ways."}]}],"game":{"pairs":[["Telegraph plant","Leaflets swing round in circles"],["Squirting cucumber","Fires its seeds out in a jet"],["Tumbleweed","Snaps off and rolls, scattering seed"]]},"quiz":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What do the telegraph plant's small leaflets do?","options":["Open only at night","Fold up when touched","Swing round in circles"],"answer":2,"explain":"The two small leaflets at the base of each leaf rotate along a path, round and round."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What stops the telegraph plant's leaflets from moving?","options":["Wind and rain","Cold and night","Hot sunshine"],"answer":1,"explain":"It moves only in daylight and only when the air is warm. Cold stops it. Night stops it."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What makes a squirting cucumber shoot its seeds?","options":["A strong gust of wind from the sea","A hungry bird tugging at the stalk","Pressure built up inside the fruit"],"answer":2,"explain":"The pressure builds up over days and is spent in a fraction of a second."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How does a tumbleweed come away from its root?","options":["The stem snaps just above the root","Animals pull it out of the ground","Farmers cut it down with a blade"],"answer":0,"explain":"There is a weak spot just above the root, and when the seed is ripe the stem gives way there."},{"type":"choice","prompt":"About how many seeds can one big Russian thistle carry?","options":["About 200","About 2 million","About 200,000"],"answer":2,"explain":"One large Russian thistle carries about 200,000 seeds."}]}];
